var SEARCH = [{"id": "E1D1", "p": "part1", "l": "1D1", "la": "Per *causam sui* intelligo id, cuius essentia involvit existentiam, sive id, cuius natura non potest concipi nisi existens.", "en": "By cause of itself I understand that whose essence involves existence, or that whose nature cannot be conceived except as existing."}, {"id": "E1D2", "p": "part1", "l": "1D2", "la": "Ea *res* dicitur in suo genere finita, quae alia eiusdem naturae terminari potest. Ex. gr. corpus dicitur finitum, quia aliud semper maius concipimus. Sic cogitatio alia cogitatione terminatur. At corpus non terminatur cogitatione, nec cogitatio corpore.", "en": "That thing is said to be finite in its own kind which can be limited by another of the same nature. For example, a body is called finite because we always conceive another that is greater. So a thought is limited by another thought. But a body is not limited by a thought, nor a thought by a body."}, {"id": "E1D3", "p": "part1", "l": "1D3", "la": "Per *substantiam* intelligo id quod in se est et per se concipitur; hoc est id cuius conceptus non indiget conceptu alterius rei, a quo formari debeat.", "en": "By substance I understand what is in itself and is conceived through itself, that is, that whose concept does not require the concept of another thing, from which it must be formed."}, {"id": "E1D4", "p": "part1", "l": "1D4", "la": "Per *attributum* intelligo id quod intellectus de substantia percipit tamquam eiusdem essentiam constituens.", "en": "By attribute I understand what the intellect perceives of a substance, as constituting its essence."}, {"id": "E1D5", "p": "part1", "l": "1D5", "la": "Per *modum* intelligo substantiae affectiones, sive id quod in alio est, per quod etiam concipitur.", "en": "By mode I understand the affections of a substance, or that which is in another, through which it is also conceived."}, {"id": "E1D6", "p": "part1", "l": "1D6", "la": "Per *Deum* intelligo ens absolute infinitum, hoc est, substantiam constantem infinitis attributis, quorum unumquodque aeternam et infinitam essentiam exprimit.", "en": "By God I understand a being absolutely infinite, that is, a substance consisting of infinite attributes, each of which expresses an eternal and infinite essence."}, {"id": "E1D6Ex", "p": "part1", "l": "1D6Ex", "la": "Dico absolute infinitum, non autem in suo genere. Quicquid enim in suo genere tantum infinitum est, infinita de eo attributa negare possumus; quod autem absolute infinitum est, ad eius essentiam pertinet, quicquid essentiam exprimit et negationem nullam involvit.", "en": "I say absolutely infinite, not infinite in its own kind. For of whatever is infinite only in its own kind, we can deny infinite attributes; but to the essence of what is absolutely infinite there pertains whatever expresses essence and involves no negation."}, {"id": "E1D7", "p": "part1", "l": "1D7", "la": "Ea *res libera* dicetur, quae ex sola suae naturae necessitate existit et a se sola ad agendum determinatur: *necessaria* autem, vel potius *coacta*, quae ab alio determinatur ad existendum et operandum certa ac determinata ratione.", "en": "That thing will be called free which exists from the necessity of its own nature alone, and is determined to act by itself alone. That thing, however, will be called necessary, or rather compelled, which is determined by another to exist and to operate in a certain and determinate manner."}, {"id": "E1D8", "p": "part1", "l": "1D8", "la": "Per *aeternitatem* intelligo ipsam existentiam, quatenus ex sola rei aeternae definitione necessario sequi concipitur.", "en": "By eternity I understand existence itself, insofar as it is conceived to follow necessarily from the definition alone of the eternal thing."}, {"id": "E1D8Ex", "p": "part1", "l": "1D8Ex", "la": "Talis enim existentia, ut aeterna veritas, sicut rei essentia, concipitur, proptereaque per durationem aut tempus explicari non potest, tametsi duratio principio et fine carere concipiatur.", "en": "For such existence is conceived as an eternal truth, like the essence of the thing, and therefore cannot be explained through duration or time, even if the duration is conceived to lack a beginning and an end."}, {"id": "E1A1", "p": "part1", "l": "1A1", "la": "Omnia quae sunt vel in se vel in alio sunt.", "en": "Whatever is, is either in itself or in another."}, {"id": "E1A2", "p": "part1", "l": "1A2", "la": "Id quod per aliud non potest concipi, per se concipi debet.", "en": "What cannot be conceived through another must be conceived through itself."}, {"id": "E1A3", "p": "part1", "l": "1A3", "la": "Ex data causa determinata necessario sequitur effectus, et contra si nulla detur determinata causa, impossibile est ut effectus sequatur.", "en": "From a given determinate cause the effect follows necessarily; and conversely, if no determinate cause is given, it is impossible for an effect to follow."}, {"id": "E1A4", "p": "part1", "l": "1A4", "la": "Effectus cognitio a cognitione causae dependet et eandem involvit.", "en": "Knowledge of an effect depends on, and involves, knowledge of its cause."}, {"id": "E1A5", "p": "part1", "l": "1A5", "la": "Quae nihil commune cum se invicem habent, etiam per se invicem intelligi non possunt, sive conceptus unius alterius conceptum non involvit.", "en": "Things that have nothing in common with one another also cannot be understood through one another, or the concept of the one does not involve the concept of the other."}, {"id": "E1A6", "p": "part1", "l": "1A6", "la": "Idea vera debet cum suo ideato convenire.", "en": "A true idea must agree with its object."}, {"id": "E1A7", "p": "part1", "l": "1A7", "la": "Quicquid ut non existens potest concipi, eius essentia non involvit existentiam.", "en": "If a thing can be conceived as not existing, its essence does not involve existence."}, {"id": "E1P1", "p": "part1", "l": "1P1", "la": "Substantia prior est natura suis affectionibus.", "en": "Substance is prior in nature to its affections."}, {"id": "E1P1D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P1D", "la": "Patet ex defin. 3. et 5.", "en": "This is evident from Defs. 3 and 5."}, {"id": "E1P2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P2", "la": "Duae substantiae diversa attributa habentes nihil inter se commune habent.", "en": "Two substances having different attributes have nothing in common with one another."}, {"id": "E1P2D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P2D", "la": "Patet etiam ex defin. 3. Unaquaeque enim in se debet esse et per se debet concipi, sive conceptus unius conceptum alterius non involvit.", "en": "This too is evident from Def. 3. For each must be in itself and must be conceived through itself, or the concept of the one does not involve the concept of the other."}, {"id": "E1P3", "p": "part1", "l": "1P3", "la": "Quae res nihil commune inter se habent, earum una alterius causa esse non potest.", "en": "Of things that have nothing in common with one another, one cannot be the cause of the other."}, {"id": "E1P3D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P3D", "la": "Si nihil commune cum se invicem habent, ergo (per axiom. 5.) nec per se invicem possunt intelligi, adeoque (per axiom. 4.) una alterius causa esse non potest. Q. E. D.", "en": "If they have nothing in common with one another, then (by Ax. 5) they cannot be understood through one another, and so (by Ax. 4) one cannot be the cause of the other. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P4", "p": "part1", "l": "1P4", "la": "Duae aut plures res distinctae vel inter se distinguuntur ex diversitate attributorum substantiarum, vel ex diversitate earundem affectionum.", "en": "Two or more distinct things are distinguished from one another either by a diversity of the attributes of the substances or by a diversity of their affections."}, {"id": "E1P4D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P4D", "la": "Omnia, quae sunt, vel in se, vel in alio sunt (per axiom. 1.), hoc est (per defin. 3. et 5.) extra intellectum nihil datur praeter substantias earumque affectiones. Nihil ergo extra intellectum datur, per quod plures res distingui inter se possunt praeter substantias, sive quod idem est (per defin. 4.) earum attributa earumque affectiones. Q. E. D.", "en": "Whatever is, is either in itself or in another (by Ax. 1), that is (by Defs. 3 and 5), outside the intellect there is nothing except substances and their affections. Therefore there is nothing outside the intellect through which a number of things can be distinguished from one another except substances, or what is the same (by Def. 4), their attributes, and their affections. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P5", "p": "part1", "l": "1P5", "la": "In rerum natura non possunt dari duae aut plures substantiae eiusdem naturae sive attributi.", "en": "In the nature of things there cannot be two or more substances of the same nature or attribute."}, {"id": "E1P5D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P5D", "la": "Si darentur plures distinctae, deberent inter se distingui vel ex diversitate attributorum, vel ex diversitate affectionum (per prop. praeced.). Si tantum ex diversitate attributorum, concedetur ergo, non dari nisi unam eiusdem attributi.", "en": "If there were several distinct substances, they would have to be distinguished from one another either by a diversity of attributes or by a diversity of affections (by the preceding Prop.). If only by a diversity of attributes, then it will be conceded that there is only one of the same attribute."}, {"id": "E1P5D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P5D", "la": "At si ex diversitate affectionum, cum substantia sit prior natura suis affectionibus (per prop. 1.), depositis ergo affectionibus et in se considerata, hoc est (per defin. 3. et axiom. 6.) vere considerata, non poterit concipi ab alia distingui, hoc est (per prop. praeced.) non poterunt dari plures, sed tantum una. Q. E. D.", "en": "But if by a diversity of affections, then, since substance is prior in nature to its affections (by Prop. 1), if the affections are put to one side and it is considered in itself, that is (by Def. 3 and Ax. 6), considered truly, it cannot be conceived to be distinguished from another, that is (by the preceding Prop.), there cannot be several substances, but only one. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P6", "p": "part1", "l": "1P6", "la": "Una substantia non potest produci ab alia substantia.", "en": "One substance cannot be produced by another substance."}, {"id": "E1P6D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P6D", "la": "In rerum natura non possunt dari duae substantiae eiusdem attributi (per prop. praeced.), hoc est (per prop. 2.) quae aliquid inter se commune habent; adeoque (per prop. 3.) una alterius causa esse nequit, sive una ab alia non potest produci. Q. E. D.", "en": "In the nature of things there cannot be two substances of the same attribute (by the preceding Prop.), that is (by Prop. 2), which have something in common with one another; and so (by Prop. 3) one cannot be the cause of the other, or one cannot be produced by the other. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P6C", "p": "part1", "l": "1P6C", "la": "Hinc sequitur substantiam ab alio produci non posse. Nam in rerum natura nihil datur praeter substantias, earumque affectiones, ut patet ex axiom. 1. et defin. 3. et 5. Atqui a substantia produci non potest (per prop. praeced.). Ergo substantia absolute ab alio produci non potest. Q. E. D.", "en": "From this it follows that a substance cannot be produced by anything else. For in the nature of things there is nothing except substances and their affections, as is evident from Ax. 1 and Defs. 3 and 5. But it cannot be produced by a substance (by the preceding Prop.). Therefore a substance absolutely cannot be produced by anything else. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P6CAlt", "p": "part1", "l": "1P6CAlt", "la": "Demonstratur hoc etiam facilius ex absurdo contradictorio. Nam si substantia ab alio posset produci, eius cognitio a cognitione suae causae deberet pendere (per axiom. 4.); adeoque (per defin. 3.) non esset substantia.", "en": "This is demonstrated even more easily from the absurdity of its contradictory. For if a substance could be produced by something else, the knowledge of it would have to depend on the knowledge of its cause (by Ax. 4); and so (by Def. 3) it would not be a substance."}, {"id": "E1P7", "p": "part1", "l": "1P7", "la": "Ad naturam substantiae pertinet existere.", "en": "It pertains to the nature of a substance to exist."}, {"id": "E1P7D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P7D", "la": "Substantia non potest produci ab alio (per coroll. prop. praeced.); erit itaque causa sui, id est (per defin. 1.) ipsius essentia involvit necessario existentiam, sive ad eius naturam pertinet existere. Q. E. D.", "en": "A substance cannot be produced by anything else (by the Cor. of the preceding Prop.); it will therefore be the cause of itself, that is (by Def. 1), its essence necessarily involves existence, or it pertains to its nature to exist. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P8", "p": "part1", "l": "1P8", "la": "Omnis substantia est necessario infinita.", "en": "Every substance is necessarily infinite."}, {"id": "E1P8D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P8D", "la": "Substantia unius attributi non nisi unica existit (per prop. 5.) et ad ipsius naturam pertinet existere (per prop. 7.). Erit ergo de ipsius natura vel finita vel infinita existere. At non finita. Nam (per defin. 2.) deberet terminari ab alia eiusdem naturae, quae etiam necessario deberet existere (per prop. 7.); adeoque darentur duae substantiae eiusdem attributi, quod est absurdum (per prop. 5.). Existit ergo infinita. Q. E. D.", "en": "A substance of one attribute exists only as unique (by Prop. 5), and it pertains to its nature to exist (by Prop. 7). It will therefore be of its nature to exist either as finite or as infinite. But not as finite. For (by Def. 2) it would have to be limited by another of the same nature, which would also have to exist necessarily (by Prop. 7); and so there would be two substances of the same attribute, which is absurd (by Prop. 5). It exists, therefore, as infinite. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P8S1", "p": "part1", "l": "1P8S1", "la": "Cum finitum esse revera sit ex parte negatio, et infinitum absoluta affirmatio existentiae alicuius naturae, sequitur ergo ex sola prop. 7. omnem substantiam debere esse infinitam.", "en": "Since being finite is in truth partly a negation, and being infinite the absolute affirmation of the existence of some nature, it follows from Prop. 7 alone that every substance must be infinite."}, {"id": "E1P8S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P8S2", "la": "Non dubito, quin omnibus, qui de rebus confuse iudicant, nec res per primas suas causas noscere consueverunt, difficile sit, demonstrationem prop. 7. concipere; nimirum quia non distinguunt inter modificationes substantiarum et ipsas substantias, neque sciunt, quomodo res producuntur.", "en": "I do not doubt that for all who judge of things confusedly, and are not accustomed to know things through their first causes, it is difficult to conceive the demonstration of Prop. 7; namely, because they do not distinguish between the modifications of substances and substances themselves, nor do they know how things are produced."}, {"id": "E1P8S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P8S2", "la": "Unde fit, ut principium, quod res naturales habere vident, substantiis affingant. Qui enim veras rerum causas ignorant, omnia confundunt et sine ulla mentis repugnantia tam arbores, quam homines, loquentes fingunt et homines tam ex lapidibus quam ex semine formari et quascumque formas in alias quascumque mutari imaginantur. Sic etiam, qui naturam divinam cum humana confundunt, facile Deo affectus humanos tribuunt, praesertim quamdiu etiam ignorant, quomodo affectus in mente producuntur.", "en": "Hence it comes about that they fictitiously ascribe to substances the beginning which they see natural things have. For those who do not know the true causes of things confuse everything, and without any repugnance of mind feign that trees, no less than men, speak, and imagine that men are formed from stones as well as from seed, and that any forms whatever are changed into any others whatever. So also those who confuse the divine nature with the human easily ascribe human affects to God, especially so long as they are also ignorant of how affects are produced in the mind."}, {"id": "E1P8S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P8S2", "la": "Si autem homines ad naturam substantiae attenderent, minime de veritate prop. 7. dubitarent; imo haec prop. omnibus axioma esset et inter notiones communes numeraretur. Nam per *substantiam* intelligerent id quod in se est et per se concipitur, hoc est, id cuius cognitio non indiget cognitione alterius rei. Per *modificationes* autem id quod in alio est et quarum conceptus a conceptu rei, in qua sunt, formatur.", "en": "But if men would attend to the nature of substance, they would have no doubt at all of the truth of Prop. 7; indeed, this proposition would be an axiom for all, and would be numbered among the common notions. For by substance they would understand what is in itself and is conceived through itself, that is, that the knowledge of which does not require the knowledge of another thing; but by modifications, what is in another, and whose concept is formed from the concept of the thing in which they are."}, {"id": "E1P8S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P8S2", "la": "Quocirca modificationum non existentium veras ideas possumus habere; quandoquidem, quamvis non existant actu extra intellectum, earum tamen essentia ita in alio comprehenditur, ut per idem concipi possint. Verum substantiarum veritas extra intellectum non est, nisi in se ipsis, quia per se concipiuntur.", "en": "This is why we can have true ideas of modifications that do not exist; for although they do not actually exist outside the intellect, their essence is nevertheless comprehended in another in such a way that they can be conceived through it. But the truth of substances is not outside the intellect except in themselves, because they are conceived through themselves."}, {"id": "E1P8S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P8S2", "la": "Si quis ergo diceret, se claram et distinctam, hoc est, veram ideam substantiae habere et nihilominus dubitare, num talis substantia existat, idem hercle esset, ac si diceret, se veram habere ideam et nihilominus dubitare, num falsa sit (ut satis attendenti fit manifestum); vel, si quis statuat, substantiam creari, simul statuit, ideam falsam factam esse veram, quo sane nihil absurdius concipi potest. Adeoque fatendum necessario est, substantiae existentiam, sicut eius essentiam, aeternam esse veritatem.", "en": "If someone, therefore, were to say that he had a clear and distinct, that is, a true idea of a substance, and nevertheless doubted whether such a substance existed, that would really be the same as if he were to say that he had a true idea and nevertheless doubted whether it was false (as is manifest to anyone who attends sufficiently); or if someone maintains that a substance is created, he maintains at the same time that a false idea has become true, than which surely nothing more absurd can be conceived. So it must necessarily be admitted that the existence of a substance, like its essence, is an eternal truth."}, {"id": "E1P8S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P8S2", "la": "Atque hinc alio modo concludere possumus, non dari nisi unicam eiusdem naturae; quod hic ostendere, operae pretium esse duxi. Ut autem hoc ordine faciam, notandum est, 1. veram uniuscuiusque rei definitionem nihil involvere, neque exprimere praeter rei definitae naturam. Ex quo sequitur hoc 2. nempe nullam definitionem certum aliquem numerum individuorum involvere, neque exprimere, quandoquidem nihil aliud exprimit, quam naturam rei definitae. Ex. gr. definitio trianguli nihil aliud exprimit, quam simplicem naturam trianguli; at non certum aliquem triangulorum numerum.", "en": "And from this we can conclude in another way that there is only one substance of the same nature, which I have thought it worthwhile to show here. But to do this in order, it must be noted, 1. that the true definition of each thing involves and expresses nothing except the nature of the thing defined. From which there follows this, 2. namely, that no definition involves or expresses any certain number of individuals, since it expresses nothing other than the nature of the thing defined. For example, the definition of the triangle expresses nothing other than the simple nature of the triangle, but not any certain number of triangles."}, {"id": "E1P8S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P8S2", "la": "3. Notandum, dari necessario uniuscuiusque rei existentis certam aliquam causam, propter quam existit. 4. Denique notandum, hanc causam, propter quam aliqua res existit, vel debere contineri in ipsa natura et definitione rei existentis (nimirum quod ad ipsius naturam pertinet existere), vel debere extra ipsam dari.", "en": "3. It must be noted that for each existing thing there is necessarily some certain cause on account of which it exists. 4. Finally, it must be noted that this cause, on account of which some thing exists, either must be contained in the very nature and definition of the existing thing (namely, because it pertains to its nature to exist), or must be given outside it."}, {"id": "E1P8S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P8S2", "la": "His positis sequitur, quod, si in natura certus aliquis numerus individuorum existat, debeat necessario dari causa, cur illa individua et cur non plura nec pauciora existunt. Si ex. gr. in rerum natura viginti homines existant (quos maioris perspicuitatis causa suppono simul existere, nec alios antea in natura exstitisse), non satis erit (ut scilicet rationem reddamus, cur viginti homines existant) causam naturae humanae in genere ostendere; sed insuper necesse erit, causam ostendere, cur non plures, nec pauciores, quam viginti existant; quandoquidem (per notam 3.) uniuscuiusque debet necessario dari causa, cur existat.", "en": "With these points established, it follows that if some certain number of individuals exists in nature, there must necessarily be a cause why those individuals exist, and why neither more nor fewer. If, for example, twenty men exist in the nature of things (whom, for the sake of greater clarity, I suppose to exist at the same time, and that no others existed in nature before), it will not be enough (in order, that is, to give a reason why twenty men exist) to show the cause of human nature in general; but it will be necessary in addition to show the cause why not more and not fewer than twenty exist, since (by note 3) there must necessarily be a cause for each, why it exists."}, {"id": "E1P8S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P8S2", "la": "At haec causa (per notam 2. et 3.) non potest in ipsa natura humana contineri, quandoquidem vera hominis definitio numerum vicenarium non involvit. Adeoque (per notam. 4.) causa, cur hi viginti homines existunt et consequenter cur unusquisque existit, debet necessario extra unumquemque dari et propterea absolute concludendum, omne id, cuius naturae plura individua existere possunt, debere necessario, ut existant, causam externam habere.", "en": "But this cause (by notes 2 and 3) cannot be contained in human nature itself, since the true definition of man does not involve the number twenty. And so (by note 4) the cause why these twenty men exist, and consequently why each of them exists, must necessarily be given outside each of them; and therefore it must be concluded absolutely that whatever is of such a nature that several individuals of it can exist must necessarily, in order that they may exist, have an external cause."}, {"id": "E1P8S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P8S2", "la": "Iam quoniam ad naturam substantiae (per iam ostensa in hoc schol.) pertinet existere, debet eius definitio necessariam existentiam involvere et consequenter ex sola eius definitione debet ipsius existentia concludi. At ex ipsius definitione (ut iam ex nota 2. et 3. ostendimus) non potest sequi plurium substantiarum existentia. Sequitur ergo ex ea necessario, unicam tantum eiusdem naturae existere, ut proponebatur.", "en": "Now since it pertains to the nature of substance to exist (by what has already been shown in this Scholium), its definition must involve necessary existence, and consequently its existence must be inferred from its definition alone. But from its definition (as we have already shown from notes 2 and 3) the existence of several substances cannot follow. It follows necessarily from it, therefore, that there exists only one substance of the same nature, as was proposed."}, {"id": "E1P9", "p": "part1", "l": "1P9", "la": "Quo plus realitatis aut esse unaquaeque res habet, eo plura attributa ipsi competunt.", "en": "The more reality or being each thing has, the more attributes belong to it."}, {"id": "E1P9D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P9D", "la": "Patet ex defin. 4.", "en": "This is evident from Def. 4."}, {"id": "E1P10", "p": "part1", "l": "1P10", "la": "Unumquodque unius substantiae attributum per se concipi debet.", "en": "Each attribute of one substance must be conceived through itself."}, {"id": "E1P10D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P10D", "la": "Attributum enim est id quod intellectus de substantia percipit tamquam eius essentiam constituens (per defin. 4.); adeoque (per defin. 3.) per se concipi debet. Q. E. D.", "en": "For an attribute is what the intellect perceives of a substance as constituting its essence (by Def. 4); and so (by Def. 3) it must be conceived through itself. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P10S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P10S", "la": "Ex his apparet, quod, quamvis duo attributa realiter distincta concipiantur, hoc est, unum sine ope alterius, non possumus tamen inde concludere, ipsa duo entia, sive duas diversas substantias constituere. Id enim est de natura substantiae, ut unumquodque eius attributorum per se concipiatur; quandoquidem omnia quae habet attributa simul in ipsa semper fuerunt, nec unum ab alio produci potuit; sed unumquodque realitatem sive esse substantiae exprimit.", "en": "From these things it is apparent that although two attributes may be conceived to be really distinct, that is, one without the aid of the other, we still cannot infer from this that they constitute two beings, or two different substances. For it is of the nature of substance that each of its attributes is conceived through itself, since all the attributes it has have always been in it together, and one could not be produced by another; but each expresses the reality, or being, of substance."}, {"id": "E1P10S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P10S", "la": "Longe ergo abest, ut absurdum sit, uni substantiae plura attributa tribuere; quin nihil in natura clarius, quam quod unumquodque ens sub aliquo attributo debeat concipi, et quo plus realitatis aut esse habeat, eo plura attributa, quae et necessitatem sive aeternitatem et infinitatem exprimunt, habeat; et consequenter nihil etiam clarius, quam quod ens absolute infinitum necessario sit definiendum (ut defin. 6. tradidimus) ens, quod constat infinitis attributis, quorum unumquodque aeternam et infinitam certam essentiam exprimit.", "en": "So it is far from absurd to ascribe several attributes to one substance; indeed nothing in nature is clearer than that each being must be conceived under some attribute, and that the more reality or being it has, the more attributes it has which express necessity, or eternity, and infinity; and consequently nothing is clearer, either, than that a being absolutely infinite must necessarily be defined (as we taught in Def. 6) as a being which consists of infinite attributes, each of which expresses a certain eternal and infinite essence."}, {"id": "E1P10S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P10S", "la": "Si quis autem iam quaerit, ex quo ergo signo diversitatem substantiarum poterimus dignoscere, legat sequentes propositiones, quae ostendunt in rerum natura non nisi unicam substantiam existere, eamque absolute infinitam esse, quapropter id signum frustra quaereretur.", "en": "But if someone now asks by what sign, then, we shall be able to recognize a diversity of substances, let him read the following propositions, which show that in the nature of things there exists only one substance, and that it is absolutely infinite; so that such a sign would be sought in vain."}, {"id": "E1P11", "p": "part1", "l": "1P11", "la": "Deus, sive substantia constans infinitis attributis, quorum unumquodque aeternam et infinitam essentiam exprimit, necessario existit.", "en": "God, or a substance consisting of infinite attributes, each of which expresses an eternal and infinite essence, necessarily exists."}, {"id": "E1P11D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P11D", "la": "Si negas, concipe, si fieri potest, Deum non existere. Ergo (per axiom. 7.) eius essentia non involvit existentiam. Atqui hoc (per prop. 7.) est absurdum. Ergo Deus necessario existit. Q. E. D.", "en": "If you deny this, conceive, if it can be done, that God does not exist. Then (by Ax. 7) his essence does not involve existence. But this (by Prop. 7) is absurd. Therefore God necessarily exists. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P11D2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P11D2", "la": "Cuiuscumque rei assignari debet causa seu ratio, tam cur existit, quam cur non existit. Ex. gr. si triangulus existit, ratio seu causa dari debet, cur existit; si autem non existit, ratio etiam seu causa dari debet, quae impedit quominus existat, sive quae eius existentiam tollat. Haec vero ratio seu causa vel in natura rei contineri debet, vel extra ipsam.", "en": "For each thing there must be assigned a cause, or reason, as much for why it exists as for why it does not exist. For example, if a triangle exists, there must be a reason or cause why it exists; but if it does not exist, there must also be a reason or cause which prevents it from existing, or which takes its existence away. This reason, or cause, must either be contained in the nature of the thing, or be outside it."}, {"id": "E1P11D2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P11D2", "la": "Ex. gr. rationem, cur circulus quadratus non existat, ipsa eius natura indicat; nimirum, quia contradictionem involvit. Cur autem contra substantia existat, ex sola etiam eius natura sequitur, quia scilicet existentiam involvit (vide prop. 7.).", "en": "For example, the reason why a square circle does not exist is indicated by its very nature, namely, because it involves a contradiction. Why, on the other hand, a substance exists also follows from its nature alone, namely, because it involves existence (see Prop. 7)."}, {"id": "E1P11D2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P11D2", "la": "At ratio, cur circulus vel triangulus existit, vel cur non existit, ex eorum natura non sequitur, sed ex ordine universae naturae corporeae. Ex eo enim sequi debet, vel iam triangulum necessario existere, vel impossibile esse ut iam existat. Atque haec per se manifesta sunt.", "en": "But the reason why a circle or a triangle exists, or why it does not exist, does not follow from their nature, but from the order of the whole of corporeal nature. For from this it must follow either that the triangle now necessarily exists, or that it is impossible that it should now exist. And these things are self-evident."}, {"id": "E1P11D2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P11D2", "la": "Ex quibus sequitur, id necessario existere, cuius nulla ratio nec causa datur, quae impedit quominus existat. Si itaque nulla ratio nec causa dari possit, quae impedit quominus Deus existat, vel quae eius existentiam tollat, omnino concludendum est, eundem necessario existere.", "en": "From which it follows that a thing necessarily exists if there is no reason or cause which prevents it from existing. Therefore, if there can be no reason or cause which prevents God from existing, or which takes his existence away, it must certainly be concluded that he necessarily exists."}, {"id": "E1P11D2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P11D2", "la": "At si talis ratio seu causa daretur, ea vel in ipsa Dei natura, vel extra ipsam dari deberet, hoc est, in alia substantia alterius naturae. Nam si eiusdem naturae esset, eo ipso concederetur dari Deum. At substantia, quae alterius esset naturae, nihil cum Deo commune habere (per prop. 2.), adeoque neque eius existentiam ponere neque tollere posset.", "en": "But if there were such a reason, or cause, it would have to be either in the very nature of God or outside it, that is, in another substance of another nature. For if it were of the same nature, by that very fact it would be granted that God exists. But a substance that was of another nature would have nothing in common with God (by Prop. 2), and so could neither posit nor take away his existence."}, {"id": "E1P11D2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P11D2", "la": "Cum igitur ratio seu causa, quae divinam existentiam tollat, extra divinam naturam dari non possit, debebit necessario dari, siquidem non existit, in ipsa eius natura, quae propterea contradictionem involveret. Atqui hoc de ente absolute infinito et summe perfecto affirmare, absurdum est; ergo nec in Deo, nec extra Deum ulla causa seu ratio datur, quae eius existentiam tollat, ac proinde Deus necessario existit. Q. E. D.", "en": "Since, therefore, a reason or cause which would take away the divine existence cannot be outside the divine nature, it will necessarily have to be, if indeed God does not exist, in his very nature, which would therefore involve a contradiction. But to affirm this of a being absolutely infinite and supremely perfect is absurd; therefore, neither in God nor outside God is there any cause or reason which takes his existence away, and hence God necessarily exists. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P11D3", "p": "part1", "l": "1P11D3", "la": "Posse non existere impotentia est et contra posse existere potentia est (ut per se notum). Si itaque id, quod iam necessario existit, non nisi entia finita sunt, sunt ergo entia finita potentiora ente absolute infinito: atque hoc (ut per se notum) absurdum est. Ergo vel nihil existit, vel ens absolute infinitum necessario etiam existit.", "en": "To be able not to exist is lack of power, and conversely, to be able to exist is power (as is self-evident). So, if what now necessarily exists is only finite beings, then finite beings are more powerful than a being absolutely infinite: and this (as is self-evident) is absurd. So either nothing exists, or a being absolutely infinite also necessarily exists."}, {"id": "E1P11D3", "p": "part1", "l": "1P11D3", "la": "Atqui nos, vel in nobis, vel in alio, quod necessario existit, existimus (vid.axiom. 1. et prop. 7.). Ergo ens absolute infinitum, hoc est (per defin. 6.), Deus necessario existit. Q. E. D.", "en": "But we exist, either in ourselves or in something else which necessarily exists (see Ax. 1 and Prop. 7). Therefore a being absolutely infinite, that is (by Def. 6), God, necessarily exists. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P11S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P11S", "la": "In hac ultima demonstratione Dei existentiam a posteriori ostendere volui, ut demonstratio facilius perciperetur; non autem propterea, quod ex hoc eodem fundamento Dei existentia a priori non sequatur. Nam cum posse existere potentia sit, sequitur, quo plus realitatis alicuius rei naturae competit, eo plus virium a se habere, ut existat; adeoque ens absolute infinitum, sive Deum infinitam absolute potentiam existendi a se habere, qui propterea absolute existit.", "en": "In this last demonstration I wanted to show God’s existence a posteriori, so that the demonstration would be perceived more easily; but not because God’s existence does not follow a priori from this same foundation. For since being able to exist is power, it follows that the more reality belongs to the nature of a thing, the more powers it has, of itself, to exist; and so a being absolutely infinite, or God, has of himself an absolutely infinite power of existing, and therefore he exists absolutely."}, {"id": "E1P11S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P11S", "la": "Multi tamen forsan non facile huius demonstrationis evidentiam videre poterunt, quia assueti sunt, eas solummodo res contemplari, quae a causis externis fluunt; et ex his, quae cito fiunt, hoc est, quae facile existunt, eas etiam facile perire vident et contra eas res factu difficiliores iudicant, hoc est, ad existendum non adeo faciles, ad quas plura pertinere concipiunt. Verum ut ab his praeiudiciis liberentur, non opus habeo hic ostendere, qua ratione hoc enunciatum: quod cito fit, cito perit, verum sit, nec etiam, an respectu totius naturae omnia aeque facilia sint, an secus.", "en": "Still, perhaps many will not easily be able to see the evidentness of this demonstration, because they have been accustomed to contemplate only those things that flow from external causes; and of these they see that those which quickly come to be, that is, which easily exist, also easily perish, and conversely they judge those things more difficult to bring about, that is, not so easy to exist, to which they conceive more things to pertain. But to free them from these prejudices, I have no need to show here in what way this saying, what quickly comes to be quickly perishes, is true, nor even whether, in respect of the whole of nature, all things are equally easy, or not."}, {"id": "E1P11S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P11S", "la": "Sed hoc tantum notare sufficit, me hic non loqui de rebus, quae a causis externis fiunt, sed de solis substantiis, quae (per prop. 6.) a nulla causa externa produci possunt. Res enim, quae a causis externis fiunt, sive eae multis partibus constent sive paucis, quicquid perfectionis sive realitatis habent, id omne virtuti causae externae debetur, adeoque earum existentia ex sola perfectione causae externae, non autem suae oritur. Contra, quicquid substantia perfectionis habet, nulli causae externae debetur; quare eius etiam existentia ex sola eius natura sequi debet, quae proinde nihil aliud est, quam eius essentia.", "en": "But it suffices to note only this, that I am not speaking here of things that come to be from external causes, but only of substances, which (by Prop. 6) can be produced by no external cause. For things that come to be from external causes, whether they consist of many parts or of few, owe all the perfection, or reality, they have to the power of the external cause; and so their existence arises only from the perfection of the external cause, and not from their own. On the other hand, whatever perfection a substance has is owed to no external cause; so its existence too must follow from its nature alone, and is therefore nothing other than its essence."}, {"id": "E1P11S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P11S", "la": "Perfectio igitur rei existentiam non tollit, sed contra ponit; imperfectio autem contra eandem tollit, adeoque de nullius rei existentia certiores esse possumus, quam de existentia entis absolute infiniti seu perfecti, hoc est, Dei. Nam quandoquidem eius essentia omnem imperfectionem secludit absolutamque perfectionem involvit, eo ipso omnem causam dubitandi de ipsius existentia tollit, summamque de eadem certitudinem dat, quod mediocriter attendenti perspicuum fore credo.", "en": "Perfection, therefore, does not take away the existence of a thing, but on the contrary posits it; imperfection, on the other hand, takes it away; and so there is no existence of which we can be more certain than of the existence of a being absolutely infinite, or perfect, that is, God. For since his essence excludes all imperfection and involves absolute perfection, by that very fact it takes away every cause of doubting his existence and gives the greatest certainty concerning it, which I believe will be clear to anyone who attends even moderately."}, {"id": "E1P12", "p": "part1", "l": "1P12", "la": "Nullum substantiae attributum potest vere concipi, ex quo sequatur, substantiam posse dividi.", "en": "No attribute of a substance can be truly conceived from which it would follow that the substance can be divided."}, {"id": "E1P12D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P12D", "la": "Partes enim, in quas substantia sic concepta divideretur, vel naturam substantiae retinebunt, vel non. Si primum, tum (per prop. 8.) unaquaeque pars debebit esse infinita et (per prop. 6.) causa sui et (per prop. 5.) constare debebit ex diverso attributo, adeoque ex una substantia plures constitui poterunt, quod (per prop. 6.) est absurdum.", "en": "For the parts into which a substance so conceived would be divided either will retain the nature of substance or will not. If the first, then (by Prop. 8) each part will have to be infinite, and (by Prop. 6) cause of itself, and (by Prop. 5) will have to consist of a different attribute; and so many substances could be constituted from one, which (by Prop. 6) is absurd."}, {"id": "E1P12D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P12D", "la": "Adde, quod partes (per prop. 2.) nihil commune cum suo toto haberent, et totum (per defin. 4. et prop. 10.) absque suis partibus et esse et concipi posset, quod absurdum esse, nemo dubitare poterit. Si autem secundum ponatur, quod scilicet partes naturam substantiae non retinebunt; ergo, cum tota substantia in aequales partes esset divisa, naturam substantiae amitteret et esse desineret, quod (per prop. 7.) est absurdum.", "en": "Add that the parts (by Prop. 2) would have nothing in common with their whole, and the whole (by Def. 4 and Prop. 10) could both be and be conceived without its parts, which no one will be able to doubt is absurd. But if the second is asserted, namely, that the parts will not retain the nature of substance, then, when the whole substance had been divided into equal parts, it would lose the nature of substance and would cease to be, which (by Prop. 7) is absurd."}, {"id": "E1P13", "p": "part1", "l": "1P13", "la": "Substantia absolute infinita est indivisibilis.", "en": "A substance which is absolutely infinite is indivisible."}, {"id": "E1P13D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P13D", "la": "Si enim divisibilis esset, partes, in quas divideretur, vel naturam substantiae absolute infinitae retinebunt, vel non. Si primum, dabuntur ergo plures substantiae eiusdem naturae, quod (per prop. 5.) est absurdum. Si secundum ponatur, ergo (ut supra) poterit substantia absolute infinita desinere esse, quod (per prop. 11.) est etiam absurdum.", "en": "For if it were divisible, the parts into which it would be divided either will retain the nature of an absolutely infinite substance or will not. If the first, there will be several substances of the same nature, which (by Prop. 5) is absurd. If the second is asserted, then (as above) a substance absolutely infinite could cease to be, which (by Prop. 11) is also absurd."}, {"id": "E1P13C", "p": "part1", "l": "1P13C", "la": "Ex his sequitur, nullam substantiam et consequenter nullam substantiam corpoream, quatenus substantia est, esse divisibilem.", "en": "From these it follows that no substance, and consequently no corporeal substance, insofar as it is a substance, is divisible."}, {"id": "E1P13S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P13S", "la": "Quod substantia sit indivisibilis, simplicius ex hoc solo intelligitur, quod natura substantiae non potest concipi nisi infinita et quod per partem substantiae nihil aliud intelligi potest, quam substantia finita, quod (per prop. 8.) manifestam contradictionem implicat.", "en": "That substance is indivisible is understood more simply from this alone, that the nature of substance cannot be conceived except as infinite, and that by a part of a substance nothing can be understood except a finite substance, which (by Prop. 8) implies a manifest contradiction."}, {"id": "E1P14", "p": "part1", "l": "1P14", "la": "Praeter Deum nulla dari neque concipi potest substantia.", "en": "Except God, no substance can be or be conceived."}, {"id": "E1P14D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P14D", "la": "Cum Deus sit ens absolute infinitum, de quo nullum attributum, quod essentiam substantiae exprimit, negari potest (per defin. 6.), isque necessario existat (per prop. 11.), si aliqua substantia praeter Deum daretur, ea explicari deberet per aliquod attributum Dei, sicque duae substantiae eiusdem attributi existerent, quod (per prop. 5.) est absurdum; adeoque nulla substantia extra Deum dari potest et consequenter non etiam concipi.", "en": "Since God is a being absolutely infinite, of whom no attribute which expresses an essence of substance can be denied (by Def. 6), and he necessarily exists (by Prop. 11), if there were any substance except God, it would have to be explained through some attribute of God, and so two substances of the same attribute would exist, which (by Prop. 5) is absurd; and so no substance outside God can be, and consequently none can be conceived either."}, {"id": "E1P14D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P14D", "la": "Nam si posset concipi, deberet necessario concipi, ut existens; atqui hoc (per primam partem huius demonst.) est absurdum. Ergo extra Deum nulla dari, neque concipi potest substantia. Q. E. D.", "en": "For if it could be conceived, it would necessarily have to be conceived as existing; but this (by the first part of this demonstration) is absurd. Therefore, outside God no substance can be or be conceived. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P14C1", "p": "part1", "l": "1P14C1", "la": "Hinc clarissime sequitur 1. Deum esse unicum, hoc est (per defin. 6.) in rerum natura non nisi unam substantiam dari, eamque absolute infinitam esse, ut in scholio prop. 10. iam innuimus.", "en": "From this it follows most clearly, 1. that God is unique, that is (by Def. 6), that in the nature of things there is only one substance, and that it is absolutely infinite, as we have already indicated in the Scholium to Prop. 10."}, {"id": "E1P14C2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P14C2", "la": "Sequitur 2. rem extensam et rem cogitantem, vel Dei attributa esse, vel (per axiom. 1.) affectiones attributorum Dei.", "en": "It follows, 2. that the extended thing and the thinking thing are either attributes of God, or (by Ax. 1) affections of the attributes of God."}, {"id": "E1P15", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15", "la": "Quicquid est in Deo est, et nihil sine Deo esse neque concipi potest.", "en": "Whatever is, is in God, and nothing can be or be conceived without God."}, {"id": "E1P15D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15D", "la": "Praeter Deum nulla datur neque concipi potest substantia (per prop. 14.), hoc est (per defin. 3.) res, quae in se est et per se concipitur. Modi autem (per defin. 5.) sine substantia nec esse, nec concipi possunt; quare hi in sola divina natura esse et per ipsam solam concipi possunt. Atqui praeter substantias et modos nil datur (per axiom. 1.). Ergo nihil sine Deo esse, neque concipi potest. Q. E. D.", "en": "Except God there is no substance, nor can any be conceived (by Prop. 14), that is (by Def. 3), a thing that is in itself and is conceived through itself. But modes (by Def. 5) can neither be nor be conceived without substance; so they can be in the divine nature alone, and can be conceived through it alone. But except substances and modes there is nothing (by Ax. 1). Therefore nothing can be or be conceived without God. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P15S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Sunt qui Deum instar hominis corpore et mente constantem atque passionibus obnoxium fingunt. Sed quam longe hi a vera Dei cognitione aberrent, satis ex iam demonstratis constat. Sed hos mitto. Nam omnes, qui naturam divinam aliquo modo contemplati sunt, Deum esse corporeum, negant. Quod etiam optime probant ex eo, quod per corpus intelligimus quamcumque quantitatem, longam, latam et profundam, certa aliqua figura terminatam, quo nihil absurdius de Deo, ente scilicet absolute infinito, dici potest.", "en": "There are those who feign a God, like man, consisting of a body and a mind, and subject to passions. But how far they wander from the true knowledge of God is sufficiently established by what has already been demonstrated. But these I dismiss. For all who have in any way contemplated the divine nature deny that God is corporeal. They also prove this very well from the fact that by body we understand any quantity, with length, breadth and depth, bounded by some certain figure; and nothing more absurd than this can be said of God, a being, that is, absolutely infinite."}, {"id": "E1P15S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Attamen interim aliis rationibus, quibus hoc idem demonstrare conantur, clare ostendunt, se substantiam ipsam corpoream sive extensam a natura divina omnino removere, atque ipsam a Deo creatam statuunt. Ex qua autem divina potentia creari potuerit, prorsus ignorant; quod clare ostendit, illos id quod ipsimet dicunt, non intelligere. Ego saltem satis clare, meo quidem iudicio, demonstravi (vide coroll. prop. 6. et schol. 2. prop. 8.) nullam substantiam ab alio posse produci vel creari.", "en": "But meanwhile, by other arguments by which they try to demonstrate this same point, they clearly show that they entirely remove corporeal, or extended, substance itself from the divine nature, and they maintain that it is created by God. But by what divine power it could have been created they are utterly ignorant; which clearly shows that they do not understand what they themselves say. I, at least, have demonstrated clearly enough, in my judgment at any rate, that no substance can be produced or created by anything else (see Cor. Prop. 6 and Schol. 2 Prop. 8)."}, {"id": "E1P15S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Porro prop. 14. ostendimus, praeter Deum nullam dari neque concipi posse substantiam. Atque hinc conclusimus, substantiam extensam unum ex infinitis Dei attributis esse. Verum, ad pleniorem explicationem adversariorum argumenta refutabo, quae omnia huc redeunt.", "en": "Further, we have shown in Prop. 14 that except God no substance can be or be conceived. And from this we have concluded that extended substance is one of the infinite attributes of God. But for a fuller explanation I shall refute my adversaries’ arguments, which all come down to these."}, {"id": "E1P15S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Primo, quod substantia corporea, quatenus substantia, constat, ut putant, partibus; et ideo eandem infinitam posse esse, et consequenter ad Deum pertinere posse negant. Atque hoc multis exemplis explicant, ex quibus unum, aut alterum afferam.", "en": "First, that corporeal substance, insofar as it is substance, consists, as they think, of parts; and therefore they deny that it can be infinite, and consequently that it can pertain to God. And they explain this by many examples, of which I shall bring forward one or two."}, {"id": "E1P15S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Si substantia corporea, aiunt, est infinita, concipiatur in duas partes dividi; erit unaquaeque pars, vel finita, vel infinita. Si illud, componitur ergo infinitum ex duabus partibus finitis, quod est absurdum. Si hoc, datur ergo infinitum duplo maius alio infinito, quod etiam est absurdum.", "en": "If corporeal substance, they say, is infinite, let it be conceived to be divided into two parts; each part will be either finite or infinite. If the former, then the infinite is composed of two finite parts, which is absurd. If the latter, then there is an infinite twice as great as another infinite, which is also absurd."}, {"id": "E1P15S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Porro, si quantitas infinita mensuratur partibus pedes aequantibus, infinitis talibus partibus constare debebit, ut et si partibus mensuretur digitos aequantibus; ac propterea unus numerus infinitus erit duodecies maior alio infinito.", "en": "Further, if an infinite quantity is measured by parts each equal to a foot, it will have to consist of infinitely many such parts, as it will also if it is measured by parts each equal to an inch; and therefore one infinite number will be twelve times greater than another infinite number."}, {"id": "E1P15S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Denique, si ex uno puncto infinitae cuiusdam quantitatis concipiatur, duas lineas, ut AB, AC, certa ac determinata in initio distantia in infinitum protendi; certum est, distantiam inter B et C continuo augeri et tandem ex determinata indeterminabilem fore.", "en": "Finally, if from one point of some infinite quantity two lines, such as AB and AC, are conceived to be extended to infinity, at a certain and determinate distance from one another at the beginning, it is certain that the distance between B and C is continually increased, and at length, from being determinate, will become indeterminable."}, {"id": "E1P15S.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Cum igitur haec absurda sequantur, ut putant, ex eo, quod quantitas infinita supponitur: inde concludunt, substantiam corpoream debere esse finitam, et consequenter ad Dei essentiam non pertinere. Secundum argumentum petitur etiam a summa Dei perfectione. Deus enim, inquiunt, cum sit ens summe perfectum, pati non potest; atqui substantia corporea, quandoquidem divisibilis est, pati potest; sequitur ergo, ipsam ad Dei essentiam non pertinere.", "en": "Since, therefore, these absurdities follow, as they think, from the supposition of an infinite quantity, they conclude that corporeal substance must be finite, and consequently does not pertain to God’s essence. The second argument is also drawn from God’s supreme perfection. For God, they say, since he is a supremely perfect being, cannot be acted on; but corporeal substance, since it is divisible, can be acted on; it follows, therefore, that it does not pertain to God’s essence."}, {"id": "E1P15S.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Haec sunt, quae apud scriptores invenio argumenta, quibus ostendere conantur, substantiam corpoream divina natura indignam esse, nec ad eandem posse pertinere. Verumenimvero si quis recte attendat, me ad haec iam respondisse comperiet; quandoquidem haec argumenta in eo tantum fundantur, quod substantiam corpoream ex partibus componi supponunt, quod iam (prop. 12. cum coroll. prop. 13.) absurdum esse ostendi.", "en": "These are the arguments I find in the writers by which they try to show that corporeal substance is unworthy of the divine nature and cannot pertain to it. But in truth, if someone attends rightly, he will find that I have already replied to these, since these arguments are founded only on their supposing that corporeal substance is composed of parts, which I have already shown to be absurd (Prop. 12 with Cor. Prop. 13)."}, {"id": "E1P15S.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Deinde si quis rem recte perpendere velit, videbit, omnia illa absurda (siquidem omnia absurda sunt, de quo iam non disputo), ex quibus concludere volunt, substantiam extensam finitam esse, minime ex eo sequi, quod quantitas infinita supponatur, sed quod quantitatem infinitam mensurabilem et ex partibus finitis conflari supponunt; quare ex absurdis, quae inde sequuntur, nihil aliud concludere possunt, quam quod quantitas infinita non sit mensurabilis et quod ex partibus finitis conflari non possit. Atque hoc idem est, quod nos supra (prop. 12. etc.) iam demonstravimus. Quare telum, quod in nos intendunt, in se ipsos revera coniiciunt.", "en": "Next, if someone wishes to consider the matter rightly, he will see that all those absurdities (if indeed they are all absurdities, which I am not now disputing), from which they wish to conclude that extended substance is finite, do not at all follow from the supposition of an infinite quantity, but from their supposing that infinite quantity is measurable and composed of finite parts; so from the absurdities which follow from this they can conclude nothing but that infinite quantity is not measurable and cannot be composed of finite parts. And this is the same thing we have already demonstrated above (Prop. 12 etc.). So the weapon they aim at us, they in fact turn against themselves."}, {"id": "E1P15S.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Si igitur ipsi ex suo hoc absurdo concludere tamen volunt, substantiam extensam debere esse finitam, nihil aliud hercle faciunt, quam si quis ex eo, quod finxit, circulum quadrati proprietates habere, concludit, circulum non habere centrum, ex quo omnes ad circumferentiam ductae lineae sunt aequales. Nam substantiam corpoream, quae non nisi infinita, non nisi unica et non nisi indivisibilis potest concipi (vid. prop. 8. 5. et 12.), eam ipsi ad concludendum, eandem esse finitam, ex partibus finitis conflari et multiplicem esse et divisibilem, concipiunt.", "en": "If, therefore, from this absurdity of theirs they nevertheless wish to conclude that extended substance must be finite, they are really doing nothing other than if someone, from having feigned that a circle has the properties of a square, concluded that a circle does not have a centre from which all lines drawn to the circumference are equal. For corporeal substance, which can be conceived only as infinite, only as unique, and only as indivisible (see Props. 8, 5 and 12), they conceive, in order to conclude that it is finite, as composed of finite parts, and as multiple and divisible."}, {"id": "E1P15S.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Sic etiam alii, postquam fingunt, lineam ex punctis componi, multa sciunt invenire argumenta, quibus ostendant, lineam non posse in infinitum dividi. Et profecto non minus absurdum est ponere, quod substantia corporea ex corporibus sive partibus componatur, quam quod corpus ex superficiebus, superficies ex lineis, lineae denique ex punctis componantur. Atque hoc omnes, qui claram rationem infallibilem esse sciunt, fateri debent, et imprimis ii, qui negant, dari vacuum.", "en": "So also others, after they have feigned that a line is composed of points, know how to find many arguments by which they may show that a line cannot be divided to infinity. And indeed it is no less absurd to assert that corporeal substance is composed of bodies, or parts, than that a body is composed of surfaces, surfaces of lines, and lines, finally, of points. And all who know clear reason to be infallible must admit this, and especially those who deny that there is a vacuum."}, {"id": "E1P15S.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Nam si substantia corporea ita posset dividi, ut eius partes realiter distinctae essent, cur ergo una pars non posset annihilari manentibus reliquis, ut ante, inter se connexis? et cur omnes ita aptari debent, ne detur vacuum? Sane rerum, quae realiter ab invicem distinctae sunt, una sine alia esse et in suo statu manere potest. Cum igitur vacuum in natura non detur (de quo alias), sed omnes partes ita concurrere debent, ne detur vacuum, sequitur hinc etiam, easdem non posse realiter distingui, hoc est, substantiam corpoream, quatenus substantia est, non posse dividi.", "en": "For if corporeal substance could be so divided that its parts were really distinct, why, then, could one part not be annihilated, the rest remaining connected with one another as before? And why must they all fit together so that there is no vacuum? Surely, of things that are really distinct from one another, one can be without the other and remain in its own state. Since, therefore, there is no vacuum in nature (of which elsewhere), but all the parts must so concur that there is no vacuum, it follows from this also that they cannot be really distinguished, that is, that corporeal substance, insofar as it is substance, cannot be divided."}, {"id": "E1P15S.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Si quis tamen iam quaerat, cur nos ex natura ita propensi simus ad dividendam quantitatem? ei respondeo, quod quantitas duobus modis a nobis concipitur, abstracte scilicet sive superficialiter, prout nempe ipsam imaginamur, vel ut substantia, quod a solo intellectu fit. Si itaque ad quantitatem attendimus, prout in imaginatione est, quod saepe et facilius a nobis fit, reperietur finita, divisibilis et ex partibus conflata; si autem ad ipsam, prout in intellectu est, attendimus et eam, quatenus substantia est, concipimus, quod difficillime fit, tum, ut iam satis demonstravimus, infinita, unica et indivisibilis reperietur.", "en": "But if someone should now ask why we are by nature so inclined to divide quantity, I answer that quantity is conceived by us in two ways: abstractly, or superficially, namely, as we imagine it, or as substance, which is done by the intellect alone. So if we attend to quantity as it is in the imagination, which we do often and more easily, it will be found to be finite, divisible, and composed of parts; but if we attend to it as it is in the intellect, and conceive it insofar as it is substance, which happens with great difficulty, then, as we have already sufficiently demonstrated, it will be found to be infinite, unique, and indivisible."}, {"id": "E1P15S.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Quod omnibus, qui inter imaginationem et intellectum distinguere sciverint, satis manifestum erit; praecipue si ad hoc etiam attendatur, quod materia ubique eadem est, nec partes in eadem distinguuntur, nisi quatenus materiam diversimode affectam esse concipimus, unde eius partes modaliter tantum distinguuntur, non autem realiter. Ex. gr. aquam, quatenus aqua est, dividi concipimus eiusque partes ab invicem separari; at non, quatenus substantia est corporea; eatenus enim neque separatur neque dividitur. Porro aqua, quatenus aqua, generatur et corrumpitur; at, quatenus substantia, nec generatur, nec corrumpitur.", "en": "This will be sufficiently manifest to all who have known how to distinguish between the imagination and the intellect, especially if attention is also paid to this, that matter is everywhere the same, and parts are not distinguished in it except insofar as we conceive matter to be affected in different ways, so that its parts are distinguished only modally, but not really. For example, we conceive that water, insofar as it is water, is divided, and that its parts are separated from one another; but not insofar as it is corporeal substance; for in that respect it is neither separated nor divided. Further, water, insofar as it is water, is generated and corrupted; but insofar as it is substance, it is neither generated nor corrupted."}, {"id": "E1P15S.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Atque his me ad secundum argumentum etiam respondisse puto; quandoquidem id in eo etiam fundatur, quod materia, quatenus substantia, divisibilis sit et ex partibus confletur. Et quamvis hoc non esset, nescio, cur divina natura indigna esset, quandoquidem (per prop. 14.) extra Deum nulla substantia dari potest, a qua ipsa pateretur.", "en": "And with this I think I have also replied to the second argument, since it too is founded on this, that matter, insofar as it is substance, is divisible and composed of parts. And even if this were not so, I do not know why it would be unworthy of the divine nature, since (by Prop. 14) there can be no substance outside God by which it would be acted on."}, {"id": "E1P15S.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P15S", "la": "Omnia, inquam, in Deo sunt et omnia, quae fiunt, per solas leges infinitae Dei naturae fiunt et ex necessitate eius essentiae (ut mox ostendam) sequuntur. Quare nulla ratione dici potest, Deum ab alio pati, aut substantiam extensam divina natura indignam esse; tametsi divisibilis supponatur, dummodo aeterna et infinita concedatur. Sed de his impraesentiarum satis.", "en": "All things, I say, are in God, and all things that happen happen only through the laws of God’s infinite nature, and follow (as I shall soon show) from the necessity of his essence. So it cannot be said in any way that God is acted on by another, or that extended substance is unworthy of the divine nature, even if it is supposed to be divisible, so long as it is granted to be eternal and infinite. But enough of these matters for the present."}, {"id": "E1P16", "p": "part1", "l": "1P16", "la": "Ex necessitate divinae naturae infinita infinitis modis (hoc est, omnia, quae sub intellectum infinitum cadere possunt) sequi debent.", "en": "From the necessity of the divine nature there must follow infinite things in infinite modes (that is, everything that can fall under an infinite intellect)."}, {"id": "E1P16D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P16D", "la": "Haec propositio unicuique manifesta esse debet, si modo ad hoc attendat, quod ex data cuiuscumque rei definitione plures proprietates intellectus concludit, quae revera ex eadem (hoc est, ipsa rei essentia) necessario sequuntur et eo plures, quo plus realitatis rei definitio exprimit, hoc est, quo plus realitatis rei definitae essentia involvit.", "en": "This proposition must be plain to everyone, provided he attends to this: that from the given definition of any thing the intellect infers a number of properties which really do follow necessarily from it (that is, from the very essence of the thing), and the more properties, the more reality the definition of the thing expresses, that is, the more reality the essence of the thing defined involves."}, {"id": "E1P16D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P16D", "la": "Cum autem natura divina infinita absolute attributa habeat (per defin. 6.), quorum etiam unumquodque infinitam essentiam in suo genere exprimit, ex eiusdem ergo necessitate infinita infinitis modis (hoc est, omnia, quae sub intellectum infinitum cadere possunt) necessario sequi debent. Q. E. D.", "en": "But since the divine nature has absolutely infinite attributes (by Def. 6), each of which also expresses an essence infinite in its own kind, from its necessity, therefore, there must necessarily follow infinite things in infinite modes (that is, everything that can fall under an infinite intellect). Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P16C1", "p": "part1", "l": "1P16C1", "la": "Hinc sequitur, Deum omnium rerum, quae sub intellectum infinitum cadere possunt, esse causam efficientem.", "en": "From this it follows that God is the efficient cause of all things that can fall under an infinite intellect."}, {"id": "E1P16C2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P16C2", "la": "Sequitur 2. Deum causam esse per se, non vero per accidens.", "en": "It follows, 2. that God is a cause through himself, and not by accident."}, {"id": "E1P16C3", "p": "part1", "l": "1P16C3", "la": "Sequitur 3. Deum esse absolute causam primam.", "en": "It follows, 3. that God is absolutely the first cause."}, {"id": "E1P17", "p": "part1", "l": "1P17", "la": "Deus ex solis suae naturae legibus et a nemine coactus agit.", "en": "God acts from the laws of his nature alone, and is compelled by no one."}, {"id": "E1P17D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P17D", "la": "Ex sola divinae naturae necessitate, vel (quod idem est) ex solis eiusdem naturae legibus, infinita absolute sequi, modo prop. 16. ostendimus; et prop. 15. demonstravimus, nihil sine Deo esse nec concipi posse, sed omnia in Deo esse. Quare nihil extra ipsum esse potest, a quo ad agendum determinetur vel cogatur; atque adeo Deus ex solis suae naturae legibus et a nemine coactus agit. Q. E. D.", "en": "We have just shown in Prop. 16 that from the necessity of the divine nature alone, or (what is the same) from the laws of that nature alone, absolutely infinite things follow; and we have demonstrated in Prop. 15 that nothing can be or be conceived without God, but that all things are in God. So there can be nothing outside him by which he is determined or compelled to act; and therefore God acts from the laws of his nature alone, and is compelled by no one. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P17C1", "p": "part1", "l": "1P17C1", "la": "Hinc sequitur 1. nullam dari causam, quae Deum extrinsece vel intrinsece praeter ipsius naturae perfectionem incitet ad agendum.", "en": "From this it follows, 1. that there is no cause, either extrinsically or intrinsically, except the perfection of his own nature, which moves God to act."}, {"id": "E1P17C2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P17C2", "la": "Sequitur 2. solum Deum esse causam liberam. Deus enim solus ex sola suae naturae necessitate existit (per prop. 11. et coroll. 1. prop. 14.), et ex sola suae naturae necessitate agit (per prop. praeced.). Adeoque (per defin. 7.) solus est causa libera. Q. E. D.", "en": "It follows, 2. that God alone is a free cause. For God alone exists from the necessity of his nature alone (by Prop. 11 and Cor. 1 of Prop. 14), and acts from the necessity of his nature alone (by the preceding Prop.). And so (by Def. 7) he alone is a free cause. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P17S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P17S", "la": "Alii putant, Deum esse causam liberam, propterea quod potest, ut putant, efficere, ut ea, quae ex eius natura sequi diximus, hoc est, quae in eius potestate sunt, non fiant, sive ut ab ipso non producantur. Sed hoc idem est, ac si dicerent, quod Deus potest efficere, ut ex natura trianguli non sequatur, eius tres angulos aequales esse duobus rectis; sive ut ex data causa non sequatur effectus, quod est absurdum.", "en": "Others think that God is a free cause because he can, as they think, bring it about that the things we have said follow from his nature, that is, which are in his power, should not come about, or should not be produced by him. But this is the same as if they were to say that God can bring it about that it should not follow from the nature of a triangle that its three angles are equal to two right angles, or that from a given cause the effect should not follow, which is absurd."}, {"id": "E1P17S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P17S", "la": "Porro infra absque ope huius propositionis ostendam, ad Dei naturam neque intellectum, neque voluntatem pertinere. Scio equidem plures esse qui putant, se posse demonstrare, ad Dei naturam summum intellectum et liberam voluntatem pertinere; nihil enim perfectius cognoscere sese aiunt, quod Deo tribuere possunt, quam id quod in nobis summa est perfectio. Porro, tametsi Deum actu summe intelligentem concipiant, non tamen credunt, eum posse omnia, quae actu intelligit, efficere ut existant; nam se eo modo Dei potentiam destruere putant.", "en": "Further, I shall show below, without the help of this proposition, that neither intellect nor will pertains to the nature of God. I know, of course, that there are many who think they can demonstrate that a supreme intellect and a free will pertain to God’s nature; for they say they know nothing more perfect, which they can attribute to God, than what is the highest perfection in us. Further, although they conceive God to understand, actually, in the highest degree, they do not believe that he can bring it about that everything he actually understands should exist; for they think that in that way they would destroy God’s power."}, {"id": "E1P17S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P17S", "la": "Si omnia, inquiunt, quae in eius intellectu sunt, creavisset, nihil tum amplius creare potuisset, quod credunt Dei omnipotentiae repugnare; ideoque maluerunt Deum ad omnia indifferentem statuere, nec aliud creantem praeter id quod absoluta quadam voluntate decrevit creare. Verum ego me satis clare ostendisse puto (vid. prop. 16.), a summa Dei potentia, sive infinita natura infinita infinitis modis, hoc est, omnia necessario effluxisse, vel semper eadem necessitate sequi; eodem modo, ac ex natura trianguli ab aeterno et in aeternum sequitur, eius tres angulos aequari duobus rectis. Quare Dei omnipotentia actu ab aeterno fuit et in aeternum in eadem actualitate manebit.", "en": "If, they say, he had created everything that is in his intellect, he would then have been unable to create anything more, which they believe to be inconsistent with God’s omnipotence; and so they have preferred to maintain that God is indifferent to all things, and creates nothing except what he has decreed to create by some absolute will. But I think I have shown clearly enough (see Prop. 16) that from God’s supreme power, or infinite nature, infinite things in infinite modes, that is, all things, have necessarily flowed, or always follow with the same necessity, in the same way as it follows from the nature of a triangle, from eternity and to eternity, that its three angles are equal to two right angles. So God’s omnipotence has been actual from eternity and will remain in the same actuality to eternity."}, {"id": "E1P17S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P17S", "la": "Et hoc modo Dei omnipotentia longe, meo quidem iudicio, perfectior statuitur. Imo adversarii Dei omnipotentiam (liceat aperte loqui) negare videntur. Coguntur enim fateri, Deum infinita creabilia intelligere, quae tamen nunquam creare poterit. Nam alias, si scilicet omnia quae intelligit crearet, suam iuxta ipsos exhauriret omnipotentiam et se imperfectum redderet. Ut igitur Deum perfectum statuant, eo rediguntur, ut simul statuere debeant, ipsum non posse omnia efficere, ad quae eius potentia se extendit; quo absurdius aut Dei omnipotentiae magis repugnans non video, quid fingi possit.", "en": "And in this way God’s omnipotence is maintained, in my judgment at least, far more perfectly. Indeed, my adversaries seem (if I may speak openly) to deny God’s omnipotence. For they are compelled to confess that God understands infinitely many creatable things which he will nevertheless never be able to create. For otherwise, if, that is, he created everything he understands, he would, according to them, exhaust his omnipotence and render himself imperfect. So in order to maintain that God is perfect, they are reduced to having to maintain at the same time that he cannot bring about everything to which his power extends; and I do not see what could be feigned that is more absurd than this, or more inconsistent with God’s omnipotence."}, {"id": "E1P17S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P17S", "la": "Porro (ut de intellectu et voluntate, quos Deo communiter tribuimus, hic etiam aliquid dicam) si ad aeternam Dei essentiam intellectus scilicet et voluntas pertinent, aliud sane per utrumque hoc attributum intelligendum est, quam quod vulgo solent homines. Nam intellectus et voluntas, qui Dei essentiam constituerent, a nostro intellectu et voluntate toto coelo differre deberent, nec in ulla re, praeterquam in nomine, convenire possent; non aliter scilicet, quam inter se conveniunt canis signum coeleste, et canis animal latrans. Quod sic demonstrabo.", "en": "Further (to say something here also about the intellect and will that we commonly attribute to God), if intellect and will pertain to the eternal essence of God, something different must surely be understood by each of these attributes from what men commonly understand. For the intellect and will that would constitute God’s essence would have to differ by all the breadth of heaven from our intellect and will, and could agree with them in nothing except the name; no more, that is, than the Dog, a heavenly constellation, and the dog, a barking animal, agree with one another. This I shall demonstrate as follows."}, {"id": "E1P17S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P17S", "la": "Si intellectus ad divinam naturam pertinet, non poterit, uti noster intellectus, posterior (ut plerisque placet), vel simul natura esse cum rebus intellectis, quandoquidem Deus omnibus rebus prior est causalitate (per coroll. 1. prop. 16.); sed contra veritas et formalis rerum essentia ideo talis est, quia talis in Dei intellectu existit obiective. Quare Dei intellectus, quatenus Dei essentiam constituere concipitur, est revera causa rerum tam earum essentiae, quam earum existentiae; quod ab iis videtur etiam fuisse animadversum, qui Dei intellectum, voluntatem et potentiam unum et idem esse asseruerunt.", "en": "If intellect pertains to the divine nature, it will not be able, like our intellect, to be by nature posterior to (as most hold), or simultaneous with, the things understood, since God is prior in causality to all things (by Cor. 1 of Prop. 16); on the contrary, the truth and formal essence of things is what it is because it exists objectively in that way in God’s intellect. So God’s intellect, insofar as it is conceived to constitute God’s essence, is really the cause of things, both of their essence and of their existence; which seems to have been noticed also by those who have asserted that God’s intellect, will and power are one and the same."}, {"id": "E1P17S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P17S", "la": "Cum itaque Dei intellectus sit unica rerum causa, videlicet (ut ostendimus) tam earum essentiae, quam earum existentiae, debet ipse necessario ab iisdem differre tam ratione essentiae, quam ratione existentiae. Nam causatum differt a sua causa praecise in eo, quod a causa habet. Ex. gr. homo est causa existentiae, non vero essentiae alterius hominis (est enim haec aeterna veritas); et ideo secundum essentiam prorsus convenire possunt, in existendo autem differre debent; et propterea, si unius existentia pereat, non ideo alterius peribit; sed, si unius essentia destrui posset et fieri falsa, destrueretur etiam alterius essentia.", "en": "Since, therefore, God’s intellect is the only cause of things, namely (as we have shown), both of their essence and of their existence, it must necessarily differ from them both in respect of essence and in respect of existence. For what is caused differs from its cause precisely in what it has from the cause. For example, a man is the cause of the existence, but not of the essence, of another man (for the latter is an eternal truth); and so they can agree entirely in essence, but must differ in existing; and for that reason, if the existence of one perishes, the existence of the other will not perish on that account; but if the essence of one could be destroyed and become false, the essence of the other would also be destroyed."}, {"id": "E1P17S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P17S", "la": "Quapropter res, quae et essentiae et existentiae alicuius effectus est causa, a tali effectu differre debet tam ratione essentiae, quam ratione existentiae. Atqui Dei intellectus est et essentiae et existentiae nostri intellectus causa; ergo Dei intellectus, quatenus divinam essentiam constituere concipitur, a nostro intellectu tam ratione essentiae, quam ratione existentiae differt, nec in ulla re, praeterquam in nomine, cum eo convenire potest, ut volebamus. Circa voluntatem eodem modo proceditur, ut facile unusquisque videre potest.", "en": "So a thing that is the cause both of the essence and of the existence of some effect must differ from such an effect both in respect of essence and in respect of existence. But God’s intellect is the cause both of the essence and of the existence of our intellect; therefore God’s intellect, insofar as it is conceived to constitute the divine essence, differs from our intellect both in respect of essence and in respect of existence, and can agree with it in nothing except the name, as we wished to show. The procedure is the same concerning the will, as anyone can easily see."}, {"id": "E1P18", "p": "part1", "l": "1P18", "la": "Deus est omnium rerum causa immanens, non vero transiens.", "en": "God is the immanent, not the transitive, cause of all things."}, {"id": "E1P18D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P18D", "la": "Omnia quae sunt in Deo sunt et per Deum concipi debent (per prop. 15.), adeoque (per coroll. 1. prop. 16.) Deus rerum, quae in ipso sunt, est causa; quod est primum. Deinde extra Deum nulla potest dari substantia (per prop. 14.), hoc est (per defin. 3.) res, quae extra Deum in se sit; quod erat secundum. Deus ergo est omnium rerum causa immanens, non vero transiens. Q. E. D.", "en": "All things that are, are in God, and must be conceived through God (by Prop. 15), and so (by Cor. 1 of Prop. 16) God is the cause of the things that are in him; which is the first point. Next, outside God there can be no substance (by Prop. 14), that is (by Def. 3), a thing which is in itself outside God; which was the second point. God, therefore, is the immanent, not the transitive, cause of all things. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P19", "p": "part1", "l": "1P19", "la": "Deus sive omnia Dei attributa sunt aeterna.", "en": "God, or all of God’s attributes, are eternal."}, {"id": "E1P19D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P19D", "la": "Deus enim (per defin. 6.) est substantia, quae (per prop. 11.) necessario existit, hoc est (per prop. 7.) ad cuius naturam pertinet existere, sive (quod idem est) ex cuius definitione sequitur ipsum existere, adeoque (per defin. 8.) est aeternus.", "en": "For God (by Def. 6) is a substance which (by Prop. 11) necessarily exists, that is (by Prop. 7), to whose nature it pertains to exist, or (what is the same) from whose definition it follows that he exists; and so (by Def. 8) he is eternal."}, {"id": "E1P19D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P19D", "la": "Deinde per Dei attributa intelligendum est id quod (per defin. 4.) divinae substantiae essentiam exprimit, hoc est, id quod ad substantiam pertinet; id ipsum, inquam, ipsa attributa involvere debent. Atqui ad naturam substantiae (ut iam ex prop. 7. demonstravi) pertinet aeternitas; ergo unumquodque attributorum aeternitatem involvere debet, adeoque omnia sunt aeterna. Q. E. D.", "en": "Next, by God’s attributes must be understood that which (by Def. 4) expresses the essence of the divine substance, that is, that which pertains to substance: that very thing, I say, the attributes themselves must involve. But eternity pertains to the nature of substance (as I have already demonstrated from Prop. 7); therefore each of the attributes must involve eternity, and so all are eternal. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P19S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P19S", "la": "Haec propositio quam clarissime etiam patet ex modo, quo (prop. 11.) Dei existentiam demonstravi. Ex ea, inquam, demonstratione constat, Dei existentiam, sicut eius essentiam, aeternam esse veritatem. Deinde (prop. 19. Principiorum Cartesii) alio etiam modo Dei aeternitatem demonstravi, nec opus est eum hic repetere.", "en": "This proposition is also evident, as clearly as possible, from the way in which I demonstrated God’s existence (Prop. 11). From that demonstration, I say, it is established that God’s existence, like his essence, is an eternal truth. Further, I have also demonstrated God’s eternity in another way (Prop. 19 of the Principles of Descartes), and there is no need to repeat it here."}, {"id": "E1P20", "p": "part1", "l": "1P20", "la": "Dei existentia eiusque essentia unum et idem sunt.", "en": "God’s existence and his essence are one and the same."}, {"id": "E1P20D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P20D", "la": "Deus (per anteced. prop.) eiusque omnia attributa sunt aeterna, hoc est (per defin. 8.) unumquodque eius attributorum existentiam exprimit. Eadem ergo Dei attributa, quae (per defin. 4.) Dei aeternam essentiam explicant, eius simul aeternam existentiam explicant, hoc est, illud ipsum, quod essentiam Dei constituit, constituit simul ipsius existentiam; adeoque haec et ipsius essentia unum et idem sunt. Q. E. D.", "en": "God (by the preceding Prop.) and all his attributes are eternal, that is (by Def. 8), each of his attributes expresses existence. Therefore the same attributes of God which (by Def. 4) explain his eternal essence, at the same time explain his eternal existence, that is, that very thing which constitutes God’s essence constitutes at the same time his existence; and so his existence and his essence are one and the same. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P20C1", "p": "part1", "l": "1P20C1", "la": "Hinc sequitur 1. Dei existentiam, sicut eius essentiam, aeternam esse veritatem.", "en": "From this it follows, 1. that God’s existence, like his essence, is an eternal truth."}, {"id": "E1P20C2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P20C2", "la": "Sequitur 2. Deum, sive omnia Dei attributa esse immutabilia. Nam, si ratione existentiae mutarentur, deberent etiam (per prop. praeced.) ratione essentiae mutari, hoc est (ut per se notum) ex veris falsa fieri; quod est absurdum.", "en": "It follows, 2. that God, or all of God’s attributes, are immutable. For if they changed in respect of existence, they would also (by the preceding Prop.) have to change in respect of essence, that is (as is self-evident), from being true become false; which is absurd."}, {"id": "E1P21", "p": "part1", "l": "1P21", "la": "Omnia, quae ex absoluta natura alicuius attributi Dei sequuntur, semper et infinita existere debuerunt, sive per idem attributum aeterna et infinita sunt.", "en": "All the things that follow from the absolute nature of any of God’s attributes have had to exist always and as infinite, or are, through the same attribute, eternal and infinite."}, {"id": "E1P21D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P21D", "la": "Concipe, si fieri potest (siquidem neges), aliquid in aliquo Dei attributo ex ipsius absoluta natura sequi, quod finitum sit et determinatam habeat existentiam sive durationem, ex. gr. ideam Dei in cogitatione. At cogitatio, quandoquidem Dei attributum supponitur, est necessario (per prop. 11.) sua natura infinita. Verum quatenus ipsa ideam Dei habet, finita supponitur.", "en": "Conceive, if it can be done (if indeed you deny this), that something which is finite and has a determinate existence or duration follows in some attribute of God from its absolute nature, for example, the idea of God in thought. But thought, since it is supposed to be an attribute of God, is necessarily (by Prop. 11) infinite by its nature. But insofar as it has the idea of God, it is supposed to be finite."}, {"id": "E1P21D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P21D", "la": "At (per defin. 2.) finita concipi non potest, nisi per ipsam cogitationem determinetur; sed non per ipsam cogitationem, quatenus ideam Dei constituit (eatenus enim finita supponitur esse); ergo per cogitationem, quatenus ideam Dei non constituit, quae tamen (per prop. 11.) necessario existere debet. Datur igitur cogitatio non constituens ideam Dei, ac propterea ex eius natura, quatenus est absoluta cogitatio, non sequitur necessario idea Dei (concipitur enim ideam Dei constituens et non constituens); quod est contra hypothesin.", "en": "But (by Def. 2) it cannot be conceived as finite unless it is determined by thought itself; but not by thought itself insofar as it constitutes the idea of God (for to that extent it is supposed to be finite); therefore by thought insofar as it does not constitute the idea of God, which nevertheless (by Prop. 11) must necessarily exist. There is, therefore, thought which does not constitute the idea of God, and on that account the idea of God does not follow necessarily from its nature insofar as it is absolute thought (for it is conceived both as constituting and as not constituting the idea of God); which is contrary to the hypothesis."}, {"id": "E1P21D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P21D", "la": "Quare si idea Dei in cogitatione, aut aliquid (perinde est, quicquid sumatur, quandoquidem demonstratio universalis est) in aliquo Dei attributo ex necessitate absolutae naturae ipsius attributi sequatur, id debet necessario esse infinitum; quod erat primum.", "en": "So if the idea of God in thought, or anything else (it makes no difference what is taken, since the demonstration is universal), follows in some attribute of God from the necessity of the absolute nature of that attribute, it must necessarily be infinite; which was the first point."}, {"id": "E1P21D.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P21D", "la": "Deinde id, quod ex necessitate naturae alicuius attributi ita sequitur, non potest determinatam habere existentiam sive durationem. Nam si neges, supponatur res, quae ex necessitate naturae alicuius attributi sequitur, dari in aliquo Dei attributo, ex. gr. idea Dei in cogitatione, eaque supponatur aliquando non exstitisse, vel non exstitura. Cum autem cogitatio Dei attributum supponatur, debet et necessario et immutabilis existere (per prop. 11. et coroll. 2. prop. 20.).", "en": "Next, what follows in this way from the necessity of the nature of some attribute cannot have a determinate existence, or duration. For if you deny this, suppose that a thing which follows from the necessity of the nature of some attribute is in some attribute of God, for example, the idea of God in thought, and suppose that it at some time did not exist, or will not exist. But since thought is supposed to be an attribute of God, it must exist both necessarily and as immutable (by Prop. 11 and Cor. 2 of Prop. 20)."}, {"id": "E1P21D.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P21D", "la": "Quare ultra limites durationis ideae Dei (supponitur enim aliquando non exstitisse, aut non exstitura) cogitatio sine idea Dei existere debebit; atqui hoc est contra hypothesin; supponitur enim, ex data cogitatione necessario sequi ideam Dei. Ergo idea Dei in cogitatione, aut aliquid quod necessario ex absoluta natura alicuius attributi Dei sequitur, non potest determinatam habere durationem, sed per idem attributum aeternum est; quod erat secundum. Nota, hoc idem esse affirmandum de quacumque re, quae in aliquo Dei attributo ex Dei absoluta natura necessario sequitur.", "en": "So beyond the limits of the duration of the idea of God (for it is supposed at some time not to have existed, or not to be going to exist) thought will have to exist without the idea of God; but this is contrary to the hypothesis, for it is supposed that, given thought, the idea of God follows necessarily. Therefore the idea of God in thought, or anything that follows necessarily from the absolute nature of some attribute of God, cannot have a determinate duration, but is, through the same attribute, eternal; which was the second point. Note that the same is to be affirmed of any thing that follows necessarily, in some attribute of God, from God’s absolute nature."}, {"id": "E1P22", "p": "part1", "l": "1P22", "la": "Quicquid ex aliquo Dei attributo, quatenus modificatum est tali modificatione, quae et necessario et infinita per idem existit, sequitur, debet quoque et necessario et infinitum existere.", "en": "Whatever follows from some attribute of God insofar as it is modified by a modification which, through the same attribute, exists both necessarily and as infinite, must also exist both necessarily and as infinite."}, {"id": "E1P22D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P22D", "la": "Huius propositionis demonstratio procedit eodem modo, ac demonstratio praecedentis.", "en": "The demonstration of this proposition proceeds in the same way as the demonstration of the preceding one."}, {"id": "E1P23", "p": "part1", "l": "1P23", "la": "Omnis modus, qui et necessario et infinitus existit, necessario sequi debuit vel ex absoluta natura alicuius attributi Dei, vel ex aliquo attributo modificato modificatione, quae et necessario et infinita existit.", "en": "Every mode which exists both necessarily and as infinite must necessarily have followed either from the absolute nature of some attribute of God, or from some attribute modified by a modification which exists both necessarily and as infinite."}, {"id": "E1P23D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P23D", "la": "Modus enim in alio est, per quod concipi debet (per defin. 5.), hoc est (per prop. 15.) in solo Deo est et per solum Deum concipi potest. Si ergo modus concipitur necessario existere et infinitus esse, utrumque hoc debet necessario concludi sive percipi per aliquod Dei attributum, quatenus idem concipitur infinitatem et necessitatem existentiae, sive (quod per defin. 8. idem est) aeternitatem exprimere, hoc est (per defin. 6. et prop. 19.) quatenus absolute consideratur.", "en": "For a mode is in another, through which it must be conceived (by Def. 5), that is (by Prop. 15), it is in God alone, and can be conceived through God alone. If, therefore, a mode is conceived to exist necessarily and to be infinite, both of these must necessarily be inferred, or perceived, through some attribute of God, insofar as that attribute is conceived to express infinity and necessity of existence, or (what is the same, by Def. 8) eternity, that is (by Def. 6 and Prop. 19), insofar as it is considered absolutely."}, {"id": "E1P23D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P23D", "la": "Modus ergo, qui et necessario et infinitus existit, ex absoluta natura alicuius Dei attributi sequi debuit; hocque vel immediate (de quo prop. 21.) vel mediante aliqua modificatione, quae ex eius absoluta natura sequitur, hoc est (per prop. praeced.) quae et necessario et infinita existit. Q. E. D.", "en": "A mode, therefore, which exists both necessarily and as infinite, must have followed from the absolute nature of some attribute of God; and this either immediately (concerning which see Prop. 21), or by the mediation of some modification which follows from its absolute nature, that is (by the preceding Prop.), which exists both necessarily and as infinite. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P24", "p": "part1", "l": "1P24", "la": "Rerum a Deo productarum essentia non involvit existentiam.", "en": "The essence of things produced by God does not involve existence."}, {"id": "E1P24D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P24D", "la": "Patet ex defin. 1. Id enim, cuius natura (in se scilicet considerata) involvit existentiam, causa est sui et ex sola suae naturae necessitate existit.", "en": "This is evident from Def. 1. For that whose nature (considered, that is, in itself) involves existence is the cause of itself, and exists from the necessity of its own nature alone."}, {"id": "E1P24C", "p": "part1", "l": "1P24C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, Deum non tantum esse causam, ut res incipiant existere; sed etiam, ut in existendo perseverent, sive (ut termino scholastico utar) Deum esse causam essendi rerum. Nam sive res existant, sive non existant, quotiescumque ad earum essentiam attendimus, eandem nec existentiam nec durationem involvere comperimus; adeoque earum essentia neque suae existentiae, neque suae durationis potest esse causa, sed tantum Deus, ad cuius solam naturam pertinet existere (per coroll. 1. prop. 14.).", "en": "From this it follows that God is not only the cause of things’ beginning to exist, but also of their persevering in existing, or (to use a scholastic term) that God is the cause of the being of things. For whether things exist or do not exist, whenever we attend to their essence we find that it involves neither existence nor duration; and so their essence can be the cause neither of their existence nor of their duration, but only God, to whose nature alone it pertains to exist (by Cor. 1 of Prop. 14)."}, {"id": "E1P25", "p": "part1", "l": "1P25", "la": "Deus non tantum est causa efficiens rerum existentiae, sed etiam essentiae.", "en": "God is the efficient cause not only of the existence of things, but also of their essence."}, {"id": "E1P25D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P25D", "la": "Si negas, ergo rerum essentiae Deus non est causa; adeoque (per axiom. 4.) potest rerum essentia sine Deo concipi. Atqui hoc (per prop. 15.) est absurdum. Ergo rerum etiam essentiae Deus est causa. Q. E. D.", "en": "If you deny this, then God is not the cause of the essence of things; and so (by Ax. 4) the essence of things can be conceived without God. But this (by Prop. 15) is absurd. Therefore God is the cause also of the essence of things. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P25S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P25S", "la": "Haec propositio clarius sequitur ex prop. 16. Ex ea enim sequitur, quod ex data natura divina tam rerum essentia quam existentia debeat necessario concludi; et, ut verbo dicam, eo sensu, quo Deus dicitur causa sui, etiam omnium rerum causa dicendus est, quod adhuc clarius ex sequenti corollario constabit.", "en": "This proposition follows more clearly from Prop. 16. For from it it follows that, from the given divine nature, both the essence and the existence of things must necessarily be inferred; and, in a word, God must be called the cause of all things in the same sense in which he is called the cause of himself, which will be established still more clearly from the following corollary."}, {"id": "E1P25C", "p": "part1", "l": "1P25C", "la": "Res particulares nihil sunt nisi Dei attributorum affectiones, sive modi, quibus Dei attributa certo et determinato modo exprimuntur. Demonstratio patet ex prop. 15. et defin. 5.", "en": "Particular things are nothing but affections of God’s attributes, or modes by which God’s attributes are expressed in a certain and determinate way. The demonstration is evident from Prop. 15 and Def. 5."}, {"id": "E1P26", "p": "part1", "l": "1P26", "la": "Res, quae ad aliquid operandum determinata est, a Deo necessario sic fuit determinata; et quae a Deo non est determinata, non potest se ipsam ad operandum determinare.", "en": "A thing which has been determined to produce some effect has necessarily been determined in this way by God; and one which has not been determined by God cannot determine itself to operate."}, {"id": "E1P26D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P26D", "la": "Id, per quod res determinatae ad aliquid operandum dicuntur, necessario quid positivum est (ut per se notum). Adeoque, tam eius essentiae, quam existentiae, Deus ex necessitate suae naturae est causa efficiens (per prop. 25. et 16.); quod erat primum. Ex quo etiam, quod secundo proponitur, clarissime sequitur. Nam si res, quae a Deo determinata non est, se ipsam determinare posset, prima pars huius falsa esset, quod est absurdum, ut ostendimus.", "en": "That through which things are said to be determined to produce some effect is necessarily something positive (as is self-evident). And so God, from the necessity of his nature, is the efficient cause both of its essence and of its existence (by Props. 25 and 16); which was the first point. From this the second point proposed also follows very clearly. For if a thing which has not been determined by God could determine itself, the first part of this proposition would be false, which is absurd, as we have shown."}, {"id": "E1P27", "p": "part1", "l": "1P27", "la": "Res, quae a Deo ad aliquid operandum determinata est, se ipsam indeterminatam reddere non potest.", "en": "A thing which has been determined by God to produce some effect cannot render itself undetermined."}, {"id": "E1P27D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P27D", "la": "Haec propositio patet ex axiom. 3.", "en": "This proposition is evident from Ax. 3."}, {"id": "E1P28", "p": "part1", "l": "1P28", "la": "Quodcumque singulare, sive quaevis res quae finita est et determinatam habet existentiam, non potest existere nec ad operandum determinari, nisi ad existendum et operandum determinetur ab alia causa, quae etiam finita est et determinatam habet existentiam; et rursus haec causa non potest etiam existere neque ad operandum determinari, nisi ab alia, quae etiam finita est et determinatam habet existentiam, determinetur ad existendum et operandum, et sic in infinitum.", "en": "Every singular thing, or any thing which is finite and has a determinate existence, can neither exist nor be determined to operate unless it is determined to exist and to operate by another cause, which is also finite and has a determinate existence; and again this cause also can neither exist nor be determined to operate unless it is determined to exist and to operate by another, which is also finite and has a determinate existence, and so on to infinity."}, {"id": "E1P28D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P28D", "la": "Quicquid determinatum est ad existendum et operandum, a Deo sic determinatum est (per prop. 26. et coroll. prop. 24.). At id, quod finitum est et determinatam habet existentiam, ab absoluta natura alicuius Dei attributi produci non potuit; quicquid enim ex absoluta natura alicuius Dei attributi sequitur, id infinitum et aeternum est (per prop. 21.).", "en": "Whatever has been determined to exist and to operate has been so determined by God (by Prop. 26 and Cor. of Prop. 24). But what is finite and has a determinate existence could not have been produced by the absolute nature of any attribute of God; for whatever follows from the absolute nature of any attribute of God is infinite and eternal (by Prop. 21)."}, {"id": "E1P28D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P28D", "la": "Debuit ergo ex Deo, vel aliquo eius attributo sequi, quatenus aliquo modo affectum consideratur; praeter enim substantiam et modos nil datur (per axiom. 1. et defin. 3. et 5.) et modi (per coroll. prop. 25.) nihil sunt, nisi Dei attributorum affectiones. At ex Deo vel aliquo eius attributo, quatenus affectum est modificatione, quae aeterna et infinita est, sequi etiam non potuit (per prop. 22.).", "en": "It must, therefore, have followed from God, or some attribute of God, insofar as it is considered to be affected by some mode; for except substance and modes there is nothing (by Ax. 1 and Defs. 3 and 5), and modes (by Cor. of Prop. 25) are nothing but affections of God’s attributes. But it also could not have followed from God, or some attribute of God, insofar as it is affected by a modification which is eternal and infinite (by Prop. 22)."}, {"id": "E1P28D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P28D", "la": "Debuit ergo sequi, vel ad existendum et operandum determinari a Deo, vel aliquo eius attributo, quatenus modificatum est modificatione, quae finita est et determinatam habet existentiam. Quod erat primum.", "en": "It must, therefore, have followed from, or have been determined to exist and to operate by, God or some attribute of God insofar as it is modified by a modification which is finite and has a determinate existence. Which was the first point."}, {"id": "E1P28D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P28D", "la": "Deinde haec rursus causa, sive hic modus (per eandem rationem, qua primam partem huius iam iam demonstravimus) debuit etiam determinari ab alia, quae etiam finita est et determinatam habet existentiam et rursus haec ultima (per eandem rationem) ab alia, et sic semper (per eandem rationem) in infinitum. Q. E. D.", "en": "Next, this cause in turn, or this mode (by the same reasoning by which we have just now demonstrated the first part of this proposition), must also have been determined by another, which is also finite and has a determinate existence, and again this last (by the same reasoning) by another, and so always (by the same reasoning) to infinity. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P28S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P28S", "la": "Cum quaedam a Deo immediate produci debuerunt, videlicet ea quae ex absoluta eius natura necessario sequuntur, et alia mediantibus his primis, quae tamen sine Deo nec esse, nec concipi possunt; hinc sequitur 1. quod Deus sit rerum immediate ab ipso productarum causa absolute proxima; non vero in suo genere, ut aiunt. Nam Dei effectus sine sua causa nec esse nec concipi possunt (per prop. 15. et coroll. prop. 24.).", "en": "Since certain things had to be produced by God immediately, namely those which follow necessarily from his absolute nature, and others by the mediation of these first ones, which nevertheless can neither be nor be conceived without God, it follows, 1. that God is the absolutely proximate cause of the things produced immediately by him; but not proximate in his own kind, as they say. For God’s effects can neither be nor be conceived without their cause (by Prop. 15 and Cor. of Prop. 24)."}, {"id": "E1P28S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P28S", "la": "Sequitur 2. quod Deus non potest proprie dici causa esse remota rerum singularium, nisi forte ea de causa, ut scilicet has ab iis, quas immediate produxit, vel potius, quae ex absoluta eius natura sequuntur, distinguamus. Nam per causam remotam talem intelligimus, quae cum effectu nullo modo coniuncta est. At omnia quae sunt in Deo sunt et a Deo ita dependent, ut sine ipso nec esse, nec concipi possint.", "en": "It follows, 2. that God cannot properly be called the remote cause of singular things, unless perhaps for this reason, namely, that we may distinguish them from those which he has produced immediately, or rather, which follow from his absolute nature. For by a remote cause we understand one which is in no way conjoined with its effect. But all things that are, are in God, and so depend on God that without him they can neither be nor be conceived."}, {"id": "E1P29", "p": "part1", "l": "1P29", "la": "In rerum natura nullum datur contingens, sed omnia ex necessitate divinae naturae determinata sunt ad certo modo existendum et operandum.", "en": "In the nature of things there is nothing contingent, but all things have been determined from the necessity of the divine nature to exist and to operate in a certain way."}, {"id": "E1P29D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P29D", "la": "Quicquid est, in Deo est (per prop. 15.). Deus autem non potest dici res contingens. Nam (per prop. 11.) necessario, non vero contingenter existit. Modi deinde divinae naturae ex eadem etiam necessario, non vero contingenter secuti sunt (per prop. 16.); idque vel quatenus divina natura absolute (per prop. 21.), vel quatenus certo modo ad agendum determinata consideratur (per prop. 27.).", "en": "Whatever is, is in God (by Prop. 15). But God cannot be called a contingent thing. For (by Prop. 11) he exists necessarily, not contingently. Next, the modes of the divine nature have also followed from it necessarily, not contingently (by Prop. 16); and that either insofar as the divine nature is considered absolutely (by Prop. 21) or insofar as it is considered to be determined to act in a certain way (by Prop. 27)."}, {"id": "E1P29D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P29D", "la": "Porro horum modorum Deus non tantum est causa, quatenus simpliciter existunt (per coroll. prop. 24.), sed etiam (per prop. 26.), quatenus ad aliquid operandum determinati considerantur. Quod si a Deo (per eand. prop.) determinati non sint, impossibile, non vero contingens est, ut se ipsos determinent; et contra (per prop. 27.) si a Deo determinati sint, impossibile, non vero contingens est, ut se ipsos indeterminatos reddant.", "en": "Further, God is the cause of these modes not only insofar as they simply exist (by Cor. of Prop. 24), but also (by Prop. 26) insofar as they are considered to be determined to produce some effect. If they have not been determined by God (by the same proposition), it is impossible, not contingent, that they should determine themselves; and conversely (by Prop. 27), if they have been determined by God, it is impossible, not contingent, that they should render themselves undetermined."}, {"id": "E1P29D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P29D", "la": "Quare omnia ex necessitate divinae naturae determinata sunt, non tantum ad existendum, sed etiam ad certo modo existendum et operandum, nullumque datur contingens. Q. E. D.", "en": "So all things have been determined from the necessity of the divine nature, not only to exist, but also to exist and to operate in a certain way, and there is nothing contingent. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P29S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P29S", "la": "Antequam ulterius pergam, hic quid nobis per *naturam naturantem* et quid per *naturam naturatam* intelligendum sit, explicare volo, vel potius monere. Nam ex antecedentibus iam constare existimo, nempe, quod per *naturam naturantem* nobis intelligendum est id quod in se est et per se concipitur, sive talia substantiae attributa, quae aeternam et infinitam essentiam exprimunt, hoc est (per coroll. 1. prop. 14. et coroll. 2. prop. 17.) Deus, quatenus ut causa libera consideratur. Per *naturatam* autem intelligo id omne quod ex necessitate Dei naturae sive uniuscuiusque Dei attributorum sequitur, hoc est, omnes Dei attributorum modos, quatenus considerantur ut res, quae in Deo sunt et quae sine Deo nec esse nec concipi possunt.", "en": "Before I proceed further, I wish to explain here, or rather to advise the reader, what we must understand by Natura naturans and what by Natura naturata. For from the preceding I think it is already established that by Natura naturans we must understand what is in itself and is conceived through itself, or such attributes of substance as express an eternal and infinite essence, that is (by Cor. 1 of Prop. 14 and Cor. 2 of Prop. 17), God, insofar as he is considered as a free cause. But by naturata I understand everything that follows from the necessity of God’s nature, or of each of God’s attributes, that is, all the modes of God’s attributes, insofar as they are considered as things which are in God, and which can neither be nor be conceived without God."}, {"id": "E1P30", "p": "part1", "l": "1P30", "la": "Intellectus actu finitus aut actu infinitus Dei attributa Deique affectiones comprehendere debet et nihil aliud.", "en": "An intellect, actually finite or actually infinite, must comprehend the attributes of God and the affections of God, and nothing else."}, {"id": "E1P30D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P30D", "la": "Idea vera debet convenire cum suo ideato (per axiom. 6.), hoc est (ut per se notum) id, quod in intellectu obiective continetur, debet necessario in natura dari. Atqui in natura (per coroll. 1. prop. 14.) non nisi una substantia datur, nempe Deus, nec ullae aliae affectiones (per prop. 15.) quam quae in Deo sunt et quae (per eandem prop.) sine Deo nec esse, nec concipi possunt.", "en": "A true idea must agree with its object (by Ax. 6), that is (as is self-evident), what is contained objectively in the intellect must necessarily be in nature. But in nature (by Cor. 1 of Prop. 14) there is only one substance, namely God, and there are no other affections (by Prop. 15) than those which are in God and which (by the same proposition) can neither be nor be conceived without God."}, {"id": "E1P30D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P30D", "la": "Ergo intellectus actu finitus aut actu infinitus Dei attributa Deique affectiones comprehendere debet et nihil aliud. Q. E. D.", "en": "Therefore an intellect, actually finite or actually infinite, must comprehend the attributes of God and the affections of God, and nothing else. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P31", "p": "part1", "l": "1P31", "la": "Intellectus actu, sive is finitus sit sive infinitus, ut et voluntas, cupiditas, amor etc. ad naturam naturatam, non vero ad naturantem referri debent.", "en": "The actual intellect, whether it be finite or infinite, as also will, desire, love, etc., must be referred to Natura naturata, not to Natura naturans."}, {"id": "E1P31D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P31D", "la": "Per intellectum enim (ut per se notum) non intelligimus absolutam cogitationem, sed certum tantum modum cogitandi, qui modus ab aliis, scilicet cupiditate, amore etc. differt, adeoque (per defin. 5.) per absolutam cogitationem concipi debet; nempe (per prop. 15. et defin. 6.) per aliquod Dei attributum, quod aeternam et infinitam cogitationis essentiam exprimit, ita concipi debet, ut sine ipso nec esse nec concipi possit.", "en": "For by intellect (as is self-evident) we do not understand absolute thought, but only a certain mode of thinking, which mode differs from others, namely desire, love, etc., and so (by Def. 5) must be conceived through absolute thought; namely (by Prop. 15 and Def. 6) it must be so conceived through some attribute of God which expresses the eternal and infinite essence of thought, that without it it can neither be nor be conceived."}, {"id": "E1P31D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P31D", "la": "Ac propterea (per schol. prop. 29.) ad naturam naturatam, non vero naturantem referri debet, ut etiam reliqui modi cogitandi. Q. E. D.", "en": "And therefore (by Schol. of Prop. 29) it must be referred to Natura naturata, not to naturans, as must the other modes of thinking also. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P31S", "p": "part1", "l": "1P31S", "la": "Ratio, cur hic loquar de intellectu actu, non est, quia concedo, ullum dari intellectum potentia; sed quia omnem confusionem vitare cupio, nolui loqui nisi de re nobis quam clarissime percepta, de ipsa scilicet intellectione, qua nihil nobis clarius percipitur. Nihil enim intelligere possumus, quod ad perfectiorem intellectionis cognitionem non conducat.", "en": "The reason why I speak here of actual intellect is not that I grant that there is any potential intellect; but because I wish to avoid all confusion, I have been unwilling to speak of anything except what is perceived by us as clearly as possible, namely of intellection itself, than which nothing is perceived more clearly by us. For we can understand nothing that does not lead to a more perfect knowledge of intellection."}, {"id": "E1P32", "p": "part1", "l": "1P32", "la": "Voluntas non potest vocari causa libera, sed tantum necessaria.", "en": "The will cannot be called a free cause, but only a necessary one."}, {"id": "E1P32D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P32D", "la": "Voluntas certus tantum cogitandi modus est, sicuti intellectus; adeoque (per prop. 28.) unaquaeque volitio non potest existere, neque ad operandum determinari, nisi ab alia causa determinetur, et haec rursus ab alia, et sic porro in infinitum. Quod si voluntas infinita supponatur, debet etiam ad existendum et operandum determinari a Deo, non quatenus substantia absolute infinita est, sed quatenus attributum habet, quod infinitam et aeternam cogitationis essentiam exprimit (per prop. 23.).", "en": "The will is only a certain mode of thinking, like the intellect; and so (by Prop. 28) each volition can neither exist nor be determined to operate unless it is determined by another cause, and this cause again by another, and so on to infinity. But if the will is supposed to be infinite, it too must be determined to exist and to operate by God, not insofar as he is absolutely infinite substance, but insofar as he has an attribute which expresses the infinite and eternal essence of thought (by Prop. 23)."}, {"id": "E1P32D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P32D", "la": "Quocumque igitur modo sive finita sive infinita concipiatur, causam requirit, a qua ad existendum et operandum determinetur; adeoque (per defin. 7.) non potest dici causa libera, sed tantum necessaria vel coacta. Q. E. D.", "en": "So in whatever way it is conceived, whether as finite or as infinite, it requires a cause by which it is determined to exist and to operate; and so (by Def. 7) it cannot be called a free cause, but only a necessary or compelled one. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P32C1", "p": "part1", "l": "1P32C1", "la": "Hinc sequitur 1. Deum non operari ex libertate voluntatis.", "en": "From this it follows, 1. that God does not operate from freedom of the will."}, {"id": "E1P32C2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P32C2", "la": "Sequitur 2. voluntatem et intellectum ad Dei naturam ita sese habere, ut motus et quies, et absolute, ut omnia naturalia, quae (per prop. 29.) a Deo ad existendum et operandum certo modo determinari debent. Nam voluntas, ut reliqua omnia, causa indiget, a qua ad existendum et operandum certo modo determinetur.", "en": "It follows, 2. that will and intellect are related to God’s nature as motion and rest are, and absolutely, as are all natural things, which (by Prop. 29) must be determined by God to exist and to operate in a certain way. For the will, like all other things, requires a cause by which it is determined to exist and to operate in a certain way."}, {"id": "E1P32C2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P32C2", "la": "Et quamvis ex data voluntate sive intellectu infinita sequantur, non tamen propterea Deus magis dici potest ex libertate voluntatis agere, quam propter ea, quae ex motu et quiete sequuntur (infinita enim ex his etiam sequuntur), dici potest ex libertate motus et quietis agere. Quare voluntas ad Dei naturam non magis pertinet, quam reliqua naturalia, sed ad ipsam eodem modo sese habet, ut motus et quies et omnia reliqua, quae ostendimus ex necessitate divinae naturae sequi, et ab eadem ad existendum et operandum certo modo determinari.", "en": "And although infinitely many things follow from a given will or intellect, God cannot on that account be said to act from freedom of the will any more than, on account of the things that follow from motion and rest (for infinitely many things follow from these also), he can be said to act from freedom of motion and rest. So will does not pertain to God’s nature any more than other natural things do, but is related to it in the same way as motion and rest and all the other things which, as we have shown, follow from the necessity of the divine nature and are determined by it to exist and to operate in a certain way."}, {"id": "E1P33", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33", "la": "Res nullo alio modo, neque alio ordine a Deo produci potuerunt, quam productae sunt.", "en": "Things could have been produced by God in no other way, and in no other order, than that in which they have been produced."}, {"id": "E1P33D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33D", "la": "Res enim omnes ex data Dei natura necessario secutae sunt (per prop. 16.), et ex necessitate naturae Dei determinatae sunt ad certo modo existendum et operandum (per prop. 29.). Si itaque res alterius naturae potuissent esse, vel alio modo ad operandum determinari, ut naturae ordo alius esset, ergo Dei etiam natura alia posset esse, quam iam est; ac proinde (per prop. 11.) illa etiam deberet existere et consequenter duo, vel plures possent dari dii; quod (per coroll. 1. prop. 14.) est absurdum.", "en": "For all things have followed necessarily from the given nature of God (by Prop. 16), and have been determined from the necessity of God’s nature to exist and to operate in a certain way (by Prop. 29). So if things could have been of another nature, or have been determined to operate in another way, so that the order of nature would be different, then God’s nature also could be other than it now is; and therefore (by Prop. 11) that nature too would have to exist, and consequently there could be two or more Gods; which (by Cor. 1 of Prop. 14) is absurd."}, {"id": "E1P33D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33D", "la": "Quapropter res nullo alio modo, neque alio ordine etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "Therefore things could have been produced in no other way and in no other order, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P33S1", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S1", "la": "Quoniam his luce meridiana clarius ostendi, nihil absolute in rebus dari, propter quod contingentes dicantur, explicare iam paucis volo, quid nobis per *contingens* erit intelligendum; sed prius, quid per *necessarium* et *impossibile*.", "en": "Since by the foregoing I have shown more clearly than the noonday light that there is absolutely nothing in things on account of which they may be called contingent, I wish now to explain briefly what we must understand by contingent; but first, what by necessary and impossible."}, {"id": "E1P33S1", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S1", "la": "Res aliqua *necessaria* dicitur vel ratione suae essentiae vel ratione causae. Rei enim alicuius existentia vel ex ipsius essentia et definitione, vel ex data causa efficiente necessario sequitur. Deinde his etiam de causis res aliqua *impossibilis* dicitur; nimirum quia vel ipsius essentia seu definitio contradictionem involvit, vel quia nulla causa externa datur ad talem rem producendam determinata.", "en": "A thing is called necessary either by reason of its essence or by reason of its cause. For the existence of any thing follows necessarily either from its essence and definition, or from a given efficient cause. Next, a thing is also called impossible for these same reasons: namely, either because its essence or definition involves a contradiction, or because there is no external cause determined to produce such a thing."}, {"id": "E1P33S1", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S1", "la": "At res aliqua nulla alia de causa *contingens* dicitur, nisi respectu defectus nostrae cognitionis. Res enim, cuius essentiam contradictionem involvere ignoramus, vel de qua probe scimus, eandem nullam contradictionem involvere, et tamen de ipsius existentia nihil certo affirmare possumus, propterea quod ordo causarum nos latet, ea nunquam nec ut necessaria nec ut impossibilis videri nobis potest; ideoque eandem vel contingentem vel possibilem vocamus.", "en": "But a thing is called contingent for no other reason than a defect in our knowledge. For a thing whose essence we do not know to involve a contradiction, or of which we know well that it involves no contradiction, and yet about whose existence we can affirm nothing with certainty, because the order of causes is hidden from us, can never seem to us either necessary or impossible; and so we call it either contingent or possible."}, {"id": "E1P33S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S2", "la": "Ex praecedentibus clare sequitur, res summa perfectione a Deo fuisse productas, quandoquidem ex data perfectissima natura necessario secutae sunt. Neque hoc Deum ullius arguit imperfectionis; ipsius enim perfectio hoc nos affirmare coegit. Imo ex huius contrario clare sequeretur (ut modo ostendi), Deum non esse summe perfectum; nimirum quia, si res alio modo fuissent productae, Deo alia natura esset tribuenda diversa ab ea, quam ex consideratione entis perfectissimi coacti sumus ei tribuere.", "en": "From the foregoing it follows clearly that things have been produced by God with the highest perfection, since they have followed necessarily from a given most perfect nature. Nor does this convict God of any imperfection; for it is his perfection that has compelled us to affirm it. Indeed, from the contrary it would clearly follow (as I have just shown) that God is not supremely perfect; namely because, if things had been produced in another way, another nature would have to be attributed to God, different from the one we have been compelled to attribute to him from the consideration of the most perfect being."}, {"id": "E1P33S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S2", "la": "Verum non dubito, quin multi hanc sententiam ut absurdam explodant, nec animum ad eandem perpendendam instituere velint; idque nulla alia de causa, quam quia Deo aliam libertatem assueti sunt tribuere, longe diversam ab illa, quae a nobis (defin. 7.) tradita est, videlicet absolutam voluntatem. Verum neque etiam dubito, si rem meditari vellent, nostrarumque demonstrationum seriem recte secum perpendere, quin tandem talem libertatem, qualem iam Deo tribuunt, non tantum ut nugatoriam, sed ut magnum scientiae obstaculum plane reiiciant. Nec opus est ut ea, quae in schol. prop. 17. dicta sunt, hic repetam.", "en": "But I do not doubt that many will dismiss this view as absurd and will not be willing to apply their minds to weighing it; and for no other reason than that they have been accustomed to attribute to God another freedom, far different from the one laid down by us (Def. 7), namely an absolute will. But neither do I doubt that, if they were willing to meditate on the matter and to weigh the sequence of our demonstrations rightly for themselves, they would in the end entirely reject such a freedom as they now attribute to God, not only as trifling but as a great obstacle to science. Nor is there any need for me to repeat here what was said in the Scholium to Prop. 17."}, {"id": "E1P33S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S2", "la": "Attamen in eorum gratiam adhuc ostendam, quod, quamvis concedatur, voluntatem ad Dei essentiam pertinere, ex eius perfectione nihilominus sequatur, res nullo alio potuisse modo neque ordine a Deo creari; quod facile erit ostendere, si prius consideremus id quod ipsimet concedunt, videlicet ex solo Dei decreto et voluntate pendere, ut unaquaeque res id quod est sit; nam alias Deus omnium rerum causa non esset.", "en": "Nevertheless, for their sake I shall show further that, even if it is granted that will pertains to God’s essence, it still follows from his perfection that things could have been created by God in no other way or order, which will be easy to show if we first consider what they themselves grant, namely that it depends on God’s decree and will alone that each thing is what it is; for otherwise God would not be the cause of all things."}, {"id": "E1P33S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S2", "la": "Deinde quod omnia Dei decreta ab aeterno ab ipso Deo sancita fuerunt. Nam alias imperfectionis et inconstantiae argueretur. At cum in aeterno non detur *quando*, *ante*, nec *post*, hinc, ex sola scilicet Dei perfectione, sequitur, Deum aliud decernere nunquam posse nec unquam potuisse; sive Deum ante sua decreta non fuisse, nec sine ipsis esse posse.", "en": "Next, that all God’s decrees have been established by God himself from eternity. For otherwise he would be convicted of imperfection and inconstancy. But since in eternity there is no when, before, or after, it follows from this, namely from God’s perfection alone, that God can never decree anything else, and never could have; or that God was not prior to his decrees, and cannot be without them."}, {"id": "E1P33S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S2", "la": "At dicent, quod, quamvis supponeretur, quod Deus aliam rerum naturam fecisset, vel quod ab aeterno aliud de natura eiusque ordine decrevisset, nulla inde in Deo sequeretur imperfectio. Verum si hoc dicant, concedent simul, Deum posse sua mutare decreta.", "en": "But they will say that, even if it were supposed that God had made another nature of things, or that from eternity he had decreed something else concerning nature and its order, no imperfection in God would follow from this. But if they say this, they will grant at the same time that God can change his decrees."}, {"id": "E1P33S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S2", "la": "Nam si Deus de natura eiusque ordine aliud, quam decrevit, decrevisset, hoc est, ut aliud de natura voluisset et concepisset, alium necessario quam iam habet intellectum, et aliam quam iam habet voluntatem habuisset.", "en": "For if God had decreed something concerning nature and its order other than he has decreed, that is, if he had willed and conceived something else concerning nature, he would necessarily have had another intellect than the one he now has, and another will than the one he now has."}, {"id": "E1P33S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S2", "la": "Et si Deo alium intellectum aliamque voluntatem tribuere licet absque ulla eius essentiae eiusque perfectionis mutatione; quid causae est, cur iam non possit sua de rebus creatis decreta mutare et nihilominus aeque perfectus manere? Eius enim intellectus et voluntas circa res creatas et earum ordinem in respectu suae essentiae et perfectionis perinde est quomodocumque concipiatur.", "en": "And if it is permissible to attribute to God another intellect and another will without any change of his essence and of his perfection, what reason is there why he cannot now change his decrees concerning created things and nevertheless remain equally perfect? For with respect to his essence and perfection, it makes no difference how his intellect and will concerning created things and their order are conceived."}, {"id": "E1P33S2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S2", "la": "Deinde omnes quos vidi philosophi concedunt, nullum in Deo dari intellectum potentia, sed tantum actu. Cum autem et eius intellectus et eius voluntas ab eiusdem essentia non distinguantur, ut etiam omnes concedunt; sequitur ergo hinc etiam, quod, si Deus alium intellectum actu habuisset et aliam voluntatem, eius etiam essentia alia necessario esset; ac proinde (ut a principio conclusi) si aliter res, quam iam sunt, a Deo productae essent, Dei intellectus eiusque voluntas, hoc est (ut conceditur) eius essentia alia esse deberet; quod est absurdum.", "en": "Further, all the philosophers I have seen grant that there is no potential intellect in God, but only actual intellect. But since his intellect and his will are not distinguished from his essence, as they all grant as well, it follows from this too that if God had had another actual intellect and another will, his essence would also necessarily be other; and therefore (as I concluded at the beginning), if things had been produced by God otherwise than they now are, God’s intellect and his will, that is (as is granted), his essence, would have to be other; which is absurd."}, {"id": "E1P33S2.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S2", "la": "Cum itaque res nullo alio modo nec ordine a Deo produci potuerint, et hoc verum esse ex summa Dei perfectione sequatur; nulla profecto sana ratio persuadere nobis potest, ut credamus, quod Deus noluerit omnia, quae in suo intellectu sunt, eadem illa perfectione, qua ipsa intelligit, creare.", "en": "Since, therefore, things could have been produced by God in no other way or order, and since the truth of this follows from God’s supreme perfection, there is surely no sound reason that can persuade us to believe that God did not wish to create all the things that are in his intellect with the same perfection with which he understands them."}, {"id": "E1P33S2.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S2", "la": "At dicent, in rebus nullam esse perfectionem neque imperfectionem, sed id quod in ipsis est, propter quod perfectae sunt aut imperfectae, et bonae aut malae dicuntur, a Dei tantum voluntate pendere; atque adeo si Deus voluisset, potuisset efficere, ut id quod iam perfectio est, summa esset imperfectio, et contra. Verum quid hoc aliud esset, quam aperte affirmare, quod Deus, qui id quod vult necessario intelligit, sua voluntate efficere potest, ut res alio modo, quam intelligit, intelligat? Quod (ut modo ostendi) magnum est absurdum.", "en": "But they will say that there is no perfection or imperfection in things, but that what is in them, on account of which they are perfect or imperfect, and are called good or evil, depends on God’s will alone; and so, if God had willed, he could have brought it about that what is now perfection would be the greatest imperfection, and conversely. But what else would this be than to affirm openly that God, who necessarily understands what he wills, can by his will bring it about that he understands things in another way than he understands them? Which (as I have just shown) is a great absurdity."}, {"id": "E1P33S2.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S2", "la": "Quare argumentum in ipsos retorquere possum, hoc modo: „Omnia a Dei potestate pendent. Ut res itaque aliter se habere possint, Dei necessario voluntas aliter se habere etiam deberet; atqui Dei voluntas aliter se habere nequit (ut modo ex Dei perfectione evidentissime ostendimus); ergo neque res aliter se habere possunt.”", "en": "So I can turn the argument against them, in this way: ‘All things depend on God’s power. For things to be able to be otherwise, therefore, God’s will would necessarily also have to be otherwise; but God’s will cannot be otherwise (as we have just shown most evidently from God’s perfection); therefore neither can things be otherwise.’"}, {"id": "E1P33S2.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S2", "la": "Fateor, hanc opinionem, quae omnia indifferenti cuidam Dei voluntati subiicit et ab ipsius beneplacito omnia pendere statuit, minus a vero aberrare, quam illorum, qui statuunt, Deum omnia sub ratione boni agere.", "en": "I confess that this opinion, which subjects all things to a certain indifferent will of God and maintains that all things depend on his good pleasure, is less far from the truth than the opinion of those who maintain that God does all things under the aspect of the good."}, {"id": "E1P33S2.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1P33S2", "la": "Nam hi aliquid extra Deum videntur ponere, quod a Deo non dependet, ad quod Deus tamquam ad exemplar in operando attendit, vel ad quod tamquam ad certum scopum collimat. Quod profecto nihil aliud est, quam Deum fato subiicere, quo nihil de Deo absurdius statui potest, quem ostendimus tam omnium rerum essentiae, quam earum existentiae primam et unicam liberam causam esse. Quare non est, ut in hoc absurdo refutando tempus consumam.", "en": "For these seem to posit something outside God which does not depend on God, to which God attends as to a model in operating, or at which he aims as at a definite goal. This is surely nothing else than to subject God to fate, than which nothing more absurd can be maintained about God, whom we have shown to be the first and only free cause both of the essence of all things and of their existence. So there is no reason for me to waste time refuting this absurdity."}, {"id": "E1P34", "p": "part1", "l": "1P34", "la": "Dei potentia est ipsa ipsius essentia.", "en": "God’s power is his very essence."}, {"id": "E1P34D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P34D", "la": "Ex sola enim necessitate Dei essentiae sequitur, Deum esse causam sui (per prop. 11.) et (per prop. 16. eiusque coroll.) omnium rerum. Ergo potentia Dei, qua ipse et omnia sunt et agunt, est ipsa ipsius essentia. Q. E. D.", "en": "For from the necessity of God’s essence alone it follows that God is the cause of himself (by Prop. 11) and (by Prop. 16 and its Cor.) of all things. Therefore God’s power, by which he and all things are and act, is his very essence. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P35", "p": "part1", "l": "1P35", "la": "Quicquid concipimus in Dei potestate esse, id necessario est.", "en": "Whatever we conceive to be in God’s power necessarily is."}, {"id": "E1P35D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P35D", "la": "Quicquid enim in Dei potestate est, id (per prop. praeced.) in eius essentia ita debet comprehendi, ut ex ea necessario sequatur, adeoque necessario est. Q. E. D.", "en": "For whatever is in God’s power must (by the preceding Prop.) be so comprehended in his essence that it follows necessarily from it, and so it necessarily is. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1P36", "p": "part1", "l": "1P36", "la": "Nihil existit, ex cuius natura aliquis effectus non sequatur.", "en": "Nothing exists from whose nature some effect does not follow."}, {"id": "E1P36D", "p": "part1", "l": "1P36D", "la": "Quicquid existit, Dei naturam sive essentiam certo et determinato modo exprimit (per coroll. prop. 25.), hoc est (per prop. 34.) quicquid existit, Dei potentiam, quae omnium rerum causa est, certo et determinato modo exprimit, adeoque (per prop. 16.) ex eo aliquis effectus sequi debet. Q. E. D.", "en": "Whatever exists expresses God’s nature, or essence, in a certain and determinate way (by Cor. of Prop. 25), that is (by Prop. 34), whatever exists expresses in a certain and determinate way God’s power, which is the cause of all things; and so (by Prop. 16) some effect must follow from it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E1App", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "His Dei naturam, eiusque proprietates explicui, ut quod *necessario existit; quod sit unicus; quod ex sola suae naturae necessitate sit et agat; quod sit omnium rerum causa libera et quomodo; quod omnia in Deo sint et ab ipso ita pendeant, ut sine ipso nec esse nec concipi possint; et denique quod omnia a Deo fuerint praedeterminata, non quidem ex libertate voluntatis, sive absoluto beneplacito, sed ex absoluta Dei natura, sive infinita potentia*.", "en": "In the foregoing I have explained the nature of God and its properties: that he exists necessarily; that he is unique; that he is and acts from the necessity of his nature alone; that he is the free cause of all things, and how; that all things are in God and so depend on him that without him they can neither be nor be conceived; and finally, that all things have been predetermined by God, not from freedom of the will, or absolute good pleasure, but from God’s absolute nature, or infinite power."}, {"id": "E1App", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Porro ubicumque data fuit occasio, praeiudicia, quae impedire poterant, quominus meae demonstrationes perciperentur, amovere curavi. Sed quia non pauca adhuc restant praeiudicia, quae etiam, imo maxime impedire poterant et possunt, quominus homines rerum concatenationem eo quo ipsam explicui modo amplecti possint, eadem hic ad examen rationis vocare operae pretium duxi.", "en": "Further, wherever the occasion arose, I have taken care to remove prejudices that could prevent my demonstrations from being perceived. But because not a few prejudices still remain which could also prevent, and still can prevent, indeed most of all, men from embracing the concatenation of things in the way I have explained it, I have thought it worth while to summon them here before the judgment of reason."}, {"id": "E1App", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Et quoniam omnia, quae hic indicare suscipio, praeiudicia pendent ab hoc uno, quod scilicet communiter supponant homines, omnes res naturales ut ipsos propter finem agere; imo ipsum Deum omnia ad certum aliquem finem dirigere, pro certo statuant (dicunt enim, Deum omnia propter hominem fecisse, hominem autem, ut ipsum coleret): hoc igitur unum prius considerabo, quaerendo scilicet, primo causam, cur plerique hoc in praeiudicio acquiescant et omnes natura adeo propensi sint ad idem amplectendum, deinde eiusdem falsitatem ostendam et tandem, quomodo ex hoc orta sint praeiudicia de *bono* et *malo*, *merito* et *peccato*, *laude* et *vituperio*, *ordine* et *confusione*, *pulchritudine* et *deformitate* et de aliis huius generis.", "en": "And since all the prejudices that I undertake to point out here depend on this one, namely that men commonly suppose that all natural things act, as they themselves do, for the sake of an end, and indeed hold it as certain that God himself directs all things to some definite end (for they say that God has made all things for man, and man that he might worship him), I shall therefore consider this one first. I shall ask, first, why most men acquiesce in this prejudice, and why all are by nature so inclined to embrace it; next I shall show its falsity; and finally, how from it have arisen the prejudices about good and evil, merit and sin, praise and blame, order and confusion, beauty and ugliness, and others of this kind."}, {"id": "E1App", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Verum, haec ab humanae mentis natura deducere, non est huius loci. Satis hic erit, si pro fundamento id capiam, quod apud omnes debet esse in confesso; nempe hoc, quod omnes homines rerum causarum ignari nascuntur, et quod omnes appetitum habent suum utile quaerendi, cuius rei sunt conscii.", "en": "To deduce these things from the nature of the human mind, however, is not the business of this place. It will be enough here if I take as a foundation what must be acknowledged by all: namely, that all men are born ignorant of the causes of things, and that all have an appetite to seek their own advantage, of which they are conscious."}, {"id": "E1App", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Ex his enim sequitur primo, quod homines se liberos esse opinentur, quandoquidem suarum volitionum suique appetitus sunt conscii, et de causis, a quibus disponuntur ad appetendum et volendum, quia earum sunt ignari, nec per somnium cogitant.", "en": "For from these it follows, first, that men think themselves free, since they are conscious of their volitions and their appetite, and do not think, even in their dreams, of the causes by which they are disposed to want and to will, because they are ignorant of them."}, {"id": "E1App", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Sequitur secundo, homines omnia propter finem agere, videlicet propter utile, quod appetunt. Unde fit, ut semper rerum peractarum causas finales tantum scire expetant, et ubi ipsas audiverint, quiescant; nimirum, quia nullam habent causam ulterius dubitandi. Sin autem easdem ex alio audire nequeant, nihil iis restat, nisi ut ad semet se convertant, et ad fines, a quibus ipsi ad similia determinari solent, reflectant; et sic ex suo ingenio ingenium alterius necessario iudicant.", "en": "It follows, second, that men do all things for the sake of an end, namely for the sake of the advantage they want. Hence it comes about that they always want to know only the final causes of things done, and when they have heard them, they are at rest; namely, because they have no further cause for doubt. But if they cannot hear them from another, nothing remains for them but to turn to themselves and reflect on the ends by which they themselves are usually determined to similar things; and so they necessarily judge the temperament of another from their own."}, {"id": "E1App", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Porro cum in se et extra se non pauca reperiant media, quae ad suum utile assequendum non parum conducant, ut ex. gr. oculos ad videndum, dentes ad masticandum, herbas et animantia ad alimentum, solem ad illuminandum, mare ad alendum pisces etc., hinc factum, ut omnia naturalia tamquam ad suum utile media considerent; et quia illa media ab ipsis inventa, non autem parata esse sciunt, hinc causam credendi habuerunt, aliquem alium esse, qui illa media in eorum usum paraverit. Nam postquam res ut media consideraverunt, credere non potuerunt, easdem se ipsas fecisse; sed ex mediis, quae sibi ipsi parare solent, concludere debuerunt, dari aliquem vel aliquos naturae rectores humana praeditos libertate, qui ipsis omnia curaverint et in eorum usum omnia fecerint.", "en": "Further, since they find in themselves and outside themselves not a few means that are of no little help in attaining their own advantage, as, for example, eyes for seeing, teeth for chewing, plants and animals for food, the sun for giving light, the sea for feeding fish, etc., it has come about that they consider all natural things as means to their own advantage. And because they know that they found these means, and did not provide them, they had cause to believe that there is someone else who provided these means for their use. For after they had considered things as means, they could not believe that these things had made themselves; but from the means that they are accustomed to provide for themselves, they had to conclude that there is some ruler, or some rulers, of nature, endowed with human freedom, who have taken care of all things for them and made all things for their use."}, {"id": "E1App", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Atque horum etiam ingenium, quandoquidem de eo nunquam quid audiverant, ex suo iudicare debuerunt; atque hinc statuerunt, deos omnia in hominum usum dirigere, ut homines sibi devinciant et in summo ab iisdem honore habeantur. Unde factum, ut unusquisque diversos Deum colendi modos ex suo ingenio excogitaverit, ut Deus eos supra reliquos diligeret et totam naturam in usum caecae illorum cupiditatis et insatiabilis avaritiae dirigeret.", "en": "And they had to judge the temperament of these rulers too from their own, since they had never heard anything about it; and hence they maintained that the gods direct all things for the use of men, in order to bind men to themselves and to be held by them in the highest honour. Hence it has come about that each has devised, from his own temperament, different ways of worshiping God, so that God might love him above the rest and direct the whole of nature to serve his blind desire and insatiable greed."}, {"id": "E1App", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Atque ita hoc praeiudicium in superstitionem versum et altas in mentibus egit radices; quod in causa fuit, ut unusquisque maximo conatu omnium rerum causas finales intelligere, easque explicare studeret. Sed dum quaesiverunt ostendere, naturam nihil frustra (hoc est, quod in usum hominum non sit) agere, nihil aliud videntur ostendisse, quam naturam deosque aeque ac homines delirare.", "en": "And so this prejudice was turned into superstition and struck deep roots in their minds; which was the reason why each strove with the greatest effort to understand and explain the final causes of all things. But while they sought to show that nature does nothing in vain (that is, nothing that is not for the use of men), they seem to have shown only that nature and the gods are as mad as men."}, {"id": "E1App", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Vide quaeso, quo res tandem evasit! Inter tot naturae commoda non pauca reperire debuerunt incommoda, tempestates scilicet, terrae motus, morbos etc., atque haec statuerunt propterea evenire, quod dii irati essent ob iniurias sibi ab hominibus factas, sive ob peccata in suo cultu commissa; et quamvis experientia in dies reclamaret ac infinitis exemplis ostenderet, commoda atque incommoda piis aeque ac impiis promiscue evenire, non ideo ab inveterato praeiudicio destiterunt.", "en": "See, I ask you, what the matter has come to in the end! Among so many benefits of nature they had to find not a few harms: storms, earthquakes, diseases, etc.; and they maintained that these happen because the gods are angry on account of wrongs done to them by men, or on account of sins committed in their worship. And although experience protested daily, and showed by infinitely many examples that benefits and harms befall the pious and the impious alike, indiscriminately, they did not on that account give up their inveterate prejudice."}, {"id": "E1App", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Facilius enim iis fuit, hoc inter alia incognita, quorum usum ignorabant, ponere et sic praesentem suum et innatum statum ignorantiae retinere, quam totam illam fabricam destruere et novam excogitare. Unde pro certo statuerunt, deorum iudicia humanum captum longissime superare: quae sane unica fuisset causa, ut veritas humanum genus in aeternum lateret, nisi mathesis, quae non circa fines, sed tantum circa figurarum essentias et proprietates versatur, aliam veritatis normam hominibus ostendisset.", "en": "For it was easier for them to put this among other unknown things whose use they did not know, and so to keep their present and innate state of ignorance, than to destroy that whole fabric and devise a new one. Hence they held it as certain that the judgments of the gods far surpass human understanding: which would surely have been the only cause why the truth should have been hidden from the human race to eternity, had not mathematics, which is concerned not with ends but only with the essences and properties of figures, shown men another standard of truth."}, {"id": "E1App", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Et praeter mathesin aliae etiam adsignari possunt causae (quas hic enumerare supervacaneum est), a quibus fieri potuit, ut homines communia haec praeiudicia animadverterent et in veram rerum cognitionem ducerentur.", "en": "And besides mathematics other causes can also be assigned (which it is superfluous to enumerate here) by which it could happen that men noticed these common prejudices and were led to the true knowledge of things."}, {"id": "E1App.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "His satis explicui id, quod primo loco promisi. Ut iam autem ostendam, naturam finem nullum sibi praefixum habere, et omnes causas finales nihil nisi humana esse figmenta, non opus est multis. Credo enim id iam satis constare tam ex fundamentis et causis, unde hoc praeiudicium originem suam traxisse ostendi, quam ex prop. 16. et coroll. 1. et 2. prop. 32., et praeterea ex iis omnibus, quibus ostendi, omnia naturae aeterna quadam necessitate summaque perfectione procedere.", "en": "With this I have sufficiently explained what I promised in the first place. Now to show that nature has no end set before it, and that all final causes are nothing but human fictions, there is no need of many words. For I believe that this is already sufficiently established, both from the foundations and causes from which I have shown that this prejudice took its origin, and from Prop. 16 and Cors. 1 and 2 of Prop. 32, and further from all those propositions by which I have shown that all things in nature proceed by a certain eternal necessity and with the highest perfection."}, {"id": "E1App.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Hoc tamen adhuc addam, nempe, hanc de fine doctrinam naturam omnino evertere. Nam id quod revera causa est, ut effectum considerat, et contra; deinde id quod natura prius est, facit posterius; et denique id quod supremum et perfectissimum est, reddit imperfectissimum.", "en": "This, however, I shall add, namely that this doctrine about the end entirely overturns nature. For what is in reality a cause it considers as an effect, and conversely; next, it makes what is by nature prior, posterior; and finally, it renders what is supreme and most perfect, most imperfect."}, {"id": "E1App.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Nam (duobus prioribus omissis, quia per se manifesta sunt) ut ex prop. 21. 22. et 23. constat, ille effectus perfectissimus est, qui a Deo immediate producitur, et quo aliquid pluribus causis intermediis indiget ut producatur, eo imperfectius est. At si res, quae immediate a Deo productae sunt, ea de causa factae essent, ut Deus finem assequeretur suum, tum necessario ultimae, quarum de causa priores factae sunt, omnium praestantissimae essent.", "en": "For (passing over the first two, because they are self-evident) as is established from Props. 21, 22 and 23, that effect is most perfect which is produced by God immediately, and the more intermediate causes something needs in order to be produced, the more imperfect it is. But if the things produced immediately by God had been made so that God might attain his end, then the last things, for the sake of which the earlier were made, would necessarily be the most excellent of all."}, {"id": "E1App.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Deinde haec doctrina Dei perfectionem tollit; nam, si Deus propter finem agit, aliquid necessario appetit, quo caret. Et quamvis theologi et metaphysici distinguant inter finem indigentiae et finem assimilationis, fatentur tamen Deum omnia propter se, non vero propter res creandas egisse; quia nihil ante creationem praeter Deum assignare possunt, propter quod Deus ageret; adeoque necessario fateri coguntur, Deum iis, propter quae media parare voluit, caruisse, eaque cupivisse, ut per se clarum.", "en": "Next, this doctrine takes away God’s perfection; for if God acts for the sake of an end, he necessarily wants something that he lacks. And although theologians and metaphysicians distinguish between an end of need and an end of assimilation, they nevertheless confess that God did all things for his own sake, not for the sake of the things to be created; because before creation they can assign nothing except God for whose sake God would act; and so they are necessarily compelled to confess that God lacked the things for whose sake he willed to provide means, and that he desired them, as is self-evident."}, {"id": "E1App.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Nec hic praetereundum est, quod huius doctrinae sectatores, qui in assignandis rerum finibus suum ingenium ostentare voluerunt, ad hanc suam doctrinam probandam novum attulerunt modum argumentandi, reducendo scilicet non ad impossibile, sed ad ignorantiam; quod ostendit nullum aliud fuisse huic doctrinae argumentandi medium. Nam si ex. gr. ex culmine aliquo lapis in alicuius caput ceciderit eumque interfecerit, hoc modo demonstrabunt, lapidem ad hominem interficiendum cecidisse; ni enim eum in finem Deo id volente ceciderit, quomodo tot circumstantiae (saepe enim multae simul concurrunt) casu concurrere potuerunt?", "en": "Nor should it be passed over here that the followers of this doctrine, who have wished to show off their ingenuity in assigning the ends of things, have introduced a new way of arguing to prove this doctrine of theirs: namely, reduction not to the impossible, but to ignorance; which shows that this doctrine had no other means of argument. For if, for example, a stone has fallen from a roof onto someone’s head and killed him, they will demonstrate in this way that the stone fell in order to kill the man: for if it did not fall for that end, by the will of God, how could so many circumstances (for often many concur at once) have concurred by chance?"}, {"id": "E1App.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Respondebis fortasse, id ex eo, quod ventus flavit et quod homo illac iter habebat, evenisse. At instabunt: cur ventus illo tempore flavit? cur homo illo eodemque tempore illac iter habebat? Si iterum respondeas, ventum tum ortum, quia mare praecedenti die tempore adhuc tranquillo agitari inceperat, et quod homo ab amico invitatus fuerat; instabunt iterum, quia nullus rogandi finis: cur autem mare agitabatur? cur homo in illud tempus invitatus fuit?", "en": "You will perhaps answer that it happened because the wind was blowing and the man was going that way. But they will insist: why was the wind blowing at that time? Why was the man going that way at that very time? If you answer again that the wind arose then because on the preceding day, while the weather was still calm, the sea had begun to be agitated, and that the man had been invited by a friend, they will insist again, since there is no end to asking: but why was the sea agitated? Why was the man invited at that time?"}, {"id": "E1App.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Et sic porro causarum causas rogare non cessabunt, donec ad Dei voluntatem, hoc est, ignorantiae asylum confugeris. Sic etiam, ubi corporis humani fabricam vident, stupescunt et ex eo, quod tantae artis causas ignorant, concludunt, eandem non mechanica, sed divina vel supernaturali arte fabricari, talique modo constitui, ut una pars alteram non laedat.", "en": "And so they will not cease to ask for the causes of causes until you take refuge in the will of God, that is, the sanctuary of ignorance. So too, when they see the structure of the human body, they are astonished, and because they do not know the causes of so much art, they conclude that it is fashioned not by mechanical but by divine or supernatural art, and constituted in such a way that one part does not injure another."}, {"id": "E1App.2", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Atque hinc fit, ut qui miraculorum causas veras quaerit, quique res naturales ut doctus intelligere, non autem ut stultus admirari studet, passim pro haeretico et impio habeatur et proclametur ab iis, quos vulgus tamquam naturae deorumque interpretes adorat. Nam sciunt, quod sublata ignorantia stupor, hoc est, unicum argumentandi, tuendaeque suae auctoritatis medium, quod habent, tollitur. Sed haec relinquo et ad id, quod tertio loco hic agere constitui, pergo.", "en": "And hence it comes about that one who seeks the true causes of miracles, and strives to understand natural things as a learned man, and not to wonder at them as a fool, is everywhere held and proclaimed a heretic and impious by those whom the common people worship as interpreters of nature and of the gods. For they know that if ignorance is taken away, astonishment, that is, the only means they have of arguing and of defending their authority, is taken away too. But I leave these things and pass on to what I decided to treat here in the third place."}, {"id": "E1App.3", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Postquam homines sibi persuaserunt, omnia quae fiunt propter ipsos fieri; id in unaquaque re praecipuum iudicare debuerunt quod ipsis utilissimum, et illa omnia praestantissima aestimare, a quibus optime afficiebantur. Unde has formare debuerunt notiones, quibus rerum naturas explicarent, scilicet *bonum, malum, ordinem, confusionem, calidum, frigidum, pulchritudinem* et *deformitatem*; et quia se liberos existimant, inde hae notiones ortae sunt, scilicet *laus* et *vituperium*, *peccatum* et *meritum*.", "en": "After men persuaded themselves that everything that happens happens for their sake, they had to judge that the principal thing in each thing is what is most useful to them, and to rate as most excellent all those things by which they were most agreeably affected. Hence they had to form these notions, by which to explain the natures of things: good, evil, order, confusion, hot, cold, beauty and ugliness; and because they think themselves free, these notions have arisen: praise and blame, sin and merit."}, {"id": "E1App.3", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Sed has infra, postquam de natura humana egero, illas autem hic breviter explicabo. Nempe id omne, quod ad valetudinem et Dei cultum conducit, bonum; quod autem iis contrarium est, malum vocaverunt.", "en": "But the latter I shall explain below, after I have treated human nature; the former I shall explain briefly here. Namely, whatever conduces to health and to the worship of God they have called good; what is contrary to these, evil."}, {"id": "E1App.3", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Et quia ii, qui rerum naturam non intelligunt, nihil de rebus affirmant, sed res tantummodo imaginantur et imaginationem pro intellectu capiunt, ideo ordinem in rebus esse firmiter credunt rerum suaeque naturae ignari. Nam cum ita sint dispositae, ut, cum nobis per sensus repraesentantur, eas facile imaginari et consequenter earum facile recordari possimus, easdem bene ordinatas, si vero contra, ipsas male ordinatas, sive confusas esse dicimus.", "en": "And because those who do not understand the nature of things affirm nothing about things, but only imagine them and take the imagination for the intellect, they firmly believe, ignorant of things and of their own nature, that there is order in things. For when things are so disposed that, when they are presented to us through the senses, we can easily imagine them and consequently easily remember them, we say that they are well ordered; but if the contrary, that they are badly ordered, or confused."}, {"id": "E1App.3", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Et quoniam ea nobis prae ceteris grata sunt, quae facile imaginari possumus, ideo homines ordinem confusioni praeferunt; quasi ordo aliquid in natura praeter respectum ad nostram imaginationem esset; dicuntque Deum omnia ordine creasse, et hoc modo ipsi nescientes Deo imaginationem tribuunt; nisi velint forte, Deum humanae imaginationi providentem res omnes eo disposuisse modo, quo ipsas facillime imaginari possent; nec moram forsan iis iniiciet, quod infinita reperiantur, quae nostram imaginationem longe superant, et plurima, quae ipsam propter eius imbecillitatem confundunt. Sed de hac re satis.", "en": "And since those things are more pleasing to us than others which we can easily imagine, men prefer order to confusion, as if order were something in nature beyond its relation to our imagination; and they say that God has created all things in order, and in this way, without knowing it, attribute imagination to God; unless perhaps they mean that God, providing for human imagination, has disposed all things in the way in which they could most easily imagine them. Nor perhaps will it give them pause that infinitely many things are found which far surpass our imagination, and very many which confuse it on account of its weakness. But enough of this."}, {"id": "E1App.3", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Ceterae deinde notiones etiam praeter imaginandi modos, quibus imaginatio diversimode afficitur, nihil sunt, et tamen ab ignaris tamquam praecipua rerum attributa considerantur; quia, ut iam diximus, res omnes propter ipsos factas esse credunt, et rei alicuius naturam bonam vel malam, sanam vel putridam et corruptam dicunt, prout ab eadem afficiuntur.", "en": "Next, the other notions too are nothing but modes of imagining, by which the imagination is affected in various ways; and yet the ignorant consider them the principal attributes of things, because, as we have already said, they believe that all things have been made for their sake, and they call the nature of a thing good or evil, healthy or rotten and corrupt, according as they are affected by it."}, {"id": "E1App.3", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Ex. gr. si motus, quem nervi ab obiectis per oculos repraesentatis accipiunt, valetudini conducat, obiecta, a quibus causatur, pulchra dicuntur, quae autem contrarium motum cient, deformia. Quae deinde per nares sensum movent, odorifera vel faetida vocant, quae per linguam, dulcia aut amara, sapida aut insipida etc.; quae autem per tactum, dura aut mollia, aspera aut laevia, etc.", "en": "For example, if the motion which the nerves receive from objects presented through the eyes conduces to health, the objects by which it is caused are called beautiful, and those which cause the contrary motion, ugly. Those, next, which move the sense through the nostrils they call fragrant or fetid; through the tongue, sweet or bitter, tasty or tasteless, etc.; and through touch, hard or soft, rough or smooth, etc."}, {"id": "E1App.3", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Et quae denique aures movent, strepitum, sonum vel harmoniam edere dicuntur, quorum postremum homines adeo dementavit, ut Deum etiam harmonia delectari crederent. Nec desunt philosophi, qui sibi persuaserint, motus coelestes harmoniam componere.", "en": "And finally, those which move the ears are said to produce noise, sound or harmony, the last of which has driven men so mad that they have believed that God too delights in harmony. Nor are there lacking philosophers who have persuaded themselves that the celestial motions compose a harmony."}, {"id": "E1App.3", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Quae omnia satis ostendunt, unumquemque pro dispositione cerebri de rebus iudicasse, vel potius imaginationis affectiones pro rebus accepisse. Quare non mirum est (ut hoc etiam obiter notemus), quod inter homines tot, quot experimur, controversiae ortae sint, ex quibus tandem scepticismus.", "en": "All of which shows sufficiently that each has judged things according to the disposition of his brain, or rather has taken the affections of the imagination for things. So it is no wonder (to note this too in passing) that as many controversies as we experience have arisen among men, and from them, finally, scepticism."}, {"id": "E1App.3", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Nam, quamvis humana corpora in multis conveniant, in plurimis tamen discrepant, et ideo id quod uni bonum, alteri malum videtur; quod uni ordinatum, alteri confusum; quod uni gratum, alteri ingratum est; et sic de ceteris, quibus hic supersedeo, cum quia huius loci non est de his ex professo agere, tum quia hoc omnes satis experti sunt.", "en": "For although human bodies agree in many things, they differ in very many, and so what seems good to one seems evil to another; what seems ordered to one, confused to another; what is pleasing to one is displeasing to another; and so of the rest, which I pass over here, both because this is not the place to treat them expressly, and because everyone has experienced this sufficiently."}, {"id": "E1App.3", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Omnibus enim in ore est: quot capita, tot sensus, suo quemque sensu abundare, non minora cerebrorum, quam palatorum esse discrimina; quae sententiae satis ostendunt, homines pro dispositione cerebri de rebus iudicare, resque potius imaginari, quam intelligere. Res enim si intellexissent, illae omnes teste mathesi, si non allicerent, ad minimum convincerent.", "en": "For everyone has these sayings on his lips: ‘So many heads, so many opinions’, ‘everyone abounds in his own sense’, ‘there are no fewer differences of brains than of palates’: which sayings show sufficiently that men judge things according to the disposition of the brain, and imagine things rather than understand them. For if they had understood things, these would all, as mathematics testifies, if not attract them, at least convince them."}, {"id": "E1App.4", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Videmus itaque omnes notiones, quibus vulgus solet naturam explicare, modos esse tantummodo imaginandi, nec ullius rei naturam, sed tantum imaginationis constitutionem indicare; et quia nomina habent, quasi essent entium, extra imaginationem existentium, eadem entia non rationis, sed imaginationis voco; atque adeo omnia argumenta, quae contra nos ex similibus notionibus petuntur, facile propulsari possunt.", "en": "We see, therefore, that all the notions by which the common people are accustomed to explain nature are only modes of imagining, and indicate the nature of nothing, but only the constitution of the imagination; and because they have names, as if they were notions of beings existing outside the imagination, I call them beings not of reason but of imagination; and so all the arguments that are drawn against us from such notions can easily be repelled."}, {"id": "E1App.4", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Solent enim multi sic argumentari: si omnia ex necessitate perfectissimae Dei naturae sunt consecuta, unde ergo tot imperfectiones in natura ortae? Videlicet rerum corruptio ad faetorem usque, rerum deformitas, quae nauseam moveat, confusio, malum, peccatum etc.", "en": "For many are accustomed to argue thus: if all things have followed from the necessity of God’s most perfect nature, where then have so many imperfections in nature come from? For instance, the corruption of things to the point of stench, the ugliness of things that moves nausea, confusion, evil, sin, etc."}, {"id": "E1App.4", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Sed, ut modo dixi, facile confutantur. Nam rerum perfectio ex sola earum natura et potentia est aestimanda; nec ideo res magis aut minus perfectae sunt, propterea quod hominum sensum delectant vel offendunt, quod humanae naturae conducunt, vel quod eidem repugnant.", "en": "But, as I have just said, these are easily refuted. For the perfection of things is to be judged from their nature and power alone; nor are things more or less perfect because they please or offend men’s senses, or because they are of use to human nature or are repugnant to it."}, {"id": "E1App.4", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Iis autem, qui quaerunt: cur Deus omnes homines non ita creavit, ut solo rationis ductu gubernarentur? nihil aliud respondeo, quam: quia ei non defuit materia ad omnia ex summo nimirum ad infimum perfectionis gradum creanda; vel magis proprie loquendo: quia ipsius naturae leges adeo amplae fuerunt, ut sufficerent ad omnia quae ab aliquo infinito intellectu concipi possunt producenda, ut prop. 16. demonstravi.", "en": "But to those who ask why God did not create all men so that they would be governed by the guidance of reason alone, I answer nothing but this: because he did not lack material for creating all things, namely from the highest degree of perfection to the lowest; or, to speak more properly, because the laws of his nature were so ample that they sufficed for producing all things that can be conceived by some infinite intellect, as I have demonstrated in Prop. 16."}, {"id": "E1App.4", "p": "part1", "l": "1App", "la": "Haec sunt, quae hic notare suscepi praeiudicia. Si quaedam huius farinae adhuc restant, poterunt eadem ab unoquoque mediocri meditatione emendari.", "en": "These are the prejudices I undertook to note here. If some of this sort still remain, anyone can correct them with a little meditation."}, {"id": "E2Pref", "p": "part2", "l": "2Pref", "la": "Transeo iam ad ea explicanda, quae ex Dei, sive entis aeterni et infiniti essentia necessario debuerunt sequi. Non quidem omnia (infinita enim infinitis modis ex ipsa debere sequi P. 1. prop. 16. demonstravimus), sed ea solummodo, quae nos ad *mentis humanae* eiusque summae beatitudinis cognitionem quasi manu ducere possunt.", "en": "I pass now to explaining the things that must necessarily have followed from the essence of God, or of the eternal and infinite being. Not all of them, indeed (for we have demonstrated, Part I, Prop. 16, that infinitely many things must follow from it in infinitely many ways), but only those that can lead us, as if by the hand, to the knowledge of the human mind and its highest blessedness."}, {"id": "E2D1", "p": "part2", "l": "2D1", "la": "Per *corpus* intelligo modum, qui Dei essentiam, quatenus ut res extensa consideratur, certo et determinato modo exprimit. Vide P. 1. prop. 25. coroll.", "en": "By body I understand a mode which expresses God’s essence, insofar as he is considered as an extended thing, in a certain and determinate way. See Part I, Cor. of Prop. 25."}, {"id": "E2D2", "p": "part2", "l": "2D2", "la": "Ad *essentiam* alicuius rei id pertinere dico, quo dato res necessario ponitur et quo sublato res necessario tollitur; vel id, sine quo res, et vice versa quod sine re nec esse nec concipi potest.", "en": "I say that there pertains to the essence of a thing that which, being given, the thing is necessarily posited and which, being taken away, the thing is necessarily taken away; or that without which the thing, and which in turn without the thing, can neither be nor be conceived."}, {"id": "E2D3", "p": "part2", "l": "2D3", "la": "Per *ideam* intelligo mentis conceptum, quem mens format, propterea quod res est cogitans.", "en": "By idea I understand a concept of the mind, which the mind forms because it is a thinking thing."}, {"id": "E2D3Ex", "p": "part2", "l": "2D3Ex", "la": "Dico potius conceptum, quam perceptionem, quia perceptionis nomen indicare videtur, mentem ab obiecto pati. At conceptus actionem mentis exprimere videtur.", "en": "I say concept rather than perception, because the word perception seems to indicate that the mind is acted on by the object. But concept seems to express an action of the mind."}, {"id": "E2D4", "p": "part2", "l": "2D4", "la": "Per *ideam adaequatam* intelligo ideam, quae, quatenus in se sine relatione ad obiectum consideratur, omnes verae ideae proprietates sive denominationes intrinsecas habet.", "en": "By adequate idea I understand an idea which, insofar as it is considered in itself, without relation to its object, has all the properties, or intrinsic denominations, of a true idea."}, {"id": "E2D4Ex", "p": "part2", "l": "2D4Ex", "la": "Dico intrinsecas, ut illam secludam, quae extrinseca est, nempe convenientiam ideae cum suo ideato.", "en": "I say intrinsic, so as to exclude the one that is extrinsic, namely the agreement of the idea with its object."}, {"id": "E2D5", "p": "part2", "l": "2D5", "la": "*Duratio* est indefinita existendi continuatio.", "en": "Duration is an indefinite continuation of existing."}, {"id": "E2D5Ex", "p": "part2", "l": "2D5Ex", "la": "Dico indefinitam, quia per ipsam rei existentis naturam determinari nequaquam potest, neque etiam a causa efficiente, quae scilicet rei existentiam necessario ponit, non autem tollit.", "en": "I say indefinite, because it can by no means be determined through the very nature of the existing thing, nor even by the efficient cause, which necessarily posits the existence of the thing, but does not take it away."}, {"id": "E2D6", "p": "part2", "l": "2D6", "la": "Per *realitatem* et *perfectionem* idem intelligo.", "en": "By reality and perfection I understand the same thing."}, {"id": "E2D7", "p": "part2", "l": "2D7", "la": "Per *res singulares* intelligo res, quae finitae sunt et determinatam habent existentiam. Quod si plura individua in una actione ita concurrant, ut omnia simul unius effectus sint causa, eadem omnia eatenus ut unam rem singularem considero.", "en": "By singular things I understand things that are finite and have a determinate existence. And if several individuals so concur in one action that all are together the cause of one effect, I consider them all, to that extent, as one singular thing."}, {"id": "E2A1", "p": "part2", "l": "2A1", "la": "Hominis essentia non involvit necessariam existentiam, hoc est, ex naturae ordine tam fieri potest, ut hic et ille homo existat, quam ut non existat.", "en": "The essence of man does not involve necessary existence, that is, from the order of nature it can happen equally that this or that man exists, or that he does not exist."}, {"id": "E2A2", "p": "part2", "l": "2A2", "la": "Homo cogitat.", "en": "Man thinks."}, {"id": "E2A3", "p": "part2", "l": "2A3", "la": "Modi cogitandi, ut amor, cupiditas, vel quicumque nomine affectus animi insigniuntur, non dantur nisi in eodem individuo detur idea rei amatae, desideratae, etc. At idea dari potest, quamvis nullus alius detur cogitandi modus.", "en": "Modes of thinking, such as love, desire, or whatever is designated by the name of affects of the mind, are not given unless there is in the same individual the idea of the thing loved, desired, etc. But an idea can be given though no other mode of thinking is given."}, {"id": "E2A4", "p": "part2", "l": "2A4", "la": "Nos corpus quoddam multis modis affici sentimus.", "en": "We feel that a certain body is affected in many ways."}, {"id": "E2A5", "p": "part2", "l": "2A5", "la": "Nullas res singulares praeter corpora et cogitandi modos sentimus, nec percipimus.", "en": "We neither feel nor perceive any singular things except bodies and modes of thinking."}, {"id": "E2A5.2", "p": "part2", "l": "2A5", "la": "Postulata vide post prop. 13.", "en": "For the postulates, see after Prop. 13."}, {"id": "E2P1", "p": "part2", "l": "2P1", "la": "*Cogitatio* attributum Dei est, sive Deus est res cogitans.", "en": "Thought is an attribute of God, or God is a thinking thing."}, {"id": "E2P1D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P1D", "la": "Singulares cogitationes sive haec et illa cogitatio modi sunt, qui Dei naturam certo et determinato modo exprimunt (per coroll. prop. 25. P. 1.). Competit ergo Deo (per defin. 5. P. 1.) attributum, cuius conceptum singulares omnes cogitationes involvunt, per quod etiam concipiuntur. Est igitur cogitatio unum ex infinitis Dei attributis, quod Dei aeternam et infinitam essentiam exprimit (vide defin. 6. P. 1.), sive Deus est res cogitans. Q. E. D.", "en": "Singular thoughts, or this and that thought, are modes which express God’s nature in a certain and determinate way (by Cor. of Prop. 25, Part I). There pertains to God, therefore (by Def. 5, Part I), an attribute whose concept all singular thoughts involve, and through which they are also conceived. Thought, therefore, is one of God’s infinite attributes, which expresses God’s eternal and infinite essence (see Def. 6, Part I), or God is a thinking thing. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P1S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P1S", "la": "Patet etiam haec propositio ex hoc, quod nos possumus ens cogitans infinitum concipere. Nam quo plura ens cogitans potest cogitare, eo plus realitatis sive perfectionis idem continere concipimus. Ergo ens, quod infinita infinitis modis cogitare potest, est necessario virtute cogitandi infinitum. Cum itaque ad solam cogitationem attendendo ens infinitum concipiamus, est necessario (per defin. 4.et 6. P. 1.) cogitatio unum ex infinitis Dei attributis, ut volebamus.", "en": "This proposition is also evident from the fact that we can conceive an infinite thinking being. For the more things a thinking being can think, the more reality or perfection we conceive it to contain. Therefore a being that can think infinitely many things in infinitely many ways is necessarily infinite in its power of thinking. Since, therefore, attending only to thought, we conceive an infinite being, thought is necessarily (by Defs. 4 and 6, Part I) one of God’s infinite attributes, as we wished."}, {"id": "E2P2", "p": "part2", "l": "2P2", "la": "*Extensio* attributum Dei est, sive Deus est res extensa.", "en": "Extension is an attribute of God, or God is an extended thing."}, {"id": "E2P2D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P2D", "la": "Huius eodem modo procedit, ac demonstratio praeced. prop.", "en": "The demonstration of this proceeds in the same way as the demonstration of the preceding proposition."}, {"id": "E2P3", "p": "part2", "l": "2P3", "la": "In Deo datur necessario idea tam eius essentiae, quam omnium, quae ex ipsius essentia necessario sequuntur.", "en": "In God there is necessarily an idea both of his essence and of all things that necessarily follow from his essence."}, {"id": "E2P3D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P3D", "la": "Deus enim (per prop. 1. huius) infinita infinitis modis cogitare, sive (quod idem est per prop. 16. P. 1.) ideam suae essentiae et omnium, quae necessario ex ea sequuntur, formare potest. Atqui omne id, quod in Dei potestate est, necessario est (per prop. 35. P. 1.). Ergo datur necessario talis idea, et (per prop. 15. P. 1.) non nisi in Deo. Q. E. D.", "en": "For God (by Prop. 1 of this Part) can think infinitely many things in infinitely many ways, or (what is the same, by Prop. 16, Part I) can form the idea of his essence and of all things that necessarily follow from it. But all that is in God’s power necessarily is (by Prop. 35, Part I). Therefore there is necessarily such an idea, and (by Prop. 15, Part I) only in God. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P3S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P3S", "la": "Vulgus per Dei potentiam intelligit Dei liberam voluntatem et ius in omnia quae sunt, quaeque propterea communiter ut contingentia considerantur. Deum enim potestatem omnia destruendi habere dicunt et in nihilum redigendi. Dei porro potentiam cum potentia regum saepissime comparant.", "en": "By God’s power the common people understand God’s free will and his right over all things that are, which are therefore commonly considered as contingent. For they say that God has the power to destroy all things and reduce them to nothing. Further, they very often compare God’s power with the power of kings."}, {"id": "E2P3S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P3S", "la": "Sed hoc in coroll. 1. et 2. prop. 32. P. 1. refutavimus; et prop. 16. P. 1. ostendimus, Deum eadem necessitate agere, qua se ipsum intelligit; hoc est, sicuti ex necessitate divinae naturae sequitur (sicut omnes uno ore statuunt), ut Deus se ipsum intelligat, eadem etiam necessitate sequitur, ut Deus infinita infinitis modis agat. Deinde prop. 34. P. 1. ostendimus, Dei potentiam nihil esse, praeterquam Dei actuosam essentiam; adeoque tam nobis impossibile est concipere, Deum non agere, quam Deum non esse.", "en": "But we have refuted this in Cors. 1 and 2 of Prop. 32, Part I; and in Prop. 16, Part I we have shown that God acts with the same necessity by which he understands himself; that is, just as it follows from the necessity of the divine nature (as all maintain with one voice) that God understands himself, with the same necessity it also follows that God does infinitely many things in infinitely many ways. Next, in Prop. 34, Part I we have shown that God’s power is nothing but God’s active essence; and so it is as impossible for us to conceive that God does not act as that God does not exist."}, {"id": "E2P3S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P3S", "la": "Porro si haec ulterius persequi liberet, possem hic etiam ostendere, potentiam illam, quam vulgus Deo affingit, non tantum humanam esse (quod ostendit Deum hominem, vel instar hominis a vulgo concipi), sed etiam impotentiam involvere. Sed nolo de eadem re toties sermonem instituere.", "en": "Further, if it pleased me to pursue this matter further, I could also show here that the power which the common people ascribe to God is not only human (which shows that God is conceived by the common people as a man, or like a man), but also involves lack of power. But I do not wish to speak about the same thing so often."}, {"id": "E2P3S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P3S", "la": "Lectorem solummodo iterum atque iterum rogo, ut quae in P. 1. ex prop. 16. usque ad finem de hac re dicta sunt, semel atque iterum perpendat. Nam nemo ea quae volo percipere recte poterit, nisi magnopere caveat, ne Dei potentiam cum humana regum potentia vel iure confundat.", "en": "I only ask the reader again and again to weigh, once and again, what has been said on this matter in Part I from Prop. 16 to the end. For no one will be able to perceive rightly the things I intend unless he takes great care not to confuse God’s power with the human power or right of kings."}, {"id": "E2P4", "p": "part2", "l": "2P4", "la": "Idea Dei, ex qua infinita infinitis modis sequuntur, unica tantum esse potest.", "en": "The idea of God, from which infinitely many things follow in infinitely many ways, can only be unique."}, {"id": "E2P4D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P4D", "la": "Intellectus infinitus nihil praeter Dei attributa eiusque affectiones comprehendit (per prop. 30. P. 1.). Atqui Deus est unicus (per coroll. 1. prop. 14. P. 1.). Ergo idea Dei, ex qua infinita infinitis modis sequuntur, unica tantum esse potest. Q. E. D.", "en": "The infinite intellect comprehends nothing except God’s attributes and his affections (by Prop. 30, Part I). But God is unique (by Cor. 1 of Prop. 14, Part I). Therefore the idea of God, from which infinitely many things follow in infinitely many ways, can only be unique. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P5", "p": "part2", "l": "2P5", "la": "Esse formale idearum Deum, quatenus tantum ut res cogitans consideratur, pro causa agnoscit et non, quatenus alio attributo explicatur; hoc est, tam Dei attributorum, quam rerum singularium ideae non ipsa ideata sive res perceptas pro causa efficiente agnoscunt, sed ipsum Deum, quatenus est res cogitans.", "en": "The formal being of ideas admits God as a cause only insofar as he is considered as a thinking thing, and not insofar as he is explained by any other attribute; that is, the ideas both of God’s attributes and of singular things admit as their efficient cause not the objects themselves, or the things perceived, but God himself, insofar as he is a thinking thing."}, {"id": "E2P5D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P5D", "la": "Patet quidem ex prop. 3. huius. Ibi enim concludebamus, Deum ideam suae essentiae et omnium, quae ex ea necessario sequuntur, formare posse ex hoc solo, nempe quod Deus est res cogitans, et non ex eo, quod sit suae ideae obiectum. Quare esse formale idearum Deum, quatenus est res cogitans, pro causa agnoscit.", "en": "This is evident from Prop. 3 of this Part. For there we concluded that God can form the idea of his essence and of all things that necessarily follow from it from this alone, namely that God is a thinking thing, and not from his being the object of his idea. So the formal being of ideas admits God as a cause insofar as he is a thinking thing."}, {"id": "E2P5D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P5D", "la": "Sed aliter hoc modo demonstratur. Esse formale idearum modus est cogitandi (ut per se notum), hoc est (per coroll. prop. 25. P. 1.) modus, qui Dei naturam, quatenus est res cogitans, certo modo exprimit, adeoque (per prop. 10. P. 1.) nullius alterius attributi Dei conceptum involvit, et consequenter (per axiom. 4. P. 1.) nullius alterius attributi, nisi cogitationis, est effectus. Adeoque esse formale idearum Deum, quatenus tantum ut res cogitans consideratur etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "But it is demonstrated otherwise in this way. The formal being of ideas is a mode of thinking (as is self-evident), that is (by Cor. of Prop. 25, Part I), a mode which expresses God’s nature, insofar as he is a thinking thing, in a certain way, and so (by Prop. 10, Part I) involves the concept of no other attribute of God, and consequently (by Ax. 4, Part I) is the effect of no other attribute except thought. And so the formal being of ideas admits God as a cause insofar as he is considered only as a thinking thing, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P6", "p": "part2", "l": "2P6", "la": "Cuiuscumque attributi modi Deum, quatenus tantum sub illo attributo, cuius modi sunt, et non quatenus sub ullo alio consideratur, pro causa habent.", "en": "The modes of each attribute have God for their cause only insofar as he is considered under the attribute of which they are modes, and not insofar as he is considered under any other."}, {"id": "E2P6D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P6D", "la": "Unumquodque enim attributum per se absque alio concipitur (per prop. 10. P. 1.). Quare uniuscuiusque attributi modi conceptum sui attributi, non autem alterius involvunt; adeoque (per axiom. 4. P. 1.) Deum, quatenus tantum sub illo attributo, cuius modi sunt, et non, quatenus sub ullo alio consideratur, pro causa habent. Q. E. D.", "en": "For each attribute is conceived through itself without any other (by Prop. 10, Part I). So the modes of each attribute involve the concept of their own attribute, but not of another; and so (by Ax. 4, Part I) they have God for their cause only insofar as he is considered under the attribute of which they are modes, and not insofar as he is considered under any other. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P6C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P6C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, quod esse formale rerum, quae modi non sunt cogitandi, non sequitur ideo ex divina natura, quia res prius cognovit; sed eodem modo eademque necessitate res ideatae ex suis attributis consequuntur et concluduntur, ac ideas ex attributo cogitationis consequi ostendimus.", "en": "From this it follows that the formal being of things which are not modes of thinking does not follow from the divine nature because God first knew the things; rather, the things that are the objects of ideas follow and are inferred from their own attributes in the same way and with the same necessity as we have shown ideas to follow from the attribute of thought."}, {"id": "E2P7", "p": "part2", "l": "2P7", "la": "Ordo et connexio idearum idem est, ac ordo et connexio rerum.", "en": "The order and connection of ideas is the same as the order and connection of things."}, {"id": "E2P7D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P7D", "la": "Patet ex axiom. 4. P. 1. Nam cuiuscumque causati idea a cognitione causae, cuius est effectus, dependet.", "en": "This is evident from Ax. 4, Part I. For the idea of each thing caused depends on the knowledge of the cause of which it is the effect."}, {"id": "E2P7C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P7C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, quod Dei cogitandi potentia aequalis est ipsius actuali agendi potentiae; hoc est, quicquid ex infinita Dei natura sequitur formaliter, id omne ex Dei idea eodem ordine eademque connexione sequitur in Deo obiective.", "en": "From this it follows that God’s power of thinking is equal to his actual power of acting; that is, whatever follows formally from God’s infinite nature, all of it follows objectively in God, in the same order and with the same connection, from the idea of God."}, {"id": "E2P7S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P7S", "la": "Hic antequam ulterius pergamus, revocandum nobis in memoriam est id quod supra ostendimus; nempe, quod quicquid ab infinito intellectu percipi potest tanquam substantiae essentiam constituens, id omne ad unicam tantum substantiam pertinet; et consequenter quod substantia cogitans et substantia extensa una eademque est substantia, quae iam sub hoc, iam sub illo attributo comprehenditur.", "en": "Before we proceed further, we must recall here what we have shown above: namely, that whatever can be perceived by an infinite intellect as constituting an essence of substance pertains to one substance only, and consequently that the thinking substance and the extended substance are one and the same substance, which is comprehended now under this attribute, now under that."}, {"id": "E2P7S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P7S", "la": "Sic etiam modus extensionis et idea illius modi una eademque est res, sed duobus modis expressa; quod quidam Hebraeorum quasi per nebulam vidisse videntur, qui scilicet statuunt, Deum, Dei intellectum resque ab ipso intellectas unum et idem esse. Ex. gr. circulus in natura existens et idea circuli existentis, quae etiam in Deo est, una eademque est res, quae per diversa attributa explicatur.", "en": "So also a mode of extension and the idea of that mode are one and the same thing, but expressed in two ways; which some of the Hebrews seem to have seen as if through a cloud, those, namely, who maintain that God, God’s intellect, and the things understood by him are one and the same. For example, a circle existing in nature and the idea of the existing circle, which is also in God, are one and the same thing, which is explained through different attributes."}, {"id": "E2P7S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P7S", "la": "Et ideo sive naturam sub attributo extensionis, sive sub attributo cogitationis, sive sub alio quocumque concipiamus, unum eundemque ordinem sive unam eandemque causarum connexionem, hoc est, easdem res invicem sequi reperiemus.", "en": "And therefore, whether we conceive nature under the attribute of extension, or under the attribute of thought, or under any other, we shall find one and the same order, or one and the same connection of causes, that is, that the same things follow one another."}, {"id": "E2P7S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P7S", "la": "Nec ulla alia de causa dixi, quod Deus sit causa ideae ex. gr. circuli quatenus tantum est res cogitans, et circuli quatenus tantum est res extensa, nisi quia esse formale ideae circuli non nisi per alium cogitandi modum, tanquam causam proximam, et ille iterum per alium, et sic in infinitum, potest percipi, ita ut, quamdiu res ut cogitandi modi considerantur, ordinem totius naturae sive causarum connexionem, per solum cogitationis attributum explicare debemus; et quatenus ut modi extensionis considerantur, ordo etiam totius naturae per solum extensionis attributum explicari debet, et idem de aliis attributis intelligo.", "en": "Nor did I say for any other reason that God is the cause of the idea of, for example, a circle insofar only as he is a thinking thing, and of the circle insofar only as he is an extended thing, than this: that the formal being of the idea of the circle can be perceived only through another mode of thinking, as its proximate cause, and that one again through another, and so on to infinity; so that as long as things are considered as modes of thinking, we must explain the order of the whole of nature, or the connection of causes, through the attribute of thought alone; and insofar as they are considered as modes of extension, the order of the whole of nature must be explained through the attribute of extension alone; and I understand the same of the other attributes."}, {"id": "E2P7S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P7S", "la": "Quare rerum, ut in se sunt, Deus revera est causa, quatenus infinitis constat attributis; nec impraesentiarum haec clarius possum explicare.", "en": "So God is really the cause of things as they are in themselves insofar as he consists of infinite attributes; and for the present I cannot explain these matters more clearly."}, {"id": "E2P8", "p": "part2", "l": "2P8", "la": "Ideae rerum singularium sive modorum non existentium ita debent comprehendi in Dei infinita idea, ac rerum singularium sive modorum essentiae formales in Dei attributis continentur.", "en": "The ideas of singular things, or of modes, that do not exist must be comprehended in God’s infinite idea in the same way as the formal essences of singular things, or modes, are contained in God’s attributes."}, {"id": "E2P8D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P8D", "la": "Haec propositio patet ex praeced., sed intelligitur clarius ex praeced. schol.", "en": "This proposition is evident from the preceding one, but it is understood more clearly from the preceding scholium."}, {"id": "E2P8C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P8C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, quod, quamdiu res singulares non existunt, nisi quatenus in Dei attributis comprehenduntur, earum esse obiectivum sive ideae non existunt, nisi quatenus infinita Dei idea existit; et ubi res singulares dicuntur existere, non tantum quatenus in Dei attributis comprehenduntur, sed quatenus etiam durare dicuntur, earum ideae etiam existentiam, per quam durare dicuntur, involvent.", "en": "From this it follows that, as long as singular things do not exist except insofar as they are comprehended in God’s attributes, their objective being, or ideas, do not exist except insofar as God’s infinite idea exists; and when singular things are said to exist, not only insofar as they are comprehended in God’s attributes, but insofar also as they are said to endure, their ideas also will involve existence, through which they are said to endure."}, {"id": "E2P8S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P8S", "la": "Si quis ad uberiorem huius rei explicationem exemplum desideret, nullum sane dare potero, quod rem, de qua hic loquor, utpote unicam adaequate explicet; conabor tamen rem, ut fieri potest, illustrare.", "en": "If anyone desires an example for a fuller explanation of this matter, I shall certainly not be able to give one that adequately explains the thing I am speaking of here, since it is unique. I shall nevertheless try to illustrate the matter as far as possible."}, {"id": "E2P8S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P8S", "la": "Nempe circulus talis est naturae, ut omnium linearum rectarum in eodem sese invicem secantium rectangula sub segmentis sint inter se aequalia. Quare in circulo infinita inter se aequalia rectangula continentur.", "en": "Namely, the circle is of such a nature that the rectangles formed from the segments of all the straight lines intersecting one another in it are equal to one another. So in a circle there are contained infinitely many rectangles equal to one another."}, {"id": "E2P8S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P8S", "la": "Attamen nullum eorum potest dici existere, nisi quatenus circulus existit, nec etiam alicuius horum rectangulorum idea potest dici existere, nisi quatenus in circuli idea comprehenditur. Concipiantur iam ex infinitis illis duo tantum, nempe E et D existere. Sane eorum etiam ideae iam non tantum existunt, quatenus solummodo in circuli idea comprehenduntur; sed etiam quatenus illorum rectangulorum existentiam involvunt, quo fit, ut a reliquis reliquorum rectangulorum ideis distinguantur.", "en": "Yet none of them can be said to exist except insofar as the circle exists, nor can the idea of any of these rectangles be said to exist except insofar as it is comprehended in the idea of the circle. Now conceive that of those infinitely many only two exist, namely E and D. Certainly their ideas also now exist not only insofar as they are merely comprehended in the idea of the circle, but also insofar as they involve the existence of those rectangles, which brings it about that they are distinguished from the other ideas of the other rectangles."}, {"id": "E2P9", "p": "part2", "l": "2P9", "la": "Idea rei singularis actu existentis Deum pro causa habet, non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus alia rei singularis actu existentis idea affectus consideratur, cuius etiam Deus est causa, quatenus alia tertia affectus est, et sic in infinitum.", "en": "The idea of a singular thing actually existing has God for its cause not insofar as he is infinite, but insofar as he is considered to be affected by another idea of a singular thing actually existing, of which God is also the cause insofar as he is affected by another, third idea, and so on to infinity."}, {"id": "E2P9D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P9D", "la": "Idea rei singularis actu existentis modus singularis cogitandi est et a reliquis distinctus (per coroll. et schol. prop. 8. huius); adeoque (per prop. 6. huius) Deum, quatenus est tantum res cogitans, pro causa habet. At non (per prop. 28. P. 1.) quatenus est res absolute cogitans, sed quatenus alio cogitandi modo affectus consideratur, et huius etiam Deus est causa, quatenus alio affectus est, et sic in infinitum.", "en": "The idea of a singular thing actually existing is a singular mode of thinking, distinct from the others (by Cor. and Schol. of Prop. 8 of this Part); and so (by Prop. 6 of this Part) it has God for its cause insofar as he is only a thinking thing. But not (by Prop. 28, Part I) insofar as he is a thing thinking absolutely, but insofar as he is considered to be affected by another mode of thinking; and of this too God is the cause insofar as he is affected by another, and so on to infinity."}, {"id": "E2P9D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P9D", "la": "Atqui ordo et connexio idearum (per prop. 7. huius) idem est, ac ordo et connexio causarum. Ergo unius singularis ideae alia idea sive Deus, quatenus alia idea affectus consideratur, est causa, et huius etiam, quatenus alia affectus est, et sic in infinitum. Q. E. D.", "en": "But the order and connection of ideas (by Prop. 7 of this Part) is the same as the order and connection of causes. Therefore the cause of one singular idea is another idea, or God insofar as he is considered to be affected by another idea, and of this too, insofar as he is affected by another, and so on to infinity. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P9C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P9C", "la": "Quicquid in singulari cuiuscumque ideae obiecto contingit, eius datur in Deo cognitio, quatenus tantum eiusdem obiecti ideam habet.", "en": "Whatever happens in the singular object of any idea, there is knowledge of it in God only insofar as he has the idea of that object."}, {"id": "E2P9CD", "p": "part2", "l": "2P9CD", "la": "Quicquid in obiecto cuiuscumque ideae contingit, eius datur in Deo idea (per prop. 3. huius), non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus alia rei singularis idea affectus consideratur (per prop. praeced.), sed (per prop. 7. huius) ordo et connexio idearum idem est, ac ordo et connexio rerum. Erit ergo cognitio eius, quod in singulari aliquo obiecto contingit, in Deo, quatenus tantum eiusdem obiecti habet ideam. Q. E. D.", "en": "Whatever happens in the object of any idea, there is an idea of it in God (by Prop. 3 of this Part), not insofar as he is infinite, but insofar as he is considered to be affected by another idea of a singular thing (by the preceding Prop.); but (by Prop. 7 of this Part) the order and connection of ideas is the same as the order and connection of things. Therefore knowledge of what happens in some singular object will be in God only insofar as he has the idea of that object. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P10", "p": "part2", "l": "2P10", "la": "Ad essentiam hominis non pertinet esse substantiae, sive substantia formam hominis non constituit.", "en": "The being of substance does not pertain to the essence of man, or substance does not constitute the form of man."}, {"id": "E2P10D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P10D", "la": "Esse enim substantiae involvit necessariam existentiam (per prop. 7. P. 1.). Si igitur ad hominis essentiam pertineret esse substantiae, data ergo substantia daretur necessario homo (per defin. 2. huius), et consequenter homo necessario existeret, quod (per axiom. 1. huius) est absurdum. Ergo etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "For the being of substance involves necessary existence (by Prop. 7, Part I). So if the being of substance pertained to the essence of man, then, substance being given, man would necessarily be given (by Def. 2 of this Part), and consequently man would exist necessarily, which (by Ax. 1 of this Part) is absurd. Therefore etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P10S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P10S", "la": "Demonstratur etiam haec propositio ex prop. 5. P. 1. nempe quod duae eiusdem naturae substantiae non dentur. Cum autem plures homines existere possint, ergo id, quod hominis formam constituit, non est esse substantiae. Patet praeterea haec propositio ex reliquis substantiae proprietatibus, videlicet, quod substantia sit sua natura infinita, immutabilis, indivisibilis etc., ut facile unusquisque videre potest.", "en": "This proposition is also demonstrated from Prop. 5, Part I, namely that there are not two substances of the same nature. Now since several men can exist, that which constitutes the form of man is not the being of substance. This proposition is evident, moreover, from the other properties of substance, namely that substance is by its nature infinite, immutable, indivisible, etc., as anyone can easily see."}, {"id": "E2P10C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P10C", "la": "Hinc sequitur essentiam hominis constitui a certis Dei attributorum modificationibus. Nam esse substantiae (per prop. praeced.) ad essentiam hominis non pertinet. Est ergo (per prop. 15. P. 1.) aliquid quod in Deo est, et quod sine Deo nec esse nec concipi potest, sive (per coroll. prop. 25. P. 1.) affectio sive modus, qui Dei naturam certo et determinato modo exprimit.", "en": "From this it follows that the essence of man is constituted by certain modifications of God’s attributes. For the being of substance (by the preceding Prop.) does not pertain to the essence of man. It is therefore (by Prop. 15, Part I) something which is in God, and which can neither be nor be conceived without God, or (by Cor. of Prop. 25, Part I) an affection or mode which expresses God’s nature in a certain and determinate way."}, {"id": "E2P10CS", "p": "part2", "l": "2P10CS", "la": "Omnes sane concedere debent, nihil sine Deo esse neque concipi posse. Nam apud omnes in confesso est, quod Deus omnium rerum, tam earum essentiae, quam earum existentiae, unica est causa, hoc est, Deus non tantum est causa rerum secundum fieri (ut aiunt), sed etiam secundum esse.", "en": "Everyone, surely, must grant that nothing can be or be conceived without God. For it is acknowledged by all that God is the only cause of all things, both of their essence and of their existence; that is, God is the cause of things not only according to their coming to be (as they say), but also according to their being."}, {"id": "E2P10CS", "p": "part2", "l": "2P10CS", "la": "At interim plerique id ad essentiam alicuius rei pertinere dicunt, sine quo res nec esse nec concipi potest; adeoque vel naturam Dei ad essentiam rerum creatarum pertinere, vel res creatas sine Deo vel esse vel concipi posse credunt, vel, quod certius est, sibi non satis constant. Cuius rei causam fuisse credo, quod ordinem philosophandi non tenuerint. Nam naturam divinam, quam ante omnia contemplari debebant, quia tam cognitione quam natura prior est, ordine cognitionis ultimam, et res, quae sensuum obiecta vocantur, omnibus priores esse crediderunt. Unde factum est, ut, dum res naturales contemplati sunt, de nulla re minus cogitaverint, quam de divina natura, et cum postea animum ad divinam naturam contemplandum appulerint, de nulla re minus cogitare potuerint, quam de primis suis figmentis, quibus rerum naturalium cognitionem superstruxerant, utpote quae ad cognitionem divinae naturae nihil iuvare poterant; adeoque nihil mirum, si sibi passim contradixerint.", "en": "But meanwhile most people say that there pertains to the essence of a thing that without which the thing can neither be nor be conceived; and so they believe either that the nature of God pertains to the essence of created things, or that created things can be or be conceived without God, or, what is more certain, they are not sufficiently consistent with themselves. The cause of this, I believe, was that they did not keep to the order of philosophizing. For the divine nature, which they ought to have contemplated before all things, since it is prior both in knowledge and in nature, they believed to be last in the order of knowledge, and the things that are called objects of the senses to be prior to all. Hence it came about that, while they contemplated natural things, there was nothing they thought about less than the divine nature, and when afterwards they applied their minds to contemplating the divine nature, there was nothing they could think about less than their first fictions, on which they had built the knowledge of natural things, since these could be of no help to the knowledge of the divine nature; and so it is no wonder if they have contradicted themselves everywhere."}, {"id": "E2P10CS", "p": "part2", "l": "2P10CS", "la": "Sed hoc mitto. Nam meum intentum hic tantum fuit, causam reddere, cur non dixerim, id ad essentiam alicuius rei pertinere, sine quo res nec esse nec concipi potest, nimirum, quia res singulares non possunt sine Deo esse nec concipi; et tamen Deus ad earum essentiam non pertinet; sed id necessario essentiam alicuius rei constituere dixi, quo dato res ponitur et quo sublato res tollitur, vel id, sine quo res, et vice versa id, quod sine re nec esse nec concipi potest.", "en": "But I let this go. For my intention here was only to give the reason why I did not say that there pertains to the essence of a thing that without which the thing can neither be nor be conceived: namely, because singular things cannot be or be conceived without God, and yet God does not pertain to their essence. Rather, I said that that necessarily constitutes the essence of a thing which, being given, the thing is posited, and which, being taken away, the thing is taken away; or that without which the thing, and in turn which without the thing, can neither be nor be conceived."}, {"id": "E2P11", "p": "part2", "l": "2P11", "la": "Primum, quod actuale mentis humanae esse constituit, nihil aliud est, quam idea rei alicuius singularis actu existentis.", "en": "The first thing that constitutes the actual being of a human mind is nothing but the idea of some singular thing actually existing."}, {"id": "E2P11D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P11D", "la": "Essentia hominis (per coroll. prop. praeced.) a certis Dei attributorum modis constituitur; nempe (per axiom. 2 huius) a modis cogitandi, quorum omnium (per axiom. 3. huius) idea natura prior est, et ea data reliqui modi (quibus scilicet idea natura prior est) in eodem debent esse individuo (per axiom. 3. huius). Atque adeo idea primum est, quod humanae mentis esse constituit.", "en": "The essence of man (by the Cor. of the preceding Prop.) is constituted by certain modes of God’s attributes, namely (by Ax. 2 of this Part) by modes of thinking, of all of which (by Ax. 3 of this Part) the idea is prior in nature, and, the idea being given, the other modes (to which, that is, the idea is prior in nature) must be in the same individual (by Ax. 3 of this Part). And so an idea is the first thing that constitutes the being of a human mind."}, {"id": "E2P11D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P11D", "la": "At non idea rei non existentis; nam tum (per coroll. prop. 8. huius) ipsa idea non posset dici existere. Erit ergo idea rei actu existentis. At non rei infinitae. Res namque infinita (per prop. 21. et 22. P. 1.) debet semper necessario existere. Atqui hoc (per axiom. 1. huius) est absurdum. Ergo primum, quod esse humanae mentis actuale constituit, est idea rei singularis actu existentis. Q. E. D.", "en": "But not the idea of a thing that does not exist; for then (by Cor. of Prop. 8 of this Part) the idea itself could not be said to exist. It will therefore be the idea of a thing actually existing. But not of an infinite thing. For an infinite thing (by Props. 21 and 22, Part I) must always exist necessarily. But this (by Ax. 1 of this Part) is absurd. Therefore the first thing that constitutes the actual being of a human mind is the idea of a singular thing actually existing. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P11C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P11C", "la": "Hinc sequitur mentem humanam partem esse infiniti intellectus Dei. Ac proinde cum dicimus, mentem humanam hoc vel illud percipere, nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod Deus, non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus per naturam humanae mentis explicatur, sive quatenus humanae mentis essentiam constituit, hanc vel illam habet ideam; et cum dicimus Deum hanc vel illam ideam habere, non tantum, quatenus naturam humanae mentis constituit, sed quatenus simul cum mente humana alterius rei etiam habet ideam, tum dicimus mentem humanam rem ex parte sive inadaequate percipere.", "en": "From this it follows that the human mind is part of the infinite intellect of God. And therefore, when we say that the human mind perceives this or that, we are saying nothing but that God, not insofar as he is infinite, but insofar as he is explained through the nature of the human mind, or insofar as he constitutes the essence of the human mind, has this or that idea; and when we say that God has this or that idea not only insofar as he constitutes the nature of the human mind, but insofar as he also has, together with the human mind, the idea of another thing, then we say that the human mind perceives the thing partially, or inadequately."}, {"id": "E2P11S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P11S", "la": "Hic sine dubio lectores haerebunt, multaque comminiscentur, quae moram iniiciant; et hac de causa ipsos rogo, ut lento gradu mecum pergant, nec de his iudicium ferant, donec omnia perlegerint.", "en": "Here, no doubt, readers will come to a halt and think of many things that give them pause; and for this reason I ask them to proceed slowly with me, and not to pass judgment on these matters until they have read through everything."}, {"id": "E2P12", "p": "part2", "l": "2P12", "la": "Quicquid in obiecto ideae humanam mentem constituentis contingit, id ab humana mente debet percipi, sive eius rei dabitur in mente necessario idea: hoc est, si obiectum ideae humanam mentem constituentis sit corpus, nihil in eo corpore poterit contingere, quod a mente non percipiatur.", "en": "Whatever happens in the object of the idea constituting the human mind must be perceived by the human mind, or there will necessarily be an idea of that thing in the mind; that is, if the object of the idea constituting the human mind is a body, nothing can happen in that body that is not perceived by the mind."}, {"id": "E2P12D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P12D", "la": "Quicquid enim in obiecto cuiuscumque ideae contingit, eius rei datur necessario in Deo cognitio (per coroll. prop. 9. huius), quatenus eiusdem obiecti idea affectus consideratur, hoc est (per prop. 11. huius), quatenus mentem alicuius rei constituit. Quicquid igitur in obiecto ideae humanam mentem constituentis contingit, eius datur necessario in Deo cognitio, quatenus naturam humanae mentis constituit, hoc est (per coroll. prop. 11. huius) eius rei cognitio erit necessario in mente, sive mens id percipit. Q. E. D.", "en": "For whatever happens in the object of any idea, there is necessarily knowledge of it in God (by Cor. of Prop. 9 of this Part) insofar as he is considered to be affected by the idea of that object, that is (by Prop. 11 of this Part), insofar as he constitutes the mind of some thing. Whatever happens, therefore, in the object of the idea constituting the human mind, there is necessarily knowledge of it in God insofar as he constitutes the nature of the human mind, that is (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part), knowledge of the thing will necessarily be in the mind, or the mind perceives it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P12S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P12S", "la": "Haec propositio patet etiam et clarius intelligitur ex schol. prop. 7. huius, quod vide.", "en": "This proposition is also evident, and is understood more clearly, from Schol. of Prop. 7 of this Part, which see."}, {"id": "E2P13", "p": "part2", "l": "2P13", "la": "Obiectum ideae humanam mentem constituentis est corpus, sive certus extensionis modus actu existens, et nihil aliud.", "en": "The object of the idea constituting the human mind is a body, or a certain mode of extension actually existing, and nothing else."}, {"id": "E2P13D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P13D", "la": "Si enim corpus non esset humanae mentis obiectum, ideae affectionum corporis non essent in Deo (per coroll. prop. 9. huius), quatenus mentem nostram, sed quatenus alterius rei mentem constitueret, hoc est (per coroll. prop. 11 huius), ideae affectionum corporis non essent in nostra mente. Atqui (per axiom. 4. huius) ideas affectionum corporis habemus. Ergo obiectum ideae humanam mentem constituentis est corpus, idque (per prop. 11. huius) actu existens.", "en": "For if a body were not the object of the human mind, the ideas of the affections of the body would not be in God (by Cor. of Prop. 9 of this Part) insofar as he constitutes our mind, but insofar as he constitutes the mind of another thing; that is (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part), the ideas of the affections of the body would not be in our mind. But (by Ax. 4 of this Part) we have ideas of the affections of the body. Therefore the object of the idea constituting the human mind is a body, and it (by Prop. 11 of this Part) actually existing."}, {"id": "E2P13D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P13D", "la": "Deinde, si praeter corpus etiam aliud esset mentis obiectum, cum nihil (per prop. 36. P. 1.) existat, ex quo aliquis effectus non sequatur, deberet (per prop. 12 huius) necessario alicuius eius effectus idea in mente nostra dari. Atqui (per axiom. 5. huius) nulla eius idea datur. Ergo obiectum nostrae mentis est corpus existens, et nihil aliud. Q. E. D.", "en": "Next, if besides a body there were also something else that was the object of the mind, then, since nothing exists (by Prop. 36, Part I) from which some effect does not follow, there would necessarily (by Prop. 12 of this Part) have to be in our mind an idea of some effect of it. But (by Ax. 5 of this Part) there is no idea of it. Therefore the object of our mind is an existing body, and nothing else. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P13C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P13C", "la": "Hinc sequitur *hominem mente et corpore constare, et corpus humanum, prout ipsum sentimus, existere.*", "en": "From this it follows that man consists of a mind and a body, and that the human body exists as we feel it."}, {"id": "E2P13S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P13S", "la": "Ex his non tantum intelligimus, mentem humanam unitam esse corpori, sed etiam quid per mentis et corporis unionem intelligendum sit. Verum ipsam adaequate sive distincte intelligere nemo poterit, nisi prius nostri corporis naturam adaequate cognoscat.", "en": "From these things we understand not only that the human mind is united to the body, but also what is to be understood by the union of mind and body. But no one will be able to understand it adequately, or distinctly, unless he first knows adequately the nature of our body."}, {"id": "E2P13S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P13S", "la": "Nam ea, quae hucusque ostendimus, admodum communia sunt, nec magis ad homines, quam ad reliqua individua pertinent, quae omnia, quamvis diversis gradibus, animata tamen sunt. Nam cuiuscumque rei datur necessario in Deo idea, cuius Deus est causa, eodem modo, ac humani corporis ideae: atque adeo, quicquid de idea humani corporis diximus, id de cuiuscumque rei idea necessario dicendum est.", "en": "For the things we have shown so far are very general and pertain no more to men than to other individuals, all of which, though in different degrees, are nevertheless animate. For of each thing there is necessarily an idea in God, of which God is the cause in the same way as he is of the idea of the human body; and so whatever we have said of the idea of the human body must necessarily be said of the idea of any thing."}, {"id": "E2P13S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P13S", "la": "Attamen nec etiam negare possumus, ideas inter se ut ipsa obiecta differre, unamque alia praestantiorem esse, plusque realitatis continere, prout obiectum unius obiecto alterius praestantius est, plusque realitatis continet; ac propterea ad determinandum, quid mens humana reliquis intersit, quidque reliquis praestet, necesse nobis est, eius obiecti, ut diximus, hoc est, corporis humani naturam cognoscere. Eam autem hic explicare nec possum, nec id ad ea quae demonstrare volo necesse est.", "en": "But still, we cannot deny either that ideas differ among themselves as the objects themselves do, and that one is more excellent than another and contains more reality, just as the object of the one is more excellent than the object of the other and contains more reality; and therefore, to determine how the human mind differs from the others and how it surpasses them, it is necessary for us, as we have said, to know the nature of its object, that is, of the human body. I can neither explain this here, nor is it necessary for the things I wish to demonstrate."}, {"id": "E2P13S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P13S", "la": "Hoc tamen in genere dico, quo corpus aliquod reliquis aptius est ad plura simul agendum vel patiendum, eo eius mens reliquis aptior est ad plura simul percipiendum; et quo unius corporis actiones magis ab ipso solo pendent, et quo minus alia corpora cum eodem in agendo concurrunt, eo eius mens aptior est ad distincte intelligendum. Atque ex his praestantiam unius mentis prae aliis cognoscere possumus; deinde causam etiam videre, cur nostri corporis non nisi admodum confusam habeamus cognitionem, et alia plura, quae in sequentibus ex his deducam. Qua de causa operae pretium esse duxi, haec ipsa accuratius explicare et demonstrare, ad quod necesse est, pauca de *natura corporum* praemittere.", "en": "I say this, however, in general: that the more a body is more capable than others of doing many things at once, or of being acted on in many ways at once, the more its mind is more capable than others of perceiving many things at once; and the more the actions of one body depend on itself alone, and the less other bodies concur with it in acting, the more its mind is capable of understanding distinctly. And from these things we can know the excellence of one mind over others, and also see the cause why we have only a very confused knowledge of our body, and many other things which I shall deduce from these in what follows. For this reason I have thought it worth while to explain and demonstrate these very things more accurately, for which it is necessary to premise a few things concerning the nature of bodies."}, {"id": "E2A1_prime", "p": "part2", "l": "2A1'", "la": "Omnia corpora vel moventur, vel quiescunt.", "en": "All bodies are either in motion or at rest."}, {"id": "E2A2_prime", "p": "part2", "l": "2A2'", "la": "Unumquodque corpus iam tardius, iam celerius movetur.", "en": "Each body moves now more slowly, now more quickly."}, {"id": "E2L1", "p": "part2", "l": "2L1", "la": "Corpora ratione motus et quietis, celeritatis et tarditatis, et non ratione substantiae ab invicem distinguuntur.", "en": "Bodies are distinguished from one another by reason of motion and rest, speed and slowness, and not by reason of substance."}, {"id": "E2L1D", "p": "part2", "l": "2L1D", "la": "Primam partem huius per se notam suppono. At, quod ratione substantiae non distinguantur corpora, patet tam ex prop. 5. quam 8. P. 1.; sed clarius ex iis, quae in schol. prop. 15. P. 1. dicta sunt.", "en": "The first part of this I suppose to be self-evident. But that bodies are not distinguished by reason of substance is evident both from Prop. 5 and from Prop. 8 of Part I; but more clearly from what was said in Schol. of Prop. 15, Part I."}, {"id": "E2L2", "p": "part2", "l": "2L2", "la": "Omnia corpora in quibusdam conveniunt.", "en": "All bodies agree in certain things."}, {"id": "E2L2D", "p": "part2", "l": "2L2D", "la": "In his enim omnia corpora conveniunt, quod unius eiusdemque attributi conceptum involvunt (per defin. 1. huius); deinde, quod iam tardius, iam celerius, et absolute iam moveri, iam quiescere possunt.", "en": "For all bodies agree in this, that they involve the concept of one and the same attribute (by Def. 1 of this Part); and next, that they can move now more slowly, now more quickly, and absolutely, now move, now be at rest."}, {"id": "E2L3", "p": "part2", "l": "2L3", "la": "Corpus motum vel quiescens ad motum vel quietem determinari debuit ab alio corpore, quod etiam ad motum vel quietem determinatum fuit ab alio, et illud iterum ab alio, et sic in infinitum.", "en": "A body in motion or at rest must have been determined to motion or rest by another body, which was also determined to motion or rest by another, and that again by another, and so on to infinity."}, {"id": "E2L3D", "p": "part2", "l": "2L3D", "la": "Corpora (per defin. 1. huius) res singulares sunt, quae (per lem. 1.) ratione motus et quietis ab invicem distinguuntur; adeoque (per prop. 28. P. 1.) unumquodque ad motum vel quietem necessario determinari debuit ab alia re singulari, nempe (per prop. 6. huius) ab alio corpore, quod (per axiom. 1.) etiam vel movetur, vel quiescit. At hoc etiam (per eandem rationem) moveri, vel quiescere non potuit, nisi ab alio ad motum vel quietem determinatum fuisset, et hoc iterum (per eandem rationem) ab alio, et sic in infinitum. Q. E. D.", "en": "Bodies (by Def. 1 of this Part) are singular things, which (by Lemma 1) are distinguished from one another by reason of motion and rest; and so (by Prop. 28, Part I) each must necessarily have been determined to motion or rest by another singular thing, namely (by Prop. 6 of this Part) by another body, which (by Ax. 1) is also either in motion or at rest. But this too (by the same reasoning) could not have been in motion or at rest unless it had been determined to motion or rest by another, and this again (by the same reasoning) by another, and so on to infinity. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2L3C", "p": "part2", "l": "2L3C", "la": "Hinc sequitur corpus motum tamdiu moveri, donec ab alio corpore ad quiescendum determinetur; et corpus quiescens tamdiu etiam quiescere, donec ab alio ad motum determinetur. Quod etiam per se notum est.", "en": "From this it follows that a body in motion moves until it is determined to rest by another body, and that a body at rest also remains at rest until it is determined to motion by another. This is also self-evident."}, {"id": "E2L3C", "p": "part2", "l": "2L3C", "la": "Nam cum suppono, corpus ex. gr. A quiescere, nec ad alia corpora mota attendo, nihil de corpore A dicere potero, nisi quod quiescat. Quod si postea contingat, ut corpus A moveatur, id sane evenire non potuit ex eo, quod quiescebat; ex eo enim nil aliud sequi poterat, quam ut corpus A quiesceret. Si contra supponatur A moveri, quotiescumque ad A tantum attendimus, nihil de eodem affirmare poterimus, nisi quod moveatur. Quod si postea contingat, ut A quiescat, id sane evenire etiam non potuit ex motu, quem habebat; ex motu enim nihil aliud sequi poterat, quam ut A moveretur. Contingit itaque a re, quae non erat in A, nempe a causa externa, a qua ad quiescendum determinatum fuit.", "en": "For when I suppose that a body, for example A, is at rest, and do not attend to other bodies in motion, I shall be able to say nothing about body A except that it is at rest. If afterwards it happens that body A moves, that certainly could not come about from the fact that it was at rest; for from that nothing else could follow than that body A should be at rest. If, on the contrary, A is supposed to move, then as often as we attend only to A, we shall be able to affirm nothing of it except that it moves. If afterwards it happens that A is at rest, that certainly could not come about from the motion it had either; for from the motion nothing else could follow than that A should move. It happens therefore from a thing which was not in A, namely from an external cause, by which it was determined to rest."}, {"id": "E2A1_prime_prime", "p": "part2", "l": "2A1''", "la": "Omnes modi, quibus corpus aliquod ab alio afficitur corpore, ex natura corporis affecti et simul ex natura corporis afficientis sequuntur; ita ut unum idemque corpus diversimode moveatur pro diversitate naturae corporum moventium, et contra ut diversa corpora ab uno eodemque corpore diversimode moveantur.", "en": "All the ways in which a body is affected by another body follow from the nature of the body affected and at the same time from the nature of the affecting body; so that one and the same body is moved in different ways according to the difference in the nature of the bodies moving it, and, conversely, different bodies are moved in different ways by one and the same body."}, {"id": "E2A2_prime_prime", "p": "part2", "l": "2A2''", "la": "Cum corpus motum alteri quiescenti, quod dimovere nequit, impingit, reflectitur, ut moveri pergat, et angulus lineae motus reflectionis cum plano corporis quiescentis, cui impegit, aequalis erit angulo, quem linea motus incidentiae cum eodem plano efficit.", "en": "When a body in motion strikes another at rest which it cannot move, it is reflected so as to continue moving, and the angle of the line of the motion of reflection with the plane of the body at rest which it struck will be equal to the angle which the line of the motion of incidence makes with the same plane."}, {"id": "E2A2_prime_prime.2", "p": "part2", "l": "2A2''", "la": "Atque haec de corporibus simplicissimis, quae scilicet solo motu et quiete, celeritate et tarditate ab invicem distinguuntur, iam ad composita ascendamus.", "en": "So much, then, for the simplest bodies, which are distinguished from one another only by motion and rest, speed and slowness; let us now ascend to composite bodies."}, {"id": "E2D_prime_prime", "p": "part2", "l": "2D''", "la": "Cum corpora aliquot eiusdem aut diversae magnitudinis a reliquis ita coercentur, ut invicem incumbant, vel si eodem aut diversis celeritatis gradibus moventur, ut motus suos invicem certa quadam ratione communicent, illa *corpora invicem unita* dicemus, et omnia simul *unum corpus, sive individuum* componere, quod a reliquis per hanc corporum unionem distinguitur.", "en": "When a number of bodies, of the same or of different size, are so constrained by other bodies that they lie upon one another, or, if they move with the same or with different degrees of speed, so that they communicate their motions to one another in a certain fixed ratio, we shall say that those bodies are united with one another, and that all together compose one body, or individual, which is distinguished from the others by this union of bodies."}, {"id": "E2A3_prime_prime", "p": "part2", "l": "2A3''", "la": "Quo partes individui vel corporis compositi secundum maiores vel minores superficies sibi invicem incumbunt, eo difficilius vel facilius cogi possunt, ut situm suum mutent, et consequenter eo difficilius vel facilius effici potest, ut ipsum individuum aliam figuram induat. Atque hinc corpora, quorum partes secundum magnas superficies invicem incumbunt, *dura*, quorum autem partes secundum parvas, *mollia*, et quorum denique partes inter se moventur, *fluida* vocabo.", "en": "The greater or smaller the surfaces by which the parts of an individual, or composite body, lie upon one another, the more difficult or the easier it is to compel them to change their position, and consequently the more difficult or the easier it is to bring it about that the individual itself takes on another shape. And from this I shall call bodies whose parts lie upon one another over large surfaces hard, those whose parts lie upon one another over small surfaces soft, and those whose parts move among themselves fluid."}, {"id": "E2L4", "p": "part2", "l": "2L4", "la": "Si corporis sive individui, quod ex pluribus corporibus componitur, quaedam corpora segregentur, et simul totidem alia eiusdem naturae eorum loco succedant, retinebit individuum suam naturam, uti antea, absque ulla eius formae mutatione.", "en": "If from a body, or individual, which is composed of several bodies, certain bodies are separated, and at the same time as many other bodies of the same nature take their place, the individual will retain its nature as before, without any change of its form."}, {"id": "E2L4D", "p": "part2", "l": "2L4D", "la": "Corpora enim (per lem. 1.) ratione substantiae non distinguuntur; id autem, quod formam individui constituit, in corporum unione (per defin. praeced.) consistit. Atqui haec (per hypothesin), tametsi corporum continua fiat mutatio, retinetur. Retinebit ergo individuum tam ratione substantiae, quam modi, suam naturam, uti ante. Q. E. D.", "en": "For bodies (by Lemma 1) are not distinguished by reason of substance; and that which constitutes the form of an individual consists in a union of bodies (by the preceding Def.). But this (by hypothesis), although a continual change of bodies takes place, is retained. The individual will therefore retain its nature, both in respect of substance and in respect of mode, as before. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2L5", "p": "part2", "l": "2L5", "la": "Si partes individuum componentes, maiores minoresve evadant, ea tamen proportione, ut omnes eandem, ut antea, ad invicem motus et quietis rationem servent, retinebit itidem individuum suam naturam, ut antea, absque ulla formae mutatione.", "en": "If the parts composing an individual become greater or smaller, but in such proportion that all keep the same ratio of motion and rest to one another as before, the individual will likewise retain its nature, as before, without any change of form."}, {"id": "E2L5D", "p": "part2", "l": "2L5D", "la": "Huius eadem est, ac praeced. lem.", "en": "The demonstration of this is the same as that of the preceding lemma."}, {"id": "E2L6", "p": "part2", "l": "2L6", "la": "Si corpora quaedam individuum componentia motum, quem versus unam partem habent, aliam versus flectere cogantur, at ita, ut motus suos continuare possint, atque invicem eadem, qua antea, ratione communicare; retinebit itidem individuum suam naturam absque ulla formae mutatione.", "en": "If certain bodies composing an individual are compelled to turn aside the motion they have towards one part towards another, but in such a way that they can continue their motions and communicate them to one another in the same ratio as before, the individual will likewise retain its nature without any change of form."}, {"id": "E2L6D", "p": "part2", "l": "2L6D", "la": "Per se patet. Id enim omne retinere supponitur, quod in eiusdem definitione formam ipsius constituere diximus.", "en": "This is self-evident. For it is supposed to retain all that which, in its definition, we said constitutes its form."}, {"id": "E2L7", "p": "part2", "l": "2L7", "la": "Retinet praeterea individuum sic compositum suam naturam, sive id secundum totum moveatur, sive quiescat, sive versus hanc, sive versus illam partem moveatur, dummodo unaquaeque pars motum suum retineat, eumque, uti antea, reliquis communicet.", "en": "Further, an individual so composed retains its nature whether it moves as a whole or is at rest, or moves towards this part or towards that, provided that each part retains its motion and communicates it to the others as before."}, {"id": "E2L7D", "p": "part2", "l": "2L7D", "la": "Patet ex ipsius definitione, quam vide ante lem. 4.", "en": "This is evident from its definition, which see before Lemma 4."}, {"id": "E2L7S", "p": "part2", "l": "2L7S", "la": "His itaque videmus, qua ratione individuum compositum possit multis modis affici, eius nihilominus natura servata. Atque hucusque individuum concepimus, quod non, nisi ex corporibus, quae solo motu et quiete, celeritate et tarditate inter se distinguuntur, hoc est, quod ex corporibus simplicissimis componitur.", "en": "By this, therefore, we see how a composite individual can be affected in many ways and yet keep its nature. And up to now we have conceived an individual composed only of bodies that are distinguished from one another only by motion and rest, speed and slowness, that is, composed of the simplest bodies."}, {"id": "E2L7S", "p": "part2", "l": "2L7S", "la": "Quod si iam aliud concipiamus ex pluribus diversae naturae individuis compositum, idem pluribus aliis modis posse affici reperiemus, ipsius nihilominus natura servata. Nam quandoquidem eius unaquaeque pars ex pluribus corporibus est composita, poterit ergo (per lem. praeced.) unaquaeque pars, absque ulla ipsius naturae mutatione iam tardius, iam celerius moveri, et consequenter motus suos citius vel tardius reliquis communicare.", "en": "But if we now conceive another, composed of several individuals of different natures, we shall find that it can be affected in many other ways, and yet keep its nature. For since each part of it is composed of several bodies, each part will therefore be able (by the preceding lemma), without any change of its nature, to move now more slowly, now more quickly, and consequently to communicate its motions to the others now more quickly, now more slowly."}, {"id": "E2L7S", "p": "part2", "l": "2L7S", "la": "Quod si praeterea tertium individuorum genus ex his secundis compositum, concipiamus, idem multis aliis modis affici posse reperiemus absque ulla eius formae mutatione. Et si sic porro in infinitum pergamus, facile concipiemus, totam naturam unum esse individuum, cuius partes, hoc est, omnia corpora infinitis modis variant absque ulla totius individui mutatione.", "en": "And if we conceive further a third kind of individuals, composed of these second ones, we shall find that it can be affected in many other ways without any change of its form. And if we proceed in this way to infinity, we shall easily conceive that the whole of nature is one individual, whose parts, that is, all bodies, vary in infinitely many ways, without any change of the whole individual."}, {"id": "E2L7S", "p": "part2", "l": "2L7S", "la": "Atque haec si animus fuisset, de corpore ex professo agere, prolixius explicare et demonstrare debuissem. Sed iam dixi me aliud velle, nec alia de causa haec adferre, quam quia ex ipsis ea, quae demonstrare constitui, facile possum deducere.", "en": "And if it had been my intention to treat of body expressly, I should have had to explain and demonstrate these things more fully. But I have already said that I intend something else, and that I bring these things forward for no other reason than that I can easily deduce from them the things I have decided to demonstrate."}, {"id": "E2Post1", "p": "part2", "l": "2Post1", "la": "*Corpus humanum* componitur ex plurimis (diversae naturae) individuis, quorum unumquodque valde compositum est.", "en": "The human body is composed of very many individuals (of different natures), each of which is highly composite."}, {"id": "E2Post2", "p": "part2", "l": "2Post2", "la": "Individuorum, ex quibus corpus humanum componitur, quaedam *fluida, quaedam mollia* et quaedam denique *dura* sunt.", "en": "Of the individuals of which the human body is composed, some are fluid, some soft, and some, finally, hard."}, {"id": "E2Post3", "p": "part2", "l": "2Post3", "la": "Individua corpus humanum componentia, et consequenter ipsum humanum corpus a corporibus externis plurimis modis afficitur.", "en": "The individuals composing the human body, and consequently the human body itself, are affected by external bodies in very many ways."}, {"id": "E2Post4", "p": "part2", "l": "2Post4", "la": "Corpus humanum indiget, ut conservetur, plurimis aliis corporibus, a quibus continuo quasi regeneratur.", "en": "The human body needs, in order to be preserved, very many other bodies, by which it is continually, as it were, regenerated."}, {"id": "E2Post5", "p": "part2", "l": "2Post5", "la": "Cum corporis humani pars fluida a corpore externo determinatur, ut in aliam mollem saepe impingat, eius planum mutat et veluti quaedam corporis externi impellentis vestigia eidem imprimit.", "en": "When a fluid part of the human body is determined by an external body so that it frequently strikes another, soft part, it changes the surface of that part and impresses on it, as it were, certain traces of the external body that drives it."}, {"id": "E2Post6", "p": "part2", "l": "2Post6", "la": "Corpus humanum potest corpora externa plurimis modis movere plurimisque modis disponere.", "en": "The human body can move external bodies in very many ways, and arrange them in very many ways."}, {"id": "E2P14", "p": "part2", "l": "2P14", "la": "Mens humana apta est ad plurima percipiendum, et eo aptior, quo eius corpus pluribus modis disponi potest.", "en": "The human mind is capable of perceiving very many things, and is the more capable, the more its body can be disposed in more ways."}, {"id": "E2P14D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P14D", "la": "Corpus enim humanum (per postul. 3. et 6.) plurimis modis a corporibus externis afficitur, disponiturque ad corpora externa plurimis modis afficiendum. At omnia, quae in corpore humano contingunt (per prop. 12. huius), mens humana percipere debet. Est ergo mens humana apta ad plurima percipiendum, et eo aptior. Q. E. D.", "en": "For the human body (by Posts. 3 and 6) is affected in very many ways by external bodies, and is disposed to affect external bodies in very many ways. But all things that happen in the human body (by Prop. 12 of this Part) the human mind must perceive. The human mind is therefore capable of perceiving very many things, and is the more capable. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P15", "p": "part2", "l": "2P15", "la": "Idea, quae esse formale humanae mentis constituit, non est simplex, sed ex plurimis ideis composita.", "en": "The idea that constitutes the formal being of the human mind is not simple, but composed of very many ideas."}, {"id": "E2P15D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P15D", "la": "Idea, quae esse formale humanae mentis constituit, est idea corporis (per prop. 13. huius), quod (per postul. 1.) ex plurimis valde compositis individuis componitur. At cuiuscumque individui corpus componentis datur necessario (per coroll. prop. 8. huius) in Deo idea. Ergo (per prop. 7. huius) idea corporis humani ex plurimis hisce partium componentium ideis est composita. Q. E. D.", "en": "The idea that constitutes the formal being of the human mind is the idea of a body (by Prop. 13 of this Part), which (by Post. 1) is composed of very many highly composite individuals. But of each individual composing the body there is necessarily (by Cor. of Prop. 8 of this Part) an idea in God. Therefore (by Prop. 7 of this Part) the idea of the human body is composed of these very many ideas of the component parts. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P16", "p": "part2", "l": "2P16", "la": "Idea cuiuscumque modi, quo corpus humanum a corporibus externis afficitur, involvere debet naturam corporis humani et simul naturam corporis externi.", "en": "The idea of any mode in which the human body is affected by external bodies must involve the nature of the human body and at the same time the nature of the external body."}, {"id": "E2P16D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P16D", "la": "Omnes enim modi, quibus corpus aliquod afficitur, ex natura corporis affecti, et simul ex natura corporis afficientis sequuntur (per axiom. 1. post coroll. lem. 3.). Quare eorum idea (per axiom. 4. P. 1.) utriusque corporis naturam necessario involvet. Adeoque idea cuiuscumque modi, quo corpus humanum a corpore externo afficitur, corporis humani, et corporis externi naturam involvit. Q. E. D.", "en": "For all the modes in which a body is affected follow from the nature of the body affected and at the same time from the nature of the affecting body (by Ax. 1 after the Cor. of Lemma 3). So the idea of them (by Ax. 4, Part I) will necessarily involve the nature of each body. And so the idea of any mode in which the human body is affected by an external body involves the nature of the human body and of the external body. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P16C1", "p": "part2", "l": "2P16C1", "la": "Hinc sequitur primo, mentem humanam plurimorum corporum naturam una cum sui corporis natura percipere.", "en": "From this it follows, first, that the human mind perceives the nature of very many bodies together with the nature of its own body."}, {"id": "E2P16C2", "p": "part2", "l": "2P16C2", "la": "Sequitur secundo, quod ideae, quas corporum externorum habemus, magis nostri corporis constitutionem, quam corporum externorum naturam indicant; quod in appendice partis primae multis exemplis explicui.", "en": "It follows, second, that the ideas we have of external bodies indicate the constitution of our own body more than the nature of the external bodies; which I have explained by many examples in the Appendix of Part I."}, {"id": "E2P17", "p": "part2", "l": "2P17", "la": "Si humanum corpus affectum est modo, qui naturam corporis alicuius externi involvit, mens humana idem corpus externum ut actu existens, vel ut sibi praesens contemplabitur, donec corpus afficiatur affectu, qui eiusdem corporis existentiam vel praesentiam secludat.", "en": "If the human body is affected in a mode that involves the nature of some external body, the human mind will contemplate that external body as actually existing, or as present to it, until the body is affected by an affect that excludes the existence or presence of that body."}, {"id": "E2P17D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P17D", "la": "Patet. Nam quamdiu corpus humanum sic affectum est, tamdiu mens humana (per prop. 12. huius) hanc corporis affectionem contemplabitur, hoc est (per prop. praeced.), ideam habebit modi actu existentis, quae naturam corporis externi involvit, hoc est, ideam, quae existentiam, vel praesentiam naturae corporis externi non secludit, sed ponit. Adeoque mens (per coroll. 1. praeced.) corpus externum ut actu existens, vel ut praesens contemplabitur, donec afficiatur etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "This is evident. For as long as the human body is so affected, the human mind (by Prop. 12 of this Part) will contemplate this affection of the body, that is (by the preceding Prop.), it will have the idea of a mode actually existing, an idea which involves the nature of the external body, that is, an idea which does not exclude but posits the existence or presence of the nature of the external body. And so the mind (by Cor. 1 of the preceding Prop.) will contemplate the external body as actually existing, or as present, until it is affected, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P17C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P17C", "la": "Mens corpora externa, a quibus corpus humanum semel affectum fuit, quamvis non existant nec praesentia sint, contemplari tamen poterit, velut praesentia essent.", "en": "The mind will be able to contemplate external bodies by which the human body has once been affected, even though they do not exist and are not present, as if they were present."}, {"id": "E2P17CD", "p": "part2", "l": "2P17CD", "la": "Dum corpora externa corporis humani partes fluidas ita determinant, ut in molliores saepe impingant, earum plana (per postul. 5.) mutant. Unde fit (vide axiom. 2. post coroll. lem. 3.) ut inde alio modo reflectantur, quam antea solebant, et ut etiam postea iisdem novis planis spontaneo suo motu occurrendo, eodem modo reflectantur, ac cum a corporibus externis versus illa plana impulsae sunt, et consequenter, ut corpus humanum, dum sic reflexae moveri pergunt, eodem modo afficiant, de quo mens (per prop. 12. huius) iterum cogitabit, hoc est (per prop. 17. huius) mens iterum corpus externum ut praesens contemplabitur; et hoc toties, quoties corporis humani partes fluidae spontaneo suo motu iisdem planis occurrent.", "en": "While external bodies so determine the fluid parts of the human body that these often strike the softer parts, they change the surfaces of those parts (by Post. 5). From this it comes about (see Ax. 2 after the Cor. of Lemma 3) that the fluid parts are reflected from them in another way than they used to be before, and that also afterwards, meeting the same new surfaces by their own spontaneous motion, they are reflected in the same way as when they were driven towards those surfaces by the external bodies; and consequently that, while they continue to move, so reflected, they affect the human body in the same way, which the mind (by Prop. 12 of this Part) will think of again; that is (by Prop. 17 of this Part), the mind will again contemplate the external body as present; and this as often as the fluid parts of the human body meet the same surfaces by their own spontaneous motion."}, {"id": "E2P17CD", "p": "part2", "l": "2P17CD", "la": "Quare quamvis corpora externa, a quibus corpus humanum affectum semel fuit, non existant, mens tamen eadem toties ut praesentia contemplabitur, quoties haec corporis actio repetetur. Q. E. D.", "en": "So although the external bodies by which the human body has once been affected do not exist, the mind will nevertheless contemplate them as present as often as this action of the body is repeated. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P17S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P17S", "la": "Videmus itaque, qui fieri potest, ut ea, quae non sunt veluti praesentia contemplemur, ut saepe fit. Et fieri potest, ut hoc aliis de causis contingat. Sed mihi hic sufficit ostendisse unam, per quam rem sic possim explicare, ac si ipsam per veram causam ostendissem; nec tamen credo, me a vera longe aberrare, quandoquidem omnia illa quae sumpsi postulata vix quicquam continent, quod non constet experientia, de qua nobis non licet dubitare, postquam ostendimus corpus humanum, prout ipsum sentimus, existere (vide coroll. post prop. 13. huius).", "en": "We see, then, how it can come about that we contemplate things that are not as if they were present, as often happens. And it can come about that this happens from other causes. But it is enough for me here to have shown one by which I can explain the thing as if I had shown it through its true cause; and yet I do not believe that I am far from the true one, since all the postulates I have assumed contain hardly anything that is not established by experience, which we may not doubt after we have shown that the human body exists as we feel it (see the Cor. after Prop. 13 of this Part)."}, {"id": "E2P17S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P17S", "la": "Praeterea (ex coroll. praeced. et coroll. 2. prop. 16. huius) clare intelligimus, quaenam sit differentia inter ideam ex. gr. Petri, quae essentiam mentis ipsius Petri constituit, et inter ideam ipsius Petri, quae in alio homine, puta in Paulo, est. Illa enim essentiam corporis ipsius Petri directe explicat, nec existentiam involvit nisi quamdiu Petrus existit; haec autem magis constitutionem corporis Pauli, quam Petri naturam indicat, et ideo durante illa corporis Pauli constitutione mens Pauli, quamvis Petrus non existat, ipsum tamen ut sibi praesentem contemplabitur.", "en": "Further (from the preceding Cor. and Cor. 2 of Prop. 16 of this Part) we understand clearly what the difference is between the idea of, for example, Peter, which constitutes the essence of Peter’s own mind, and the idea of Peter himself which is in another man, say in Paul. For the former directly explains the essence of Peter’s own body, and does not involve existence except as long as Peter exists; but the latter indicates the constitution of Paul’s body more than the nature of Peter, and so, while that constitution of Paul’s body lasts, Paul’s mind will contemplate Peter as present to it, even though Peter does not exist."}, {"id": "E2P17S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P17S", "la": "Porro, ut verba usitata retineamus, corporis humani affectiones, quarum ideae corpora externa velut nobis praesentia repraesentant, *rerum imagines* vocabimus, tametsi rerum figuras non referunt. Et cum mens hac ratione contemplatur corpora, eandem *imaginari* dicemus.", "en": "Further, to retain the customary words, we shall call the affections of the human body whose ideas represent external bodies as present to us images of things, even though they do not reproduce the figures of things. And when the mind contemplates bodies in this way, we shall say that it imagines."}, {"id": "E2P17S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P17S", "la": "Atque hic, ut, quid sit *error*, indicare incipiam, notetis velim, *mentis imaginationes* in se spectatas nihil erroris continere, sive mentem ex eo, quod imaginatur, non errare; sed tantum quatenus consideratur, carere idea, quae existentiam illarum rerum, quas sibi praesentes imaginatur, secludat. Nam si mens, dum res non existentes ut sibi praesentes imaginatur, simul sciret, res illas revera non existere, hanc sane imaginandi potentiam virtuti suae naturae, non vitio tribueret; praesertim si haec imaginandi facultas a sola sua natura penderet, hoc est (per defin. 7. P. 1.) si haec mentis imaginandi facultas libera esset.", "en": "And here, to begin to indicate what error is, I would have you note that the imaginations of the mind, considered in themselves, contain no error, or that the mind does not err from the fact that it imagines, but only insofar as it is considered to lack an idea that excludes the existence of those things which it imagines as present to it. For if the mind, while it imagined non-existent things as present to it, at the same time knew that those things did not really exist, it would surely attribute this power of imagining to a virtue of its nature, not to a vice; especially if this faculty of imagining depended on its nature alone, that is (by Def. 7, Part I), if this faculty of the mind of imagining were free."}, {"id": "E2P18", "p": "part2", "l": "2P18", "la": "Si corpus humanum a duobus, vel pluribus corporibus simul affectum fuerit semel, ubi mens postea eorum aliquod imaginabitur, statim et aliorum recordabitur.", "en": "If the human body has once been affected by two or more bodies at the same time, then when the mind afterwards imagines one of them, it will immediately recollect the others also."}, {"id": "E2P18D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P18D", "la": "Mens (per coroll. praeced.) corpus aliquod ea de causa imaginatur, quia scilicet humanum corpus a corporis externi vestigiis eodem modo afficitur disponiturque, ac affectum est, cum quaedam eius partes ab ipso corpore externo fuerunt impulsae. Sed (per hypothesin) corpus tum ita fuit dispositum, ut mens duo simul corpora imaginaretur. Ergo iam etiam duo simul imaginabitur, atque mens ubi alterutrum imaginabitur, statim et alterius recordabitur. Q. E. D.", "en": "The mind (by the preceding Cor.) imagines some body for this reason, that the human body is affected and disposed by the traces of an external body in the same way as it was affected when certain of its parts were driven by the external body itself. But (by hypothesis) the body was then so disposed that the mind imagined two bodies at once. Therefore it will now also imagine two at once, and when the mind imagines either of them, it will immediately recollect the other too. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P18S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P18S", "la": "Hinc clare intelligimus, quid sit *memoria*. Est enim nihil aliud, quam quaedam concatenatio idearum, naturam rerum, quae extra corpus humanum sunt, involventium, quae in mente fit secundum ordinem et concatenationem affectionum corporis humani.", "en": "From this we understand clearly what memory is. For it is nothing other than a certain concatenation of ideas involving the nature of things which are outside the human body, a concatenation which takes place in the mind according to the order and concatenation of the affections of the human body."}, {"id": "E2P18S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P18S", "la": "Dico primo concatenationem esse illarum tantum idearum, quae naturam rerum, quae extra corpus humanum sunt, involvunt; non autem idearum, quae earundem rerum naturam explicant. Sunt enim revera (per prop. 16. huius) ideae affectionum corporis humani, quae tam huius, quam corporum externorum naturam involvunt. Dico secundo hanc concatenationem fieri secundum ordinem et concatenationem affectionum corporis humani, ut ipsam distinguerem a concatenatione idearum, quae fit secundum ordinem intellectus, quo res per primas suas causas mens percipit, et qui in omnibus hominibus idem est.", "en": "I say, first, that it is a concatenation only of those ideas which involve the nature of things which are outside the human body, not of ideas which explain the nature of those same things. For they are in fact (by Prop. 16 of this Part) ideas of affections of the human body, which involve the nature both of this body and of the external bodies. I say, second, that this concatenation takes place according to the order and concatenation of the affections of the human body, in order to distinguish it from the concatenation of ideas which takes place according to the order of the intellect, by which the mind perceives things through their first causes, and which is the same in all men."}, {"id": "E2P18S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P18S", "la": "Atque hinc porro clare intelligimus, cur mens ex cogitatione unius rei statim in alterius rei cogitationem incidat, quae nullam cum priore habet similitudinem; ut, ex. gr. ex cogitatione vocis pomi homo Romanus statim in cogitationem fructus incidet, qui nullam cum articulato illo sono habet similitudinem, nec aliquid commune, nisi quod eiusdem hominis corpus ab his duobus affectum saepe fuit, hoc est, quod ipse homo saepe vocem pomum audivit, dum ipsum fructum videret; et sic unusquisque ex una in aliam cogitationem incidet, prout rerum imagines uniuscuiusque consuetudo in corpore ordinavit.", "en": "And from this, further, we understand clearly why the mind passes immediately from the thought of one thing into the thought of another which has no likeness to the first; as, for example, a Roman will pass immediately from the thought of the word pomum into the thought of the fruit, which has no likeness to that articulate sound, nor anything in common with it, except that the body of the same man has often been affected by these two, that is, that the man himself often heard the word pomum while he saw the fruit; and so each one will pass from one thought into another according as habit has ordered the images of things in his body."}, {"id": "E2P18S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P18S", "la": "Nam miles ex. gr. visis in arena equi vestigiis statim ex cogitatione equi in cogitationem equitis, et inde in cogitationem belli, etc. incidet. At rusticus ex cogitatione equi in cogitationem aratri, agri, etc. incidet; et sic unusquisque, prout rerum imagines consuevit hoc vel alio modo iungere et concatenare, ex una in hanc vel in aliam incidet cogitationem.", "en": "For a soldier, for example, having seen the traces of a horse in the sand, will pass immediately from the thought of the horse into the thought of a horseman, and from there into the thought of war, etc. But a farmer will pass from the thought of the horse into the thought of a plough, of a field, etc.; and so each one, according as he has been accustomed to join and concatenate the images of things in this or that way, will pass from one thought into this or that other."}, {"id": "E2P19", "p": "part2", "l": "2P19", "la": "Mens humana ipsum humanum corpus non cognoscit, nec ipsum existere scit, nisi per ideas affectionum, quibus corpus afficitur.", "en": "The human mind does not know the human body itself, nor does it know that it exists, except through the ideas of affections by which the body is affected."}, {"id": "E2P19D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P19D", "la": "Mens enim humana est ipsa idea sive cognitio corporis humani (per prop. 13. huius), quae (per prop. 9. huius) in Deo quidem est, quatenus alia rei singularis idea affectus consideratur; vel quia (per postul. 4.) corpus humanum plurimis corporibus indiget, a quibus continuo quasi regeneratur, et ordo et connexio idearum idem est (per prop. 7. huius) ac ordo et connexio causarum, erit haec idea in Deo, quatenus plurimarum rerum singularium ideis affectus consideratur. Deus itaque ideam corporis humani habet, sive corpus humanum cognoscit, quatenus plurimis aliis ideis affectus est, et non quatenus naturam humanae mentis constituit, hoc est (per coroll. prop. 11. huius) mens humana corpus humanum non cognoscit.", "en": "For the human mind is the very idea, or knowledge, of the human body (by Prop. 13 of this Part), which (by Prop. 9 of this Part) is indeed in God insofar as he is considered to be affected by another idea of a singular thing; or, since (by Post. 4) the human body needs very many bodies by which it is continually, as it were, regenerated, and the order and connection of ideas is the same (by Prop. 7 of this Part) as the order and connection of causes, this idea will be in God insofar as he is considered to be affected by the ideas of very many singular things. God, therefore, has the idea of the human body, or knows the human body, insofar as he is affected by very many other ideas, and not insofar as he constitutes the nature of the human mind; that is (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part), the human mind does not know the human body."}, {"id": "E2P19D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P19D", "la": "At ideae affectionum corporis in Deo sunt, quatenus humanae mentis naturam constituit, sive mens humana easdem affectiones percipit (per prop. 12. huius), et consequenter (per prop. 16. huius) ipsum corpus humanum, idque (per prop. 17. huius) ut actu existens. Percipit ergo eatenus tantum mens humana ipsum humanum corpus. Q. E. D.", "en": "But the ideas of the affections of the body are in God insofar as he constitutes the nature of the human mind, or the human mind perceives those affections (by Prop. 12 of this Part), and consequently (by Prop. 16 of this Part) the human body itself, and (by Prop. 17 of this Part) as actually existing. Only to that extent, therefore, does the human mind perceive the human body itself. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P20", "p": "part2", "l": "2P20", "la": "Mentis humanae datur etiam in Deo idea, sive cognitio, quae in Deo eodem modo sequitur et ad Deum eodem modo refertur, ac idea sive cognitio corporis humani.", "en": "There is also in God an idea, or knowledge, of the human mind, which follows in God in the same way, and is related to God in the same way, as the idea, or knowledge, of the human body."}, {"id": "E2P20D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P20D", "la": "Cogitatio attributum Dei est (per prop. 1. huius); adeoque (per prop. 3. huius) tam eius, quam omnium eius affectionum, et consequenter (per prop. 11. huius) mentis etiam humanae debet necessario in Deo dari idea. Deinde haec mentis idea sive cognitio non sequitur in Deo dari, quatenus infinitus; sed quatenus alia rei singularis idea affectus est (per prop. 9. huius). Sed ordo et connexio idearum idem est, ac ordo et connexio causarum (per prop. 7. huius). Sequitur ergo haec mentis idea, sive cognitio in Deo, et ad Deum eodem modo refertur, ac idea sive cognitio corporis. Q. E. D.", "en": "Thought is an attribute of God (by Prop. 1 of this Part); and so (by Prop. 3 of this Part) there must necessarily be in God an idea both of thought and of all its affections, and consequently (by Prop. 11 of this Part) of the human mind too. Next, this idea, or knowledge, of the mind does not follow to be in God insofar as he is infinite, but insofar as he is affected by another idea of a singular thing (by Prop. 9 of this Part). But the order and connection of ideas is the same as the order and connection of causes (by Prop. 7 of this Part). This idea, or knowledge, of the mind therefore follows in God, and is related to God, in the same way as the idea, or knowledge, of the body. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P21", "p": "part2", "l": "2P21", "la": "Haec mentis idea eodem modo unita est menti, ac ipsa mens unita est corpori.", "en": "This idea of the mind is united to the mind in the same way as the mind itself is united to the body."}, {"id": "E2P21D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P21D", "la": "Mentem unitam esse corpori ex eo ostendimus, quod scilicet corpus mentis sit obiectum (vide prop. 12. et 13. huius); adeoque per eandem illam rationem idea mentis cum suo obiecto, hoc est, cum ipsa mente eodem modo unita esse debet, ac ipsa mens unita est corpori. Q. E. D.", "en": "We have shown that the mind is united to the body from the fact that the body is the object of the mind (see Props. 12 and 13 of this Part); and so, by that same reasoning, the idea of the mind must be united with its object, that is, with the mind itself, in the same way as the mind itself is united to the body. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P21S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P21S", "la": "Haec prop. longe clarius intelligitur ex dictis in schol. prop. 7. huius. Ibi enim ostendimus corporis ideam, et corpus, hoc est (per prop. 13. huius) mentem et corpus unum et idem esse individuum, quod iam sub cogitationis, iam sub extensionis attributo concipitur. Quare mentis idea et ipsa mens una eademque est res, quae sub uno eodemque attributo, nempe cogitationis, concipitur. Mentis, inquam, idea et ipsa mens in Deo eadem necessitate ex eadem cogitandi potentia sequuntur dari.", "en": "This proposition is understood far more clearly from what was said in Schol. of Prop. 7 of this Part. For there we showed that the idea of the body and the body, that is (by Prop. 13 of this Part), the mind and the body, are one and the same individual, which is conceived now under the attribute of thought, now under the attribute of extension. So the idea of the mind and the mind itself are one and the same thing, which is conceived under one and the same attribute, namely thought. The idea of the mind, I say, and the mind itself follow to be in God from the same power of thinking by the same necessity."}, {"id": "E2P21S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P21S", "la": "Nam revera idea mentis, hoc est, idea ideae nihil aliud est, quam forma ideae, quatenus haec ut modus cogitandi absque relatione ad obiectum consideratur. Simulac enim quis aliquid scit, eo ipso scit se id scire, et simul scit se scire quod scit, et sic in infinitum. Sed de his postea.", "en": "For in fact the idea of the mind, that is, the idea of the idea, is nothing other than the form of the idea insofar as this is considered as a mode of thinking without relation to its object. For as soon as someone knows something, by that very fact he knows that he knows it, and at the same time he knows that he knows that he knows, and so on to infinity. But of these matters later."}, {"id": "E2P22", "p": "part2", "l": "2P22", "la": "Mens humana non tantum corporis affectiones, sed etiam harum affectionum ideas percipit.", "en": "The human mind perceives not only the affections of the body, but also the ideas of these affections."}, {"id": "E2P22D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P22D", "la": "Affectionum idearum ideae in Deo eodem modo sequuntur et ad Deum eodem modo referuntur, ac ipsae affectionum ideae; quod eodem modo demonstratur, ac prop. 20. huius. At ideae affectionum corporis in mente humana sunt (per prop. 12. huius), hoc est (per coroll. prop. 11. huius) in Deo, quatenus humanae mentis essentiam constituit. Ergo harum idearum ideae in Deo erunt, quatenus humanae mentis cognitionem, sive ideam habet, hoc est (per prop. 21. huius) in ipsa mente humana, quae propterea non tantum corporis affectiones, sed earum etiam ideas percipit. Q. E. D.", "en": "The ideas of the ideas of the affections follow in God in the same way, and are related to God in the same way, as the ideas of the affections themselves; which is demonstrated in the same way as Prop. 20 of this Part. But the ideas of the affections of the body are in the human mind (by Prop. 12 of this Part), that is (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part), in God insofar as he constitutes the essence of the human mind. Therefore the ideas of these ideas will be in God insofar as he has the knowledge, or idea, of the human mind, that is (by Prop. 21 of this Part), in the human mind itself, which therefore perceives not only the affections of the body, but also their ideas. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P23", "p": "part2", "l": "2P23", "la": "Mens se ipsam non cognoscit, nisi quatenus corporis affectionum ideas percipit.", "en": "The mind does not know itself except insofar as it perceives the ideas of the affections of the body."}, {"id": "E2P23D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P23D", "la": "Mentis idea sive cognitio (per prop. 20. huius) in Deo eodem modo sequitur et ad Deum eodem modo refertur, ac corporis idea sive cognitio. At quoniam (per prop. 19. huius) mens humana ipsum humanum corpus non cognoscit, hoc est (per coroll. prop. 11. huius) quoniam cognitio corporis humani ad Deum non refertur, quatenus humanae mentis naturam constituit; ergo nec cognitio mentis ad Deum refertur, quatenus essentiam mentis humanae constituit; atque adeo (per idem coroll. prop. 11. huius.) mens humana eatenus se ipsam non cognoscit.", "en": "The idea, or knowledge, of the mind (by Prop. 20 of this Part) follows in God in the same way, and is related to God in the same way, as the idea, or knowledge, of the body. But since (by Prop. 19 of this Part) the human mind does not know the human body itself, that is (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part), since the knowledge of the human body is not related to God insofar as he constitutes the nature of the human mind, neither is the knowledge of the mind related to God insofar as he constitutes the essence of the human mind; and so (by the same Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part) the human mind to that extent does not know itself."}, {"id": "E2P23D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P23D", "la": "Deinde affectionum, quibus corpus afficitur, ideae naturam ipsius corporis humani involvunt (per prop. 16. huius), hoc est (per prop. 13. huius) cum natura mentis conveniunt. Quare harum idearum cognitio cognitionem mentis necessario involvet. At (per prop. praeced.) harum idearum cognitio in ipsa humana mente est. Ergo mens humana eatenus tantum se ipsam novit. Q. E. D.", "en": "Next, the ideas of the affections by which the body is affected involve the nature of the human body itself (by Prop. 16 of this Part), that is (by Prop. 13 of this Part), they agree with the nature of the mind. So the knowledge of these ideas will necessarily involve the knowledge of the mind. But (by the preceding Prop.) the knowledge of these ideas is in the human mind itself. Therefore the human mind knows itself only to that extent. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P24", "p": "part2", "l": "2P24", "la": "Mens humana partium corpus humanum componentium adaequatam cognitionem non involvit.", "en": "The human mind does not involve adequate knowledge of the parts composing the human body."}, {"id": "E2P24D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P24D", "la": "Partes corpus humanum componentes ad essentiam ipsius corporis non pertinent, nisi quatenus motus suos certa quadam ratione invicem communicant (vide defin. post coroll. lem. 3.), et non quatenus ut individua absque relatione ad humanum corpus considerari possunt. Sunt enim partes humani corporis (per postul. 1.) valde composita individua, quorum partes (per lem. 4.) a corpore humano servata omnino eiusdem natura et forma segregari possunt, motusque suos (vide axiom. 1. post lem. 3.) aliis corporibus alia ratione communicare.", "en": "The parts composing the human body do not pertain to the essence of the body itself except insofar as they communicate their motions to one another in a certain fixed ratio (see the Def. after the Cor. of Lemma 3), and not insofar as they can be considered as individuals without relation to the human body. For the parts of the human body (by Post. 1) are highly composite individuals, whose parts (by Lemma 4) can be separated from the human body while it entirely keeps its nature and form, and can communicate their motions (see Ax. 1 after Lemma 3) to other bodies in another ratio."}, {"id": "E2P24D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P24D", "la": "Adeoque (per prop. 3. huius) cuiuscumque partis idea sive cognitio in Deo erit, et quidem (per prop. 9. huius) quatenus affectus consideratur alia idea rei singularis, quae res singularis ipsa parte ordine naturae prior est (per prop. 7. huius). Quod idem praeterea etiam de quacumque parte ipsius individui corpus humanum componentis est dicendum.", "en": "And so (by Prop. 3 of this Part) the idea, or knowledge, of each part will be in God, and indeed (by Prop. 9 of this Part) insofar as he is considered to be affected by another idea of a singular thing, which singular thing is prior in the order of nature to the part itself (by Prop. 7 of this Part). The same, moreover, is to be said of any part of the individual itself composing the human body."}, {"id": "E2P24D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P24D", "la": "Adeoque cuiuscumque partis corpus humanum componentis cognitio in Deo est, quatenus plurimis rerum ideis affectus est, et non quatenus corporis humani tantum habet ideam, hoc est (per prop. 13. huius) ideam, quae humanae mentis naturam constituit. Atque adeo (per coroll. prop. 11. huius) humana mens partium corpus humanum componentium adaequatam cognitionem non involvit. Q. E. D.", "en": "And so the knowledge of each part composing the human body is in God insofar as he is affected by very many ideas of things, and not insofar as he has only the idea of the human body, that is (by Prop. 13 of this Part), the idea which constitutes the nature of the human mind. And therefore (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part) the human mind does not involve adequate knowledge of the parts composing the human body. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P25", "p": "part2", "l": "2P25", "la": "Idea cuiuscumque affectionis corporis humani adaequatam corporis externi cognitionem non involvit.", "en": "The idea of any affection of the human body does not involve adequate knowledge of the external body."}, {"id": "E2P25D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P25D", "la": "Ideam affectionis corporis humani eatenus corporis externi naturam involvere ostendimus (vide prop.16. huius), quatenus externum ipsum humanum corpus certo quodam modo determinat. At quatenus externum corpus individuum est, quod ad corpus humanum non refertur, eius idea sive cognitio in Deo est (per prop. 9. huius), quatenus Deus affectus consideratur alterius rei idea, quae (per prop. 7. huius) ipso corpore externo prior est natura. Quare corporis externi adaequata cognitio in Deo non est, quatenus ideam affectionis humani corporis habet, sive idea affectionis corporis humani adaequatam corporis externi cognitionem non involvit. Q. E. D.", "en": "We have shown that the idea of an affection of the human body involves the nature of an external body insofar as the external body determines the human body itself in a certain way (see Prop. 16 of this Part). But insofar as the external body is an individual that is not related to the human body, its idea, or knowledge, is in God (by Prop. 9 of this Part) insofar as God is considered to be affected by the idea of another thing, which (by Prop. 7 of this Part) is prior in nature to the external body itself. So adequate knowledge of the external body is not in God insofar as he has the idea of the affection of the human body; or, the idea of an affection of the human body does not involve adequate knowledge of the external body. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P26", "p": "part2", "l": "2P26", "la": "Mens humana nullum corpus externum ut actu existens percipit, nisi per ideas affectionum sui corporis.", "en": "The human mind perceives no external body as actually existing except through the ideas of the affections of its own body."}, {"id": "E2P26D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P26D", "la": "Si a corpore aliquo externo corpus humanum nullo modo affectum est, ergo (per prop. 7. huius) nec idea corporis humani, hoc est (per prop. 13. huius) nec mens humana idea existentiae illius corporis ullo etiam modo affecta est, sive existentiam illius corporis externi ullo modo percipit. At quatenus corpus humanum a corpore aliquo externo aliquo modo afficitur, eatenus (per prop. 16. huius cum coroll. 1. eiusdem) corpus externum percipit. Q. E. D.", "en": "If the human body is in no way affected by some external body, then (by Prop. 7 of this Part) neither is the idea of the human body, that is (by Prop. 13 of this Part), neither is the human mind, affected in any way by the idea of the existence of that body; or, it does not perceive the existence of that external body in any way. But insofar as the human body is affected in some way by some external body, to that extent (by Prop. 16 of this Part with its Cor. 1) the mind perceives the external body. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P26C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P26C", "la": "Quatenus mens humana corpus externum imaginatur, eatenus adaequatam eius cognitionem non habet.", "en": "Insofar as the human mind imagines an external body, it does not have adequate knowledge of it."}, {"id": "E2P26CD", "p": "part2", "l": "2P26CD", "la": "Cum mens humana per ideas affectionum sui corporis corpora externa contemplatur, eandem tum imaginari dicimus (vide schol. prop. 17. huius); nec mens alia ratione (per prop. praeced.) corpora externa ut actu existentia imaginari potest. Atque adeo (per prop. 25. huius) quatenus mens corpora externa imaginatur, eorum adaequatam cognitionem non habet. Q. E. D.", "en": "When the human mind contemplates external bodies through the ideas of the affections of its own body, we then say that it imagines (see Schol. of Prop. 17 of this Part); nor can the mind (by the preceding Prop.) imagine external bodies as actually existing in any other way. And so (by Prop. 25 of this Part), insofar as the mind imagines external bodies, it does not have adequate knowledge of them. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P27", "p": "part2", "l": "2P27", "la": "Idea cuiuscumque affectionis corporis humani adaequatam ipsius humani corporis cognitionem non involvit.", "en": "The idea of any affection of the human body does not involve adequate knowledge of the human body itself."}, {"id": "E2P27D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P27D", "la": "Quaelibet idea cuiuscumque affectionis humani corporis eatenus naturam corporis humani involvit, quatenus ipsum humanum corpus certo quodam modo affici consideratur (vide prop. 16. huius). At quatenus corpus humanum individuum est, quod multis aliis modis affici potest, eius idea etc. Vide demonstr. prop. 25. huius.", "en": "Any idea of any affection of the human body involves the nature of the human body to the extent that the human body itself is considered to be affected in a certain way (see Prop. 16 of this Part). But insofar as the human body is an individual that can be affected in many other ways, its idea etc. See the demonstration of Prop. 25 of this Part."}, {"id": "E2P28", "p": "part2", "l": "2P28", "la": "Ideae affectionum corporis humani, quatenus ad humanam mentem tantum referuntur, non sunt clarae et distinctae, sed confusae.", "en": "The ideas of the affections of the human body, insofar as they are related only to the human mind, are not clear and distinct, but confused."}, {"id": "E2P28D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P28D", "la": "Ideae enim affectionum corporis humani, tam corporum externorum, quam ipsius humani corporis naturam involvunt (per prop. 16. huius); nec tantum corporis humani, sed eius etiam partium naturam involvere debent. Affectiones namque modi sunt (per postul. 3.), quibus partes corporis humani, et consequenter totum corpus afficitur.", "en": "For the ideas of the affections of the human body involve the nature both of external bodies and of the human body itself (by Prop. 16 of this Part); and they must involve the nature not only of the human body but also of its parts. For the affections are modes (by Post. 3) in which the parts of the human body, and consequently the whole body, are affected."}, {"id": "E2P28D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P28D", "la": "At (per prop. 24. et 25. huius) corporum externorum adaequata cognitio, ut et partium corpus humanum componentium in Deo non est, quatenus humana mente, sed quatenus aliis ideis affectus consideratur. Sunt ergo hae affectionum ideae, quatenus ad solam humanam mentem referuntur, veluti consequentiae absque praemissis, hoc est (ut per se notum) ideae confusae. Q. E. D.", "en": "But (by Props. 24 and 25 of this Part) adequate knowledge of external bodies, as also of the parts composing the human body, is not in God insofar as he is considered to be affected by the human mind, but insofar as he is considered to be affected by other ideas. These ideas of affections, therefore, insofar as they are related only to the human mind, are like consequences without premises, that is (as is self-evident), confused ideas. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P28S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P28S", "la": "Idea, quae naturam mentis humanae constituit, demonstratur eodem modo non esse in se sola considerata clara et distincta; ut etiam idea mentis humanae, et ideae idearum affectionum corporis humani, quatenus ad solam mentem referuntur, quod unusquisque facile videre potest.", "en": "That the idea which constitutes the nature of the human mind, considered in itself alone, is not clear and distinct is demonstrated in the same way; as also that the idea of the human mind, and the ideas of the ideas of the affections of the human body, insofar as they are related only to the mind, are not clear and distinct; which anyone can easily see."}, {"id": "E2P29", "p": "part2", "l": "2P29", "la": "Idea ideae cuiuscumque affectionis corporis humani adaequatam humanae mentis cognitionem non involvit.", "en": "The idea of the idea of any affection of the human body does not involve adequate knowledge of the human mind."}, {"id": "E2P29D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P29D", "la": "Idea enim affectionis corporis humani (per prop. 27. huius) adaequatam ipsius corporis cognitionem non involvit, sive eius naturam adaequate non exprimit, hoc est (per prop. 13. huius) cum natura mentis non convenit adaequate. Adeoque (per axiom. 6. P. 1.) huius ideae idea adaequate humanae mentis naturam non exprimit, sive adaequatam eius cognitionem non involvit. Q. E. D.", "en": "For the idea of an affection of the human body (by Prop. 27 of this Part) does not involve adequate knowledge of the body itself, or does not express its nature adequately, that is (by Prop. 13 of this Part), does not agree adequately with the nature of the mind. And so (by Ax. 6, Part I) the idea of this idea does not express the nature of the human mind adequately, or does not involve adequate knowledge of it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P29C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P29C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, mentem humanam, quoties ex communi naturae ordine res percipit, nec sui ipsius, nec sui corporis, nec corporum externorum adaequatam, sed confusam tantum et mutilatam habere cognitionem.", "en": "From this it follows that the human mind, whenever it perceives things from the common order of nature, has neither of itself, nor of its own body, nor of external bodies an adequate knowledge, but only a confused and mutilated one."}, {"id": "E2P29C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P29C", "la": "Nam mens se ipsam non cognoscit, nisi quatenus ideas affectionum corporis percipit (per prop. 23. huius). Corpus autem suum (per prop. 19. huius) non percipit, nisi per ipsas affectionum ideas, per quas etiam tantum (per prop. 26. huius) corpora externa percipit. Atque adeo, quatenus eas habet, nec sui ipsius (per prop. 29. huius), nec sui corporis (per prop. 27. huius), nec corporum externorum (per prop. 25. huius) habet adaequatam cognitionem, sed tantum (per prop. 28. huius cum eius schol.) mutilatam, et confusam. Q. E. D.", "en": "For the mind does not know itself except insofar as it perceives the ideas of the affections of the body (by Prop. 23 of this Part). And it does not perceive its own body (by Prop. 19 of this Part) except through these very ideas of affections, through which alone also (by Prop. 26 of this Part) it perceives external bodies. And so, insofar as it has these ideas, it has an adequate knowledge neither of itself (by Prop. 29 of this Part), nor of its own body (by Prop. 27 of this Part), nor of external bodies (by Prop. 25 of this Part), but only (by Prop. 28 of this Part with its Schol.) a mutilated and confused one. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P29S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P29S", "la": "Dico expresse, quod mens nec sui ipsius, nec sui corporis, nec corporum externorum adaequatam, sed confusam tantum, cognitionem habeat, quoties ex communi naturae ordine res percipit, hoc est, quoties externe, ex rerum nempe fortuito occursu, determinatur ad hoc vel illud contemplandum, et non quoties interne, ex eo scilicet, quod res plures simul contemplatur, determinatur ad earundem convenientias, differentias et oppugnantias intelligendum. Quoties enim hoc vel alio modo interne disponitur, tum res clare et distincte contemplatur, ut infra ostendam.", "en": "I say expressly that the mind has an adequate knowledge neither of itself, nor of its own body, nor of external bodies, but only a confused one, whenever it perceives things from the common order of nature, that is, whenever it is determined externally, namely from the fortuitous encounter with things, to contemplate this or that, and not whenever it is determined internally, namely from the fact that it contemplates several things at once, to understanding their agreements, differences and oppositions. For whenever it is disposed internally, in this or another way, then it contemplates things clearly and distinctly, as I shall show below."}, {"id": "E2P30", "p": "part2", "l": "2P30", "la": "Nos de duratione nostri corporis nullam nisi admodum inadaequatam cognitionem habere possumus.", "en": "We can have only a very inadequate knowledge of the duration of our body."}, {"id": "E2P30D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P30D", "la": "Nostri corporis duratio ab eius essentia non dependet (per axiom. 1. huius), nec etiam ab absoluta Dei natura (per prop. 21. P. 1.). Sed (per prop. 28. P. 1.) ad existendum et operandum determinatur a talibus causis, quae etiam ab aliis determinatae sunt ad existendum et operandum certa ac determinata ratione, et hae iterum ab aliis, et sic in infinitum. Nostri igitur corporis duratio a communi naturae ordine et rerum constitutione pendet.", "en": "The duration of our body does not depend on its essence (by Ax. 1 of this Part), nor on the absolute nature of God either (by Prop. 21, Part I). But (by Prop. 28, Part I) it is determined to exist and to operate by such causes as are also determined by others to exist and to operate in a certain and determinate manner, and these again by others, and so on to infinity. The duration of our body therefore depends on the common order of nature and on the constitution of things."}, {"id": "E2P30D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P30D", "la": "Qua autem ratione constitutae sint, eius rei adaequata cognitio datur in Deo, quatenus earum omnium ideas, et non quatenus tantum humani corporis ideam habet (per coroll. prop. 9. huius). Quare cognitio durationis nostri corporis est in Deo admodum inadaequata, quatenus tantum naturam mentis humanae constituere consideratur, hoc est (per coroll. prop. 11. huius) haec cognitio est in nostra mente admodum inadaequata. Q. E. D.", "en": "But adequate knowledge of the manner in which things are constituted is in God insofar as he has the ideas of them all, and not insofar as he has only the idea of the human body (by Cor. of Prop. 9 of this Part). So the knowledge of the duration of our body is very inadequate in God insofar as he is considered only to constitute the nature of the human mind, that is (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part), this knowledge is very inadequate in our mind. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P31", "p": "part2", "l": "2P31", "la": "Nos de duratione rerum singularium, quae extra nos sunt, nullam nisi admodum inadaequatam cognitionem habere possumus.", "en": "We can have only a very inadequate knowledge of the duration of the singular things that are outside us."}, {"id": "E2P31D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P31D", "la": "Unaquaeque enim res singularis, sicuti humanum corpus, ab alia re singulari determinari debet ad existendum et operandum certa ac determinata ratione; et haec iterum ab alia, et sic in infinitum (per prop. 28. P. 1.). Cum autem ex hac communi rerum singularium proprietate in praeced. prop. demonstraverimus, nos de duratione nostri corporis non nisi admodum inadaequatam cognitionem habere; ergo hoc idem de rerum singularium duratione erit concludendum, quod scilicet eius non nisi admodum inadaequatam cognitionem habere possumus. Q. E. D.", "en": "For each singular thing, like the human body, must be determined by another singular thing to exist and to operate in a certain and determinate manner, and this again by another, and so on to infinity (by Prop. 28, Part I). But since from this common property of singular things we have demonstrated in the preceding Prop. that we have only a very inadequate knowledge of the duration of our body, the same will have to be concluded about the duration of singular things, namely that we can have only a very inadequate knowledge of it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P31C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P31C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, omnes res particulares contingentes et corruptibiles esse. Nam de earum duratione nullam adaequatam cognitionem habere possumus (per prop. praeced.), et hoc est id, quod per rerum contingentiam et corruptionis possibilitatem nobis est intelligendum. Vide schol. 1. prop. 33. P. 1. Nam (per prop. 29. P. 1.) praeter hoc nullum datur contingens.", "en": "From this it follows that all particular things are contingent and corruptible. For we can have no adequate knowledge of their duration (by the preceding Prop.), and that is what we must understand by the contingency of things and the possibility of their corruption. See Schol. 1 of Prop. 33, Part I. For (by Prop. 29, Part I) apart from this there is nothing contingent."}, {"id": "E2P32", "p": "part2", "l": "2P32", "la": "Omnes ideae, quatenus ad Deum referuntur, verae sunt.", "en": "All ideas, insofar as they are related to God, are true."}, {"id": "E2P32D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P32D", "la": "Omnes enim ideae, quae in Deo sunt, cum suis ideatis omnino conveniunt (per coroll. prop. 7. huius); adeoque (per axiom. 6. P. 1.) omnes verae sunt. Q. E. D.", "en": "For all ideas that are in God agree entirely with their objects (by Cor. of Prop. 7 of this Part); and so (by Ax. 6, Part I) they are all true. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P33", "p": "part2", "l": "2P33", "la": "Nihil in ideis positivum est, propter quod falsae dicuntur.", "en": "There is nothing positive in ideas on account of which they are called false."}, {"id": "E2P33D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P33D", "la": "Si negas, concipe, si fieri potest, modum positivum cogitandi, qui formam erroris, sive falsitatis constituat. Hic cogitandi modus non potest esse in Deo (per prop. praeced.); extra Deum autem etiam nec esse, nec concipi potest (per prop. 15. P. 1.). Atque adeo nihil potest dari positivum in ideis, propter quod falsae dicuntur. Q. E. D.", "en": "If you deny this, conceive, if it can be done, a positive mode of thinking which constitutes the form of error, or of falsity. This mode of thinking cannot be in God (by the preceding Prop.); and outside God it can neither be nor be conceived (by Prop. 15, Part I). And so there can be nothing positive in ideas on account of which they are called false. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P34", "p": "part2", "l": "2P34", "la": "Omnis idea, quae in nobis est absoluta sive adaequata et perfecta, vera est.", "en": "Every idea which in us is absolute, or adequate and perfect, is true."}, {"id": "E2P34D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P34D", "la": "Cum dicimus, dari in nobis ideam adaequatam et perfectam, nihil aliud dicimus (per coroll. prop. 11. huius) quam quod in Deo, quatenus nostrae mentis essentiam constituit, detur idea adaequata et perfecta, et consequenter (per prop. 32. huius) nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod talis idea sit vera. Q. E. D.", "en": "When we say that there is in us an adequate and perfect idea, we are saying nothing else (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part) than that there is an adequate and perfect idea in God insofar as he constitutes the essence of our mind, and consequently (by Prop. 32 of this Part) we are saying nothing else than that such an idea is true. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P35", "p": "part2", "l": "2P35", "la": "Falsitas consistit in cognitionis privatione, quam ideae inadaequatae sive mutilatae et confusae involvunt.", "en": "Falsity consists in the privation of knowledge which inadequate, or mutilated and confused, ideas involve."}, {"id": "E2P35D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P35D", "la": "Nihil in ideis positivum datur, quod falsitatis formam constituat (per prop. 33. huius). At falsitas in absoluta privatione consistere nequit (mentes enim, non corpora errare nec falli dicuntur), neque etiam in absoluta ignorantia; diversa enim sunt ignorare et errare; quare in cognitionis privatione, quam rerum inadaequata cognitio sive ideae inadaequatae et confusae involvunt, consistit. Q. E. D.", "en": "There is nothing positive in ideas which constitutes the form of falsity (by Prop. 33 of this Part). But falsity cannot consist in absolute privation (for it is minds, not bodies, that are said to err and be deceived), nor yet in absolute ignorance; for to be ignorant and to err are different. So it consists in the privation of knowledge which inadequate knowledge of things, or inadequate and confused ideas, involve. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P35S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P35S", "la": "In schol. prop. 17. huius explicui, qua ratione error in cognitionis privatione consistit. Sed ad uberiorem huius rei explicationem exemplum dabo.", "en": "In Schol. of Prop. 17 of this Part I explained how error consists in the privation of knowledge. But for a fuller explanation of this matter I shall give an example."}, {"id": "E2P35S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P35S", "la": "Nempe falluntur homines, quod se liberos esse putant; quae opinio in hoc solo consistit, quod suarum actionum sint conscii et ignari causarum a quibus determinantur. Haec ergo est eorum libertatis idea, quod suarum actionum nullam cognoscant causam. Nam quod aiunt, humanas actiones a voluntate pendere, verba sunt, quorum nullam habent ideam. Quid enim voluntas sit et quomodo moveat corpus, ignorant omnes; qui aliud iactant, et animae sedes et habitacula fingunt, vel risum vel nauseam movere solent.", "en": "Namely, men are deceived in that they think themselves free, an opinion which consists only in this, that they are conscious of their actions and ignorant of the causes by which they are determined. This, therefore, is their idea of freedom: that they know no cause of their actions. For when they say that human actions depend on the will, these are words of which they have no idea. For what the will is, and how it moves the body, they are all ignorant; those who boast otherwise, and feign seats and dwelling places of the soul, usually provoke either laughter or disgust."}, {"id": "E2P35S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P35S", "la": "Sic cum solem intuemur, eum ducentos circiter pedes a nobis distare imaginamur; qui error in hac sola imaginatione non consistit, sed in eo, quod dum ipsum sic imaginamur, veram eius distantiam et huius imaginationis causam ignoramus. Nam tametsi postea cognoscamus, eundem ultra sescentos terrae diametros a nobis distare, ipsum nihilominus prope adesse imaginabimur; non enim solem adeo propinquum imaginamur, propterea quod veram eius distantiam ignoramus, sed propterea, quod affectio nostri corporis essentiam solis involvit, quatenus ipsum corpus ab eodem afficitur.", "en": "So when we look at the sun, we imagine it to be about two hundred feet away from us; an error which does not consist in this imagining alone, but in the fact that while we imagine it so, we are ignorant of its true distance and of the cause of this imagining. For even if we afterwards come to know that it is more than six hundred diameters of the earth away from us, we shall nonetheless imagine it to be near; for we do not imagine the sun so near because we are ignorant of its true distance, but because an affection of our body involves the essence of the sun insofar as our body is affected by it."}, {"id": "E2P36", "p": "part2", "l": "2P36", "la": "Ideae inadaequatae et confusae eadem necessitate consequuntur, ac adaequatae sive clarae ac distinctae ideae.", "en": "Inadequate and confused ideas follow with the same necessity as adequate, or clear and distinct, ideas."}, {"id": "E2P36D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P36D", "la": "Ideae omnes in Deo sunt (per prop. 15. P. 1.) et quatenus ad Deum referuntur, sunt verae (per prop. 32. huius) et (per coroll. prop. 7. huius) adaequatae; adeoque nullae inadaequatae nec confusae sunt, nisi quatenus ad singularem alicuius mentem referuntur. Qua de re vide prop. 24. et 28. huius). Adeoque omnes tam adaequatae, quam inadaequatae eadem necessitate (per coroll. prop. 6. huius) consequuntur. Q. E. D.", "en": "All ideas are in God (by Prop. 15, Part I), and insofar as they are related to God they are true (by Prop. 32 of this Part) and (by Cor. of Prop. 7 of this Part) adequate; and so there are no inadequate or confused ideas except insofar as they are related to the singular mind of someone. (On this see Props. 24 and 28 of this Part.) And so all ideas, both adequate and inadequate, follow with the same necessity (by Cor. of Prop. 6 of this Part). Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P37", "p": "part2", "l": "2P37", "la": "Id quod omnibus commune (de his vide supra lem. 2.), quodque aeque in parte ac in toto est, nullius rei singularis essentiam constituit.", "en": "That which is common to all things (on which see Lemma 2 above), and which is equally in the part and in the whole, does not constitute the essence of any singular thing."}, {"id": "E2P37D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P37D", "la": "Si negas, concipe, si fieri potest, id essentiam alicuius rei singularis constituere, nempe essentiam B. Ergo (per defin. 2 huius) id sine B non poterit esse, neque concipi. Atqui hoc est contra hypothesin. Ergo id ad essentiam B non pertinet, nec alterius rei singularis essentiam constituit. Q. E. D.", "en": "If you deny this, conceive, if it can be done, that it constitutes the essence of some singular thing, namely the essence of B. Then (by Def. 2 of this Part) it will not be able to be or be conceived without B. But this is contrary to the hypothesis. Therefore it does not pertain to the essence of B, nor does it constitute the essence of any other singular thing. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P38", "p": "part2", "l": "2P38", "la": "Illa quae omnibus communia, quaeque aeque in parte ac in toto sunt, non possunt concipi nisi adaequate.", "en": "Those things which are common to all, and which are equally in the part and in the whole, can only be conceived adequately."}, {"id": "E2P38D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P38D", "la": "Sit A aliquid, quod omnibus corporibus commune, quodque aeque in parte cuiuscumque corporis ac in toto est. Dico A non posse concipi, nisi adaequate. Nam eius idea (per coroll. prop. 7. huius) erit necessario in Deo adaequata, tam quatenus ideam corporis humani, quam quatenus ideas habet eiusdem affectionum, quae (per prop. 16., 25. et 27. huius) tam corporis humani, quam corporum externorum naturam ex parte involvunt, hoc est (per prop. 12. et 13. huius) haec idea erit necessario in Deo adaequata, quatenus mentem humanam constituit, sive quatenus ideas habet, quae in mente humana sunt. Mens igitur (per coroll. prop. 11. huius) A necessario adaequate percipit, idque tam quatenus se, quam quatenus suum vel quodcumque externum corpus percipit; nec A alio modo potest concipi. Q. E. D.", "en": "Let A be something that is common to all bodies, and that is equally in the part of each body and in the whole. I say that A can only be conceived adequately. For its idea (by Cor. of Prop. 7 of this Part) will necessarily be adequate in God, both insofar as he has the idea of the human body and insofar as he has the ideas of its affections, which (by Props. 16, 25 and 27 of this Part) involve in part the nature both of the human body and of external bodies; that is (by Props. 12 and 13 of this Part), this idea will necessarily be adequate in God insofar as he constitutes the human mind, or insofar as he has the ideas which are in the human mind. The mind therefore (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part) necessarily perceives A adequately, and that both insofar as it perceives itself and insofar as it perceives its own or any external body; nor can A be conceived in any other way. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P38C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P38C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, dari quasdam ideas sive notiones omnibus hominibus communes. Nam (per lem. 2.) omnia corpora in quibusdam conveniunt, quae (per prop. praeced.) ab omnibus debent adaequate sive clare et distincte percipi.", "en": "From this it follows that there are certain ideas, or notions, common to all men. For (by Lemma 2) all bodies agree in certain things, which (by the preceding Prop.) must be perceived by all adequately, or clearly and distinctly."}, {"id": "E2P39", "p": "part2", "l": "2P39", "la": "Id quod corpori humano et quibusdam corporibus externis, a quibus corpus humanum affici solet, commune est, et proprium, quodque in cuiuscumque horum parte aeque ac in toto est, eius etiam idea erit in mente adaequata.", "en": "Of that which is common and proper to the human body and to certain external bodies by which the human body is usually affected, and which is equally in the part of each of these and in the whole, there will also be an adequate idea in the mind."}, {"id": "E2P39D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P39D", "la": "Sit A id quod corpori humano et quibusdam corporibus externis commune est et proprium, quodque aeque in humano corpore ac in iisdem corporibus externis, et quod denique aeque in cuiuscumque corporis externi parte ac in toto est. Ipsius A dabitur in Deo idea adaequata (per coroll. prop. 7. huius) tam quatenus ideam corporis humani, quam quatenus positorum corporum externorum ideas habet.", "en": "Let A be that which is common and proper to the human body and to certain external bodies, and which is equally in the human body and in those external bodies, and which, finally, is equally in the part of each external body and in the whole. There will be in God an adequate idea of A (by Cor. of Prop. 7 of this Part) both insofar as he has the idea of the human body and insofar as he has the ideas of the external bodies supposed."}, {"id": "E2P39D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P39D", "la": "Ponatur iam humanum corpus a corpore externo affici per id quod cum eo habet commune, hoc est, ab A. Huius affectionis idea proprietatem A involvet (per prop. 16. huius); atque adeo (per idem coroll. prop. 7. huius) idea huius affectionis, quatenus proprietatem A involvit, erit in Deo adaequata, quatenus idea corporis humani affectus est, hoc est (per prop. 13. huius) quatenus mentis humanae naturam constituit. Adeoque (per coroll. prop. 11. huius) haec idea est etiam in mente humana adaequata. Q. E. D.", "en": "Suppose now that the human body is affected by an external body through what it has in common with it, that is, by A. The idea of this affection will involve the property A (by Prop. 16 of this Part); and so (by the same Cor. of Prop. 7 of this Part) the idea of this affection, insofar as it involves the property A, will be adequate in God insofar as he is affected by the idea of the human body, that is (by Prop. 13 of this Part), insofar as he constitutes the nature of the human mind. And so (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part) this idea is also adequate in the human mind. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P39C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P39C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, quod mens eo aptior est ad plura adaequate percipiendum, quo eius corpus plura habet cum aliis corporibus communia.", "en": "From this it follows that the mind is the more capable of perceiving many things adequately, the more its body has in common with other bodies."}, {"id": "E2P40", "p": "part2", "l": "2P40", "la": "Quaecumque ideae in mente sequuntur ex ideis, quae in ipsa sunt adaequatae, sunt etiam adaequatae.", "en": "Whatever ideas follow in the mind from ideas which are adequate in it are also adequate."}, {"id": "E2P40D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P40D", "la": "Patet. Nam cum dicimus, in mente humana ideam sequi ex ideis, quae in ipsa sunt adaequatae, nihil aliud dicimus (per coroll. prop. 11. huius) quam quod in ipso divino intellectu detur idea, cuius Deus est causa, non quatenus infinitus est, nec quatenus plurimarum rerum singularium ideis affectus est; sed quatenus tantum humanae mentis essentiam constituit.", "en": "This is evident. For when we say that an idea follows in the human mind from ideas which are adequate in it, we are saying nothing else (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part) than that there is in the divine intellect itself an idea of which God is the cause, not insofar as he is infinite, nor insofar as he is affected by the ideas of very many singular things, but insofar as he constitutes only the essence of the human mind."}, {"id": "E2P40S1", "p": "part2", "l": "2P40S1", "la": "His causam notionum, quae *communes* vocantur quaeque ratiocinii nostri fundamenta sunt, explicui. Sed aliae quorundam axiomatum sive notionum causae dantur, quas hac nostra methodo explicare e re foret. Ex iis namque constaret, quaenam notiones prae reliquis utiliores, quaenam vero vix ullius usus essent; deinde quaenam communes, et quaenam iis tantum, qui praeiudiciis non laborant, clarae et distinctae; et quaenam denique male fundatae sint.", "en": "With these things I have explained the cause of the notions which are called common, and which are the foundations of our reasoning. But there are other causes of certain axioms, or notions, which it would be to the purpose to explain by this method of ours. For from them it would be established which notions are more useful than the rest, and which are of hardly any use; next, which are common, and which are clear and distinct only to those who do not labour under prejudices; and which, finally, are ill founded."}, {"id": "E2P40S1", "p": "part2", "l": "2P40S1", "la": "Praeterea constaret, unde notiones illae, quas *secundas* vocant, et consequenter axiomata, quae in iisdem fundantur, suam duxerunt originem, et alia, quae circa haec aliquando meditatus sum. Sed quoniam haec alii dicavi tractatui, et etiam, ne propter nimiam huius rei prolixitatem fastidium crearem, hac re hic supersedere decrevi.", "en": "Further, it would be established from what source those notions which they call second notions, and consequently the axioms that are founded on them, have taken their origin, and other things which I have at some time thought about in this connection. But since I have devoted these matters to another treatise, and also so as not to create tedium by too great a prolixity on this subject, I have decided to pass over this matter here."}, {"id": "E2P40S1", "p": "part2", "l": "2P40S1", "la": "Attamen ne quid horum omittam, quod scitu necessarium sit, causas breviter addam, ex quibus *termini transcendentales* dicti suam duxerunt originem, ut ens, res, aliquid. Hi termini ex hoc oriuntur, quod scilicet humanum corpus, quandoquidem limitatum est, tantum est capax certi imaginum numeri (quid imago sit explicui in schol. prop. 17. huius) in se distincte simul formandi; qui si excedatur, hae imagines confundi incipient, et si hic imaginum numerus, quarum corpus est capax, ut eas in se simul distincte formet, longe excedatur, omnes inter se plane confundentur. Cum hoc ita se habeat, patet ex coroll. prop. 17. et prop. 18. huius, quod mens humana tot corpora distincte simul imaginari poterit, quot in ipsius corpore imagines possunt simul formari. At ubi imagines in corpore plane confunduntur, mens etiam omnia corpora confuse sine ulla distinctione imaginabitur et quasi sub uno attributo comprehendet, nempe sub attributo entis, rei, etc.", "en": "Nevertheless, so as to omit nothing of these matters that is necessary to know, I shall add briefly the causes from which the terms called transcendental, such as being, thing, something, have taken their origin. These terms arise from this, that the human body, since it is limited, is capable of forming in itself distinctly at the same time only a certain number of images (what an image is I have explained in Schol. of Prop. 17 of this Part); and if this number is exceeded, these images will begin to be confused; and if this number of images which the body is capable of forming in itself distinctly at the same time is far exceeded, they will all be completely confused with one another. Since this is so, it is evident from Cor. of Prop. 17 and Prop. 18 of this Part that the human mind will be able to imagine distinctly at the same time as many bodies as there are images that can be formed at the same time in its body. But when the images in the body are completely confused, the mind too will imagine all bodies confusedly, without any distinction, and will comprehend them as it were under one attribute, namely under the attribute of being, thing, etc."}, {"id": "E2P40S1", "p": "part2", "l": "2P40S1", "la": "Potest hoc etiam ex eo deduci, quod imagines non semper aeque vigeant, et ex aliis causis his analogis, quas hic explicare non est opus; nam ad nostrum, ad quem collimamus, scopum unam tantum sufficit considerare. Nam omnes huc redeunt, quod hi termini ideas significent summo gradu confusas.", "en": "This can also be deduced from the fact that images are not always equally vivid, and from other causes analogous to these, which there is no need to explain here; for to our purpose, at which we aim, it is enough to consider only one. For they all come back to this, that these terms signify ideas confused in the highest degree."}, {"id": "E2P40S1", "p": "part2", "l": "2P40S1", "la": "Ex similibus deinde causis ortae sunt notiones illae, quas *universales* vocant, ut homo, equus, canis etc., videlicet, quia in corpore humano tot imagines, ex gr. hominum formantur simul, ut vim imaginandi, non quidem penitus, sed eo usque tamen superent, ut singulorum parvas differentias (videlicet uniuscuiusque colorem, magnitudinem etc.) eorumque determinatum numerum mens imaginari nequeat, et id tantum, in quo omnes, quatenus corpus ab iisdem afficitur, conveniunt, distincte imaginetur; nam ab eo corpus maxime, scilicet ab unoquoque singulari, affectum fuit; atque hoc nomine hominis exprimit, hocque de infinitis singularibus praedicat. Nam singularium determinatum numerum, ut diximus, imaginari nequit.", "en": "From similar causes, next, have arisen those notions which they call universal, such as man, horse, dog, etc., namely because so many images, for example of men, are formed at the same time in the human body that they surpass the power of imagining, not indeed completely, but still to the point that the mind cannot imagine the small differences of the individuals (namely the colour, size, etc. of each) and their determinate number, and imagines distinctly only that in which all agree, insofar as the body is affected by them; for by that the body has been affected most, namely by each individual; and this it expresses by the name man, and predicates it of infinitely many individuals. For, as we have said, it cannot imagine the determinate number of the individuals."}, {"id": "E2P40S1", "p": "part2", "l": "2P40S1", "la": "Sed notandum, has notiones non ab omnibus eodem modo formari, sed apud unumquemque variare pro ratione rei, a qua corpus affectum saepius fuit, quamque facilius mens imaginatur vel recordatur. Ex. gr. qui saepius cum admiratione hominum staturam contemplati sunt, sub nomine hominis intelligent animal erectae staturae; qui vero aliud assueti sunt contemplari, aliam hominum communem imaginem formabunt, nempe, hominem esse animal risibile, animal bipes, sine plumis, animal rationale; et sic de reliquis unusquisque pro dispositione sui corporis rerum universales imagines formabit. Quare non mirum est, quod inter philosophos, qui res naturales per solas rerum imagines explicare voluerunt, tot sint ortae controversiae.", "en": "But it is to be noted that these notions are not formed by all in the same way, but vary in each according to the thing by which the body has more often been affected, and which the mind imagines or recollects more easily. For example, those who have more often contemplated the stature of men with wonder will understand by the name man an animal of erect stature; but those who are accustomed to contemplate something else will form another common image of men, namely that man is an animal capable of laughter, a featherless biped, a rational animal; and so of the rest, each will form universal images of things according to the disposition of his body. So it is no wonder that so many controversies have arisen among the philosophers who wished to explain natural things through mere images of things."}, {"id": "E2P40S2", "p": "part2", "l": "2P40S2", "la": "Ex omnibus supra dictis clare apparet, nos multa percipere, et notiones universales formare 1. ex singularibus nobis per sensus mutilate, confuse et sine ordine ad intellectum repraesentatis (vide coroll. prop. 29. huius); et ideo tales perceptiones *cognitionem ab experientia vaga* vocare consuevi. 2. Ex signis, ex. gr. ex eo, quod auditis aut lectis quibusdam verbis rerum recordemur, et earum quasdam ideas formemus similes iis, per quas res imaginamur. Vide schol. prop. 18. huius. Utrumque hunc res contemplandi modum *cognitionem primi generis, opinionem, vel imaginationem* in posterum vocabo.", "en": "From all that has been said above it is clearly evident that we perceive many things and form universal notions 1. from singulars represented to us through the senses in a mutilated, confused way and without order for the intellect (see Cor. of Prop. 29 of this Part); and for that reason I have been accustomed to call such perceptions knowledge from random experience. 2. From signs, for example from the fact that, having heard or read certain words, we recollect things and form certain ideas of them similar to those through which we imagine the things (see Schol. of Prop. 18 of this Part). Both these ways of contemplating things I shall henceforth call knowledge of the first kind, opinion, or imagination."}, {"id": "E2P40S2", "p": "part2", "l": "2P40S2", "la": "3. Denique ex eo, quod notiones communes rerumque proprietatum ideas adaequatas habemus. Vide coroll. prop. 38. et 39. cum eius coroll. et prop. 40. huius. Atque hunc *rationem, et secundi generis cognitionem* vocabo. Praeter haec duo cognitionis genera datur, ut in sequentibus ostendam, aliud tertium quod *scientiam intuitivam* vocabimus. Atque hoc cognoscendi genus procedit ab adaequata idea essentiae formalis quorundam Dei attributorum ad adaequatam cognitionem essentiae rerum.", "en": "3. Finally, from the fact that we have common notions and adequate ideas of the properties of things (see Cor. of Prop. 38, and Prop. 39 with its Cor., and Prop. 40 of this Part). And this I shall call reason, and knowledge of the second kind. Besides these two kinds of knowledge there is, as I shall show in what follows, another, third kind, which we shall call intuitive knowledge. And this kind of knowing proceeds from an adequate idea of the formal essence of certain attributes of God to the adequate knowledge of the essence of things."}, {"id": "E2P40S2", "p": "part2", "l": "2P40S2", "la": "Haec omnia unius rei exemplo explicabo. Dantur ex. gr. tres numeri ad quartum obtinendum, qui sit ad tertium, ut secundus ad primum. Non dubitant mercatores secundum in tertium ducere et productum per primum dividere; quia scilicet ea, quae a magistro absque ulla demonstratione audiverunt, nondum tradiderunt oblivioni, vel quia id saepe in numeris simplicissimis experti sunt, vel ex vi demonstr. prop. 19. libr. 7. Euclid., nempe ex communi proprietate proportionalium. At in numeris simplicissimis nihil horum opus est. Ex. gr. datis numeris 1, 2, 3 nemo non videt, quartum numerum proportionalem esse 6, atque hoc multo clarius, quia ex ipsa ratione, quam primum ad secundum habere uno intuitu videmus, ipsum quartum concludimus.", "en": "I shall explain all these by the example of one thing. Suppose, for example, three numbers are given in order to obtain a fourth which is to the third as the second is to the first. Merchants do not hesitate to multiply the second by the third and divide the product by the first, either because they have not yet forgotten what they heard from their teacher without any demonstration, or because they have often found it so in the simplest numbers, or from the force of the demonstration of Prop. 19, Book VII of Euclid, namely from the common property of proportionals. But in the simplest numbers none of this is needed. For example, given the numbers 1, 2, 3, no one fails to see that the fourth proportional number is 6, and this much more clearly, because from the very ratio which we see at a glance the first to have to the second, we infer the fourth itself."}, {"id": "E2P41", "p": "part2", "l": "2P41", "la": "Cognitio primi generis unica est falsitatis causa, secundi autem et tertii est necessario vera.", "en": "Knowledge of the first kind is the only cause of falsity, but knowledge of the second and third kinds is necessarily true."}, {"id": "E2P41D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P41D", "la": "Ad primi generis cognitionem illas omnes ideas diximus in praeced. schol. pertinere, quae sunt inadaequatae, et confusae; atque adeo (per prop. 35. huius) haec cognitio unica est falsitatis causa. Deinde ad cognitionem secundi et tertii illas pertinere diximus, quae sunt adaequatae; adeoque (per prop. 34. huius) est necessario vera. Q. E. D.", "en": "We have said in the preceding Scholium that to knowledge of the first kind belong all those ideas which are inadequate and confused; and therefore (by Prop. 35 of this Part) this knowledge is the only cause of falsity. Next, we have said that to knowledge of the second and third kinds belong those which are adequate; and therefore (by Prop. 34 of this Part) it is necessarily true. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P42", "p": "part2", "l": "2P42", "la": "Secundi et tertii, et non primi generis cognitio docet nos verum a falso distinguere.", "en": "Knowledge of the second and third kinds, and not of the first kind, teaches us to distinguish the true from the false."}, {"id": "E2P42D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P42D", "la": "Haec prop. per se patet. Qui enim inter verum, et falsum scit distinguere, debet adaequatam veri, et falsi habere ideam, hoc est (per schol. 2. prop. 40. huius) verum, et falsum secundo, aut tertio cognitionis genere cognoscere.", "en": "This Proposition is evident through itself. For he who knows how to distinguish between the true and the false must have an adequate idea of the true and the false, that is (by Schol. 2 of Prop. 40 of this Part), he must know the true and the false by the second or third kind of knowledge."}, {"id": "E2P43", "p": "part2", "l": "2P43", "la": "Qui veram habet ideam, simul scit se veram habere ideam, nec de rei veritate potest dubitare.", "en": "He who has a true idea knows at the same time that he has a true idea, and cannot doubt the truth of the thing."}, {"id": "E2P43D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P43D", "la": "Idea vera in nobis est illa, quae in Deo, quatenus per naturam mentis humanae explicatur, est adaequata (per coroll. prop. 11. huius). Ponamus itaque, dari in Deo, quatenus per naturam mentis humanae explicatur, ideam adaequatam A. Huius ideae debet necessario dari etiam in Deo idea, quae ad Deum eodem modo refertur, ac idea A (per prop. 20. huius, cuius demonstratio universalis est). At idea A ad Deum referri supponitur, quatenus per naturam mentis humanae explicatur; ergo etiam idea ideae A ad Deum eodem modo debet referri, hoc est (per idem coroll. prop. 11. huius) haec adaequata idea ideae A erit in ipsa mente, quae ideam adaequatam A habet; adeoque qui adaequatam habet ideam, sive (per prop. 34. huius) qui vere rem cognoscit, debet simul suae cognitionis adaequatam habere ideam, sive veram cognitionem, hoc est (ut per se manifestum) debet simul esse certus. Q. E. D.", "en": "A true idea in us is one which is adequate in God insofar as he is explained through the nature of the human mind (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part). Let us suppose, then, that there is in God, insofar as he is explained through the nature of the human mind, an adequate idea A. Of this idea there must necessarily also be in God an idea which is related to God in the same way as idea A (by Prop. 20 of this Part, whose demonstration is universal). But idea A is supposed to be related to God insofar as he is explained through the nature of the human mind; therefore the idea of idea A must also be related to God in the same way; that is (by the same Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part), this adequate idea of idea A will be in the mind itself which has the adequate idea A. And so he who has an adequate idea, or (by Prop. 34 of this Part) who knows a thing truly, must at the same time have an adequate idea, or true knowledge, of his own knowledge; that is (as is self-evident), he must at the same time be certain. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P43S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P43S", "la": "In schol. prop. 21. huius explicui, quid sit idea ideae. Sed notandum, praeced. prop. per se satis esse manifestam. Nam nemo, qui veram habet ideam, ignorat veram ideam summam certitudinem involvere. Veram namque habere ideam, nihil aliud significat, quam perfecte, sive optime rem cognoscere; nec sane aliquis de hac re dubitare potest, nisi putet, ideam quid mutum instar picturae in tabula, et non modum cogitandi esse, nempe ipsum intelligere. Et quaeso, quis scire potest, se rem aliquam intelligere, nisi prius rem intelligat? hoc est, quis potest scire, se de aliqua re certum esse, nisi prius de ea re certus sit?", "en": "In the Scholium of Prop. 21 of this Part I have explained what the idea of an idea is. But it should be noted that the preceding Proposition is manifest enough through itself. For no one who has a true idea is ignorant that a true idea involves the highest certainty. For to have a true idea signifies nothing other than to know a thing perfectly, or in the best way; and indeed no one can doubt this unless he thinks that an idea is something mute, like a picture on a panel, and not a mode of thinking, namely understanding itself. And, I ask, who can know that he understands some thing unless he first understands the thing? That is, who can know that he is certain about some thing unless he is first certain about that thing?"}, {"id": "E2P43S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P43S", "la": "Deinde quid idea vera clarius et certius dari potest, quod norma sit veritatis? Sane sicut lux se ipsam et tenebras manifestat, sic veritas norma sui et falsi est.", "en": "Next, what can there be clearer and more certain than a true idea, to be the standard of truth? Indeed, just as light makes manifest both itself and darkness, so truth is the standard both of itself and of the false."}, {"id": "E2P43S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P43S", "la": "Atque his me ad has quaestiones respondisse puto; nempe, si idea vera, quatenus tantum dicitur cum suo ideato convenire, a falsa distinguitur, nihil ergo realitatis, aut perfectionis idea vera habet prae falsa (quandoquidem per solam denominationem extrinsecam distinguuntur), et consequenter neque etiam homo, qui veras, prae illo, qui falsas tantum ideas habet? Deinde unde fit, ut homines falsas habeant ideas? Et denique, unde aliquis certo scire potest, se ideas habere, quae cum suis ideatis conveniant? Ad has, inquam, quaestiones me iam respondisse puto.", "en": "And with this I think I have answered these questions. Namely: if a true idea is distinguished from a false one only insofar as it is said to agree with its object, does a true idea then have no more reality or perfection than a false one (since they are distinguished only by an extrinsic denomination), and consequently does a man who has true ideas have none over one who has only false ones? Next, how does it happen that men have false ideas? And finally, whence can someone know for certain that he has ideas which agree with their objects? To these questions, I say, I think I have already answered."}, {"id": "E2P43S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P43S", "la": "Nam quod ad differentiam inter ideam veram et falsam attinet, constat ex prop. 35. huius illam ad hanc sese habere, ut ens ad non-ens; falsitatis autem causas a prop. 19. usque ad 35. cum eius schol. clarissime ostendi. Ex quibus etiam apparet, quid homo, qui veras habet ideas, homini, qui non nisi falsas habet, intersit.", "en": "For as regards the difference between a true and a false idea, it is established from Prop. 35 of this Part that the former is related to the latter as being to not-being. And the causes of falsity I have shown most clearly from Prop. 19 up to 35, with its Scholium. From which it is also apparent what difference there is between a man who has true ideas and a man who has only false ones."}, {"id": "E2P43S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P43S", "la": "Quod denique ultimum attinet, nempe, undenam homo scire potest se habere ideam, quae cum suo ideato conveniat, id modo satis superque ostendi ex hoc solo oriri, quod ideam habet, quae cum suo ideato convenit, sive quod veritas sui sit norma. His adde, quod mens nostra, quatenus res vere percipit, pars est infiniti Dei intellectus (per coroll. prop. 11 huius); adeoque tam necesse est, ut mentis clarae et distinctae ideae verae sint, ac Dei ideae.", "en": "As regards, finally, the last question, namely, whence a man can know that he has an idea which agrees with its object, I have just shown more than enough that this arises solely from the fact that he has an idea which agrees with its object, or that truth is its own standard. Add to this that our mind, insofar as it perceives things truly, is part of the infinite intellect of God (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of this Part); and so it is as necessary that the clear and distinct ideas of the mind are true as that God’s ideas are."}, {"id": "E2P44", "p": "part2", "l": "2P44", "la": "De natura rationis non est res ut contingentes, sed ut necessarias contemplari.", "en": "It is of the nature of reason to contemplate things not as contingent but as necessary."}, {"id": "E2P44D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P44D", "la": "De natura rationis est res vere percipere (per prop. 41. huius), nempe (per axiom. 6. P. 1.) ut in se sunt, hoc est (per prop. 29. P. 1.) non ut contingentes, sed ut necessarias. Q. E. D.", "en": "It is of the nature of reason to perceive things truly (by Prop. 41 of this Part), namely (by Ax. 6 of Part I) as they are in themselves, that is (by Prop. 29 of Part I), not as contingent but as necessary. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P44C1", "p": "part2", "l": "2P44C1", "la": "Hinc sequitur, a sola imaginatione pendere, quod res tam respectu praeteriti, quam futuri, ut contingentes contemplemur.", "en": "From this it follows that it depends on the imagination alone that we contemplate things as contingent, with respect to the past as well as the future."}, {"id": "E2P44S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P44S", "la": "Qua autem ratione hoc fiat, paucis explicabo. Ostendimus supra (prop. 17. huius cum eius coroll.) mentem, quamvis res non existant, eas tamen semper ut sibi praesentes imaginari, nisi causae occurrant, quae earum praesentem existentiam secludant. Deinde (prop. 18. huius) ostendimus, quod, si corpus humanum semel a duobus corporibus externis simul affectum fuit, ubi mens postea eorum alterutrum imaginabitur, statim et alterius recordabitur, hoc est, ambo ut sibi praesentia contemplabitur, nisi causae occurrant, quae eorum praesentem existentiam secludant. Praeterea nemo dubitat, quin etiam tempus imaginemur, nempe ex eo, quod corpora alia aliis tardius vel celerius, vel aeque celeriter moveri imaginemur.", "en": "How this comes about I shall explain in a few words. We have shown above (Prop. 17 of this Part with its Cor.) that the mind, even though things do not exist, nevertheless always imagines them as present to itself, unless causes occur which exclude their present existence. Next (Prop. 18 of this Part) we have shown that if the human body has once been affected by two external bodies at the same time, then when the mind afterwards imagines one of them, it will immediately recollect the other also, that is, it will contemplate both as present to itself, unless causes occur which exclude their present existence. Moreover, no one doubts that we also imagine time, namely from the fact that we imagine some bodies to move more slowly than others, or more quickly, or equally quickly."}, {"id": "E2P44S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P44S", "la": "Ponamus itaque puerum, qui heri prima vice hora matutina viderit Petrum, meridiana autem Paulum et vespertina Simeonem, atque hodie iterum matutina hora Petrum. Ex prop. 18. huius patet, quod simulac matutinam lucem videt, illico solem eandem caeli, quam die praecedenti viderit, partem percurrentem sive diem integrum, et simul cum tempore matutino Petrum, cum meridiano autem Paulum et cum vespertino Simeonem imaginabitur, hoc est, Pauli et Simeonis existentiam cum relatione ad futurum tempus imaginabitur; et contra, si hora vespertina Simeonem videat, Paulum et Petrum ad tempus praeteritum referet, eosdem scilicet simul cum tempore praeterito imaginando; atque haec eo constantius, quo saepius eos eodem hoc ordine viderit.", "en": "Let us suppose, then, a boy who yesterday, for the first time, saw Peter in the morning, Paul at noon and Simeon in the evening, and today again saw Peter in the morning. From Prop. 18 of this Part it is clear that as soon as he sees the morning light, he will immediately imagine the sun traversing the same part of the sky as he saw it traverse on the preceding day, or the whole day, and Peter together with the morning time, Paul with noon and Simeon with the evening; that is, he will imagine the existence of Paul and Simeon with relation to future time. And on the other hand, if he sees Simeon in the evening, he will refer Paul and Peter to past time, namely by imagining them together with past time; and he will do this the more constantly, the more often he has seen them in this same order."}, {"id": "E2P44S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P44S", "la": "Quod si aliquando contingat, ut alia quadam vespera loco Simeonis Iacobum videat, tum sequenti mane cum tempore vespertino iam Simeonem, iam Iacobum, non vero ambos simul imaginabitur. Nam alterutrum tantum, non autem ambos simul tempore vespertino vidisse supponitur. Fluctuabitur itaque eius imaginatio, et cum futuro tempore vespertino iam hunc, iam illum imaginabitur, hoc est, neutrum certo; sed utrumque contingenter futurum contemplabitur. Atque haec imaginationis fluctuatio eadem erit, si imaginatio rerum sit, quas eodem modo cum relatione ad tempus praeteritum vel praesens contemplamur, et consequenter res tam ad tempus praesens, quam ad praeteritum vel futurum relatas ut contingentes imaginabimur.", "en": "But if it should ever happen that on some other evening he sees James instead of Simeon, then on the following morning he will imagine with the evening time now Simeon, now James, but not both together. For he is supposed to have seen only one of them in the evening, not both together. His imagination will therefore fluctuate, and with the future evening time he will imagine now this one, now that one, that is, neither of them with certainty; but he will contemplate each as contingently future. And this fluctuation of the imagination will be the same if the imagination is of things which we contemplate in the same way with relation to past or present time; and consequently we shall imagine things related to present time, as well as to past or future time, as contingent."}, {"id": "E2P44C2", "p": "part2", "l": "2P44C2", "la": "De natura rationis est res sub quadam aeternitatis specie percipere.", "en": "It is of the nature of reason to perceive things under a certain species of eternity."}, {"id": "E2P44C2D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P44C2D", "la": "De natura enim rationis est res ut necessarias et non ut contingentes contemplari (per prop. praeced.). Hanc autem rerum necessitatem (per prop. 41. huius) vere, hoc est (per axiom. 6. P. 1.) ut in se est, percipit. Sed (per prop. 16. P. 1.) haec rerum necessitas est ipsa Dei aeternae naturae necessitas. Ergo de natura rationis est res sub hac aeternitatis specie contemplari. Adde, quod fundamenta rationis notiones sint (per prop. 38. huius) quae illa explicant, quae omnibus communia sunt, quaeque (per prop. 37. huius) nullius rei singularis essentiam explicant; quaeque propterea absque ulla temporis relatione, sed sub quadam aeternitatis specie debent concipi. Q. E. D.", "en": "For it is of the nature of reason to contemplate things as necessary and not as contingent (by the preceding Prop.). And it perceives this necessity of things truly (by Prop. 41 of this Part), that is (by Ax. 6 of Part I), as it is in itself. But (by Prop. 16 of Part I) this necessity of things is the very necessity of God’s eternal nature. Therefore it is of the nature of reason to contemplate things under this species of eternity. Add that the foundations of reason are notions (by Prop. 38 of this Part) which explain those things that are common to all, and which (by Prop. 37 of this Part) explain the essence of no singular thing; and which must therefore be conceived without any relation to time, but under a certain species of eternity. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P45", "p": "part2", "l": "2P45", "la": "Unaquaeque cuiuscumque corporis, vel rei singularis actu existentis idea Dei aeternam et infinitam essentiam necessario involvit.", "en": "Each idea of each body, or of each singular thing actually existing, necessarily involves the eternal and infinite essence of God."}, {"id": "E2P45D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P45D", "la": "Idea rei singularis actu existentis ipsius rei tam essentiam, quam existentiam necessario involvit (per coroll. prop. 8. huius). At res singulares (per prop. 15. P. 1.) non possunt sine Deo concipi; sed quia (per prop. 6. huius) Deum pro causa habent, quatenus sub attributo consideratur, cuius res ipsae modi sunt, debent necessario earum ideae (per axiom. 4. P. 1.) ipsarum attributi conceptum, hoc est (per defin. 6. P. 1.) Dei aeternam et infinitam essentiam involvere. Q. E. D.", "en": "The idea of a singular thing actually existing necessarily involves both the essence and the existence of the thing itself (by Cor. of Prop. 8 of this Part). But singular things (by Prop. 15 of Part I) cannot be conceived without God; but because (by Prop. 6 of this Part) they have God for a cause insofar as he is considered under the attribute of which the things themselves are modes, their ideas must necessarily (by Ax. 4 of Part I) involve the concept of their attribute, that is (by Def. 6 of Part I), the eternal and infinite essence of God. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P45S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P45S", "la": "Hic per existentiam non intelligo durationem, hoc est, existentiam, quatenus abstracte concipitur et tanquam quaedam quantitatis species. Nam loquor de ipsa natura existentiae, quae rebus singularibus tribuitur, propterea quod ex aeterna necessitate Dei naturae infinita infinitis modis sequuntur. Vide prop. 16. P. 1. Loquor, inquam, de ipsa existentia rerum singularium, quatenus in Deo sunt.", "en": "By existence I do not understand here duration, that is, existence insofar as it is conceived abstractly, and as a certain species of quantity. For I am speaking of the very nature of existence, which is attributed to singular things because infinitely many things follow in infinitely many ways from the eternal necessity of God’s nature. See Prop. 16 of Part I. I am speaking, I say, of the very existence of singular things insofar as they are in God."}, {"id": "E2P45S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P45S", "la": "Nam etsi unaquaeque ab alia re singulari determinetur ad certo modo existendum; vis tamen, qua unaquaeque in existendo perseverat, ex aeterna necessitate naturae Dei sequitur. Qua de re vide coroll. prop. 24. P. 1.", "en": "For even though each is determined by another singular thing to exist in a certain way, still the force by which each perseveres in existing follows from the eternal necessity of the nature of God. On this see Cor. of Prop. 24 of Part I."}, {"id": "E2P46", "p": "part2", "l": "2P46", "la": "Cognitio aeternae et infinitae essentiae Dei, quam unaquaeque idea involvit, est adaequata et perfecta.", "en": "The knowledge of the eternal and infinite essence of God which each idea involves is adequate and perfect."}, {"id": "E2P46D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P46D", "la": "Demonstratio praeced. prop. universalis est, et sive res ut pars, sive ut totum consideretur, eius idea sive totius sit sive partis (per prop. praeced.), Dei aeternam et infinitam essentiam involvet. Quare id quod cognitionem aeternae et infinitae essentiae Dei dat, omnibus commune et aeque in parte, ac in toto est, adeoque (per prop. 38. huius) erit haec cognitio adaequata. Q. E. D.", "en": "The demonstration of the preceding Proposition is universal, and whether a thing is considered as a part or as a whole, its idea, whether of the whole or of a part, will (by the preceding Prop.) involve the eternal and infinite essence of God. Therefore that which gives knowledge of the eternal and infinite essence of God is common to all, and is equally in the part and in the whole; and so (by Prop. 38 of this Part) this knowledge will be adequate. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P47", "p": "part2", "l": "2P47", "la": "Mens humana adaequatam habet cognitionem aeternae et infinitae essentiae Dei.", "en": "The human mind has an adequate knowledge of the eternal and infinite essence of God."}, {"id": "E2P47D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P47D", "la": "Mens humana ideas habet (per prop. 22. huius) ex quibus (per prop. 23. huius) se suumque corpus (per prop. 19. huius) et (per coroll. 1. prop. 16. et per prop. 17. huius) corpora externa ut actu existentia percipit; adeoque (per prop. 45. et 46. huius) cognitionem aeternae et infinitae essentiae Dei habet adaequatam. Q. E. D.", "en": "The human mind has ideas (by Prop. 22 of this Part) from which (by Prop. 23 of this Part) it perceives itself, and its own body (by Prop. 19 of this Part), and (by Cor. 1 of Prop. 16 and by Prop. 17 of this Part) external bodies as actually existing; and so (by Props. 45 and 46 of this Part) it has an adequate knowledge of the eternal and infinite essence of God. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P47S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P47S", "la": "Hinc videmus, Dei infinitam essentiam eiusque aeternitatem omnibus esse notam. Cum autem omnia in Deo sint et per Deum concipiantur, sequitur, nos ex cognitione hac plurima posse deducere, quae adaequate cognoscamus, atque adeo tertium illud cognitionis genus formare, de quo diximus in schol. 2. prop. 40. huius, et de cuius praestantia et utilitate in P. 5. erit nobis dicendi locus.", "en": "From this we see that God’s infinite essence and his eternity are known to all. And since all things are in God and are conceived through God, it follows that we can deduce from this knowledge very many things which we know adequately, and so form that third kind of knowledge of which we spoke in Schol. 2 of Prop. 40 of this Part, and of whose excellence and usefulness there will be occasion to speak in Part V."}, {"id": "E2P47S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P47S", "la": "Quod autem homines non aeque claram Dei, ac notionum communium habeant cognitionem, inde fit, quod Deum imaginari nequeant ut corpora, et quod nomen Deus iunxerunt imaginibus rerum, quas videre solent; quod homines vix vitare possunt, quia continuo a corporibus externis afficiuntur. Et profecto plerique errores in hoc solo consistunt, quod scilicet nomina rebus non recte applicamus. Cum enim aliquis ait, lineas, quae ex centro circuli ad eiusdem circumferentiam ducuntur, esse inaequales, ille sane aliud tum saltem per circulum intelligit, quam mathematici.", "en": "That men do not have as clear a knowledge of God as they have of the common notions comes from this: that they cannot imagine God as they do bodies, and that they have joined the name God to the images of things which they are accustomed to see; which men can hardly avoid, because they are continually affected by external bodies. And indeed most errors consist in this alone, that we do not apply names to things correctly. For when someone says that the lines drawn from the centre of a circle to its circumference are unequal, he certainly understands by a circle, at least at that moment, something other than mathematicians do."}, {"id": "E2P47S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P47S", "la": "Sic cum homines in calculo errant, alios numeros in mente, alios in charta habent. Quare si ipsorum mentem spectes, non errant sane; videntur tamen errare, quia ipsos in mente putamus habere numeros, qui in charta sunt. Si hoc non esset, nihil eosdem errare crederemus; ut non credidi quendam errare, quem nuper audivi clamantem, suum atrium volasse in gallinam vicini, quia scilicet ipsius mens satis perspecta mihi videbatur.", "en": "So when men err in calculating, they have some numbers in their mind and others on the paper. And so if you look to their mind, they certainly do not err; they seem to err, however, because we think that they have in their mind the numbers which are on the paper. If this were not so, we would not believe that they err at all; just as I did not believe that a man was in error whom I recently heard shouting that his courtyard had flown into his neighbour’s hen, because what he had in mind seemed to me plain enough."}, {"id": "E2P47S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P47S", "la": "Atque hinc pleraeque oriuntur controversiae, nempe quia homines mentem suam non recte explicant, vel quia alterius mentem male interpretantur. Nam revera dum sibi maxime contradicunt, vel eadem vel diversa cogitant, ita ut, quos in alio errores et absurda esse putant, non sint.", "en": "And from this arise most controversies: namely, because men do not explain their mind correctly, or because they interpret another’s mind badly. For in truth, while they contradict one another most, they think either the same things or different things, so that what they think are errors and absurdities in the other are not."}, {"id": "E2P48", "p": "part2", "l": "2P48", "la": "In mente nulla est absoluta sive libera voluntas, sed mens ad hoc vel illud volendum determinatur a causa, quae etiam ab alia determinata est, et haec iterum ab alia, et sic in infinitum.", "en": "In the mind there is no absolute, or free, will; but the mind is determined to will this or that by a cause which is also determined by another, and this again by another, and so to infinity."}, {"id": "E2P48D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P48D", "la": "Mens certus et determinatus modus cogitandi est (per prop. 11. huius), adeoque (per coroll. 2. prop. 17. P. 1.) suarum actionum non potest esse causa libera, sive absolutam facultatem volendi et nolendi habere non potest; sed ad hoc vel illud volendum (per prop. 28. P. 1.) determinari debet a causa, quae etiam ab alia determinata est, et haec iterum ab alia, etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "The mind is a certain and determinate mode of thinking (by Prop. 11 of this Part), and so (by Cor. 2 of Prop. 17 of Part I) it cannot be the free cause of its own actions, or cannot have an absolute faculty of willing and not willing; but it must be determined to will this or that (by Prop. 28 of Part I) by a cause which is also determined by another, and this again by another, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P48S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P48S", "la": "Eodem hoc modo demonstratur in mente nullam dari facultatem absolutam intelligendi, cupiendi, amandi, etc. Unde sequitur, has et similes facultates vel prorsus fictitias, vel nihil esse praeter entia metaphysica sive universalia, quae ex particularibus formare solemus; adeo ut intellectus, et voluntas ad hanc et illam ideam, vel ad hanc et illam volitionem eodem modo sese habeant, ac lapideitas ad hunc et illum lapidem, vel ut homo ad Petrum et Paulum. Causam autem, cur homines se liberos esse putent, explicuimus in app. P. 1.", "en": "In this same way it is demonstrated that there is in the mind no absolute faculty of understanding, desiring, loving, etc. From this it follows that these and similar faculties are either altogether fictitious, or nothing but metaphysical beings, or universals, which we are accustomed to form from particulars; so that intellect and will are related to this or that idea, or to this or that volition, in the same way as stoneness is related to this or that stone, or man to Peter and Paul. The cause why men think themselves free, however, we have explained in the Appendix of Part I."}, {"id": "E2P48S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P48S", "la": "Verum, antequam ulterius pergam, venit hic notandum, me per voluntatem affirmandi et negandi facultatem, non autem cupiditatem intelligere; facultatem, inquam, intelligo, qua mens, quid verum quidve falsum sit, affirmat vel negat, et non cupiditatem, qua mens res appetit vel aversatur.", "en": "But before I go further, it should be noted here that by will I understand the faculty of affirming and denying, and not desire; I understand, I say, the faculty by which the mind affirms or denies what is true or what is false, and not the desire by which the mind wants things or is averse to them."}, {"id": "E2P48S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P48S", "la": "At postquam demonstravimus, has facultates notiones esse universales, quae a singularibus, ex quibus easdem formamus, non distinguuntur, inquirendum iam est, an ipsae volitiones aliquid sint praeter ipsas rerum ideas. Inquirendum, inquam, est, an in mente alia affirmatio et negatio detur praeter illam, quam idea, quatenus idea est, involvit, qua de re vide seq. prop., ut et defin. 3. huius, ne cogitatio in picturas incidat. Non enim per ideas imagines, quales in fundo oculi, et, si placet, in medio cerebro formantur, sed cogitationis conceptus intelligo.", "en": "But now that we have demonstrated that these faculties are universal notions, which are not distinguished from the singulars from which we form them, we must inquire whether the volitions themselves are anything besides the very ideas of things. We must inquire, I say, whether there is in the mind any other affirmation and negation besides that which an idea, insofar as it is an idea, involves (on which see the following Proposition, and also Def. 3 of this Part, so that thought does not fall into pictures). For by ideas I do not understand images such as are formed at the back of the eye and, if you like, in the middle of the brain, but concepts of thought."}, {"id": "E2P49", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49", "la": "In mente nulla datur volitio sive affirmatio et negatio praeter illam, quam idea, quatenus idea est, involvit.", "en": "There is in the mind no volition, or affirmation and negation, besides that which an idea, insofar as it is an idea, involves."}, {"id": "E2P49D", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49D", "la": "In mente (per prop. praeced.) nulla datur absoluta facultas volendi et nolendi, sed tantum singulares volitiones, nempe haec et illa affirmatio, et haec et illa negatio. Concipiamus itaque singularem aliquam volitionem, nempe modum cogitandi, quo mens affirmat, tres angulos trianguli aequales esse duobus rectis. Haec affirmatio conceptum, sive ideam trianguli involvit, hoc est, sine idea trianguli non potest concipi. Idem enim est, si dicam, quod A conceptum B debeat involvere, ac quod A sine B non possit concipi. Deinde haec affirmatio (per axiom. 3. huius) non potest etiam sine idea trianguli esse. Haec ergo affirmatio sine idea trianguli nec esse, nec concipi potest. Porro haec trianguli idea hanc eandem affirmationem involvere debet, nempe, quod tres eius anguli aequentur duobus rectis. Quare et vice versa haec trianguli idea sine hac affirmatione nec esse nec concipi potest; adeoque (per defin. 2. huius) haec affirmatio ad essentiam ideae trianguli pertinet, nec aliud praeter ipsam est. Et quod de hac volitione diximus (quandoquidem eam ad libitum sumpsimus), dicendum etiam est de quacumque volitione, nempe, quod praeter ideam nihil sit. Q. E. D.", "en": "There is in the mind (by the preceding Prop.) no absolute faculty of willing and not willing, but only singular volitions, namely this and that affirmation, and this and that negation. Let us conceive, then, some singular volition, namely the mode of thinking by which the mind affirms that the three angles of a triangle are equal to two right angles. This affirmation involves the concept, or idea, of the triangle, that is, it cannot be conceived without the idea of the triangle. For it is the same whether I say that A must involve the concept of B, or that A cannot be conceived without B. Next, this affirmation (by Ax. 3 of this Part) also cannot be without the idea of the triangle. This affirmation, therefore, can neither be nor be conceived without the idea of the triangle. Further, this idea of the triangle must involve this same affirmation, namely that its three angles are equal to two right angles. Therefore, conversely, this idea of the triangle can neither be nor be conceived without this affirmation; and so (by Def. 2 of this Part) this affirmation pertains to the essence of the idea of the triangle, and is nothing besides it. And what we have said of this volition (since we took it at random) must also be said of any volition, namely that it is nothing besides the idea. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P49C", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49C", "la": "Voluntas et intellectus unum et idem sunt.", "en": "Will and intellect are one and the same."}, {"id": "E2P49CD", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49CD", "la": "Voluntas et intellectus nihil praeter ipsas singulares volitiones et ideas sunt (per prop. 48. huius et eiusdem schol.). At singularis volitio et idea (per prop. praeced.) unum et idem sunt. Ergo voluntas et intellectus unum et idem sunt. Q. E. D.", "en": "Will and intellect are nothing besides the singular volitions and ideas themselves (by Prop. 48 of this Part and its Scholium). But a singular volition and idea (by the preceding Prop.) are one and the same. Therefore will and intellect are one and the same. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E2P49S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "His causam, quae communiter erroris esse statuitur, sustulimus. Supra autem ostendimus, falsitatem in sola privatione, quam ideae mutilatae, et confusae involvunt, consistere. Quare idea falsa, quatenus falsa est, certitudinem non involvit. Cum itaque dicimus, hominem in falsis acquiescere, nec de iis dubitare, non ideo ipsum certum esse, sed tantum non dubitare dicimus, vel quod in falsis acquiescit, quia nullae causae dantur, quae efficiant, ut ipsius imaginatio fluctuetur. Qua de re vide schol. prop. 44. huius. Quantumvis igitur homo falsis adhaerere supponatur, nunquam tamen ipsum certum esse dicemus. Nam per certitudinem quid positivum intelligimus (vide prop. 43. huius cum eiusdem schol.), non vero dubitationis privationem. At per certitudinis privationem falsitatem intelligimus.", "en": "With this we have removed what is commonly held to be the cause of error. We have shown above, moreover, that falsity consists only in the privation which mutilated and confused ideas involve. Therefore a false idea, insofar as it is false, does not involve certainty. So when we say that a man acquiesces in what is false and does not doubt it, we do not on that account say that he is certain, but only that he does not doubt, or that he acquiesces in what is false because there are no causes which bring it about that his imagination fluctuates. On this see the Scholium of Prop. 44 of this Part. However much, therefore, a man may be supposed to adhere to what is false, we shall never say that he is certain. For by certainty we understand something positive (see Prop. 43 of this Part with its Scholium), not the privation of doubt. But by the privation of certainty we understand falsity."}, {"id": "E2P49S", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "Sed ad uberiorem explicationem praeced. prop. quaedam monenda supersunt. Superest deinde, ut ad obiectiones, quae in nostram hanc doctrinam obiici possunt, respondeam; et denique, ut omnem amoveam scrupulum, operae pretium esse duxi, huius doctrinae quasdam utilitates indicare. Quasdam, inquam; nam praecipuae ex iis, quae in P. 5. dicemus, melius intelligentur.", "en": "But for a fuller explanation of the preceding Proposition some things remain to be pointed out. It remains, next, for me to reply to the objections which can be raised against this doctrine of ours; and finally, to remove every scruple, I have thought it worthwhile to indicate certain advantages of this doctrine. Certain advantages, I say; for the chief ones will be better understood from what we shall say in Part V."}, {"id": "E2P49S.2", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "Incipio igitur a primo, lectoresque moneo, ut accurate distinguant inter ideam sive mentis conceptum, et inter imagines rerum, quas imaginamur. Deinde necesse est, ut distinguant inter ideas, et verba, quibus res significamus. Nam quia haec tria, imagines scilicet, verba et ideae a multis vel plane confunduntur, vel non satis accurate, vel denique non satis caute distinguuntur, ideo hanc de voluntate doctrinam, scitu prorsus necessariam, tam ad speculationem, quam ad vitam sapienter instituendam, plane ignorarunt.", "en": "I begin, then, with the first, and I warn my readers to distinguish accurately between an idea, or concept of the mind, and the images of things which we imagine. Next, it is necessary that they distinguish between ideas and the words by which we signify things. For because these three, namely images, words and ideas, are by many either altogether confused, or not distinguished accurately enough, or finally not cautiously enough, they have been altogether ignorant of this doctrine of the will, which it is absolutely necessary to know, both for speculation and for arranging life wisely."}, {"id": "E2P49S.2", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "Quippe qui putant ideas consistere in imaginibus, quae in nobis ex corporum occursu formantur, sibi persuadent, ideas illas rerum, quarum similem nullam imaginem formare possumus, non esse ideas, sed tantum figmenta, quae ex libero voluntatis arbitrio fingimus; ideas igitur veluti picturas in tabula mutas, aspiciunt, et hoc praeiudicio praeoccupati non vident, ideam, quatenus idea est, affirmationem aut negationem involvere. Deinde qui verba confundunt cum idea, vel cum ipsa affirmatione, quam idea involvit, putant se posse contra id, quod sentiunt, velle; quando aliquid solis verbis contra id, quod sentiunt, affirmant aut negant. Haec autem praeiudicia exuere facile is poterit, qui ad naturam cogitationis attendit, quae extensionis conceptum minime involvit; atque adeo clare intelliget, ideam (quandoquidem modus cogitandi est) neque in rei alicuius imagine, neque in verbis consistere. Verborum namque et imaginum essentia a solis motibus corporeis constituitur, qui cogitationis conceptum minime involvunt.", "en": "Indeed, those who think that ideas consist in images which are formed in us from the encounter of bodies persuade themselves that those ideas of things of which we can form no similar image are not ideas, but only fictions which we feign from the free choice of the will; they look on ideas, therefore, as mute pictures on a panel, and, preoccupied by this prejudice, do not see that an idea, insofar as it is an idea, involves affirmation or negation. Next, those who confuse words with the idea, or with the very affirmation which the idea involves, think that they can will contrary to what they feel, when by words alone they affirm or deny something contrary to what they feel. But anyone who attends to the nature of thought, which does not at all involve the concept of extension, will easily be able to put off these prejudices; and so he will understand clearly that an idea (since it is a mode of thinking) consists neither in the image of some thing nor in words. For the essence of words and of images is constituted by bodily motions alone, which do not at all involve the concept of thought."}, {"id": "E2P49S.3", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "Atque haec pauca de his monuisse sufficiat, quare ad praedictas *obiectiones* transeo. Harum prima est, quod constare putant, voluntatem latius se extendere, quam intellectum; atque adeo ab eodem diversam esse. Ratio autem, cur putant, voluntatem latius se extendere quam intellectum, est, quia se experiri aiunt, se non maiore assentiendi sive affirmandi et negandi facultate indigere ad infinitis aliis rebus, quas non percipimus, assentiendum, quam iam habemus; at quidem maiore facultate intelligendi. Distinguitur ergo voluntas ab intellectu, quod finitus hic sit, illa autem infinita.", "en": "Let it suffice to have pointed out these few things about these matters; so I pass to the objections mentioned above. The first of them is that they think it established that the will extends more widely than the intellect, and is therefore different from it. And the reason why they think that the will extends more widely than the intellect is that they say they experience that they do not need a greater faculty of assenting, or of affirming and denying, than we already have, in order to assent to infinitely many other things which we do not perceive; but that we do need a greater faculty of understanding. The will is therefore distinguished from the intellect in that the latter is finite and the former infinite."}, {"id": "E2P49S.3", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "Secundo nobis obiici potest, quod experientia nihil clarius videatur docere, quam quod nostrum iudicium possumus suspendere, ne rebus, quas percipimus, assentiamur; quod hinc etiam confirmatur, quod nemo dicitur decipi, quatenus aliquid percipit, sed tantum, quatenus assentitur aut dissentitur. Ex. gr. qui equum alatum fingit, non ideo concedit dari equum alatum, hoc est, non ideo decipitur, nisi simul concedat, dari equum alatum. Nihil igitur clarius videtur docere experientia, quam quod voluntas sive facultas assentiendi libera sit et a facultate intelligendi diversa.", "en": "Second, it can be objected to us that experience seems to teach nothing more clearly than that we can suspend our judgment so as not to assent to things which we perceive; and this is confirmed also by the fact that no one is said to be deceived insofar as he perceives something, but only insofar as he assents or dissents. For example, he who feigns a winged horse does not on that account grant that there is a winged horse, that is, he is not on that account deceived, unless at the same time he grants that there is a winged horse. Experience seems, therefore, to teach nothing more clearly than that the will, or faculty of assenting, is free, and different from the faculty of understanding."}, {"id": "E2P49S.3", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "Tertio obiici potest, quod una affirmatio non plus realitatis videtur continere, quam alia, hoc est, non maiore potentia indigere videmur ad affirmandum, verum esse id quod verum est, quam ad aliquid quod falsum est verum esse affirmandum. At unam ideam plus realitatis sive perfectionis, quam aliam habere percipimus; quantum enim obiecta alia aliis praestantiora, tantum etiam eorum ideae aliae aliis perfectiores sunt; ex quibus etiam constare videtur differentia inter voluntatem et intellectum.", "en": "Third, it can be objected that one affirmation does not seem to contain more reality than another, that is, we do not seem to need greater power to affirm that what is true is true than to affirm that something which is false is true. But we perceive that one idea has more reality, or perfection, than another; for in proportion as some objects are more excellent than others, so also some of their ideas are more perfect than others; and from this also the difference between will and intellect seems to be established."}, {"id": "E2P49S.3", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "Quarto obiici potest: si homo non operatur ex libertate voluntatis, quid ergo fiet, si in aequilibrio sit, ut Buridani asina? Famene, et siti peribit? Quod si concedam, viderer asinam vel hominis statuam, non hominem concipere; si autem negem, ergo se ipsum determinabit, et consequenter eundi facultatem et faciendi quicquid velit, habet. Praeter haec alia forsan possunt obiici; sed quia inculcare non teneor, quid unusquisque somniare potest, ad has obiectiones tantum respondere curabo, idque quam potero breviter.", "en": "Fourth, it can be objected: if man does not operate from freedom of the will, what then will happen if he is in equilibrium, like Buridan’s ass? Will he perish of hunger and thirst? If I grant this, I would seem to conceive an ass, or a statue of a man, not a man; but if I deny it, then he will determine himself, and consequently has the faculty of going, and of doing whatever he wills. Besides these, other things can perhaps be objected; but since I am not bound to go over whatever anyone can dream up, I shall take care to reply only to these objections, and that as briefly as I can."}, {"id": "E2P49S.3", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "Et quidem ad primam dico, me concedere, voluntatem latius se extendere, quam intellectum, si per intellectum claras tantummodo et distinctas ideas intelligant; sed nego voluntatem latius se extendere, quam perceptiones sive concipiendi facultatem. Nec sane video, cur facultas volendi potius dicenda est infinita, quam sentiendi facultas; sicut enim infinita (unum tamen post aliud; nam infinita simul affirmare non possumus) eadem volendi facultate possumus affirmare, sic etiam infinita corpora (unum nempe post aliud) eadem sentiendi facultate possumus sentire sive percipere. Quod si dicant, infinita dari, quae percipere non possumus? regero, nos ea ipsa nulla cogitatione, et consequenter nulla volendi facultate posse assequi. At dicunt, si Deus vellet efficere, ut ea etiam perciperemus, maiorem quidem facultatem percipiendi deberet nobis dare, sed non maiorem, quam dedit, volendi facultatem; quod idem est, ac si dicerent, quod si Deus velit efficere, ut infinita alia entia intelligeremus, necesse quidem esset, ut nobis daret maiorem intellectum; sed non universaliorem entis ideam, quam dedit, ad eadem infinita entia amplectendum. Ostendimus enim voluntatem ens esse universale sive ideam, qua omnes singulares volitiones, hoc est, id quod iis omnibus commune est, explicamus. Cum itaque hanc omnium volitionum communem sive universalem ideam facultatem esse credant, minime mirum, si hanc facultatem ultra limites intellectus in infinitum se extendere dicant. Universale enim aeque de uno, ac de pluribus ac de infinitis individuis dicitur.", "en": "And indeed to the first I say that I grant that the will extends more widely than the intellect, if by intellect they understand only clear and distinct ideas; but I deny that the will extends more widely than perceptions, or the faculty of conceiving. Nor indeed do I see why the faculty of willing should be called infinite rather than the faculty of sensing; for just as we can affirm infinitely many things by the same faculty of willing (one after another, however, for we cannot affirm infinitely many things at once), so also we can sense, or perceive, infinitely many bodies (one after another, that is) by the same faculty of sensing. If they say that there are infinitely many things which we cannot perceive, I reply that we can reach them by no thought, and consequently by no faculty of willing. But, they say, if God willed to bring it about that we perceived these too, he would indeed have to give us a greater faculty of perceiving, but not a greater faculty of willing than he has given. This is the same as if they said that if God willed to bring it about that we understood infinitely many other beings, it would indeed be necessary that he give us a greater intellect, but not a more universal idea of being than he has given, in order to embrace those same infinitely many beings. For we have shown that the will is a universal being, or idea, by which we explain all singular volitions, that is, that which is common to them all. Since, therefore, they believe that this common, or universal, idea of all volitions is a faculty, it is not at all surprising if they say that this faculty extends itself beyond the limits of the intellect to infinity. For a universal is said equally of one, of many and of infinitely many individuals."}, {"id": "E2P49S.3", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "Ad secundam obiectionem respondeo negando, nos liberam habere potestatem iudicium suspendendi. Nam cum dicimus, aliquem iudicium suspendere, nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod videt, se rem non adaequate percipere. Est igitur iudicii suspensio revera perceptio, et non libera voluntas. Quod ut clare intelligatur, concipiamus puerum equum alatum imaginantem, nec aliud quicquam percipientem. Quandoquidem haec imaginatio equi existentiam involvit (per coroll. prop. 17. huius) nec puer quicquam percipit, quod equi existentiam tollat (ille necessario equum ut praesentem contemplabitur), nec de eius existentia poterit dubitare, quamvis de eadem non sit certus. Atque hoc quotidie in somnis experimur, nec credo aliquem esse, qui putet, se, dum somniat, liberam habere potestatem suspendendi de iis, quae somniat, iudicium, efficiendique ut ea, quae se videre somniat, non somniet; et nihilominus contingit, ut etiam in somnis iudicium suspendamus, nempe cum somniamus, nos somniare.", "en": "To the second objection I reply by denying that we have a free power of suspending judgment. For when we say that someone suspends judgment, we say nothing other than that he sees that he does not perceive the thing adequately. Suspension of judgment, therefore, is in truth a perception, and not free will. That this may be clearly understood, let us conceive a boy imagining a winged horse and perceiving nothing else. Since this imagination involves the existence of the horse (by Cor. of Prop. 17 of this Part), and the boy perceives nothing which excludes the existence of the horse, he will necessarily contemplate the horse as present, nor will he be able to doubt its existence, although he is not certain of it. And this we experience daily in dreams; nor do I believe that there is anyone who thinks that, while he is dreaming, he has a free power of suspending judgment about what he dreams, and of bringing it about that he does not dream what he dreams that he sees; and nevertheless it happens that even in dreams we suspend judgment, namely when we dream that we are dreaming."}, {"id": "E2P49S.3", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "Porro concedo neminem decipi, quatenus percipit, hoc est, mentis imaginationes in se consideratas nihil erroris involvere concedo (vide schol. prop. 17. huius); sed nego, hominem nihil affirmare, quatenus percipit. Nam quid aliud est equum alatum percipere, quam alas de equo affirmare? Si enim mens praeter equum alatum nihil aliud perciperet, eundem sibi praesentem contemplaretur, nec causam haberet ullam dubitandi de eiusdem existentia, nec ullam dissentiendi facultatem, nisi imaginatio equi alati iuncta sit ideae, quae existentiam eiusdem equi tollit, vel quod percipit, ideam equi alati, quam habet, esse inadaequatam, atque tum vel eiusdem equi existentiam necessario negabit, vel de eadem necessario dubitabit.", "en": "Further, I grant that no one is deceived insofar as he perceives, that is, I grant that the imaginations of the mind, considered in themselves, involve no error (see Schol. of Prop. 17 of this Part); but I deny that a man affirms nothing insofar as he perceives. For what else is perceiving a winged horse than affirming wings of a horse? For if the mind perceived nothing else besides the winged horse, it would contemplate it as present to itself, and would have no cause to doubt its existence, nor any faculty of dissenting, unless the imagination of the winged horse is joined to an idea which excludes the existence of the same horse, or it perceives that the idea of the winged horse which it has is inadequate; and then it will either necessarily deny the existence of the horse, or necessarily doubt it."}, {"id": "E2P49S.3", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "Atque his puto me ad tertiam etiam obiectionem respondisse, nempe quod voluntas universale quid sit, quod de omnibus ideis praedicatur, quodque id tantum significat, quod omnibus ideis commune est, nempe affirmationem, cuius propterea adaequata essentia, quatenus sic abstracte concipitur, debet esse in unaquaque idea, et hac ratione tantum in omnibus eadem; sed non quatenus consideratur essentiam ideae constituere; nam eatenus singulares affirmationes aeque inter se differunt, ac ipsae ideae. Ex. gr. affirmatio, quam idea circuli ab illa, quam idea trianguli involvit, aeque differt, ac idea circuli ab idea trianguli. Deinde absolute nego, nos aequali cogitandi potentia indigere ad affirmandum, verum esse id, quod verum est, quam ad affirmandum, verum esse id, quod falsum est. Nam hae duae affirmationes, si mentem spectes, se habent ad invicem, ut ens ad non-ens; nihil enim in ideis positivum est, quod falsitatis formam constituit. Vide prop. 35. huius cum eius schol. et schol. prop. 47. huius. Quare hic apprime venit notandum, quam facile decipimur, quando universalia cum singularibus et entia rationis et abstracta cum realibus confundimus.", "en": "And with this I think I have also replied to the third objection: namely, that the will is something universal, which is predicated of all ideas, and which signifies only what is common to all ideas, namely affirmation, whose adequate essence, therefore, insofar as it is thus conceived abstractly, must be in each idea, and only in this respect the same in all; but not insofar as it is considered to constitute the essence of the idea, for in that respect singular affirmations differ from one another as much as the ideas themselves do. For example, the affirmation which the idea of a circle involves differs from that which the idea of a triangle involves as much as the idea of the circle differs from the idea of the triangle. Next, I absolutely deny that we need an equal power of thinking to affirm that what is true is true as to affirm that what is false is true. For these two affirmations, if you look to the mind, are related to one another as being to not-being; for there is nothing positive in ideas which constitutes the form of falsity. See Prop. 35 of this Part with its Scholium, and the Scholium of Prop. 47 of this Part. Therefore it is especially to be noted here how easily we are deceived when we confuse universals with singulars, and beings of reason and abstractions with real beings."}, {"id": "E2P49S.3", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "Quod denique ad quartam obiectionem attinet, dico, me omnino concedere, quod homo in tali aequilibrio positus (nempe qui nihil aliud percipit, quam sitim et famem, talem cibum et talem potum, qui aeque ab eo distant), fame et siti peribit. Si me rogant, an talis homo non potius asinus, quam homo sit aestimandus? dico me nescire, ut etiam nescio, quanti aestimandus sit ille, qui se pensilem facit, et quanti aestimandi sint pueri, stulti, vesani etc.", "en": "Finally, as regards the fourth objection, I say that I entirely grant that a man placed in such an equilibrium (namely, one who perceives nothing else but thirst and hunger, and such food and such drink, which are equally distant from him) will perish of hunger and thirst. If they ask me whether such a man is not to be reckoned an ass rather than a man, I say that I do not know; just as I also do not know at what to reckon one who hangs himself, or at what to reckon children, fools, madmen, etc."}, {"id": "E2P49S.4", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "Superest tandem indicare, quantum huius doctrinae cognitio ad usum vitae conferat, quod facile ex his animadvertemus. Nempe I. Quatenus docet nos ex solo Dei nutu agere, divinaeque naturae esse participes, et eo magis quo perfectiores actiones agimus et quo magis magisque Deum intelligimus. Haec ergo doctrina, praeterquam quod animum omnimode quietum reddit, hoc etiam habet, quod nos docet, in quo nostra summa felicitas sive beatitudo consistit, nempe in sola Dei cognitione, ex qua ad ea tantum agenda inducimur, quae amor et pietas suadent. Unde clare intelligimus, quantum illi a vera virtutis aestimatione aberrant, qui pro virtute et optimis actionibus, tanquam pro summa servitute, summis praemiis a Deo decorari exspectant, quasi ipsa virtus Deique servitus non esset ipsa felicitas et summa libertas.", "en": "It remains, finally, to indicate how much the knowledge of this doctrine contributes to the conduct of life, which we shall easily see from the following. Namely: I. Insofar as it teaches that we act by God’s bidding alone, and are sharers in the divine nature, and the more so, the more perfect the actions we do and the more and more we understand God. This doctrine, then, besides making the mind entirely at peace, has this also, that it teaches us in what our highest happiness, or blessedness, consists: namely in the knowledge of God alone, by which we are led to do only those things which love and piety urge. From this we understand clearly how far they stray from the true estimate of virtue who, for virtue and the best actions, as if for the utmost servitude, expect to be honoured by God with the highest rewards, as if virtue itself and servitude to God were not happiness itself and the highest freedom."}, {"id": "E2P49S.4", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "II. Quatenus docet, quomodo circa res fortunae, sive quae in nostra potestate non sunt, hoc est, circa res, quae ex nostra natura non sequuntur, nos gerere debeamus; nempe utramque fortunae faciem aequo animo exspectare et ferre; nimirum quia omnia ab aeterno Dei decreto eadem necessitate sequuntur, ac ex essentia trianguli sequitur, quod tres eius anguli sunt aequales duobus rectis.", "en": "II. Insofar as it teaches how we must conduct ourselves with respect to matters of fortune, or things which are not in our power, that is, things which do not follow from our nature: namely, to expect and to bear both faces of fortune with an equal mind; because all things follow from God’s eternal decree with the same necessity as it follows from the essence of a triangle that its three angles are equal to two right angles."}, {"id": "E2P49S.4", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "III. Confert haec doctrina ad vitam socialem, quatenus docet neminem odio habere, contemnere, irridere, nemini irasci, invidere. Praeterea quatenus docet, ut unusquisque suis sit contentus et proximo auxilio; non ex muliebri misericordia, partialitate, neque superstitione, sed ex solo rationis ductu, prout scilicet tempus et res postulat, ut in quarta parte ostendam.", "en": "III. This doctrine contributes to social life, insofar as it teaches us to hate no one, to despise no one, to mock no one, to be angry with no one, to envy no one. Further, insofar as it teaches that each should be content with his own and a help to his neighbour, not from womanish compassion, partiality or superstition, but from the guidance of reason alone, namely as the time and the matter require, as I shall show in Part IV."}, {"id": "E2P49S.4", "p": "part2", "l": "2P49S", "la": "IV. Denique confert etiam haec doctrina non parum ad communem societatem, quatenus docet, qua ratione cives gubernandi sint et ducendi, nempe non ut serviant, sed ut libere ea quae optima sunt, agant. Atque his, quae in hoc schol. agere constitueram, absolvi et eo finem huic nostrae secundae parti impono, in qua puto me naturam mentis humanae eiusque proprietates satis prolixe, et quantum rei difficultas fert, clare explicuisse, atque talia tradidisse, ex quibus multa praeclara, maxime utilia et cognitu necessaria concludi possunt, ut partim ex sequentibus constabit.", "en": "IV. Finally, this doctrine also contributes not a little to the common society, insofar as it teaches in what way citizens are to be governed and led: namely, not so that they may be slaves, but so that they may freely do the things which are best. And with this I have completed what I had resolved to treat in this Scholium, and with it I put an end to this our Second Part, in which I think I have explained the nature of the human mind and its properties at sufficient length and, as far as the difficulty of the matter allows, clearly; and have set out things from which many excellent things, highly useful and necessary to know, can be concluded, as will be established partly from what follows."}, {"id": "E3Pref", "p": "part3", "l": "3Pref", "la": "Plerique qui de *affectibus* et *hominum vivendi ratione* scripserunt, videntur non de rebus naturalibus, quae communes naturae leges sequuntur, sed de rebus, quae extra naturam sunt, agere. Imo hominem in natura, veluti imperium in imperio, concipere videntur. Nam hominem naturae ordinem magis perturbare, quam sequi, ipsumque in suas actiones absolutam habere potentiam, nec aliunde, quam a se ipso determinari credunt. Humanae deinde impotentiae et inconstantiae causam non communi naturae potentiae, sed nescio cui naturae humanae vitio tribuunt, quam propterea flent, rident, contemnunt vel quod plerumque fit, detestantur; et qui humanae mentis impotentiam eloquentius vel argutius carpere novit, veluti divinus habetur.", "en": "Most of those who have written about the affects and about men’s way of living seem to treat not of natural things, which follow the common laws of nature, but of things which are outside nature. Indeed, they seem to conceive man in nature as a dominion within a dominion. For they believe that man disturbs the order of nature rather than follows it, that he has absolute power over his actions, and that he is determined by nothing other than himself. Next, they attribute the cause of human lack of power and inconstancy not to the common power of nature, but to I know not what vice of human nature, which they therefore weep over, laugh at, despise, or, as usually happens, detest; and he who knows how to censure the lack of power of the human mind more eloquently or more cleverly is regarded as if he were divine."}, {"id": "E3Pref", "p": "part3", "l": "3Pref", "la": "Non defuerunt tamen viri praestantissimi (quorum labori et industriae nos multum debere fatemur), qui de recta vivendi ratione praeclara multa scripserint et plena prudentiae consilia mortalibus dederint; verum affectuum naturam et vires, et quid contra mens in iisdem moderandis possit, nemo, quod sciam, determinavit. Scio equidem celeberrimum *Cartesium*, licet etiam crediderit, mentem in suas actiones absolutam habere potentiam, affectus tamen humanos per primas suas causas explicare, simulque viam ostendere studuisse, qua mens in affectus absolutum habere possit imperium; sed mea quidem sententia nihil praeter magni sui ingenii acumen ostendit, ut suo loco demonstrabo. Nam ad illos revertere volo, qui hominum affectus et actiones detestari vel ridere malunt, quam intelligere.", "en": "There have not been lacking, however, very eminent men (to whose labour and industry we confess that we owe much) who have written many excellent things about the right way of living and have given mortals counsels full of prudence; but no one, so far as I know, has determined the nature and powers of the affects, and what, on the other hand, the mind can do in moderating them. I know, indeed, that the most celebrated Descartes, although he too believed that the mind has absolute power over its actions, nevertheless tried to explain human affects through their first causes, and at the same time to show the way by which the mind can have absolute dominion over the affects; but in my opinion he showed nothing but the acumen of his great ingenuity, as I shall demonstrate in its place. For I want to return to those who prefer to detest or laugh at the affects and actions of men rather than to understand them."}, {"id": "E3Pref", "p": "part3", "l": "3Pref", "la": "His sine dubio mirum videbitur, quod hominum vitia et ineptias more geometrico tractare aggrediar, et certa ratione demonstrare velim ea, quae rationi repugnare, quaeque vana, absurda et horrenda esse clamitant. Sed mea haec est ratio. Nihil in natura fit, quod ipsius vitio possit tribui; est namque natura semper eadem et ubique una eademque eius virtus et agendi potentia, hoc est, naturae leges et regulae, secundum quas omnia fiunt et ex unis formis in alias mutantur, sunt ubique et semper eaedem, atque adeo una eademque etiam debet esse ratio rerum qualiumcumque naturam intelligendi, nempe per leges et regulas naturae universales. Affectus itaque odii, irae, invidiae etc. in se considerati ex eadem naturae necessitate et virtute consequuntur, ac reliqua singularia; ac proinde certas causas agnoscunt, per quas intelliguntur, certasque proprietates habent, cognitione nostra aeque dignas ac proprietates cuiuscumque alterius rei cuius sola contemplatione delectamur. De *affectuum* itaque *natura et viribus* ac *mentis in eosdem potentia* eadem methodo agam qua in praecedentibus de *Deo* et *mente* egi, et humanas actiones atque appetitus considerabo perinde, ac si quaestio de lineis, planis aut de corporibus esset.", "en": "To them it will doubtless seem strange that I undertake to treat men’s vices and follies in the geometrical manner, and that I wish to demonstrate by sure reasoning things which they proclaim to be contrary to reason, vain, absurd and horrible. But my reasoning is this. Nothing happens in nature which can be attributed to a vice of nature; for nature is always the same, and its virtue and power of acting are everywhere one and the same; that is, the laws and rules of nature, according to which all things happen and change from one form into another, are always and everywhere the same; and so there must also be one and the same way of understanding the nature of things of any kind whatever, namely through the universal laws and rules of nature. The affects, therefore, of hatred, anger, envy, etc., considered in themselves, follow from the same necessity and virtue of nature as other singular things; and accordingly they acknowledge certain causes, through which they are understood, and have certain properties, as worthy of our knowledge as the properties of any other thing by the mere contemplation of which we are delighted. I shall treat, therefore, of the nature and powers of the affects, and of the power of the mind over them, by the same method by which I treated of God and the mind in the preceding Parts, and I shall consider human actions and appetites just as if the question were of lines, planes or bodies."}, {"id": "E3D1", "p": "part3", "l": "3D1", "la": "*Causam adaequatam* appello eam, cuius effectus potest clare et distincte per eandem percipi. *Inadaequatam* autem seu *partialem* illam voco, cuius effectus per ipsam solam intelligi nequit.", "en": "I call that an adequate cause whose effect can be perceived clearly and distinctly through it. But I call that inadequate, or partial, whose effect cannot be understood through it alone."}, {"id": "E3D2", "p": "part3", "l": "3D2", "la": "Nos tum *agere* dico cum aliquid in nobis aut extra nos fit, cuius adaequata sumus causa hoc est (per defin. praeced.) cum ex nostra natura aliquid in nobis aut extra nos sequitur, quod per eandem solam potest clare et distincte intelligi. At contra nos *pati* dico, cum in nobis aliquid fit vel ex nostra natura aliquid sequitur, cuius nos non nisi partialis sumus causa.", "en": "I say that we act when something happens, in us or outside us, of which we are the adequate cause, that is (by the preceding Def.), when something follows from our nature, in us or outside us, which can be clearly and distinctly understood through it alone. On the other hand, I say that we are acted on when something happens in us, or something follows from our nature, of which we are only a partial cause."}, {"id": "E3D3", "p": "part3", "l": "3D3", "la": "Per *affectum* intelligo corporis affectiones, quibus ipsius corporis agendi potentia augetur vel minuitur, iuvatur vel coercetur, et simul harum affectionum ideas.", "en": "By affect I understand affections of the body by which the body’s power of acting is increased or diminished, aided or restrained, and at the same time the ideas of these affections."}, {"id": "E3D3Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3D3Ex", "la": "Si itaque alicuius harum affectionum adaequata possimus esse causa, tum per affectum *actionem* intelligo; alias *passionem.*", "en": "If, therefore, we can be the adequate cause of one of these affections, then by the affect I understand an action; otherwise a passion."}, {"id": "E3Post1", "p": "part3", "l": "3Post1", "la": "Corpus humanum potest multis affici modis, quibus ipsius agendi potentia augetur vel minuitur, et etiam aliis qui eiusdem agendi potentiam nec maiorem nec minorem reddunt.", "en": "The human body can be affected in many ways by which its power of acting is increased or diminished, and also in others which render its power of acting neither greater nor less."}, {"id": "E3Post1.2", "p": "part3", "l": "3Post1", "la": "Hoc postulatum seu axioma nititur postul. 1. et lem. 5. et 7., quae vide post prop. 13. P. 2.", "en": "This postulate, or axiom, rests on Post. 1 and Lemmas 5 and 7, which see after Prop. 13 of Part II."}, {"id": "E3Post2", "p": "part3", "l": "3Post2", "la": "Corpus humanum multas pati potest mutationes, et nihilominus retinere obiectorum impressiones seu vestigia (de quibus vide postul. 5. P. 2.), et consequenter easdem rerum imagines; quarum defin. vide schol. prop. 17. P. 2.", "en": "The human body can undergo many changes and nevertheless retain the impressions, or traces, of objects (on which see Post. 5 of Part II), and consequently the same images of things; for the definition of which see the Schol. of Prop. 17 of Part II."}, {"id": "E3P1", "p": "part3", "l": "3P1", "la": "Mens nostra quaedam agit, quaedam vero patitur; nempe quatenus adaequatas habet ideas, eatenus quaedam necessario agit, et quatenus ideas habet inadaequatas, eatenus necessario quaedam patitur.", "en": "Our mind acts in some things and is acted on in others; namely, insofar as it has adequate ideas, it necessarily acts in some things, and insofar as it has inadequate ideas, it is necessarily acted on in some things."}, {"id": "E3P1D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P1D", "la": "Cuiuscumque humanae mentis ideae aliae adaequatae sunt, aliae autem mutilatae et confusae (per schol. prop. 40. P. 2.). Ideae autem, quae in alicuius mente sunt adaequatae, sunt in Deo adaequatae, quatenus eiusdem mentis essentiam constituit (per coroll. prop. 11. P. 2.), et quae deinde inadaequatae sunt in mente, sunt etiam in Deo (per idem coroll.) adaequatae, non quatenus eiusdem solummodo mentis essentiam, sed etiam quatenus aliarum rerum mentes in se simul continet. Deinde ex data quacumque idea aliquis effectus sequi necessario debet (per prop. 36. P. 1.), cuius effectus Deus causa est adaequata (vide defin. 1. huius), non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus data illa idea affectus consideratur (vide prop. 9. P. 2.). At eius effectus, cuius Deus est causa, quatenus affectus est idea, quae in alicuius mente est adaequata, illa eadem mens est causa adaequata (per coroll. prop. 11. P. 2.). Ergo mens nostra (per defin. 2. huius) quatenus ideas habet adaequatas, quaedam necessario agit; quod erat primum. Deinde quicquid necessario sequitur ex idea, quae in Deo est adaequata, non quatenus mentem unius hominis tantum, sed quatenus aliarum rerum mentes simul cum eiusdem hominis mente in se habet, eius (per idem coroll. prop. 11 P. 2.) illius hominis mens non est causa adaequata, sed partialis. Ac proinde (per defin. 2. huius) mens quatenus ideas inadaequatas habet, quaedam necessario patitur; quod erat secundum. Ergo mens nostra etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "Of each human mind some ideas are adequate, others mutilated and confused (by the Schol. of Prop. 40 of Part II). But the ideas which are adequate in someone’s mind are adequate in God insofar as he constitutes the essence of that mind (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of Part II); and those which are inadequate in the mind are also adequate in God (by the same Corollary), not insofar as he contains in himself the essence of that mind only, but also insofar as he contains in himself the minds of other things at the same time. Next, from any given idea some effect must necessarily follow (by Prop. 36 of Part I), of which effect God is the adequate cause (see Def. 1 of this Part), not insofar as he is infinite, but insofar as he is considered as affected by that given idea (see Prop. 9 of Part II). But of that effect of which God is the cause insofar as he is affected by an idea which is adequate in someone’s mind, that same mind is the adequate cause (by Cor. of Prop. 11 of Part II). Therefore our mind (by Def. 2 of this Part), insofar as it has adequate ideas, necessarily acts in some things; which was the first point. Next, whatever necessarily follows from an idea which is adequate in God, not insofar as he has in himself the mind of one man only, but insofar as he has in himself the minds of other things together with the mind of that man, of that (by the same Cor. of Prop. 11 of Part II) the man’s mind is not the adequate cause, but a partial cause. And therefore (by Def. 2 of this Part) the mind, insofar as it has inadequate ideas, is necessarily acted on in some things; which was the second point. Therefore our mind, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P1C", "p": "part3", "l": "3P1C", "la": "Hinc sequitur mentem eo pluribus passionibus esse obnoxiam, quo plures ideas inadaequatas habet, et contra eo plura agere, quo plures habet adaequatas.", "en": "From this it follows that the mind is subject to more passions the more inadequate ideas it has, and, on the other hand, acts more the more adequate ideas it has."}, {"id": "E3P2", "p": "part3", "l": "3P2", "la": "Nec corpus mentem ad cogitandum, nec mens corpus ad motum, neque ad quietem, nec ad aliquid (si quid est) aliud determinare potest.", "en": "The body cannot determine the mind to thinking, nor can the mind determine the body to motion, or to rest, or to anything else (if there is anything else)."}, {"id": "E3P2D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P2D", "la": "Omnes cogitandi modi Deum, quatenus res est cogitans et non quatenus alio attributo explicatur, pro causa habent (per prop. 6. P. 2.). Id ergo, quod mentem ad cogitandum determinat, modus cogitandi est, et non extensionis, hoc est (per defin. 1. P. 2.) non est corpus; quod erat primum. Corporis deinde motus et quies ab alio oriri debet corpore, quod etiam ad motum vel quietem determinatum fuit ab alio, et absolute quicquid in corpore oritur, id a Deo oriri debuit, quatenus aliquo extensionis modo et non quatenus aliquo cogitandi modo affectus consideratur (per eandem prop. 6. P. 2.), hoc est, a mente, quae (per prop. 11. P. 2.) modus cogitandi est, oriri non potest; quod erat secundum. Ergo nec corpus mentem etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "All modes of thinking have God for their cause insofar as he is a thinking thing, and not insofar as he is explained by another attribute (by Prop. 6 of Part II). What determines the mind to thinking, therefore, is a mode of thinking and not of extension, that is (by Def. 1 of Part II), it is not a body; which was the first point. Next, the motion and rest of a body must arise from another body, which was also determined to motion or rest by another; and absolutely, whatever arises in a body must have arisen from God insofar as he is considered as affected by some mode of extension, and not insofar as he is considered as affected by some mode of thinking (by the same Prop. 6 of Part II); that is, it cannot arise from the mind, which (by Prop. 11 of Part II) is a mode of thinking; which was the second point. Therefore neither can the body determine the mind, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P2S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P2S", "la": "Haec clarius intelliguntur ex iis, quae in schol. prop. 7. P. 2. dicta sunt, quod scilicet mens et corpus una eademque res sit, quae iam sub cogitationis, iam sub extensionis attributo concipitur. Unde fit, ut ordo sive rerum concatenatio una sit, sive natura sub hoc sive sub illo attributo concipiatur, consequenter ut ordo actionum et passionum corporis nostri simul sit natura cum ordine actionum et passionum mentis. Quod etiam patet ex modo, quo prop. 12. P. 2. demonstravimus.", "en": "These things are understood more clearly from what was said in the Scholium of Prop. 7 of Part II, namely that the mind and the body are one and the same thing, which is conceived now under the attribute of thought, now under the attribute of extension. Hence it comes about that the order, or connection, of things is one, whether nature is conceived under this attribute or under that; and consequently that the order of actions and passions of our body is simultaneous in nature with the order of actions and passions of the mind. This is also evident from the way in which we demonstrated Prop. 12 of Part II."}, {"id": "E3P2S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P2S", "la": "At quamvis haec ita se habeant, ut nulla dubitandi ratio supersit, vix tamen credo, nisi rem experientia comprobavero, homines induci posse ad haec aequo animo perpendendum; adeo firmiter persuasi sunt, corpus ex solo mentis nutu iam moveri, iam quiescere, plurimaque agere, quae a sola mentis voluntate et excogitandi arte pendent. Etenim quid corpus possit, nemo hucusque determinavit, hoc est, neminem hucusque experientia docuit, quid corpus ex solis legibus naturae quatenus corporea tantum consideratur, possit agere, et quid non possit, nisi a mente determinetur. Nam nemo hucusque corporis fabricam tam accurate novit, ut omnes eius functiones potuerit explicare, ut iam taceam quod in brutis plura observentur, quae humanam sagacitatem longe superant, et quod somnambuli in somnis plurima agant, quae vigilando non auderent; quod satis ostendit, ipsum corpus ex solis suae naturae legibus multa posse, quae ipsius mens admiratur. Deinde nemo scit, qua ratione quibusve mediis mens moveat corpus, neque quot motus gradus possit corpori tribuere, quantaque cum celeritate idem movere queat. Unde sequitur, cum homines dicunt, hanc vel illam actionem corporis oriri a mente, quae imperium in corpus habet, eos nescire, quid dicant, nec aliud agere, quam speciosis verbis fateri, se veram illius actionis causam absque admiratione ignorare.", "en": "But although these things are so, so that no reason for doubting remains, I hardly believe that men can be induced to weigh them with an equal mind unless I confirm the matter by experience; so firmly are they persuaded that the body now moves, now is at rest, at the mind’s bidding alone, and does a great many things which depend only on the will of the mind and its art of thinking things out. For what the body can do, no one has so far determined; that is, experience has so far taught no one what the body can do from the laws of nature alone, insofar as nature is considered only as corporeal, and what it cannot do unless it is determined by the mind. For no one has so far known the structure of the body so accurately as to be able to explain all its functions, not to mention that many things are observed in brutes which far surpass human ingenuity, and that sleepwalkers do a great many things in their sleep which they would not dare to do awake; which shows well enough that the body itself, from the laws of its own nature alone, can do many things at which its mind wonders. Next, no one knows in what way, or by what means, the mind moves the body, nor how many degrees of motion it can give to the body, and with what speed it can move it. From this it follows that when men say that this or that action of the body arises from the mind, which has dominion over the body, they do not know what they are saying, and do nothing but confess, in fine-sounding words, that they are ignorant of the true cause of that action, and do not wonder at it."}, {"id": "E3P2S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P2S", "la": "At dicent, sive sciant sive nesciant, quibus mediis mens moveat corpus, se tamen experiri, quod nisi mens humana apta esset ad excogitandum, corpus iners esset; deinde se experiri, in sola mentis potestate esse, tam loqui quam tacere, et alia multa, quae proinde a mentis decreto pendere credunt. Sed quod ad primum attinet, ipsos rogo, num experientia non etiam doceat, quod si contra corpus iners sit, mens simul ad cogitandum sit inepta? Nam cum corpus somno quiescit, mens simul cum ipso sopita manet, nec potestatem habet, veluti cum vigilat, excogitandi. Deinde omnes expertos esse credo, mentem non semper aeque aptam esse ad cogitandum de eodem obiecto; sed prout corpus aptius est, ut in eo huius vel illius obiecti imago excitetur, ita mentem aptiorem esse ad hoc vel illud obiectum contemplandum. At dicent ex solis legibus naturae, quatenus corporea tantum consideratur, fieri non posse, ut causae aedificiorum, picturarum rerumque huiusmodi, quae sola humana arte fiunt, possint deduci, nec corpus humanum, nisi a mente determinaretur ducereturque, pote esset ad templum aliquod aedificandum. Verum ego iam ostendi, ipsos nescire, quid corpus possit, quidve ex sola ipsius naturae contemplatione possit deduci, ipsosque plurima experiri ex solis naturae legibus fieri, quae nunquam credidissent posse fieri, nisi ex mentis directione, ut sunt ea, quae somnambuli in somnis agunt, quaeque ipsi, dum vigilant, admirantur. Addo hic ipsam corporis humani fabricam, quae artificio longissime superat omnes, quae humana arte fabricatae sunt, ut iam taceam, quod supra ostenderim, ex natura sub quovis attributo considerata infinita sequi.", "en": "But they will say that, whether or not they know by what means the mind moves the body, they nevertheless experience that unless the human mind were fit for thinking things out, the body would be inactive; and next, that they experience that it is in the power of the mind alone both to speak and to be silent, and many other things, which they therefore believe depend on the mind’s decree. But as regards the first, I ask them whether experience does not also teach that if, on the other hand, the body is inactive, the mind is at the same time unfit for thinking? For when the body is at rest in sleep, the mind remains asleep together with it, and does not have the power of thinking things out, as it does when awake. Next, I believe everyone has experienced that the mind is not always equally fit for thinking about the same object, but that as the body is more fit for the image of this or that object to be aroused in it, so the mind is more fit to contemplate this or that object. But they will say that it cannot be that the causes of buildings, pictures and things of this kind, which are made by human art alone, could be deduced from the laws of nature alone, insofar as it is considered only as corporeal; nor would the human body, unless it were determined and guided by the mind, be able to build a temple. But I have already shown that they do not know what the body can do, or what can be deduced from the contemplation of its nature alone, and that they experience a great many things happening from the laws of nature alone which they would never have believed could happen except by the direction of the mind, such as the things sleepwalkers do in their sleep, at which they themselves wonder when they are awake. I add here the very structure of the human body, which in artfulness far surpasses all the things constructed by human art; not to mention that I have shown above that infinitely many things follow from nature, under whatever attribute it is considered."}, {"id": "E3P2S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P2S", "la": "Quod porro ad secundum attinet, sane longe felicius sese res humanae haberent, si aeque in hominis potestate esset tam tacere, quam loqui. At experientia satis superque docet, homines nihil minus in potestate habere quam linguam, nec minus posse, quam appetitus moderari suos. Unde factum, ut plerique credant, nos ea tantum libere agere, quae leviter petimus, quia earum rerum appetitus facile contrahi potest memoria alterius rei, cuius frequenter recordamur; sed illa minime, quae magno cum affectu petimus et qui alterius rei memoria sedari nequit. Verumenimvero nisi experti essent, nos plura agere quorum postea poenitet, nosque saepe, quando scilicet contrariis affectibus conflictamur, meliora videre et deteriora sequi, nihil impediret, quominus crederent nos omnia libere agere. Sic infans se lac libere appetere credit, puer autem iratus vindictam velle et timidus fugam. Ebrius deinde credit, se ex libero mentis decreto ea loqui, quae postea sobrius vellet tacuisse. Sic delirans, garrula, puer et huius farinae plurimi ex libero mentis decreto credunt loqui, cum tamen loquendi impetum, quem habent, continere nequeant, ita ut ipsa experientia non minus clare, quam ratio doceat, quod homines ea sola de causa liberos se esse credant, quia suarum actionum sunt conscii et causarum, a quibus determinantur, ignari; et praeterea quod mentis decreta nihil sint praeter ipsos appetitus, quae propterea varia sunt pro varia corporis dispositione. Nam unusquisque ex suo affectu omnia moderatur, et qui praeterea contrariis affectibus conflictantur, quid velint, nesciunt; qui autem nullo, facili momento huc atque illuc pelluntur.", "en": "As regards the second, human affairs would certainly go much more happily if it were equally in man’s power to be silent as to speak. But experience teaches more than enough that men have nothing less in their power than their tongue, and can do nothing less than moderate their appetites. That is why most believe that we do freely only those things which we seek lightly, because the appetite for these things can easily be reduced by the memory of another thing which we frequently recollect; but by no means those things which we seek with a great affect, one which cannot be calmed by the memory of another thing. But in truth, if they had not experienced that we do many things which we afterwards regret, and that often, namely when we are torn by contrary affects, we see the better and follow the worse, nothing would prevent them from believing that we do all things freely. So the infant believes that it freely wants milk, the angry boy that he wants vengeance, and the timid one flight. The drunk, again, believes that he says from a free decree of the mind what afterwards, when sober, he would wish he had kept silent about. So the delirious man, the chattering woman, the child and very many of this stamp believe that they speak from a free decree of the mind, although they cannot contain the impulse to speak which they have; so that experience itself teaches no less clearly than reason that men believe themselves free for this reason only, that they are conscious of their actions and ignorant of the causes by which they are determined; and, moreover, that the decrees of the mind are nothing but the appetites themselves, which therefore vary according to the varying disposition of the body. For each moderates everything from his own affect, and those who, besides, are torn by contrary affects do not know what they want; while those who are torn by none are driven this way and that by the slightest impulse."}, {"id": "E3P2S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P2S", "la": "Quae omnia profecto clare ostendunt, mentis tam decretum, quam appetitum et corporis determinationem simul esse natura, vel potius unam eandemque rem, quam, quando sub cogitationis attributo consideratur et per ipsum explicatur, *decretum* appellamus, et quando sub extensionis attributo consideratur et ex legibus motus et quietis deducitur, *determinationem* vocamus; quod adhuc clarius ex iam dicendis patebit. Nam aliud est, quod hic apprime notari vellem, nempe quod nos nihil ex mentis decreto agere possumus, nisi eius recordemur. Ex. gr. non possumus verbum loqui, nisi eiusdem recordemur. Deinde in libera mentis potestate non est, rei alicuius recordari vel eiusdem oblivisci. Quare hoc tantum in mentis potestate esse creditur, quod rem, cuius recordamur, vel tacere vel loqui ex solo mentis decreto possumus. Verum cum nos loqui somniamus, credimus nos ex libero mentis decreto loqui, nec tamen loquimur, vel si loquimur, id ex corporis spontaneo motu fit. Somniamus deinde, nos quaedam homines celare, idque eodem mentis decreto, quo, dum vigilamus, ea quae scimus tacemus. Somniamus denique, nos ex mentis decreto quaedam agere, quae, dum vigilamus, non audemus; atque adeo pervelim scire, an in mente duo decretorum genera dentur, phantasticorum unum et liberorum alterum? Quod si eo usque insanire non libet, necessario concedendum est, hoc mentis decretum, quod liberum esse creditur, ab ipsa imaginatione sive memoria non distingui, nec aliud esse praeter illam affirmationem, quam idea, quatenus idea est, necessario involvit. Vide prop. 49. P. 2. Atque adeo haec mentis decreta eadem necessitate in mente oriuntur, ac ideae rerum actu existentium. Qui igitur credunt, se ex libero mentis decreto loqui vel tacere vel quicquam agere, oculis apertis somniant.", "en": "All these things certainly show clearly that both the decree of the mind and the appetite and determination of the body are simultaneous in nature, or rather are one and the same thing, which we call a decree when it is considered under the attribute of thought and explained through it, and a determination when it is considered under the attribute of extension and deduced from the laws of motion and rest; which will be still more clearly evident from what is to be said now. For there is something else which I should like to be especially noted here, namely that we can do nothing from a decree of the mind unless we recollect it. For example, we cannot speak a word unless we recollect it. Next, it is not in the free power of the mind to recollect a thing or to forget it. That is why it is believed that only this is in the power of the mind: that we can, from the mind’s decree alone, either be silent about or speak of a thing which we recollect. But when we dream that we speak, we believe that we speak from a free decree of the mind, and yet we do not speak, or, if we do speak, it happens from a spontaneous motion of the body. We dream, next, that we conceal certain things from men, and that by the same decree of the mind by which, while awake, we are silent about the things we know. We dream, finally, that we do from a decree of the mind certain things which we do not dare to do while awake; and so I should very much like to know whether there are in the mind two kinds of decrees, the fantastic and the free. But if one does not wish to go so far in madness, it must necessarily be granted that this decree of the mind which is believed to be free is not distinguished from the imagination itself, or memory, and is nothing but that affirmation which an idea, insofar as it is an idea, necessarily involves. See Prop. 49 of Part II. And so these decrees of the mind arise in the mind with the same necessity as the ideas of things actually existing. Those, therefore, who believe that they speak or are silent or do anything from a free decree of the mind dream with their eyes open."}, {"id": "E3P3", "p": "part3", "l": "3P3", "la": "Mentis actiones ex solis ideis adaequatis oriuntur, passiones autem a solis inadaequatis pendent.", "en": "The actions of the mind arise from adequate ideas alone; but the passions depend on inadequate ideas alone."}, {"id": "E3P3D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P3D", "la": "Primum, quod mentis essentiam constituit, nihil aliud est, quam idea corporis actu existentis (per prop. 11. et 13. P. 2.), quae (per prop. 15. P. 2.) ex multis aliis componitur, quarum quaedam (per coroll. prop. 38. P. 2.) sunt adaequatae, quaedam autem inadaequatae (per coroll. prop. 29. P. 2.). Quicquid ergo ex mentis natura sequitur et cuius mens causa est proxima, per quam id debet intelligi, necessario ex idea adaequata vel inadaequata sequi debet. At quatenus mens (per prop. 1. huius) ideas habet inadaequatas, eatenus necessario patitur. Ergo mentis actiones ex solis ideis adaequatis sequuntur, et mens propterea tantum patitur quia ideas habet inadaequatas. Q. E. D.", "en": "The first thing that constitutes the essence of the mind is nothing but the idea of a body actually existing (by Props. 11 and 13 of Part II), which (by Prop. 15 of Part II) is composed of many other ideas, of which some (by Cor. of Prop. 38 of Part II) are adequate, others inadequate (by Cor. of Prop. 29 of Part II). Whatever, therefore, follows from the nature of the mind, and of which the mind is the proximate cause through which it must be understood, must necessarily follow from an adequate or an inadequate idea. But insofar as the mind (by Prop. 1 of this Part) has inadequate ideas, it is necessarily acted on. Therefore the actions of the mind follow from adequate ideas alone, and the mind is acted on only because it has inadequate ideas. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P3S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P3S", "la": "Videmus itaque passiones ad mentem non referri, nisi quatenus aliquid habet, quod negationem involvit, sive quatenus consideratur ut naturae pars, quae per se absque aliis non potest clare et distincte percipi; et hac ratione ostendere possem, passiones eodem modo ad res singulares, ac ad mentem referri, nec alia ratione posse percipi. Sed meum institutum est, de sola mente humana agere.", "en": "We see, therefore, that passions are not related to the mind except insofar as it has something which involves negation, or insofar as it is considered as a part of nature which cannot be perceived clearly and distinctly through itself without others; and in this way I could show that passions are related to singular things in the same way as to the mind, and cannot be perceived in any other way. But my purpose is to treat only of the human mind."}, {"id": "E3P4", "p": "part3", "l": "3P4", "la": "Nulla res nisi a causa externa potest destrui.", "en": "No thing can be destroyed except by an external cause."}, {"id": "E3P4D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P4D", "la": "Haec propositio per se patet. Definitio enim cuiuscumque rei ipsius rei essentiam affirmat, sed non negat; sive rei essentiam ponit, sed non tollit. Dum itaque ad rem ipsam tantum, non autem ad causas externas attendimus, nihil in eadem poterimus invenire, quod ipsam possit destruere. Q. E. D.", "en": "This Proposition is evident through itself. For the definition of any thing affirms the essence of the thing itself, but does not deny it; or it posits the essence of the thing, but does not take it away. So while we attend only to the thing itself, and not to external causes, we shall be able to find nothing in it which could destroy it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P5", "p": "part3", "l": "3P5", "la": "Res eatenus contrariae sunt naturae, hoc est, eatenus in eodem subiecto esse nequeunt, quatenus una alteram potest destruere.", "en": "Things are of a contrary nature, that is, cannot be in the same subject, insofar as one can destroy the other."}, {"id": "E3P5D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P5D", "la": "Si enim inter se convenire vel in eodem subiecto simul esse possent, posset ergo in eodem subiecto aliquid dari, quod ipsum posset destruere, quod (per prop. praeced.) est absurdum. Ergo res etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "For if they could agree with one another, or be in the same subject at the same time, then something could be in the same subject which could destroy it, which (by the preceding Prop.) is absurd. Therefore things, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P6", "p": "part3", "l": "3P6", "la": "Unaquaeque res, quantum in se est, in suo esse perseverare conatur.", "en": "Each thing, as far as it is in itself, strives to persevere in its being."}, {"id": "E3P6D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P6D", "la": "Res enim singulares modi sunt, quibus Dei attributa certo et determinato modo exprimuntur (per coroll. prop. 25. P. 1.), hoc est (per prop. 34. P. 1.) res, quae Dei potentiam qua Deus est et agit, certo et determinato modo exprimunt. Neque ulla res aliquid in se habet, a quo possit destrui, sive quod eius existentiam tollat (per prop. 4. huius); sed contra ei omni, quod eiusdem existentiam potest tollere, opponitur (per prop. praeced.). Adeoque quantum potest et in se est, in suo esse perseverare conatur. Q. E. D.", "en": "For singular things are modes by which God’s attributes are expressed in a certain and determinate way (by Cor. of Prop. 25 of Part I), that is (by Prop. 34 of Part I), things which express in a certain and determinate way the power of God by which God is and acts. And no thing has in itself anything by which it could be destroyed, or which takes its existence away (by Prop. 4 of this Part); on the contrary, it is opposed to everything which can take its existence away (by the preceding Prop.). And so, as far as it can and is in itself, it strives to persevere in its being. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P7", "p": "part3", "l": "3P7", "la": "Conatus, quo unaquaeque res in suo esse perseverare conatur, nihil est praeter ipsius rei actualem essentiam.", "en": "The striving by which each thing strives to persevere in its being is nothing but the actual essence of the thing itself."}, {"id": "E3P7D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P7D", "la": "Ex data cuiuscumque rei essentia quaedam necessario sequuntur (per prop. 36. P. 1.), nec res aliud possunt, quam id quod ex determinata earum natura necessario sequitur (per prop. 29. P. 1.). Quare cuiuscumque rei potentia sive conatus, quo ipsa vel sola vel cum aliis quidquam agit, vel agere conatur, hoc est (per prop. 6. huius) potentia sive conatus, quo in suo esse perseverare conatur, nihil est praeter ipsius rei datam sive actualem essentiam. Q. E. D.", "en": "From the given essence of any thing certain things necessarily follow (by Prop. 36 of Part I), and things can do nothing but what necessarily follows from their determinate nature (by Prop. 29 of Part I). Therefore the power of any thing, or the striving by which it, either alone or with others, does anything or strives to do anything, that is (by Prop. 6 of this Part), the power, or striving, by which it strives to persevere in its being, is nothing but the given, or actual, essence of the thing itself. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P8", "p": "part3", "l": "3P8", "la": "Conatus, quo unaquaeque res in suo esse perseverare conatur, nullum tempus finitum, sed indefinitum involvit.", "en": "The striving by which each thing strives to persevere in its being involves no finite time, but an indefinite time."}, {"id": "E3P8D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P8D", "la": "Si enim tempus limitatum involveret, quod rei durationem determinaret, tum ex sola ipsa potentia, qua res existit, sequeretur, quod res post limitatum illud tempus non posset existere, sed quod deberet destrui. Atqui hoc (per prop. 4. huius) est absurdum. Ergo conatus, quo res existit, nullum tempus definitum involvit, sed contra, quoniam (per eandem prop. 4. huius) si a nulla externa causa destruatur, eadem potentia, qua iam existit, existere perget semper, ergo hic conatus tempus indefinitum involvit. Q. E. D.", "en": "For if it involved a limited time, which would determine the duration of the thing, then it would follow from the very power alone by which the thing exists that the thing could not exist after that limited time, but would have to be destroyed. But this (by Prop. 4 of this Part) is absurd. Therefore the striving by which a thing exists involves no definite time; on the contrary, since (by the same Prop. 4 of this Part) if it is destroyed by no external cause, it will always continue to exist by the same power by which it now exists, this striving involves an indefinite time. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P9", "p": "part3", "l": "3P9", "la": "Mens tam quatenus claras et distinctas, quam quatenus confusas habet ideas, conatur in suo esse perseverare indefinita quadam duratione, et huius sui conatus est conscia.", "en": "The mind, both insofar as it has clear and distinct ideas and insofar as it has confused ideas, strives to persevere in its being for an indefinite duration, and is conscious of this striving of its own."}, {"id": "E3P9D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P9D", "la": "Mentis essentia ex ideis adaequatis et inadaequatis constituitur (ut in prop. 3. huius ostendimus), adeoque (per prop. 7. huius) tam quatenus has, quam quatenus illas habet, in suo esse perseverare conatur; idque (per prop. 8. huius) indefinita quadam duratione. Cum autem mens (per prop. 23. P. 2.) per ideas affectionum corporis necessario sui sit conscia, est ergo (per prop. 7. huius) mens sui conatus conscia. Q. E. D.", "en": "The essence of the mind is constituted by adequate and inadequate ideas (as we showed in Prop. 3 of this Part), and so (by Prop. 7 of this Part) it strives to persevere in its being both insofar as it has the former and insofar as it has the latter; and that (by Prop. 8 of this Part) for an indefinite duration. But since the mind (by Prop. 23 of Part II) is necessarily conscious of itself through the ideas of the affections of the body, the mind is therefore (by Prop. 7 of this Part) conscious of its striving. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P9S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P9S", "la": "Hic conatus cum ad mentem solam refertur, *voluntas* appellatur; sed cum ad mentem et corpus simul refertur, vocatur *appetitus*, qui proinde nihil aliud est, quam ipsa hominis essentia, ex cuius natura ea, quae ipsius conservationi inserviunt, necessario sequuntur; atque adeo homo ad eadem agendum determinatus est. Deinde inter appetitum et cupiditatem nulla est differentia, nisi quod cupiditas ad homines plerumque referatur, quatenus sui appetitus sunt conscii; et propterea sic definiri potest, nempe, *cupiditas est appetitus cum eiusdem conscientia*. Constat itaque ex his omnibus, nihil nos conari, velle, appetere neque cupere, quia id bonum esse iudicamus; sed contra nos propterea aliquid bonum esse iudicare, quia id conamur, volumus, appetimus atque cupimus.", "en": "This striving, when it is related to the mind alone, is called will; but when it is related to the mind and body together, it is called appetite, which is therefore nothing but the very essence of man, from whose nature there necessarily follow those things which serve his preservation; and so man is determined to do those things. Next, between appetite and desire there is no difference, except that desire is generally related to men insofar as they are conscious of their appetite; and so it can be defined thus: desire is appetite together with consciousness of it. From all these things, then, it is established that we strive for, will, want and desire nothing because we judge it to be good; but on the contrary, we judge something to be good because we strive for it, will it, want it and desire it."}, {"id": "E3P10", "p": "part3", "l": "3P10", "la": "Idea, quae corporis nostri existentiam secludit, in nostra mente dari nequit, sed eidem est contraria.", "en": "An idea which excludes the existence of our body cannot be in our mind, but is contrary to it."}, {"id": "E3P10D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P10D", "la": "Quicquid corpus nostrum potest destruere, in eodem dari nequit (per prop. 5. huius). Adeoque neque eius rei idea potest in Deo dari, quatenus nostri corporis ideam habet (per coroll. prop. 9. P. 2.), hoc est (per prop. 11. et 13. P. 2.) eius rei idea in nostra mente dari nequit; sed contra, quoniam (per prop. 11. et 13. P. 2.) primum, quod mentis essentiam constituit, est idea corporis actu existentis, primum et praecipuum nostrae mentis conatus est (per prop. 7. huius) corporis nostri existentiam affirmare. Atque adeo idea, quae corporis nostri existentiam negat, nostrae menti est contraria etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "Whatever can destroy our body cannot be in it (by Prop. 5 of this Part). And so the idea of that thing cannot be in God insofar as he has the idea of our body (by Cor. of Prop. 9 of Part II); that is (by Props. 11 and 13 of Part II), the idea of that thing cannot be in our mind. On the contrary, since (by Props. 11 and 13 of Part II) the first thing that constitutes the essence of the mind is the idea of a body actually existing, the first and principal thing in the striving of our mind is (by Prop. 7 of this Part) to affirm the existence of our body. And so an idea which denies the existence of our body is contrary to our mind, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P11", "p": "part3", "l": "3P11", "la": "Quicquid corporis nostri agendi potentiam auget vel minuit, iuvat vel coercet, eiusdem rei idea mentis nostrae cogitandi potentiam auget vel minuit, iuvat vel coercet.", "en": "Whatever increases or diminishes, aids or restrains, the power of acting of our body, the idea of that same thing increases or diminishes, aids or restrains, the power of thinking of our mind."}, {"id": "E3P11D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P11D", "la": "Haec propositio patet ex prop. 7. P. 2. vel etiam ex prop. 14. P. 2.", "en": "This Proposition is evident from Prop. 7 of Part II, or also from Prop. 14 of Part II."}, {"id": "E3P11S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P11S", "la": "Videmus itaque mentem magnas posse pati mutationes, et iam ad maiorem, iam autem ad minorem perfectionem transire, quae quidem passiones nobis explicant *affectus laetitiae* et *tristitiae*. Per *laetitiam* itaque in sequentibus intelligam *passionem, qua mens ad maiorem perfectionem transit*. Per *tristitiam* autem *passionem, qua ipsa ad minorem transit perfectionem*.", "en": "We see, then, that the mind can undergo great changes, and pass now to a greater, now to a lesser perfection; and these passions explain to us the affects of joy and sadness. By joy, therefore, I shall understand in what follows the passion by which the mind passes to a greater perfection. By sadness, however, the passion by which it passes to a lesser perfection."}, {"id": "E3P11S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P11S", "la": "Porro affectum laetitiae ad mentem et corpus simul relatum *titillationem* vel *hilaritatem* voco; tristitiae autem *dolorem* vel *melancholiam*. Sed notandum, titillationem et dolorem ad hominem referri, quando una eius pars prae reliquis est affecta; hilaritatem autem et melancholiam, quando omnes pariter sunt affectae.", "en": "Further, the affect of joy related to the mind and body together I call pleasure or cheerfulness; that of sadness, pain or melancholy. But it should be noted that pleasure and pain are related to a man when one part of him is affected more than the rest; cheerfulness and melancholy, when all parts are equally affected."}, {"id": "E3P11S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P11S", "la": "Quid deinde *cupiditas* sit in schol. prop. 9. huius partis explicui et praeter hos tres nullum alium agnosco affectum primarium; nam reliquos ex his tribus oriri in seqq. ostendam. Sed antequam ulterius pergam, lubet hic fusius prop. 10. huius explicare, ut clarius intelligatur, qua ratione idea ideae sit contraria.", "en": "Next, what desire is I have explained in the Scholium of Prop. 9 of this Part, and apart from these three I acknowledge no other primary affect; for I shall show in what follows that the rest arise from these three. But before I go further, I should like to explain Prop. 10 of this Part more fully here, so that it may be understood more clearly in what way one idea is contrary to another."}, {"id": "E3P11S.2", "p": "part3", "l": "3P11S", "la": "In schol. prop. 17. P. 2. ostendimus, ideam quae mentis essentiam constituit, corporis existentiam tamdiu involvere, quamdiu ipsum corpus existit. Deinde ex iis quae in coroll. prop. 8. P. 2. et in eiusdem schol. ostendimus, sequitur, praesentem nostrae mentis existentiam ab hoc solo pendere, quod sc. mens actualem corporis existentiam involvit. Denique mentis potentiam, qua ipsa res imaginatur earumque recordatur, ab hoc etiam pendere ostendimus (vide prop. 17. et 18. P. 2. cum eius schol.), quod ipsa actualem corporis existentiam involvit. Ex quibus sequitur, mentis praesentem existentiam eiusque imaginandi potentiam tolli, simulatque mens praesentem corporis existentiam affirmare desinit. At causa, cur mens hanc corporis existentiam affirmare desinit, non potest esse ipsa mens (per prop. 4. huius), nec etiam quod corpus esse desinit. Nam (per prop. 6. P. 2.) causa, cur mens corporis existentiam affirmat, non est, quia corpus existere incepit (quare, per eandem rationem nec ipsius corporis existentiam affirmare desinit quia corpus esse desinit); sed (per prop. 8. P. 2.) hoc ab alia idea oritur, quae nostri corporis, et consequenter nostrae mentis praesentem existentiam secludit; quaeque adeo ideae, quae nostrae mentis essentiam constituit, est contraria.", "en": "In the Scholium of Prop. 17 of Part II we have shown that the idea which constitutes the essence of the mind involves the existence of the body for as long as the body itself exists. Next, from what we have shown in the Cor. of Prop. 8 of Part II and in its Scholium, it follows that the present existence of our mind depends only on this, that the mind involves the actual existence of the body. Finally, we have shown that the power of the mind by which it imagines things and recollects them also depends on this, that it involves the actual existence of the body (see Props. 17 and 18 of Part II, with its Scholium). From this it follows that the present existence of the mind and its power of imagining are taken away as soon as the mind ceases to affirm the present existence of the body. But the cause why the mind ceases to affirm this existence of the body cannot be the mind itself (by Prop. 4 of this Part), nor also that the body ceases to be. For (by Prop. 6 of Part II) the cause why the mind affirms the existence of the body is not that the body has begun to exist (so, by the same reasoning, it does not cease to affirm the existence of its body because the body ceases to be); but (by Prop. 8 of Part II) this arises from another idea, which excludes the present existence of our body, and consequently of our mind, and which is therefore contrary to the idea which constitutes the essence of our mind."}, {"id": "E3P12", "p": "part3", "l": "3P12", "la": "Mens quantum potest, ea imaginari conatur, quae corporis agendi potentiam augent vel iuvant.", "en": "The mind, as far as it can, strives to imagine those things which increase or aid the body’s power of acting."}, {"id": "E3P12D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P12D", "la": "Quamdiu humanum corpus affectum est modo, qui naturam corporis alicuius externi involvit, tamdiu mens humana idem corpus ut praesens contemplabitur (per prop. 17. P. 2.); et consequenter (per prop. 7. P. 2.) quamdiu mens humana aliquod externum corpus ut praesens contemplatur, hoc est (per eiusdem prop. 17 schol.) imaginatur, tamdiu humanum corpus affectum est modo, qui naturam eiusdem corporis externi involvit. Atque adeo quamdiu mens ea imaginatur, quae corporis nostri agendi potentiam augent vel iuvant, tamdiu corpus affectum est modis, qui eiusdem agendi potentiam augent vel iuvant (vide postul. 1. huius); et consequenter (per prop. 11. huius) tamdiu mentis cogitandi potentia augetur vel iuvatur. Ac proinde (per prop. 6. vel 9. huius) mens quantum potest, eadem imaginari conatur. Q. E. D.", "en": "As long as the human body is affected in a way which involves the nature of some external body, so long will the human mind contemplate that body as present (by Prop. 17 of Part II); and consequently (by Prop. 7 of Part II), as long as the human mind contemplates some external body as present, that is (by the Schol. of the same Prop. 17), imagines it, so long is the human body affected in a way which involves the nature of that external body. And so, as long as the mind imagines those things which increase or aid our body’s power of acting, so long is the body affected in ways which increase or aid its power of acting (see Post. 1 of this Part); and consequently (by Prop. 11 of this Part) so long is the mind’s power of thinking increased or aided. And therefore (by Prop. 6 or 9 of this Part) the mind, as far as it can, strives to imagine those things. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P13", "p": "part3", "l": "3P13", "la": "Cum mens ea imaginatur, quae corporis agendi potentiam minuunt vel coercent, conatur, quantum potest, rerum recordari, quae horum existentiam secludunt.", "en": "When the mind imagines those things which diminish or restrain the body’s power of acting, it strives, as far as it can, to recollect things which exclude their existence."}, {"id": "E3P13D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P13D", "la": "Quamdiu mens quicquam tale imaginatur, tamdiu mentis et corporis potentia minuitur vel coercetur (ut in praeced. prop. demonstravimus), et nihilominus id tamdiu imaginabitur, donec mens aliud imaginetur, quod huius praesentem existentiam secludat (per prop. 17. P. 2.), hoc est (ut modo ostendimus) mentis et corporis potentia tamdiu minuitur vel coercetur, donec mens aliud imaginetur, quod huius existentiam secludit, quodque adeo mens (per prop. 9. huius) quantum potest, imaginari vel recordari conabitur. Q. E. D.", "en": "As long as the mind imagines anything of this kind, so long is the power of mind and body diminished or restrained (as we have demonstrated in the preceding Prop.); and nevertheless it will go on imagining it until the mind imagines something else which excludes its present existence (by Prop. 17 of Part II); that is (as we have just shown), the power of mind and body is diminished or restrained until the mind imagines something else which excludes the existence of this thing; and this, therefore, the mind (by Prop. 9 of this Part) will strive, as far as it can, to imagine or recollect. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P13C", "p": "part3", "l": "3P13C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, quod mens ea imaginari aversatur, quae ipsius et corporis potentiam minuunt vel coercent.", "en": "From this it follows that the mind is averse to imagining those things which diminish or restrain its power and the body’s."}, {"id": "E3P13S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P13S", "la": "Ex his clare intelligimus quid *amor* quidque *odium* sit. Nempe amor nihil aliud est, quam laetitia concomitante idea causae externae et odium nihil aliud quam tristitia concomitante idea causae externae. Videmus deinde quod ille, qui amat, necessario conatur rem, quam amat, praesentem habere et conservare; et contra qui odit, rem, quam odio habet, amovere et destruere conatur. Sed de his omnibus in seqq. prolixius.", "en": "From this we understand clearly what love and hatred are. Namely, love is nothing but joy accompanied by the idea of an external cause, and hatred nothing but sadness accompanied by the idea of an external cause. We see, next, that one who loves necessarily strives to have present and to preserve the thing he loves; and on the other hand one who hates strives to remove and destroy the thing he hates. But of all these things more fully in what follows."}, {"id": "E3P14", "p": "part3", "l": "3P14", "la": "Si mens duobus affectibus simul affecta semel fuit, ubi postea eorum alterutro afficietur, afficietur etiam altero.", "en": "If the mind has once been affected by two affects at the same time, then when it is afterwards affected by one of them, it will also be affected by the other."}, {"id": "E3P14D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P14D", "la": "Si corpus humanum a duobus corporibus simul affectum semel fuit, ubi mens postea eorum alterutrum imaginatur, statim et alterius recordabitur (per prop. 18. P. 2.). At mentis imaginationes magis nostri corporis affectus, quam corporum externorum naturam indicant (per coroll. 2. prop. 16. P. 2.). Ergo si corpus et consequenter mens (vide defin. 3. huius) duobus affectibus semel affecta fuit, ubi postea eorum alterutro afficietur, afficietur etiam altero. Q. E. D.", "en": "If the human body has once been affected by two bodies at the same time, then when the mind afterwards imagines one of them, it will immediately recollect the other also (by Prop. 18 of Part II). But the imaginations of the mind indicate the affects of our body more than the nature of external bodies (by Cor. 2 of Prop. 16 of Part II). Therefore if the body, and consequently the mind (see Def. 3 of this Part), has once been affected by two affects, then when it is afterwards affected by one of them, it will also be affected by the other. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P15", "p": "part3", "l": "3P15", "la": "Res quaecumque potest esse per accidens causa laetitiae, tristitiae vel cupiditatis.", "en": "Any thing can be, accidentally, the cause of joy, sadness or desire."}, {"id": "E3P15D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P15D", "la": "Ponatur mens duobus affectibus simul affici, uno scilicet, qui eius agendi potentiam neque auget neque minuit et altero, qui eandem vel auget vel minuit (vide postul. 1. huius). Ex praeced. prop. patet, quod ubi mens postea illo a sua vera causa, quae (per hypothesin) per se eius cogitandi potentiam nec auget nec minuit, afficietur, statim et hoc altero, qui ipsius cogitandi potentiam auget vel minuit, hoc est (per schol. prop. 11. huius) laetitia vel tristitia afficietur; atque adeo illa res non per se, sed per accidens causa erit laetitiae vel tristitiae. Atque hac eadem via facile ostendi potest, rem illam posse per accidens causam esse cupiditatis. Q. E. D.", "en": "Let the mind be supposed to be affected by two affects at the same time, one which neither increases nor diminishes its power of acting, and the other which either increases or diminishes it (see Post. 1 of this Part). From the preceding Prop. it is evident that when the mind is afterwards affected by the former through its true cause, which (by hypothesis) of itself neither increases nor diminishes its power of thinking, it will immediately be affected also by the latter, which increases or diminishes its power of thinking, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part), it will be affected with joy or sadness; and so that thing will be the cause of joy or sadness not through itself but accidentally. And in this same way it can easily be shown that that thing can accidentally be the cause of desire. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P15C", "p": "part3", "l": "3P15C", "la": "Ex eo solo, quod rem aliquam affectu laetitiae vel tristitiae, cuius ipsa non est causa efficiens, contemplati sumus, eandem amare vel odio habere possumus.", "en": "From the mere fact that we have contemplated some thing with an affect of joy or sadness of which it is not itself the efficient cause, we can love it or hate it."}, {"id": "E3P15CD", "p": "part3", "l": "3P15CD", "la": "Nam ex hoc solo fit (per prop. 14. huius), ut mens hanc rem postea imaginando affectu laetitiae vel tristitiae afficiatur, hoc est (per schol. prop. 11. huius) ut mentis et corporis potentia augeatur vel minuatur etc. Et consequenter (per prop. 12. huius) ut mens eandem imaginari cupiat vel (per coroll. prop. 13. huius) aversetur, hoc est (per schol. prop. 13. huius) ut eandem amet vel odio habeat. Q. E. D.", "en": "For from this alone it comes about (by Prop. 14 of this Part) that the mind, afterwards imagining this thing, is affected with an affect of joy or sadness, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part), that the power of mind and body is increased or diminished, etc. And consequently (by Prop. 12 of this Part) that the mind desires to imagine it, or (by Cor. of Prop. 13 of this Part) is averse to imagining it, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), that it loves it or hates it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P15S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P15S", "la": "Hinc intelligimus, qui fieri potest, ut quaedam amemus vel odio habeamus absque ulla causa nobis cognita; sed tantum ex *sympathia* (ut aiunt) et *antipathia*. Atque huc referenda etiam ea obiecta, quae nos laetitia vel tristitia afficiunt ex eo solo, quod aliquid simile habent obiectis, quae nos iisdem affectibus afficere solent, ut in seq. prop. ostendam. Scio equidem auctores, qui primi haec nomina sympathiae et antipathiae introduxerunt, significare iisdem voluisse rerum occultas quasdam qualitates; sed nihilominus credo nobis licere, per eadem notas vel manifestas etiam qualitates intelligere.", "en": "From this we understand how it can happen that we love or hate certain things without any cause known to us, but only from sympathy (as they say) and antipathy. And to this must be referred also those objects which affect us with joy or sadness from the mere fact that they have something similar to objects which usually affect us with the same affects, as I shall show in the following Prop. I know, indeed, that the authors who first introduced these names of sympathy and antipathy wished to signify by them certain occult qualities of things; but nevertheless I believe that we may also understand by them qualities which are known, or manifest."}, {"id": "E3P16", "p": "part3", "l": "3P16", "la": "Ex eo solo, quod rem aliquam aliquid habere imaginamur simile obiecto, quod mentem laetitia vel tristitia afficere solet, quamvis id, in quo res obiecto est similis, non sit horum affectuum efficiens causa, eam tamen amabimus vel odio habebimus.", "en": "From the mere fact that we imagine some thing to have something similar to an object which usually affects the mind with joy or sadness, we shall love it or hate it, even though that in which the thing is similar to the object is not the efficient cause of these affects."}, {"id": "E3P16D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P16D", "la": "Id quod simile est obiecto, in ipso obiecto (per hypothesin) cum affectu laetitiae vel tristitiae contemplati sumus atque adeo (per prop. 14. huius) cum mens eius imagine afficietur, statim etiam hoc vel illo afficietur affectu, et consequenter res, quam hoc idem habere percipimus, erit (per prop. 15. huius) per accidens laetitiae vel tristitiae causa. Adeoque (per praeced. coroll.) quamvis id, in quo obiecto est similis, non sit horum affectuum causa efficiens, eam tamen amabimus vel odio habebimus. Q. E. D.", "en": "That which is similar to the object, we have contemplated (by hypothesis) in the object itself with an affect of joy or sadness; and so (by Prop. 14 of this Part) when the mind is affected by its image, it will immediately be affected also with this or that affect; and consequently the thing which we perceive to have this same feature will (by Prop. 15 of this Part) be accidentally the cause of joy or sadness. And so (by the preceding Cor.), even though that in which it is similar to the object is not the efficient cause of these affects, we shall nevertheless love it or hate it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P17", "p": "part3", "l": "3P17", "la": "Si rem, quae nos tristitiae affectu afficere solet, aliquid habere imaginamur simile alteri, quae nos aeque magno laetitiae affectu solet afficere, eandem odio habebimus et simul amabimus.", "en": "If we imagine that a thing which usually affects us with an affect of sadness has something similar to another which usually affects us with an equally great affect of joy, we shall hate it and at the same time love it."}, {"id": "E3P17D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P17D", "la": "Est enim (per hypothesin) haec res per se tristitiae causa, et (per schol. prop. 13. huius) quatenus eandem hoc affectu imaginamur, eandem odio habemus; et quatenus praeterea aliquid habere imaginamur simile alteri quae nos aeque magno laetitiae affectu afficere solet, aeque magno laetitiae conamine amabimus (per prop. praeced.). Atque adeo eandem odio habebimus et simul amabimus. Q. E. D.", "en": "For (by hypothesis) this thing is through itself a cause of sadness, and (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part) insofar as we imagine it with this affect, we hate it; and insofar as we imagine, besides, that it has something similar to another which usually affects us with an equally great affect of joy, we shall love it with an equally great effort of joy (by the preceding Prop.). And so we shall hate it and at the same time love it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P17S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P17S", "la": "Haec mentis constitutio quae scilicet ex duobus contrariis affectibus oritur, animi vocatur fluctuatio, quae proinde affectum respicit, ut dubitatio imaginationem (vide schol. prop. 44. P. 2.); nec animi fluctuatio et dubitatio inter se differunt, nisi secundum maius et minus. Sed notandum, me in prop. praeced. has animi fluctuationes ex causis deduxisse, quae per se unius et per accidens alterius affectus sunt causa. Quod ideo feci, quia sic facilius ex praecedentibus deduci poterant; at non, quod negem, animi fluctuationes plerumque oriri ab obiecto, quod utriusque affectus sit efficiens causa. Nam corpus humanum (per postul. 1. P. 2.) ex plurimis diversae naturae individuis componitur, atque adeo (per axiom. 1. post lem. 3. quod vide post prop. 13. P. 2.) ab uno eodemque corpore plurimis diversisque modis potest affici; et contra, quia una eademque res multis modis potest affici, multis ergo etiam diversisque modis unam eandemque corporis partem afficere poterit. Ex quibus facile concipere possumus, unum idemque obiectum posse esse causam multorum contrariorumque affectuum.", "en": "This constitution of the mind, which arises from two contrary affects, is called fluctuation of mind, which is therefore related to the affect as doubt is to the imagination (see Schol. of Prop. 44 of Part II); and fluctuation of mind and doubt do not differ from one another except in respect of more and less. But it should be noted that in the preceding Prop. I deduced these fluctuations of mind from causes which are through themselves the cause of one affect and accidentally the cause of the other. I did this because they could thus be deduced more easily from what preceded; not because I deny that fluctuations of mind usually arise from an object which is the efficient cause of both affects. For the human body (by Post. 1 of Part II) is composed of very many individuals of different natures, and so (by Ax. 1 after Lemma 3, which see after Prop. 13 of Part II) it can be affected in very many different ways by one and the same body; and on the other hand, because one and the same thing can be affected in many ways, it will also be able to affect one and the same part of the body in many different ways. From this we can easily conceive that one and the same object can be the cause of many contrary affects."}, {"id": "E3P18", "p": "part3", "l": "3P18", "la": "Homo ex imagine rei praeteritae aut futurae eodem laetitiae et tristitiae affectu afficitur, ac ex imagine rei praesentis.", "en": "A man is affected by the image of a past or future thing with the same affect of joy and sadness as by the image of a present thing."}, {"id": "E3P18D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P18D", "la": "Quamdiu homo rei alicuius imagine affectus est, rem ut praesentem, tametsi non existat, contemplabitur (per prop. 17. P. 2. cum eiusdem coroll.), nec ipsam ut praeteritam aut futuram imaginatur, nisi quatenus eius imago iuncta est imagini temporis praeteriti aut futuri (vide schol. prop. 44. P. 2.). Quare rei imago in se sola considerata eadem est, sive ad tempus futurum vel praeteritum, sive ad praesens referatur, hoc est (per coroll. 2. prop. 16. P. 2.) corporis constitutio seu affectus idem est, sive imago sit rei praeteritae vel futurae, sive praesentis. Atque adeo affectus laetitiae et tristitiae idem est, sive imago sit rei praeteritae aut futurae, sive praesentis. Q. E. D.", "en": "As long as a man is affected by the image of some thing, he will contemplate the thing as present, even though it does not exist (by Prop. 17 of Part II with its Cor.), and he does not imagine it as past or future except insofar as its image is joined to the image of past or future time (see Schol. of Prop. 44 of Part II). Therefore the image of the thing, considered in itself alone, is the same whether it is referred to future or past time or to the present; that is (by Cor. 2 of Prop. 16 of Part II), the constitution, or affect, of the body is the same whether the image is of a past or future thing or of a present one. And so the affect of joy and sadness is the same whether the image is of a past or future thing or of a present one. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P18S1", "p": "part3", "l": "3P18S1", "la": "Rem eatenus *praeteritam* aut *futuram* hic voco, quatenus ab eadem affecti fuimus aut afficiemur. Ex. gr. quatenus ipsam vidimus aut videbimus, nos refecit aut reficiet, nos laesit aut laedet etc. Quatenus enim eandem sic imaginamur, eatenus eius existentiam affirmamus, hoc est, corpus nullo affectu afficitur, qui rei existentiam secludat; atque adeo (per prop. 17. P. 2.) corpus eiusdem rei imagine eodem modo afficitur, ac si res ipsa praesens adesset. Verumenimvero quia plerumque fit, ut ii qui plura sunt experti fluctuent, quamdiu rem ut futuram vel praeteritam contemplantur, deque rei eventu ut plurimum dubitent (vide schol. prop. 44. P. 2.), hinc fit, ut affectus, qui ex similibus rerum imaginibus oriuntur, non sint adeo constantes, sed ut plerumque aliarum rerum imaginibus perturbentur, donec homines de rei eventu certiores fiant.", "en": "I call a thing past or future here insofar as we have been affected by it or shall be affected by it. For example, insofar as we have seen it or shall see it, it has refreshed us or will refresh us, it has harmed us or will harm us, etc. For insofar as we imagine it in this way, we affirm its existence; that is, the body is affected by no affect which excludes the existence of the thing; and so (by Prop. 17 of Part II) the body is affected by the image of this thing in the same way as if the thing itself were present. But because it usually happens that those who have experienced more things fluctuate as long as they contemplate a thing as future or past, and are generally in doubt about its outcome (see Schol. of Prop. 44 of Part II), it comes about that the affects which arise from like images of things are not so constant, but are generally disturbed by the images of other things, until men become more certain of the thing’s outcome."}, {"id": "E3P18S2", "p": "part3", "l": "3P18S2", "la": "Ex modo dictis intelligimus, quid sit *spes, metus, securitas, desperatio, gaudium* et *conscientiae morsus*. Spes namque nihil aliud est quam inconstans laetitia orta ex imagine rei futurae vel praeteritae, de cuius eventu dubitamus; metus contra inconstans tristitia ex rei dubiae imagine etiam orta. Porro si horum affectuum dubitatio tollatur, ex spe fit securitas et ex metu desperatio; nempe laetitia vel tristitia orta ex imagine rei, quam metuimus vel speravimus. Gaudium deinde est laetitia orta ex imagine rei praeteritae, de cuius eventu dubitavimus. Conscientiae denique morsus est tristitia opposita gaudio.", "en": "From what has just been said we understand what hope, fear, confidence, despair, gladness and remorse are. For hope is nothing but an inconstant joy arising from the image of a future or past thing of whose outcome we are in doubt; fear, on the other hand, an inconstant sadness, arising also from the image of a doubtful thing. Further, if the doubt is removed from these affects, hope becomes confidence and fear despair; namely, a joy or sadness arising from the image of a thing which we feared or hoped for. Gladness, next, is joy arising from the image of a past thing of whose outcome we were in doubt. Remorse, finally, is the sadness opposed to gladness."}, {"id": "E3P19", "p": "part3", "l": "3P19", "la": "Qui id quod amat destrui imaginatur, contristabitur; si autem conservari, laetabitur.", "en": "He who imagines that what he loves is destroyed will be saddened; but if he imagines that it is preserved, he will rejoice."}, {"id": "E3P19D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P19D", "la": "Mens quantum potest, ea imaginari conatur, quae corporis agendi potentiam augent vel iuvant (per prop. 12. huius), hoc est (per schol. prop. 13. huius) ea quae amat. At imaginatio ab iis iuvatur, quae rei existentiam ponunt, et contra coercetur iis quae rei existentiam secludunt (per prop. 17. P. 2.). Ergo rerum imagines, quae rei amatae existentiam ponunt, mentis conatum, quo rem amatam imaginari conatur, iuvant, hoc est (per schol. prop. 11. huius) laetitia mentem afficiunt; et quae contra rei amatae existentiam secludunt, eundem mentis conatum coercent, hoc est (per idem schol.) tristitia mentem afficiunt. Qui itaque id quod amat destrui imaginatur, contristabitur, etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "The mind, as far as it can, strives to imagine those things which increase or aid the body’s power of acting (by Prop. 12 of this Part), that is (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), the things it loves. But the imagination is aided by those things which posit the existence of a thing, and on the other hand is restrained by those which exclude the existence of a thing (by Prop. 17 of Part II). Therefore the images of things which posit the existence of the thing loved aid the striving of the mind by which it strives to imagine the thing loved, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part), they affect the mind with joy; and those which, on the other hand, exclude the existence of the thing loved restrain that same striving of the mind, that is (by the same Scholium), they affect the mind with sadness. He, therefore, who imagines that what he loves is destroyed will be saddened, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P20", "p": "part3", "l": "3P20", "la": "Qui id quod odio habet destrui imaginatur, laetabitur.", "en": "He who imagines that what he hates is destroyed will rejoice."}, {"id": "E3P20D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P20D", "la": "Mens (per prop. 13. huius) ea imaginari conatur, quae rerum existentiam, quibus corporis agendi potentia minuitur vel coercetur, secludunt, hoc est (per schol. eiusdem prop.) ea imaginari conatur; quae rerum quas odio habet, existentiam secludunt. Atque adeo rei imago, quae existentiam eius quod mens odio habet secludit, hunc mentis conatum iuvat, hoc est (per schol. prop. 11. huius) mentem laetitia afficit. Qui itaque id quod odio habet destrui imaginatur, laetabitur. Q. E. D.", "en": "The mind (by Prop. 13 of this Part) strives to imagine those things which exclude the existence of things by which the body’s power of acting is diminished or restrained, that is (by the Schol. of the same Prop.), it strives to imagine those things which exclude the existence of things it hates. And so the image of a thing which excludes the existence of what the mind hates aids this striving of the mind, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part), it affects the mind with joy. He, therefore, who imagines that what he hates is destroyed will rejoice. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P21", "p": "part3", "l": "3P21", "la": "Qui id quod amat laetitia vel tristitia affectum imaginatur, laetitia etiam vel tristitia afficietur; et uterque hic affectus maior aut minor erit in amante, prout uterque maior aut minor est in re amata.", "en": "He who imagines that what he loves is affected with joy or sadness will also be affected with joy or sadness; and each of these affects will be greater or less in the lover as it is greater or less in the thing loved."}, {"id": "E3P21D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P21D", "la": "Rerum imagines (ut in prop. 19. huius demonstravimus) quae rei amatae existentiam ponunt, mentis conatum, quo ipsam rem amatam imaginari conatur, iuvant. Sed laetitia existentiam rei laetae ponit, et eo magis, quo laetitiae affectus maior est; est enim (per schol. prop. 11. huius) transitio ad maiorem perfectionem. Ergo imago laetitiae rei amatae in amante ipsius mentis conatum iuvat, hoc est (per schol. prop. 11. huius) amantem laetitia afficit, et eo maiore quo maior hic affectus in re amata fuerit. Quod erat primum. Deinde quatenus res aliqua tristitia afficitur, eatenus destruitur, et eo magis, quo maiore afficitur tristitia (per idem schol. prop. 11. huius); adeoque (per prop. 19. huius) qui id quod amat tristitia affici imaginatur, tristitia etiam afficietur, et eo maiore quo maior hic affectus in re amata fuerit. Q. E. D.", "en": "The images of things which posit the existence of the thing loved (as we have demonstrated in Prop. 19 of this Part) aid the striving of the mind by which it strives to imagine the thing loved itself. But joy posits the existence of the thing that rejoices, and the more so, the greater the affect of joy is; for it is (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part) a passage to a greater perfection. Therefore the image of the joy of the thing loved aids the striving of the lover’s mind, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part), it affects the lover with joy, and the more so, the greater this affect was in the thing loved. This was the first point. Next, insofar as a thing is affected with sadness, it is to that extent destroyed, and the more so, the greater the sadness with which it is affected (by the same Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part); and so (by Prop. 19 of this Part) he who imagines that what he loves is affected with sadness will also be affected with sadness, and the more so, the greater this affect was in the thing loved. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P22", "p": "part3", "l": "3P22", "la": "Si aliquem imaginamur laetitia afficere rem, quam amamus, amore erga eum afficiemur. Si contra eundem imaginamur tristitia eandem afficere, econtra odio etiam contra ipsum afficiemur.", "en": "If we imagine someone to affect with joy a thing we love, we shall be affected with love towards him. If, on the other hand, we imagine him to affect it with sadness, we shall, on the contrary, also be affected with hatred towards him."}, {"id": "E3P22D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P22D", "la": "Qui rem, quam amamus, laetitia vel tristitia afficit, ille nos laetitia vel tristitia etiam afficit, si nimirum rem amatam laetitia illa vel tristitia affectam imaginamur (per praeced. prop.). At haec laetitia vel tristitia in nobis supponitur dari concomitante idea causae externae. Ergo (per schol. prop. 13. huius) si aliquem imaginamur laetitia vel tristitia afficere rem, quam amamus, erga eundem amore vel odio afficiemur. Q. E. D.", "en": "He who affects a thing we love with joy or sadness also affects us with joy or sadness, namely if we imagine the thing loved to be affected with that joy or sadness (by the preceding Prop.). But this joy or sadness is supposed to be in us accompanied by the idea of an external cause. Therefore (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), if we imagine someone to affect with joy or sadness a thing we love, we shall be affected with love or hatred towards him. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P22S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P22S", "la": "Prop. 21. nobis explicat quid sit commiseratio quam definire possumus quod sit tristitia orta ex alterius damno. Quo autem nomine appellanda sit laetitia, quae ex alterius bono oritur, nescio. Porro amorem erga illum, qui alteri benefecit, favorem, et contra odium erga illum, qui alteri malefecit, indignationem appellabimus. Denique notandum nos non tantum misereri rei, quam amavimus (ut in prop. 21. ostendimus), sed etiam eius, quam antea nullo affectu prosecuti sumus, modo eam nobis similem iudicemus (ut infra ostendam); atque adeo ei etiam favere, qui simili benefecit et contra in eum indignari, qui simili damnum intulit.", "en": "Prop. 21 explains to us what pity is, which we can define as sadness arising from another’s harm. But by what name we should call the joy which arises from another’s good, I do not know. Further, love towards one who has done good to another we shall call favour, and on the other hand hatred towards one who has done evil to another we shall call indignation. Finally, it should be noted that we pity not only a thing we have loved (as we have shown in Prop. 21), but also one towards which we have previously had no affect, provided that we judge it to be like us (as I shall show below); and so we also favour one who has done good to someone like us, and on the other hand are indignant with one who has done harm to someone like us."}, {"id": "E3P23", "p": "part3", "l": "3P23", "la": "Qui id quod odio habet, tristitia affectum imaginatur, laetabitur; si contra idem laetitia affectum esse imaginetur, contristabitur; et uterque hic affectus maior aut minor erit, prout eius contrarius maior aut minor est in eo, quod odio habet.", "en": "He who imagines that what he hates is affected with sadness will rejoice; if, on the other hand, he imagines it to be affected with joy, he will be saddened; and each of these affects will be greater or less as its contrary is greater or less in what he hates."}, {"id": "E3P23D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P23D", "la": "Quatenus res odiosa tristitia afficitur, eatenus destruitur, et eo magis quo maiore tristitia afficitur (per schol. prop. 11. huius). Qui igitur (per prop. 20. huius) rem, quam odio habet, tristitia affici imaginatur, laetitia contra afficietur; et eo maiore, quo maiore tristitia rem odiosam affectam esse imaginatur. Quod erat primum. Deinde laetitia existentiam rei laetae ponit (per idem schol. prop. 11. huius), et eo magis, quo maior laetitia concipitur. Si quis eum, quem odio habet, laetitia affectum imaginatur, haec imaginatio (per prop. 13. huius) eiusdem conatum coercebit, hoc est (per schol. prop. 11. huius) is, qui odio habet, tristitia afficietur etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "Insofar as a hated thing is affected with sadness, it is to that extent destroyed, and the more so, the greater the sadness with which it is affected (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part). He, therefore, who (by Prop. 20 of this Part) imagines that the thing he hates is affected with sadness will, on the contrary, be affected with joy; and the more so, the greater the sadness with which he imagines the hated thing to be affected. This was the first point. Next, joy posits the existence of the thing that rejoices (by the same Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part), and the more so, the greater the joy is conceived to be. If someone imagines the one he hates to be affected with joy, this imagination (by Prop. 13 of this Part) will restrain his striving, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part), he who hates will be affected with sadness, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P23S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P23S", "la": "Haec laetitia vix solida et absque ullo animi conflictu esse potest. Nam (ut statim in prop. 27. huius ostendam) quatenus rem sibi similem tristitiae affectu affici imaginatur, eatenus contristari debet; et contra, si eandem laetitia affici imaginetur. Sed hic ad solum odium attendimus.", "en": "This joy can hardly be solid and without any conflict of mind. For (as I shall show presently in Prop. 27 of this Part), insofar as he imagines a thing like himself to be affected with an affect of sadness, he must to that extent be saddened; and the contrary, if he imagines it to be affected with joy. But here we attend only to hatred."}, {"id": "E3P24", "p": "part3", "l": "3P24", "la": "Si aliquem imaginamur laetitia afficere rem, quam odio habemus, odio etiam erga eum afficiemur. Si contra eundem imaginamur tristitia eandem rem afficere, amore erga ipsum afficiemur.", "en": "If we imagine someone to affect with joy a thing we hate, we shall be affected with hatred towards him also. If, on the other hand, we imagine him to affect that same thing with sadness, we shall be affected with love towards him."}, {"id": "E3P24D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P24D", "la": "Demonstratur eodem modo haec prop. ac prop. 22. huius, quam vide.", "en": "This Proposition is demonstrated in the same way as Prop. 22 of this Part, which see."}, {"id": "E3P24S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P24S", "la": "Hi et similes odii affectus ad invidiam referuntur, quae propterea nihil aliud est, quam ipsum odium, quatenus id consideratur hominem ita disponere, ut malo alterius gaudeat, et contra ut eiusdem bono contristetur.", "en": "These and similar affects of hatred are related to envy, which is therefore nothing but hatred itself, insofar as it is considered to dispose a man so that he rejoices in another’s ill and, on the other hand, is saddened by his good."}, {"id": "E3P25", "p": "part3", "l": "3P25", "la": "Id omne de nobis deque re amata affirmare conamur, quod nos vel rem amatam laetitia afficere imaginamur; et contra id omne negare, quod nos vel rem amatam tristitia afficere imaginamur.", "en": "We strive to affirm of ourselves and of the thing loved whatever we imagine to affect us or the thing loved with joy; and, on the other hand, to deny whatever we imagine to affect us or the thing loved with sadness."}, {"id": "E3P25D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P25D", "la": "Quod rem amatam laetitia vel tristitia afficere imaginamur, id nos laetitia vel tristitia afficit (per prop. 21. huius). At mens (per prop. 12. huius) ea quae nos laetitia afficiunt, quantum potest, conatur imaginari, hoc est (per prop. 17. P. 2. et eius coroll.) ut praesentia contemplari; et contra (per prop. 13. huius) quae nos tristitia afficiunt, eorum existentiam secludere. Ergo id omne de nobis deque re amata affirmare conamur, quod nos vel rem amatam laetitia afficere imaginamur, et contra. Q. E. D.", "en": "What we imagine to affect the thing loved with joy or sadness affects us with joy or sadness (by Prop. 21 of this Part). But the mind (by Prop. 12 of this Part) strives, as far as it can, to imagine the things which affect us with joy, that is (by Prop. 17 of Part II and its Cor.), to contemplate them as present; and, on the other hand (by Prop. 13 of this Part), to exclude the existence of the things which affect us with sadness. Therefore we strive to affirm of ourselves and of the thing loved whatever we imagine to affect us or the thing loved with joy, and the contrary. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P26", "p": "part3", "l": "3P26", "la": "Id omne de re, quam odio habemus, affirmare conamur, quod ipsam tristitia afficere imaginamur, et id contra negare, quod ipsam laetitia afficere imaginamur.", "en": "We strive to affirm of a thing we hate whatever we imagine to affect it with sadness, and, on the other hand, to deny whatever we imagine to affect it with joy."}, {"id": "E3P26D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P26D", "la": "Sequitur haec prop. ex prop. 23. ut praec. ex prop. 21. huius.", "en": "This Proposition follows from Prop. 23, as the preceding one follows from Prop. 21 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3P26S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P26S", "la": "His videmus, facile contingere, ut homo de se deque re amata plus iusto, et contra de re quam odit minus iusto sentiat; quae quidem imaginatio, quando ipsum hominem respicit, qui de se plus iusto sentit, superbia vocatur et species delirii est, quia homo oculis apertis somniat, se omnia illa posse, quae sola imaginatione assequitur, quaeque propterea veluti realia contemplatur, iisque exultat, quamdiu ea imaginari non potest, quae horum existentiam secludunt et ipsius agendi potentiam determinant. Est igitur superbia laetitia ex eo orta, quod homo de se plus iusto sentit. Deinde laetitia, quae ex eo oritur, quod homo de alio plus iusto sentit, existimatio vocatur; et illa denique despectus, quae ex eo oritur, quod de alio minus iusto sentit.", "en": "From these things we see that it easily happens that a man thinks more highly of himself and of the thing loved than is just, and, on the other hand, less highly than is just of the thing he hates. When this imagination concerns the man himself who thinks more highly of himself than is just, it is called pride, and it is a species of madness, because the man dreams with his eyes open that he can do all those things which he attains by imagination alone, and which he therefore contemplates as real, and exults in them, as long as he cannot imagine those things which exclude their existence and determine his own power of acting. Pride, therefore, is joy arising from the fact that a man thinks more highly of himself than is just. Next, the joy which arises from a man’s thinking more highly of another than is just is called overestimation; and finally that which arises from his thinking less highly of another than is just, scorn."}, {"id": "E3P27", "p": "part3", "l": "3P27", "la": "Ex eo, quod rem nobis similem et quam nullo affectu prosecuti sumus, aliquo affectu affici imaginamur, eo ipso simili affectu afficimur.", "en": "From the fact that we imagine a thing like us, towards which we have had no affect, to be affected with some affect, we are thereby affected with a like affect."}, {"id": "E3P27D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P27D", "la": "Rerum imagines sunt corporis humani affectiones, quarum ideae corpora externa veluti nobis praesentia repraesentant (per schol. prop. 17. P. 2.), hoc est (per prop. 16. P. 2.) quarum ideae naturam nostri corporis et simul praesentem externi corporis naturam involvunt. Si igitur corporis externi natura similis sit naturae nostri corporis, tum idea corporis externi, quod imaginamur, affectionem nostri corporis involvet similem affectioni corporis externi; et consequenter, si aliquem nobis similem aliquo affectu affectum imaginamur, haec imaginatio affectionem nostri corporis huic affectui similem exprimet. Adeoque ex hoc, quod rem aliquam nobis similem aliquo affectu affici imaginamur, simili cum ipsa affectu afficimur. Quod si rem nobis similem odio habeamus, eatenus (per prop. 23. huius) contrario affectu cum ipsa afficiemur, non autem simili. Q. E. D.", "en": "The images of things are affections of the human body whose ideas represent external bodies to us as present (by Schol. of Prop. 17 of Part II), that is (by Prop. 16 of Part II), whose ideas involve the nature of our body and at the same time the present nature of the external body. If, therefore, the nature of the external body is like the nature of our body, then the idea of the external body we imagine will involve an affection of our body like the affection of the external body; and consequently, if we imagine someone like us to be affected with some affect, this imagination will express an affection of our body like this affect. And so, from the fact that we imagine a thing like us to be affected with some affect, we are affected with a like affect together with it. But if we hate a thing like us, to that extent (by Prop. 23 of this Part) we shall be affected with a contrary affect together with it, not a like one. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P27S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P27S", "la": "Haec affectuum imitatio quando ad tristitiam refertur, vocatur commiseratio (de qua vide schol. prop. 22. huius); sed ad cupiditatem relata aemulatio, quae proinde nihil aliud est, quam alicuius rei cupiditas, quae in nobis ingeneratur ex eo, quod alios nobis similes eandem cupiditatem habere imaginamur.", "en": "This imitation of the affects, when it is related to sadness, is called pity (on which see the Schol. of Prop. 22 of this Part); but when related to desire it is called emulation, which is therefore nothing but the desire for some thing which is generated in us from the fact that we imagine others like us to have the same desire."}, {"id": "E3P27C1", "p": "part3", "l": "3P27C1", "la": "Si aliquem, quem nullo affectu prosecuti sumus, imaginamur laetitia afficere rem nobis similem, amore erga eundem afficiemur. Si contra eundem imaginamur eandem tristitia afficere, odio erga ipsum afficiemur.", "en": "If we imagine that someone towards whom we have had no affect affects with joy a thing like us, we shall be affected with love towards him. If, on the other hand, we imagine him to affect it with sadness, we shall be affected with hatred towards him."}, {"id": "E3P27C1D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P27C1D", "la": "Hoc eodem modo ex prop. praec. demonstratur ac prop. 22. huius ex prop. 21.", "en": "This is demonstrated from the preceding Prop. in the same way as Prop. 22 of this Part from Prop. 21."}, {"id": "E3P27C2", "p": "part3", "l": "3P27C2", "la": "Rem cuius nos miseret, odio habere non possumus ex eo, quod ipsius miseria nos tristitia afficit.", "en": "We cannot hate a thing we pity from the fact that its misery affects us with sadness."}, {"id": "E3P27C2D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P27C2D", "la": "Si enim ex eo nos eandem odio habere possemus, tum (per prop. 23. huius) ex ipsius tristitia laetaremur, quod est contra hypothesin.", "en": "For if we could hate it from that fact, then (by Prop. 23 of this Part) we would rejoice in its sadness, which is contrary to the hypothesis."}, {"id": "E3P27C3", "p": "part3", "l": "3P27C3", "la": "Rem, cuius nos miseret, a miseria, quantum possumus, liberare conabimur.", "en": "A thing we pity we shall strive, as far as we can, to free from its misery."}, {"id": "E3P27C3D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P27C3D", "la": "Id quod rem, cuius nos miseret, tristitia afficit, nos simili etiam tristitia afficit (per prop. praeced.); adeoque omne id, quod eius rei existentiam tollit, sive quod rem destruit, comminisci conabimur (per prop. 13. huius), hoc est (per schol. prop. 9. huius) id destruere appetemus, sive ad id destruendum determinabimur; atque adeo rem, cuius miseremur, a sua miseria liberare conabimur. Q. E. D.", "en": "That which affects with sadness a thing we pity affects us also with a like sadness (by the preceding Prop.); and so we shall strive to think up whatever takes away the existence of that thing, or destroys it (by Prop. 13 of this Part), that is (by Schol. of Prop. 9 of this Part), we shall want to destroy it, or shall be determined to destroy it; and so we shall strive to free the thing we pity from its misery. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P27C3S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P27C3S", "la": "Haec voluntas sive appetitus benefaciendi qui ex eo oritur, quod rei, in quam beneficium conferre volumus, nos miseret, benevolentia vocatur, quae proinde nihil aliud est, quam cupiditas ex commiseratione orta. Ceterum de amore et odio erga illum, qui rei, quam nobis similem esse imaginamur, bene aut male fecit, vide schol. prop. 22. huius.", "en": "This will, or appetite, to do good, which arises from our pitying the thing on which we want to confer a benefit, is called benevolence, which is therefore nothing but a desire arising from pity. As for love and hatred towards one who has done good or evil to a thing we imagine to be like us, see the Schol. of Prop. 22 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3P28", "p": "part3", "l": "3P28", "la": "Id omne, quod ad laetitiam conducere imaginamur, conamur promovere, ut fiat; quod vero eidem repugnare sive ad tristitiam conducere imaginamur, amovere vel destruere conamur.", "en": "We strive to promote the occurrence of whatever we imagine leads to joy; but whatever we imagine is opposed to it, or leads to sadness, we strive to remove or destroy."}, {"id": "E3P28D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P28D", "la": "Quod ad laetitiam conducere imaginamur, quantum possumus, imaginari conamur (per prop. 12. huius), hoc est (per prop. 17. P. 2.) id, quantum possumus, conabimur ut praesens sive ut actu existens contemplari. Sed mentis conatus seu potentia in cogitando aequalis, et simul natura est cum corporis conatu seu potentia in agendo (ut clare sequitur ex coroll. prop. 7. et coroll. prop. 11. P. 2.). Ergo ut id existat, absolute conamur, sive (quod per schol. prop. 9. huius idem est) appetimus et intendimus; quod erat primum. Deinde si id, quod tristitiae causam esse credimus, hoc est (per schol. prop. 13. huius) si id, quod odio habemus destrui imaginamur, laetabimur (per prop. 20. huius). Adeoque idem (per primam huius partem) conabimur destruere sive (per prop. 13. huius) a nobis amovere, ne ipsum ut praesens contemplemur; quod erat secundum. Ergo id omne, quod ad laetitiam etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "What we imagine leads to joy we strive, as far as we can, to imagine (by Prop. 12 of this Part), that is (by Prop. 17 of Part II), we shall strive, as far as we can, to contemplate it as present, or as actually existing. But the mind’s striving, or power in thinking, is equal to, and simultaneous in nature with, the body’s striving, or power in acting (as follows clearly from Cor. of Prop. 7 and Cor. of Prop. 11 of Part II). Therefore we strive absolutely that it should exist, or (which by Schol. of Prop. 9 of this Part is the same) we want it and aim at it; which was the first point. Next, if we imagine that what we believe to be the cause of sadness, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), what we hate, is destroyed, we shall rejoice (by Prop. 20 of this Part). And so (by the first part of this Demonstration) we shall strive to destroy it, or (by Prop. 13 of this Part) to remove it from us, so that we do not contemplate it as present; which was the second point. Therefore whatever we imagine leads to joy, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P29", "p": "part3", "l": "3P29", "la": "Nos id omne etiam agere conabimur, quod homines cum laetitia aspicere imaginamur, et contra id agere aversabimur, quod homines aversari imaginamur.", "en": "We shall also strive to do whatever we imagine men to look on with joy, and, on the other hand, we shall be averse to doing what we imagine men to be averse to."}, {"id": "E3P29D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P29D", "la": "Ex eo, quod imaginamur homines aliquid amare vel odio habere, nos idem amabimus vel odio habebimus (per prop. 27. huius), hoc est (per schol. prop. 13. huius) eo ipso eius rei praesentia laetabimur vel contristabimur; adeoque (per praeced. prop.) id omne, quod homines amare sive cum laetitia aspicere imaginamur, conabimur agere etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "From the fact that we imagine men to love or hate something, we shall love or hate the same thing (by Prop. 27 of this Part), that is (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), we shall thereby rejoice or be saddened at the presence of that thing; and so (by the preceding Prop.) we shall strive to do whatever we imagine men to love, or to look on with joy, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P29S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P29S", "la": "Hic conatus aliquid agendi et etiam omittendi, ea sola de causa, ut hominibus placeamus, vocatur *ambitio* praesertim quando adeo impense vulgo placere conamur, ut cum nostro aut alterius damno quaedam agamus vel omittamus; alias *humanitas* appellari solet. Deinde laetitiam, qua alterius actionem, qua nos conatus est delectari, imaginamur, *laudem* voco; tristitiam vero, qua contra eiusdem actionem aversamur, *vituperium* voco.", "en": "This striving to do something, and also to omit something, solely in order to please men, is called ambition, especially when we strive so eagerly to please the multitude that we do or omit certain things to our own harm or another’s; otherwise it is usually called courtesy. Next, the joy with which we imagine an action of another by which he has striven to please us I call praise; the sadness, on the other hand, with which we are averse to his action I call blame."}, {"id": "E3P30", "p": "part3", "l": "3P30", "la": "Si quis aliquid egit, quod reliquos laetitia afficere imaginatur, is laetitia concomitante idea sui, tamquam causa, afficietur, sive se ipsum cum laetitia contemplabitur. Si contra aliquid egit, quod reliquos tristitia afficere imaginatur, se ipsum cum tristitia contra contemplabitur.", "en": "If someone has done something which he imagines affects others with joy, he will be affected with joy accompanied by the idea of himself as cause, or he will contemplate himself with joy. If, on the other hand, he has done something which he imagines affects others with sadness, he will, on the contrary, contemplate himself with sadness."}, {"id": "E3P30D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P30D", "la": "Qui se reliquos laetitia vel tristitia afficere imaginatur, eo ipso (per prop. 27. huius) laetitia vel tristitia afficietur. Cum autem homo (per prop. 19. et 23. P. 2.) sui sit conscius per affectiones, quibus ad agendum determinatur, ergo qui aliquid egit, quod ipse imaginatur, reliquos laetitia afficere, laetitia cum conscientia sui tamquam causa afficietur, sive se ipsum cum laetitia contemplabitur, et contra. Q. E. D.", "en": "He who imagines that he affects others with joy or sadness will thereby (by Prop. 27 of this Part) be affected with joy or sadness. But since man (by Props. 19 and 23 of Part II) is conscious of himself through the affections by which he is determined to act, he who has done something which he imagines affects others with joy will be affected with joy together with the consciousness of himself as cause, or he will contemplate himself with joy; and the contrary. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P30S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P30S", "la": "Cum amor (per schol. prop. 13. huius) sit laetitia concomitante idea causae externae, et odium tristitia concomitante etiam idea causae externae, erit ergo haec laetitia et tristitia amoris et odii species. Sed quia amor et odium ad obiecta externa referuntur, ideo hos affectus aliis nominibus significabimus; nempe laetitiam concomitante idea causae externae *gloriam* et tristitiam huic contrariam *pudorem* appellabimus; intellige, quando laetitia vel tristitia ex eo oritur, quod homo se laudari vel vituperari credit. Alias laetitiam concomitante idea causae internae *acquiescentiam in se ipso*, tristitiam vero eidem contrariam *poenitentiam* vocabo. Deinde quia (per coroll. prop. 17. P. 2.) fieri potest, ut laetitia, qua aliquis se reliquos afficere imaginatur, imaginaria tantum sit, et (per prop. 25. huius) unusquisque de se id omne conatur imaginari, quod se laetitia afficere imaginatur, facile ergo fieri potest, ut gloriosus superbus sit, et se omnibus gratum esse imaginetur, quando omnibus molestus est.", "en": "Since love (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part) is joy accompanied by the idea of an external cause, and hatred sadness accompanied also by the idea of an external cause, this joy and sadness will be species of love and hatred. But because love and hatred are related to external objects, we shall signify these affects by other names: namely, joy accompanied by the idea of an external cause we shall call glory, and the sadness contrary to it shame, understand, when the joy or sadness arises from a man’s believing that he is praised or blamed. Otherwise, joy accompanied by the idea of an internal cause I shall call self-contentment, and the sadness contrary to it repentance. Next, because (by Cor. of Prop. 17 of Part II) it can happen that the joy with which someone imagines that he affects others is only imaginary, and (by Prop. 25 of this Part) each strives to imagine of himself whatever he imagines affects him with joy, it can easily happen that the vainglorious man is proud and imagines himself to be pleasing to all when he is a nuisance to all."}, {"id": "E3P31", "p": "part3", "l": "3P31", "la": "Si aliquem imaginamur amare vel cupere vel odio habere aliquid, quod ipsi amamus, cupimus vel odio habemus, eo ipso rem constantius amabimus, etc. Si autem id, quod amamus, eum aversari imaginamur, vel contra, tum animi fluctuationem patiemur.", "en": "If we imagine that someone loves, desires or hates something which we ourselves love, desire or hate, we shall thereby love it, etc., more constantly. But if we imagine that he is averse to what we love, or the contrary, then we shall suffer fluctuation of mind."}, {"id": "E3P31D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P31D", "la": "Ex eo solo, quod aliquem aliquid amare imaginamur, eo ipso idem amabimus (per prop. 27. huius). At sine hoc nos idem amare supponimus. Accedit ergo amori nova causa, a qua fovetur; atque adeo id, quod amamus hoc ipso constantius amabimus. Deinde ex eo, quod aliquem aliquid aversari imaginamur, idem aversabimur (per eandem prop.). At si supponamus, nos eodem tempore id ipsum amare, eodem ergo tempore hoc idem amabimus et aversabimur sive (vide schol. prop. 17. huius) animi fluctuationem patiemur. Q. E. D.", "en": "From the mere fact that we imagine someone to love something, we shall thereby love the same thing (by Prop. 27 of this Part). But we suppose that we love it without this. A new cause of love is therefore added, by which it is fostered; and so we shall thereby love what we love more constantly. Next, from the fact that we imagine someone to be averse to something, we shall be averse to it (by the same Proposition). But if we suppose that we love that very thing at the same time, we shall therefore at the same time love and be averse to it, or (see Schol. of Prop. 17 of this Part) we shall suffer fluctuation of mind. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P31C", "p": "part3", "l": "3P31C", "la": "Hinc et ex prop. 28. huius sequitur, unumquemque, quantum potest, conari ut unusquisque id quod ipse amat amet, et quod ipse odit odio etiam habeat; unde illud poetae:", "en": "From this and from Prop. 28 of this Part it follows that each strives, as far as he can, that everyone should love what he loves and hate what he hates; whence that line of the poet:"}, {"id": "E3P31C.2", "p": "part3", "l": "3P31C", "la": "Speremus pariter, pariter metuamus amantes;", "en": "As lovers, let us hope alike and fear alike;"}, {"id": "E3P31C.3", "p": "part3", "l": "3P31C", "la": "Ferreus est, si quis, quod sinit alter, amat.", "en": "He is made of iron who loves what another allows."}, {"id": "E3P31S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P31S", "la": "Hic conatus efficiendi, ut unusquisque probet id, quod ipse amat vel odio habet, revera est *ambitio* (vide schol. prop. 29. huius); atque adeo videmus, unumquemque ex natura appetere, ut reliqui ex ipsius ingenio vivant, quod dum omnes pariter appetunt, pariter sibi impedimento, et dum omnes ab omnibus laudari seu amari volunt, odio invicem sunt.", "en": "This striving to bring it about that everyone should approve what one loves or hates is in truth ambition (see Schol. of Prop. 29 of this Part); and so we see that each by nature wants the others to live according to his temperament; and while all want this alike, they are alike an obstacle to one another, and while all want to be praised, or loved, by all, they hate one another."}, {"id": "E3P32", "p": "part3", "l": "3P32", "la": "Si aliquem re aliqua, qua unus solus potiri potest, gaudere imaginamur, conabimur efficere ne ille illa re potiatur.", "en": "If we imagine that someone enjoys some thing which only one can possess, we shall strive to bring it about that he does not possess it."}, {"id": "E3P32D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P32D", "la": "Ex eo solo, quod aliquem re aliqua gaudere imaginamur, (per prop. 27. huius cum eiusdem coroll. 1.) rem illam amabimus, eaque gaudere cupiemus. At (per hypothesin) huic laetitiae obstare imaginamur, quod ille eadem hac re gaudeat. Ergo (per prop. 28. huius) ne ille eadem potiatur, conabimur. Q. E. D.", "en": "From the mere fact that we imagine someone to enjoy some thing (by Prop. 27 of this Part with its Cor. 1), we shall love that thing and desire to enjoy it. But (by hypothesis) we imagine that the fact that he enjoys this same thing stands in the way of this joy. Therefore (by Prop. 28 of this Part) we shall strive that he should not possess it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P32S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P32S", "la": "Videmus itaque cum hominum natura plerumque ita comparatum, esse ut eorum, quibus male est, misereantur, et quibus bene est, invideant, et (per prop. praeced.) eo maiore odio, quo rem, qua alium potiri imaginantur, magis amant. Videmus deinde, ex eadem naturae humanae proprietate, ex qua sequitur homines esse misericordes, sequi etiam eosdem esse invidos et ambitiosos. Denique si ipsam experientiam consulere velimus, ipsam haec omnia docere experiemur; praesertim si ad priores nostrae aetatis annos attenderimus. Nam pueros, quia eorum corpus continuo veluti in aequilibrio est, ex hoc solo ridere vel flere experimur, quod alios ridere vel flere vident; et quicquid praeterea vident alios facere, id imitari statim cupiunt, et omnia denique sibi cupiunt, quibus alios delectari imaginantur; nimirum quia rerum imagines, uti diximus, sunt ipsae humani corporis affectiones, sive modi, quibus corpus humanum a causis externis afficitur, disponiturque ad hoc vel illud agendum.", "en": "We see, then, that the nature of men is generally so constituted that they pity those who fare badly and envy those who fare well, and (by the preceding Prop.) with a hatred the greater, the more they love the thing they imagine another to possess. We see, next, that from the same property of human nature from which it follows that men are compassionate, it also follows that they are envious and ambitious. Finally, if we are willing to consult experience itself, we shall find that it teaches all these things, especially if we attend to the first years of our life. For we find that children, because their body is continually, as it were, in equilibrium, laugh or cry simply because they see others laugh or cry; and whatever else they see others do, they immediately desire to imitate it; and finally they desire for themselves all the things in which they imagine others take delight; namely, because, as we have said, the images of things are the very affections of the human body, or modes by which the human body is affected by external causes and disposed to do this or that."}, {"id": "E3P33", "p": "part3", "l": "3P33", "la": "Cum rem nobis similem amamus, conamur, quantum possumus, efficere ut nos contra amet.", "en": "When we love a thing like us, we strive, as far as we can, to bring it about that it loves us in return."}, {"id": "E3P33D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P33D", "la": "Rem, quam amamus, prae reliquis, quantum possumus, imaginari conamur (per prop. 12. huius). Si igitur res nobis sit similis, ipsam prae reliquis laetitia afficere conabimur (per prop. 29. huius), sive conabimur, quantum possumus, efficere, ut res amata laetitia afficiatur concomitante idea nostri, hoc est (per schol. prop. 13. huius) ut nos contra amet. Q. E. D.", "en": "We strive, as far as we can, to imagine a thing we love before others (by Prop. 12 of this Part). If, therefore, the thing is like us, we shall strive to affect it with joy before others (by Prop. 29 of this Part), or we shall strive, as far as we can, to bring it about that the thing loved is affected with joy accompanied by the idea of us, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), that it loves us in return. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P34", "p": "part3", "l": "3P34", "la": "Quo maiore affectu rem amatam erga nos affectam esse imaginamur, eo magis gloriabimur.", "en": "The greater the affect with which we imagine a thing we love to be affected towards us, the more we shall exult in glory."}, {"id": "E3P34D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P34D", "la": "Nos (per prop. praeced.) conamur, quantum possumus, ut res amata nos contra amet hoc est (per schol. prop. 13. huius) ut res amata laetitia afficiatur concomitante idea nostri. Quo itaque rem amatam maiore laetitia nostra de causa affectam esse imaginamur, eo magis hic conatus iuvatur hoc est (per prop. 11. huius cum eius schol.) eo maiore laetitia afficimur. At cum ex eo laetemur, quod alium nobis similem laetitia affecimus, tum nosmet cum laetitia contemplamur (per prop. 30. huius). Ergo quo maiore affectu rem amatam erga nos affectam esse imaginamur, eo maiore laetitia nosmet contemplabimur, sive (per schol. prop. 30. huius) eo magis gloriabimur. Q. E. D.", "en": "We strive (by the preceding Prop.), as far as we can, that the thing loved should love us in return, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), that the thing loved should be affected with joy accompanied by the idea of us. The greater, therefore, the joy with which we imagine the thing loved to be affected on our account, the more this striving is aided, that is (by Prop. 11 of this Part with its Schol.), the greater the joy with which we are affected. But when we rejoice that we have affected with joy another like us, then we contemplate ourselves with joy (by Prop. 30 of this Part). Therefore the greater the affect with which we imagine the thing loved to be affected towards us, the greater the joy with which we shall contemplate ourselves, or (by Schol. of Prop. 30 of this Part) the more we shall exult in glory. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P35", "p": "part3", "l": "3P35", "la": "Si quis imaginatur rem amatam eodem vel arctiore vinculo amicitiae, quo ipse eadem solus potiebatur, alium sibi iungere, odio erga ipsam rem amatam afficietur, et illi alteri invidebit.", "en": "If someone imagines that a thing he loves joins another to itself with the same bond of friendship, or a closer one, than that by which he alone possessed it, he will be affected with hatred towards the thing loved, and will envy that other."}, {"id": "E3P35D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P35D", "la": "Quo quis maiore amore rem amatam erga se affectam esse imaginatur, eo magis gloriabitur (per praeced. prop.), hoc est (per schol. prop. 30. huius) laetabitur; adeoque (per prop. 28. huius) conabitur, quantum potest, imaginari rem amatam ipsi quam arctissime devinctam, qui quidem conatus sive appetitus fomentatur, si alium idem sibi cupere imaginatur (per prop. 31. huius). At hic conatus sive appetitus ab ipsius rei amatae imagine concomitante imagine illius, quem res amata sibi iungit, coerceri supponitur. Ergo (per schol. prop. 11. huius) eo ipso tristitia afficietur concomitante idea rei amatae, tamquam causa, et simul imagine alterius, hoc est (per schol. prop. 13. huius) odio erga rem amatam afficietur, et simul erga illum alterum (per coroll. prop. 15. huius), cui propterea (per prop. 23. huius) quod re amata delectatur, invidebit. Q. E. D.", "en": "The greater the love with which someone imagines the thing loved to be affected towards him, the more he will exult in glory (by the preceding Prop.), that is (by Schol. of Prop. 30 of this Part), the more he will rejoice; and so (by Prop. 28 of this Part) he will strive, as far as he can, to imagine the thing loved bound to him as closely as possible; and this striving, or appetite, is fostered if he imagines that another desires the same thing for himself (by Prop. 31 of this Part). But this striving, or appetite, is supposed to be restrained by the image of the thing loved accompanied by the image of him whom the thing loved joins to itself. Therefore (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part) he will thereby be affected with sadness, accompanied by the idea of the thing loved as cause and at the same time by the image of the other, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), he will be affected with hatred towards the thing loved, and at the same time towards that other (by Cor. of Prop. 15 of this Part), whom (by Prop. 23 of this Part) he will envy because that other takes delight in the thing loved. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P35S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P35S", "la": "Hoc odium erga rem amatam invidiae iunctum *zelotypia* vocatur, quae proinde nihil aliud est, quam animi fluctuatio orta ex amore et odio simul, concomitante idea alterius, cui invidetur. Praeterea hoc odium erga rem amatam maius erit pro ratione laetitiae, qua zelotypus ex reciproco rei amatae amore solebat affici, et etiam pro ratione affectus quo erga illum, quem sibi rem amatam iungere imaginatur, affectus erat. Nam si eum oderat, eo ipso rem amatam (per prop. 24. huius) odio habebit, quia ipsam id, quod ipse odio habet, laetitia afficere imaginatur; et etiam (per coroll. prop. 15. huius) ex eo, quod rei amatae imaginem imagini eius, quem odit, iungere cogitur, quae ratio plerumque locum habet in amore erga feminam. Qui enim imaginatur mulierem, quam amat, alteri sese prostituere, non solum ex eo, quod ipsius appetitus coercetur, contristabitur, sed etiam quia rei amatae imaginem pudendis et excrementis alterius iungere cogitur, eandem aversatur. Ad quod denique accedit, quod zelotypus non eodem vultu, quem res amata ei praebere solebat, ab eadem excipiatur, qua etiam de causa amans contristatur, ut iam ostendam.", "en": "This hatred towards a thing loved, joined with envy, is called jealousy, which is therefore nothing but a fluctuation of mind arising from love and hatred together, accompanied by the idea of another who is envied. Moreover, this hatred towards the thing loved will be greater in proportion to the joy with which the jealous man used to be affected by the reciprocal love of the thing loved, and also in proportion to the affect with which he was affected towards him whom he imagines the thing loved to join to itself. For if he hated him, he will thereby hate the thing loved (by Prop. 24 of this Part), because he imagines that it affects with joy what he himself hates; and also (by Cor. of Prop. 15 of this Part) from the fact that he is compelled to join the image of the thing loved to the image of him whom he hates; which reason generally has place in love towards a woman. For he who imagines that the woman he loves gives herself to another will not only be saddened because his own appetite is restrained, but will also be averse to her because he is compelled to join the image of the thing loved to the private parts and excretions of the other. To which, finally, is added that the jealous man is not received by the thing loved with the same countenance that it used to show him; for this reason also the lover is saddened, as I shall now show."}, {"id": "E3P36", "p": "part3", "l": "3P36", "la": "Qui rei, qua semel delectatus est, recordatur, cupit eadem cum iisdem potiri circumstantiis ac cum primo ipsa delectatus est.", "en": "He who recollects a thing in which he once took delight desires to possess it with the same circumstances as when he first took delight in it."}, {"id": "E3P36D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P36D", "la": "Quicquid homo simul cum re, quae ipsum delectavit, vidit, id omne (per prop. 15. huius) erit per accidens laetitiae causa; adeoque (per prop. 28. huius) omni eo simul cum re, quae ipsum delectavit, potiri cupiet, sive re cum omnibus iisdem circumstantiis potiri cupiet, ac cum primo eadem delectatus est. Q. E. D.", "en": "Whatever a man has seen together with the thing that delighted him will (by Prop. 15 of this Part) be accidentally a cause of joy; and so (by Prop. 28 of this Part) he will desire to possess all of it together with the thing that delighted him, or he will desire to possess the thing with all the same circumstances as when he first took delight in it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P36C", "p": "part3", "l": "3P36C", "la": "Si itaque unam ex iis circumstantiis deficere compererit, amans contristabitur.", "en": "If, therefore, he finds that one of those circumstances is lacking, the lover will be saddened."}, {"id": "E3P36CD", "p": "part3", "l": "3P36CD", "la": "Nam quatenus aliquam circumstantiam deficere comperit, eatenus aliquid imaginatur, quod eius rei existentiam secludit. Cum autem eius rei sive circumstantiae (per prop. praeced.) sit prae amore cupidus, ergo (per prop. 19. huius) quatenus eandem deficere imaginatur, contristabitur. Q. E. D.", "en": "For insofar as he finds that some circumstance is lacking, he imagines something which excludes the existence of that thing. But since (by the preceding Prop.) he desires that thing, or circumstance, from love, he will therefore (by Prop. 19 of this Part) be saddened insofar as he imagines it to be lacking. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P36S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P36S", "la": "Haec tristitia, quatenus absentiam eius, quod amamus, respicit, *desiderium* vocatur.", "en": "This sadness, insofar as it concerns the absence of what we love, is called longing."}, {"id": "E3P37", "p": "part3", "l": "3P37", "la": "Cupiditas, quae prae tristitia vel laetitia praeque odio vel amore oritur, eo est maior, quo affectus maior est.", "en": "The desire which arises from sadness or joy, and from hatred or love, is the greater, the greater the affect is."}, {"id": "E3P37D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P37D", "la": "Tristitia hominis agendi potentiam (per schol. prop. 11. huius) minuit vel coercet, hoc est (per prop. 7. huius) conatum quo homo in suo esse perseverare conatur, minuit vel coercet; adeoque (per prop. 5. huius) huic conatui est contraria; et quicquid homo tristitia affectus conatur, est tristitiam amovere. At (per tristitiae defin.) quo tristitia maior est, eo maiori parti hominis agendi potentiae necesse est opponi. Ergo quo maior tristitia est, eo maiore agendi potentia conabitur homo contra tristitiam amovere, hoc est (per schol. prop. 9. huius) eo maiore cupiditate sive appetitu conabitur tristitiam amovere. Deinde quoniam laetitia (per idem schol. prop. 11. huius) hominis agendi potentiam auget vel iuvat, facile eadem via demonstratur, quod homo laetitia affectus nihil aliud cupit, quam eandem conservare, idque eo maiore cupiditate quo laetitia maior erit. Denique quoniam odium et amor sunt ipsi tristitiae vel laetitiae affectus, sequitur eodem modo, quod conatus, appetitus sive cupiditas, quae prae odio vel amore oritur, maior erit pro ratione odii et amoris. Q. E. D.", "en": "Sadness diminishes or restrains a man’s power of acting (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part), that is (by Prop. 7 of this Part), it diminishes or restrains the striving by which a man strives to persevere in his being; and so (by Prop. 5 of this Part) it is contrary to this striving; and whatever a man affected with sadness strives for is to remove the sadness. But (by the definition of sadness) the greater the sadness is, the greater the part of the man’s power of acting to which it is necessarily opposed. Therefore the greater the sadness is, the greater the power of acting with which the man will strive, on the other hand, to remove the sadness, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 9 of this Part), the greater the desire, or appetite, with which he will strive to remove the sadness. Next, since joy (by the same Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part) increases or aids a man’s power of acting, it is easily demonstrated in the same way that a man affected with joy desires nothing but to preserve it, and that with a desire the greater, the greater the joy will be. Finally, since hatred and love are themselves affects of sadness or joy, it follows in the same way that the striving, appetite or desire which arises from hatred or love will be greater in proportion to the hatred and love. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P38", "p": "part3", "l": "3P38", "la": "Si quis rem amatam odio habere inceperit, ita ut amor plane aboleatur, eandem maiore odio ex pari causa prosequetur, quam si ipsam nunquam amavisset, et eo maiore, quo amor antea maior fuerat.", "en": "If someone begins to hate a thing he loved, so that the love is completely destroyed, he will, from an equal cause, hate it with a greater hatred than if he had never loved it, and with a hatred the greater, the greater the love had been before."}, {"id": "E3P38D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P38D", "la": "Nam si quis rem, quam amat, odio habere incipit, plures eius appetitus coercentur, quam si eandem non amavisset. Amor namque laetitia est (per schol. prop. 13. huius), quam homo, quantum potest (per prop. 28. huius) conservare conatur; idque (per idem schol.) rem amatam ut praesentem contemplando, eandemque (per prop. 21. huius) laetitia, quantum potest, afficiendo, qui quidem conatus (per prop. praeced.) eo est maior, quo amor maior est, ut et conatus efficiendi, ut res amata ipsum contra amet (vide prop. 33. huius). At hi conatus odio erga rem amatam coercentur (per coroll. prop. 13. et per prop. 23. huius). Ergo amans (per schol. prop. 11. huius) hac etiam de causa tristitia afficietur, et eo maiore quo amor maior fuerat, hoc est, praeter tristitiam, quae odii fuit causa, alia ex eo oritur quod rem amavit, et consequenter maiore tristitiae affectu rem amatam contemplabitur, hoc est (per schol. prop. 13. huius) maiore odio prosequetur, quam si eandem non amavisset, et eo maiore, quo amor maior fuerat. Q. E. D.", "en": "For if someone begins to hate a thing he loves, more of his appetites are restrained than if he had not loved it. For love is a joy (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), which a man strives to preserve as far as he can (by Prop. 28 of this Part); and that (by the same Scholium) by contemplating the thing loved as present, and (by Prop. 21 of this Part) by affecting it with joy as far as he can; and this striving (by the preceding Prop.) is the greater, the greater the love is, as is also the striving to bring it about that the thing loved loves him in return (see Prop. 33 of this Part). But these strivings are restrained by hatred towards the thing loved (by Cor. of Prop. 13 and by Prop. 23 of this Part). Therefore the lover (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part) will for this reason also be affected with sadness, and the more so, the greater the love had been; that is, besides the sadness which was the cause of the hatred, another arises from the fact that he loved the thing; and consequently he will contemplate the thing loved with a greater affect of sadness, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), he will hate it with a greater hatred than if he had not loved it, and with a hatred the greater, the greater the love had been. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P39", "p": "part3", "l": "3P39", "la": "Qui aliquem odio habet, ei malum inferre conabitur, nisi ex eo maius sibi malum oriri timeat; et contra, qui aliquem amat, ei eadem lege benefacere conabitur.", "en": "He who hates someone will strive to do him evil, unless he fears that a greater evil to himself will arise from it; and, on the other hand, he who loves someone will strive, by the same law, to do him good."}, {"id": "E3P39D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P39D", "la": "Aliquem odio habere est (per schol. prop. 13. huius) aliquem ut tristitiae causam imaginari; adeoque (per prop. 28. huius) is, qui aliquem odio habet, eundem amovere vel destruere conabitur. Sed si inde aliquid tristius sive (quod idem est) maius malum sibi timeat, idque se vitare posse credit non inferendo ei, quem odit, malum quod meditabatur, a malo inferendo (per eandem prop. 28. huius) abstinere cupiet; idque (per prop. 37. huius) maiore conatu, quam quo tenebatur inferendi malum, qui propterea praevalebit, ut volebamus. Secundae partis demonstratio eodem modo procedit. Ergo qui aliquem odio habet etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "To hate someone is (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part) to imagine someone as a cause of sadness; and so (by Prop. 28 of this Part) he who hates someone will strive to remove or destroy him. But if he fears from this something sadder, or (which is the same) a greater evil to himself, and believes that he can avoid it by not inflicting on the one he hates the evil he was planning, he will desire (by the same Prop. 28 of this Part) to abstain from inflicting the evil; and that (by Prop. 37 of this Part) with a greater striving than that by which he was held to inflict the evil, which will therefore prevail, as we maintained. The demonstration of the second part proceeds in the same way. Therefore he who hates someone, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P39S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P39S", "la": "Per *bonum* hic intelligo omne genus laetitiae et quicquid porro ad eandem conducit, et praecipue id, quod desiderio qualecumque illud sit, satisfacit; per *malum* autem omne tristitiae genus et praecipue id, quod desiderium frustratur. Supra enim (in schol. prop. 9. huius) ostendimus, nos nihil cupere, quia id bonum esse iudicamus, sed contra id bonum vocamus, quod cupimus; et consequenter id, quod aversamur malum appellamus. Quare unusquisque ex suo affectu iudicat seu aestimat, quid bonum, quid malum, quid melius, quid peius et quid denique optimum quidve pessimum sit. Sic avarus argenti copiam optimum, eius autem inopiam pessimum iudicat. Ambitiosus autem nihil aeque ac gloriam cupit, et contra nihil aeque ac pudorem reformidat. Invido deinde nihil iucundius, quam alterius infelicitas, et nihil molestius, quam aliena felicitas; ac sic unusquisque ex suo affectu rem aliquam bonam aut malam, utilem aut inutilem esse iudicat.", "en": "By good I understand here every kind of joy, and whatever, further, leads to it, and especially what satisfies a longing, whatever it may be; by evil, every kind of sadness, and especially what frustrates a longing. For we have shown above (in Schol. of Prop. 9 of this Part) that we desire nothing because we judge it to be good, but on the contrary call good what we desire; and consequently what we are averse to we call evil. So each judges, or evaluates, from his own affect what is good, what evil, what better, what worse, and finally what best or what worst. So the greedy man judges an abundance of money best and its lack worst. The ambitious man, again, desires nothing so much as glory, and on the other hand fears nothing so much as shame. To the envious man, next, nothing is more pleasant than another’s unhappiness, and nothing more troublesome than another’s happiness; and so each, from his own affect, judges a thing good or evil, useful or useless."}, {"id": "E3P39S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P39S", "la": "Ceterum hic affectus, quo homo ita disponitur, ut id quod vult nolit, vel ut id quod non vult velit, *timor* vocatur, qui proinde nihil aliud est quam metus quatenus homo ab eodem disponitur ad malum, quod futurum iudicat, minore vitandum. Vide prop. 28. huius. Sed si malum, quod timet, pudor sit, tum timor appellatur verecundia. Denique si cupiditas malum futurum vitandi coercetur timore alterius mali, ita ut quid potius velit nesciat, tum metus vocatur *consternatio* praecipue si utrumque malum, quod timetur, ex maximis sit.", "en": "But this affect by which a man is so disposed that he does not will what he wills, or wills what he does not will, is called timidity, which is therefore nothing but fear insofar as a man is disposed by it to avoid an evil which he judges to be future by a lesser one. See Prop. 28 of this Part. But if the evil he fears is shame, then the timidity is called shamefacedness. Finally, if the desire to avoid a future evil is restrained by timidity regarding another evil, so that he does not know which he would rather will, then the fear is called consternation, especially if both of the evils feared are among the greatest."}, {"id": "E3P40", "p": "part3", "l": "3P40", "la": "Qui se odio haberi ab aliquo imaginatur, nec se ullam odii causam illi dedisse credit, eundem odio contra habebit.", "en": "He who imagines that he is hated by someone, and believes that he has given him no cause for hatred, will hate him in return."}, {"id": "E3P40D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P40D", "la": "Qui aliquem odio affectum imaginatur, eo ipso etiam odio afficietur (per prop. 27. huius), hoc est (per schol. prop. 13. huius) tristitia concomitante idea causae externae. At ipse (per hypothesin) nullam huius tristitiae causam imaginatur praeter illum, qui ipsum odio habet. Ergo ex hoc, quod se odio haberi ab aliquo imaginatur, tristitia afficietur concomitante idea eius, qui ipsum odio habet, sive (per idem schol.) eundem odio habebit. Q. E. D.", "en": "He who imagines someone to be affected with hatred will thereby also be affected with hatred (by Prop. 27 of this Part), that is (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), with sadness accompanied by the idea of an external cause. But he himself (by hypothesis) imagines no cause of this sadness except the one who hates him. Therefore from the fact that he imagines that he is hated by someone, he will be affected with sadness accompanied by the idea of the one who hates him, or (by the same Scholium) he will hate him. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P40S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P40S", "la": "Quod si se iustam odii causam praebuisse imaginatur, tum (per prop. 30. huius et eiusdem schol.) pudore afficietur. Sed hoc (per prop. 25. huius) raro contingit. Praeterea haec odii reciprocatio oriri etiam potest ex eo, quod odium sequatur conatus malum inferendi ei, qui odio habetur (per prop. 39. huius). Qui igitur se odio haberi ab aliquo imaginatur, eundem alicuius mali sive tristitiae causam imaginabitur; atque adeo tristitia afficietur, seu metu concomitante idea eius, qui ipsum odio habet, tamquam causa, hoc est, odio contra afficietur, ut supra.", "en": "But if he imagines that he has given just cause for the hatred, then (by Prop. 30 of this Part and its Scholium) he will be affected with shame. But this (by Prop. 25 of this Part) rarely happens. Moreover, this reciprocation of hatred can also arise from the fact that hatred is followed by a striving to inflict evil on the one who is hated (by Prop. 39 of this Part). He, therefore, who imagines that he is hated by someone will imagine him as the cause of some evil, or sadness; and so he will be affected with sadness, or fear, accompanied by the idea of the one who hates him as cause; that is, he will be affected with hatred in return, as above."}, {"id": "E3P40C1", "p": "part3", "l": "3P40C1", "la": "Qui, quem amat, odio erga se affectum imaginatur, odio et amore simul conflictabitur. Nam quatenus imaginatur, ab eodem se odio haberi, determinatur (per prop. praeced.) ad eundem contra odio habendum. At (per hypothesin) ipsum nihilominus amat. Ergo odio et amore simul conflictabitur.", "en": "He who imagines that one he loves is affected with hatred towards him will be torn by hatred and love together. For insofar as he imagines that he is hated by him, he is determined (by the preceding Prop.) to hate him in return. But (by hypothesis) he nevertheless loves him. Therefore he will be torn by hatred and love together."}, {"id": "E3P40C2", "p": "part3", "l": "3P40C2", "la": "Si aliquis imaginatur, ab aliquo, quem antea nullo affectu prosecutus est, malum aliquod prae odio sibi illatum esse, statim idem malum eidem referre conabitur.", "en": "If someone imagines that some evil has been done to him from hatred by someone towards whom he previously had no affect, he will immediately strive to return the same evil to him."}, {"id": "E3P40C2D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P40C2D", "la": "Qui aliquem odio erga se affectum esse imaginatur, eum contra (per praeced. prop.) odio habebit et (per prop. 26. huius) id omne comminisci conabitur, quod eundem possit tristitia afficere; atque id eidem (per prop. 39. huius) inferre studebit. At (per hypothesin) primum, quod huiusmodi imaginatur, est malum sibi illatum. Ergo idem statim eidem inferre conabitur. Q. E. D.", "en": "He who imagines someone to be affected with hatred towards him will hate him in return (by the preceding Prop.), and (by Prop. 26 of this Part) will strive to think up everything which can affect him with sadness, and will be eager (by Prop. 39 of this Part) to inflict it on him. But (by hypothesis) the first thing of this kind that he imagines is the evil done to himself. Therefore he will immediately strive to inflict the same on him. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P40C2S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P40C2S", "la": "Conatus malum inferendi ei, quem odimus, *ira* vocatur; conatus autem malum nobis illatum referendi *vindicta* appellatur.", "en": "The striving to inflict evil on one we hate is called anger; but the striving to return an evil done to us is called vengeance."}, {"id": "E3P41", "p": "part3", "l": "3P41", "la": "Si quis ab aliquo se amari imaginatur, nec se ullam ad id causam dedisse credit (quod per coroll. prop. 15. et per prop. 16. huius fieri potest), eundem contra amabit.", "en": "If someone imagines that he is loved by someone, and does not believe that he has given any cause for it (which can happen by Cor. of Prop. 15 and by Prop. 16 of this Part), he will love him in return."}, {"id": "E3P41D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P41D", "la": "Haec propositio eadem via demonstratur ac praeced., cuius etiam schol. vide.", "en": "This Proposition is demonstrated in the same way as the preceding one; see also its Scholium."}, {"id": "E3P41S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P41S", "la": "Quod si se iustam amoris causam praebuisse crediderit, gloriabitur (per prop. 30. huius cum eiusdem schol.); quod quidem (per prop. 25. huius) frequentius contingit, et cuius contrarium evenire diximus, quando aliquis ab aliquo se odio haberi imaginatur (vide schol. prop. praeced.). Porro hic reciprocus amor, et consequenter (per prop. 39. huius) conatus benefaciendi ei, qui nos amat, quique (per eandem prop. 39. huius) nobis benefacere conatur, *gratia* seu *gratitudo* vocatur. Atque adeo apparet, homines longe paratiores esse ad vindictam, quam ad referendum beneficium.", "en": "But if he believes that he has given just cause for the love, he will exult in glory (by Prop. 30 of this Part with its Scholium); which indeed (by Prop. 25 of this Part) happens more frequently, and the contrary of which, we said, happens when someone imagines that he is hated by someone (see the Schol. of the preceding Prop.). Further, this reciprocal love, and consequently (by Prop. 39 of this Part) the striving to do good to one who loves us, and who (by the same Prop. 39 of this Part) strives to do good to us, is called thankfulness, or gratitude. And so it is evident that men are far readier for vengeance than for returning a benefit."}, {"id": "E3P41C", "p": "part3", "l": "3P41C", "la": "Qui ab eo, quem odio habet, se amari imaginatur, odio et amore simul conflictabitur. Quod eadem via, qua primum propositionis praecedentis coroll. demonstratur.", "en": "He who imagines that he is loved by one he hates will be torn by hatred and love together. This is demonstrated in the same way as the first Corollary of the preceding Proposition."}, {"id": "E3P41CS", "p": "part3", "l": "3P41CS", "la": "Quod si odium praevaluerit, ei, a quo amatur, malum inferre conabitur, qui quidem affectus *crudelitas* appellatur, praecipue si illum, qui amat, nullam odii communem causam praebuisse creditur.", "en": "But if hatred prevails, he will strive to inflict evil on the one by whom he is loved; and this affect is called cruelty, especially if it is believed that the one who loves has given no common cause for hatred."}, {"id": "E3P42", "p": "part3", "l": "3P42", "la": "Qui in aliquem amore aut spe gloriae motus beneficium contulit, contristabitur, si viderit, beneficium ingrato animo accipi.", "en": "He who has conferred a benefit on someone, moved by love or by hope of glory, will be saddened if he sees that the benefit is received with an ungrateful spirit."}, {"id": "E3P42D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P42D", "la": "Qui rem aliquam sibi similem amat, conatur, quantum potest, efficere ut ab ipsa contra ametur (per prop. 33. huius). Qui igitur prae amore in aliquem beneficium contulit, id facit desiderio, quo tenetur, ut contra ametur, hoc est (per prop. 34. huius) spe gloriae sive (per schol. prop. 30. huius) laetitiae; adeoque (per prop. 12. huius) hanc gloriae causam, quantum potest, imaginari, sive ut actu existentem contemplari conabitur. At (per hypothesin) aliud imaginatur, quod eiusdem causae existentiam secludit. Ergo (per prop. 19. huius) eo ipso contristabitur. Q. E. D.", "en": "He who loves a thing like himself strives, as far as he can, to bring it about that he is loved by it in return (by Prop. 33 of this Part). He, therefore, who has conferred a benefit on someone from love does it from the longing by which he is held, to be loved in return, that is (by Prop. 34 of this Part), from hope of glory, or (by Schol. of Prop. 30 of this Part) of joy; and so (by Prop. 12 of this Part) he will strive, as far as he can, to imagine this cause of glory, or to contemplate it as actually existing. But (by hypothesis) he imagines something else which excludes the existence of this cause. Therefore (by Prop. 19 of this Part) he will thereby be saddened. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P43", "p": "part3", "l": "3P43", "la": "Odium reciproco odio augetur, et amore contra deleri potest.", "en": "Hatred is increased by reciprocal hatred, and can, on the other hand, be destroyed by love."}, {"id": "E3P43D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P43D", "la": "Qui eum, quem odit, odio contra erga se affectum esse imaginatur, eo ipso (per prop. 40. huius) novum odium oritur durante (per hypothesin) adhuc primo. Sed si contra eundem amore erga se affectum esse imaginetur, quatenus hoc imaginatur, eatenus (per prop. 30. huius) se ipsum cum laetitia contemplatur, et eatenus (per prop. 29. huius) eidem placere conabitur, hoc est (per prop. 41. huius) eatenus conatur ipsum odio non habere nullaque tristitia afficere; qui quidem conatus (per prop. 37. huius) maior vel minor erit pro ratione affectus, ex quo oritur. Atque adeo si maior fuerit illo, qui ex odio oritur, et quo rem, quam odit (per prop. 26. huius) tristitia afficere conatur, ei praevalebit, et odium ex animo delebit. Q. E. D.", "en": "He who imagines that the one he hates is affected with hatred towards him in return thereby (by Prop. 40 of this Part) has a new hatred arise, while (by hypothesis) the first still lasts. But if, on the other hand, he imagines that the same person is affected with love towards him, then insofar as he imagines this, he contemplates himself with joy (by Prop. 30 of this Part), and to that extent (by Prop. 29 of this Part) he will strive to please him, that is (by Prop. 41 of this Part), to that extent he strives not to hate him and to affect him with no sadness; and this striving (by Prop. 37 of this Part) will be greater or less in proportion to the affect from which it arises. And so, if it is greater than that which arises from hatred, and by which he strives (by Prop. 26 of this Part) to affect the thing he hates with sadness, it will prevail over it and destroy the hatred from his mind. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P44", "p": "part3", "l": "3P44", "la": "Odium, quod amore plane vincitur, in amorem transit; et amor propterea maior est, quam si odium non praecessisset.", "en": "Hatred which is completely conquered by love passes into love; and the love is therefore greater than if hatred had not preceded it."}, {"id": "E3P44D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P44D", "la": "Eodem modo procedit, ac prop. 38. huius. Nam qui rem, quam odit, sive quam cum tristitia contemplari solebat, amare incipit, eo ipso, quod amat, laetatur et huic laetitiae, quam amor involvit (vide eius defin. in schol. prop. 13. huius), illa etiam accedit, quae ex eo oritur, quod conatus amovendi tristitiam, quam odium involvit (ut in prop. 37. huius ostendimus), prorsus iuvatur, concomitante idea eius, quem odio habuit, tamquam causa.", "en": "It proceeds in the same way as Prop. 38 of this Part. For he who begins to love a thing he hates, or which he used to contemplate with sadness, rejoices from the very fact that he loves; and to this joy which love involves (see its definition in the Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part) there is also added the joy which arises from the fact that the striving to remove the sadness which hatred involves (as we have shown in Prop. 37 of this Part) is entirely aided, accompanied by the idea of the one he hated as cause."}, {"id": "E3P44S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P44S", "la": "Quamvis res ita se habeat, nemo tamen conabitur rem aliquam odio habere vel tristitia affici, ut maiore hac laetitia fruatur; hoc est, nemo spe damnum recuperandi damnum sibi inferri cupiet, nec aegrotare desiderabit spe convalescendi. Nam unusquisque suum esse conservare et tristitiam, quantum potest, amovere semper conabitur. Quod si contra concipi posset, hominem posse cupere aliquem odio habere, ut eum postea maiore amore prosequatur, tum eundem odio habere semper desiderabit. Nam quo odium maius fuerit, eo amor erit maior, atque adeo desiderabit semper, ut odium magis magisque augeatur, et eadem de causa homo magis ac magis aegrotare conabitur, ut maiore laetitia ex restauranda valetudine postea fruatur; atque adeo semper aegrotare conabitur, quod (per prop. 6. huius) est absurdum.", "en": "Although this is so, no one will strive to hate a thing, or to be affected with sadness, in order to enjoy this greater joy; that is, no one will desire to suffer harm in the hope of recovering from it, nor long to be ill in the hope of getting well. For each will always strive to preserve his being and to remove sadness as far as he can. But if, on the contrary, it could be conceived that a man could desire to hate someone in order to love him the more afterwards, then he would always desire to hate him. For the greater the hatred had been, the greater the love will be; and so he would always desire that the hatred should be increased more and more; and for the same reason a man would strive to be more and more ill, in order to enjoy a greater joy afterwards from the restoring of his health; and so he would always strive to be ill, which (by Prop. 6 of this Part) is absurd."}, {"id": "E3P45", "p": "part3", "l": "3P45", "la": "Si quis aliquem sibi similem odio in rem sibi similem, quam amat, affectum esse imaginatur, eum odio habebit.", "en": "If someone imagines that someone like himself is affected with hatred towards a thing like himself which he loves, he will hate him."}, {"id": "E3P45D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P45D", "la": "Nam res amata eum, qui ipsam odit, odio contra habet (per prop. 40. huius). Adeoque amans, qui aliquem imaginatur rem amatam odio habere, eo ipso rem amatam odio, hoc est (per schol. prop. 13. huius) tristitia affectam esse imaginatur, et consequenter (per prop. 21. huius) contristatur, idque concomitante idea eius, qui rem amatam odit, tamquam causa, hoc est (per schol. prop. 13. huius) ipsum odio habebit. Q. E. D.", "en": "For the thing loved hates in return the one who hates it (by Prop. 40 of this Part). And so the lover, who imagines that someone hates the thing loved, thereby imagines that the thing loved is affected with hatred, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), with sadness; and consequently (by Prop. 21 of this Part) he is saddened, and that accompanied by the idea of the one who hates the thing loved as cause, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part), he will hate him. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P46", "p": "part3", "l": "3P46", "la": "Si quis ab aliquo cuiusdam classis, sive nationis a sua diversae, laetitia vel tristitia affectus fuerit, concomitante eius idea sub nomine universali classis vel nationis tamquam causa, is non tantum illum, sed omnes eiusdem classis vel nationis amabit vel odio habebit.", "en": "If someone has been affected with joy or sadness by someone of a class, or nation, different from his own, accompanied by the idea of him under the universal name of the class or nation as cause, he will love or hate not only him, but all of the same class or nation."}, {"id": "E3P46D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P46D", "la": "Huius rei demonstratio patet ex prop. 16. huius partis.", "en": "The demonstration of this is evident from Prop. 16 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3P47", "p": "part3", "l": "3P47", "la": "Laetitia, quae ex eo oritur, quod scilicet rem, quam odimus, destrui aut alio malo affici imaginamur, non oritur absque ulla animi tristitia.", "en": "The joy which arises from our imagining that a thing we hate is destroyed or affected with some other evil does not arise without some sadness of mind."}, {"id": "E3P47D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P47D", "la": "Patet ex prop. 27. huius. Nam quatenus rem nobis similem tristitia affici imaginamur, eatenus contristamur.", "en": "This is evident from Prop. 27 of this Part. For insofar as we imagine a thing like us to be affected with sadness, we are saddened."}, {"id": "E3P47S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P47S", "la": "Potest haec propositio etiam demonstrari ex coroll. prop. 17. P. 2. Quoties enim rei recordamur, quamvis ipsa actu non existat, eandem tamen ut praesentem contemplamur, corpusque eodem modo afficitur. Quare quatenus rei memoria viget, eatenus homo determinatur ad eandem cum tristitia contemplandum; quae determinatio manente adhuc rei imagine, coercetur quidem memoria illarum rerum, quae huius existentiam secludunt, sed non tollitur. Atque adeo homo eatenus tantum laetatur, quatenus haec determinatio coercetur; et hinc fit, ut haec laetitia, quae ex rei, quam odimus malo oritur, toties repetatur, quoties eiusdem rei recordamur. Nam, uti diximus, quando eiusdem rei imago excitatur, quia haec ipsius rei existentiam involvit, hominem determinat ad rem cum eadem tristitia contemplandum, qua eandem contemplari solebat, cum ipsa existeret. Sed quia eiusdem rei imagini alias iunxit, quae eiusdem existentiam secludunt, ideo haec ad tristitiam determinatio statim coercetur, et homo de novo laetatur, et hoc toties, quoties haec repetitio fit. Atque haec eadem est causa, cur homines laetantur, quoties alicuius iam praeteriti mali recordantur, et cur pericula, a quibus liberati sunt, narrare gaudeant. Nam ubi aliquod periculum imaginantur, idem veluti adhuc futurum contemplantur, et ad id metuendum determinantur, quae determinatio de novo coercetur idea libertatis, quam huius periculi ideae iunxerunt, cum ab eodem liberati sunt, quaeque eos de novo securos reddit; atque adeo de novo laetantur.", "en": "This Proposition can also be demonstrated from the Cor. of Prop. 17 of Part II. For whenever we recollect a thing, even though it does not actually exist, we nevertheless contemplate it as present, and the body is affected in the same way. So insofar as the memory of the thing is vivid, the man is determined to contemplate it with sadness; and this determination, while the image of the thing still remains, is indeed restrained by the memory of those things which exclude its existence, but is not taken away. And so the man rejoices only insofar as this determination is restrained; and hence it comes about that this joy which arises from the evil of the thing we hate is repeated as often as we recollect that thing. For, as we have said, when the image of that thing is aroused, because it involves the existence of the thing itself, it determines the man to contemplate the thing with the same sadness with which he used to contemplate it when it existed. But because he has joined to the image of the thing other images which exclude its existence, this determination to sadness is immediately restrained, and the man rejoices anew; and this as often as the repetition occurs. And this is the same cause why men rejoice whenever they recollect some evil already past, and why they enjoy telling of the dangers from which they have been freed. For when they imagine some danger, they contemplate it as if still future, and are determined to fear it; and this determination is restrained anew by the idea of freedom which they joined to the idea of this danger when they were freed from it, and which makes them confident anew; and so they rejoice anew."}, {"id": "E3P48", "p": "part3", "l": "3P48", "la": "Amor et odium ex. gr. erga Petrum destruitur, si tristitia, quam hoc, et laetitia, quam ille involvit, ideae alterius causae iungatur; et eatenus uterque diminuitur, quatenus imaginamur Petrum non solum fuisse alterutrius causam.", "en": "Love and hatred, for example towards Peter, are destroyed if the sadness which the latter involves, and the joy which the former involves, are joined to the idea of another cause; and each is diminished insofar as we imagine that Peter was not the only cause of either."}, {"id": "E3P48D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P48D", "la": "Patet ex sola amoris et odii definitione, quam vide in schol. prop. 13. huius. Nam propter hoc solum laetitia vocatur amor, et tristitia odium erga Petrum, quia scilicet Petrus huius vel illius affectus causa esse consideratur. Hoc itaque prorsus vel ex parte sublato affectus quoque erga Petrum prorsus vel ex parte diminuitur. Q. E. D.", "en": "This is evident from the mere definition of love and hatred, which see in the Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part. For joy is called love, and sadness hatred, towards Peter only because Peter is considered to be the cause of this or that affect. So when this is taken away, entirely or in part, the affect towards Peter is also diminished, entirely or in part. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P49", "p": "part3", "l": "3P49", "la": "Amor et odium erga rem, quam liberam esse imaginamur, maior ex pari causa uterque debet esse, quam erga necessariam.", "en": "Love and hatred towards a thing we imagine to be free must both, from an equal cause, be greater than towards a necessary thing."}, {"id": "E3P49D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P49D", "la": "Res, quam liberam esse imaginamur, debet (per defin. 7. P. 1.) per se absque aliis percipi. Si igitur eandem laetitiae vel tristitiae causam esse imaginemur, eo ipso (per schol. prop. 13. huius) eandem amabimus vel odio habebimus, idque (per prop. praeced.) summo amore vel odio, qui ex dato affectu oriri potest. Sed si rem, quae eiusdem affectus est causa, ut necessariam imaginemur, tum (per eandem defin. 7. P. 1.) ipsam non solam, sed cum aliis eiusdem affectus causam esse imaginabimur; atque adeo (per prop. praeced.) amor et odium erga ipsam minor erit. Q. E. D.", "en": "A thing which we imagine to be free must (by Def. 7 of Part I) be perceived through itself without others. If, therefore, we imagine it to be the cause of joy or sadness, we shall thereby (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part) love or hate it, and that (by the preceding Prop.) with the greatest love or hatred which can arise from the given affect. But if we imagine the thing which is the cause of this affect as necessary, then (by the same Def. 7 of Part I) we shall imagine it to be the cause of this affect not alone but with others; and so (by the preceding Prop.) love and hatred towards it will be less. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P49S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P49S", "la": "Hinc sequitur homines, quia se liberos esse existimant, maiore amore vel odio se invicem prosequi, quam alia; ad quod accedit affectuum imitatio, de qua vide prop. 27., 34., 40. et 43. huius.", "en": "From this it follows that men, because they think themselves free, pursue one another with greater love or hatred than they do other things; to which is added the imitation of the affects, on which see Props. 27, 34, 40 and 43 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3P50", "p": "part3", "l": "3P50", "la": "Res quaecumque potest esse per accidens spei aut metus causa.", "en": "Any thing can be, accidentally, the cause of hope or fear."}, {"id": "E3P50D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P50D", "la": "Haec propositio eadem via demonstratur, qua prop. 15. huius, quam vide una cum schol. prop. 18. huius.", "en": "This Proposition is demonstrated in the same way as Prop. 15 of this Part, which see together with the Scholium of Prop. 18 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3P50S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P50S", "la": "Res, quae per accidens spei aut metus sunt causae, *bona* aut *mala omina* vocantur. Deinde quatenus haec eadem omina sunt spei aut metus causa, eatenus (per defin. spei et metus, quam vide in schol. 2. prop. 18. huius) laetitiae aut tristitiae sunt causa, et consequenter (per coroll. prop. 15. huius) eatenus eadem amamus vel odio habemus, et (per prop. 28. huius) tamquam media ad ea, quae speramus, adhibere, vel tamquam obstacula aut metus causas amovere conamur. Praeterea ex prop. 25. huius sequitur, nos natura ita esse constitutos, ut ea quae speramus facile, quae autem timemus, difficile credamus, et ut de iis plus minusve iusto sentiamus. Atque ex his ortae sunt superstitiones, quibus homines ubique conflictantur.", "en": "Things which are accidentally causes of hope or fear are called good or bad omens. Next, insofar as these same omens are causes of hope or fear, they are to that extent (by the definition of hope and fear, which see in the Schol. 2 of Prop. 18 of this Part) causes of joy or sadness; and consequently (by Cor. of Prop. 15 of this Part) we love or hate them to that extent, and (by Prop. 28 of this Part) strive to use them as means to the things we hope for, or to remove them as obstacles or causes of fear. Moreover, it follows from Prop. 25 of this Part that we are so constituted by nature that we easily believe the things we hope for, but with difficulty those we fear, and that we think more or less highly of them than is just. And from these have arisen the superstitions by which men are everywhere troubled."}, {"id": "E3P50S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P50S", "la": "Ceterum non puto operae esse pretium, animi hic ostendere fluctuationes, quae ex spe et metu oriuntur; quandoquidem ex sola horum affectuum definitione sequitur, non dari spem sine metu, neque metum sine spe (ut fusius suo loco explicabimus); et praeterea quandoquidem quatenus aliquid speramus aut metuimus, eatenus idem amamus vel odio habemus. Atque adeo quicquid de amore et odio diximus, facile unusquisque spei et metui applicare poterit.", "en": "But I do not think it worth the effort to show here the fluctuations of mind which arise from hope and fear; since it follows from the mere definition of these affects that there is no hope without fear, nor fear without hope (as we shall explain more fully in its place); and moreover, since insofar as we hope for or fear something, we love or hate it. And so whatever we have said of love and hatred, anyone can easily apply to hope and fear."}, {"id": "E3P51", "p": "part3", "l": "3P51", "la": "Diversi homines ab uno eodemque obiecto diversimode affici possunt, et unus idemque homo ab uno eodemque obiecto potest diversis temporibus diversimode affici.", "en": "Different men can be affected in different ways by one and the same object, and one and the same man can be affected in different ways by one and the same object at different times."}, {"id": "E3P51D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P51D", "la": "Corpus humanum (per postul. 3. P. 2.) a corporibus externis plurimis modis afficitur. Possunt igitur eodem tempore duo homines diversimode esse affecti; atque adeo (per axiom. 1. quod est post lem. 3. quod vide post prop. 13. P. 2.) ab uno eodemque obiecto possunt diversimode affici. Deinde (per idem postul.) corpus humanum potest iam hoc, iam alio modo esse affectum; et consequenter (per idem axiom.) ab uno eodemque obiecto diversis temporibus diversimode affici. Q. E. D.", "en": "The human body (by Post. 3 of Part II) is affected by external bodies in very many ways. Two men, therefore, can be affected in different ways at the same time; and so (by Ax. 1, which is after Lemma 3, which see after Prop. 13 of Part II) they can be affected in different ways by one and the same object. Next (by the same Postulate), the human body can be affected now in this way, now in another; and consequently (by the same Axiom) it can be affected in different ways by one and the same object at different times. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P51S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P51S", "la": "Videmus itaque fieri posse, ut quod hic amat, alter odio habeat; et quod hic metuit, alter non metuat; et ut unus idemque homo iam amet, quod antea oderit, et ut iam audeat, quod antea timuit etc. Deinde quia unusquisque ex suo affectu iudicat, quid bonum, quid malum, quid melius et quid peius sit (vide schol. prop. 39. huius), sequitur homines tam iudicio, quam affectu variare posse; et hinc fit, ut, cum alios aliis comparamus, ex sola affectuum differentia a nobis distinguantur, et ut alios intrepidos, alios timidos, alios denique alio nomine appellemus. Ex. gr. illum ego intrepidum vocabo, qui malum contemnit, quod ego timere soleo; et si praeterea ad hoc attendam, quod eius cupiditas malum inferendi ei quem odit, et benefaciendi ei quem amat, non coercetur timore mali, a quo ego contineri soleo, ipsum audacem appellabo. Deinde ille mihi timidus videbitur, qui malum timet, quod ego contemnere soleo, et si insuper ad hoc attendam, quod eius cupiditas coercetur timore mali, quod me continere nequit, ipsum pusillanimem esse dicam, et sic unusquisque iudicabit.", "en": "We see, then, that it can happen that what one man loves another hates; that what one man fears another does not fear; and that one and the same man now loves what he previously hated, and now dares what he previously feared, etc. Next, because each judges from his own affect what is good, what evil, what better and what worse (see Schol. of Prop. 39 of this Part), it follows that men can vary as much in judgment as in affect; and hence it comes about that, when we compare some with others, they are distinguished by us only by the difference of their affects, and that we call some fearless, others timid, others by another name. For example, I shall call him fearless who despises an evil which I usually fear; and if, besides, I attend to the fact that his desire to inflict evil on one he hates and to do good to one he loves is not restrained by timidity regarding an evil by which I am usually held back, I shall call him daring. Next, he will seem timid to me who fears an evil which I usually despise; and if, moreover, I attend to the fact that his desire is restrained by timidity regarding an evil which cannot hold me back, I shall say that he is cowardly; and so each will judge."}, {"id": "E3P51S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P51S", "la": "Denique ex hac hominis natura et iudicii inconstantia, ut et quod homo saepe ex solo affectu de rebus iudicat, et quod res, quas ad laetitiam vel tristitiam facere credit, quasque propterea (per prop. 28. huius) ut fiant, promovere vel amovere conatur, saepe non nisi imaginariae sint, ut iam taceam alia, quae in P. 2. ostendimus, de rerum incertitudine, facile concipimus hominem posse saepe in causa esse, tam ut contristetur, quam ut laetetur, sive ut tam tristitia, quam laetitia, afficiatur, concomitante idea sui tamquam causa. Atque adeo facile intelligimus, quid poenitentia et quid acquiescentia in se ipso sit. Nempe poenitentia est tristitia concomitante idea sui, et acquiescentia in se ipso est laetitia concomitante idea sui tamquam causa, et hi affectus vehementissimi sunt, quia homines se liberos esse credunt. Vide prop. 49. huius.", "en": "Finally, from this nature of man and the inconstancy of his judgment, and also from the fact that man often judges of things from his affect alone, and that the things which he believes make for joy or sadness, and which he therefore (by Prop. 28 of this Part) strives to promote or remove, are often only imaginary, not to mention the other things we have shown in Part II about the uncertainty of things, we easily conceive that a man can often be the cause both of his being saddened and of his rejoicing, or of his being affected both with sadness and with joy, accompanied by the idea of himself as cause. And so we easily understand what repentance and what self-contentment are. Namely, repentance is sadness accompanied by the idea of oneself, and self-contentment is joy accompanied by the idea of oneself as cause; and these affects are very violent, because men believe themselves free. See Prop. 49 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3P52", "p": "part3", "l": "3P52", "la": "Obiectum, quod simul cum aliis antea vidimus, vel quod nihil habere imaginamur, nisi quod commune est pluribus, non tamdiu contemplabimur, ac illud, quod aliquid singulare habere imaginamur.", "en": "An object which we have previously seen together with others, or which we imagine to have nothing but what is common to many, we shall not contemplate for as long as one which we imagine to have something singular."}, {"id": "E3P52D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P52D", "la": "Simulatque obiectum, quod cum aliis vidimus, imaginamur, statim et aliorum recordamur (per prop. 18. P. 2., cuius etiam schol. vide), et sic ex unius contemplatione statim in contemplationem alterius incidimus. Atque eadem est ratio obiecti, quod nihil habere imaginamur, nisi quod commune est pluribus. Nam eo ipso supponimus, nos nihil in eo contemplari, quod antea cum aliis non viderimus. Verum cum supponimus, nos in obiecto aliquo aliquid singulare, quod antea nunquam vidimus, imaginari, nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod mens, dum illud obiectum contemplatur, nullum aliud in se habeat, in cuius contemplationem ex contemplatione illius incidere potest. Atque adeo ad illud solum contemplandum determinata est. Ergo obiectum etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "As soon as we imagine an object which we have seen with others, we immediately recollect the others too (by Prop. 18 of Part II, whose Scholium see also), and so from the contemplation of one we immediately fall into the contemplation of another. And the reasoning is the same for an object which we imagine to have nothing but what is common to many. For we thereby suppose that we contemplate nothing in it which we have not previously seen with others. But when we suppose that we imagine in some object something singular which we have never seen before, we are saying nothing but that the mind, while it contemplates that object, has in itself no other into whose contemplation it can fall from the contemplation of that one. And so it is determined to contemplate only that object. Therefore an object, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P52S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P52S", "la": "Haec mentis affectio sive rei singularis imaginatio, quatenus sola in mente versatur, vocatur *admiratio*, quae si ab obiecto, quod timemus, moveatur, *consternatio* dicitur, quia mali admiratio hominem suspensum in sola sui contemplatione ita tenet, ut de aliis cogitare non valeat, quibus illud malum vitare posset. Sed si id, quod admiramur, sit hominis alicuius prudentia, industria vel aliquid huiusmodi, quia eo ipso hominem nobis longe antecellere contemplamur, tum admiratio vocatur *veneratio*; alias *horror*, si hominis iram, invidiam etc. admiramur. Deinde, si hominis, quem amamus, prudentiam, industriam etc. admiramur, amor eo ipso (per prop. 12. huius) maior erit, et hunc amorem admirationi sive venerationi iunctum *devotionem* vocamus. Et ad hunc modum concipere etiam possumus odium, spem, securitatem et alios affectus admirationi iunctos; atque adeo plures affectus deducere poterimus, quam qui receptis vocabulis indicari solent. Unde apparet, affectuum nomina inventa esse magis ex eorum vulgari usu, quam ex eorundem accurata cognitione.", "en": "This affection of the mind, or imagination of a singular thing, insofar as it alone occupies the mind, is called wonder; which, if it is moved by an object we fear, is called consternation, because wonder at an evil holds a man so suspended in the contemplation of it alone that he is not able to think of other things by which he could avoid that evil. But if what we wonder at is some man’s prudence, industry or something of this kind, then, because we thereby contemplate the man as far surpassing us, the wonder is called veneration; otherwise horror, if we wonder at a man’s anger, envy, etc. Next, if we wonder at the prudence, industry, etc., of a man we love, the love will thereby (by Prop. 12 of this Part) be greater, and this love joined to wonder, or veneration, we call devotion. And in this way we can also conceive hatred, hope, confidence and other affects joined to wonder; and so we shall be able to deduce more affects than are usually indicated by the accepted words. From this it is evident that the names of the affects have been invented more from their common use than from an accurate knowledge of them."}, {"id": "E3P52S.2", "p": "part3", "l": "3P52S", "la": "Admirationi opponitur *contemptus* cuius tamen causa haec plerumque est, quod scilicet ex eo, quod aliquem rem aliquam admirari, amare, metuere etc. videmus, vel ex eo, quod res aliqua primo aspectu apparet similis rebus, quas admiramur, amamus, metuimus etc. (per prop. 15 cum eius coroll. et prop. 27. huius) determinamur ad eandem rem admirandum, amandum, metuendum etc. Sed si ex ipsius rei praesentia vel accuratiore contemplatione id omne de eadem negare cogamur, quod causa admirationis, amoris, metus etc. esse potest, tum mens ex ipsa rei praesentia magis ad ea cogitandum, quae in obiecto non sunt quam quae in ipso sunt, determinata manet; cum tamen contra ex obiecti praesentia id praecipue cogitare soleat, quod in obiecto est. Porro sicut devotio ex rei, quam amamus, admiratione; sic *irrisio* ex rei, quam odimus vel metuimus, contemptu oritur, et *dedignatio* ex stultitiae contemptu, sicuti veneratio ex admiratione prudentiae. Possumus denique amorem, spem, gloriam et alios affectus iunctos contemptui concipere, atque inde alios praeterea affectus deducere, quos etiam nullo singulari vocabulo ab aliis distinguere solemus.", "en": "To wonder is opposed contempt, whose cause, however, is generally this: from the fact that we see someone wonder at, love, fear, etc., some thing, or from the fact that some thing at first sight appears like things we wonder at, love, fear, etc., we are determined (by Prop. 15 with its Cor. and Prop. 27 of this Part) to wonder at, love, fear, etc., that same thing. But if, from the presence of the thing itself or from a more accurate contemplation of it, we are compelled to deny of it everything which can be a cause of wonder, love, fear, etc., then the mind remains determined by the very presence of the thing to think more of what is not in the object than of what is in it; whereas, on the contrary, from the presence of an object it usually thinks chiefly of what is in the object. Further, as devotion arises from wonder at a thing we love, so mockery arises from contempt of a thing we hate or fear, and disdain from contempt of folly, as veneration from wonder at prudence. Finally, we can conceive love, hope, glory and other affects joined to contempt, and from these deduce yet other affects, which we are also not accustomed to distinguish from the others by any particular word."}, {"id": "E3P53", "p": "part3", "l": "3P53", "la": "Cum mens se ipsam suamque agendi potentiam contemplatur, laetatur; et eo magis, quo se suamque agendi potentiam distinctius imaginatur.", "en": "When the mind contemplates itself and its power of acting, it rejoices; and the more so, the more distinctly it imagines itself and its power of acting."}, {"id": "E3P53D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P53D", "la": "Homo se ipsum non cognoscit, nisi per affectiones sui corporis earumque ideas (per prop. 19. et 23. P. 2.). Cum ergo fit, ut mens se ipsam possit contemplari, eo ipso ad maiorem perfectionem transire, hoc est (per schol. prop. 11. huius) laetitia affici supponitur, et eo maiore quo se suamque agendi potentiam distinctius imaginari potest. Q. E. D.", "en": "A man does not know himself except through the affections of his body and their ideas (by Props. 19 and 23 of Part II). When, therefore, it happens that the mind can contemplate itself, it is thereby supposed to pass to a greater perfection, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part), to be affected with joy, and the more so, the more distinctly it can imagine itself and its power of acting. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P53C", "p": "part3", "l": "3P53C", "la": "Haec laetitia magis magisque fovetur, quo magis homo se ab aliis laudari imaginatur. Nam quo magis se ab aliis laudari imaginatur, eo maiore laetitia alios ab ipso affici imaginatur, idque concomitante idea sui (per schol. prop. 29. huius). Atque adeo (per prop. 27 huius) ipse maiore laetitia concomitante idea sui afficitur. Q. E. D.", "en": "This joy is fostered more and more, the more a man imagines himself to be praised by others. For the more he imagines himself to be praised by others, the greater the joy with which he imagines others to be affected by him, and that accompanied by the idea of himself (by Schol. of Prop. 29 of this Part). And so (by Prop. 27 of this Part) he himself is affected with a greater joy accompanied by the idea of himself. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P54", "p": "part3", "l": "3P54", "la": "Mens ea tantum imaginari conatur, quae ipsius agendi potentiam ponunt.", "en": "The mind strives to imagine only those things which posit its power of acting."}, {"id": "E3P54D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P54D", "la": "Mentis conatus sive potentia est ipsa ipsius mentis essentia (per prop. 7. huius). Mentis autem essentia (ut per se notum) id tantum, quod mens est et potest, affirmat; at non id, quod non est neque potest. Adeoque id tantum imaginari conatur, quod ipsius agendi potentiam affirmat sive ponit. Q. E. D.", "en": "The striving, or power, of the mind is the very essence of the mind (by Prop. 7 of this Part). But the essence of the mind (as is self-evident) affirms only what the mind is and can do, not what it is not and cannot do. And so it strives to imagine only what affirms, or posits, its power of acting. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P55", "p": "part3", "l": "3P55", "la": "Cum mens suam impotentiam imaginatur, eo ipso contristatur.", "en": "When the mind imagines its lack of power, it is thereby saddened."}, {"id": "E3P55D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P55D", "la": "Mentis essentia id tantum, quod mens est et potest, affirmat, sive de natura mentis est ea tantummodo imaginari, quae ipsius agendi potentiam ponunt (per prop. praeced.). Cum itaque dicimus, quod mens, dum se ipsam contemplatur, suam imaginatur impotentiam, nihil aliud dicimus, quam quod, dum mens aliquid imaginari conatur, quod ipsius agendi potentiam ponit, hic eius conatus coercetur, sive (per schol. prop. 11. huius) quod ipsa contristatur. Q. E. D.", "en": "The essence of the mind affirms only what the mind is and can do, or it is of the nature of the mind to imagine only those things which posit its power of acting (by the preceding Prop.). When, therefore, we say that the mind, while it contemplates itself, imagines its lack of power, we are saying nothing but that, while the mind strives to imagine something which posits its power of acting, this striving of it is restrained, or (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part) that it is saddened. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P55C1", "p": "part3", "l": "3P55C1", "la": "Haec tristitia magis ac magis fovetur, si se ab aliis vituperari imaginatur; quod eodem modo demonstratur ac coroll. prop. 53. huius.", "en": "This sadness is fostered more and more if he imagines himself to be blamed by others; which is demonstrated in the same way as the Cor. of Prop. 53 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3P55S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P55S", "la": "Haec tristitia concomitante idea nostrae imbecillitatis *humilitas* appellatur; laetitia autem, quae ex contemplatione nostri oritur, *philautia* vel *acquiescentia in se ipso* vocatur. Et quoniam haec toties repetitur, quoties homo suas virtutes sive suam agendi potentiam contemplatur, hinc ergo etiam fit, ut unusquisque facta sua narrare, suique tam corporis, quam animi vires ostentare gestiat, et ut homines hac de causa sibi invicem molesti sint. Ex quibus iterum sequitur, homines natura esse invidos (vide schol. prop. 24. et schol. prop. 32. huius) sive ob suorum aequalium imbecillitatem gaudere, et contra propter eorundem virtutem contristari. Nam quoties unusquisque suas actiones imaginatur, toties laetitia (per prop. 53. huius) afficitur, et eo maiore, quo actiones plus perfectionis exprimere et easdem distinctius imaginatur, hoc est (per illa quae in schol. 1. prop. 40. P. 2. dicta sunt) quo magis easdem ab aliis distinguere et ut res singulares contemplari potest.", "en": "This sadness, accompanied by the idea of our weakness, is called humility; but the joy which arises from the contemplation of ourselves is called self-love or self-contentment. And since this is repeated as often as a man contemplates his virtues, or his power of acting, it also comes about that everyone is eager to tell of his deeds and to show off his powers, both of body and of mind, and that men for this reason are troublesome to one another. From this it follows again that men are by nature envious (see Schol. of Prop. 24 and Schol. of Prop. 32 of this Part), or are glad of the weakness of their equals and, on the other hand, saddened by their virtue. For whenever someone imagines his own actions, he is affected with joy (by Prop. 53 of this Part), and with a joy the greater, the more perfection he imagines his actions to express, and the more distinctly he imagines them, that is (by what was said in Schol. 1 of Prop. 40 of Part II), the more he can distinguish them from others and contemplate them as singular things."}, {"id": "E3P55S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P55S", "la": "Quare unusquisque ex contemplatione sui tunc maxime gaudebit, quando aliquid in se contemplatur, quod de reliquis negat. Sed si id, quod de se affirmat, ad universalem hominis vel animalis ideam refert, non tantopere gaudebit; et contra contristabitur, si suas ad aliorum actiones comparatas imbecilliores esse imaginetur, quam quidem tristitiam (per prop. 28. huius) amovere conabitur, idque suorum aequalium actiones perperam interpretando, vel suas quantum potest adornando. Apparet igitur homines natura proclives esse ad odium et invidiam, ad quam accedit ipsa educatio. Nam parentes solo honoris et invidiae stimulo liberos ad virtutem concitare solent. Sed scrupulus forsan remanet, quod non raro hominum virtutes admiremur eosque veneremur. Hunc ergo ut amoveam sequens addam corollarium.", "en": "Therefore each will rejoice most in the contemplation of himself when he contemplates something in himself which he denies of the others. But if he refers what he affirms of himself to the universal idea of man or of animal, he will not rejoice so much; and, on the other hand, he will be saddened if he imagines that his actions, compared with those of others, are weaker; and this sadness (by Prop. 28 of this Part) he will strive to remove, either by misinterpreting the actions of his equals or by embellishing his own as much as he can. It is evident, therefore, that men are by nature inclined to hatred and envy, to which their very education contributes. For parents usually spur their children to virtue only by the goad of honour and envy. But perhaps a doubt remains, because we not infrequently wonder at men’s virtues and venerate the men themselves. To remove it, then, I shall add the following corollary."}, {"id": "E3P55C2", "p": "part3", "l": "3P55C2", "la": "Nemo virtutem alicui nisi aequali invidet.", "en": "No one envies anyone’s virtue unless he is his equal."}, {"id": "E3P55C2D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P55C2D", "la": "Invidia est ipsum odium (vide schol. prop. 24. huius) sive (per schol. prop. 13. huius) tristitia hoc est (per schol. prop. 11. huius) affectio, qua hominis agendi potentia seu conatus coercetur. At homo (per schol. prop. 9. huius) nihil agere conatur, neque cupit, nisi quod ex data sua natura sequi potest. Ergo homo nullam de se agendi potentiam seu (quod idem est) virtutem praedicari cupiet, quae naturae alterius est propria et suae aliena. Adeoque eius cupiditas coerceri, hoc est (per schol. prop. 11. huius) ipse contristari nequit ex eo, quod aliquam virtutem in aliquo ipsi dissimili contemplatur, et consequenter neque ei invidere poterit; at quidem suo aequali qui cum ipso eiusdem naturae supponitur. Q. E. D.", "en": "Envy is hatred itself (see Schol. of Prop. 24 of this Part), or (by Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part) sadness, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part), an affection by which a man’s power of acting, or striving, is restrained. But a man (by Schol. of Prop. 9 of this Part) strives to do and desires nothing but what can follow from his given nature. Therefore a man will desire no power of acting, or (which is the same) virtue, to be predicated of himself which is proper to another’s nature and foreign to his own. And so his desire cannot be restrained, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part), he cannot be saddened, by the fact that he contemplates some virtue in someone unlike himself, and consequently he will not be able to envy him either; but he can indeed envy his equal, who is supposed to be of the same nature as himself. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P55C2S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P55C2S", "la": "Cum igitur supra in schol. prop. 52. huius dixerimus, nos hominem venerari ex eo, quod ipsius prudentiam, fortitudinem etc. admiramur, id fit (ut ex ipsa prop. patet) quia has virtutes ei singulariter inesse, et non ut nostrae naturae communes imaginamur; adeoque easdem ipsi non magis invidebimus quam arboribus altitudinem et leonibus fortitudinem etc.", "en": "Since, therefore, we said above, in the Schol. of Prop. 52 of this Part, that we venerate a man because we wonder at his prudence, strength of character, etc., this happens (as is evident from the Proposition itself) because we imagine these virtues to be in him singularly, and not as common to our nature; and so we shall not envy him them any more than we envy trees their height or lions their strength, etc."}, {"id": "E3P56", "p": "part3", "l": "3P56", "la": "Laetitiae, tristitiae et cupiditatis, et consequenter uniuscuiusque affectus, qui ex his componitur, ut animi fluctuationis, vel qui ab his derivatur, nempe amoris, odii, spei, metus etc. tot species dantur, quot sunt species obiectorum, a quibus afficimur.", "en": "There are as many species of joy, sadness and desire, and consequently of each affect which is composed of these, like fluctuation of mind, or which is derived from them, namely love, hatred, hope, fear, etc., as there are species of objects by which we are affected."}, {"id": "E3P56D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P56D", "la": "Laetitia et tristitia, et consequenter affectus, qui ex his componuntur vel ex his derivantur, passiones sunt (per schol. prop. 11. huius); nos autem (per prop. 1. huius) necessario patimur, quatenus ideas habemus inadaequatas; et quatenus easdem habemus (per prop. 3. huius), eatenus tantum patimur, hoc est (vide schol. prop. 40. P. 2.) eatenus tantum necessario patimur, quatenus imaginamur, sive (vide prop. 17. P. 2. cum eius schol.) quatenus afficimur affectu, qui naturam nostri corporis et naturam corporis externi involvit. Natura igitur uniuscuiusque passionis ita necessario debet explicari, ut obiecti a quo afficimur, natura exprimatur. Nempe laetitia, quae ex obiecto ex. gr. A oritur, naturam ipsius obiecti A, et laetitia, quae ex obiecto B oritur, ipsius obiecti B naturam involvit; atque adeo hi duo laetitiae affectus natura sunt diversi, quia ex causis diversae naturae oriuntur. Sic etiam tristitiae affectus, qui ex uno obiecto oritur, diversus natura est a tristitia, quae ab alia causa oritur; quod etiam de amore, odio, spe, metu, animi fluctuatione etc. intelligendum est, ac proinde laetitiae, tristitiae, amoris, odii etc. tot species necessario dantur, quot sunt species obiectorum, a quibus afficimur.", "en": "Joy and sadness, and consequently the affects which are composed of them or derived from them, are passions (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part); but we (by Prop. 1 of this Part) are necessarily acted on insofar as we have inadequate ideas; and only insofar as we have them (by Prop. 3 of this Part) are we acted on, that is (see the Schol. of Prop. 40 of Part II), we are necessarily acted on only insofar as we imagine, or (see Prop. 17 of Part II with its Schol.) insofar as we are affected with an affect which involves the nature of our body and the nature of an external body. The nature of each passion, therefore, must necessarily be so explained that the nature of the object by which we are affected is expressed. Namely, the joy which arises from object A, for example, involves the nature of object A itself, and the joy which arises from object B involves the nature of object B itself; and so these two affects of joy are different in nature, because they arise from causes of a different nature. So also the affect of sadness which arises from one object is different in nature from the sadness which arises from another cause; which is also to be understood of love, hatred, hope, fear, fluctuation of mind, etc.; and therefore there are necessarily as many species of joy, sadness, love, hatred, etc., as there are species of objects by which we are affected."}, {"id": "E3P56D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P56D", "la": "At cupiditas est ipsa uniuscuiusque essentia seu natura, quatenus ex data quacumque eius constitutione determinata concipitur ad aliquid agendum. Vide schol. prop. 9. huius. Ergo, prout unusquisque a causis externis hac aut illa laetitiae, tristitiae, amoris, odii etc. specie afficitur, hoc est prout eius natura hoc aut alio modo constituitur, ita eius cupiditas alia atque alia esse et natura unius a natura alterius cupiditatis tantum differre necesse est, quantum affectus, a quibus unaquaeque oritur, inter se differunt. Dantur itaque tot species cupiditatis, quot sunt species laetitiae, tristitiae, amoris etc. et consequenter (per iam ostensa) quot sunt obiectorum species, a quibus afficimur. Q. E. D.", "en": "But desire is the very essence, or nature, of each man insofar as it is conceived to be determined, from any given constitution of it, to do something. See Schol. of Prop. 9 of this Part. Therefore, as each is affected by external causes with this or that species of joy, sadness, love, hatred, etc., that is, as his nature is constituted in this or that way, so his desire must be different and different, and the nature of one desire must differ from the nature of another as much as the affects from which each arises differ from one another. There are, therefore, as many species of desire as there are species of joy, sadness, love, etc., and consequently (by what has already been shown) as there are species of objects by which we are affected. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P56S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P56S", "la": "Inter affectuum species, quae (per prop. praeced.) perplurimae esse debent, insignes sunt *luxuria, ebrietas, libido, avaritia* et *ambitio*, quae non nisi amoris vel cupiditatis sunt notiones, quae huius utriusque affectus naturam explicant per obiecta, ad quae referuntur. Nam per luxuriam, ebrietatem, libidinem, avaritiam et ambitionem nihil aliud intelligimus, quam convivandi, potandi, coeundi, divitiarum et gloriae immoderatum amorem vel cupiditatem. Praeterea hi affectus, quatenus eos per solum obiectum, ad quod referuntur, ab aliis distinguimus, contrarios non habent. Nam temperantia, quam luxuriae, et sobrietas, quam ebrietati, et denique castitas, quam libidini opponere solemus, affectus seu passiones non sunt; sed animi indicant potentiam, quae hos affectus moderatur.", "en": "Among the species of affects, which (by the preceding Prop.) must be very many, notable are gluttony, drunkenness, lust, greed and ambition, which are only notions of love or desire that explain the nature of each of these two affects through the objects to which they are related. For by gluttony, drunkenness, lust, greed and ambition we understand nothing but an immoderate love or desire of feasting, drinking, sexual union, riches and glory. Moreover, these affects, insofar as we distinguish them from others only through the object to which they are related, have no contraries. For temperance, which we usually oppose to gluttony, sobriety, which we oppose to drunkenness, and finally chastity, which we oppose to lust, are not affects, or passions, but indicate a power of the mind which moderates these affects."}, {"id": "E3P56S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P56S", "la": "Ceterum reliquas affectuum species hic explicare nec possum (quia tot sunt quot obiectorum species), nec, si possem, necesse est. Nam ad id quod intendimus, nempe ad affectuum vires et mentis in eosdem potentiam determinandum, nobis sufficit, uniuscuiusque affectus generalem habere definitionem. Sufficit, inquam, nobis affectuum et mentis communes proprietates intelligere, ut determinare possimus, qualis et quanta sit mentis potentia in moderandis et coercendis affectibus. Quamvis itaque magna sit differentia inter hunc et illum amoris, odii vel cupiditatis affectum, ex. gr. inter amorem erga liberos et inter amorem erga uxorem, nobis tamen has differentias cognoscere et affectuum naturam et originem ulterius indagare non est opus.", "en": "But I cannot explain the remaining species of affects here (because there are as many as there are species of objects), nor, if I could, is it necessary. For for our purpose, namely to determine the powers of the affects and the power of the mind over them, it is enough for us to have a general definition of each affect. It is enough, I say, for us to understand the common properties of the affects and of the mind, so that we can determine what kind of power, and how great, the mind has in moderating and restraining the affects. Although, therefore, there is a great difference between this and that affect of love, hatred or desire, for example between love towards children and love towards a wife, it is nevertheless not necessary for us to know these differences, and to investigate further the nature and origin of the affects."}, {"id": "E3P57", "p": "part3", "l": "3P57", "la": "Quilibet uniuscuiusque individui affectus ab affectu alterius tantum discrepat, quantum essentia unius ab essentia alterius differt.", "en": "Each affect of each individual differs from the affect of another as much as the essence of the one differs from the essence of the other."}, {"id": "E3P57D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P57D", "la": "Haec propositio patet ex axiom. 1., quod vide post lem. 3. schol. prop. 13. P. 2. At nihilominus eandem ex trium primitivorum affectuum definitionibus demonstrabimus.", "en": "This Proposition is evident from Ax. 1, which see after Lemma 3, after the Schol. of Prop. 13 of Part II. But nevertheless we shall demonstrate it from the definitions of the three primary affects."}, {"id": "E3P57D.2", "p": "part3", "l": "3P57D", "la": "Omnes affectus ad cupiditatem, laetitiam vel tristitiam referuntur, ut eorum quas dedimus definitiones ostendunt. At cupiditas est ipsa uniuscuiusque natura seu essentia (vide eius defin. in schol. prop. 9. huius); ergo uniuscuiusque individui cupiditas a cupiditate alterius tantum discrepat, quantum natura seu essentia unius ab essentia alterius differt. Laetitia deinde et tristitia passiones sunt, quibus uniuscuiusque potentia seu conatus in suo esse perseverandi augetur vel minuitur, iuvatur vel coercetur (per prop. 11 huius et eius schol.). At per conatum in suo esse perseverandi, quatenus ad mentem et corpus simul refertur, appetitum et cupiditatem intelligimus (vide schol. prop. 9. huius); ergo laetitia et tristitia est ipsa cupiditas sive appetitus quatenus a causis externis augetur vel minuitur, iuvatur vel coercetur, hoc est (per idem schol.) est ipsa cuiusque natura. Atque adeo uniuscuiusque laetitia vel tristitia a laetitia vel tristitia alterius tantum etiam discrepat, quantum natura seu essentia unius ab essentia alterius differt; et consequenter quilibet uniuscuiusque individui affectus ab affectu alterius tantum discrepat etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "All the affects are related to desire, joy or sadness, as the definitions we have given of them show. But desire is the very nature, or essence, of each (see its definition in the Schol. of Prop. 9 of this Part); therefore the desire of each individual differs from the desire of another as much as the nature, or essence, of the one differs from the essence of the other. Joy and sadness, next, are passions by which the power, or striving, of each to persevere in its being is increased or diminished, aided or restrained (by Prop. 11 of this Part and its Schol.). But by the striving to persevere in its being, insofar as it is related to the mind and body together, we understand appetite and desire (see Schol. of Prop. 9 of this Part); therefore joy and sadness are the very desire, or appetite, insofar as it is increased or diminished, aided or restrained by external causes, that is (by the same Scholium), they are the very nature of each. And so the joy or sadness of each also differs from the joy or sadness of another as much as the nature, or essence, of the one differs from the essence of the other; and consequently each affect of each individual differs from the affect of another, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P57S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P57S", "la": "Hinc sequitur affectus animalium, quae irrationalia dicuntur (bruta enim sentire nequaquam dubitare possumus, postquam mentis novimus originem), ab affectibus hominum tantum differre, quantum eorum natura a natura humana differt. Fertur quidem equus et homo libidine procreandi; at ille libidine equina, hic autem humana. Sic etiam libidines et appetitus insectorum, piscium et avium alii atque alii esse debent. Quamvis itaque unumquodque individuum sua qua constat natura, contentum vivat eaque gaudeat; vita tamen illa, qua unumquodque est contentum, et gaudium nihil aliud est, quam idea seu anima eiusdem individui; atque adeo gaudium unius a gaudio alterius tantum natura discrepat, quantum essentia unius ab essentia alterius differt.", "en": "From this it follows that the affects of the animals which are called irrational (for we can by no means doubt that the brutes feel, now that we know the origin of the mind) differ from the affects of men as much as their nature differs from human nature. Both the horse and the man are indeed carried away by the lust to procreate; but the former by an equine lust, the latter by a human one. So also the lusts and appetites of insects, fish and birds must be different and different. Although, therefore, each individual lives content with the nature of which it consists, and rejoices in it, yet that life with which each is content, and its gladness, are nothing but the idea, or soul, of that individual; and so the gladness of one differs in nature from the gladness of another as much as the essence of the one differs from the essence of the other."}, {"id": "E3P57S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P57S", "la": "Denique ex praec. prop. sequitur, non parum etiam interesse inter gaudium, quo ebrius ex. gr. ducitur, et inter gaudium, quo potitur philosophus, quod hic in transitu monere volui. Atque haec de affectibus, qui ad hominem referuntur, quatenus patitur. Superest, ut pauca addam de iis, qui ad eundem referuntur, quatenus agit.", "en": "Finally, from the preceding Proposition it follows that there is no small difference between the gladness by which a drunkard, for example, is led, and the gladness which a philosopher possesses, which I wanted to point out here in passing. So much for the affects which are related to man insofar as he is acted on. It remains for me to add a few things about those which are related to him insofar as he acts."}, {"id": "E3P58", "p": "part3", "l": "3P58", "la": "Praeter laetitiam et cupiditatem quae passiones sunt, alii laetitiae et cupiditatis affectus dantur, qui ad nos, quatenus agimus, referuntur.", "en": "Apart from the joy and desire which are passions, there are other affects of joy and desire which are related to us insofar as we act."}, {"id": "E3P58D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P58D", "la": "Cum mens se ipsam suamque agendi potentiam concipit, laetatur (per prop. 53. huius). Mens autem se ipsam necessario contemplatur, quando veram sive adaequatam ideam concipit (per prop. 43. P. 2.). At mens quasdam ideas adaequatas concipit (per schol. 2. prop. 40. P. 2.). Ergo eatenus etiam laetatur, quatenus ideas adaequatas concipit, hoc est (per prop. 1. huius) quatenus agit. Deinde mens tam quatenus claras et distinctas, quam quatenus confusas habet ideas, in suo esse perseverare conatur (per prop. 9. huius). At per conatum cupiditatem intelligimus (per eiusdem schol.). Ergo cupiditas ad nos refertur etiam quatenus intelligimus, sive (per prop. 1. huius) quatenus agimus. Q. E. D.", "en": "When the mind conceives itself and its power of acting, it rejoices (by Prop. 53 of this Part). But the mind necessarily contemplates itself when it conceives a true, or adequate, idea (by Prop. 43 of Part II). But the mind conceives some adequate ideas (by Schol. 2 of Prop. 40 of Part II). Therefore it also rejoices insofar as it conceives adequate ideas, that is (by Prop. 1 of this Part), insofar as it acts. Next, the mind, both insofar as it has clear and distinct ideas and insofar as it has confused ideas, strives to persevere in its being (by Prop. 9 of this Part). But by striving we understand desire (by the Schol. of the same Prop.). Therefore desire is related to us also insofar as we understand, or (by Prop. 1 of this Part) insofar as we act. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P59", "p": "part3", "l": "3P59", "la": "Inter omnes affectus, qui ad mentem, quatenus agit, referuntur, nulli sunt, quam qui ad laetitiam vel cupiditatem referuntur.", "en": "Among all the affects which are related to the mind insofar as it acts, there are none but those which are related to joy or desire."}, {"id": "E3P59D", "p": "part3", "l": "3P59D", "la": "Omnes affectus ad cupiditatem, laetitiam vel tristitiam referuntur, ut eorum quas dedimus definitiones ostendunt. Per tristitiam autem intelligimus, quod mentis cogitandi potentia minuitur vel coercetur (per prop. 11. huius et eius schol.); adeoque mens quatenus contristatur, eatenus eius intelligendi, hoc est eius agendi potentia (per prop. 1. huius) minuitur vel coercetur. Adeoque nulli tristitiae affectus ad mentem referri possunt, quatenus agit, sed tantum affectus laetitiae et cupiditatis, qui (per prop. praeced.) eatenus etiam ad mentem referuntur. Q. E. D.", "en": "All the affects are related to desire, joy or sadness, as the definitions we have given of them show. But by sadness we understand that the mind’s power of thinking is diminished or restrained (by Prop. 11 of this Part and its Schol.); and so, insofar as the mind is saddened, its power of understanding, that is, its power of acting (by Prop. 1 of this Part), is diminished or restrained. And so no affects of sadness can be related to the mind insofar as it acts, but only affects of joy and desire, which (by the preceding Prop.) are also related to the mind to that extent. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E3P59S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P59S", "la": "Omnes actiones, quae sequuntur ex affectibus, qui ad mentem referuntur, quatenus intelligit, ad *fortitudinem* refero quam in *animositatem* et *generositatem* distinguo. Nam per animositatem intelligo cupiditatem, qua unusquisque conatur suum esse ex solo rationis dictamine conservare. Per generositatem autem cupiditatem intelligo, qua unusquisque ex solo rationis dictamine conatur reliquos homines iuvare et sibi amicitia iungere. Eas itaque actiones, quae solum agentis utile intendunt, ad animositatem, et quae alterius etiam utile intendunt, ad generositatem refero. Temperantia igitur, sobrietas et animi in periculis praesentia etc. animositatis sunt species; modestia autem, clementia etc. species generositatis sunt.", "en": "All the actions which follow from the affects which are related to the mind insofar as it understands I relate to strength of character, which I divide into tenacity and nobility. For by tenacity I understand the desire by which each strives, from the dictate of reason alone, to preserve his being. By nobility, however, I understand the desire by which each strives, from the dictate of reason alone, to aid other men and join them to himself in friendship. Those actions, therefore, which aim only at the agent’s advantage I relate to tenacity, and those which aim also at the advantage of another I relate to nobility. Temperance, therefore, sobriety, presence of mind in dangers, etc., are species of tenacity; but modesty, clemency, etc., are species of nobility."}, {"id": "E3P59S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P59S", "la": "Atque his puto me praecipuos affectus animique fluctuationes, quae ex compositione trium primitivorum affectuum, nempe cupiditatis, laetitiae et tristitiae oriuntur, explicuisse perque primas suas causas ostendisse. Ex quibus apparet, nos a causis externis multis modis agitari, nosque perinde ut maris undae a contrariis ventis agitatae, fluctuari nostri eventus atque fati inscios. At dixi, me praecipuos tantum, non omnes, qui dari possunt, animi conflictus ostendisse. Nam eadem via, qua supra, procedendo facile possumus ostendere amorem esse iunctum poenitentiae, dedignationi, pudori etc. Imo unicuique ex iam dictis clare constare credo, affectus tot modis, alii cum aliis, posse componi, indeque tot variationes oriri, ut nullo numero definiri queant. Sed ad meum institutum praecipuos tantum enumeravisse sufficit; nam reliqui, quos omisi, plus curiositatis, quam utilitatis haberent.", "en": "And with this I think I have explained, and shown through their first causes, the principal affects and fluctuations of mind which arise from the composition of the three primary affects, namely desire, joy and sadness. From this it is evident that we are driven about in many ways by external causes, and that, like waves of the sea driven by contrary winds, we fluctuate, ignorant of our outcome and fate. But I have said that I have shown only the principal conflicts of mind, not all that there can be. For by proceeding in the same way as above we can easily show that love is joined to repentance, disdain, shame, etc. Indeed, I believe it is clearly established for everyone from what has already been said that the affects can be compounded with one another in so many ways, and that so many variations arise from this, that they cannot be determined by any number. But for my purpose it is enough to have enumerated only the principal ones; for the rest, which I have omitted, would be more a matter of curiosity than of use."}, {"id": "E3P59S", "p": "part3", "l": "3P59S", "la": "Attamen de amore hoc notandum restat, quod scilicet saepissime contingit, dum re, quam appetebamus, fruimur, ut corpus ex ea fruitione novam acquirat constitutionem, a qua aliter determinatur, et aliae rerum imagines in eo excitantur, et simul mens alia imaginari aliaque cupere incipit. Ex. gr. cum aliquid, quod nos sapore delectare solet, imaginamur, eodem frui, nempe comedere cupimus. At quamdiu eodem sic fruimur, stomachus adimpletur corpusque aliter constituitur. Si igitur corpore iam aliter disposito eiusdem cibi imago, quia ipse praesens adest, fomentetur, et consequenter conatus etiam sive cupiditas eundem comedendi huic cupiditati seu conatui nova illa constitutio repugnabit, et consequenter cibi, quem appetebamus, praesentia odiosa erit, et hoc est quod *fastidium* et *taedium* vocamus. Ceterum corporis affectiones externas, quae in affectibus observantur, ut sunt tremor, livor, singultus, risus etc. neglexi quia ad solum corpus absque ulla ad mentem relatione referuntur. Denique de *affectuum definitionibus* quaedam notanda sunt, quas propterea hic ordine repetam, et quid in unaquaque observandum est, iisdem interponam.", "en": "Yet this remains to be noted about love: it very often happens that, while we are enjoying the thing we wanted, the body acquires from that enjoyment a new constitution, by which it is determined differently, and other images of things are aroused in it, and at the same time the mind begins to imagine other things and to desire other things. For example, when we imagine something which usually delights us with its flavour, we desire to enjoy it, namely to eat it. But while we are thus enjoying it, the stomach is filled and the body is constituted differently. If, therefore, now that the body is differently disposed, the image of the same food is fostered, because the food itself is present, and consequently also the striving, or desire, to eat it, this new constitution will be opposed to this desire, or striving; and consequently the presence of the food we wanted will be hateful; and this is what we call disgust and weariness. As for the external affections of the body which are observed in the affects, such as trembling, pallor, sobbing, laughter, etc., I have neglected them, because they are related to the body alone, without any relation to the mind. Finally, some things must be noted about the definitions of the affects, which I shall therefore repeat here in order, inserting among them what is to be observed in each."}, {"id": "E3DA1", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA1", "la": "*Cupiditas* est ipsa hominis essentia, quatenus ex data quacumque eius affectione determinata concipitur ad aliquid agendum.", "en": "Desire is the very essence of man, insofar as it is conceived to be determined, from any given affection of it, to do something."}, {"id": "E3DA1Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA1Ex", "la": "Diximus supra in schol. prop. 9. huius partis, *cupiditatem* esse appetitum cum eiusdem conscientia; appetitum autem esse ipsam hominis essentiam, quatenus determinata est ad ea agendum, quae ipsius conservationi inserviunt. Sed in eodem schol. etiam monui, me revera inter humanum appetitum et cupiditatem nullam agnoscere differentiam. Nam sive homo sui appetitus sit conscius, sive non sit, manet tamen appetitus unus idemque; atque adeo ne tautologiam committere viderer, cupiditatem per appetitum explicare nolui, sed eandem ita definire studui, ut omnes humanae naturae conatus, quos nomine appetitus, voluntatis, cupiditatis vel impetus significamus, una comprehenderem. Potueram enim dicere, cupiditatem esse ipsam hominis essentiam, quatenus determinata concipitur ad aliquid agendum; sed ex hac definitione (per prop. 23. P. 2.) non sequeretur, quod mens possit suae cupiditatis sive appetitus esse conscia. Igitur ut huius conscientiae causam involverem, necesse fuit (per eandem prop.) addere “quatenus ex data quacumque eius affectione determinata etc.”.", "en": "We said above, in the Schol. of Prop. 9 of this Part, that desire is appetite together with consciousness of it; and that appetite is the very essence of man, insofar as it is determined to do those things which serve his preservation. But in the same Scholium I also pointed out that in truth I acknowledge no difference between human appetite and desire. For whether a man is conscious of his appetite or not, the appetite remains one and the same; and so, lest I should seem to commit a tautology, I did not want to explain desire by appetite, but took care to define it so as to comprehend together all the strivings of human nature which we signify by the name of appetite, will, desire or impulse. For I could have said that desire is the very essence of man insofar as it is conceived to be determined to do something; but from this definition (by Prop. 23 of Part II) it would not follow that the mind can be conscious of its desire, or appetite. Therefore, in order to involve the cause of this consciousness, it was necessary (by the same Proposition) to add ‘insofar as it is conceived to be determined, from any given affection of it, etc.’."}, {"id": "E3DA1Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA1Ex", "la": "Nam per affectionem humanae essentiae quamcumque eiusdem essentiae constitutionem intelligimus, sive ea sit innata, sive quod ipsa per solum cogitationis, sive per solum extensionis attributum concipiatur, sive denique quod ad utrumque simul referatur. Hic igitur cupiditatis nomine intelligo hominis quoscumque conatus, impetus, appetitus et volitiones, qui pro varia eiusdem hominis constitutione varii et non raro adeo sibi invicem oppositi sunt, ut homo diversimode trahatur et quo se vertat, nesciat.", "en": "For by an affection of the human essence we understand any constitution of that essence, whether it is innate, or whether it is conceived through the attribute of thought alone, or through the attribute of extension alone, or finally whether it is related to both at once. Here, therefore, by the name of desire I understand any strivings, impulses, appetites and volitions of a man, which vary according to the varying constitution of the same man, and are not seldom so opposed to one another that the man is pulled in different directions and does not know where to turn."}, {"id": "E3DA2", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA2", "la": "*Laetitia* est hominis transitio a minore ad maiorem perfectionem.", "en": "Joy is a man’s passage from a lesser to a greater perfection."}, {"id": "E3DA3", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA3", "la": "*Tristitia* est hominis transitio a maiore ad minorem perfectionem.", "en": "Sadness is a man’s passage from a greater to a lesser perfection."}, {"id": "E3DA3Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA3Ex", "la": "Dico transitionem. Nam laetitia non est ipsa perfectio. Si enim homo cum perfectione, ad quam transit, nasceretur, eiusdem absque laetitiae affectu compos esset; quod clarius apparet ex tristitiae affectu, qui huic est contrarius. Nam quod tristitia in transitione ad minorem perfectionem consistit, non autem in ipsa minore perfectione, nemo negare potest, quandoquidem homo eatenus contristari nequit, quatenus alicuius perfectionis est particeps. Nec dicere possumus, quod tristitia in privatione maioris perfectionis consistat; nam privatio nihil est. Tristitiae autem affectus actus est, qui propterea nullus alius esse potest, quam actus transeundi ad minorem perfectionem, hoc est, actus quo hominis agendi potentia minuitur vel coercetur. Vide schol. prop. 11. huius. Ceterum definitiones *hilaritatis, titillationis, melancholiae* et *doloris* omitto, quia ad corpus potissimum referuntur et non nisi laetitiae aut tristitiae sunt species.", "en": "I say ‘passage’. For joy is not perfection itself. For if a man were born with the perfection to which he passes, he would possess it without the affect of joy; which appears more clearly from the affect of sadness, which is contrary to it. For no one can deny that sadness consists in the passage to a lesser perfection, not in the lesser perfection itself, since a man cannot be saddened insofar as he participates in some perfection. Nor can we say that sadness consists in the privation of a greater perfection; for privation is nothing. But the affect of sadness is an act, which therefore can be no other than the act of passing to a lesser perfection, that is, the act by which a man’s power of acting is diminished or restrained. See the Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part. For the rest, I omit the definitions of cheerfulness, pleasure, melancholy and pain, because they are related chiefly to the body, and are only species of joy or sadness."}, {"id": "E3DA4", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA4", "la": "*Admiratio* est rei alicuius imaginatio, in qua mens defixa propterea manet, quia haec singularis imaginatio nullam cum reliquis habet connexionem. Vide prop. 52. cum eiusdem schol.", "en": "Wonder is an imagination of some thing in which the mind remains fixed because this singular imagination has no connection with the others. See Prop. 52 with its Scholium."}, {"id": "E3DA4Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA4Ex", "la": "In schol. prop. 18. P. 2. ostendimus, quaenam sit causa, cur mens ex contemplatione unius rei statim in alterius rei cogitationem incidat, videlicet, quia earum rerum imagines invicem concatenatae et ita ordinatae sunt, ut alia aliam sequatur, quod quidem concipi nequit, quando rei imago nova est; sed mens in eiusdem rei contemplatione detinebitur, donec ab aliis causis ad alia cogitandum determinetur. Rei itaque novae imaginatio in se considerata eiusdem naturae est, ac reliquae, et hac de causa ego admirationem inter affectus non numero, nec causam video, cur id facerem, quandoquidem haec mentis distractio ex nulla causa positiva, quae mentem ab aliis distrahat, oritur, sed tantum ex eo, quod causa, cur mens ex unius rei contemplatione ad alia cogitandum determinatur, deficit. *Tres* igitur (ut in schol. prop. 11. huius monui) tantum *affectus primitivos* seu primarios agnosco, nempe *laetitiae, tristitiae* et *cupiditatis*, nec alia de causa verba de admiratione feci, quam quia usu factum est ut quidam affectus, qui ex tribus primitivis derivantur, aliis nominibus indicari soleant quando ad obiecta, quae admiramur, referuntur; quae quidem ratio me ex aequo movet, ut etiam contemptus definitionem his adiungam.", "en": "In the Schol. of Prop. 18 of Part II we have shown what the cause is why the mind, from the contemplation of one thing, immediately falls into the thought of another: namely, because the images of those things are linked together and so ordered that one follows another; which cannot be conceived when the image of the thing is new; but the mind will be held in the contemplation of that thing until it is determined by other causes to think of other things. The imagination of a new thing, therefore, considered in itself, is of the same nature as the others; and for this reason I do not number wonder among the affects, nor do I see a cause why I should, since this distraction of the mind does not arise from any positive cause which distracts the mind from other things, but only from the fact that the cause is lacking by which the mind is determined, from the contemplation of one thing, to think of others. I acknowledge, therefore (as I pointed out in the Schol. of Prop. 11 of this Part), only three primitive, or primary, affects, namely those of joy, sadness and desire; nor have I spoken of wonder for any other reason than that it has become customary to indicate certain affects which are derived from the three primitive ones by other names when they are related to objects we wonder at; which reason equally moves me to add to these the definition of contempt as well."}, {"id": "E3DA5", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA5", "la": "*Contemptus* est rei alicuius imaginatio, quae mentem adeo parum tangit, ut ipsa mens ex rei praesentia magis moveatur ad ea imaginandum, quae in ipsa re non sunt, quam quae in ipsa sunt. Vide schol. prop. 52. huius.", "en": "Contempt is an imagination of some thing which touches the mind so little that the mind itself is moved by the presence of the thing to imagine what is not in the thing rather than what is in it. See the Schol. of Prop. 52 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3DA5.2", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA5", "la": "Definitiones *venerationis* et *dedignationis* missas hic facio, quia nulli, quod sciam, affectus ex his nomen trahunt.", "en": "I pass over here the definitions of veneration and disdain, because no affects, so far as I know, take their name from them."}, {"id": "E3DA6", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA6", "la": "*Amor* est laetitia concomitante idea causae externae.", "en": "Love is joy accompanied by the idea of an external cause."}, {"id": "E3DA6Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA6Ex", "la": "Haec definitio satis clare amoris essentiam explicat. Illa vero auctorum, qui definiunt amorem esse voluntatem amantis se iungendi rei amatae, non amoris essentiam, sed eius proprietatem exprimit; et quia amoris essentia non satis ab auctoribus perspecta fuit, ideo neque eius proprietatis ullum clarum conceptum habere potuerunt, et hinc factum, ut eorum definitionem admodum obscuram esse omnes iudicaverint. Verum notandum, cum dico, proprietatem esse in amante, se voluntate iungere rei amatae, me per voluntatem non intelligere consensum vel animi deliberationem seu liberum decretum (nam hoc fictitium esse demonstravimus prop. 48. P. 2.), nec etiam cupiditatem sese iungendi rei amatae, quando abest, vel perseverandi in ipsius praesentia quando adest; potest namque amor absque hac aut illa cupiditate concipi; sed per voluntatem me acquiescentiam intelligere, quae est in amante ob rei amatae praesentiam, a qua laetitia amantis corroboratur aut saltem fovetur.", "en": "This definition explains the essence of love clearly enough. But that of the authors who define love as the will of the lover to join himself to the thing loved expresses not the essence of love but a property of it; and because the essence of love was not sufficiently perceived by these authors, they could not have any clear concept of its property either, and hence it has come about that everyone has judged their definition to be very obscure. But it should be noted that when I say that it is a property in the lover that he joins himself by will to the thing loved, I do not understand by will a consent, or deliberation of the mind, or free decree (for we have demonstrated in Prop. 48 of Part II that this is a fiction), nor even a desire to join oneself to the thing loved when it is absent, or to persevere in its presence when it is present; for love can be conceived without the one or the other of these desires; but by will I understand the contentment which is in the lover on account of the presence of the thing loved, by which the joy of the lover is strengthened, or at least fostered."}, {"id": "E3DA7", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA7", "la": "*Odium* est tristitia concomitante idea causae externae.", "en": "Hatred is sadness accompanied by the idea of an external cause."}, {"id": "E3DA7Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA7Ex", "la": "Quae hic notanda sunt, ex dictis in praecedentis definitionis explicatione facile percipiuntur. Vide praeterea schol. prop. 13. huius.", "en": "What is to be noted here is easily perceived from what was said in the Explanation of the preceding Definition. See, besides, the Schol. of Prop. 13 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3DA8", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA8", "la": "*Propensio* est laetitia concomitante idea alicuius rei, quae per accidens causa est laetitiae.", "en": "Inclination is joy accompanied by the idea of some thing which is accidentally the cause of joy."}, {"id": "E3DA9", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA9", "la": "*Aversio* est tristitia concomitante idea alicuius rei, quae per accidens causa est tristitiae. De his vide schol. prop. 15. huius.", "en": "Aversion is sadness accompanied by the idea of some thing which is accidentally the cause of sadness. On these see the Schol. of Prop. 15 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3DA10", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA10", "la": "*Devotio* est amor erga eum, quem admiramur.", "en": "Devotion is love towards one whom we wonder at."}, {"id": "E3DA10Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA10Ex", "la": "Admirationem oriri ex rei novitate, ostendimus prop. 52. huius. Si igitur contingat, ut id quod admiramur saepe imaginemur, idem admirari desinemus; atque adeo videmus, devotionis affectum facile in simplicem amorem degenerare.", "en": "We have shown in Prop. 52 of this Part that wonder arises from the novelty of a thing. If, therefore, it happens that we often imagine what we wonder at, we shall cease to wonder at it; and so we see that the affect of devotion easily degenerates into simple love."}, {"id": "E3DA11", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA11", "la": "*Irrisio* est laetitia orta ex eo, quod aliquid, quod contemnimus in re, quam odimus, inesse imaginamur.", "en": "Mockery is joy arising from our imagining that something we despise is in a thing we hate."}, {"id": "E3DA11Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA11Ex", "la": "Quatenus rem, quam odimus, contemnimus, eatenus de eadem existentiam negamus (vide schol. prop. 52. huius), et eatenus (per prop. 20. huius) laetamur. Sed quoniam supponimus, hominem id quod irridet, odio tamen habere, sequitur, hanc laetitiam solidam non esse. Vide schol. prop. 47. huius.", "en": "Insofar as we despise a thing we hate, we deny existence of it (see the Schol. of Prop. 52 of this Part), and to that extent (by Prop. 20 of this Part) we rejoice. But since we suppose that a man nevertheless hates what he mocks, it follows that this joy is not solid. See the Schol. of Prop. 47 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3DA12", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA12", "la": "*Spes* est inconstans laetitia orta ex idea rei futurae vel praeteritae, de cuius eventu aliquatenus dubitamus.", "en": "Hope is an inconstant joy arising from the idea of a future or past thing of whose outcome we are to some extent in doubt."}, {"id": "E3DA13", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA13", "la": "*Metus* est inconstans tristitia orta ex idea rei futurae vel praeteritae, de cuius eventu aliquatenus dubitamus. Vide de his schol. 2. prop. 18. huius.", "en": "Fear is an inconstant sadness arising from the idea of a future or past thing of whose outcome we are to some extent in doubt. See on these the Schol. 2 of Prop. 18 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3DA13Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA13Ex", "la": "Ex his definitionibus sequitur, non dari spem sine metu, neque metum sine spe. Qui enim spe pendet et de rei eventu dubitat, is aliquid imaginari supponitur, quod rei futurae existentiam secludit; atque adeo eatenus contristari (per prop. 19. huius) et consequenter, dum spe pendet, metuere, ut res eveniat. Qui autem contra in metu est, hoc est, de rei quam odit eventu dubitat, aliquid etiam imaginatur, quod eiusdem rei existentiam secludit; atque adeo (per prop. 20. huius) laetatur, et consequenter eatenus spem habet, ne eveniat.", "en": "From these definitions it follows that there is no hope without fear, nor fear without hope. For he who hangs on hope and is in doubt about the outcome of a thing is supposed to imagine something which excludes the existence of the future thing; and so to that extent to be saddened (by Prop. 19 of this Part), and consequently, while he hangs on hope, to fear that the thing will not happen. But he who, on the other hand, is in fear, that is, is in doubt about the outcome of a thing he hates, also imagines something which excludes the existence of that thing; and so (by Prop. 20 of this Part) he rejoices, and consequently to that extent has hope that it will not happen."}, {"id": "E3DA14", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA14", "la": "*Securitas* est laetitia orta ex idea rei futurae vel praeteritae, de qua dubitandi causa sublata est.", "en": "Confidence is joy arising from the idea of a future or past thing about which the cause of doubting has been removed."}, {"id": "E3DA15", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA15", "la": "*Desperatio* est tristitia orta ex idea rei futurae vel praeteritae, de qua dubitandi causa sublata est.", "en": "Despair is sadness arising from the idea of a future or past thing about which the cause of doubting has been removed."}, {"id": "E3DA15Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA15Ex", "la": "Oritur itaque ex spe securitas et ex metu desperatio, quando de rei eventu dubitandi causa tollitur, quod fit, quia homo rem praeteritam vel futuram adesse imaginatur et ut praesentem contemplatur; vel quia alia imaginatur, quae existentiam earum rerum secludunt, quae ipsi dubium iniiciebant. Nam tametsi de rerum singularium eventu (per coroll. prop. 31. P. 2.) nunquam possumus esse certi; fieri tamen potest, ut de earum eventu non dubitemus. Aliud enim esse ostendimus (vide schol. prop. 49. P. 2.) de re non dubitare, aliud rei certitudinem habere; atque adeo fieri potest, ut ex imagine rei praeteritae aut futurae eodem laetitiae vel tristitiae affectu afficiamur ac ex rei praesentis imagine, ut in prop. 18 huius demonstravimus, quam cum eiusdem scholiis vide.", "en": "Confidence, therefore, arises from hope, and despair from fear, when the cause of doubting about the outcome of a thing is removed; which happens because a man imagines that the past or future thing is there and contemplates it as present; or because he imagines other things which exclude the existence of those things which were causing him to doubt. For although we can never be certain about the outcome of singular things (by Cor. of Prop. 31 of Part II), it can nevertheless happen that we do not doubt about their outcome. For we have shown (see the Schol. of Prop. 49 of Part II) that it is one thing not to doubt about a thing, another to have certainty of it; and so it can happen that we are affected by the image of a past or future thing with the same affect of joy or sadness as by the image of a present thing, as we have demonstrated in Prop. 18 of this Part, which see together with its Scholia."}, {"id": "E3DA16", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA16", "la": "*Gaudium* est laetitia concomitante idea rei praeteritae, quae praeter spem evenit.", "en": "Gladness is joy accompanied by the idea of a past thing which has turned out contrary to expectation."}, {"id": "E3DA17", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA17", "la": "*Conscientiae morsus* est tristitia concomitante idea rei praeteritae, quae praeter spem evenit.", "en": "Remorse is sadness accompanied by the idea of a past thing which has turned out contrary to expectation."}, {"id": "E3DA18", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA18", "la": "*Commiseratio* est tristitia concomitante idea mali, quod alteri, quem nobis similem esse imaginamur, evenit. Vide schol. prop. 22. et schol. prop. 27. huius.", "en": "Pity is sadness accompanied by the idea of an evil which has happened to another whom we imagine to be like us. See the Schol. of Prop. 22 and the Schol. of Prop. 27 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3DA18Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA18Ex", "la": "Inter commiserationem et misericordiam nulla videtur esse differentia, nisi forte, quod commiseratio singularem affectum respiciat, misericordia autem eius habitum.", "en": "Between pity and compassion there seems to be no difference, except perhaps that pity regards the single affect, compassion the habit of it."}, {"id": "E3DA19", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA19", "la": "*Favor* est amor erga aliquem, qui alteri benefecit.", "en": "Favour is love towards someone who has done good to another."}, {"id": "E3DA20", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA20", "la": "*Indignatio* est odium erga aliquem, qui alteri malefecit.", "en": "Indignation is hatred towards someone who has done evil to another."}, {"id": "E3DA20Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA20Ex", "la": "Haec nomina ex communi usu aliud significare scio. Sed meum institutum non est, verborum significationem, sed rerum naturam explicare, easque iis vocabulis indicare, quorum significatio, quam ex usu habent, a significatione, qua eadem usurpare volo, non omnino abhorret, quod semel monuisse sufficiat. Ceterum horum affectuum causam vide in coroll. 1. prop. 27. et schol. prop. 22. huius.", "en": "I know that these names mean something else in common usage. But my purpose is to explain not the meaning of words but the nature of things, and to indicate them by words whose meaning from usage is not wholly at odds with the meaning in which I wish to use them; which it is enough to have pointed out once. As for the cause of these affects, see Cor. 1 of Prop. 27 and the Schol. of Prop. 22 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3DA21", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA21", "la": "*Existimatio* est de aliquo prae amore plus iusto sentire.", "en": "Overestimation is thinking more highly of someone than is just, out of love."}, {"id": "E3DA22", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA22", "la": "*Despectus* est de aliquo prae odio minus iusto sentire.", "en": "Scorn is thinking less highly of someone than is just, out of hatred."}, {"id": "E3DA22Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA22Ex", "la": "Est itaque existimatio, amoris et despectus odii effectus sive proprietas; atque adeo potest *existimatio* etiam definiri, quod sit amor, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut de re amata plus iusto sentiat, et contra *despectus*, quod sit odium, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut de eo quem odio habet, minus iusto sentiat. Vide de his schol. prop. 26. huius.", "en": "Overestimation, therefore, is an effect, or property, of love, and scorn of hatred; and so overestimation can also be defined as love insofar as it so affects a man that he thinks more highly of the thing loved than is just; and, on the other hand, scorn as hatred insofar as it so affects a man that he thinks less highly than is just of the one he hates. See on these the Schol. of Prop. 26 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3DA23", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA23", "la": "*Invidia* est odium, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut ex alterius felicitate contristetur, et contra ut ex alterius malo gaudeat.", "en": "Envy is hatred insofar as it so affects a man that he is saddened by another’s happiness and, on the other hand, rejoices in another’s ill."}, {"id": "E3DA23Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA23Ex", "la": "Invidiae opponitur communiter misericordia, quae proinde invita vocabuli significatione sic definiri potest:", "en": "To envy is commonly opposed compassion, which can therefore, against the meaning of the word, be defined thus:"}, {"id": "E3DA24", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA24", "la": "*Misericordia* est amor, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut ex bono alterius gaudeat, et contra ut ex alterius malo contristetur.", "en": "Compassion is love insofar as it so affects a man that he rejoices in another’s good and, on the other hand, is saddened by another’s ill."}, {"id": "E3DA24Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA24Ex", "la": "Ceterum de invidia vide schol. prop. 24. et schol. prop. 32. huius. Atque hi affectus laetitiae et tristitiae sunt, quos idea rei externae comitatur tamquam causa per se vel per accidens. Hinc ad alios transeo, quos idea rei internae comitatur tamquam causa.", "en": "As for envy, see the Schol. of Prop. 24 and the Schol. of Prop. 32 of this Part. And these are the affects of joy and sadness which are accompanied by the idea of an external thing as cause, through itself or accidentally. From here I pass to the others, which are accompanied by the idea of an internal thing as cause."}, {"id": "E3DA25", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA25", "la": "*Acquiescentia in se ipso* est laetitia orta ex eo, quod homo se ipsum suamque agendi potentiam contemplatur.", "en": "Self-contentment is joy arising from a man’s contemplating himself and his power of acting."}, {"id": "E3DA26", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA26", "la": "*Humilitas* est tristitia orta ex eo, quod homo suam impotentiam sive imbecillitatem contemplatur.", "en": "Humility is sadness arising from a man’s contemplating his lack of power, or weakness."}, {"id": "E3DA26Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA26Ex", "la": "Acquiescentia in se ipso humilitati opponitur, quatenus per eandem intelligimus laetitiam, quae ex eo oritur, quod nostram agendi potentiam contemplamur. Sed quatenus per ipsam etiam intelligimus laetitiam concomitante idea alicuius facti, quod nos ex mentis libero decreto fecisse credimus, tum poenitentiae opponitur, quae a nobis sic definitur:", "en": "Self-contentment is opposed to humility insofar as we understand by it the joy which arises from our contemplating our power of acting. But insofar as we also understand by it joy accompanied by the idea of some deed which we believe we have done from a free decree of the mind, it is opposed to repentance, which is defined by us thus:"}, {"id": "E3DA27", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA27", "la": "*Poenitentia est tristitia concomitante idea alicuius facti, quod nos ex libero mentis decreto fecisse credimus.*", "en": "Repentance is sadness accompanied by the idea of some deed which we believe we have done from a free decree of the mind."}, {"id": "E3DA27Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA27Ex", "la": "Horum affectuum causas ostendimus in schol. prop. 51. huius et prop. 53., 54. et 55. huius eiusque schol. De libero autem mentis decreto vide schol. prop. 35. P. 2. Sed hic praeterea notandum venit, mirum non esse, quod omnes omnino actus, qui ex consuetudine *pravi* vocantur, sequatur tristitia, et illos, qui *recti* dicuntur, laetitia. Nam hoc ab educatione potissimum pendere, facile ex supra dictis intelligimus. Parentes nimirum illos exprobrando liberosque propter eosdem saepe obiurgando, hos contra suadendo et laudando effecerunt, ut tristitiae commotiones illis, laetitiae vero his iungerentur. Quod ipsa etiam experientia comprobatur. Nam consuetudo et religio non est omnibus eadem; sed contra, quae apud alios sacra, apud alios profana, et quae apud alios honesta, apud alios turpia sunt. Prout igitur unusquisque educatus est, ita facti alicuius poenitet vel eodem gloriatur.", "en": "We have shown the causes of these affects in the Schol. of Prop. 51 of this Part and in Props. 53, 54 and 55 of this Part and its Scholium. On the free decree of the mind, see the Schol. of Prop. 35 of Part II. But here it should be noted besides that it is not surprising that all acts whatever which are by custom called wrong are followed by sadness, and those which are called right by joy. For we easily understand from what has been said above that this depends chiefly on education. Parents, namely, by reproaching the former and often scolding their children for them, and on the other hand by recommending and praising the latter, have brought it about that commotions of sadness were joined to the former and of joy to the latter. This is also confirmed by experience itself. For custom and religion are not the same for all; on the contrary, what is sacred among some is profane among others, and what is honourable among some is base among others. According, therefore, as each has been educated, so he repents of a deed or glories in it."}, {"id": "E3DA28", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA28", "la": "*Superbia* est de se prae amore sui plus iusto sentire.", "en": "Pride is thinking more highly of oneself than is just, out of self-love."}, {"id": "E3DA28Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA28Ex", "la": "Differt igitur superbia ab existimatione, quod haec ad obiectum externum, superbia autem ad ipsum hominem de se plus iusto sentientem referatur. Ceterum ut existimatio amoris sic *superbia* philautiae effectus vel proprietas est, quae propterea etiam definiri potest, quod sit amor sui sive acquiescentia in se ipso, quatenus hominem ita afficit, ut de se plus iusto sentiat. Vide schol. prop. 26. huius. Huic affectui non datur contrarius. Nam nemo de se prae odio sui minus iusto sentit; imo nemo de se minus iusto sentit, quatenus imaginatur, se hoc vel illud non posse. Nam quicquid homo imaginatur se non posse, id necessario imaginatur, et hac imaginatione ita disponitur, ut id agere revera non possit, quod se non posse imaginatur. Quamdiu enim imaginatur se hoc vel illud non posse, tamdiu ad agendum non est determinatus; et consequenter tamdiu impossibile ei est, ut id agat.", "en": "Pride, therefore, differs from overestimation in that the latter is related to an external object, while pride is related to the man himself, who thinks more highly of himself than is just. For the rest, as overestimation is an effect or property of love, so pride is an effect or property of self-love, and can therefore also be defined as love of oneself, or self-contentment, insofar as it so affects a man that he thinks more highly of himself than is just. See the Schol. of Prop. 26 of this Part. There is no contrary to this affect. For no one thinks less highly of himself than is just out of hatred of himself; indeed, no one thinks less highly of himself than is just insofar as he imagines that he cannot do this or that. For whatever a man imagines he cannot do, he imagines necessarily, and by this imagination he is so disposed that he really cannot do what he imagines he cannot do. For as long as he imagines that he cannot do this or that, so long is he not determined to do it; and consequently so long is it impossible for him to do it."}, {"id": "E3DA28Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA28Ex", "la": "Verumenimvero si ad illa attendamus, quae a sola opinione pendent, concipere poterimus fieri posse, ut homo de se minus iusto sentiat. Fieri enim potest, ut aliquis, dum tristis imbecillitatem contemplatur suam, imaginetur, se ab omnibus contemni; idque, dum reliqui nihil minus cogitant, quam ipsum contemnere. Potest praeterea homo de se minus iusto sentire, si aliquid de se in praesenti neget cum relatione ad futurum tempus, cuius est incertus; ut quod neget, se nihil certi posse concipere nihilque nisi prava vel turpia posse cupere vel agere etc. Possumus deinde dicere, aliquem de se minus iusto sentire, cum videmus, ipsum ex nimio pudoris metu ea non audere, quae alii ipsi aequales audent. Hunc igitur affectum possumus superbiae opponere, quem *abiectionem* vocabo. Nam ut ex acquiescentia in se ipso superbia, sic ex humilitate abiectio oritur; quae proinde a nobis sic definitur:", "en": "But if we attend to those things which depend on opinion alone, we shall be able to conceive that it can happen that a man thinks less highly of himself than is just. For it can happen that someone, while he sadly contemplates his weakness, imagines that he is despised by all, and that while the others think of nothing less than of despising him. Moreover, a man can think less highly of himself than is just if in the present he denies something of himself with relation to a future time of which he is uncertain; as if he denies that he can conceive anything certain, or that he can desire or do anything but what is wrong or base, etc. We can say, next, that someone thinks less highly of himself than is just when we see that, from an excessive fear of shame, he does not dare the things which others equal to him dare. This affect, then, we can oppose to pride; I shall call it self-abasement. For as pride arises from self-contentment, so self-abasement arises from humility; and it is therefore defined by us thus:"}, {"id": "E3DA29", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA29", "la": "*Abiectio* est de se prae tristitia minus iusto sentire.", "en": "Self-abasement is thinking less highly of oneself than is just, out of sadness."}, {"id": "E3DA29Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA29Ex", "la": "Solemus tamen saepe superbiae humilitatem opponere; sed tum magis ad utriusque effectus, quam naturam attendimus. Solemus namque illum *superbum* vocare, qui nimis gloriatur (vide schol. prop. 30. huius), qui non nisi virtutes suas et aliorum non nisi vitia narrat, qui omnibus praeferri vult, et qui denique ea gravitate et ornatu incedit, quo solent alii, qui longe supra ipsum sunt positi. Contra illum *humilem* vocamus, qui saepius erubescit, qui sua vitia fatetur et aliorum virtutes narrat, qui omnibus cedit, et qui denique submisso capite ambulat et se ornare negligit. Ceterum hi affectus, nempe humilitas et abiectio, rarissimi sunt. Nam natura humana in se considerata contra eosdem, quantum potest nititur (vide prop. 13. et 54. huius); et ideo qui maxime creduntur abiecti et humiles esse, maxime plerumque ambitiosi et invidi sunt.", "en": "We are nevertheless accustomed often to oppose humility to pride; but then we attend more to the effects of the two than to their nature. For we usually call him proud who glories too much (see the Schol. of Prop. 30 of this Part), who tells of nothing but his own virtues and others’ vices, who wants to be preferred to all, and who, finally, goes about with the gravity and finery usual for others who are placed far above him. On the other hand, we call him humble who blushes rather often, who confesses his own vices and tells of others’ virtues, who yields to all, and who, finally, walks with head bowed and neglects to adorn himself. For the rest, these affects, namely humility and self-abasement, are very rare. For human nature, considered in itself, strives against them as much as it can (see Props. 13 and 54 of this Part); and so those who are most believed to be self-abased and humble are generally most ambitious and envious."}, {"id": "E3DA30", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA30", "la": "*Gloria* est laetitia concomitante idea alicuius nostrae actionis, quam alios laudare imaginamur.", "en": "Glory is joy accompanied by the idea of some action of ours which we imagine others to praise."}, {"id": "E3DA31", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA31", "la": "*Pudor* est tristitia concomitante idea alicuius actionis, quam alios vituperare imaginamur.", "en": "Shame is sadness accompanied by the idea of some action which we imagine others to blame."}, {"id": "E3DA31Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA31Ex", "la": "De his vide schol. prop. 30. huius. Sed hic notanda est differentia, quae est inter pudorem et verecundiam. Est enim *pudor* tristitia, quae sequitur factum cuius pudet; *verecundia* autem est metus seu timor pudoris, quo homo continetur, ne aliquid turpe committat. Verecundiae opponi solet *impudentia*, quae revera affectus non est, ut suo loco ostendam; sed affectuum nomina (ut iam monui) magis eorum usum, quam naturam respiciunt. Atque his laetitiae et tristitiae affectus, quos explicare proposueram, absolvi. Pergo itaque ad illos, quos ad cupiditatem refero.", "en": "On these see the Schol. of Prop. 30 of this Part. But here the difference between shame and shamefacedness should be noted. For shame is the sadness which follows a deed of which one is ashamed; shamefacedness, however, is the fear, or timidity, of shame, by which a man is held back from committing something base. To shamefacedness shamelessness is usually opposed, which in truth is not an affect, as I shall show in its place; but the names of the affects (as I have already pointed out) regard their usage more than their nature. And with these I have completed the affects of joy and sadness which I had proposed to explain. I proceed, therefore, to those which I relate to desire."}, {"id": "E3DA32", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA32", "la": "*Desiderium* est cupiditas sive appetitus re aliqua potiundi, quae eiusdem rei memoria fovetur, et simul aliarum rerum memoria, quae eiusdem rei appetendae existentiam secludunt, coercetur.", "en": "Longing is the desire, or appetite, to possess some thing, which is fostered by the memory of that thing and at the same time restrained by the memory of other things which exclude the existence of the thing wanted."}, {"id": "E3DA32Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA32Ex", "la": "Cum alicuius rei recordamur, ut iam saepe diximus, eo ipso disponimur ad eandem eodem affectu contemplandum, ac si res praesens adesset; sed haec dispositio seu conatus, dum vigilamus, plerumque cohibetur ab imaginibus rerum, quae existentiam eius, cuius recordamur, secludunt. Quando itaque rei meminimus, quae nos aliquo laetitiae genere afficit, eo ipso conamur eandem cum eodem laetitiae affectu ut praesentem contemplari; qui quidem conatus statim cohibetur memoria rerum, quae illius existentiam secludunt. Quare desiderium revera tristitia est, quae laetitiae opponitur illi, quae ex absentia rei, quam odimus, oritur, de qua vide schol. prop. 47. huius. Sed quia nomen desiderium cupiditatem respicere videtur, ideo hunc affectum ad cupiditatis affectus refero.", "en": "When we recollect a thing, as we have often said, we are thereby disposed to contemplate it with the same affect as if the thing were present; but this disposition, or striving, while we are awake, is generally checked by the images of things which exclude the existence of what we recollect. When, therefore, we remember a thing which affects us with some kind of joy, we thereby strive to contemplate it as present with the same affect of joy; and this striving is immediately checked by the memory of things which exclude its existence. Longing, therefore, is in truth a sadness, which is opposed to the joy which arises from the absence of a thing we hate, on which see the Schol. of Prop. 47 of this Part. But because the name desiderium seems to refer to desire, I relate this affect to the affects of desire."}, {"id": "E3DA33", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA33", "la": "*Aemulatio* est alicuius rei cupiditas, quae nobis ingeneratur ex eo, quod alios eandem cupiditatem habere imaginamur.", "en": "Emulation is the desire for some thing which is generated in us from the fact that we imagine others to have the same desire."}, {"id": "E3DA33Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA33Ex", "la": "Qui fugit, quia alios fugere, vel qui timet, quia alios timere videt, vel etiam ille, qui ex eo, quod aliquem manum suam combussisse videt, manum ad se contrahit corpusque movet, quasi ipsius manus combureretur, eum imitari quidem alterius affectum, sed non eundem aemulari dicemus; non quia aliam aemulationis, aliam imitationis novimus causam, sed quia usu factum est, ut illum tantum vocemus aemulum, qui id, quod honestum, utile vel iucundum esse iudicamus, imitatur. Ceterum de aemulationis causa vide prop. 27. huius cum eius schol. Cur autem huic affectui plerumque iuncta sit invidia, de eo vide prop. 32. huius cum eiusdem schol.", "en": "He who flees because he sees others flee, or who fears because he sees others fear, or even he who, because he sees that someone has burned his hand, draws back his own hand and moves his body as if his own hand were being burned, we shall say imitates the other’s affect, but not that he emulates it; not because we know one cause of emulation and another of imitation, but because it has become customary to call him only an emulator who imitates what we judge to be honourable, useful or pleasant. For the rest, on the cause of emulation see Prop. 27 of this Part with its Scholium. Why envy is generally joined to this affect, see Prop. 32 of this Part with its Scholium."}, {"id": "E3DA34", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA34", "la": "*Gratia* seu *gratitudo* est cupiditas seu amoris studium, quo ei benefacere conamur, qui in nos pari amoris affectu beneficium contulit. Vide prop. 39. cum schol. prop. 41. huius.", "en": "Thankfulness, or gratitude, is the desire, or eagerness of love, by which we strive to do good to one who has conferred a benefit on us from a like affect of love. See Prop. 39 with the Schol. of Prop. 41 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3DA35", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA35", "la": "*Benevolentia* est cupiditas benefaciendi ei, cuius nos miseret. Vide schol. prop. 27. huius.", "en": "Benevolence is the desire to do good to one we pity. See the Schol. of Prop. 27 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3DA36", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA36", "la": "*Ira* est cupiditas, qua ex odio incitamur ad illi quem odimus malum inferendum. Vide prop. 39. huius.", "en": "Anger is the desire by which we are spurred, from hatred, to inflict evil on one we hate. See Prop. 39 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3DA37", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA37", "la": "*Vindicta* est cupiditas, qua ex reciproco odio concitamur ad malum inferendum ei, qui nobis pari affectu damnum intulit. Vide coroll. 2. prop. 40. huius cum eiusdem schol.", "en": "Vengeance is the desire by which we are roused, from reciprocal hatred, to inflict evil on one who, from a like affect, has done us harm. See Cor. 2 of Prop. 40 of this Part with its Scholium."}, {"id": "E3DA38", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA38", "la": "*Crudelitas* seu *saevitia* est cupiditas, qua aliquis concitatur ad malum inferendum ei, quem amamus, vel cuius nos miseret.", "en": "Cruelty, or savagery, is the desire by which someone is roused to inflict evil on one whom we love or whom we pity."}, {"id": "E3DA38Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA38Ex", "la": "Crudelitati opponitur *clementia*, quae passio non est, sed animi potentia, qua homo iram et vindictam moderatur.", "en": "To cruelty is opposed clemency, which is not a passion, but a power of the mind by which a man moderates anger and vengeance."}, {"id": "E3DA39", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA39", "la": "*Timor* est cupiditas maius quod metuimus malum minore vitandi. Vide schol. prop. 39. huius.", "en": "Timidity is the desire to avoid a greater evil, which we fear, by a lesser one. See the Schol. of Prop. 39 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3DA40", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA40", "la": "*Audacia* est cupiditas, qua aliquis incitatur ad aliquid agendum cum periculo, quod eius aequales subire metuunt.", "en": "Daring is the desire by which someone is spurred to do something with a danger which his equals fear to face."}, {"id": "E3DA41", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA41", "la": "*Pusillanimitas* dicitur de eo, cuius cupiditas coercetur timore periculi, quod eius aequales subire audent.", "en": "Cowardice is said of one whose desire is restrained by the timidity of a danger which his equals dare to face."}, {"id": "E3DA41Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA41Ex", "la": "Est igitur pusillanimitas nihil aliud, quam metus alicuius mali quod plerique non solent metuere; quare ipsam ad cupiditatis affectus non refero. Eandem tamen hic explicare volui, quia quatenus ad cupiditatem attendimus, affectui audaciae revera opponitur.", "en": "Cowardice, therefore, is nothing but the fear of some evil which most men are not accustomed to fear; that is why I do not relate it to the affects of desire. But I wanted to explain it here because, insofar as we attend to desire, it is really opposed to the affect of daring."}, {"id": "E3DA42", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA42", "la": "*Consternatio* dicitur de eo, cuius cupiditas malum vitandi coercetur admiratione mali, quod timet.", "en": "Consternation is said of one whose desire to avoid an evil is restrained by wonder at the evil he fears."}, {"id": "E3DA42Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA42Ex", "la": "Est itaque consternatio pusillanimitatis species. Sed quia consternatio ex duplici timore oritur, ideo commodius definiri potest, quod sit metus, qui hominem stupefactum aut fluctuantem ita continet, ut is malum amovere non possit. Dico stupefactum, quatenus eius cupiditatem malum amovendi admiratione coerceri intelligimus. Fluctuantem autem dico, quatenus concipimus eandem cupiditatem coerceri timore alterius mali, quod ipsum aeque cruciat; unde fit ut quodnam ex duobus avertat, nesciat. De his vide schol. prop. 39. et schol. prop. 52. huius. Ceterum de pusillanimitate et audacia vide schol. prop. 51. huius.", "en": "Consternation, therefore, is a species of cowardice. But because consternation arises from a twofold timidity, it can more conveniently be defined as a fear which holds a man stupefied or fluctuating so that he cannot remove the evil. I say stupefied insofar as we understand his desire to remove the evil to be restrained by wonder. I say fluctuating, however, insofar as we conceive the same desire to be restrained by the timidity of another evil which torments him equally, so that he does not know which of the two to avert. On these see the Schol. of Prop. 39 and the Schol. of Prop. 52 of this Part. For the rest, on cowardice and daring see the Schol. of Prop. 51 of this Part."}, {"id": "E3DA43", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA43", "la": "*Humanitas* seu *modestia* est cupiditas ea faciendi quae hominibus placent, et omittendi quae displicent.", "en": "Courtesy, or modesty, is the desire to do what pleases men and to omit what displeases them."}, {"id": "E3DA44", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA44", "la": "*Ambitio* est immodica gloriae cupiditas.", "en": "Ambition is an immoderate desire for glory."}, {"id": "E3DA44Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA44Ex", "la": "Ambitio est cupiditas, qua omnes affectus (per prop. 27. et 31. huius) foventur et corroborantur; et ideo hic affectus vix superari potest. Nam quamdiu homo aliqua cupiditate tenetur, hac simul necessario tenetur. “Optimus quisque, inquit Cicero, maxime gloria ducitur. Philosophi etiam libris, quos de contemnenda gloria scribunt, nomen suum inscribunt” etc.", "en": "Ambition is a desire by which all the affects (by Props. 27 and 31 of this Part) are fostered and strengthened; and for this reason this affect can hardly be overcome. For as long as a man is held by any desire, he is necessarily held by this one at the same time. ‘Every best man’, says Cicero, ‘is led most by glory. Even the philosophers inscribe their names on the books which they write on despising glory’, etc."}, {"id": "E3DA45", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA45", "la": "*Luxuria* est immoderata convivandi cupiditas vel etiam amor.", "en": "Gluttony is an immoderate desire, or also love, of feasting."}, {"id": "E3DA46", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA46", "la": "*Ebrietas* est immoderata potandi cupiditas et amor.", "en": "Drunkenness is an immoderate desire and love of drinking."}, {"id": "E3DA47", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA47", "la": "*Avaritia* est immoderata divitiarum cupiditas et amor.", "en": "Greed is an immoderate desire and love of riches."}, {"id": "E3DA48", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA48", "la": "*Libido* est etiam cupiditas et amor in commiscendis corporibus.", "en": "Lust is also a desire and love in the joining of bodies."}, {"id": "E3DA48Ex", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA48Ex", "la": "Sive haec coeundi cupiditas moderata sit sive non sit, libido appellari solet. Porro hi quinque affectus (ut in schol. prop. 56. huius monui) contrarios non habent. Nam modestia species est ambitionis, de qua vide schol. prop. 29. huius. Temperantiam deinde, sobrietatem et castitatem mentis potentiam, non autem passionem indicare, iam etiam monui. Et tametsi fieri potest ut homo avarus, ambitiosus vel timidus a nimio cibo, potu et coitu abstineat, avaritia tamen, ambitio et timor luxuriae, ebrietati vel libidini non sunt contrarii. Nam avarus in cibum et potum alienum se ingurgitare plerumque desiderat. Ambitiosus autem, modo speret fore clam, in nulla re sibi temperabit, et si inter ebrios vivat et libidinosos, ideo quia ambitiosus est, proclivior erit ad eadem vitia. Timidus denique id quod non vult, facit. Nam quamvis mortis vitandae causa divitias in mare proiiciat, manet tamen avarus; et si libidinosus tristis est, quod sibi morem gerere nequeat, non desinit propterea libidinosus esse. Et absolute hi affectus non tam ipsos actus convivandi, potandi etc. respiciunt, quam ipsum appetitum et amorem. Nihil igitur his affectibus opponi potest praeter generositatem et animositatem, de quibus in seqq.", "en": "Whether this desire for sexual union is moderate or not, it is usually called lust. Further, these five affects (as I pointed out in the Schol. of Prop. 56 of this Part) have no contraries. For modesty is a species of ambition, on which see the Schol. of Prop. 29 of this Part. Next, I have also already pointed out that temperance, sobriety and chastity indicate a power of the mind, not a passion. And although it can happen that a greedy, ambitious or timid man abstains from too much food, drink and sexual union, greed, ambition and timidity are nevertheless not contrary to gluttony, drunkenness or lust. For the greedy man generally longs to gorge himself on another’s food and drink. The ambitious man, again, provided he hopes it will be secret, will restrain himself in nothing, and if he lives among drunkards and the lustful, he will, precisely because he is ambitious, be more inclined to the same vices. The timid man, finally, does what he does not will. For although he throws his riches into the sea to avoid death, he remains greedy; and if the lustful man is sad that he cannot indulge himself, he does not on that account cease to be lustful. And absolutely, these affects regard not so much the acts themselves of feasting, drinking, etc., as the appetite and love itself. Nothing, therefore, can be opposed to these affects except nobility and tenacity, of which in what follows."}, {"id": "E3DA48Ex.2", "p": "part3", "l": "3DA48Ex", "la": "Definitiones *zelotypiae* et reliquarum animi fluctuationum silentio praetermitto, tam quia ex compositione affectuum, quos iam definivimus, oriuntur, quam quia pleraeque nomina non habent, quod ostendit ad usum vitae sufficere, easdem in genere tantummodo noscere. Ceterum ex definitionibus affectuum, quos explicuimus, liquet eos *omnes* a *cupiditate, laetitia vel tristitia* oriri, seu potius nihil praeter hos tres esse, quorum unusquisque variis nominibus appellari solet propter varias eorum relationes et denominationes extrinsecas. Si iam ad hos primitivos et ad eam, quae de natura mentis supra diximus, attendere velimus, affectus, quatenus ad solam mentem referuntur, sic definire poterimus.", "en": "I pass over in silence the definitions of jealousy and the other fluctuations of mind, both because they arise from the composition of affects we have already defined and because most of them have no names, which shows that for the conduct of life it is enough to know them only in general. For the rest, it is clear from the definitions of the affects we have explained that they all arise from desire, joy or sadness, or rather are nothing but these three, each of which is usually called by various names on account of their various relations and extrinsic denominations. If we now wish to attend to these primitive affects and to what we said above about the nature of the mind, we shall be able to define the affects, insofar as they are related to the mind alone, as follows."}, {"id": "E3GDA.2", "p": "part3", "l": "3GDA", "la": "Affectus, qui *animi pathema* dicitur, est confusa idea, qua mens maiorem vel minorem sui corporis vel alicuius eius partis existendi vim, quam antea, affirmat, et qua data ipsa mens ad hoc potius, quam ad illud cogitandum determinatur.", "en": "An affect, which is called a passion of the mind, is a confused idea by which the mind affirms of its body, or of some part of it, a greater or lesser force of existing than before, and by which, when it is given, the mind itself is determined to think of this rather than of that."}, {"id": "E3GDAEx", "p": "part3", "l": "3GDAEx", "la": "Dico primo affectum seu passionem animi esse “confusam ideam”. Nam mentem eatenus tantum pati ostendimus (vide prop. 3 huius), quatenus ideas inadaequatas sive confusas habet. Dico deinde “qua mens maiorem vel minorem sui corporis vel alicuius eius partis existendi vim quam antea affirmat”. Omnes enim corporum ideae, quas habemus, magis nostri corporis actualem constitutionem (per coroll. 2. prop. 16. P. 2.), quam corporis externi naturam indicant; at haec, quae affectus formam constituit, corporis vel alicuius eius partis constitutionem indicare vel exprimere debet, quam ipsum corpus vel aliqua eius pars habet, ex eo, quod ipsius agendi potentia sive existendi vis augetur vel minuitur, iuvatur vel coercetur.", "en": "I say first that an affect, or passion of the mind, is ‘a confused idea’. For we have shown that the mind is acted on only insofar as it has inadequate, or confused, ideas (see Prop. 3 of this Part). I say next ‘by which the mind affirms of its body, or of some part of it, a greater or lesser force of existing than before’. For all the ideas of bodies which we have indicate the actual constitution of our body (by Cor. 2 of Prop. 16 of Part II) more than the nature of the external body; but this idea, which constitutes the form of the affect, must indicate or express the constitution of the body, or of some part of it, which the body itself, or some part of it, has because its power of acting, or force of existing, is increased or diminished, aided or restrained."}, {"id": "E3GDAEx", "p": "part3", "l": "3GDAEx", "la": "Sed notandum cum dico “maiorem vel minorem existendi vim, quam antea”, me non intelligere, quod mens praesentem corporis constitutionem cum praeterita comparat, sed quod idea, quae affectus formam constituit, aliquid de corpore affirmat, quod plus minusve realitatis revera involvit, quam antea. Et quia essentia mentis in hoc consistit (per prop. 11. et 13. P. 2.), quod sui corporis actualem existentiam affirmat, et nos per perfectionem ipsam rei essentiam intelligimus; sequitur ergo, quod mens ad maiorem minoremve perfectionem transit, quando ei aliquid de suo corpore vel aliqua eius parte affirmare contingit, quod plus minusve realitatis involvit, quam antea. Cum igitur supra dixerim mentis cogitandi potentiam augeri vel minui, nihil aliud intelligere volui, quam quod mens ideam sui corporis vel alicuius eius partis formaverit, quae plus minusve realitatis exprimit, quam de suo corpore affirmaverat. Nam idearum praestantia et actualis cogitandi potentia ex obiecti praestantia aestimatur. Addidi denique “et qua data ipsa mens ad hoc potius quam ad aliud cogitandum determinatur”, ut praeter laetitiae et tristitiae naturam, quam prima definitionis pars explicat, cupiditatis etiam naturam exprimerem.", "en": "But it should be noted that when I say ‘a greater or lesser force of existing than before’, I do not understand that the mind compares the present constitution of the body with a past one, but that the idea which constitutes the form of the affect affirms something of the body which really involves more or less reality than before. And because the essence of the mind consists in this (by Props. 11 and 13 of Part II), that it affirms the actual existence of its body, and we understand by perfection the very essence of a thing, it follows that the mind passes to a greater or lesser perfection when it happens to affirm something of its body, or of some part of it, which involves more or less reality than before. When, therefore, I said above that the mind’s power of thinking is increased or diminished, I wanted to understand nothing but that the mind has formed an idea of its body, or of some part of it, which expresses more or less reality than it had affirmed of its body. For the excellence of ideas and the actual power of thinking are estimated from the excellence of the object. I added, finally, ‘and by which, when it is given, the mind itself is determined to think of this rather than of anything else’, so as to express, besides the nature of joy and sadness, which the first part of the definition explains, the nature of desire as well."}, {"id": "E4Pref", "p": "part4", "l": "4Pref", "la": "Humanam impotentiam in moderandis et coercendis affectibus *servitutem* voco. Homo enim affectibus obnoxius sui iuris non est, sed fortunae, in cuius potestate ita est, ut saepe coactus sit, quamquam meliora sibi videat, deteriora tamen sequi. Huius rei causam, et quid praeterea affectus boni vel mali habent, in hac parte demonstrare proposui. Sed antequam incipiam, pauca de *perfectione* et *imperfectione*, deque *bono* et *malo* praefari lubet.", "en": "Man’s lack of power in moderating and restraining the affects I call bondage. For a man who is subject to the affects is not under his own control, but under that of fortune, in whose power he is to such an extent that he is often compelled, although he sees the better for himself, still to follow the worse. The cause of this, and what, besides, the affects have that is good or evil, I have proposed to demonstrate in this Part. But before I begin, I should like to say a few things first about perfection and imperfection, and about good and evil."}, {"id": "E4Pref.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4Pref", "la": "Qui rem aliquam facere constituit eamque perfecit, rem suam perfectam esse non tantum ipse, sed etiam unusquisque, qui mentem auctoris illius operis et scopum recte noverit aut se novisse crediderit, dicet. Ex. gr. si quis aliquod opus (quod suppono nondum esse peractum) viderit, noveritque scopum auctoris illius operis esse domum aedificare, is domum imperfectam esse dicet, et contra perfectam, simulatque opus ad finem, quem eius auctor eidem dare constituerat, perductum viderit. Verum si quis opus aliquod videt, cuius simile nunquam viderat, nec mentem opificis novit, is sane scire non poterit, opusne illud perfectum, an imperfectum sit. Atque haec videtur *prima* fuisse horum vocabulorum *significatio*.", "en": "If someone has decided to make something and has finished it, he will call his thing perfect; and so will anyone who rightly knows, or believes he knows, the mind and aim of the author of that work. For example, if someone sees a work (which I suppose not yet to be completed) and knows that the aim of its author is to build a house, he will say that the house is imperfect; and on the other hand perfect, as soon as he sees that the work has been brought to the end which its author had decided to give it. But if someone sees a work whose like he had never seen, and does not know the mind of the maker, he will certainly not be able to know whether that work is perfect or imperfect. And this seems to have been the first meaning of these words."}, {"id": "E4Pref.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4Pref", "la": "Sed postquam homines ideas universales formare, et domuum, aedificiorum, turrium etc. exemplaria excogitare, et alia rerum exemplaria aliis praeferre inceperunt, factum est, ut unusquisque id perfectum vocaret, quod cum universali idea, quam eiusmodi rei formaverat, videret convenire, et id contra imperfectum, quod cum concepto suo exemplari minus convenire videret, quamquam ex opificis sententia consummatum plane esset. Nec alia videtur esse ratio, cur res naturales etiam, quae scilicet humana manu non sunt factae, perfectas aut imperfectas vulgo appellent; solent namque homines tam rerum naturalium, quam artificialium ideas formare universales, quas rerum veluti exemplaria habent et quas naturam (quam nihil nisi alicuius finis causa agere existimant) intueri credunt sibique exemplaria proponere. Cum itaque aliquid in natura fieri vident, quod cum concepto exemplari, quod rei eiusmodi habent, minus convenit, ipsam naturam tum defecisse vel peccavisse, remque illam imperfectam reliquisse credunt. Videmus itaque homines consuevisse, res naturales perfectas aut imperfectas vocare magis ex praeiudicio, quam ex earum vera cognitione.", "en": "But after men began to form universal ideas, and to think out models of houses, buildings, towers, etc., and to prefer some models of things to others, it came about that each called perfect what he saw agree with the universal idea he had formed of a thing of this kind, and imperfect, on the other hand, what he saw agree less with the model he had conceived, even though in the opinion of the maker it had been entirely finished. Nor does there seem to be any other reason why men commonly call natural things too, which have not been made by human hand, perfect or imperfect; for men are accustomed to form universal ideas of natural things as well as of artificial ones, which they hold as models of things, and which they believe nature (which they think does nothing except for the sake of some end) looks to and sets before itself as models. When, therefore, they see something happen in nature which agrees less with the model they have conceived of a thing of this kind, they believe that nature itself has then failed or erred, and left that thing imperfect. We see, therefore, that men have been accustomed to call natural things perfect or imperfect more from prejudice than from true knowledge of them."}, {"id": "E4Pref.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4Pref", "la": "Ostendimus enim in primae partis appendice naturam propter finem non agere; aeternum namque illud et infinitum ens, quod Deum seu naturam appellamus, eadem, qua existit necessitate agit. Ex qua enim naturae necessitate existit, ex eadem ipsum agere ostendimus (prop. 16. P. 1.). Ratio igitur seu causa, cur Deus seu natura agit et cur existit, una eademque est. Ut ergo nullius finis causa existit, nullius etiam finis causa agit; sed ut existendi, sic et agendi principium vel finem habet nullum.", "en": "For we have shown in the Appendix of the first Part that nature does not act for the sake of an end; for that eternal and infinite being which we call God, or nature, acts by the same necessity by which it exists. For we have shown that it acts by the same necessity of nature by which it exists (Prop. 16 of Part I). The reason, therefore, or cause, why God, or nature, acts and why it exists is one and the same. As, therefore, it exists for the sake of no end, so it also acts for the sake of no end; but as it has no principle or end of existing, so it has none of acting either."}, {"id": "E4Pref.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4Pref", "la": "Causa autem, quae finalis dicitur, nihil est praeter ipsum humanum appetitum, quatenus is alicuius rei veluti principium seu causa primaria consideratur. Ex. gr. cum dicimus habitationem causam fuisse finalem huius aut illius domus, nihil tum sane intelligimus aliud, quam quod homo ex eo, quod vitae domesticae commoda imaginatus est, appetitum habuit aedificandi domum. Quare habitatio, quatenus ut finalis causa consideratur, nihil est praeter hunc singularem appetitum, qui revera causa est efficiens, quae ut prima consideratur, quia homines suorum appetituum causas communiter ignorant. Sunt namque, ut iam saepe dixi, suarum quidem actionum et appetituum conscii, sed ignari causarum, a quibus ad aliquid appetendum determinantur. Quod praeterea vulgo aiunt, naturam aliquando deficere vel peccare, resque imperfectas producere, inter commenta numero, de quibus in app. P. 1. egi.", "en": "But what is called a final cause is nothing but human appetite itself, insofar as it is considered as the principle, or primary cause, of some thing. For example, when we say that habitation was the final cause of this or that house, we certainly understand nothing but that a man, because he imagined the advantages of domestic life, had an appetite to build a house. So habitation, insofar as it is considered as a final cause, is nothing but this singular appetite, which is really an efficient cause, which is considered as first because men are commonly ignorant of the causes of their appetites. For they are, as I have often said, conscious of their actions and appetites, but ignorant of the causes by which they are determined to want something. As for what they commonly say, that nature sometimes fails or errs and produces imperfect things, I number this among the fictions which I treated in the Appendix of Part I."}, {"id": "E4Pref.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4Pref", "la": "Perfectio igitur et imperfectio revera modi solummodo cogitandi sunt, nempe notiones, quas fingere solemus ex eo, quod eiusdem speciei aut generis individua ad invicem comparamus. Et hac de causa supra (def. 6. P. 2.) dixi me per realitatem et perfectionem idem intelligere; solemus enim omnia naturae individua ad unum genus, quod generalissimum appellatur, revocare; nempe ad notionem entis, quae ad omnia absolute naturae individua pertinet. Quatenus itaque naturae individua ad hoc genus revocamus, et ad invicem comparamus, et alia plus entitatis seu realitatis, quam alia habere comperimus, eatenus alia aliis perfectiora esse dicimus; et quatenus iisdem aliquid tribuimus, quod negationem involvit, ut terminus, finis, impotentia etc., eatenus ipsa imperfecta appellamus, quia nostram mentem non aeque afficiunt, ac illa, quae perfecta vocamus, et non quod ipsis aliquid, quod suum sit, deficiat vel quod natura peccaverit. Nihil enim naturae alicuius rei competit, nisi id quod ex necessitate naturae causae efficientis sequitur; et quicquid ex necessitate naturae causae efficientis sequitur, id necessario fit.", "en": "Perfection and imperfection, therefore, are in truth only modes of thinking, namely notions which we are accustomed to feign from the fact that we compare individuals of the same species or genus with one another. And for this reason I said above (Def. 6 of Part II) that by reality and perfection I understand the same thing; for we are accustomed to refer all the individuals of nature to one genus, which is called the most general, namely to the notion of being, which pertains absolutely to all the individuals of nature. Insofar, therefore, as we refer the individuals of nature to this genus, and compare them with one another, and find that some have more being, or reality, than others, to that extent we say that some are more perfect than others; and insofar as we attribute to them something which involves negation, such as limit, end, lack of power, etc., to that extent we call them imperfect, because they do not affect our mind as much as those which we call perfect, and not because they lack something which is theirs, or because nature has erred. For nothing belongs to the nature of any thing except what follows from the necessity of the nature of the efficient cause; and whatever follows from the necessity of the nature of the efficient cause necessarily happens."}, {"id": "E4Pref.3", "p": "part4", "l": "4Pref", "la": "Bonum et malum quod attinet, nihil etiam positivum in rebus, in se scilicet consideratis, indicant, nec aliud sunt praeter cogitandi modos seu notiones, quas formamus ex eo, quod res ad invicem comparamus. Nam una eademque res potest eodem tempore bona et mala, et etiam indifferens esse. Ex. gr. musica bona est melancholico, mala lugenti; surdo autem neque bona neque mala. Verum quamvis se res ita habeat, nobis tamen haec vocabula retinenda sunt.", "en": "As for good and evil, they too indicate nothing positive in things, considered in themselves, and are nothing but modes of thinking, or notions, which we form from the fact that we compare things with one another. For one and the same thing can at the same time be good and bad, and also indifferent. For example, music is good for one who is melancholy, bad for one who mourns, and neither good nor bad for one who is deaf. But although this is so, we must nevertheless retain these words."}, {"id": "E4Pref.3", "p": "part4", "l": "4Pref", "la": "Nam quia ideam hominis, tamquam naturae humanae exemplar, quod intueamur, formare cupimus, nobis ex usu erit, haec eadem vocabula eo, quo dixi, sensu retinere. Per bonum itaque in seqq. intelligam id, quod certo scimus medium esse, ut ad exemplar humanae naturae, quod nobis proponimus, magis magisque accedamus; per malum autem id, quod certo scimus impedire, quominus idem exemplar referamus. Deinde homines perfectiores aut imperfectiores dicemus, quatenus ad hoc idem exemplar magis aut minus accedunt.", "en": "For because we desire to form an idea of man, as a model of human nature which we may look to, it will be useful to us to retain these same words in the sense I have indicated. By good, therefore, I shall understand in what follows that which we certainly know to be a means of approaching nearer and nearer to the model of human nature which we set before ourselves; by evil, that which we certainly know prevents us from resembling that model. Next, we shall say that men are more perfect or more imperfect insofar as they come nearer to or are further from this same model."}, {"id": "E4Pref.3", "p": "part4", "l": "4Pref", "la": "Nam apprime notandum est, cum dico, aliquem a minore ad maiorem perfectionem transire, et contra, me non intelligere quod ex una essentia seu forma in aliam mutatur (equus namque ex. gr. tam destruitur, si in hominem, quam si in insectum mutetur); sed quod eius agendi potentiam, quatenus haec per ipsius naturam intelligitur, augeri vel minui concipimus. Denique per perfectionem in genere realitatem, uti dixi, intelligam, hoc est rei cuiuscumque essentiam, quatenus certo modo existit et operatur, nulla ipsius durationis habita ratione. Nam nulla res singularis potest ideo dici perfectior, quia plus temporis in existendo perseveravit; quippe rerum duratio ex earum essentia determinari nequit, quandoquidem rerum essentia nullum certum et determinatum existendi tempus involvit; sed res quaecumque, sive ea perfectior sit sive minus, eadem vi, qua existere incipit, semper in existendo perseverare poterit, ita ut omnes hac in re aequales sint.", "en": "For it should be especially noted that when I say that someone passes from a lesser to a greater perfection, and the contrary, I do not understand that he is changed from one essence, or form, into another (for a horse, for example, is destroyed as much if it is changed into a man as if into an insect); but that we conceive his power of acting, insofar as it is understood through his nature, to be increased or diminished. Finally, by perfection in general I shall understand reality, as I have said, that is, the essence of any thing insofar as it exists and operates in a certain way, without any regard to its duration. For no singular thing can be called more perfect because it has persevered in existing for a longer time; for the duration of things cannot be determined from their essence, since the essence of things involves no certain and determinate time of existing; but any thing whatever, whether it is more perfect or less, will always be able to persevere in existing by the same force by which it begins to exist, so that in this respect all are equal."}, {"id": "E4D1", "p": "part4", "l": "4D1", "la": "Per *bonum* id intelligam, quod certo scimus nobis esse utile.", "en": "By good I shall understand that which we certainly know to be useful to us."}, {"id": "E4D2", "p": "part4", "l": "4D2", "la": "Per *malum* autem id, quod certo scimus impedire, quominus boni alicuius simus compotes.", "en": "By evil, however, that which we certainly know prevents us from possessing some good."}, {"id": "E4D2.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4D2", "la": "De his praecedentem vide praefationem sub finem.", "en": "On these, see the preceding Preface towards the end."}, {"id": "E4D3", "p": "part4", "l": "4D3", "la": "Res singulares voco *contingentes, quatenus, dum ad earum solam essentiam attendimus, nihil invenimus, quod earum existentiam necessario ponat, vel quod ipsam necessario secludat.*", "en": "I call singular things contingent insofar as, when we attend only to their essence, we find nothing which necessarily posits their existence or which necessarily excludes it."}, {"id": "E4D4", "p": "part4", "l": "4D4", "la": "Easdem res singulares voco *possibiles, quatenus, dum ad causas, ex quibus produci debent, attendimus, nescimus, an ipsae determinatae sint ad easdem producendum.*", "en": "I call the same singular things possible insofar as, when we attend to the causes from which they must be produced, we do not know whether these are determined to produce them."}, {"id": "E4D4.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4D4", "la": "In schol. 1. prop. 33. P. 1. inter possibile et contingens nullam feci differentiam, quia ibi non opus erat haec accurate distinguere.", "en": "In Schol. 1 of Prop. 33 of Part I I made no difference between the possible and the contingent, because there was no need there to distinguish them accurately."}, {"id": "E4D5", "p": "part4", "l": "4D5", "la": "Per *contrarios affectus* in seqq. intelligam eos, qui hominem diversum trahunt, quamvis eiusdem sint generis, ut luxuries et avaritia, quae amoris sunt species; nec natura, sed per accidens sunt contrarii.", "en": "By contrary affects I shall understand in what follows those which draw a man in different directions, even though they are of the same genus, like gluttony and greed, which are species of love; and they are contrary not by nature but accidentally."}, {"id": "E4D6", "p": "part4", "l": "4D6", "la": "Quid per *affectum erga rem futuram, praesentem* et *praeteritam* intelligam, explicui in schol. 1. et 2. prop. 18. P. 3., quod vide.", "en": "What I understand by an affect towards a future, present and past thing I have explained in Schols. 1 and 2 of Prop. 18 of Part III, which see."}, {"id": "E4D6.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4D6", "la": "Sed venit hic praeterea notandum, quod ut loci, sic etiam temporis, distantiam non nisi usque ad certum quendam limitem possumus distincte imaginari, hoc est, sicut omnia illa obiecta, quae ultra ducentos pedes a nobis distant, seu quorum distantia a loco, in quo sumus, illam superat, quam distincte imaginamur, aeque longe a nobis distare, et perinde ac si in eodem plano essent, imaginari solemus; sic etiam obiecta, quorum existendi tempus longiore a praesenti intervallo abesse imaginamur, quam quod distincte imaginari solemus, omnia aeque longe a praesenti distare imaginamur et ad unum quasi temporis momentum referimus.", "en": "But it should be noted here besides that, as with distance of place, so also with distance of time, we can imagine distinctly only up to a certain limit; that is, just as we usually imagine all those objects which are more than two hundred feet away from us, or whose distance from the place where we are exceeds that which we imagine distinctly, to be equally far from us, and as if they were in the same plane, so also we imagine all objects whose time of existing we imagine to be separated from the present by a longer interval than we usually imagine distinctly to be equally far from the present, and we refer them as it were to one moment of time."}, {"id": "E4D7", "p": "part4", "l": "4D7", "la": "Per *finem, cuius causa aliquid facimus, appetitum intelligo.*", "en": "By the end for the sake of which we do something I understand appetite."}, {"id": "E4D8", "p": "part4", "l": "4D8", "la": "Per *virtutem* et *potentiam* idem intelligo, hoc est (per prop. 7. P. 3.) virtus, quatenus ad hominem refertur, est ipsa hominis essentia seu natura, quatenus potestatem habet quaedam efficiendi, quae per solas ipsius naturae leges possunt intelligi.", "en": "By virtue and power I understand the same thing; that is (by Prop. 7 of Part III), virtue, insofar as it is related to man, is the very essence, or nature, of man insofar as he has the power of bringing about certain things which can be understood through the laws of his nature alone."}, {"id": "E4A1", "p": "part4", "l": "4A1", "la": "Nulla res singularis in rerum natura datur, qua potentior et fortior non detur alia. Sed quacumque data datur alia potentior, a qua illa data potest destrui.", "en": "There is no singular thing in the nature of things than which there is not another more powerful and stronger. But given any one, there is another more powerful by which the given one can be destroyed."}, {"id": "E4P1", "p": "part4", "l": "4P1", "la": "Nihil, quod idea falsa positivum habet, tollitur praesentia veri, quatenus verum.", "en": "Nothing positive which a false idea has is removed by the presence of the true, insofar as it is true."}, {"id": "E4P1D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P1D", "la": "Falsitas in sola privatione cognitionis, quam ideae inadaequatae involvunt, consistit (per prop. 35. P. 2.), nec ipsae aliquid habent positivum, propter quod falsae dicuntur (per prop. 33. P. 2.); sed contra, quatenus ad Deum referuntur, verae sunt (per prop. 32. P. 2.). Si igitur id quod idea falsa positivum habet, praesentia veri, quatenus verum est, tolleretur, tolleretur ergo idea vera a se ipsa, quod (per prop. 4. P. 3.) est absurdum. Ergo nihil, quod idea etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "Falsity consists only in the privation of knowledge which inadequate ideas involve (by Prop. 35 of Part II), nor do they have anything positive on account of which they are called false (by Prop. 33 of Part II); on the contrary, insofar as they are related to God, they are true (by Prop. 32 of Part II). If, therefore, what a false idea has that is positive were removed by the presence of the true, insofar as it is true, a true idea would be removed by itself, which (by Prop. 4 of Part III) is absurd. Therefore nothing positive which a false idea, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P1S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P1S", "la": "Intelligitur haec propositio clarius ex coroll. 2. prop. 16. P. 2. Nam imaginatio idea est, quae magis corporis humani praesentem constitutionem, quam corporis externi naturam indicat, non quidem distincte, sed confuse; unde fit, ut mens errare dicatur. Ex. gr. cum solem intuemur, eundem ducentos circiter pedes a nobis distare imaginamur; in quo tamdiu fallimur, quamdiu veram eius distantiam ignoramus; sed cognita eiusdem distantia tollitur quidem error, sed non imaginatio, hoc est, idea solis, quae eiusdem naturam eatenus tantum explicat, quatenus corpus ab eodem afficitur; adeoque quamvis veram eiusdem distantiam noscamus, ipsum nihilominus prope nobis adesse imaginabimur.", "en": "This Proposition is understood more clearly from Cor. 2 of Prop. 16 of Part II. For an imagination is an idea which indicates the present constitution of the human body more than the nature of the external body, not distinctly, indeed, but confusedly; from which it comes about that the mind is said to err. For example, when we look at the sun, we imagine it to be about two hundred feet away from us; in which we are deceived as long as we are ignorant of its true distance; but when its distance is known, the error is indeed removed, but not the imagination, that is, the idea of the sun, which explains its nature only insofar as the body is affected by it; and so, although we know its true distance, we shall nevertheless imagine it as near us."}, {"id": "E4P1S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P1S", "la": "Nam ut in schol. prop. 35. P. 2. diximus, non ea de causa solem adeo propinquum imaginamur, quia eius veram distantiam ignoramus, sed quia mens eatenus magnitudinem solis concipit, quatenus corpus ab eodem afficitur. Sic cum solis radii aquae superficiei incidentes ad nostros oculos reflectuntur, eundem perinde, ac si in aqua esset, imaginamur; tametsi verum eius locum noverimus. Et sic reliquae imaginationes, quibus mens fallitur, sive eae naturalem corporis constitutionem, sive quod eiusdem agendi potentiam augeri vel minui indicant, vero non sunt contrariae, nec eiusdem praesentia evanescunt. Fit quidem, cum falso aliquod malum timemus, ut timor evanescat audito vero nuntio; sed contra etiam fit, cum malum quod certe venturum est, timemus, ut timor etiam evanescat audito falso nuntio. Atque adeo imaginationes non praesentia veri, quatenus verum, evanescunt; sed quia aliae occurrunt iis fortiores, quae rerum, quas imaginamur, praesentem existentiam secludunt, ut prop. 17. P. 2. ostendimus.", "en": "For as we said in the Schol. of Prop. 35 of Part II, we do not imagine the sun so near because we are ignorant of its true distance, but because the mind conceives the size of the sun insofar as the body is affected by it. So when the rays of the sun, falling on the surface of water, are reflected to our eyes, we imagine it as if it were in the water, even though we know its true place. And so the other imaginations by which the mind is deceived, whether they indicate the natural constitution of the body or that its power of acting is increased or diminished, are not contrary to the true, nor do they vanish by its presence. It does indeed happen, when we falsely fear some evil, that the fear vanishes on hearing true news; but on the contrary it also happens, when we fear an evil which is certainly going to come, that the fear also vanishes on hearing false news. And so imaginations do not vanish by the presence of the true, insofar as it is true, but because others occur which are stronger than them and exclude the present existence of the things we imagine, as we showed in Prop. 17 of Part II."}, {"id": "E4P2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P2", "la": "Nos eatenus patimur, quatenus naturae sumus pars, quae per se absque aliis non potest concipi.", "en": "We are acted on insofar as we are a part of nature which cannot be conceived through itself without others."}, {"id": "E4P2D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P2D", "la": "Nos tum pati dicimur, cum aliquid in nobis oritur, cuius non nisi partialis sumus causa (per defin. 2. P. 3.), hoc est (per defin. 1. P. 3.) aliquid, quod ex solis legibus nostrae naturae deduci nequit. Patimur igitur, quatenus naturae sumus pars, quae per se absque aliis nequit concipi. Q. E. D.", "en": "We are said to be acted on when something arises in us of which we are only a partial cause (by Def. 2 of Part III), that is (by Def. 1 of Part III), something which cannot be deduced from the laws of our nature alone. We are acted on, therefore, insofar as we are a part of nature which cannot be conceived through itself without others. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P3", "p": "part4", "l": "4P3", "la": "Vis, qua homo in existendo perseverat, limitata est et a potentia causarum externarum infinite superatur.", "en": "The force by which a man perseveres in existing is limited, and is infinitely surpassed by the power of external causes."}, {"id": "E4P3D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P3D", "la": "Patet ex axiom. huius. Nam dato homine datur aliquid aliud, puta A potentius, et dato A datur deinde aliud, puta B, ipso A potentius et hoc in infinitum. Ac proinde potentia hominis potentia alterius rei definitur, et a potentia causarum externarum infinite superatur. Q. E. D.", "en": "This is evident from the Axiom of this Part. For given a man, there is something else, say A, more powerful; and given A, there is then something else, say B, more powerful than A; and so to infinity. And therefore the power of a man is defined by the power of another thing, and is infinitely surpassed by the power of external causes. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P4", "p": "part4", "l": "4P4", "la": "Fieri non potest, ut homo non sit naturae pars et ut nullas possit pati mutationes, nisi quae per solam suam naturam possint intelligi, quarumque adaequata sit causa.", "en": "It cannot happen that a man should not be a part of nature, and that he should be able to undergo no changes except those which can be understood through his own nature alone, and of which he is the adequate cause."}, {"id": "E4P4D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P4D", "la": "Potentia, qua res singulares, et consequenter homo suum esse conservat, est ipsa Dei sive naturae potentia (per coroll. prop. 24. P. 1.), non quatenus infinita est, sed quatenus per humanam actualem essentiam explicari potest (per prop. 7. P. 3.). Potentia itaque hominis, quatenus per ipsius actualem essentiam explicatur, pars est infinitae Dei seu naturae potentiae, hoc est (per prop. 34. P. 1.), essentiae. Quod erat primum.", "en": "The power by which singular things, and consequently man, preserve their being is the very power of God, or nature (by Cor. of Prop. 24 of Part I), not insofar as it is infinite, but insofar as it can be explained through the actual essence of man (by Prop. 7 of Part III). The power of man, therefore, insofar as it is explained through his actual essence, is a part of the infinite power of God, or nature, that is (by Prop. 34 of Part I), of his essence. This was the first point."}, {"id": "E4P4D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P4D", "la": "Deinde si fieri posset, ut homo nullas posset pati mutationes, nisi quae per solam ipsius hominis naturam possint intelligi, sequeretur (per prop. 4. et 6. P. 3.) ut non posset perire, sed ut semper necessario existeret. Atque hoc sequi deberet ex causa, cuius potentia finita aut infinita sit, nempe vel ex sola hominis potentia, qui scilicet potis esset, ut a se removeret reliquas mutationes, quae a causis externis oriri possent; vel infinita naturae potentia, a qua omnia singularia ita dirigerentur, ut homo nullas alias posset pati mutationes, nisi quae ipsius conservationi inserviunt. At primum (per prop. praeced. cuius demonstratio universalis est et ad omnes res singulares applicari potest) est absurdum.", "en": "Next, if it could happen that a man could undergo no changes except those which can be understood through the nature of man himself alone, it would follow (by Props. 4 and 6 of Part III) that he could not perish, but that he would always necessarily exist. And this would have to follow from a cause whose power is finite or infinite: namely, either from the power of man alone, who would then be able to remove from himself the other changes which could arise from external causes; or from the infinite power of nature, by which all singular things would be so directed that a man could undergo no other changes except those which serve his preservation. But the first (by the preceding Prop., whose demonstration is universal and can be applied to all singular things) is absurd."}, {"id": "E4P4D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P4D", "la": "Ergo si fieri posset, ut homo nullas pateretur mutationes, nisi quae per solam ipsius hominis naturam possent intelligi, et consequenter (sicut iam ostendimus) ut semper necessario existeret, id sequi deberet ex Dei infinita potentia; et consequenter (per prop. 16. P. 1.) ex necessitate divinae naturae, quatenus alicuius hominis idea affectus consideratur, totius naturae ordo, quatenus ipsa sub extensionis et cogitationis attributis concipitur, deduci deberet. Atque adeo (per prop. 21. P. 1.) sequeretur, ut homo esset infinitus, quod (per primam partem huius demonstrationis) est absurdum. Fieri itaque nequit, ut homo nullas alias patiatur mutationes, nisi quarum ipse adaequata sit causa. Q. E. D.", "en": "Therefore if it could happen that a man underwent no changes except those which could be understood through the nature of man himself alone, and consequently (as we have just shown) that he always necessarily existed, this would have to follow from the infinite power of God; and consequently (by Prop. 16 of Part I) the order of the whole of nature, insofar as it is conceived under the attributes of extension and thought, would have to be deduced from the necessity of the divine nature insofar as it is considered as affected by the idea of some man. And so (by Prop. 21 of Part I) it would follow that the man was infinite, which (by the first part of this demonstration) is absurd. It cannot happen, therefore, that a man should undergo no other changes except those of which he himself is the adequate cause. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P4C", "p": "part4", "l": "4P4C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, hominem necessario passionibus esse semper obnoxium, communemque naturae ordinem sequi et eidem parere, seseque eidem, quantum rerum natura exigit, accommodare.", "en": "Hence it follows that man is necessarily always subject to passions, and that he follows the common order of nature and obeys it, and accommodates himself to it as much as the nature of things requires."}, {"id": "E4P5", "p": "part4", "l": "4P5", "la": "Vis et incrementum cuiuscumque passionis, eiusque in existendo perseverantia non definitur potentia, qua nos in existendo perseverare conamur, sed causae externae potentia cum nostra comparata.", "en": "The force and increase of any passion, and its perseverance in existing, are not defined by the power by which we strive to persevere in existing, but by the power of an external cause compared with our own."}, {"id": "E4P5D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P5D", "la": "Passionis essentia non potest per solam nostram essentiam explicari (per defin. 1. et 2. P. 3.), hoc est (per prop. 7. P. 3.), passionis potentia definiri nequit potentia, qua in nostro esse perseverare conamur; sed (ut prop. 16. P. 2. ostensum est) definiri necessario debet potentia causae externae cum nostra comparata. Q. E. D.", "en": "The essence of a passion cannot be explained through our essence alone (by Defs. 1 and 2 of Part III), that is (by Prop. 7 of Part III), the power of a passion cannot be defined by the power by which we strive to persevere in our being; but (as was shown in Prop. 16 of Part II) it must necessarily be defined by the power of an external cause compared with our own. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P6", "p": "part4", "l": "4P6", "la": "Vis alicuius passionis seu affectus reliquas hominis actiones seu potentiam superare potest, ita ut affectus pertinaciter homini adhaereat.", "en": "The force of some passion, or affect, can surpass the other actions, or power, of a man, so that the affect stubbornly clings to the man."}, {"id": "E4P6D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P6D", "la": "Vis et incrementum cuiuscumque passionis eiusque in existendo perseverantia definitur potentia causae externae cum nostra comparata (per prop. praec.); adeoque (per prop. 3. huius) hominis potentiam superare potest etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "The force and increase of any passion, and its perseverance in existing, are defined by the power of an external cause compared with our own (by the preceding Prop.); and so (by Prop. 3 of this Part) it can surpass the power of a man, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P7", "p": "part4", "l": "4P7", "la": "Affectus nec coerceri nec tolli potest, nisi per affectum contrarium et fortiorem affectu coercendo.", "en": "An affect can be neither restrained nor removed except by an affect contrary to it and stronger than the affect to be restrained."}, {"id": "E4P7D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P7D", "la": "Affectus, quatenus ad mentem refertur, est idea qua mens maiorem vel minorem sui corporis existendi vim, quam antea, affirmat (per gener. aff. defin. quae reperitur sub finem P. 3.). Cum igitur mens aliquo affectu conflictatur, corpus afficitur simul affectione, qua eius agendi potentia augetur vel minuitur.", "en": "An affect, insofar as it is related to the mind, is an idea by which the mind affirms of its body a greater or lesser force of existing than before (by the General Definition of the Affects, which is found at the end of Part III). When, therefore, the mind is troubled by some affect, the body is at the same time affected with an affection by which its power of acting is increased or diminished."}, {"id": "E4P7D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P7D", "la": "Porro haec corporis affectio (per prop. 5. huius) vim a sua causa accipit perseverandi in suo esse; quae proinde nec coerceri nec tolli potest, nisi a causa corporea (per prop. 6. P. 2.), quae corpus afficiat affectione illi contraria (per prop. 5. P. 3.) et fortiore (per axiom. huius). Atque adeo (per prop. 12. P. 2.) mens afficitur idea affectionis fortioris et contrariae priori, hoc est (per gener. aff. defin.) mens afficietur affectu fortiori et contrario priori, qui scilicet prioris existentiam secludet vel tollet; ac proinde affectus nec tolli nec coerceri potest, nisi per affectum contrarium et fortiorem. Q. E. D.", "en": "Further, this affection of the body (by Prop. 5 of this Part) receives from its cause the force of persevering in its being; which, therefore, can be neither restrained nor removed except by a corporeal cause (by Prop. 6 of Part II) which affects the body with an affection contrary to it (by Prop. 5 of Part III) and stronger (by the Axiom of this Part). And so (by Prop. 12 of Part II) the mind is affected by the idea of an affection stronger than and contrary to the former, that is (by the General Definition of the Affects), the mind will be affected by an affect stronger than and contrary to the former, which will exclude or remove the existence of the former; and accordingly an affect can be neither removed nor restrained except by a contrary and stronger affect. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P7C", "p": "part4", "l": "4P7C", "la": "Affectus, quatenus ad mentem refertur, nec coerceri nec tolli potest nisi per ideam corporis affectionis contrariae et fortioris affectione, qua patimur. Nam affectus, quo patimur, nec coerceri nec tolli potest, nisi per affectum eodem fortiorem eique contrarium (per prop. praec.), hoc est (per gener. aff. defin.), nisi per ideam corporis affectionis fortioris et contrariae affectioni, qua patimur.", "en": "An affect, insofar as it is related to the mind, can be neither restrained nor removed except by the idea of an affection of the body contrary to, and stronger than, the affection by which we are acted on. For an affect by which we are acted on can be neither restrained nor removed except by an affect stronger than it and contrary to it (by the preceding Prop.), that is (by the General Definition of the Affects), except by the idea of an affection of the body stronger than and contrary to the affection by which we are acted on."}, {"id": "E4P8", "p": "part4", "l": "4P8", "la": "Cognitio boni et mali nihil aliud est, quam laetitiae vel tristitiae affectus, quatenus eius sumus conscii.", "en": "The knowledge of good and evil is nothing but the affect of joy or sadness, insofar as we are conscious of it."}, {"id": "E4P8D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P8D", "la": "Id bonum aut malum vocamus, quod nostro esse conservando prodest vel obest (per defin. 1. et 2. huius), hoc est (per prop. 7. P. 3.), quod nostram agendi potentiam auget vel minuit, iuvat vel coercet. Quatenus itaque (per defin. laetitiae et tristitiae, quas vide in schol. prop. 11. P. 3.) rem aliquam nos laetitia vel tristitia afficere percipimus, eandem bonam aut malam vocamus; atque adeo boni et mali cognitio nihil aliud est, quam laetitiae vel tristitiae idea, quae ex ipso laetitiae vel tristitiae affectu necessario sequitur (per prop. 22. P. 2.).", "en": "We call good or evil what is of use or hindrance to preserving our being (by Defs. 1 and 2 of this Part), that is (by Prop. 7 of Part III), what increases or diminishes, aids or restrains, our power of acting. Insofar, therefore, as we perceive that some thing affects us with joy or sadness (by the definitions of joy and sadness, which see in Schol. of Prop. 11 of Part III), we call it good or evil; and so the knowledge of good and evil is nothing but the idea of joy or sadness which necessarily follows from the affect of joy or sadness itself (by Prop. 22 of Part II)."}, {"id": "E4P8D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P8D", "la": "At haec idea eodem modo unita est affectui, ac mens unita est corpori (per prop. 21. P. 2.), hoc est (ut in schol. eiusdem prop. ostensum), haec idea ab ipso affectu, sive (per gener. aff. defin.) ab idea corporis affectionis revera non distinguitur nisi solo conceptu. Ergo haec cognitio boni et mali nihil est aliud, quam ipse affectus, quatenus eiusdem sumus conscii. Q. E. D.", "en": "But this idea is united to the affect in the same way as the mind is united to the body (by Prop. 21 of Part II), that is (as was shown in the Schol. of the same Prop.), this idea is not really distinguished from the affect itself, or (by the General Definition of the Affects) from the idea of the affection of the body, except by concept alone. Therefore this knowledge of good and evil is nothing but the affect itself, insofar as we are conscious of it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P9", "p": "part4", "l": "4P9", "la": "Affectus, cuius causam in praesenti nobis adesse imaginamur, fortior est, quam si eandem non adesse imaginaremur.", "en": "An affect whose cause we imagine as now present to us is stronger than if we imagined it not to be present."}, {"id": "E4P9D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P9D", "la": "Imaginatio est idea, qua mens rem ut praesentem contemplatur (vide eius defin. in schol. prop. 17. P. 2.), quae tamen magis corporis humani constitutionem, quam rei externae naturam indicat (per coroll. 2. prop. 16. P. 2.). Est igitur affectus (per gener. aff. defin.) imaginatio, quatenus corporis constitutionem indicat. At imaginatio (per prop. 17. P. 2.) intensior est, quamdiu nihil imaginamur, quod rei externae praesentem existentiam secludit. Ergo etiam affectus, cuius causam in praesenti nobis adesse imaginamur, intensior seu fortior est, quam si eandem non adesse imaginaremur. Q. E. D.", "en": "An imagination is an idea by which the mind contemplates a thing as present (see its definition in Schol. of Prop. 17 of Part II), but which indicates the constitution of the human body more than the nature of the external thing (by Cor. 2 of Prop. 16 of Part II). An affect, therefore (by the General Definition of the Affects), is an imagination, insofar as it indicates the constitution of the body. But an imagination (by Prop. 17 of Part II) is more intense as long as we imagine nothing which excludes the present existence of the external thing. Therefore an affect too, whose cause we imagine as now present to us, is more intense, or stronger, than if we imagined it not to be present. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P9S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P9S", "la": "Cum supra in prop. 18. P. 3. dixerim, nos ex rei futurae vel praeteritae imagine eodem affectu affici, ac si res, quam imaginamur, praesens esset, expresse monui, id verum esse, quatenus ad solam ipsius rei imaginem attendimus (est enim eiusdem naturae, sive res ut praesentes imaginati simus, sive non simus); sed non negavi eandem debiliorem reddi, quando alias res nobis praesentes contemplamur, quae rei futurae praesentem existentiam secludunt, quod tum monere neglexi, quia in hac parte de affectuum viribus agere constitueram.", "en": "When I said above in Prop. 18 of Part III that we are affected by the image of a future or past thing with the same affect as if the thing we imagine were present, I expressly warned that this is true insofar as we attend only to the image of the thing itself (for it is of the same nature whether we have imagined things as present or not); but I did not deny that the image is made weaker when we contemplate other things present to us which exclude the present existence of the future thing, which I neglected to point out then because I had decided to treat of the strength of the affects in this Part."}, {"id": "E4P9C", "p": "part4", "l": "4P9C", "la": "Imago rei futurae vel praeteritae, hoc est, rei, quam cum relatione ad tempus futurum vel praeteritum secluso praesenti contemplamur, ceteris paribus debilior est imagine rei praesentis, et consequenter affectus erga rem futuram vel praeteritam, ceteris paribus remissior est affectu erga rem praesentem.", "en": "The image of a future or past thing, that is, of a thing which we contemplate with relation to future or past time, the present being excluded, is weaker, other things being equal, than the image of a present thing; and consequently an affect towards a future or past thing is less intense, other things being equal, than an affect towards a present thing."}, {"id": "E4P10", "p": "part4", "l": "4P10", "la": "Erga rem futuram, quam cito affuturam imaginamur, intensius afficimur quam si eius existendi tempus longius a praesenti distare imaginaremur; et memoria rei, quam non diu praeteriisse imaginamur, intensius etiam afficimur, quam si eandem diu praeteriisse imaginaremur.", "en": "We are affected more intensely towards a future thing which we imagine will soon be present than if we imagined its time of existing to be further distant from the present; and we are also affected more intensely by the memory of a thing which we imagine to have passed not long ago than if we imagined it to have passed long ago."}, {"id": "E4P10D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P10D", "la": "Quatenus enim rem cito affuturam, vel non diu praeteriisse imaginamur, eo ipso aliquid imaginamur, quod rei praesentiam minus secludit, quam si eiusdem futurum existendi tempus longius a praesenti distare, vel quod dudum praeterierit, imaginaremur (ut per se notum); adeoque (per praec. prop.) eatenus intensius erga eandem afficiemur. Q. E. D.", "en": "For insofar as we imagine that a thing will soon be present, or has passed not long ago, by that very fact we imagine something which excludes the presence of the thing less than if we imagined its future time of existing to be further distant from the present, or that it had passed long ago (as is self-evident); and so (by the preceding Prop.) to that extent we shall be affected more intensely towards it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P10S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P10S", "la": "Ex iis, quae ad defin. 6. huius partis notavimus, sequitur nos erga obiecta, quae a praesenti longiore temporis intervallo distant, quam quod imaginando determinare possumus, quamvis ab invicem longo temporis intervallo distare intelligamus, aeque tamen remisse affici.", "en": "From what we noted on Def. 6 of this Part it follows that we are affected equally faintly towards objects which are distant from the present by a longer interval of time than we can determine by imagining, even though we understand them to be distant from one another by a long interval of time."}, {"id": "E4P11", "p": "part4", "l": "4P11", "la": "Affectus erga rem, quam ut necessariam imaginamur, ceteris paribus intensior est, quam erga possibilem vel contingentem, sive non necessariam.", "en": "An affect towards a thing which we imagine as necessary is, other things being equal, more intense than one towards a thing possible or contingent, or not necessary."}, {"id": "E4P11D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P11D", "la": "Quatenus rem aliquam necessariam esse imaginamur, eatenus eius existentiam affirmamus, et contra rei existentiam negamus, quatenus eandem non necessariam esse imaginamur (per schol. 1. prop. 33. P. 1.); ac proinde (per prop. 9. huius) affectus erga rem necessariam ceteris paribus intensior est, quam erga non necessariam. Q. E. D.", "en": "Insofar as we imagine some thing to be necessary, to that extent we affirm its existence, and on the contrary we deny a thing’s existence insofar as we imagine it not to be necessary (by Schol. 1 of Prop. 33 of Part I); and accordingly (by Prop. 9 of this Part) an affect towards a necessary thing is, other things being equal, more intense than one towards a thing not necessary. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P12", "p": "part4", "l": "4P12", "la": "Affectus erga rem, quam scimus in praesenti non existere et quam ut possibilem imaginamur, ceteris paribus intensior est, quam erga contingentem.", "en": "An affect towards a thing which we know not to exist at present, and which we imagine as possible, is, other things being equal, more intense than one towards a contingent thing."}, {"id": "E4P12D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P12D", "la": "Quatenus rem ut contingentem imaginamur, nulla alterius rei imagine afficimur, quae rei existentiam ponat (per defin. 3. huius); sed contra (secundum hypothesin) quaedam imaginamur, quae eiusdem praesentem existentiam secludunt. At quatenus rem in futurum possibilem esse imaginamur, eatenus quaedam imaginamur, quae eiusdem existentiam ponunt (per defin. 4. huius), hoc est (per prop. 18. P. 3.) quae spem vel metum fovent; atque adeo affectus erga rem possibilem vehementior est. Q. E. D.", "en": "Insofar as we imagine a thing as contingent, we are affected by no image of another thing which posits the thing’s existence (by Def. 3 of this Part); on the contrary (according to the hypothesis), we imagine certain things which exclude its present existence. But insofar as we imagine a thing to be possible in the future, to that extent we imagine certain things which posit its existence (by Def. 4 of this Part), that is (by Prop. 18 of Part III), which encourage hope or fear; and so an affect towards a possible thing is more vehement. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P12C", "p": "part4", "l": "4P12C", "la": "Affectus erga rem, quam scimus in praesenti non existere et quam ut contingentem imaginamur, multo remissior est, quam si rem in praesenti nobis adesse imaginaremur.", "en": "An affect towards a thing which we know not to exist at present, and which we imagine as contingent, is much less intense than if we imagined the thing as now present to us."}, {"id": "E4P12CD", "p": "part4", "l": "4P12CD", "la": "Affectus erga rem, quam in praesenti existere imaginamur, intensior est, quam si eandem ut futuram imaginaremur (per coroll. prop. 9. huius), et multo vehementior est, quam si tempus futurum a praesenti multum distare imaginaremur (per prop. 10. huius). Est itaque affectus erga rem, cuius existendi tempus longe a praesenti distare imaginamur, multo remissior, quam si eandem ut praesentem imaginaremur et nihilominus (per prop. praec.) intensior est quam si eandem rem ut contingentem imaginaremur. Atque adeo affectus erga rem contingentem multo remissior erit, quam si rem in praesenti nobis adesse imaginaremur. Q. E. D.", "en": "An affect towards a thing which we imagine to exist at present is more intense than if we imagined it as future (by Cor. of Prop. 9 of this Part), and much more vehement than if we imagined the future time to be far distant from the present (by Prop. 10 of this Part). The affect towards a thing whose time of existing we imagine to be far distant from the present is therefore much less intense than if we imagined it as present; and nevertheless (by the preceding Prop.) it is more intense than if we imagined the same thing as contingent. And so an affect towards a contingent thing will be much less intense than if we imagined the thing as now present to us. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P13", "p": "part4", "l": "4P13", "la": "Affectus erga rem contingentem, quam scimus in praesenti non existere, ceteris paribus remissior est, quam affectus erga rem praeteritam.", "en": "An affect towards a contingent thing which we know not to exist at present is, other things being equal, less intense than an affect towards a past thing."}, {"id": "E4P13D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P13D", "la": "Quatenus rem ut contingentem imaginamur, nulla alterius rei imagine afficimur, quae rei existentiam ponat (per defin. 3. huius). Sed contra (secundum hypothesin) quaedam imaginamur, quae eiusdem praesentem existentiam secludunt. Verum quatenus eandem cum relatione ad tempus praeteritum imaginamur, eatenus aliquid imaginari supponimur, quod ipsam ad memoriam redigit sive quod rei imaginem excitat (vide prop. 18. P. 2. cum eiusdem schol.), ac proinde eatenus efficit, ut ipsam ac si praesens esset contemplemur (per coroll. prop. 17. P. 2.). Atque adeo (per prop. 9. huius) affectus erga rem contingentem, quam scimus in praesenti non existere, ceteris paribus remissior erit, quam affectus erga rem praeteritam. Q. E. D.", "en": "Insofar as we imagine a thing as contingent, we are affected by no image of another thing which posits the thing’s existence (by Def. 3 of this Part). On the contrary (according to the hypothesis), we imagine certain things which exclude its present existence. But insofar as we imagine it with relation to past time, to that extent we are supposed to imagine something which brings it back to memory, or which arouses the image of the thing (see Prop. 18 of Part II with its Schol.), and which accordingly to that extent brings it about that we contemplate it as if it were present (by Cor. of Prop. 17 of Part II). And so (by Prop. 9 of this Part) an affect towards a contingent thing which we know not to exist at present will be, other things being equal, less intense than an affect towards a past thing. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P14", "p": "part4", "l": "4P14", "la": "Vera boni et mali cognitio, quatenus vera, nullum affectum coercere potest; sed tantum quatenus ut affectus consideratur.", "en": "The true knowledge of good and evil, insofar as it is true, cannot restrain any affect, but only insofar as it is considered as an affect."}, {"id": "E4P14D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P14D", "la": "Affectus est idea, qua mens maiorem vel minorem sui corporis existendi vim, quam antea, affirmat (per gener. aff. defin.); atque adeo (per prop. 1. huius) nihil positivum habet, quod praesentia veri tolli possit; et consequenter vera boni et mali cognitio, quatenus vera, nullum affectum coercere potest. At quatenus affectus est (vide prop. 8. huius), si fortior affectu coercendo sit, eatenus tantum (per prop. 7. huius) affectum coercere poterit. Q. E. D.", "en": "An affect is an idea by which the mind affirms of its body a greater or lesser force of existing than before (by the General Definition of the Affects); and so (by Prop. 1 of this Part) it has nothing positive which could be removed by the presence of the true; and consequently the true knowledge of good and evil, insofar as it is true, cannot restrain any affect. But insofar as it is an affect (see Prop. 8 of this Part), if it is stronger than the affect to be restrained, to that extent only (by Prop. 7 of this Part) will it be able to restrain the affect. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P15", "p": "part4", "l": "4P15", "la": "Cupiditas, quae ex vera boni et mali cognitione oritur, multis aliis cupiditatibus, quae ex affectibus, quibus conflictamur, oriuntur, restingui vel coerceri potest.", "en": "A desire which arises from the true knowledge of good and evil can be extinguished or restrained by many other desires which arise from the affects by which we are troubled."}, {"id": "E4P15D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P15D", "la": "Ex vera boni et mali cognitione, quatenus haec (per prop. 8. huius) affectus est, oritur necessario cupiditas (per aff. defin. 1.), quae eo est maior, quo affectus, ex quo oritur, maior est (per prop. 37. P. 3.). Sed quia haec cupiditas (per hypothesin) ex eo, quod aliquid vere intelligimus, oritur, sequitur ergo ipsa in nobis, quatenus agimus (per prop. 3. P. 3.). Atque adeo per solam nostram essentiam debet intelligi (per defin. 2. P. 3.); et consequenter (per prop. 7. P. 3.) eius vis et incrementum sola humana potentia definiri debet.", "en": "From the true knowledge of good and evil, insofar as this (by Prop. 8 of this Part) is an affect, there necessarily arises a desire (by Def. Aff. 1), which is the greater, the greater the affect from which it arises (by Prop. 37 of Part III). But because this desire (by hypothesis) arises from the fact that we understand something truly, it follows in us insofar as we act (by Prop. 3 of Part III). And so it must be understood through our essence alone (by Def. 2 of Part III); and consequently (by Prop. 7 of Part III) its force and increase must be defined by human power alone."}, {"id": "E4P15D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P15D", "la": "Porro cupiditates, quae ex affectibus, quibus conflictamur, oriuntur, eo etiam maiores sunt, quo hi affectus vehementiores erunt; atque adeo earum vis et incrementum (per prop. 5. huius) potentia causarum externarum definiri debet, quae, si cum nostra comparetur, nostram potentiam indefinite superat (per prop. 3. huius). Atque adeo cupiditates, quae ex similibus affectibus oriuntur, vehementiores esse possunt illa, quae ex vera boni et mali cognitione oritur; ac proinde (per prop. 7. huius) eandem coercere vel restinguere poterunt. Q. E. D.", "en": "Further, the desires which arise from the affects by which we are troubled are also the greater, the more vehement these affects are; and so their force and increase (by Prop. 5 of this Part) must be defined by the power of external causes, which, if it is compared with ours, indefinitely surpasses our power (by Prop. 3 of this Part). And so the desires which arise from such affects can be more vehement than the one which arises from the true knowledge of good and evil, and accordingly (by Prop. 7 of this Part) they will be able to restrain or extinguish it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P16", "p": "part4", "l": "4P16", "la": "Cupiditas, quae ex cognitione boni et mali, quatenus haec cognitio futurum respicit, oritur, facilius rerum cupiditate, quae in praesentia suaves sunt, coerceri vel restingui potest.", "en": "A desire which arises from the knowledge of good and evil, insofar as this knowledge looks to the future, can be more easily restrained or extinguished by a desire for things which are pleasant in the present."}, {"id": "E4P16D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P16D", "la": "Affectus erga rem, quam futuram imaginamur, remissior est, quam erga praesentem (per coroll. prop. 9. huius). At cupiditas, quae ex vera boni et mali cognitione oritur, tametsi haec cognitio circa res, quae in praesentia bonae sunt, versetur, restingui vel coerceri potest aliqua temeraria cupiditate (per prop. praeced. cuius demonstratio universalis est). Ergo cupiditas, quae ex eadem cognitione, quatenus haec futurum respicit, oritur, facilius coerceri vel restingui poterit etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "An affect towards a thing which we imagine as future is less intense than one towards a present thing (by Cor. of Prop. 9 of this Part). But a desire which arises from the true knowledge of good and evil, even if this knowledge concerns things which are good in the present, can be extinguished or restrained by some rash desire (by the preceding Prop., whose demonstration is universal). Therefore a desire which arises from the same knowledge, insofar as this looks to the future, will be able to be restrained or extinguished more easily, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P17", "p": "part4", "l": "4P17", "la": "Cupiditas, quae oritur ex vera boni et mali cognitione, quatenus haec circa res contingentes versatur, multo adhuc facilius coerceri potest cupiditate rerum, quae praesentes sunt.", "en": "A desire which arises from the true knowledge of good and evil, insofar as this concerns contingent things, can be restrained still much more easily by a desire for things which are present."}, {"id": "E4P17D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P17D", "la": "Propositio haec eodem modo, ac prop. praeced. demonstratur ex coroll. prop. 12. huius.", "en": "This Proposition is demonstrated in the same way as the preceding one, from Cor. of Prop. 12 of this Part."}, {"id": "E4P17S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P17S", "la": "His me causam ostendisse credo, cur homines opinione magis, quam vera ratione commoveantur, et cur vera boni et mali cognitio animi commotiones excitet et saepe omni libidinis generi cedat. Unde illud poetae natum: Video meliora proboque, deteriora sequor. Quod idem etiam Ecclesiastes in mente habuisse videtur, cum dixit: Qui auget scientiam, auget dolorem.", "en": "With these I believe I have shown the cause why men are moved more by opinion than by true reason, and why the true knowledge of good and evil arouses disturbances of the mind and often yields to every kind of lust. Hence the saying of the poet: I see and approve the better, I follow the worse. Ecclesiastes too seems to have had the same thing in mind when he said: He who increases knowledge increases pain."}, {"id": "E4P17S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P17S", "la": "Atque haec non eum in finem dico, ut inde concludam, praestabilius esse ignorare, quam scire, vel quod stulto intelligens in moderandis affectibus nihil intersit; sed ideo quia necesse est, nostrae naturae tam potentiam, quam impotentiam noscere, ut determinare possimus, quid ratio in moderandis affectibus possit et quid non possit; et in hac parte de sola humana impotentia me acturum dixi. Nam de rationis in affectus potentia separatim agere constitui.", "en": "I do not say these things in order to conclude from them that it is better to be ignorant than to know, or that there is no difference between a fool and a man of understanding in moderating the affects, but because it is necessary to know both the power and the lack of power of our nature, so that we can determine what reason can do in moderating the affects and what it cannot do; and I said that in this Part I would treat only of human lack of power. For I have decided to treat of the power of reason over the affects separately."}, {"id": "E4P18", "p": "part4", "l": "4P18", "la": "Cupiditas, quae ex laetitia oritur, ceteris paribus fortior est cupiditate, quae ex tristitia oritur.", "en": "A desire which arises from joy is, other things being equal, stronger than a desire which arises from sadness."}, {"id": "E4P18D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P18D", "la": "Cupiditas est ipsa hominis essentia (per aff. defin. 1.) hoc est (per prop. 7. P. 3.) conatus, quo homo in suo esse perseverare conatur. Quare cupiditas, quae ex laetitia oritur, ipso laetitiae affectu (per defin. laetitiae, quam vide in schol. prop. 11. P. 3.) iuvatur vel augetur; quae autem contra ex tristitia oritur, ipso tristitiae affectu (per idem schol.) minuitur vel coercetur. Atque adeo vis cupiditatis, quae ex laetitia oritur, potentia humana, simul et potentia causae externae, quae autem ex tristitia, sola humana potentia definiri debet; ac proinde hac illa fortior est. Q. E. D.", "en": "Desire is the very essence of man (by Def. Aff. 1), that is (by Prop. 7 of Part III), the striving by which a man strives to persevere in his being. So a desire which arises from joy is aided or increased by the affect of joy itself (by the definition of joy, which see in Schol. of Prop. 11 of Part III); but one which arises from sadness, on the contrary, is diminished or restrained by the affect of sadness itself (by the same Schol.). And so the force of a desire which arises from joy must be defined by human power and at the same time by the power of the external cause, whereas that of one which arises from sadness must be defined by human power alone; and accordingly the former is stronger than the latter. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P18S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P18S", "la": "His paucis humanae impotentiae et inconstantiae causas, et cur homines rationis praecepta non servent, explicui. Superest iam, ut ostendam, quid id sit, quod ratio nobis praescribit, et quinam affectus cum rationis humanae regulis conveniant, quinam contra iisdem contrarii sint. Sed antequam haec prolixo nostro geometrico ordine demonstrare incipiam, lubet ipsa *rationis dictamina* hic prius breviter ostendere, ut ea, quae sentio, facilius ab unoquoque percipiantur.", "en": "With these few words I have explained the causes of human lack of power and inconstancy, and why men do not keep the precepts of reason. It remains now for me to show what it is that reason prescribes to us, and which affects agree with the rules of human reason, and which, on the other hand, are contrary to them. But before I begin to demonstrate these things in our prolix geometrical order, I should like first to show briefly here the dictates of reason themselves, so that what I think may be more easily grasped by everyone."}, {"id": "E4P18S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P18S", "la": "Cum ratio nihil contra naturam postulet, postulat ergo ipsa, ut unusquisque seipsum amet, suum utile, quod revera utile est, quaerat, et id omne, quod hominem ad maiorem perfectionem revera ducit, appetat, et absolute ut unusquisque suum esse, quantum in se est, conservare conetur. Quod quidem tam necessario verum est, quam quod totum sit sua parte maius (vide prop. 4. P. 3.).", "en": "Since reason demands nothing contrary to nature, it demands, therefore, that each man love himself, seek his own advantage, what is really useful to him, and want everything which really leads man to a greater perfection, and, absolutely, that each strive to preserve his own being as far as it is in him. And this is indeed as necessarily true as that the whole is greater than its part (see Prop. 4 of Part III)."}, {"id": "E4P18S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P18S", "la": "Deinde quandoquidem virtus (per defin. 8. huius) nihil aliud est, quam ex legibus propriae naturae agere, et nemo suum esse (per prop. 7. P. 3.) conservare conetur, nisi ex propriae suae naturae legibus, hinc sequitur primo, virtutis fundamentum esse ipsum conatum proprium esse conservandi, et felicitatem in eo consistere quod homo suum esse conservare potest. Secundo sequitur, virtutem propter se esse appetendam, nec quicquam, quod ipsa praestabilius aut quod utilius nobis sit, dari, cuius causa deberet appeti. Tertio denique sequitur, eos, qui se interficiunt, animo esse impotentes, eosque a causis externis suae naturae repugnantibus prorsus vinci.", "en": "Next, since virtue (by Def. 8 of this Part) is nothing but acting from the laws of one’s own nature, and no one strives to preserve his being (by Prop. 7 of Part III) except from the laws of his own nature, it follows, first, that the foundation of virtue is the very striving to preserve one’s own being, and that happiness consists in a man’s being able to preserve his being. It follows, secondly, that virtue is to be wanted for its own sake, and that there is nothing more excellent than it, or more useful to us, for the sake of which it ought to be wanted. It follows, finally, thirdly, that those who kill themselves lack power of mind and are completely conquered by external causes opposed to their nature."}, {"id": "E4P18S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P18S", "la": "Porro ex postul. 4. P. 2. sequitur, nos efficere nunquam posse, ut nihil extra nos indigeamus ad nostrum esse conservandum, et ut ita vivamus, ut nullum commercium cum rebus quae extra nos sunt, habeamus; et si praeterea nostram mentem spectemus, sane noster intellectus imperfectior esset, si mens sola esset, nec quicquam praeter se ipsam intelligeret. Multa igitur extra nos dantur, quae nobis utilia quaeque propterea appetenda sunt. Ex his nulla praestantiora excogitari possunt, quam ea quae cum nostra natura prorsus conveniunt.", "en": "Further, from Post. 4 of Part II it follows that we can never bring it about that we need nothing outside ourselves to preserve our being, or that we live without any commerce with things which are outside us; and if, moreover, we consider our mind, our intellect would certainly be less perfect if the mind were alone and understood nothing except itself. There are, therefore, many things outside us which are useful to us, and which are therefore to be wanted. Of these none more excellent can be thought of than those which agree entirely with our nature."}, {"id": "E4P18S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P18S", "la": "Si enim duo ex. gr. eiusdem prorsus naturae individua invicem iunguntur, individuum componunt singulo duplo potentius. Homini igitur nihil homine utilius; nihil, inquam, homines praestantius ad suum esse conservandum optare possunt, quam quod omnes in omnibus ita conveniant, ut omnium mentes et corpora unam quasi mentem, unumque corpus componant, et omnes simul, quantum possunt, suum esse conservare conentur, omnesque simul omnium commune utile sibi quaerant.", "en": "For if, for example, two individuals of entirely the same nature are joined to one another, they compose an individual twice as powerful as each one. To man, therefore, nothing is more useful than man; nothing, I say, can men wish for that is more excellent for preserving their being than that all should so agree in all things that the minds and bodies of all should compose, as it were, one mind and one body, and that all together should strive, as far as they can, to preserve their being, and that all together should seek for themselves the common advantage of all."}, {"id": "E4P18S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P18S", "la": "Ex quibus sequitur, homines, qui ratione gubernantur, hoc est, homines qui ex ductu rationis suum utile quaerunt, nihil sibi appetere, quod reliquis hominibus non cupiant, atque adeo eosdem iustos, fidos atque honestos esse.", "en": "From this it follows that men who are governed by reason, that is, men who seek their own advantage from the guidance of reason, want nothing for themselves which they do not desire for other men, and so are just, faithful and honourable."}, {"id": "E4P18S.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P18S", "la": "Haec illa rationis dictamina sunt, quae hic paucis ostendere proposueram, antequam eadem prolixiore ordine demonstrare inciperem, quod ea de causa feci, ut, si fieri posset, eorum attentionem mihi conciliarem, qui credunt, hoc principium, quod scilicet unusquisque suum utile quaerere tenetur, impietatis, non autem virtutis et pietatis esse fundamentum. Postquam igitur rem sese contra habere breviter ostenderim, pergo ad eandem eadem via, qua hucusque progressi sumus, demonstrandum.", "en": "These are those dictates of reason which I had proposed to show here in a few words before beginning to demonstrate them in a more prolix order, which I did in order to win, if possible, the attention of those who believe that this principle, that each is bound to seek his own advantage, is the foundation of impiety, not of virtue and piety. Having, therefore, shown briefly that the matter is the other way round, I go on to demonstrate it by the same way by which we have proceeded so far."}, {"id": "E4P19", "p": "part4", "l": "4P19", "la": "Id unusquisque ex legibus suae naturae necessario appetit vel aversatur, quod bonum vel malum esse iudicat.", "en": "Each one, from the laws of his own nature, necessarily wants or is averse to what he judges to be good or evil."}, {"id": "E4P19D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P19D", "la": "Boni et mali cognitio est (per prop. 8. huius) ipse laetitiae vel tristitiae affectus quatenus eiusdem sumus conscii; ac proinde (per prop. 28. P. 3.) id unusquisque necessario appetit, quod bonum, et contra id aversatur, quod malum esse iudicat. Sed hic appetitus nihil aliud est, quam ipsa hominis essentia seu natura (per defin. appetitus, quam vide in schol. prop. 9. P. 3. et aff. defin. 1.). Ergo unusquisque ex solis suae naturae legibus id necessario appetit vel aversatur etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "The knowledge of good and evil is (by Prop. 8 of this Part) the affect of joy or sadness itself, insofar as we are conscious of it; and accordingly (by Prop. 28 of Part III) each one necessarily wants what he judges to be good, and on the contrary is averse to what he judges to be evil. But this appetite is nothing but the very essence, or nature, of man (by the definition of appetite, which see in Schol. of Prop. 9 of Part III, and Def. Aff. 1). Therefore each one, from the laws of his own nature alone, necessarily wants or is averse to, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P20", "p": "part4", "l": "4P20", "la": "Quo magis unusquisque suum utile quaerere, hoc est, suum esse conservare conatur et potest, eo magis virtute praeditus est; et contra, quatenus unusquisque suum utile, hoc est, suum esse conservare negligit, eatenus est impotens.", "en": "The more each one strives, and is able, to seek his own advantage, that is, to preserve his being, the more he is endowed with virtue; and on the contrary, insofar as each one neglects his own advantage, that is, to preserve his being, to that extent he lacks power."}, {"id": "E4P20D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P20D", "la": "Virtus est ipsa humana potentia, quae sola hominis essentia definitur (per defin. 8. huius), hoc est (per prop. 7. P. 3.), quae solo conatu, quo homo in suo esse perseverare conatur, definitur. Quo ergo unusquisque magis suum esse conservare conatur et potest, eo magis virtute praeditus est, et consequenter (per prop. 4. et 6. P. 3.), quatenus aliquis suum esse conservare negligit, eatenus est impotens. Q. E. D.", "en": "Virtue is human power itself, which is defined by the essence of man alone (by Def. 8 of this Part), that is (by Prop. 7 of Part III), which is defined by the striving alone by which man strives to persevere in his being. The more, therefore, each one strives, and is able, to preserve his being, the more he is endowed with virtue, and consequently (by Props. 4 and 6 of Part III), insofar as someone neglects to preserve his being, to that extent he lacks power. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P20S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P20S", "la": "Nemo igitur nisi a causis externis et suae naturae contrariis victus suum utile appetere sive suum esse conservare negligit. Nemo, inquam, ex necessitate suae naturae, sed a causis externis coactus alimenta aversatur vel se ipsum interficit, quod multis modis fieri potest.", "en": "No one, therefore, neglects to want his own advantage, or to preserve his being, unless he is conquered by external causes contrary to his nature. No one, I say, from the necessity of his own nature, but only when compelled by external causes, is averse to food or kills himself, which can happen in many ways."}, {"id": "E4P20S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P20S", "la": "Nempe interficit aliquis se ipsum coactus ab alio, qui eius dexteram, qua ensem casu prehenderat, contorquet et cogit versus cor ipsum gladium dirigere; vel quod ex mandato tyranni, ut Seneca, cogatur venas aperire suas, hoc est maius malum minore vitare cupiat; vel denique ex eo, quod causae latentes externae eius imaginationem ita disponunt et corpus ita afficiunt, ut id aliam naturam priori contrariam induat et cuius idea in mente dari nequit (per prop. 10. P. 3.). At quod homo ex necessitate suae naturae conetur non existere vel in aliam formam mutari, tam est impossibile, quam quod ex nihilo aliquid fiat, ut unusquisque mediocri meditatione videre potest.", "en": "Namely, someone kills himself compelled by another, who twists the right hand in which he happened to have taken up a sword and compels him to direct the sword against his own heart; or because he is compelled by the command of a tyrant, as Seneca was, to open his veins, that is, he desires to avoid a greater evil by a lesser; or finally because hidden external causes so dispose his imagination and so affect his body that it takes on another nature, contrary to the former, of which there cannot be an idea in the mind (by Prop. 10 of Part III). But that a man should, from the necessity of his own nature, strive not to exist, or to be changed into another form, is as impossible as that something should come from nothing, as anyone can see with a little meditation."}, {"id": "E4P21", "p": "part4", "l": "4P21", "la": "Nemo potest cupere beatum esse, bene agere et bene vivere, qui simul non cupiat esse, agere et vivere, hoc est, actu existere.", "en": "No one can desire to be blessed, to act well and to live well, who does not at the same time desire to be, to act and to live, that is, actually to exist."}, {"id": "E4P21D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P21D", "la": "Huius propositionis demonstratio seu potius res ipsa per se patet, et etiam ex cupiditatis definitione. Est enim cupiditas (per aff. defin. 1.) beate seu bene vivendi, agendi etc. ipsa hominis essentia, hoc est (per prop. 7. P. 3.), conatus, quo unusquisque suum esse conservare conatur. Ergo nemo potest cupere etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "The demonstration of this proposition, or rather the thing itself, is evident through itself, and also from the definition of desire. For the desire (by Def. Aff. 1) of living blessedly, or well, of acting, etc., is the very essence of man, that is (by Prop. 7 of Part III), the striving by which each one strives to preserve his being. Therefore no one can desire, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P22", "p": "part4", "l": "4P22", "la": "Nulla virtus potest prior hac (nempe conatu sese conservandi) concipi.", "en": "No virtue can be conceived prior to this one (namely, the striving to preserve oneself)."}, {"id": "E4P22D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P22D", "la": "Conatus sese conservandi est ipsa rei essentia (per prop. 7. P. 3.). Si igitur aliqua virtus posset hac, nempe hoc conatu, prior concipi, conciperetur ergo (per defin. 8. huius) ipsa rei essentia se ipsa prior; quod (ut per se notum) est absurdum. Ergo nulla virtus etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "The striving to preserve oneself is the very essence of a thing (by Prop. 7 of Part III). If, therefore, some virtue could be conceived prior to this one, namely, to this striving, the very essence of the thing would then be conceived (by Def. 8 of this Part) prior to itself, which (as is self-evident) is absurd. Therefore no virtue, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P22C", "p": "part4", "l": "4P22C", "la": "Conatus sese conservandi primum et unicum virtutis est fundamentum. Nam hoc principio nullum aliud potest prius concipi (per prop. praeced.), et absque ipso (per prop. 21. huius) nulla virtus potest concipi.", "en": "The striving to preserve oneself is the first and only foundation of virtue. For no other principle can be conceived prior to this one (by the preceding Prop.), and without it (by Prop. 21 of this Part) no virtue can be conceived."}, {"id": "E4P23", "p": "part4", "l": "4P23", "la": "Homo quatenus ad aliquid agendum determinatur ex eo, quod ideas habet inadaequatas, non potest absolute dici ex virtute agere; sed tantum quatenus determinatur ex eo, quod intelligit.", "en": "A man, insofar as he is determined to do something from the fact that he has inadequate ideas, cannot absolutely be said to act from virtue, but only insofar as he is determined from the fact that he understands."}, {"id": "E4P23D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P23D", "la": "Quatenus homo ad agendum determinatur ex eo, quod inadaequatas habet ideas, eatenus (per prop. 1. P. 3.) patitur, hoc est (per defin. 1. et 2. P. 3.) aliquid agit, quod per solam eius essentiam non potest percipi; hoc est (per defin. 8. huius), quod ex ipsius virtute non sequitur. At quatenus ad aliquid agendum determinatur ex eo, quod intelligit, eatenus (per eandem prop. 1. P. 3.) agit, hoc est (per defin. 2. P. 3.) aliquid agit, quod per solam ipsius essentiam percipitur, sive (per defin. 8. huius) quod ex ipsius virtute adaequate sequitur. Q. E. D.", "en": "Insofar as a man is determined to act from the fact that he has inadequate ideas, to that extent (by Prop. 1 of Part III) he is acted on, that is (by Defs. 1 and 2 of Part III), he does something which cannot be perceived through his essence alone, that is (by Def. 8 of this Part), which does not follow from his virtue. But insofar as he is determined to do something from the fact that he understands, to that extent (by the same Prop. 1 of Part III) he acts, that is (by Def. 2 of Part III), he does something which is perceived through his essence alone, or (by Def. 8 of this Part) which follows adequately from his virtue. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P24", "p": "part4", "l": "4P24", "la": "Ex virtute absolute agere nihil aliud in nobis est, quam ex ductu rationis agere, vivere, suum esse conservare (haec tria idem significant) idque ex fundamento proprium utile quaerendi.", "en": "To act absolutely from virtue is nothing else in us but to act, to live, to preserve our being (these three signify the same) from the guidance of reason, and that from the foundation of seeking one’s own advantage."}, {"id": "E4P24D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P24D", "la": "Ex virtute absolute agere nihil aliud est (per defin. 8. huius), quam ex legibus propriae naturae agere. At nos eatenus tantummodo agimus, quatenus intelligimus (per prop. 3. P. 3.). Ergo ex virtute agere nihil aliud in nobis est, quam ex ductu rationis agere, vivere, suum esse conservare idque (per coroll. prop. 22. huius) ex fundamento suum utile quaerendi. Q. E. D.", "en": "To act absolutely from virtue is nothing else (by Def. 8 of this Part) but to act from the laws of one’s own nature. But we act only insofar as we understand (by Prop. 3 of Part III). Therefore to act from virtue is nothing else in us but to act, to live, to preserve our being from the guidance of reason, and that (by Cor. of Prop. 22 of this Part) from the foundation of seeking one’s own advantage. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P25", "p": "part4", "l": "4P25", "la": "Nemo suum esse alterius rei causa conservare conatur.", "en": "No one strives to preserve his being for the sake of another thing."}, {"id": "E4P25D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P25D", "la": "Conatus, quo unaquaeque res in suo esse perseverare conatur, sola ipsius rei essentia definitur (per prop. 7. P. 3.), eaque sola data, non autem ex alterius rei essentia necessario sequitur (per prop. 6. P. 3.), ut unusquisque suum esse conservare conetur. Patet praeterea haec propositio ex coroll. prop. 22. huius partis. Nam si homo alterius rei causa suum esse conservare conaretur, tum res illa primum esset virtutis fundamentum (ut per se notum), quod (per praedict. coroll.) est absurdum. Ergo nemo suum esse etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "The striving by which each thing strives to persevere in its being is defined by the essence of the thing itself alone (by Prop. 7 of Part III), and from this alone being given, and not from the essence of another thing, it necessarily follows (by Prop. 6 of Part III) that each one strives to preserve his being. This Proposition is evident, moreover, from Cor. of Prop. 22 of this Part. For if a man strove to preserve his being for the sake of another thing, that thing would then be the first foundation of virtue (as is self-evident), which (by the aforesaid Cor.) is absurd. Therefore no one strives to preserve his being, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P26", "p": "part4", "l": "4P26", "la": "Quicquid ex ratione conamur, nihil aliud est quam intelligere; nec mens quatenus ratione utitur, aliud sibi utile esse iudicat nisi id quod ad intelligendum conducit.", "en": "Whatever we strive for from reason is nothing but understanding; and the mind, insofar as it uses reason, judges nothing else to be useful to itself except what leads to understanding."}, {"id": "E4P26D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P26D", "la": "Conatus sese conservandi nihil est praeter ipsius rei essentiam (per prop. 7. P. 3.), quae quatenus talis existit, vim habere concipitur ad perseverandum in existendo (per prop. 6. P. 3.), et ea agendum, quae ex data sua natura necessario sequuntur (vide defin. appetitus in schol. prop. 9. P. 3.). At rationis essentia nihil aliud est quam mens nostra quatenus clare et distincte intelligit (vide eius defin. in schol. 2. prop. 40. P. 2.). Ergo (per prop. 40. P. 2.) quicquid ex ratione conamur, nihil aliud est quam intelligere.", "en": "The striving to preserve oneself is nothing but the essence of the thing itself (by Prop. 7 of Part III), which, insofar as it exists as such, is conceived to have a force for persevering in existing (by Prop. 6 of Part III) and for doing those things which necessarily follow from its given nature (see the definition of appetite in Schol. of Prop. 9 of Part III). But the essence of reason is nothing but our mind insofar as it understands clearly and distinctly (see its definition in Schol. 2 of Prop. 40 of Part II). Therefore (by Prop. 40 of Part II) whatever we strive for from reason is nothing but understanding."}, {"id": "E4P26D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P26D", "la": "Deinde quoniam hic mentis conatus, quo mens quatenus ratiocinatur, suum esse conatur conservare, nihil aliud est quam intelligere (per primam partem huius), est ergo hic intelligendi conatus (per coroll. prop. 22. huius) primum et unicum virtutis fundamentum, nec alicuius finis causa (per prop. 25. huius) res intelligere conabimur; sed contra mens quatenus ratiocinatur, nihil sibi bonum esse concipere poterit, nisi id quod ad intelligendum conducit (per defin. 1. huius). Q. E. D.", "en": "Next, since this striving of the mind, by which the mind, insofar as it reasons, strives to preserve its being, is nothing but understanding (by the first part of this demonstration), this striving for understanding is (by Cor. of Prop. 22 of this Part) the first and only foundation of virtue, and we shall not strive to understand things for the sake of some end (by Prop. 25 of this Part); but on the contrary the mind, insofar as it reasons, will be able to conceive nothing to be good for itself except what leads to understanding (by Def. 1 of this Part). Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P27", "p": "part4", "l": "4P27", "la": "Nihil certo scimus bonum aut malum esse, nisi id quod ad intelligendum revera conducit, vel quod impedire potest quominus intelligamus.", "en": "We know nothing to be certainly good or evil except what really leads to understanding, or what can prevent us from understanding."}, {"id": "E4P27D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P27D", "la": "Mens quatenus ratiocinatur, nihil aliud appetit quam intelligere, nec aliud sibi utile esse iudicat, nisi id quod ad intelligendum conducit (per prop. praeced.). At mens (per prop. 41. et 43. P. 2., cuius etiam schol. vide) rerum certitudinem non habet, nisi quatenus ideas habet adaequatas, sive (quod per schol. prop. 40. P. 2. idem est) quatenus ratiocinatur. Ergo nihil certo scimus bonum esse, nisi id quod ad intelligendum revera conducit; et contra id malum, quod impedire potest quominus intelligamus. Q. E. D.", "en": "The mind, insofar as it reasons, wants nothing but to understand, and judges nothing else to be useful to itself except what leads to understanding (by the preceding Prop.). But the mind (by Props. 41 and 43 of Part II; see also its Schol.) has certainty of things only insofar as it has adequate ideas, or (what by the Schol. of Prop. 40 of Part II is the same) insofar as it reasons. Therefore we know nothing to be certainly good except what really leads to understanding, and on the contrary we know that to be evil which can prevent us from understanding. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P28", "p": "part4", "l": "4P28", "la": "Summum mentis bonum est Dei cognitio, et summa mentis virtus Deum cognoscere.", "en": "The highest good of the mind is the knowledge of God, and the highest virtue of the mind is to know God."}, {"id": "E4P28D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P28D", "la": "Summum, quod mens intelligere potest, Deus est, hoc est (per defin. 6. P. 1.) ens absolute infinitum, et sine quo (per prop. 15. P. 1.) nihil esse neque concipi potest. Adeoque (per prop. 26. et 27. huius) summum mentis utile sive (per defin. 1. huius) bonum est Dei cognitio.", "en": "The highest thing which the mind can understand is God, that is (by Def. 6 of Part I), a being absolutely infinite, and without which (by Prop. 15 of Part I) nothing can either be or be conceived. And so (by Props. 26 and 27 of this Part) the highest advantage, or (by Def. 1 of this Part) good, of the mind is the knowledge of God."}, {"id": "E4P28D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P28D", "la": "Deinde mens quatenus intelligit, eatenus tantum agit (per prop. 1. et 3. P. 3.), et eatenus tantum (per prop. 23. huius) potest absolute dici, quod ex virtute agit. Est igitur mentis absoluta virtus intelligere. At summum, quod mens intelligere potest, Deus est (ut iam iam demonstravimus). Ergo mentis summa virtus est Deum intelligere seu cognoscere. Q. E. D.", "en": "Next, the mind acts only insofar as it understands (by Props. 1 and 3 of Part III), and only to that extent (by Prop. 23 of this Part) can it absolutely be said to act from virtue. The absolute virtue of the mind, therefore, is to understand. But the highest thing which the mind can understand is God (as we have just now demonstrated). Therefore the highest virtue of the mind is to understand, or know, God. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P29", "p": "part4", "l": "4P29", "la": "Res quaecumque singularis, cuius natura a nostra prorsus est diversa, nostram agendi potentiam nec iuvare nec coercere potest, et absolute res nulla potest nobis bona aut mala esse, nisi commune aliquid nobiscum habeat.", "en": "Any singular thing whose nature is entirely different from ours can neither aid nor restrain our power of acting, and absolutely, nothing can be good or evil for us unless it has something in common with us."}, {"id": "E4P29D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P29D", "la": "Cuiuscumque rei singularis, et consequenter (per coroll. prop. 10. P. 2.) hominis potentia, qua existit et operatur, non determinatur nisi ab alia re singulari (per prop. 28. P. 1.), cuius natura (per prop. 6. P. 2.) per idem attributum debet intelligi, per quod natura humana concipitur. Nostra igitur agendi potentia, quomodocumque ea concipiatur, determinari et consequenter iuvari vel coerceri potest potentia alterius rei singularis, quae aliquid commune nobiscum habet, et non potentia rei, cuius natura a nostra prorsus est diversa; et quia id bonum aut malum vocamus, quod causa est laetitiae aut tristitiae (per prop. 8. huius), hoc est (per schol. prop. 11. P. 3.) quod nostram agendi potentiam auget vel minuit, iuvat vel coercet; ergo res, cuius natura a nostra prorsus est diversa, nobis neque bona neque mala esse potest. Q. E. D.", "en": "The power of any singular thing, and consequently (by Cor. of Prop. 10 of Part II) of man, by which it exists and operates, is determined only by another singular thing (by Prop. 28 of Part I), whose nature (by Prop. 6 of Part II) must be understood through the same attribute through which human nature is conceived. Our power of acting, therefore, however it is conceived, can be determined, and consequently aided or restrained, by the power of another singular thing which has something in common with us, and not by the power of a thing whose nature is entirely different from ours; and since we call good or evil what is the cause of joy or sadness (by Prop. 8 of this Part), that is (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of Part III), what increases or diminishes, aids or restrains, our power of acting, a thing whose nature is entirely different from ours can be neither good nor evil for us. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P30", "p": "part4", "l": "4P30", "la": "Res nulla per id, quod cum nostra natura commune habet, potest esse mala; sed quatenus nobis mala est, eatenus est nobis contraria.", "en": "No thing can be evil through what it has in common with our nature; but insofar as it is evil for us, to that extent it is contrary to us."}, {"id": "E4P30D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P30D", "la": "Id malum vocamus, quod causa est tristitiae (per prop. 8. huius), hoc est (per eius defin., quam vide in schol. prop. 11. P. 3.) quod nostram agendi potentiam minuit vel coercet. Si igitur res aliqua per id, quod nobiscum habet commune, nobis esset mala, posset ergo res id ipsum, quod nobiscum commune habet, minuere vel coercere; quod (per prop. 4. P. 3.) est absurdum. Nulla igitur res per id, quod nobiscum commune habet, potest nobis esse mala; sed contra quatenus mala est, hoc est (ut iam iam ostendimus) quatenus nostram agendi potentiam minuere vel coercere potest, eatenus (per prop. 5. P. 3.) nobis est contraria. Q. E. D.", "en": "We call evil what is the cause of sadness (by Prop. 8 of this Part), that is (by its definition, which see in Schol. of Prop. 11 of Part III), what diminishes or restrains our power of acting. If, therefore, some thing were evil for us through what it has in common with us, it could diminish or restrain that very thing which it has in common with us, which (by Prop. 4 of Part III) is absurd. No thing, therefore, can be evil for us through what it has in common with us; but on the contrary, insofar as it is evil, that is (as we have just shown), insofar as it can diminish or restrain our power of acting, to that extent (by Prop. 5 of Part III) it is contrary to us. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P31", "p": "part4", "l": "4P31", "la": "Quatenus res aliqua cum nostra natura convenit, eatenus necessario bona est.", "en": "Insofar as a thing agrees with our nature, to that extent it is necessarily good."}, {"id": "E4P31D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P31D", "la": "Quatenus enim res aliqua cum nostra natura convenit, non potest (per prop. praeced.) esse mala. Erit ergo necessario vel bona vel indifferens. Si hoc ponatur, nempe quod neque bona sit neque mala, nihil ergo (per axiom. 3. huius) ex ipsius natura sequetur, quod nostrae naturae conservationi inservit, hoc est (per hypothesin) quod ipsius rei naturae conservationi inservit. Sed hoc est absurdum (per prop. 6. P. 3.). Erit ergo, quatenus cum nostra natura convenit, necessario bona. Q. E. D.", "en": "For insofar as a thing agrees with our nature, it cannot (by the preceding Prop.) be evil. It will therefore necessarily be either good or indifferent. If the latter is supposed, namely that it is neither good nor evil, then (by Axiom 3 of this Part) nothing will follow from its nature which serves the preservation of our nature, that is (by hypothesis), which serves the preservation of the nature of the thing itself. But this is absurd (by Prop. 6 of Part III). It will therefore, insofar as it agrees with our nature, necessarily be good. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P31C", "p": "part4", "l": "4P31C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, quod quo res aliqua magis cum nostra natura convenit, eo nobis est utilior seu magis bona, et contra quo res aliqua nobis est utilior, eatenus cum nostra natura magis convenit. Nam quatenus cum nostra natura non convenit, erit necessario a nostra natura diversa vel eidem contraria. Si diversa, tum (per prop. 29. huius) neque bona neque mala esse poterit; si autem contraria, erit ergo etiam ei contraria, quae cum nostra natura convenit, hoc est (per prop. praeced.) contraria bono seu mala. Nihil igitur nisi quatenus cum nostra natura convenit, potest esse bonum; atque adeo quo res aliqua magis cum nostra natura convenit, eo est utilior et contra. Q. E. D.", "en": "Hence it follows that the more a thing agrees with our nature, the more useful, or the better, it is for us, and conversely, the more useful a thing is to us, the more it agrees with our nature. For insofar as it does not agree with our nature, it will necessarily be different from our nature or contrary to it. If different, then (by Prop. 29 of this Part) it will be able to be neither good nor evil; but if contrary, it will therefore also be contrary to that which agrees with our nature, that is (by the preceding Prop.), contrary to the good, or evil. Nothing, therefore, can be good except insofar as it agrees with our nature; and so the more a thing agrees with our nature, the more useful it is, and conversely. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P32", "p": "part4", "l": "4P32", "la": "Quatenus homines passionibus sunt obnoxii, non possunt eatenus dici, quod natura conveniant.", "en": "Insofar as men are subject to passions, they cannot, to that extent, be said to agree in nature."}, {"id": "E4P32D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P32D", "la": "Quae natura convenire dicuntur, potentia convenire intelliguntur (per prop. 7. P. 3.); non autem impotentia seu negatione, et consequenter (vide schol. prop. 3. P. 3.) neque etiam passione. Quare homines quatenus passionibus sunt obnoxii, non possunt dici, quod natura conveniant. Q. E. D.", "en": "Things which are said to agree in nature are understood to agree in power (by Prop. 7 of Part III), but not in lack of power, or negation, and consequently (see Schol. of Prop. 3 of Part III) not in passion either. Therefore men, insofar as they are subject to passions, cannot be said to agree in nature. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P32S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P32S", "la": "Res etiam per se patet. Qui enim ait, album et nigrum in eo solummodo convenire, quod neutrum sit rubrum, is absolute affirmat album et nigrum nulla in re convenire. Sic etiam si quis ait, lapidem et hominem in hoc tantum convenire, quod uterque sit finitus, impotens vel quod ex necessitate suae naturae non existit, vel denique quod a potentia causarum externarum indefinite superatur, is omnino affirmat lapidem et hominem nulla in re convenire. Quae enim in sola negatione sive in eo, quod non habent, conveniunt, ea revera nulla in re conveniunt.", "en": "The thing is also evident through itself. For he who says that white and black agree only in this, that neither is red, affirms absolutely that white and black agree in nothing. So also if someone says that a stone and a man agree only in this, that each is finite, lacking in power, or that it does not exist from the necessity of its nature, or finally that it is indefinitely surpassed by the power of external causes, he affirms entirely that a stone and a man agree in nothing. For things which agree only in a negation, or in what they do not have, really agree in nothing."}, {"id": "E4P33", "p": "part4", "l": "4P33", "la": "Homines natura discrepare possunt, quatenus affectibus, qui passiones sunt, conflictantur, et eatenus etiam unus idemque homo varius est et inconstans.", "en": "Men can differ in nature insofar as they are troubled by affects which are passions, and to that extent also one and the same man is changeable and inconstant."}, {"id": "E4P33D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P33D", "la": "Affectuum natura seu essentia non potest per solam nostram essentiam seu naturam explicari (per defin. 1. et 2. P. 3.); sed potentia, hoc est (per prop. 7. P. 3.) natura causarum externarum cum nostra comparata definiri debet. Unde fit, ut uniuscuiusque affectus tot species dentur, quot sunt species obiectorum, a quibus afficimur (vide prop. 56. P. 3.), et ut homines ab uno eodemque obiecto diversimode afficiantur (vide prop. 51. P. 3.), atque eatenus natura discrepent, et denique ut unus idemque homo (per eandem prop. 51. P. 3.) erga idem obiectum diversimode afficiatur, atque eatenus varius sit etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "The nature, or essence, of the affects cannot be explained through our essence, or nature, alone (by Defs. 1 and 2 of Part III), but must be defined by the power, that is (by Prop. 7 of Part III), the nature, of external causes compared with ours. Hence it comes about that there are as many species of each affect as there are species of objects by which we are affected (see Prop. 56 of Part III), and that men are affected in different ways by one and the same object (see Prop. 51 of Part III), and to that extent differ in nature, and finally that one and the same man (by the same Prop. 51 of Part III) is affected in different ways towards the same object, and to that extent is changeable, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P34", "p": "part4", "l": "4P34", "la": "Quatenus homines affectibus, qui passiones sunt, conflictantur, possunt invicem esse contrarii.", "en": "Insofar as men are troubled by affects which are passions, they can be contrary to one another."}, {"id": "E4P34D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P34D", "la": "Homo, ex. gr. Petrus, potest esse causa, ut Paulus contristetur, propterea quod aliquid habet simile rei, quam Paulus odit (per prop. 16. P. 3.); vel propterea, quod Petrus solus re aliqua potitur, quam ipse Paulus etiam amat (vide prop. 32. P. 3. cum eiusdem schol.); vel ob alias causas (harum praecipuas vide in schol. prop. 55. P. 3.). Atque adeo inde fiet (per aff. defin. 7.) ut Paulus Petrum odio habeat, et consequenter facile fiet (per prop. 40. P. 3. cum eius schol.) ut Petrus Paulum contra odio habeat, atque adeo (per prop. 39. P. 3.) ut invicem malum inferre conentur, hoc est (per prop. 30. huius) ut invicem sint contrarii.", "en": "A man, for example Peter, can be the cause of Paul’s being saddened, because he has something like a thing which Paul hates (by Prop. 16 of Part III), or because Peter alone possesses something which Paul himself also loves (see Prop. 32 of Part III with its Schol.), or for other causes (for the chief of these see Schol. of Prop. 55 of Part III). And so from this it will come about (by Def. Aff. 7) that Paul hates Peter, and consequently it will easily come about (by Prop. 40 of Part III with its Schol.) that Peter hates Paul in return, and so (by Prop. 39 of Part III) that they strive to do evil to one another, that is (by Prop. 30 of this Part), that they are contrary to one another."}, {"id": "E4P34D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P34D", "la": "At affectus tristitiae semper passio est (per prop. 59. P. 3.); ergo homines, quatenus conflictantur affectibus, qui passiones sunt, possunt invicem esse contrarii. Q. E. D.", "en": "But the affect of sadness is always a passion (by Prop. 59 of Part III); therefore men, insofar as they are troubled by affects which are passions, can be contrary to one another. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P34S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P34S", "la": "Dixi, quod Paulus odio Petrum habeat, quia imaginatur, id eundem possidere, quod ipse Paulus etiam amat. Unde prima fronte videtur sequi, quod hi duo ex eo, quod idem amant, et consequenter ex eo, quod natura conveniunt, sibi invicem damno sint; atque adeo, si hoc verum est, falsae essent prop. 30. et 31. huius partis. Sed si rem aequa lance examinare velimus, haec omnia convenire omnino videbimus.", "en": "I said that Paul hates Peter because he imagines that Peter possesses what Paul himself also loves. At first sight it seems to follow from this that these two, from the fact that they love the same thing, and consequently from the fact that they agree in nature, are harmful to one another; and so, if this is true, Props. 30 and 31 of this Part would be false. But if we are willing to examine the matter with a fair balance, we shall see that all these things agree entirely."}, {"id": "E4P34S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P34S", "la": "Nam hi duo non sunt invicem molesti, quatenus natura conveniunt, hoc est quatenus uterque idem amat; sed quatenus ab invicem discrepant. Nam quatenus uterque idem amat, eo ipso utriusque amor fovetur (per prop. 31. P. 3.), hoc est (per aff. defin. 6.) eo ipso utriusque laetitia fovetur. Quare longe abest, ut quatenus idem amant et natura conveniunt, invicem molesti sint. Sed huius rei causa, ut dixi, nulla alia est quam quia natura discrepare supponuntur.", "en": "For these two are not troublesome to one another insofar as they agree in nature, that is, insofar as each loves the same thing, but insofar as they differ from one another. For insofar as each loves the same thing, by that very fact the love of each is encouraged (by Prop. 31 of Part III), that is (by Def. Aff. 6), by that very fact the joy of each is encouraged. So it is far from being the case that they are troublesome to one another insofar as they love the same thing and agree in nature. But the cause of this, as I have said, is nothing else but that they are supposed to differ in nature."}, {"id": "E4P34S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P34S", "la": "Supponimus namque Petrum ideam habere rei amatae iam possessae, et Paulum contra ideam rei amatae amissae. Unde fit, ut hic tristitia, et ille contra laetitia afficiatur; atque eatenus invicem contrarii sint. Et ad hunc modum ostendere facile possumus reliquas odii causas ab hoc solo pendere, quod homines natura discrepant, et non ab eo, in quo conveniunt.", "en": "For we suppose that Peter has the idea of a loved thing already possessed, and Paul, on the contrary, the idea of a loved thing lost. Hence it comes about that the latter is affected with sadness and the former, on the contrary, with joy; and to that extent they are contrary to one another. And in this way we can easily show that the other causes of hatred depend only on the fact that men differ in nature, and not on that in which they agree."}, {"id": "E4P35", "p": "part4", "l": "4P35", "la": "Quatenus homines ex ductu rationis vivunt, eatenus tantum natura semper necessario conveniunt.", "en": "Only insofar as men live from the guidance of reason do they always necessarily agree in nature."}, {"id": "E4P35D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P35D", "la": "Quatenus homines affectibus, qui passiones sunt, conflictantur, possunt esse natura diversi (per prop. 33. huius) et invicem contrarii (per prop. praeced.). Sed eatenus homines tantum agere dicuntur, quatenus ex ductu rationis vivunt (per prop. 3. P. 3.); atque adeo quicquid ex humana natura, quatenus ratione definitur, sequitur, id (per defin. 2. P. 3.) per solam humanam naturam, tamquam per proximam suam causam, debet intelligi.", "en": "Insofar as men are troubled by affects which are passions, they can be different in nature (by Prop. 33 of this Part) and contrary to one another (by the preceding Prop.). But men are said to act only insofar as they live from the guidance of reason (by Prop. 3 of Part III); and so whatever follows from human nature insofar as it is defined by reason must (by Def. 2 of Part III) be understood through human nature alone, as through its proximate cause."}, {"id": "E4P35D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P35D", "la": "Sed quia unusquisque ex suae naturae legibus id appetit, quod bonum, et id amovere conatur, quod malum esse iudicat (per prop. 19. huius); et cum praeterea id, quod ex dictamine rationis bonum aut malum esse iudicamus, necessario bonum aut malum sit (per prop. 41. P. 2.), ergo homines quatenus ex ductu rationis vivunt, eatenus tantum ea necessario agunt, quae humanae naturae, et consequenter unicuique homini necessario bona sunt, hoc est (per coroll. prop. 31. huius) quae cum natura uniuscuiusque hominis conveniunt. Atque adeo homines etiam inter se, quatenus ex ductu rationis vivunt, necessario semper conveniunt. Q. E. D.", "en": "But since each one, from the laws of his own nature, wants what he judges to be good and strives to remove what he judges to be evil (by Prop. 19 of this Part), and since, moreover, what we judge from the dictate of reason to be good or evil is necessarily good or evil (by Prop. 41 of Part II), men, insofar as they live from the guidance of reason, to that extent only necessarily do those things which are necessarily good for human nature, and consequently for each man, that is (by Cor. of Prop. 31 of this Part), which agree with the nature of each man. And so men also necessarily always agree with one another insofar as they live from the guidance of reason. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P35C1", "p": "part4", "l": "4P35C1", "la": "Nihil singulare in rerum natura datur, quod homini sit utilius, quam homo qui ex ductu rationis vivit. Nam id homini utilissimum est, quod cum sua natura maxime convenit (per coroll. prop. 31. huius), hoc est (ut per se notum) homo. At homo ex legibus suae naturae absolute agit, quando ex ductu rationis vivit (per defin. 2. P. 3.), et eatenus tantum cum natura alterius hominis necessario semper convenit (per prop. praeced.). Ergo homini nihil inter res singulares utilius datur quam homo etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "There is no singular thing in the nature of things which is more useful to man than a man who lives from the guidance of reason. For what is most useful to man is what most agrees with his nature (by Cor. of Prop. 31 of this Part), that is (as is self-evident), man. But a man acts absolutely from the laws of his nature when he lives from the guidance of reason (by Def. 2 of Part III), and only to that extent does he necessarily always agree with the nature of another man (by the preceding Prop.). Therefore among singular things there is nothing more useful to man than a man, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P35C2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P35C2", "la": "Cum maxime unusquisque homo suum sibi utile quaerit, tum maxime homines sunt sibi invicem utiles. Nam quo magis unusquisque suum utile quaerit et se conservare conatur, eo magis virtute praeditus est (per prop. 20. huius), sive quod idem est (per defin. 8. huius), eo maiore potentia praeditus est ad agendum ex suae naturae legibus, hoc est (per prop. 3. P. 3.) ad vivendum ex ductu rationis. At homines tum maxime natura conveniunt, cum ex ductu rationis vivunt (per prop. praeced.). Ergo (per praeced. coroll.) tum maxime homines erunt sibi invicem utiles, cum maxime unusquisque suum utile sibi quaerit. Q. E. D.", "en": "When each man most seeks his own advantage for himself, then men are most useful to one another. For the more each one seeks his own advantage and strives to preserve himself, the more he is endowed with virtue (by Prop. 20 of this Part), or, what is the same (by Def. 8 of this Part), the greater the power with which he is endowed for acting from the laws of his nature, that is (by Prop. 3 of Part III), for living from the guidance of reason. But men most agree in nature when they live from the guidance of reason (by the preceding Prop.). Therefore (by the preceding Cor.) men will be most useful to one another when each most seeks his own advantage for himself. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P35S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P35S", "la": "Quae modo ostendimus, ipsa etiam experientia quotidie tot tamque luculentis testimoniis testatur, ut omnibus fere in ore sit, hominem homini Deum esse. Fit tamen raro, ut homines ex ductu rationis vivant; sed cum iis ita comparatum est, ut plerumque invidi atque invicem molesti sint. At nihilominus vitam solitariam vix transigere queunt, ita ut plerisque illa definitio, quod homo sit animal sociale, valde arriserit; et revera res ita se habet, ut ex hominum communi societate multo plura commoda oriantur, quam damna.", "en": "What we have just shown, experience itself also testifies daily with so many and such clear testimonies that it is in almost everyone’s mouth that man is a God to man. Yet it rarely happens that men live from the guidance of reason; instead, they are so constituted that they are for the most part envious and troublesome to one another. But nevertheless they can hardly lead a solitary life, so that the definition that man is a social animal has pleased most people very much; and in truth the matter stands so that far more benefits than harms arise from the common society of men."}, {"id": "E4P35S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P35S", "la": "Rideant igitur, quantum velint, res humanas satyrici, easque detestentur theologi, et laudent, quantum possunt, melancholici vitam incultam et agrestem, hominesque contemnant et admirentur bruta; experientur tamen homines mutuo auxilio ea quibus indigent, multo facilius sibi parare, et non nisi iunctis viribus pericula, quae ubique imminent, vitare posse; ut iam taceam, quod multo praestabilius sit et cognitione nostra magis dignum, hominum quam brutorum facta contemplari. Sed de his alias prolixius.", "en": "Let the satirists, then, laugh at human affairs as much as they like, let the theologians detest them, and let the melancholy praise as much as they can an uncultivated and rustic life, and despise men and admire the beasts; men will nevertheless find that they can procure for themselves what they need much more easily by mutual help, and that only by joining forces can they avoid the dangers which threaten everywhere; not to mention that it is much more excellent, and more worthy of our knowledge, to contemplate the deeds of men than those of beasts. But of these things elsewhere at greater length."}, {"id": "E4P36", "p": "part4", "l": "4P36", "la": "Summum bonum eorum, qui virtutem sectantur, omnibus commune est, eoque omnes aeque gaudere possunt.", "en": "The highest good of those who follow virtue is common to all, and all can enjoy it equally."}, {"id": "E4P36D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P36D", "la": "Ex virtute agere est ex ductu rationis agere (per prop. 24. huius), et quicquid ex ratione conamur agere, est intelligere (per prop. 26. huius). Atque adeo (per prop. 28. huius) summum bonum eorum, qui virtutem sectantur, est Deum cognoscere, hoc est (per prop. 47. P. 2. et eiusdem schol.), bonum quod omnibus hominibus commune est, et ab omnibus hominibus, quatenus eiusdem sunt naturae, possideri aeque potest. Q. E. D.", "en": "To act from virtue is to act from the guidance of reason (by Prop. 24 of this Part), and whatever we strive to do from reason is to understand (by Prop. 26 of this Part). And so (by Prop. 28 of this Part) the highest good of those who follow virtue is to know God, that is (by Prop. 47 of Part II and its Schol.), a good which is common to all men and can be possessed equally by all men insofar as they are of the same nature. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P36S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P36S", "la": "Si quis autem roget: quid si summum bonum eorum, qui virtutem sectantur, non esset omnibus commune? an non inde, ut supra (vide prop. 34. huius) sequeretur, quod homines, qui ex ductu rationis vivunt, hoc est (per prop. 35. huius) homines, quatenus natura conveniunt, essent invicem contrarii? Is hoc sibi responsum habeat, non ex accidenti, sed ex ipsa natura rationis oriri, ut hominis summum bonum omnibus sit commune, nimirum, quia ex ipsa humana essentia, quatenus ratione definitur, deducitur; et quia homo nec esse nec concipi posset, si potestatem non haberet gaudendi hoc summo bono. Pertinet namque (per prop. 47. P. 2.) ad mentis humanae essentiam, adaequatam habere cognitionem aeternae et infinitae essentiae Dei.", "en": "But if someone asks: what if the highest good of those who follow virtue were not common to all? Would it not follow from this, as above (see Prop. 34 of this Part), that men who live from the guidance of reason, that is (by Prop. 35 of this Part), men insofar as they agree in nature, would be contrary to one another? Let him have this answer: that it arises not by accident but from the very nature of reason that man’s highest good is common to all, namely because it is deduced from the very essence of man insofar as it is defined by reason, and because man could neither be nor be conceived if he did not have the power of enjoying this highest good. For it pertains (by Prop. 47 of Part II) to the essence of the human mind to have an adequate knowledge of the eternal and infinite essence of God."}, {"id": "E4P37", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37", "la": "Bonum, quod unusquisque, qui sectatur virtutem, sibi appetit, reliquis hominibus etiam cupiet, et eo magis, quo maiorem Dei habuerit cognitionem.", "en": "The good which each one who follows virtue wants for himself he will also desire for other men, and the more so, the greater the knowledge of God he has."}, {"id": "E4P37D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37D", "la": "Homines quatenus ex ductu rationis vivunt, sunt homini utilissimi (per coroll. 1. prop. 35. huius); atque adeo (per prop. 19. huius) ex ductu rationis conabimur necessario efficere, ut homines ex ductu rationis vivant. At bonum, quod unusquisque, qui ex rationis dictamine vivit, hoc est (per prop. 24. huius) qui virtutem sectatur, sibi appetit, est intelligere (per prop. 26. huius). Ergo bonum, quod unusquisque, qui virtutem sectatur, sibi appetit, reliquis hominibus etiam cupiet.", "en": "Men, insofar as they live from the guidance of reason, are most useful to man (by Cor. 1 of Prop. 35 of this Part); and so (by Prop. 19 of this Part) we shall necessarily strive, from the guidance of reason, to bring it about that men live from the guidance of reason. But the good which each one who lives from the dictate of reason, that is (by Prop. 24 of this Part), who follows virtue, wants for himself is to understand (by Prop. 26 of this Part). Therefore the good which each one who follows virtue wants for himself he will also desire for other men."}, {"id": "E4P37D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37D", "la": "Deinde cupiditas, quatenus ad mentem refertur, est ipsa mentis essentia (per aff. defin. 1.); mentis autem essentia in cognitione consistit (per prop. 11. P. 2.) quae Dei cognitionem involvit (per prop. 47. P. 2.), et sine qua (per prop. 15. P. 1.) nec esse nec concipi potest. Adeoque quo mentis essentia maiorem Dei cognitionem involvit, eo cupiditas, qua is, qui virtutem sectatur, bonum, quod sibi appetit, alteri cupit, etiam maior erit. Q. E. D.", "en": "Next, desire, insofar as it is related to the mind, is the very essence of the mind (by Def. Aff. 1); but the essence of the mind consists in knowledge (by Prop. 11 of Part II), which involves the knowledge of God (by Prop. 47 of Part II), and without which (by Prop. 15 of Part I) it can neither be nor be conceived. And so the more the essence of the mind involves knowledge of God, the greater also will be the desire by which one who follows virtue desires for another the good which he wants for himself. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P37D2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37D2", "la": "Bonum, quod homo sibi appetit et amat, constantius amabit, si viderit, alios idem amare (per prop. 31. P. 3.). Atque adeo (per coroll. eiusdem prop.) conabitur, ut reliqui idem ament. Et quia hoc bonum (per prop. praeced.) omnibus commune est, eoque omnes gaudere possunt, conabitur ergo (per eandem rationem) ut omnes eodem gaudeant, et (per prop. 37. P. 3.) eo magis, quo hoc bono magis fruetur. Q. E. D.", "en": "The good which a man wants for himself and loves he will love more constantly if he sees that others love the same thing (by Prop. 31 of Part III). And so (by Cor. of the same Prop.) he will strive that the others love the same thing. And because this good (by the preceding Prop.) is common to all, and all can enjoy it, he will therefore strive (for the same reason) that all may enjoy it, and (by Prop. 37 of Part III) the more so, the more he enjoys this good. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P37S1", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37S1", "la": "Qui ex solo affectu conatur, ut reliqui ament quod ipse amat, et ut reliqui ex ipsius ingenio vivant, solo impetu agit, et ideo odiosus est, praecipue iis, quibus alia placent, quique propterea etiam student et eodem impetu conantur, ut reliqui contra ex ipsorum ingenio vivant. Deinde quoniam summum, quod homines ex affectu appetunt, bonum saepe tale est, ut unus tantum eius possit esse compos, hinc fit, ut qui amant, mente sibi non constent, et dum laudes rei, quam amant, narrare gaudent, timeant credi. At qui reliquos conatur ratione ducere, non impetu, sed humaniter et benigne agit et sibi mente maxime constat.", "en": "He who strives, from affect alone, that others should love what he himself loves, and that others should live according to his temperament, acts from impulse alone, and is therefore hateful, especially to those to whom other things are pleasing, and who therefore also strive eagerly, and with the same impulse, that others should live, on the contrary, according to their temperament. Next, since the highest good which men want from affect is often such that only one can possess it, it comes about that those who love are not at one with themselves in mind, and, while they rejoice to tell the praises of the thing they love, fear to be believed. But he who strives to lead others by reason acts not by impulse but humanely and kindly, and is most at one with himself in mind."}, {"id": "E4P37S1", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37S1", "la": "Porro quicquid cupimus et agimus, cuius causa sumus, quatenus Dei habemus ideam, sive quatenus Deum cognoscimus, ad *religionem* refero. Cupiditatem autem bene faciendi, quae eo ingeneratur, quod ex rationis ductu vivimus, *pietatem* voco. Cupiditatem deinde, qua homo, qui ex ductu rationis vivit, tenetur ut reliquos sibi amicitia iungat, *honestatem* voco, et id *honestum*, quod homines, qui ex ductu rationis vivunt, laudant, et id contra *turpe*, quod conciliandae amicitiae repugnat.", "en": "Further, whatever we desire and do of which we are the cause insofar as we have the idea of God, or insofar as we know God, I refer to religion. The desire to do good which is generated in us by our living from the guidance of reason I call piety. The desire by which a man who lives from the guidance of reason is bound to join others to himself in friendship I call honourableness, and I call that honourable which men who live from the guidance of reason praise, and that, on the contrary, base which is opposed to the forming of friendship."}, {"id": "E4P37S1", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37S1", "la": "Praeter haec civitatis etiam quaenam sint fundamenta ostendi. Differentia deinde inter veram virtutem et impotentiam facile ex supra dictis percipitur: nempe quod vera virtus nihil aliud sit, quam ex solo rationis ductu vivere; atque adeo impotentia in hoc solo consistit, quod homo a rebus, quae extra ipsum sunt, duci se patiatur et ab iis ad ea agendum determinetur, quae rerum externarum communis constitutio, non autem ea, quae ipsa ipsius natura in se sola considerata postulat.", "en": "Besides these things, I have also shown what the foundations of the state are. The difference, next, between true virtue and lack of power is easily perceived from what has been said above: namely, that true virtue is nothing but living from the guidance of reason alone, and so lack of power consists in this alone, that a man allows himself to be led by things which are outside him, and is determined by them to do what the common constitution of external things demands, not what his own nature, considered in itself alone, demands."}, {"id": "E4P37S1", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37S1", "la": "Atque haec illa sunt, quae in schol. prop. 18. huius partis demonstrare promisi, ex quibus apparet legem illam de non mactandis brutis magis vana superstitione et muliebri misericordia, quam sana ratione fundatam esse. Docet quidem ratio nostrum utile quaerendi necessitudinem cum hominibus iungere; sed non cum brutis aut rebus, quarum natura a natura humana est diversa, sed idem ius, quod illa in nos habent, nos in ea habere. Imo quia uniuscuiusque ius virtute seu potentia uniuscuiusque definitur, longe maius homines in bruta, quam haec in homines ius habent.", "en": "And these are the things I promised to demonstrate in Schol. of Prop. 18 of this Part, from which it is evident that the law against slaughtering beasts is founded more on empty superstition and womanish compassion than on sound reason. The principle of seeking our own advantage teaches us, indeed, to form close ties with men, but not with beasts, or with things whose nature is different from human nature; it teaches that we have the same right against them that they have against us. Indeed, because the right of each is defined by the virtue, or power, of each, men have far greater right against beasts than beasts against men."}, {"id": "E4P37S1", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37S1", "la": "Nec tamen nego bruta sentire, sed nego, quod propterea non liceat nostrae utilitati consulere et iisdem ad libitum uti; eademque tractare, prout nobis magis convenit; quandoquidem nobiscum natura non conveniunt et eorum affectus ab affectibus humanis sunt natura diversi. Vide schol. prop. 57. P. 3. Superest, ut explicem, quid iustum, quid iniustum, quid peccatum et quid denique meritum sit. Sed de his vide sequens scholium.", "en": "Yet I do not deny that beasts feel; but I deny that it is therefore not permissible for us to consult our own advantage and to use them at our pleasure, and to treat them as best suits us, since they do not agree with us in nature and their affects are different in nature from human affects. See Schol. of Prop. 57 of Part III. It remains for me to explain what is just, what unjust, what sin, and finally what merit is. But on these see the following Scholium."}, {"id": "E4P37S2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37S2", "la": "In appendice partis primae explicare promisi, quid laus et vituperium, quid meritum et peccatum, quid iustum et iniustum sit. *Laudem* et *vituperium* quod attinet, in schol. prop. 29. P. 3. explicui; de reliquis autem hic iam erit dicendi locus. Sed prius pauca de *statu hominis naturali* et *civili* dicenda sunt.", "en": "In the Appendix of the first Part I promised to explain what praise and blame, merit and sin, the just and the unjust are. Praise and blame I have explained in Schol. of Prop. 29 of Part III; of the rest this will now be the place to speak. But first a few things must be said about the natural and the civil state of man."}, {"id": "E4P37S2.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37S2", "la": "Existit unusquisque summo naturae iure, et consequenter summo naturae iure unusquisque ea agit, quae ex suae naturae necessitate sequuntur; atque adeo summo naturae iure unusquisque iudicat, quid bonum, quid malum sit, suaeque utilitati ex suo ingenio consulit (vide prop. 19. et 20. huius), seseque vindicat (vide coroll. 2. prop. 40. P. 3.), et id quod amat conservare, et id quod odio habet destruere conatur (vide prop. 28. P. 3.).", "en": "Each one exists by the highest right of nature, and consequently by the highest right of nature each one does what follows from the necessity of his own nature; and so by the highest right of nature each one judges what is good and what is evil, and looks to his own advantage according to his own temperament (see Props. 19 and 20 of this Part), and avenges himself (see Cor. 2 of Prop. 40 of Part III), and strives to preserve what he loves and to destroy what he hates (see Prop. 28 of Part III)."}, {"id": "E4P37S2.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37S2", "la": "Quod si homines ex ductu rationis viverent, potiretur unusquisque (per coroll. 1. prop. 35. huius) hoc suo iure absque ullo alterius damno. Sed quia affectibus sunt obnoxii (per coroll. prop. 4. huius), qui potentiam seu virtutem humanam longe superant (per prop. 6. huius), ideo saepe diversi trahuntur (per prop. 33. huius) atque sibi invicem sunt contrarii (per prop. 34. huius), mutuo dum auxilio indigent (per schol. prop. 35. huius). Ut igitur homines concorditer vivere et sibi auxilio esse possint, necesse est, ut iure suo naturali cedant et se invicem securos reddant, se nihil acturos, quod possit in alterius damnum cedere.", "en": "If men lived from the guidance of reason, each would possess this right of his (by Cor. 1 of Prop. 35 of this Part) without any harm to another. But because they are subject to affects (by Cor. of Prop. 4 of this Part), which far surpass human power, or virtue (by Prop. 6 of this Part), they are often drawn in different directions (by Prop. 33 of this Part) and are contrary to one another (by Prop. 34 of this Part), while they need one another’s help (by Schol. of Prop. 35 of this Part). In order, therefore, that men may live harmoniously and be of help to one another, it is necessary that they give up their natural right and make one another confident that they will do nothing which could lead to harm to another."}, {"id": "E4P37S2.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37S2", "la": "Qua autem ratione hoc fieri possit, ut scilicet homines, qui affectibus necessario sunt obnoxii (per coroll. prop. 4. huius) atque inconstantes et varii (per prop. 33. huius), possint se invicem securos reddere et fidem invicem habere, patet ex prop. 7. huius partis et prop. 39. P. 3. Nempe quod nullus affectus coerceri potest, nisi affectu fortiore et contrario affectui coercendo, et quod unusquisque ab inferendo damno abstinet timore maioris damni.", "en": "But how this can come about, namely that men, who are necessarily subject to affects (by Cor. of Prop. 4 of this Part) and inconstant and changeable (by Prop. 33 of this Part), can make one another confident and have trust in one another, is evident from Prop. 7 of this Part and Prop. 39 of Part III: namely, that no affect can be restrained except by an affect stronger than and contrary to the affect to be restrained, and that each one abstains from doing harm from fear of a greater harm."}, {"id": "E4P37S2.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37S2", "la": "Hac igitur lege *societas* firmari poterit, si modo ipsa sibi vindicet ius, quod unusquisque habet, sese vindicandi et de bono et malo iudicandi; quaeque adeo potestatem habeat communem vivendi rationem praescribendi, legesque ferendi, easque non ratione, quae affectus coercere nequit (per schol. prop. 17. huius), sed minis firmandi. Haec autem societas legibus et potestate sese conservandi firmata *civitas* appellatur, et qui ipsius iure defenduntur *cives*.", "en": "By this law, therefore, a society can be established, provided that it claims for itself the right which each one has of avenging himself and of judging good and evil, and that it therefore has the power of prescribing a common rule of life and of making laws, and of enforcing them not by reason, which cannot restrain the affects (by Schol. of Prop. 17 of this Part), but by threats. This society, established by laws and by the power of preserving itself, is called a state, and those who are defended by its right are called citizens."}, {"id": "E4P37S2.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37S2", "la": "Ex quibus facile intelligimus, nihil in statu naturali dari, quod ex omnium consensu bonum aut malum sit, quandoquidem unusquisque, qui in statu est naturali, suae tantummodo utilitati consulit, et ex suo ingenio, et quatenus suae utilitatis tantum habet rationem, quid bonum quidve malum sit, decernit, et nemini nisi sibi soli obtemperare lege ulla tenetur; atque adeo in statu naturali peccatum concipi nequit. At quidem in statu civili, ubi et communi consensu decernitur, quid bonum quidve malum sit, et unusquisque civitati obtemperare tenetur. Est itaque *peccatum* nihil aliud, quam inobedientia, quae propterea solo civitatis iure punitur; et contra obedientia civi *meritum* ducitur, quia eo ipso dignus iudicatur, qui civitatis commodis gaudeat.", "en": "From this we easily understand that in the natural state there is nothing which is good or evil by the consent of all, since each one who is in the natural state looks only to his own advantage, and decides what is good and what evil according to his own temperament, and insofar as he takes account only of his own advantage, and is bound by no law to obey anyone but himself alone; and so in the natural state sin cannot be conceived. It is otherwise in the civil state, where it is decided by common consent what is good and what evil, and each one is bound to obey the state. Sin, therefore, is nothing but disobedience, which is for that reason punished only by the right of the state; and on the other hand obedience is counted as merit in a citizen, because by that very fact he is judged worthy to enjoy the advantages of the state."}, {"id": "E4P37S2.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P37S2", "la": "Deinde in statu naturali nemo ex communi consensu alicuius rei est dominus, nec in natura aliquid datur, quod possit dici huius hominis esse et non illius; sed omnia omnium sunt, ac proinde in statu naturali nulla potest concipi voluntas unicuique suum tribuendi; aut alicui id, quod eius sit, eripiendi, hoc est, in statu naturali nihil fit, quod *iustum* aut *iniustum* possit dici; at quidem in statu civili, ubi ex communi consensu decernitur, quid huius quidve illius sit. Ex quibus apparet, iustum et iniustum, peccatum et meritum notiones esse extrinsecas, non autem attributa, quae mentis naturam explicent. Sed de his satis.", "en": "Next, in the natural state no one is master of anything by common consent, nor is there anything in nature which can be said to be this man’s and not that man’s; but all things are everyone’s; and accordingly in the natural state no will can be conceived of giving to each his own, or of taking from someone what is his; that is, in the natural state nothing is done which can be called just or unjust. It is otherwise in the civil state, where it is decided by common consent what is this man’s and what is that man’s. From which it is evident that just and unjust, sin and merit, are extrinsic notions, and not attributes which explain the nature of the mind. But enough of these things."}, {"id": "E4P38", "p": "part4", "l": "4P38", "la": "Id quod corpus humanum ita disponit, ut pluribus modis possit affici, vel quod idem aptum reddit ad corpora externa pluribus modis afficiendum, homini est utile; et eo utilius, quo corpus ab eo aptius redditur, ut pluribus modis afficiatur, aliaque corpora afficiat; et contra id noxium est, quod corpus ad haec minus aptum reddit.", "en": "What so disposes the human body that it can be affected in many ways, or renders it capable of affecting external bodies in many ways, is useful to man; and the more useful, the more capable it renders the body of being affected in many ways and of affecting other bodies; and on the contrary, that is harmful which renders the body less capable of these things."}, {"id": "E4P38D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P38D", "la": "Quo corpus ad haec aptius redditur, eo mens aptior ad percipiendum redditur (per prop. 14. P. 2.); adeoque id, quod corpus hac ratione disponit aptumque ad haec reddit, est necessario bonum seu utile (per prop. 26. et 27. huius), et eo utilius, quo corpus ad haec aptius potest reddere; et contra (per eandem prop. 14. P. 2. inversam et prop. 26. et 27. huius) noxium, si corpus ad haec minus aptum reddat. Q. E. D.", "en": "The more capable the body is rendered of these things, the more capable the mind is rendered of perceiving (by Prop. 14 of Part II); and so what disposes the body in this way and renders it capable of these things is necessarily good, or useful (by Props. 26 and 27 of this Part), and the more useful, the more capable it can render the body of these things; and on the contrary (by the same Prop. 14 of Part II, inverted, and Props. 26 and 27 of this Part) it is harmful if it renders the body less capable of these things. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P39", "p": "part4", "l": "4P39", "la": "Quae efficiunt, ut motus et quietis ratio, quam corporis humani partes ad invicem habent, conservetur, bona sunt; et ea contra mala, quae efficiunt, ut corporis humani partes aliam ad invicem motus et quietis habeant rationem.", "en": "Those things which bring it about that the ratio of motion and rest which the parts of the human body have to one another is preserved are good; and on the contrary, those are evil which bring it about that the parts of the human body have a different ratio of motion and rest to one another."}, {"id": "E4P39D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P39D", "la": "Corpus humanum indiget, ut conservetur, plurimis aliis corporibus (per postul. 4. P. 2.). At id, quod formam humani corporis constituit, in hoc consistit, quod eius partes motus suos certa quadam ratione sibi invicem communicent (per defin. ante lemm. 4. quam vide post prop. 13. P. 2.). Ergo quae efficiunt, ut motus et quietis ratio, quam corporis humani partes ad invicem habent, conservetur, eadem humani corporis formam conservant, et consequenter efficiunt (per postul. 3. et 6. P. 2.) ut corpus humanum multis modis affici, et ut idem corpora externa multis modis afficere possit; adeoque (per prop. praeced.) bona sunt.", "en": "The human body needs a great many other bodies in order to be preserved (by Post. 4 of Part II). But what constitutes the form of the human body consists in this, that its parts communicate their motions to one another in a certain fixed ratio (by the Def. before Lemma 4, which see after Prop. 13 of Part II). Therefore those things which bring it about that the ratio of motion and rest which the parts of the human body have to one another is preserved also preserve the form of the human body, and consequently bring it about (by Posts. 3 and 6 of Part II) that the human body can be affected in many ways, and that it can affect external bodies in many ways; and so (by the preceding Prop.) they are good."}, {"id": "E4P39D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P39D", "la": "Deinde quae efficiunt, ut corporis humani partes aliam motus et quietis rationem obtineant, eadem (per eandem defin. P. 2.) efficiunt, ut corpus humanum aliam formam induat, hoc est (ut per se notum, et in fine praef. huius partis monuimus) ut corpus humanum destruatur, et consequenter ut omnino ineptum reddatur, ne possit pluribus modis affici, ac proinde (per prop. praeced.) mala sunt. Q. E. D.", "en": "Next, those things which bring it about that the parts of the human body obtain a different ratio of motion and rest bring it about (by the same Def. of Part II) that the human body takes on another form, that is (as is self-evident, and as we pointed out at the end of the Preface of this Part), that the human body is destroyed, and consequently that it is rendered entirely incapable of being affected in many ways; and accordingly (by the preceding Prop.) they are evil. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P39S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P39S", "la": "Quantum haec menti obesse vel prodesse possunt in P. 5. explicabitur. Sed hic notandum, quod corpus tum mortem obire intelligam, quando eius partes ita disponuntur, ut aliam motus et quietis rationem ad invicem obtineant. Nam negare non audeo corpus humanum retenta sanguinis circulatione et aliis, propter quae corpus vivere existimatur, posse nihilominus in aliam naturam a sua prorsus diversam mutari. Nam nulla ratio me cogit, ut statuam corpus non mori, nisi mutetur in cadaver; quin ipsa experientia aliud suadere videtur.", "en": "How far these things can harm or benefit the mind will be explained in Part V. But it should be noted here that I understand the body to die when its parts are so disposed that they obtain a different ratio of motion and rest to one another. For I dare not deny that the human body, while the circulation of the blood and the other things on account of which the body is thought to live are retained, can nevertheless be changed into another nature entirely different from its own. For no reason compels me to maintain that the body does not die unless it is changed into a corpse; indeed experience itself seems to suggest otherwise."}, {"id": "E4P39S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P39S", "la": "Fit namque aliquando, ut homo tales patiatur mutationes, ut non facile eundem illum esse dixerim, ut de quodam hispano poeta narrare audivi, qui morbo correptus fuerat et quamvis ex eo convaluerit, mansit tamen praeteritae suae vitae tam oblitus, ut fabulas et tragoedias, quas fecerat, suas non crediderit esse, et sane pro infante adulto haberi potuisset, si vernaculae etiam linguae fuisset oblitus. Et si hoc incredibile videtur, quid de infantibus dicemus, quorum naturam homo provectae aetatis a sua tam diversam esse credit, ut persuaderi non posset, se unquam infantem fuisse, nisi ex aliis de se coniecturam faceret? Sed ne superstitiosis materiam suppeditem movendi novas quaestiones, malo haec in medio relinquere.", "en": "For it sometimes happens that a man undergoes such changes that I should not easily say he was the same man, as I have heard told of a certain Spanish poet who had been seized by an illness and, although he recovered from it, remained so forgetful of his past life that he did not believe the stories and tragedies he had written were his own; and he could certainly have been taken for a grown-up infant if he had also forgotten his native language. And if this seems incredible, what shall we say of infants? A man of advanced age believes their nature to be so different from his own that he could not be persuaded that he had ever been an infant, if he did not make a conjecture about himself from others. But so as not to give the superstitious material for raising new questions, I prefer to leave these matters undecided."}, {"id": "E4P40", "p": "part4", "l": "4P40", "la": "Quae ad hominum communem societatem conducunt, sive quae efficiunt, ut homines concorditer vivant, utilia sunt, et illa contra mala, quae discordiam in civitatem inducunt.", "en": "Things which conduce to the common society of men, or which bring it about that men live harmoniously, are useful; and on the contrary, those are evil which introduce discord into the state."}, {"id": "E4P40D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P40D", "la": "Nam quae efficiunt ut homines concorditer vivant, simul efficiunt, ut ex ductu rationis vivant (per prop. 35. huius), atque adeo (per prop. 26. et 27. huius) bona sunt, et (per eandem rationem) illa contra mala sunt, quae discordias concitant. Q. E. D.", "en": "For things which bring it about that men live harmoniously at the same time bring it about that they live from the guidance of reason (by Prop. 35 of this Part), and so (by Props. 26 and 27 of this Part) they are good; and (by the same reasoning) on the contrary those are evil which arouse discords. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P41", "p": "part4", "l": "4P41", "la": "Laetitia directe mala non est, sed bona; tristitia autem contra directe est mala.", "en": "Joy is not directly evil, but good; sadness, on the other hand, is directly evil."}, {"id": "E4P41D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P41D", "la": "Laetitia (per prop. 11. P. 3. cum eiusdem schol.) est affectus, quo corporis agendi potentia augetur vel iuvatur; tristitia autem contra est affectus, quo corporis agendi potentia minuitur vel coercetur; adeoque (per prop. 38. huius) laetitia directe bona est etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "Joy (by Prop. 11 of Part III with its Schol.) is an affect by which the body’s power of acting is increased or aided; sadness, on the other hand, is an affect by which the body’s power of acting is diminished or restrained; and so (by Prop. 38 of this Part) joy is directly good, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P42", "p": "part4", "l": "4P42", "la": "Hilaritas excessum habere nequit, sed semper bona est, et contra melancholia semper mala.", "en": "Cheerfulness cannot be excessive, but is always good; and on the contrary, melancholy is always evil."}, {"id": "E4P42D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P42D", "la": "Hilaritas (vide eius defin. in schol. prop. 11. P. 3.) est laetitia, quae, quatenus ad corpus refertur, in hoc consistit, quod corporis omnes partes pariter sint affectae, hoc est (per prop. 11. P. 3.) quod corporis agendi potentia augetur vel iuvatur, ita ut omnes eius partes eandem ad invicem motus et quietis rationem obtineant; atque adeo (per prop. 39. huius) hilaritas semper est bona, nec excessum habere potest.", "en": "Cheerfulness (see its definition in Schol. of Prop. 11 of Part III) is a joy which, insofar as it is related to the body, consists in this, that all the parts of the body are equally affected, that is (by Prop. 11 of Part III), that the body’s power of acting is increased or aided in such a way that all its parts maintain the same ratio of motion and rest to one another; and so (by Prop. 39 of this Part) cheerfulness is always good, and cannot be excessive."}, {"id": "E4P42D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P42D", "la": "At melancholia (cuius etiam defin. vide in eodem schol. prop. 11. P. 3.) est tristitia, quae quatenus ad corpus refertur, in hoc consistit, quod corporis agendi potentia absolute minuitur vel coercetur; adeoque (per prop. 38. huius) semper est mala. Q. E. D.", "en": "But melancholy (whose definition also see in the same Schol. of Prop. 11 of Part III) is a sadness which, insofar as it is related to the body, consists in this, that the body’s power of acting is absolutely diminished or restrained; and so (by Prop. 38 of this Part) it is always evil. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P43", "p": "part4", "l": "4P43", "la": "Titillatio excessum habere potest et mala esse; dolor autem eatenus potest esse bonus, quatenus titillatio seu laetitia est mala.", "en": "Pleasure can be excessive and be evil; but pain can be good insofar as pleasure, or joy, is evil."}, {"id": "E4P43D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P43D", "la": "Titillatio est laetitia, quae, quatenus ad corpus refertur, in hoc consistit, quod una vel aliquot eius partes prae reliquis afficiuntur (vide eius defin. in schol. prop. 11. P. 3.), cuius affectus potentia tanta esse potest, ut reliquas corporis actiones superet (per prop. 6. huius), eique pertinaciter adhaereat, atque adeo impediat, quominus corpus aptum sit, ut plurimis aliis modis afficiatur; adeoque (per prop. 38. huius) mala esse potest. Deinde dolor, qui contra tristitia est, in se solo consideratus non potest esse bonus (per prop. 41. huius).", "en": "Pleasure is a joy which, insofar as it is related to the body, consists in this, that one or several of its parts are affected more than the rest (see its definition in Schol. of Prop. 11 of Part III); the power of this affect can be so great that it surpasses the other actions of the body (by Prop. 6 of this Part) and clings stubbornly to it, and so prevents the body from being capable of being affected in very many other ways; and so (by Prop. 38 of this Part) it can be evil. Next, pain, which on the contrary is a sadness, cannot, considered in itself alone, be good (by Prop. 41 of this Part)."}, {"id": "E4P43D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P43D", "la": "Verum quia eius vis et incrementum definitur potentia causae externae cum nostra comparata (per prop. 5. huius), possumus ergo huius affectus infinitos virium concipere gradus et modos (per prop. 3. huius); atque adeo eundem talem concipere, qui titillationem possit coercere, ut excessum non habeat, et eatenus (per primam partem prop. huius) efficere, ne corpus minus aptum reddatur, ac proinde eatenus erit bonus. Q. E. D.", "en": "But because its force and increase are defined by the power of an external cause compared with ours (by Prop. 5 of this Part), we can therefore conceive infinite degrees and modes of the strength of this affect (by Prop. 3 of this Part); and so we can conceive it such that it can restrain pleasure so that it is not excessive, and to that extent (by the first part of this Prop.) bring it about that the body is not rendered less capable; and accordingly to that extent it will be good. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P44", "p": "part4", "l": "4P44", "la": "Amor et cupiditas excessum habere possunt.", "en": "Love and desire can be excessive."}, {"id": "E4P44D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P44D", "la": "Amor est laetitia (per aff. defin. 6.) concomitante idea causae externae. Titillatio igitur (per schol. prop. 11. P. 3.) concomitante idea causae externae amor est; atque adeo amor (per prop. praeced.) excessum habere potest. Deinde cupiditas eo est maior, quo affectus, ex quo oritur, maior est (per prop. 37. P. 3.). Quare ut affectus (per prop. 6. huius) reliquas hominis actiones superare potest, sic etiam cupiditas, quae ex eodem affectu oritur, reliquas cupiditates superare, ac proinde eundem excessum habere poterit, quem in praeced. prop. titillationem habere ostendimus. Q. E. D.", "en": "Love is joy (by Def. Aff. 6) accompanied by the idea of an external cause. Pleasure, therefore (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of Part III), accompanied by the idea of an external cause is love; and so love (by the preceding Prop.) can be excessive. Next, desire is the greater, the greater the affect from which it arises (by Prop. 37 of Part III). Hence, as an affect (by Prop. 6 of this Part) can surpass the other actions of a man, so also the desire which arises from that affect can surpass the other desires, and accordingly can have the same excess which we showed in the preceding Prop. that pleasure has. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P44S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P44S", "la": "Hilaritas, quam bonam esse dixi, concipitur facilius, quam observatur. Nam affectus, quibus quotidie conflictamur, referuntur plerumque ad aliquam corporis partem, quae prae reliquis afficitur, ac proinde affectus ut plurimum excessum habent, et mentem in sola unius obiecti contemplatione ita detinent, ut de aliis cogitare nequeat; et quamvis homines pluribus affectibus obnoxii sint, atque adeo rari reperiantur, qui semper uno eodemque affectu conflictentur, non desunt tamen, quibus unus idemque affectus pertinaciter adhaereat.", "en": "Cheerfulness, which I said is good, is more easily conceived than observed. For the affects by which we are daily troubled are generally related to some part of the body which is affected more than the rest, and so the affects are for the most part excessive, and so hold the mind in the contemplation of one object alone that it cannot think of others; and although men are subject to many affects, and so few are found who are always troubled by one and the same affect, still there are not lacking those to whom one and the same affect stubbornly clings."}, {"id": "E4P44S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P44S", "la": "Videmus enim homines aliquando ab uno obiecto ita affici, ut, quamvis praesens non sit, ipsum tamen coram habere credant, quod quando homini non dormienti accidit, eundem delirare dicimus vel insanire; nec minus insanire creduntur, qui amore ardent, quique noctes atque dies solam amasiam vel meretricem somniant, quia risum movere solent. At cum avarus de nulla alia re, quam de lucro vel de nummis cogitet, et ambitiosus de gloria etc., hi non creduntur delirare, quia molesti solent esse et odio digni aestimantur. Sed revera avaritia, ambitio, libido etc. delirii species sunt, quamvis inter morbos non numerentur.", "en": "For we see that men are sometimes so affected by one object that, although it is not present, they still believe they have it before them; and when this happens to a man who is not asleep, we say that he is delirious or mad. Nor are those believed to be less mad who burn with love, and who dream night and day only of a mistress or a courtesan, because they usually provoke laughter. But when a greedy man thinks of nothing else but profit or money, and an ambitious man of glory, etc., they are not believed to be delirious, because they are usually troublesome and are thought worthy of hatred. But in truth greed, ambition, lust, etc., are species of madness, although they are not numbered among diseases."}, {"id": "E4P45", "p": "part4", "l": "4P45", "la": "Odium nunquam potest esse bonum.", "en": "Hatred can never be good."}, {"id": "E4P45D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P45D", "la": "Hominem quem odimus destruere conamur (per prop. 39. P. 3.) hoc est (per prop. 37. huius) aliquid conamur quod malum est. Ergo etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "We strive to destroy the man we hate (by Prop. 39 of Part III), that is (by Prop. 37 of this Part), we strive for something which is evil. Therefore, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P45S1", "p": "part4", "l": "4P45S1", "la": "Nota me hic et in seqq. per odium illud tantum intelligere quod est erga homines.", "en": "Note that here and in what follows I understand by hatred only hatred towards men."}, {"id": "E4P45C1", "p": "part4", "l": "4P45C1", "la": "Invidia, irrisio, contemptus, ira, vindicta et reliqui affectus, qui ad odium referuntur vel ex eodem oriuntur, mali sunt, quod etiam ex prop. 39. P. 3. et prop. 37. huius patet.", "en": "Envy, mockery, contempt, anger, vengeance and the other affects which are related to hatred or arise from it are evil, which is also evident from Prop. 39 of Part III and Prop. 37 of this Part."}, {"id": "E4P45C2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P45C2", "la": "Quicquid ex eo, quod odio affecti sumus, appetimus, turpe et in civitate iniustum est. Quod etiam patet ex prop. 39. P. 3., et ex defin. turpis et iniusti, quas vide in schol. prop. 37. huius.", "en": "Whatever we want from the fact that we are affected with hatred is base, and in the state unjust. This is also evident from Prop. 39 of Part III, and from the definitions of base and unjust, which see in the Scholia of Prop. 37 of this Part (Schol. 1, Schol. 2)."}, {"id": "E4P45S2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P45S2", "la": "Inter *irrisionem* (quam in coroll. 1. malam esse dixi) et *risum* magnam agnosco differentiam. Nam risus, ut et iocus, mera est laetitia; adeoque modo excessum non habeat, per se bonus est (per prop. 41. huius). Nihil profecto nisi torva et tristis superstitio delectari prohibet. Nam qui magis decet famem et sitim extinguere, quam melancholiam expellere?", "en": "Between mockery (which I said in Cor. 1 is evil) and laughter I recognize a great difference. For laughter, like jest, is pure joy; and so, provided it is not excessive, it is good in itself (by Prop. 41 of this Part). Nothing, certainly, but grim and gloomy superstition forbids us to enjoy ourselves. For why is it more fitting to extinguish hunger and thirst than to drive out melancholy?"}, {"id": "E4P45S2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P45S2", "la": "Mea haec est ratio et sic animum induxi meum. Nullum numen, nec alius nisi invidus, mea impotentia et incommodo delectatur, nec nobis lacrimas, singultus, metum et alia huiusmodi, quae animi impotentis sunt signa, virtuti ducit; sed contra, quo maiore laetitia afficimur, eo ad maiorem perfectionem transimus, hoc est, eo nos magis de natura divina participare necesse est. Rebus itaque uti et iis, quantum fieri potest, delectari (non quidem ad nauseam usque, nam hoc delectari non est) viri est sapientis.", "en": "This is my reasoning, and so I have persuaded myself. No deity, nor anyone else but an envious man, takes pleasure in my lack of power and my misfortune, or counts as virtue our tears, sobs, fear and other things of this kind, which are signs of a mind lacking in power; on the contrary, the greater the joy with which we are affected, the greater the perfection to which we pass, that is, the more we must participate in the divine nature. It is the part of a wise man, therefore, to use things and to take pleasure in them as far as possible (not, indeed, to the point of disgust, for that is not taking pleasure)."}, {"id": "E4P45S2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P45S2", "la": "Viri, inquam, sapientis est moderato et suavi cibo et potu se reficere et recreare, ut et odoribus, plantarum virentium amoenitate, ornatu, musica, ludis exercitatoriis, theatris et aliis huiusmodi, quibus unusquisque absque ullo alterius damno uti potest.", "en": "It is the part of a wise man, I say, to refresh and restore himself with moderate and pleasant food and drink, and also with scents, with the beauty of green plants, with ornament, music, sports, theatres and other things of this kind which anyone can use without any harm to another."}, {"id": "E4P45S2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P45S2", "la": "Corpus namque humanum ex plurimis diversae naturae partibus componitur, quae continuo novo alimento indigent et vario, ut totum corpus ad omnia, quae ex ipsius natura sequi possunt, aeque aptum sit, et consequenter ut mens etiam aeque apta sit ad plura simul intelligendum. Hoc itaque vivendi institutum et cum nostris principiis et cum communi praxi optime convenit; quare si quae alia, haec vivendi ratio optima est et omnibus modis commendanda, nec opus est de his clarius, neque prolixius agere.", "en": "For the human body is composed of a great many parts of different natures, which continually need new and varied nourishment, so that the whole body may be equally capable of all the things which can follow from its nature, and consequently so that the mind also may be equally capable of understanding many things at once. This way of life, therefore, agrees best both with our principles and with common practice; so, if any other, this way of living is the best and is to be commended in every way; nor is there any need to treat of these things more clearly or at greater length."}, {"id": "E4P46", "p": "part4", "l": "4P46", "la": "Qui ex ductu rationis vivit, quantum potest, conatur alterius in ipsum odium, iram, contemptum etc. amore contra sive generositate compensare.", "en": "He who lives from the guidance of reason strives as far as he can to repay another’s hatred, anger, contempt, etc., towards him with love in return, or nobility."}, {"id": "E4P46D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P46D", "la": "Omnes odii affectus mali sunt (per coroll. 1. praeced. prop.); adeoque qui ex ductu rationis vivit, quantum potest conabitur efficere, ne odii affectibus conflictetur (per prop. 19. huius), et consequenter (per prop. 37. huius) conabitur, ne etiam alius eosdem patiatur affectus. At odium odio reciproco augetur et amore contra extingui potest (per prop. 43. P. 3.), ita ut odium in amorem transeat (per prop. 44. P. 3.).", "en": "All affects of hatred are evil (by Cor. 1 of the preceding Prop.); and so he who lives from the guidance of reason will strive as far as he can to bring it about that he is not troubled by affects of hatred (by Prop. 19 of this Part), and consequently (by Prop. 37 of this Part) he will strive that no one else suffers these affects either. But hatred is increased by reciprocal hatred, and on the contrary can be extinguished by love (by Prop. 43 of Part III), so that hatred passes into love (by Prop. 44 of Part III)."}, {"id": "E4P46D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P46D", "la": "Ergo qui ex ductu rationis vivit, alterius odium etc. amore contra compensare conabitur, hoc est, generositate (cuius defin. vide in schol. prop. 59. P. 3.). Q. E. D.", "en": "Therefore he who lives from the guidance of reason will strive to repay another’s hatred, etc., with love in return, that is, with nobility (whose definition see in Schol. of Prop. 59 of Part III). Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P46S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P46S", "la": "Qui iniurias reciproco odio vindicare vult, misere profecto vivit. At qui contra studet odium amore expugnare, ille sane laetus et secure pugnat; aeque facile pluribus hominibus ac uni resistit, et fortunae auxilio quam minime indiget. Quos vero vincit, ii laeti cedunt, non quidem ex defectu sed ex incremento virium. Quae omnia adeo clare ex solis amoris et intellectus definitionibus sequuntur, ut opus non sit eadem sigillatim demonstrare.", "en": "He who wants to avenge injuries by reciprocal hatred lives miserably indeed. But he who strives, on the contrary, to conquer hatred by love certainly fights joyfully and securely; he resists many men as easily as one, and needs the help of fortune least of all. Those whom he conquers yield joyfully, not from a lack but from an increase of their powers. All these things follow so clearly from the definitions of love and intellect alone that there is no need to demonstrate them one by one."}, {"id": "E4P47", "p": "part4", "l": "4P47", "la": "Spei et metus affectus non possunt esse per se boni.", "en": "The affects of hope and fear cannot be good in themselves."}, {"id": "E4P47D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P47D", "la": "Spei et metus affectus sine tristitia non dantur. Nam metus est (per aff. defin. 13.) tristitia, et spes (vide explicationem aff. defin. 12. et 13.) non datur sine metu. Ac proinde (per prop. 41. huius) hi affectus non possunt esse per se boni, sed tantum quatenus laetitiae excessum coercere possunt (per prop. 43. huius). Q. E. D.", "en": "The affects of hope and fear are not given without sadness. For fear is (by Def. Aff. 13) sadness, and hope (see the explanation of Defs. Aff. 12 and 13) is not given without fear. And accordingly (by Prop. 41 of this Part) these affects cannot be good in themselves, but only insofar as they can restrain an excess of joy (by Prop. 43 of this Part). Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P47S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P47S", "la": "Huc accedit, quod hi affectus cognitionis defectum et mentis impotentiam indicant; et hac de causa etiam securitas, desperatio, gaudium et conscientiae morsus animi impotentis sunt signa. Nam quamvis securitas et gaudium affectus sint laetitiae, tristitiam tamen eosdem praecessisse supponunt, nempe spem et metum. Quo itaque magis ex ductu rationis vivere conamur, eo magis spe minus pendere et metu nosmet liberare, et fortunae, quantum possumus, imperare conamur, nostrasque actiones certo rationis consilio dirigere.", "en": "Add to this that these affects indicate a lack of knowledge and a lack of power of the mind; and for this reason also confidence, despair, gladness and remorse are signs of a mind lacking in power. For although confidence and gladness are affects of joy, they still presuppose that a sadness has preceded them, namely hope and fear. The more, therefore, we strive to live from the guidance of reason, the more we strive to depend less on hope, to free ourselves from fear, to command fortune as far as we can, and to direct our actions by the sure counsel of reason."}, {"id": "E4P48", "p": "part4", "l": "4P48", "la": "Affectus existimationis et despectus semper mali sunt.", "en": "The affects of overestimation and scorn are always evil."}, {"id": "E4P48D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P48D", "la": "Hi enim affectus (per aff. defin. 21. et 22.) rationi repugnant; adeoque (per prop. 26. et 27. huius) mali sunt. Q. E. D.", "en": "For these affects (by Defs. Aff. 21 and 22) are opposed to reason; and so (by Props. 26 and 27 of this Part) they are evil. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P49", "p": "part4", "l": "4P49", "la": "Existimatio facile hominem, qui existimatur, superbum reddit.", "en": "Overestimation easily makes the man who is overestimated proud."}, {"id": "E4P49D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P49D", "la": "Si videmus, aliquem de nobis plus iusto prae amore sentire, facile gloriabimur (per schol. prop. 41. P. 3.) sive laetitia afficiemur (per aff. defin. 30.), et id boni, quod de nobis praedicari audimus, facile credemus (per prop. 25. P. 3.). Atque adeo de nobis prae amore nostri plus iusto sentiemus, hoc est (per aff. defin. 28.) facile superbiemus. Q. E. D.", "en": "If we see that someone, out of love, thinks more highly of us than is just, we shall easily exult in glory (by Schol. of Prop. 41 of Part III), or be affected with joy (by Def. Aff. 30), and we shall easily believe the good which we hear predicated of us (by Prop. 25 of Part III). And so, out of love of ourselves, we shall think more highly of ourselves than is just, that is (by Def. Aff. 28), we shall easily become proud. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P50", "p": "part4", "l": "4P50", "la": "Commiseratio in homine, qui ex ductu rationis vivit, per se mala et inutilis est.", "en": "Pity, in a man who lives from the guidance of reason, is in itself evil and useless."}, {"id": "E4P50D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P50D", "la": "Commiseratio enim (per aff. defin. 18.) tristitia est, ac proinde (per prop. 41. huius) per se mala. Bonum autem, quod ex ea sequitur, quod scilicet hominem cuius nos miseret, a miseria liberare conamur (per coroll. 3. prop. 27. P. 3.), ex solo rationis dictamine facere cupimus (per prop. 37. huius), nec nisi ex solo rationis dictamine aliquid, quod certo scimus bonum esse, agere possumus (per prop. 27. huius). Atque adeo commiseratio in homine, qui ex ductu rationis vivit, per se mala est et inutilis. Q. E. D.", "en": "For pity (by Def. Aff. 18) is sadness, and accordingly (by Prop. 41 of this Part) in itself evil. But the good which follows from it, namely that we strive to free the man we pity from his misery (by Cor. 3 of Prop. 27 of Part III), we desire to do from the dictate of reason alone (by Prop. 37 of this Part), and only from the dictate of reason alone can we do something which we certainly know to be good (by Prop. 27 of this Part). And so pity, in a man who lives from the guidance of reason, is in itself evil and useless. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P50C", "p": "part4", "l": "4P50C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, quod homo qui ex dictamine rationis vivit, conatur, quantum potest, efficere ne commiseratione tangatur.", "en": "Hence it follows that a man who lives from the dictate of reason strives, as far as he can, not to be touched by pity."}, {"id": "E4P50S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P50S", "la": "Qui recte novit, omnia ex naturae divinae necessitate sequi et secundum aeternas naturae leges et regulas fieri, is sane nihil reperiet, quod odio, risu aut contemptu dignum sit, nec cuiusquam miserebitur; sed quantum humana fert virtus, conabitur bene agere, ut aiunt, et laetari.", "en": "He who rightly knows that all things follow from the necessity of the divine nature and happen according to the eternal laws and rules of nature will certainly find nothing worthy of hatred, laughter or contempt, nor will he pity anyone; but as far as human virtue allows, he will strive to act well, as they say, and to rejoice."}, {"id": "E4P50S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P50S", "la": "Huc accedit, quod is, qui commiserationis affectu facile tangitur et alterius miseria vel lacrimis movetur, saepe aliquid agit, cuius postea ipsum poenitet; tam quia ex affectu nihil agimus, quod certo scimus bonum esse, quam quia facile falsis lacrimis decipimur. Atque hic expresse loquor de homine, qui ex ductu rationis vivit. Nam qui nec ratione, nec commiseratione movetur, ut aliis auxilio sit, is recte inhumanus appellatur; nam (per prop. 27. P. 3.) homini dissimilis esse videtur.", "en": "Add to this that he who is easily touched by the affect of pity, and moved by another’s misery or tears, often does something which he afterwards regrets, both because from affect we do nothing which we certainly know to be good, and because we are easily deceived by false tears. And here I speak expressly of a man who lives from the guidance of reason. For he who is moved neither by reason nor by pity to help others is rightly called inhuman. For (by Prop. 27 of Part III) he seems to be unlike a man."}, {"id": "E4P51", "p": "part4", "l": "4P51", "la": "Favor rationi non repugnat, sed cum eadem convenire et ab eadem oriri potest.", "en": "Favour is not opposed to reason, but can agree with it and arise from it."}, {"id": "E4P51D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P51D", "la": "Est enim favor amor erga illum, qui alteri benefecit (per aff. defin. 19.). Atque adeo ad mentem referri potest, quatenus haec agere dicitur (per prop. 59. P. 3.), hoc est (per prop. 3. P. 3.) quatenus intelligit; ac proinde cum ratione convenit etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "For favour is love towards one who has benefited another (by Def. Aff. 19). And so it can be related to the mind insofar as the mind is said to act (by Prop. 59 of Part III), that is (by Prop. 3 of Part III), insofar as it understands; and accordingly it agrees with reason, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P51D2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P51D2", "la": "Qui ex ductu rationis vivit, bonum, quod sibi appetit, alteri etiam cupit (per prop. 37. huius). Quare ex eo, quod ipse aliquem videt alteri benefacere, ipsius benefaciendi conatus iuvatur, hoc est (per schol. prop. 11. P. 3.) laetabitur, idque (ex hypothesi) concomitante idea illius, qui alteri benefecit; ac proinde (per aff. defin. 19.) ei favet. Q. E. D.", "en": "He who lives from the guidance of reason desires for another also the good which he wants for himself (by Prop. 37 of this Part). Hence from the fact that he sees someone benefit another, his own striving to do good is aided, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 11 of Part III), he will rejoice, and that (by hypothesis) accompanied by the idea of the one who benefited another; and accordingly (by Def. Aff. 19) he favours him. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P51S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P51S", "la": "Indignatio, prout ipsa a nobis definitur (vide aff. defin. 20.), est necessario mala (per prop. 45. huius). Sed notandum, quod quando summa potestas desiderio, quo tenetur, tutandae pacis, civem punit, qui alteri iniuriam fecit, eandem civi indignari non dico, quia non odio percita ad perdendum civem, sed pietate mota eundem punit.", "en": "Indignation, as it is defined by us (see Def. Aff. 20), is necessarily evil (by Prop. 45 of this Part). But it should be noted that when the sovereign power, from the desire by which it is bound to preserve peace, punishes a citizen who has done an injury to another, I do not say that it is indignant with the citizen, since it punishes him not because it is roused by hatred to destroy him, but because it is moved by piety."}, {"id": "E4P52", "p": "part4", "l": "4P52", "la": "Acquiescentia in se ipso ex ratione oriri potest, et ea sola acquiescentia, quae ex ratione oritur, summa est quae potest dari.", "en": "Self-contentment can arise from reason, and only that self-contentment which arises from reason is the greatest there can be."}, {"id": "E4P52D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P52D", "la": "Acquiescentia in se ipso est laetitia orta ex eo, quod homo se ipsum suamque agendi potentiam contemplatur (per aff. defin. 25.). At vera hominis agendi potentia seu virtus est ipsa ratio (per prop. 3. P. 3.), quam homo clare et distincte contemplatur (per prop. 40. et 43. P. 2.). Ergo acquiescentia in se ipso ex ratione oritur.", "en": "Self-contentment is a joy arising from the fact that a man contemplates himself and his power of acting (by Def. Aff. 25). But a man’s true power of acting, or virtue, is reason itself (by Prop. 3 of Part III), which man contemplates clearly and distinctly (by Props. 40 and 43 of Part II). Therefore self-contentment arises from reason."}, {"id": "E4P52D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P52D", "la": "Deinde nihil homo, dum se ipsum contemplatur, clare et distincte sive adaequate percipit, nisi ea quae ex ipsius agendi potentia sequuntur (per defin. 2. P. 3.), hoc est (per prop. 3. P. 3.), quae ex ipsius intelligendi potentia sequuntur. Adeoque ex sola hac contemplatione summa, quae dari potest, acquiescentia oritur. Q. E. D.", "en": "Next, a man, while he contemplates himself, perceives nothing clearly and distinctly, or adequately, except those things which follow from his power of acting (by Def. 2 of Part III), that is (by Prop. 3 of Part III), which follow from his power of understanding. And so from this contemplation alone arises the greatest self-contentment there can be. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P52S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P52S", "la": "Est revera acquiescentia in se ipso summum, quod sperare possumus. Nam (ut prop. 25. huius ostendimus) nemo suum esse alicuius finis causa conservare conatur; et quia haec acquiescentia magis magisque fovetur et corroboratur laudibus (per coroll. prop. 53. P. 3.), et contra (per coroll. prop. 55. P. 3.) vituperio magis magisque turbatur, ideo gloria maxime ducimur et vitam cum probro vix ferre possumus.", "en": "Self-contentment is in truth the highest thing we can hope for. For (as we showed in Prop. 25 of this Part) no one strives to preserve his being for the sake of some end; and because this self-contentment is more and more encouraged and strengthened by praise (by Cor. of Prop. 53 of Part III), and on the contrary (by Cor. of Prop. 55 of Part III) is more and more disturbed by blame, we are therefore led most by glory and can hardly bear a life in disgrace."}, {"id": "E4P53", "p": "part4", "l": "4P53", "la": "Humilitas virtus non est, sive ex ratione non oritur.", "en": "Humility is not a virtue, or does not arise from reason."}, {"id": "E4P53D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P53D", "la": "Humilitas est tristitia, quae ex eo oritur, quod homo suam impotentiam contemplatur (per aff. defin. 26.). Quatenus autem homo se ipsum vera ratione cognoscit, eatenus suam essentiam intelligere supponitur, hoc est (per prop. 7. P. 3.) suam potentiam. Quare si homo, dum se ipsum contemplatur, aliquam suam impotentiam percipit, id non ex eo est, quod se intelligit, sed (ut prop. 55. P. 3. ostendimus) ex eo, quod ipsius agendi potentia coercetur.", "en": "Humility is a sadness which arises from the fact that a man contemplates his lack of power (by Def. Aff. 26). But insofar as a man knows himself by true reason, to that extent he is supposed to understand his essence, that is (by Prop. 7 of Part III), his power. So if a man, while he contemplates himself, perceives some lack of power of his, this does not come from his understanding himself, but (as we showed in Prop. 55 of Part III) from his power of acting being restrained."}, {"id": "E4P53D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P53D", "la": "Quod si supponamus, hominem suam impotentiam concipere ex eo, quod aliquid se potentius intelligit, cuius cognitione suam agendi potentiam determinat, tum nihil aliud concipimus, quam quod homo se ipsum distincte intelligit sive (per prop. 26. huius), quod ipsius agendi potentia iuvatur. Quare humilitas seu tristitia, quae ex eo oritur, quod homo suam impotentiam contemplatur, non ex vera contemplatione seu ratione oritur, nec virtus, sed passio est. Q. E. D.", "en": "But if we suppose that a man conceives his lack of power from the fact that he understands something more powerful than himself, by the knowledge of which he determines his power of acting, then we conceive nothing else but that the man understands himself distinctly, or (by Prop. 26 of this Part) that his power of acting is aided. So humility, or the sadness which arises from the fact that a man contemplates his lack of power, does not arise from true contemplation, or reason, and is not a virtue but a passion. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P54", "p": "part4", "l": "4P54", "la": "Poenitentia virtus non est, sive ex ratione non oritur, sed is, quem facti poenitet, bis miser seu impotens est.", "en": "Repentance is not a virtue, or does not arise from reason; but he who repents of what he has done is twice wretched, or lacking in power."}, {"id": "E4P54D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P54D", "la": "Huius prima pars demonstratur ut praeced. prop. Secunda autem ex sola huius affectus definitione (vide aff. defin. 27.) patet. Nam primo prava cupiditate, dein tristitia vinci se patitur.", "en": "The first part of this is demonstrated like the preceding Prop.; but the second is evident from the definition of this affect alone (see Def. Aff. 27). For he allows himself to be conquered first by an evil desire, then by sadness."}, {"id": "E4P54S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P54S", "la": "Quia homines raro ex dictamine rationis vivunt, ideo hi duo affectus, nempe humilitas et poenitentia, et praeter hos spes et metus plus utilitatis, quam damni afferunt, atque adeo quandoquidem peccandum est, in istam partem potius peccandum. Nam si homines animo impotentes aeque omnes superbirent, nullius rei ipsos puderet, nec ipsi quicquam metuerent, qui vinculis coniungi constringique possent? Terret vulgus, nisi metuat.", "en": "Because men rarely live from the dictate of reason, these two affects, namely humility and repentance, and besides these hope and fear, bring more advantage than harm; and so, since men must err, it is better that they err in that direction. For if men lacking in power of mind were all equally proud, were ashamed of nothing and feared nothing, by what bonds could they be united and held together? The mob is a terror, unless it is afraid."}, {"id": "E4P54S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P54S", "la": "Quare non mirum, quod prophetae qui non paucorum, sed communi utilitati consuluerunt, tantopere humilitatem, poenitentiam et reverentiam commendaverint. Et revera qui hisce affectibus sunt obnoxii, multo facilius, quam alii duci possunt, ut tandem ex ductu rationis vivant, hoc est ut liberi sint et beatorum vita fruantur.", "en": "So it is no wonder that the prophets, who looked to the common advantage and not to that of a few, commended humility, repentance and reverence so greatly. And in fact those who are subject to these affects can be led far more easily than others to live, in the end, from the guidance of reason, that is, to be free and to enjoy the life of the blessed."}, {"id": "E4P55", "p": "part4", "l": "4P55", "la": "Maxima superbia vel abiectio est maxima sui ignorantia.", "en": "The greatest pride or self-abasement is the greatest ignorance of oneself."}, {"id": "E4P55D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P55D", "la": "Patet ex aff. defin. 28. et 29.", "en": "This is evident from Defs. Aff. 28 and 29."}, {"id": "E4P56", "p": "part4", "l": "4P56", "la": "Maxima superbia vel abiectio maximam animi impotentiam indicat.", "en": "The greatest pride or self-abasement indicates the greatest lack of power of mind."}, {"id": "E4P56D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P56D", "la": "Primum virtutis fundamentum est suum esse conservare (per coroll. prop. 22. huius) idque ex ductu rationis (per prop. 24. huius). Qui igitur se ipsum ignorat, omnium virtutum fundamentum, et consequenter omnes virtutes ignorat.", "en": "The first foundation of virtue is to preserve one’s being (by Cor. of Prop. 22 of this Part), and that from the guidance of reason (by Prop. 24 of this Part). He, therefore, who is ignorant of himself is ignorant of the foundation of all the virtues, and consequently of all the virtues."}, {"id": "E4P56D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P56D", "la": "Deinde ex virtute agere nihil aliud est, quam ex ductu rationis agere (per prop. 24. huius), et qui ex ductu rationis agit, scire necessario debet se ex ductu rationis agere (per prop. 43. P. 2.). Qui itaque se ipsum, et consequenter (ut iam iam ostendimus) omnes virtutes maxime ignorat, is minime ex virtute agit, hoc est (ut ex defin. 8. huius patet) maxime animo est impotens; atque adeo (per prop. praeced.) maxima superbia vel abiectio maximam animi impotentiam indicat. Q. E. D.", "en": "Next, to act from virtue is nothing but to act from the guidance of reason (by Prop. 24 of this Part), and he who acts from the guidance of reason must necessarily know that he acts from the guidance of reason (by Prop. 43 of Part II). He, therefore, who is most ignorant of himself, and consequently (as we have just shown) of all the virtues, acts least from virtue, that is (as is evident from Def. 8 of this Part), is most lacking in power of mind; and so (by the preceding Prop.) the greatest pride or self-abasement indicates the greatest lack of power of mind. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P56C", "p": "part4", "l": "4P56C", "la": "Hinc clarissime sequitur, superbos et abiectos maxime affectibus esse obnoxios.", "en": "Hence it follows most clearly that the proud and the self-abased are most subject to affects."}, {"id": "E4P56S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P56S", "la": "Abiectio tamen facilius corrigi potest, quam superbia, quandoquidem haec laetitiae, illa autem tristitiae est affectus; atque adeo (per prop. 18. huius) haec illa fortior est.", "en": "Self-abasement, however, can be corrected more easily than pride, since pride is an affect of joy and self-abasement an affect of sadness; and so (by Prop. 18 of this Part) the former is stronger than the latter."}, {"id": "E4P57", "p": "part4", "l": "4P57", "la": "Superbus parasitorum seu adulatorum praesentiam amat, generosorum autem odit.", "en": "The proud man loves the presence of parasites, or flatterers, but hates that of the noble."}, {"id": "E4P57D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P57D", "la": "Superbia est laetitia orta ex eo, quod homo de se plus iusto sentit (per aff. defin. 28. et 6.), quam opinionem homo superbus, quantum potest, fovere conabitur (vide schol. prop. 13. P. 3.). Adeoque superbi parasitorum vel adulatorum (horum definitiones omisi, quia nimis noti sunt) praesentiam amabunt, et generosorum, qui de ipsis, ut par est, sentiunt, fugient. Q. E. D.", "en": "Pride is a joy arising from the fact that a man thinks more highly of himself than is just (by Defs. Aff. 28 and 6), an opinion which the proud man will strive to encourage as far as he can (see Schol. of Prop. 13 of Part III). And so the proud will love the presence of parasites or flatterers (I have omitted their definitions because they are too well known), and will flee that of the noble, who think of them as is right. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P57S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P57S", "la": "Nimis longum foret, hic omnia superbiae mala enumerare, quandoquidem omnibus affectibus obnoxii sunt superbi, sed nullis minus, quam affectibus amoris et misericordiae. Sed hic minime tacendum est, quod ille etiam superbus vocetur, qui de reliquis minus iusto sentit, atque adeo hoc sensu superbia definienda est, quod sit laetitia orta ex falsa opinione, quod homo se supra reliquos esse putat. Et abiectio huic superbiae contraria definienda esset tristitia orta ex falsa opinione, quod homo se infra reliquos esse credit.", "en": "It would take too long to enumerate here all the evils of pride, since the proud are subject to all affects, but to none less than the affects of love and compassion. But here it must by no means be passed over in silence that he too is called proud who thinks less of others than is just; and so in this sense pride is to be defined as a joy arising from the false opinion by which a man thinks he is above others. And the self-abasement contrary to this pride would be defined as a sadness arising from the false opinion by which a man believes he is below others."}, {"id": "E4P57S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P57S", "la": "At hoc posito facile concipimus, superbum necessario esse invidum (vide schol. prop. 55. P. 3.) et eos maxime odio habere, qui maxime ob virtutes laudantur, nec facile eorum odium amore aut beneficio vinci (vide schol. prop. 41. P. 3.), et eorum tantummodo praesentia delectari, qui animo eius impotenti morem gerunt et ex stulto insanum faciunt.", "en": "But on this supposition we easily conceive that the proud man is necessarily envious (see Schol. of Prop. 55 of Part III), that he hates most those who are most praised for their virtues, that his hatred of them is not easily conquered by love or benefit (see Schol. of Prop. 41 of Part III), and that he takes pleasure only in the presence of those who humour his mind lacking in power and make a madman out of a fool."}, {"id": "E4P57S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P57S", "la": "Abiectio quamvis superbiae sit contraria, est tamen abiectus superbo proximus. Nam quandoquidem eius tristitia ex eo oritur, quod suam impotentiam ex aliorum potentia seu virtute iudicat, levabitur ergo eius tristitia, hoc est, laetabitur si eius imaginatio in alienis vitiis contemplandis occupetur, unde illud proverbium natum: Solamen miseris socios habuisse malorum; et contra eo magis contristabitur, quo se magis infra reliquos esse crediderit. Unde fit, ut nulli magis ad invidiam sint proni, quam abiecti, et ut isti maxime hominum facta observare conentur ad carpendum magis, quam ad eadem corrigendum, et ut tandem solam abiectionem laudent eaque glorientur, sed ita, ut tamen abiecti videantur.", "en": "Although self-abasement is contrary to pride, the self-abased man is still very near to the proud. For since his sadness arises from the fact that he judges his lack of power from the power, or virtue, of others, his sadness will therefore be relieved, that is, he will rejoice, if his imagination is occupied in contemplating the vices of others; hence the proverb: It is a comfort to the wretched to have had companions in misfortune; and on the contrary he will be the more saddened, the more he believes himself to be below others. Hence it comes about that no one is more prone to envy than the self-abased, that they strive most to observe men’s deeds, to find fault with them rather than to correct them, and that finally they praise only self-abasement and glory in it, but in such a way that they still seem self-abased."}, {"id": "E4P57S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P57S", "la": "Atque haec ex hoc affectu tam necessario sequuntur, quam ex natura trianguli, quod eius tres anguli aequales sint duobus rectis; et iam dixi me hos et similes affectus malos vocare, quatenus ad solam humanam utilitatem attendo. Sed naturae leges communem naturae ordinem, cuius homo pars est, respiciunt; quod hic in transitu monere volui, ne quis putaret me hic hominum vitia et absurda facta narrare, non autem rerum naturam et proprietates demonstrare voluisse.", "en": "And these things follow from this affect as necessarily as it follows from the nature of a triangle that its three angles are equal to two right angles; and I have already said that I call these and similar affects evil insofar as I attend only to human advantage. But the laws of nature concern the common order of nature, of which man is a part; I wished to point this out here in passing, so that no one should think that I wanted here to tell of the vices and absurd deeds of men, rather than to demonstrate the nature and properties of things."}, {"id": "E4P57S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P57S", "la": "Nam, ut in praef. P. 3. dixi, humanos affectus eorumque proprietates perinde considero, ac reliqua naturalia. Et sane humani affectus, si non humanam, naturae saltem potentiam et artificium non minus indicant, quam multa alia, quae admiramur quorumque contemplatione delectamur. Sed pergo de affectibus ea notare, quae hominibus utilitatem adferunt, vel quae iisdem damnum inferunt.", "en": "For, as I said in the Preface of Part III, I consider human affects and their properties just like other natural things. And human affects certainly indicate, if not human power, at least the power and art of nature no less than many other things which we admire and in the contemplation of which we take pleasure. But I go on to note those things about the affects which bring advantage to men, or which inflict harm on them."}, {"id": "E4P58", "p": "part4", "l": "4P58", "la": "Gloria rationi non repugnat, sed ab ea oriri potest.", "en": "Glory is not opposed to reason, but can arise from it."}, {"id": "E4P58D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P58D", "la": "Patet ex aff. defin. 30. et ex defin. honesti, quam vide in schol. 1. prop. 37. huius.", "en": "This is evident from Def. Aff. 30 and from the definition of the honourable, which see in Schol. 1 of Prop. 37 of this Part."}, {"id": "E4P58S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P58S", "la": "Vana quae dicitur gloria est acquiescentia in se ipso, quae sola vulgi opinione fovetur, eaque cessante cessat ipsa acquiescentia, hoc est (per schol. prop. 52. huius) summum bonum, quod unusquisque amat. Unde fit, ut qui vulgi opinione gloriatur, quotidiana cura anxius nitatur, faciat, experiatur, ut famam conservet.", "en": "What is called empty glory is a self-contentment which is encouraged only by the opinion of the multitude, and when that ceases, the self-contentment itself ceases, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 52 of this Part), the highest good which each one loves. Hence it comes about that he who glories in the opinion of the multitude, anxious with daily care, strives, acts and tries in order to preserve his reputation."}, {"id": "E4P58S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P58S", "la": "Est namque vulgus varius et inconstans, atque adeo, nisi conservetur fama, cito abolescit; imo quia omnes vulgi captare applausus cupiunt, facile unusquisque alterius famam reprimit, ex quo, quandoquidem de summo, quod aestimatur, bono certatur, ingens libido oritur se invicem quocumque modo opprimendi, et qui tandem victor evadit, gloriatur magis, quod alteri obfuit, quam quod sibi profuit. Est igitur haec gloria seu acquiescentia revera vana, quia nulla est.", "en": "For the multitude is changeable and inconstant, and so, unless a reputation is preserved, it quickly vanishes; indeed, because all desire to capture the applause of the multitude, each one readily represses the reputation of another; and from this, since the contest is over what is esteemed the highest good, there arises an immense lust to crush one another in any way whatever, and he who finally emerges victorious glories more in having harmed another than in having benefited himself. This glory, or self-contentment, is therefore truly empty, because it is nothing."}, {"id": "E4P58S.2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P58S", "la": "Quae de pudore notanda sunt, colliguntur facile ex iis, quae de misericordia et poenitentia diximus. Hoc tantum addo, quod ut commiseratio, sic etiam pudor, quamvis non sit virtus, bonus tamen est, quatenus indicat, homini, qui pudore suffunditur, cupiditatem inesse honeste vivendi, sicut dolor, qui eatenus bonus dicitur, quatenus indicat, partem laesam nondum esse putrefactam. Quare quamvis homo, quem facti alicuius pudet, revera sit tristis, est tamen perfectior impudenti, qui nullam habet honeste vivendi cupiditatem.", "en": "What should be noted about shame is easily gathered from what we have said about compassion and repentance. I add only this, that, like pity, shame, although it is not a virtue, is still good insofar as it indicates that the man who is suffused with shame has a desire to live honourably, just as pain is called good insofar as it indicates that the injured part is not yet putrefied. So although a man who is ashamed of some deed is in truth sad, he is still more perfect than the shameless man, who has no desire to live honourably."}, {"id": "E4P58S.3", "p": "part4", "l": "4P58S", "la": "Atque haec sunt, quae de affectibus laetitiae et tristitiae notare susceperam. Ad cupiditates quod attinet, hae sane bonae aut malae sunt, quatenus ex bonis aut malis affectibus oriuntur. Sed omnes revera, quatenus ex affectibus, qui passiones sunt, in nobis ingenerantur, caecae sunt (ut facile colligitur ex iis, quae in schol. prop. 44. huius diximus), nec ullius usus essent, si homines facile duci possent, ut ex solo rationis dictamine viverent, ut iam paucis ostendam.", "en": "These are the things I had undertaken to note about the affects of joy and sadness. As for desires, they are certainly good or evil insofar as they arise from good or evil affects. But all of them, insofar as they are generated in us from affects which are passions, are in truth blind (as is easily gathered from what we said in Schol. of Prop. 44 of this Part), nor would they be of any use if men could easily be led to live from the dictate of reason alone, as I shall now show in a few words."}, {"id": "E4P59", "p": "part4", "l": "4P59", "la": "Ad omnes actiones, ad quas ex affectu, qui passio est, determinamur, possumus absque eo a ratione determinari.", "en": "To all the actions to which we are determined from an affect which is a passion, we can be determined by reason without it."}, {"id": "E4P59D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P59D", "la": "Ex ratione agere nihil aliud est (per prop. 3. et defin. 2. P. 3.) quam ea agere, quae ex necessitate nostrae naturae in se sola consideratae sequuntur. At tristitia eatenus mala est, quatenus hanc agendi potentiam minuit vel coercet (per prop. 41. huius). Ergo ex hoc affectu ad nullam actionem possumus determinari, quam non possemus agere, si ratione duceremur.", "en": "To act from reason is nothing but (by Prop. 3 and Def. 2 of Part III) to do those things which follow from the necessity of our nature considered in itself alone. But sadness is evil insofar as it diminishes or restrains this power of acting (by Prop. 41 of this Part). Therefore from this affect we can be determined to no action which we could not do if we were led by reason."}, {"id": "E4P59D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P59D", "la": "Praeterea laetitia eatenus mala est, quatenus impedit, quominus homo ad agendum sit aptus (per prop. 41. et 43. huius) atque adeo eatenus etiam ad nullam actionem determinari possumus, quam non possemus agere, si ratione duceremur. Denique quatenus laetitia bona est, eatenus cum ratione convenit (consistit enim in eo, quod hominis agendi potentia augetur vel iuvatur), nec passio est nisi quatenus hominis agendi potentia non eo usque augetur, ut se suasque actiones adaequate concipiat (per prop. 3. P. 3. cum eius schol.). Quare si homo laetitia affectus ad tantam perfectionem duceretur, ut se suasque actiones adaequate conciperet, ad easdem actiones, ad quas iam ex affectibus, qui passiones sunt, determinatur, aptus, imo aptior esset.", "en": "Further, joy is evil insofar as it prevents a man from being capable of acting (by Props. 41 and 43 of this Part); and so to that extent too we can be determined to no action which we could not do if we were led by reason. Finally, insofar as joy is good, to that extent it agrees with reason (for it consists in this, that a man’s power of acting is increased or aided), and it is a passion only insofar as a man’s power of acting is not increased to the point where he conceives himself and his actions adequately (by Prop. 3 of Part III with its Schol.). So if a man affected with joy were led to such perfection that he conceived himself and his actions adequately, he would be capable, indeed more capable, of the same actions to which he is now determined from affects which are passions."}, {"id": "E4P59D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P59D", "la": "At omnes affectus ad laetitiam, tristitiam vel cupiditatem referuntur (vide explicationem aff. defin. 4.), et cupiditas (per aff. defin. 1.) nihil aliud est, quam ipse agendi conatus. Ergo ad omnes actiones, ad quas ex affectu, qui passio est, determinamur, possumus absque eo sola ratione duci. Q. E. D.", "en": "But all affects are related to joy, sadness or desire (see the explanation of Def. Aff. 4), and desire (by Def. Aff. 1) is nothing but the very striving to act. Therefore to all the actions to which we are determined from an affect which is a passion, we can be led by reason alone without it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P59D2", "p": "part4", "l": "4P59D2", "la": "Actio quaecumque eatenus dicitur mala, quatenus ex eo oritur, quod odio aut aliquo malo affectu affecti sumus (vide coroll. 1. prop. 45. huius). At nulla actio in se sola considerata bona aut mala est (ut in praef. huius ostendimus) sed una eademque actio iam bona, iam mala est. Ergo ad eandem actionem, quae iam mala est, sive quae ex aliquo malo affectu oritur, ratione duci possumus (per prop. 19. huius). Q. E. D.", "en": "Any action is called evil insofar as it arises from the fact that we are affected with hatred or some evil affect (see Cor. 1 of Prop. 45 of this Part). But no action, considered in itself alone, is good or evil (as we showed in the Preface of this Part), but one and the same action is now good, now evil. Therefore we can be led by reason (by Prop. 19 of this Part) to the same action which is now evil, or which arises from some evil affect. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P59S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P59S", "la": "Explicantur haec clarius exemplo. Nempe verberandi actio quatenus physice consideratur, et ad hoc tantum attendimus, quod homo brachium tollit, manum claudit totumque brachium vi deorsum movet, virtus est, quae ex corporis humani fabrica concipitur. Si itaque homo ira vel odio commotus determinatur ad claudendam manum vel brachium movendum, id, ut in parte secunda ostendimus, fit quia una eademque actio potest iungi quibuscumque rerum imaginibus; atque adeo tam ex iis imaginibus rerum, quas confuse, quam quas clare et distincte concipimus, ad unam eandemque actionem determinari possumus.", "en": "These things are explained more clearly by an example. Namely, the action of striking, insofar as it is considered physically, and we attend only to the fact that a man raises his arm, clenches his fist and moves his whole arm forcefully downwards, is a virtue, which is conceived from the structure of the human body. If, therefore, a man moved by anger or hatred is determined to clench his fist or move his arm, this happens, as we showed in Part II, because one and the same action can be joined to any images of things whatever; and so we can be determined to one and the same action as much from the images of things which we conceive confusedly as from those which we conceive clearly and distinctly."}, {"id": "E4P59S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P59S", "la": "Apparet itaque, quod omnis cupiditas, quae ex affectu, qui passio est, oritur, nullius esset usus, si homines ratione duci possent. Videamus iam, cur cupiditas quae ex affectu, qui passio est, oritur, caeca a nobis appellatur.", "en": "It is evident, therefore, that every desire which arises from an affect which is a passion would be of no use if men could be led by reason. Let us now see why a desire which arises from an affect which is a passion is called blind by us."}, {"id": "E4P60", "p": "part4", "l": "4P60", "la": "Cupiditas, quae oritur ex laetitia vel tristitia, quae ad unam vel ad aliquot, non autem ad omnes corporis partes refertur, rationem utilitatis totius hominis non habet.", "en": "A desire which arises from a joy or sadness that is related to one or several, but not to all, of the parts of the body has no regard for the advantage of the whole man."}, {"id": "E4P60D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P60D", "la": "Ponatur ex. gr. corporis pars A vi alicuius causae externae ita corroborari, ut reliquis praevaleat (per prop. 6. huius); haec pars vires suas amittere propterea non conabitur, ut reliquae corporis partes suo fungantur officio. Deberet enim vim seu potentiam habere vires suas amittendi; quod (per prop. 6. P. 3.) est absurdum.", "en": "Suppose, for example, that part A of the body is so strengthened by the force of some external cause that it prevails over the others (by Prop. 6 of this Part); this part will not for that reason strive to lose its powers so that the other parts of the body may perform their function. For it would have to have a force, or power, of losing its powers, which (by Prop. 6 of Part III) is absurd."}, {"id": "E4P60D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P60D", "la": "Conabitur itaque illa pars, et consequenter (per prop. 7. et 12. P. 3.) mens etiam illum statum conservare; adeoque cupiditas, quae ex tali affectu laetitiae oritur, rationem totius non habet. Quod si contra supponatur pars A coerceri, ut reliquae praevaleant, eodem modo demonstratur, quod nec cupiditas, quae ex tristitia oritur, rationem totius habeat. Q. E. D.", "en": "That part will therefore strive, and consequently (by Props. 7 and 12 of Part III) the mind also will strive, to preserve that state; and so the desire which arises from such an affect of joy has no regard for the whole. But if, on the contrary, part A is supposed to be restrained, so that the others prevail, it is demonstrated in the same way that the desire which arises from sadness has no regard for the whole either. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P60S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P60S", "la": "Cum itaque laetitia plerumque (per schol. prop. 44. huius) ad unam corporis partem referatur, cupimus ergo plerumque nostrum esse conservare nulla habita ratione integrae nostrae valetudinis. Ad quod accedit, quod cupiditates quibus maxime tenemur (per coroll. prop. 9. huius), temporis tantum praesentis, non autem futuri habent rationem.", "en": "Since, therefore, joy is generally related to one part of the body (by Schol. of Prop. 44 of this Part), we generally desire to preserve our being without any regard for our health as a whole. Add to this that the desires by which we are most held (by Cor. of Prop. 9 of this Part) have regard only for the present time, not for the future."}, {"id": "E4P61", "p": "part4", "l": "4P61", "la": "Cupiditas, quae ex ratione oritur, excessum habere nequit.", "en": "A desire which arises from reason cannot be excessive."}, {"id": "E4P61D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P61D", "la": "Cupiditas (per aff. defin. 1.) absolute considerata est ipsa hominis essentia, quatenus quocumque modo determinata concipitur ad aliquid agendum. Adeoque cupiditas, quae ex ratione oritur, hoc est (per prop. 3. P. 3.) quae in nobis ingeneratur, quatenus agimus, est ipsa hominis essentia seu natura, quatenus determinata concipitur ad agendum ea, quae per solam hominis essentiam adaequate concipiuntur (per defin. 2. P. 3.). Si itaque haec cupiditas excessum habere posset, posset ergo humana natura in se sola considerata se ipsam excedere, sive plus posset, quam potest, quod manifesta est contradictio. Ac proinde haec cupiditas excessum habere nequit. Q. E. D.", "en": "Desire (by Def. Aff. 1), considered absolutely, is the very essence of man, insofar as it is conceived to be determined in any way to do something. And so a desire which arises from reason, that is (by Prop. 3 of Part III), which is generated in us insofar as we act, is the very essence, or nature, of man insofar as it is conceived to be determined to do those things which are conceived adequately through the essence of man alone (by Def. 2 of Part III). If, therefore, this desire could be excessive, human nature, considered in itself alone, could exceed itself, or could do more than it can, which is a manifest contradiction. And accordingly this desire cannot be excessive. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P62", "p": "part4", "l": "4P62", "la": "Quatenus mens ex rationis dictamine res concipit, aeque afficitur, sive idea sit rei futurae vel praeteritae, sive praesentis.", "en": "Insofar as the mind conceives things from the dictate of reason, it is equally affected whether the idea is of a future or past thing, or of a present one."}, {"id": "E4P62D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P62D", "la": "Quicquid mens ducente ratione concipit, id omne sub eadem aeternitatis seu necessitatis specie concipit (per coroll. 2. prop. 44. P. 2.), eademque certitudine afficitur (per prop. 43. P. 2. et eius schol.). Quare sive idea sit rei futurae vel praeteritae, sive praesentis, mens eadem necessitate rem concipit, eademque certitudine afficitur, et sive idea sit rei futurae vel praeteritae, sive praesentis, erit nihilominus aeque vera (per prop. 41. P. 2.), hoc est (per defin. 4. P. 2.) habebit nihilominus semper easdem ideae adaequatae proprietates. Atque adeo quatenus mens ex rationis dictamine res concipit, eodem modo afficitur, sive idea sit rei futurae vel praeteritae, sive praesentis. Q. E. D.", "en": "Whatever the mind conceives under the guidance of reason, it conceives all of it under the same species of eternity, or necessity (by Cor. 2 of Prop. 44 of Part II), and is affected with the same certainty (by Prop. 43 of Part II and its Schol.). So whether the idea is of a future or past thing, or of a present one, the mind conceives the thing with the same necessity and is affected with the same certainty; and whether the idea is of a future or past thing, or of a present one, it will nevertheless be equally true (by Prop. 41 of Part II), that is (by Def. 4 of Part II), it will nevertheless always have the same properties of an adequate idea. And so, insofar as the mind conceives things from the dictate of reason, it is affected in the same way whether the idea is of a future or past thing, or of a present one. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P62S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P62S", "la": "Si nos de rerum duratione adaequatam cognitionem habere, earumque existendi tempora ratione determinare possemus, eodem affectu res futuras ac praesentes contemplaremur, et bonum, quod mens ut futurum conciperet, perinde ac praesens appeteret, et consequenter bonum praesens minus pro maiore bono futuro necessario negligeret, et quod in praesenti bonum esset, sed causa futuri alicuius mali, minime appeteret, ut mox demonstrabimus.", "en": "If we could have adequate knowledge of the duration of things, and determine their times of existing by reason, we would contemplate future things with the same affect as present ones, and the mind would want the good which it conceived as future just as if it were present; and consequently it would necessarily neglect a lesser present good for a greater future good, and would by no means want what was good in the present but the cause of some future evil, as we shall soon demonstrate."}, {"id": "E4P62S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P62S", "la": "Sed nos de duratione rerum (per prop. 31. P. 2.) non nisi admodum inadaequatam cognitionem habere possumus, et rerum existendi tempora (per schol. prop. 44. P. 2.) sola imaginatione determinamus, quae non aeque afficitur imagine rei praesentis ac futurae. Unde fit, ut vera boni et mali cognitio, quam habemus, non nisi abstracta sive universalis sit, et iudicium, quod de rerum ordine et causarum nexu facimus, ut determinare possimus, quid nobis in praesenti bonum aut malum sit, sit potius imaginarium, quam reale. Atque adeo mirum non est, si cupiditas, quae ex boni et mali cognitione, quatenus haec futurum prospicit, oritur, facilius rerum cupiditate, quae in praesentia suaves sunt, coerceri potest, de quo vide prop. 16. huius partis.", "en": "But we can have only a very inadequate knowledge of the duration of things (by Prop. 31 of Part II), and we determine the times of existing of things by the imagination alone (by Schol. of Prop. 44 of Part II), which is not equally affected by the image of a present and of a future thing. Hence it comes about that the true knowledge of good and evil which we have is only abstract, or universal, and the judgment which we make about the order of things and the connection of causes, in order to determine what is good or evil for us in the present, is imaginary rather than real. And so it is no wonder if a desire which arises from the knowledge of good and evil, insofar as this looks to the future, can be more easily restrained by a desire for things which are pleasant in the present; on which see Prop. 16 of this Part."}, {"id": "E4P63", "p": "part4", "l": "4P63", "la": "Qui metu ducitur et bonum, ut malum vitet, agit, is ratione non ducitur.", "en": "He who is led by fear, and does good in order to avoid evil, is not led by reason."}, {"id": "E4P63D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P63D", "la": "Omnes affectus, qui ad mentem, quatenus agit, hoc est (per prop. 3. P. 3.), qui ad rationem referuntur, nulli alii sunt, quam affectus laetitiae et cupiditatis (per prop. 59. P. 3.). Atque adeo (per aff. defin. 13.) qui metu ducitur et bonum timore mali agit, is ratione non ducitur. Q. E. D.", "en": "All the affects which are related to the mind insofar as it acts, that is (by Prop. 3 of Part III), which are related to reason, are none other than affects of joy and desire (by Prop. 59 of Part III). And so (by Def. Aff. 13) he who is led by fear, and does good from fear of evil, is not led by reason. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P63S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P63S", "la": "Superstitiosi, qui vitia exprobrare magis, quam virtutes docere norunt et qui homines non ratione ducere, sed metu ita continere student, ut malum potius fugiant, quam virtutes ament, nil aliud intendunt, quam ut reliqui aeque ac ipsi fiant miseri; et ideo non mirum, si plerumque molesti et odiosi sint hominibus.", "en": "The superstitious, who know how to reproach vices rather than to teach virtues, and who strive not to lead men by reason but to hold them in check by fear, so that they flee evil rather than love virtues, intend nothing else but that others should become as wretched as they are themselves; and so it is no wonder that they are generally troublesome and hateful to men."}, {"id": "E4P63C", "p": "part4", "l": "4P63C", "la": "Cupiditate, quae ex ratione oritur, bonum directe sequimur et malum indirecte fugimus.", "en": "By a desire which arises from reason we follow the good directly and flee evil indirectly."}, {"id": "E4P63CD", "p": "part4", "l": "4P63CD", "la": "Nam cupiditas, quae ex ratione oritur, ex solo laetitiae affectu, quae passio non est, oriri potest (per prop. 59. P. 3.), hoc est, ex laetitia, quae excessum habere nequit (per prop. 61. huius), non autem ex tristitia. Ac proinde haec cupiditas (per prop. 8. huius) ex cognitione boni, non autem mali oritur atque adeo ex ductu rationis bonum directe appetimus, et eatenus tantum malum fugimus. Q. E. D.", "en": "For a desire which arises from reason can arise only from an affect of joy which is not a passion (by Prop. 59 of Part III), that is, from a joy which cannot be excessive (by Prop. 61 of this Part), and not from sadness. And accordingly this desire (by Prop. 8 of this Part) arises from the knowledge of good, not of evil; and so from the guidance of reason we want the good directly, and only to that extent flee evil. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P63CS", "p": "part4", "l": "4P63CS", "la": "Explicatur hoc corollarium exemplo aegri et sani. Comedit aeger id, quod aversatur, timore mortis; sanus autem cibo gaudet et vita sic melius fruitur, quam si mortem timeret, eamque directe vitare cuperet. Sic iudex qui non odio aut ira etc., sed solo amore salutis publicae reum mortis damnat, sola ratione ducitur.", "en": "This Corollary is explained by the example of the sick man and the healthy man. The sick man eats what he is averse to from fear of death; but the healthy man enjoys his food, and in this way enjoys life better than if he feared death and desired to avoid it directly. So a judge who condemns a guilty man to death not from hatred or anger, etc., but only from love of the public welfare, is led by reason alone."}, {"id": "E4P64", "p": "part4", "l": "4P64", "la": "Cognitio mali cognitio est inadaequata.", "en": "The knowledge of evil is inadequate knowledge."}, {"id": "E4P64D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P64D", "la": "Cognitio mali (per prop. 8. huius) est ipsa tristitia, quatenus eiusdem sumus conscii. Tristitia autem est transitio ad minorem perfectionem (per aff. defin. 3.) quae propterea per ipsam hominis essentiam intelligi nequit (per prop. 6. et 7. P. 3.). Ac proinde (per defin. 2. P. 3.) passio est, quae (per prop. 3. P. 3.) ab ideis inadaequatis pendet, et consequenter (per prop. 29. P. 2.) eius cognitio, nempe mali cognitio, est inadaequata. Q. E. D.", "en": "The knowledge of evil (by Prop. 8 of this Part) is sadness itself, insofar as we are conscious of it. But sadness is a passage to a lesser perfection (by Def. Aff. 3), which therefore cannot be understood through the very essence of man (by Props. 6 and 7 of Part III). And accordingly (by Def. 2 of Part III) it is a passion, which (by Prop. 3 of Part III) depends on inadequate ideas, and consequently (by Prop. 29 of Part II) the knowledge of it, namely the knowledge of evil, is inadequate. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P64C", "p": "part4", "l": "4P64C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, quod si mens humana non nisi adaequatas haberet ideas, nullam mali formaret notionem.", "en": "Hence it follows that if the human mind had only adequate ideas, it would form no notion of evil."}, {"id": "E4P65", "p": "part4", "l": "4P65", "la": "De duobus bonis maius et de duobus malis minus ex rationis ductu sequemur.", "en": "Of two goods we shall follow, from the guidance of reason, the greater, and of two evils the lesser."}, {"id": "E4P65D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P65D", "la": "Bonum quod impedit, quominus maiore bono fruamur, est revera malum; malum enim et bonum (ut in praef. huius partis ostendimus) de rebus dicitur, quatenus easdem ad invicem comparamus, et (per eandem rationem) malum minus revera bonum est. Quare (per coroll. prop. 63. huius) ex rationis ductu bonum tantum maius et malum minus appetemus seu sequemur. Q. E. D.", "en": "A good which prevents us from enjoying a greater good is in truth an evil; for evil and good (as we showed in the Preface of this Part) are said of things insofar as we compare them with one another; and (for the same reason) a lesser evil is in truth a good. So (by Cor. of Prop. 63 of this Part) from the guidance of reason we shall want, or follow, only the greater good and the lesser evil. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P65C", "p": "part4", "l": "4P65C", "la": "Malum minus pro maiore bono ex rationis ductu sequemur, et bonum minus, quod causa est maioris mali, negligemus. Nam malum quod hic dicitur minus, revera bonum est, et bonum contra malum; quare (per coroll. prop. 63. huius) illud appetemus, et hoc negligemus. Q. E. D.", "en": "From the guidance of reason we shall follow a lesser evil for the sake of a greater good, and neglect a lesser good which is the cause of a greater evil. For the evil which is here called lesser is in truth a good, and the good, on the contrary, an evil; so (by Cor. of Prop. 63 of this Part) we shall want the former and neglect the latter. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P66", "p": "part4", "l": "4P66", "la": "Bonum maius futurum prae minore praesenti, et malum praesens minus prae maiore futuro ex rationis ductu appetemus.", "en": "From the guidance of reason we shall want a greater future good in preference to a lesser present one, and a lesser present evil in preference to a greater future one."}, {"id": "E4P66D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P66D", "la": "Si mens rei futurae adaequatam posset habere cognitionem, eodem affectu erga rem futuram ac erga praesentem afficeretur (per prop. 62. huius). Quare quatenus ad ipsam rationem attendimus, ut in hac propositione nos facere supponimus, res eadem est, sive maius bonum vel malum futurum, sive praesens supponatur. Ac proinde (per prop. 65. huius) bonum futurum maius prae minore praesenti etc. appetemus. Q. E. D.", "en": "If the mind could have adequate knowledge of a future thing, it would be affected with the same affect towards a future thing as towards a present one (by Prop. 62 of this Part). So insofar as we attend to reason itself, as we suppose ourselves to do in this proposition, the thing is the same whether the greater good or evil is supposed to be future or present. And accordingly (by Prop. 65 of this Part) we shall want a greater future good in preference to a lesser present one, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P66C", "p": "part4", "l": "4P66C", "la": "Malum praesens minus, quod est causa maioris futuri boni, ex rationis ductu appetemus, et bonum praesens minus, quod causa est maioris futuri mali, negligemus. Hoc coroll. se habet ad praeced. prop. ut coroll. prop. 65. ad ipsam prop. 65.", "en": "From the guidance of reason we shall want a lesser present evil which is the cause of a greater future good, and neglect a lesser present good which is the cause of a greater future evil. This Corollary is related to the preceding Prop. as the Cor. of Prop. 65 is to Prop. 65 itself."}, {"id": "E4P66S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P66S", "la": "Si igitur haec cum iis conferantur, quae in hac parte usque ad prop. 18. de affectuum viribus ostendimus, facile videbimus, quid homo, qui solo affectu seu opinione, homini, qui ratione ducitur, intersit. Ille enim, velit nolit, ea quae maxime ignorat, agit; hic autem nemini nisi sibi morem gerit, et ea tantum agit, quae in vita prima esse novit, quaeque propterea maxime cupit; et ideo illum *servum*, hunc autem *liberum* voco, de cuius ingenio et vivendi ratione pauca adhuc notare libet.", "en": "If, therefore, these things are compared with those which we have shown in this Part up to Prop. 18 about the strength of the affects, we shall easily see the difference between a man who is led by affect alone, or opinion, and a man who is led by reason. For the former, whether he will or not, does the things of which he is most ignorant; but the latter complies with no one’s wishes but his own, and does only those things which he knows to be most important in life, and which he therefore desires most; and so I call the former a slave, but the latter free; about whose temperament and way of life I should like to note a few things more."}, {"id": "E4P67", "p": "part4", "l": "4P67", "la": "Homo liber de nulla re minus, quam de morte cogitat, et eius sapientia non mortis, sed vitae meditatio est.", "en": "A free man thinks of nothing less than of death, and his wisdom is a meditation not on death but on life."}, {"id": "E4P67D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P67D", "la": "Homo liber, hoc est, qui ex solo rationis dictamine vivit, mortis metu non ducitur (per prop. 63. huius), sed bonum directe cupit (per coroll. eiusdem prop.), hoc est (per prop. 24. huius) agere, vivere, suum esse conservare ex fundamento proprium utile quaerendi. Atque adeo nihil minus, quam de morte cogitat, sed eius sapientia vitae est meditatio. Q. E. D.", "en": "A free man, that is, one who lives from the dictate of reason alone, is not led by fear of death (by Prop. 63 of this Part), but desires the good directly (by Cor. of the same Prop.), that is (by Prop. 24 of this Part), to act, to live, to preserve his being from the foundation of seeking his own advantage. And so he thinks of nothing less than of death, but his wisdom is a meditation on life. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P68", "p": "part4", "l": "4P68", "la": "Si homines liberi nascerentur, nullum boni et mali formarent conceptum, quamdiu liberi essent.", "en": "If men were born free, they would form no concept of good and evil so long as they remained free."}, {"id": "E4P68D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P68D", "la": "Illum liberum esse dixi, qui sola ducitur ratione. Qui itaque liber nascitur et liber manet, non nisi adaequatas ideas habet, ac proinde mali conceptum habet nullum (per coroll. prop. 64. huius), et consequenter (nam bonum et malum correlata sunt) neque boni. Q. E. D.", "en": "I have said that he is free who is led by reason alone. He, therefore, who is born free and remains free has only adequate ideas, and accordingly has no concept of evil (by Cor. of Prop. 64 of this Part), and consequently (for good and evil are correlative) none of good either. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P68S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P68S", "la": "Huius propositionis hypothesin falsam esse, nec posse concipi, nisi quatenus ad solam naturam humanam, seu potius ad Deum attendimus, non quatenus infinitus, sed quatenus tantummodo causa est, cur homo existat, patet ex prop. 4. huius partis. Atque hoc et alia, quae iam demonstravimus, videntur a Mose significari in illa primi hominis historia.", "en": "It is evident from Prop. 4 of this Part that the hypothesis of this proposition is false and cannot be conceived except insofar as we attend to human nature alone, or rather to God, not insofar as he is infinite, but only insofar as he is the cause of man’s existing. And this, and other things which we have now demonstrated, seem to have been signified by Moses in that history of the first man."}, {"id": "E4P68S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P68S", "la": "In ea enim nulla alia Dei potentia concipitur, quam illa, qua hominem creavit, hoc est, potentia, qua hominis solummodo utilitati consuluit, atque eatenus narratur, quod Deus homini libero prohibuerit, ne de arbore cognitionis boni et mali comederet, et quod, simulac de ea comederet, statim mortem metueret potius, quam vivere cuperet.", "en": "For in it no other power of God is conceived than that by which he created man, that is, a power by which he consulted only man’s advantage; and to that extent it is narrated that God forbade the free man to eat of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, and that as soon as he ate of it, he would at once fear death rather than desire to live."}, {"id": "E4P68S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P68S", "la": "Deinde, quod inventa ab homine uxore, quae cum sua natura prorsus conveniebat, cognovit nihil posse in natura dari, quod ipsi posset illa esse utilius; sed quod, postquam bruta sibi similia esse credidit, statim eorum affectus imitari inceperit (vide prop. 27. P. 3.) et libertatem suam amittere, quam patriarchae postea recuperaverunt, ducti spiritu Christi, hoc est, Dei idea, a qua sola pendet, ut homo liber sit, et ut bonum quod sibi cupit, reliquis hominibus cupiat, ut supra (per prop. 37. huius) demonstravimus.", "en": "Next, it is narrated that when the man had found a wife who agreed entirely with his nature, he knew that there could be nothing in nature which could be more useful to him than she; but that after he believed the beasts to be like himself, he at once began to imitate their affects (see Prop. 27 of Part III) and to lose his freedom, which the patriarchs afterwards recovered, led by the spirit of Christ, that is, by the idea of God, on which alone it depends that man is free, and that he desires for other men the good which he desires for himself, as we have demonstrated above (by Prop. 37 of this Part)."}, {"id": "E4P69", "p": "part4", "l": "4P69", "la": "Hominis liberi virtus aeque magna cernitur in declinandis, quam in superandis periculis.", "en": "The virtue of a free man is seen to be as great in declining dangers as in overcoming them."}, {"id": "E4P69D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P69D", "la": "Affectus coerceri nec tolli potest, nisi affectu contrario et fortiore affectu coercendo (per prop. 7. huius). At caeca audacia et metus affectus sunt, qui aeque magni possunt concipi (per prop. 5. et 3. huius). Ergo aeque magna animi virtus seu fortitudo (huius defin. vide in schol. prop. 59. P. 3.) requiritur ad audaciam, quam ad metum coercendum, hoc est (per aff. defin. 40. et 41.) homo liber eadem animi virtute pericula declinat, qua eadem superare tentat. Q. E. D.", "en": "An affect can be neither restrained nor removed except by an affect contrary to and stronger than the affect to be restrained (by Prop. 7 of this Part). But blind daring and fear are affects which can be conceived to be equally great (by Props. 5 and 3 of this Part). Therefore an equally great virtue of mind, or strength of character (whose definition see in Schol. of Prop. 59 of Part III), is required to restrain daring as to restrain fear, that is (by Defs. Aff. 40 and 41), the free man declines dangers by the same virtue of mind by which he tries to overcome them. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P69C", "p": "part4", "l": "4P69C", "la": "Homini igitur libero aeque magnae animositati fuga in tempore, ac pugna ducitur; sive homo liber eadem animositate seu animi praesentia, qua certamen, fugam eligit.", "en": "To a free man, therefore, timely flight is ascribed to as great a tenacity as battle; or, the free man chooses flight with the same tenacity, or presence of mind, as combat."}, {"id": "E4P69S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P69S", "la": "Quid animositas sit, vel quid per ipsam intelligam, in schol. prop. 59. P. 3. explicui. Per *periculum* autem id omne intelligo, quod potest esse causa alicuius mali, nempe tristitiae, odii, discordiae etc.", "en": "What tenacity is, or what I understand by it, I have explained in Schol. of Prop. 59 of Part III. But by danger I understand everything which can be the cause of some evil, namely of sadness, hatred, discord, etc."}, {"id": "E4P70", "p": "part4", "l": "4P70", "la": "Homo liber qui inter ignaros vivit, eorum, quantum potest, beneficia declinare studet.", "en": "A free man who lives among the ignorant strives as far as he can to decline their favours."}, {"id": "E4P70D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P70D", "la": "Unusquisque ex suo ingenio iudicat, quid bonum sit (vide schol. prop. 39. P. 3.). Ignarus igitur, qui in aliquem beneficium contulit, id ex suo ingenio aestimabit, et si minoris ab eo, cui datum est, aestimari videt, contristabitur (per prop. 42. P. 3.).", "en": "Each one judges from his own temperament what is good (see Schol. of Prop. 39 of Part III). An ignorant man, therefore, who has conferred a favour on someone, will value it according to his own temperament, and if he sees that it is valued less by the one to whom it was given, he will be saddened (by Prop. 42 of Part III)."}, {"id": "E4P70D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P70D", "la": "At homo liber reliquos homines amicitia sibi iungere (per prop. 37. huius), nec paria hominibus beneficia ex eorum affectu referre, sed se et reliquos libero rationis iudicio ducere et ea tantum agere studet, quae ipse prima esse novit. Ergo homo liber, ne ignaris odio sit, et ne eorum appetitui, sed soli rationi obsequatur, eorum beneficia, quantum potest, declinare conabitur. Q. E. D.", "en": "But the free man strives to join other men to himself in friendship (by Prop. 37 of this Part), and not to repay men with favours that are equal by the measure of their affect, but to lead himself and others by the free judgment of reason, and to do only those things which he himself knows to be most important. Therefore the free man, so as not to be hated by the ignorant, and so as to comply not with their appetite but with reason alone, will strive as far as he can to decline their favours. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P70S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P70S", "la": "Dico quantum potest. Nam quamvis homines ignari sint, sunt tamen homines, qui in necessitatibus humanum auxilium, quo nullum praestabilius est, adferre queunt. Atque adeo saepe fit, ut necesse sit ab iisdem beneficium accipere, et consequenter iisdem contra ex eorum ingenio congratulari. Ad quod accedit, quod etiam in declinandis beneficiis cautio esse debet, ne videamur eosdem contemnere, vel prae avaritia remunerationem timere, atque ita, dum eorum odium fugimus, eo ipso in eorum offensionem incurramus. Quare in declinandis beneficiis ratio utilis et honesti habenda est.", "en": "I say as far as he can. For although men are ignorant, they are still men, who in times of need can bring human help, than which nothing is more excellent. And so it often happens that it is necessary to accept a favour from them, and consequently to thank them in return according to their temperament. Add to this that caution must be exercised even in declining favours, so that we do not seem to despise them, or to fear repayment out of greed, and so, while we flee their hatred, by that very fact incur their offence. So in declining favours account must be taken of what is useful and honourable."}, {"id": "E4P71", "p": "part4", "l": "4P71", "la": "Soli homines liberi erga invicem gratissimi sunt.", "en": "Only free men are most grateful to one another."}, {"id": "E4P71D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P71D", "la": "Soli homines liberi sibi invicem utilissimi sunt et maxima amicitiae necessitudine invicem iunguntur (per prop. 35. huius et eius coroll. 1.), parique amoris studio sibi invicem benefacere conantur (per prop. 37. huius). Adeoque (per aff. defin. 34.) soli homines liberi erga se invicem gratissimi sunt. Q. E. D.", "en": "Only free men are most useful to one another and are joined to one another by the closest bond of friendship (by Prop. 35 of this Part and its Cor. 1), and strive to do good to one another with equal zeal of love (by Prop. 37 of this Part). And so (by Def. Aff. 34) only free men are most grateful to one another. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P71S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P71S", "la": "Gratia, quam homines, qui caeca cupiditate ducuntur, invicem habent, mercatura seu aucupium potius, quam gratia plerumque est. Porro ingratitudo affectus non est. Est tamen ingratitudo turpis, quia plerumque hominem nimio odio, ira vel superbia vel avaritia etc. affectum esse indicat.", "en": "The thankfulness which men led by blind desire have for one another is for the most part a bargain or a hunt for gain rather than thankfulness. Further, ingratitude is not an affect. Yet ingratitude is base, because it generally indicates that a man is affected with excessive hatred, anger, or pride, or greed, etc."}, {"id": "E4P71S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P71S", "la": "Nam qui prae stultitia dona compensare nescit, ingratus non est, et multo minus ille, qui donis non movetur meretricis, ut ipsius libidini inserviat, nec furis, ut ipsius furta celet, vel alterius similis. Nam hic contra animum habere constantem ostendit, qui scilicet se nullis donis ad suam vel communem perniciem patitur corrumpi.", "en": "For he who from foolishness does not know how to repay gifts is not ungrateful, and much less he who is not moved by the gifts of a courtesan to serve her lust, nor by those of a thief to conceal his thefts, or by those of anyone similar. For such a man, on the contrary, shows that he has a constant mind, namely one which does not allow itself to be corrupted by any gifts to its own or the common ruin."}, {"id": "E4P72", "p": "part4", "l": "4P72", "la": "Homo liber nunquam dolo malo, sed semper cum fide agit.", "en": "A free man never acts deceptively, but always in good faith."}, {"id": "E4P72D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P72D", "la": "Si liber homo quicquam dolo malo, quatenus liber est, ageret, id ex dictamine rationis ageret (nam eatenus tantum liber a nobis appellatur); atque adeo dolo malo agere virtus esset (per prop. 24. huius), et consequenter (per eandem prop.) unicuique ad suum esse conservandum consultius esset, dolo malo agere, hoc est (ut per se notum) hominibus consultius esset verbis solummodo convenire, re autem invicem esse contrarios; quod (per coroll. prop. 31. huius) est absurdum. Ergo homo liber etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "If a free man, insofar as he is free, did anything deceptively, he would do it from the dictate of reason (for only to that extent do we call him free); and so to act deceptively would be a virtue (by Prop. 24 of this Part), and consequently (by the same Prop.) it would be more advisable for everyone, for preserving his being, to act deceptively, that is (as is self-evident), it would be more advisable for men to agree only in words, but in fact to be contrary to one another, which (by Cor. of Prop. 31 of this Part) is absurd. Therefore a free man, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P72S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P72S", "la": "Si iam quaeratur: “Quid si homo se perfidia a praesenti mortis periculo posset liberare, an non ratio suum esse conservandi omnino suadet ut perfidus sit?” respondebitur eodem modo: “Quod si ratio id suadeat, suadet ergo id omnibus hominibus, atque adeo ratio omnino suadet hominibus, ne nisi dolo malo paciscantur, vires coniungere et iura habere communia, hoc est, ne revera iura habeant communia, quod est absurdum.”", "en": "If it is now asked, ‘What if a man could free himself by perfidy from a present danger of death? Would not reason, for preserving his being, entirely advise him to be perfidious?’, the answer will be the same: ‘If reason advises this, it therefore advises it to all men, and so reason entirely advises men to make no agreements to join their forces and to have common rights except deceptively, that is, not really to have common rights, which is absurd.’"}, {"id": "E4P73", "p": "part4", "l": "4P73", "la": "Homo, qui ratione ducitur, magis in civitate, ubi ex communi decreto vivit, quam in solitudine, ubi sibi soli obtemperat, liber est.", "en": "A man who is led by reason is more free in a state, where he lives according to the common decree, than in solitude, where he obeys himself alone."}, {"id": "E4P73D", "p": "part4", "l": "4P73D", "la": "Homo, qui ratione ducitur, non ducitur metu ad obtemperandum (per prop. 63. huius), sed quatenus suum esse ex rationis dictamine conservare conatur, hoc est (per schol. prop. 66. huius) quatenus libere vivere conatur, communis vitae et utilitatis rationem tenere (per prop. 37. huius), et consequenter (ut in schol. 2. prop. 37. huius ostendimus) ex communi civitatis decreto vivere cupit. Cupit ergo homo, qui ratione ducitur, ut liberius vivat, communia civitatis iura tenere. Q. E. D.", "en": "A man who is led by reason is not led to obey by fear (by Prop. 63 of this Part); but insofar as he strives to preserve his being from the dictate of reason, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 66 of this Part), insofar as he strives to live freely, he desires to keep to the principle of common life and common advantage (by Prop. 37 of this Part), and consequently (as we showed in Schol. 2 of Prop. 37 of this Part) to live according to the common decree of the state. A man who is led by reason desires, therefore, in order to live more freely, to keep the common laws of the state. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E4P73S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P73S", "la": "Haec et similia, quae de *vera hominis libertate* ostendimus, ad fortitudinem, hoc est (per schol. prop. 59. P. 3.) ad animositatem et generositatem referuntur. Nec operae pretium duco, omnes fortitudinis proprietates hic separatim demonstrare, et multo minus, quod vir fortis neminem odio habeat, nemini irascatur, invideat, indignetur, neminem despiciat minimeque superbiat. Nam haec et omnia, quae ad *veram vitam* et *religionem* spectant, facile ex prop. 37. et 46. huius partis convincuntur; nempe quod odium amore contra vincendum sit, et quod unusquisque, qui ratione ducitur, bonum quod sibi appetit, reliquis etiam ut sit, cupiat.", "en": "These and similar things which we have shown about the true freedom of man are related to strength of character, that is (by Schol. of Prop. 59 of Part III), to tenacity and nobility. Nor do I think it worthwhile to demonstrate here separately all the properties of strength of character, and much less that a strong man hates no one, is angry with no one, envies no one, is indignant with no one, despises no one, and is least of all proud. For these things, and all that concern true life and religion, are easily proved from Props. 37 and 46 of this Part: namely, that hatred is to be conquered by love in return, and that each one who is led by reason desires for others also the good which he wants for himself."}, {"id": "E4P73S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P73S", "la": "Ad quod accedit id, quod in schol. prop. 50. huius partis et aliis in locis notavimus, quod scilicet vir fortis hoc apprime consideret, nempe quod omnia ex necessitate divinae naturae sequantur, ac proinde quicquid molestum et malum esse cogitat et quicquid praeterea impium, horrendum, iniustum et turpe videtur, ex eo oritur, quod res ipsas perturbate, mutilate et confuse concipit; et hac de causa apprime conatur res, ut in se sunt, concipere et verae cognitionis impedimenta amovere, ut sunt odium, ira, invidia, irrisio, superbia et reliqua huiusmodi, quae in praecedentibus notavimus; atque adeo, quantum potest, conatur, uti diximus, bene agere et laetari.", "en": "Add to this what we noted in Schol. of Prop. 50 of this Part and in other places, namely that the strong man considers this above all, that all things follow from the necessity of the divine nature, and accordingly that whatever he thinks troublesome and evil, and whatever besides seems impious, horrible, unjust and base, arises from his conceiving things in a disturbed, mutilated and confused way; and for this reason he strives above all to conceive things as they are in themselves, and to remove the obstacles to true knowledge, such as hatred, anger, envy, mockery, pride and the rest of this kind which we noted in what precedes; and so, as far as he can, he strives, as we have said, to act well and to rejoice."}, {"id": "E4P73S", "p": "part4", "l": "4P73S", "la": "Quousque autem humana virtus ad haec consequenda se extendat et quid possit, in sequenti parte demonstrabo.", "en": "But how far human virtue extends in attaining these things, and what it can do, I shall demonstrate in the following Part."}, {"id": "E4App", "p": "part4", "l": "4App", "la": "Quae in hac parte de recta vivendi ratione tradidi, non sunt ita disposita, ut uno aspectu videri possint, sed disperse a me demonstrata sunt, prout scilicet unum ex alio facilius deducere potuerim. Eadem igitur hic recolligere et ad summa capita redigere proposui.", "en": "The things I have taught in this Part about the right way of living have not been so arranged that they could be seen at a glance, but have been demonstrated by me piecemeal, namely as I could more easily deduce one from another. I have proposed, therefore, to collect them here and to bring them under main headings."}, {"id": "E4App1", "p": "part4", "l": "4App1", "la": "Omnes nostri conatus seu cupiditates ex necessitate nostrae naturae ita sequuntur, ut vel per ipsam solam tamquam per proximam suam causam possint intelligi vel quatenus naturae sumus pars, quae per se absque aliis individuis non potest adaequate concipi.", "en": "All our strivings, or desires, follow from the necessity of our nature in such a way that they can be understood either through it alone, as through their proximate cause, or insofar as we are a part of nature which cannot be conceived adequately through itself without other individuals."}, {"id": "E4App2", "p": "part4", "l": "4App2", "la": "Cupiditates, quae ex nostra natura ita sequuntur, ut per ipsam solam possit intelligi, sunt illae quae ad mentem referuntur, quatenus haec ideis adaequatis constare concipitur; reliquae vero cupiditates ad mentem non referuntur, nisi quatenus res inadaequate concipit, et quarum vis et incrementum non humana, sed rerum, quae extra nos sunt, potentia definiri debet. Et ideo illae recte *actiones*, hae autem *passiones* vocantur. Illae namque nostram potentiam semper indicant, et hae contra nostram impotentiam et mutilatam cognitionem.", "en": "Desires which follow from our nature in such a way that they can be understood through it alone are those which are related to the mind insofar as it is conceived to consist of adequate ideas; the other desires are not related to the mind except insofar as it conceives things inadequately, and their force and increase must be defined not by human power but by the power of things which are outside us. And so the former are rightly called actions, and the latter passions. For the former always indicate our power, and the latter, on the contrary, our lack of power and mutilated knowledge."}, {"id": "E4App3", "p": "part4", "l": "4App3", "la": "Nostrae actiones, hoc est, cupiditates illae, quae hominis potentia seu ratione definiuntur, semper bonae sunt; reliquae autem tam bonae, quam malae possunt esse.", "en": "Our actions, that is, those desires which are defined by man’s power, or reason, are always good; the others can be good as well as evil."}, {"id": "E4App4", "p": "part4", "l": "4App4", "la": "In vita itaque apprime utile est, intellectum seu rationem, quantum possumus, perficere, et in hoc uno summa hominis felicitas seu beatitudo consistit; quippe *beatitudo* nihil aliud est, quam ipsa animi acquiescentia, quae ex Dei intuitiva cognitione oritur. At intellectum perficere nihil etiam aliud est, quam Deum Deique attributa et actiones, quae ex ipsius naturae necessitate consequuntur, intelligere. Quare hominis, qui ratione ducitur, finis ultimus, hoc est, summa cupiditas, qua reliquas omnes moderari studet, est illa, qua fertur ad se resque omnes, quae sub ipsius intelligentiam cadere possunt, adaequate concipiendum.", "en": "In life, therefore, it is above all useful to perfect the intellect, or reason, as far as we can, and in this one thing man’s highest happiness, or blessedness, consists; for blessedness is nothing but the very contentment of mind which arises from the intuitive knowledge of God. But to perfect the intellect is also nothing else but to understand God, and the attributes and actions of God which follow from the necessity of his nature. So the final end of a man who is led by reason, that is, the highest desire by which he strives to moderate all the others, is the one by which he is carried to conceive adequately himself and all the things which can fall within his understanding."}, {"id": "E4App5", "p": "part4", "l": "4App5", "la": "Nulla igitur *vita rationalis* est sine intelligentia, et res eatenus tantum bonae sunt, quatenus hominem iuvant, ut mentis vita fruatur, quae *intelligentia* definitur. Quae autem contra impediunt, quominus homo rationem perficere et rationali vita frui possit, eas solummodo malas esse dicimus.", "en": "There is, therefore, no rational life without understanding, and things are good only insofar as they aid man to enjoy the life of the mind, which is defined by understanding. Those things, on the contrary, which prevent man from being able to perfect his reason and to enjoy the rational life, those only we say are evil."}, {"id": "E4App6", "p": "part4", "l": "4App6", "la": "Sed quia omnia illa, quorum homo efficiens est causa, necessario bona sunt, nihil ergo mali homini evenire potest, nisi a causis externis; nempe quatenus pars est totius naturae, cuius legibus humana natura obtemperare, et cui infinitis modis pene sese accommodare cogitur.", "en": "But because all those things of which man is the efficient cause are necessarily good, no evil can happen to man except from external causes, namely insofar as he is a part of the whole of nature, whose laws human nature is compelled to obey, and to which it is compelled to accommodate itself in almost infinite ways."}, {"id": "E4App7", "p": "part4", "l": "4App7", "la": "Nec fieri potest, ut homo non sit naturae pars, et communem eius ordinem non sequatur; sed si inter talia individua versetur, quae cum ipsius hominis natura conveniunt, eo ipso hominis agendi potentia iuvabitur et fovebitur. At si contra inter talia sit, quae cum ipsius natura minime conveniunt, vix absque magna ipsius mutatione iisdem sese accommodare poterit.", "en": "And it cannot happen that man should not be a part of nature and not follow its common order; but if he lives among such individuals as agree with man’s nature, by that very fact his power of acting will be aided and encouraged. But if, on the contrary, he is among such as agree very little with his nature, he will hardly be able to accommodate himself to them without a great change in himself."}, {"id": "E4App8", "p": "part4", "l": "4App8", "la": "Quicquid in rerum natura datur, quod iudicamus *malum* esse, sive posse impedire, quominus existere et vita rationali frui queamus, id a nobis removere ea via, quae securior videtur, licet; et quicquid contra datur quod iudicamus *bonum* sive *utile* esse ad nostrum esse conservandum, et vita rationali fruendum, id ad nostrum usum capere et eo quocumque modo uti nobis licet. Et absolute id unicuique summo naturae iure facere licet, quod ad ipsius utilitatem conferre iudicat.", "en": "Whatever there is in the nature of things which we judge to be evil, or able to prevent us from existing and enjoying the rational life, it is permissible for us to remove from ourselves by the way which seems safer; and whatever, on the contrary, there is which we judge to be good, or useful, for preserving our being and enjoying the rational life, it is permissible for us to take for our use and to use in any way whatever. And absolutely, it is permissible for each one, by the highest right of nature, to do what he judges to contribute to his advantage."}, {"id": "E4App9", "p": "part4", "l": "4App9", "la": "Nihil magis cum natura alicuius rei convenire potest, quam reliqua eiusdem speciei individua; adeoque (per cap. 7.) nihil homini ad suum esse conservandum et vita rationali fruendum utilius datur, quam *homo, qui ratione ducitur*. Deinde quia inter res singulares nihil novimus, quod homine, qui ratione ducitur, sit praestantius, nulla ergo re magis potest unusquisque ostendere, quantum arte et ingenio valeat, quam in hominibus ita educandis, ut tandem ex proprio rationis imperio vivant.", "en": "Nothing can agree more with the nature of any thing than other individuals of the same species; and so (by ch. 7) nothing is more useful to man for preserving his being and enjoying the rational life than a man who is led by reason. Next, because among singular things we know nothing more excellent than a man who is led by reason, there is no way in which each one can better show how much he is worth in skill and talent than by so educating men that they finally live under the rule of their own reason."}, {"id": "E4App10", "p": "part4", "l": "4App10", "la": "Quatenus homines invidia aut aliquo odii affectu in se invicem feruntur, eatenus invicem contrarii sunt, et consequenter eo magis timendi, quo plus possunt, quam reliqua naturae individua.", "en": "Insofar as men are carried against one another by envy or some affect of hatred, to that extent they are contrary to one another, and consequently are the more to be feared, the more they can do than the other individuals of nature."}, {"id": "E4App11", "p": "part4", "l": "4App11", "la": "Animi tamen non armis, sed *amore* et *generositate* vincuntur.", "en": "Minds, however, are conquered not by arms, but by love and nobility."}, {"id": "E4App12", "p": "part4", "l": "4App12", "la": "Hominibus apprime utile est, *consuetudines* iungere, seseque iis vinculis astringere, quibus aptius de se omnibus unum efficiant, et absolute ea agere, quae firmandis amicitiis inserviunt.", "en": "It is above all useful to men to form close associations, and to bind themselves by those bonds by which they may more aptly make one out of all of them, and absolutely to do those things which serve to strengthen friendships."}, {"id": "E4App13", "p": "part4", "l": "4App13", "la": "Sed ad haec *ars* et *vigilantia* requiritur. Sunt enim homines varii (nam rari sunt, qui ex rationis praescripto vivunt), et tamen plerumque invidi et magis ad vindictam, quam ad misericordiam proclives. Unumquemque igitur ex ipsius ingenio ferre et sese continere, ne eorum affectus imitetur, singularis animi potentiae opus est.", "en": "But for this skill and watchfulness are required. For men are changeable (for those who live by the prescription of reason are rare), and yet for the most part envious and more inclined to vengeance than to compassion. So to bear with each one according to his temperament, and to restrain oneself so as not to imitate their affects, is a work of singular power of mind."}, {"id": "E4App13", "p": "part4", "l": "4App13", "la": "At qui contra homines carpere et vitia potius exprobrare, quam virtutes docere et hominum animos non firmare, sed frangere norunt, ii et sibi et reliquis molesti sunt. Unde multi prae nimia scilicet animi impatientia falsoque religionis studio inter bruta potius, quam inter homines vivere maluerunt; ut pueri vel adolescentes, qui parentum iurgia aequo animo ferre nequeunt, militatum confugiunt et incommoda belli et imperium tyrannidis prae domesticis commodis et paternis admonitionibus eligunt, et quidvis oneris sibi imponi patiuntur, dummodo parentes ulciscantur.", "en": "But those who, on the contrary, know how to find fault with men, and to reproach vices rather than teach virtues, and to break men’s minds rather than strengthen them, are troublesome both to themselves and to others. Hence many, no doubt from excessive impatience of mind and a false zeal for religion, have preferred to live among the beasts rather than among men; like boys or young men who cannot bear their parents’ scolding with equanimity, run off to the army, choose the hardships of war and the rule of a tyrant over the comforts of home and paternal admonitions, and allow any burden whatever to be imposed on them, provided they can take revenge on their parents."}, {"id": "E4App14", "p": "part4", "l": "4App14", "la": "Quamvis igitur homines omnia plerumque ex sua libidine moderentur, ex eorum tamen *communi societate* multo plura commoda, quam damna sequuntur. Quare satius est eorum iniurias aequo animo ferre et studium iis adhibere, quae concordiae et amicitiae conciliandae inserviunt.", "en": "Although, therefore, men generally govern everything by their own lust, still far more advantages than harms follow from their common society. So it is better to bear their injuries with equanimity, and to apply our zeal to those things which serve to bring about concord and friendship."}, {"id": "E4App15", "p": "part4", "l": "4App15", "la": "Quae *concordiam* gignunt sunt illa, quae ad *iustitiam, aequitatem* et *honestatem* referuntur. Nam homines praeter id, quod iniustum et iniquum est, etiam aegre ferunt, quod turpe habetur, sive quod aliquis receptos civitatis mores aspernatur. Amori autem conciliando illa apprime necessaria sunt, quae ad *religionem* et *pietatem* spectant. De quibus vide schol. 1. et 2. prop. 37. et schol. prop. 46. et schol. prop. 73. P. 4.", "en": "The things which beget concord are those which are related to justice, equity and honourableness. For besides what is unjust and inequitable, men also take badly what is held to be base, or the fact that someone spurns the accepted customs of the state. But for winning love, those things are above all necessary which concern religion and piety. On these see Schols. 1 and 2 of Prop. 37, Schol. of Prop. 46, and Schol. of Prop. 73 of Part IV."}, {"id": "E4App16", "p": "part4", "l": "4App16", "la": "Solet praeterea concordia ex metu plerumque gigni; sed sine fide. Adde, quod metus ex animi impotentia oritur et propterea ad rationis usum non pertinet, ut nec commiseratio, quamvis pietatis speciem prae se ferre videatur.", "en": "Concord, moreover, is usually begotten from fear, but without good faith. Add that fear arises from lack of power of mind, and therefore does not pertain to the use of reason; nor does pity, although it seems to present a show of piety."}, {"id": "E4App17", "p": "part4", "l": "4App17", "la": "Vincuntur praeterea homines etiam largitate, praecipue ii, qui non habent unde comparare possint illa, quae ad vitam sustentandam necessaria sunt. Attamen unicuique indigenti auxilium ferre, vires et utilitatem viri privati longe superat. Divitiae namque viri privati longe impares sunt ad id suppeditandum. Unius praeterea viri facultas limitatior est, quam ut omnes sibi possit amicitia iungere; quare *pauperum cura* integrae societati incumbit et ad communem tantum utilitatem spectat.", "en": "Men are also conquered by largesse, especially those who do not have the means to procure what is necessary to sustain life. Yet to bring help to everyone in need far surpasses the powers and the advantage of a private man. For the riches of a private man are far from sufficient to supply it. Moreover, one man’s capacity is too limited for him to be able to join all men to himself in friendship; so care of the poor falls on the whole of society and concerns only the common advantage."}, {"id": "E4App18", "p": "part4", "l": "4App18", "la": "In beneficiis accipiendis et gratia referenda alia prorsus debet esse cura, de qua vide schol. prop. 70. et schol. prop. 71. P. 4.", "en": "In accepting favours and returning thanks an altogether different care must be taken, on which see Schol. of Prop. 70 and Schol. of Prop. 71 of Part IV."}, {"id": "E4App19", "p": "part4", "l": "4App19", "la": "Amor praeterea meretricius, hoc est generandi libido, quae ex forma oritur, et absolute omnis amor, qui aliam causam praeter animi libertatem agnoscit, facile in odium transit, nisi, quod peius est, species delirii sit, atque tum magis discordia, quam concordia fovetur. Vide schol. prop. 31. P. 3.", "en": "Further, meretricious love, that is, the lust to procreate which arises from bodily beauty, and absolutely every love which recognizes any cause other than freedom of mind, easily passes into hatred, unless, which is worse, it is a species of madness; and then discord rather than concord is fostered. See Schol. of Prop. 31 of Part III."}, {"id": "E4App20", "p": "part4", "l": "4App20", "la": "Ad *matrimonium* quod attinet, certum est, ipsum cum ratione convenire, si cupiditas miscendi corpora non ex sola forma, sed etiam ex amore liberos procreandi et sapienter educandi, ingeneretur; et praeterea, si utriusque, viri scilicet et feminae, amor non solam formam, sed animi praecipue libertatem pro causa habeat.", "en": "As for marriage, it is certain that it agrees with reason if the desire for bodily union is generated not from beauty alone but also from love of begetting children and educating them wisely; and if, moreover, the love of each, namely of the man and of the woman, has as its cause not beauty alone but chiefly freedom of mind."}, {"id": "E4App21", "p": "part4", "l": "4App21", "la": "Gignit praeterea adulatio concordiam, sed foedo servitutis crimine vel perfidia; nulli quippe magis adulatione capiuntur, quam superbi, qui primi esse volunt, nec sunt.", "en": "Flattery, too, begets concord, but by the foul crime of servitude, or by perfidy; for none are more taken in by flattery than the proud, who want to be first and are not."}, {"id": "E4App22", "p": "part4", "l": "4App22", "la": "Abiectioni falsa pietatis et religionis species inest. Et quamvis abiectio superbiae sit contraria, est tamen abiectus superbo proximus. Vide schol. prop. 57. P. 4.", "en": "Self-abasement has a false appearance of piety and religion. And although self-abasement is contrary to pride, the self-abased man is still very near to the proud. See Schol. of Prop. 57 of Part IV."}, {"id": "E4App23", "p": "part4", "l": "4App23", "la": "Confert praeterea concordiae *pudor* in iis tantum, quae celari non possunt. Deinde, quia ipse pudor species est tristitiae, ad rationis usum non spectat.", "en": "Shame, too, contributes to concord, but only in matters which cannot be concealed. Next, because shame itself is a species of sadness, it does not pertain to the use of reason."}, {"id": "E4App24", "p": "part4", "l": "4App24", "la": "Ceteri tristitiae erga homines affectus directe iustitiae, aequitati, honestati, pietati et religioni opponuntur, et quamvis indignatio aequitatis speciem prae se ferre videatur, ibi tamen *sine lege vivitur* ubi unicuique de factis alterius iudicium ferre, et suum vel alterius ius vindicare licet.", "en": "The other affects of sadness towards men are directly opposed to justice, equity, honourableness, piety and religion; and although indignation seems to present an appearance of equity, yet men live without law where each one is permitted to pass judgment on the deeds of another and to vindicate his own or another’s right."}, {"id": "E4App25", "p": "part4", "l": "4App25", "la": "*Modestia*, hoc est, cupiditas hominibus placendi, quae ex ratione determinatur, ad pietatem (ut in schol. 1. prop. 37. P. 4. diximus) refertur. Sed si ex affectu oriatur, ambitio est sive cupiditas, qua homines falsa pietatis imagine plerumque discordias et seditiones concitant.", "en": "Modesty, that is, the desire to please men which is determined by reason, is related to piety (as we said in Schol. 1 of Prop. 37 of Part IV). But if it arises from affect, it is ambition, or a desire by which men, under a false image of piety, generally stir up discords and seditions."}, {"id": "E4App25", "p": "part4", "l": "4App25", "la": "Nam qui reliquos consilio aut re iuvare cupit, ut simul summo fruantur bono, is apprime studebit, eorum sibi amorem conciliare; non autem eos in admirationem traducere, ut disciplina ex ipso habeat vocabulum, nec ullas absolute invidiae causas dare. In communibus deinde colloquiis cavebit hominum vitia referre, et de humana impotentia non nisi parce loqui curabit; at largiter de humana virtute seu potentia, et qua via possit perfici, ut sic homines non ex metu aut aversione, sed solo laetitiae affectu, moti ex rationis praescripto, quantum in se est, conentur vivere.", "en": "For he who desires to help others by counsel or deed, so that they may enjoy the highest good together, will above all strive to win their love, not to lead them into wonder so that a school may take its name from him, nor absolutely to give any causes for envy. Then in common conversation he will take care not to report men’s vices, and to speak only sparingly of human lack of power, but abundantly of human virtue, or power, and of the way in which it can be perfected, so that men, moved not by fear or aversion but only by the affect of joy, may strive, as far as it is in them, to live according to the prescription of reason."}, {"id": "E4App26", "p": "part4", "l": "4App26", "la": "Praeter homines nihil singulare in natura novimus, cuius mente gaudere et quod nobis amicitia aut aliquo consuetudinis genere iungere possumus; adeoque quicquid in rerum natura extra homines datur, id nostrae utilitatis ratio conservare non postulat, sed pro eius vario usu conservare, destruere, vel quocumque modo ad nostrum usum adaptare nos docet.", "en": "Apart from men, we know no singular thing in nature whose mind we can enjoy, and which we can join to ourselves in friendship or in any kind of association; and so whatever there is in the nature of things apart from men, the principle of our advantage does not require us to preserve, but teaches us to preserve it, destroy it, or adapt it to our use in any way whatever, according to its various uses."}, {"id": "E4App27", "p": "part4", "l": "4App27", "la": "Utilitas, quam ex rebus, quae extra nos sunt, capimus, est praeter experientiam et cognitionem, quam acquirimus ex eo, quod easdem observamus et ex his formis in alias mutamus, praecipua corporis conservatio; et hac ratione res illae imprimis utiles sunt, quae corpus ita alere et nutrire possunt, ut eius omnes partes officio suo recte fungi queant. Nam quo corpus aptius est, ut pluribus modis possit affici et corpora externa pluribus modis afficere, eo mens ad cogitandum est aptior. Vide prop. 38. et 39. P. 4.", "en": "The advantage which we take from things outside us, besides the experience and knowledge which we acquire from observing them and changing them from one form into another, is chiefly the preservation of the body; and in this respect those things are most useful which can so feed and nourish the body that all its parts can perform their function properly. For the more capable the body is of being affected in many ways, and of affecting external bodies in many ways, the more capable the mind is of thinking. See Props. 38 and 39 of Part IV."}, {"id": "E4App27", "p": "part4", "l": "4App27", "la": "At huius notae perpauca in natura esse videntur. Quare ad corpus, ut requiritur, nutriendum necesse est multis naturae diversae alimentis uti. Quippe humanum corpus ex plurimis diversae naturae partibus componitur, quae continuo alimento indigent et vario, ut totum corpus ad omnia, quae ex ipsius natura sequi possunt, aeque aptum sit, et consequenter ut mens etiam aeque apta sit ad plura concipiendum.", "en": "But there seem to be very few things of this kind in nature. So to nourish the body as it requires, it is necessary to use many foods of different natures. For the human body is composed of a great many parts of different natures, which need continual and varied nourishment, so that the whole body may be equally capable of all the things which can follow from its nature, and consequently so that the mind also may be equally capable of conceiving many things."}, {"id": "E4App28", "p": "part4", "l": "4App28", "la": "Ad haec autem comparandum vix uniuscuiusque vires sufficerent nisi homines operas mutuas traderent. Verum omnium rerum compendium *pecunia* attulit. Unde factum, ut eius imago mentem vulgi maxime occupare soleat; quia vix ullam laetitiae speciem imaginari possunt, nisi concomitante nummorum idea tamquam causa.", "en": "But to procure these things the powers of each one would hardly suffice, if men did not give their labour to one another. But money has furnished a compendium of all things. Hence it has come about that its image usually occupies the mind of the multitude most, because they can hardly imagine any species of joy except one accompanied by the idea of coins as its cause."}, {"id": "E4App29", "p": "part4", "l": "4App29", "la": "Sed hoc vitium eorum tantum est, qui non ex indigentia, nec propter necessitates nummos quaerunt, sed quia lucri artes didicerunt, quibus se magnifice efferunt. Ceterum corpus ex consuetudine pascunt; sed parce, quia tantum de suis bonis se perdere credunt, quantum sui corporis conservationi impendunt. At qui verum nummorum usum norunt et divitiarum modum ex sola indigentia moderantur, paucis contenti vivunt.", "en": "But this vice belongs only to those who seek coins not from need or for necessities, but because they have learned the arts of gain, on which they pride themselves greatly. For the rest, they feed the body according to habit, but sparingly, because they believe that they lose as much of their goods as they spend on the preservation of their body. But those who know the true use of coins, and set the measure of their wealth by need alone, live content with little."}, {"id": "E4App30", "p": "part4", "l": "4App30", "la": "Cum igitur res illae sint bonae, quae corporis partes iuvant, ut suo officio fungantur, et laetitia in eo consistat, quod hominis potentia, quatenus mente et corpore constat, iuvatur vel augetur, sunt ergo illa omnia, quae laetitiam afferunt, *bona*. Attamen quoniam contra non eum in finem res agunt, ut nos laetitia afficiant, nec earum agendi potentia ex nostra utilitate temperatur, et denique quoniam laetitia plerumque ad unam corporis partem potissimum refertur, habent ergo plerumque laetitiae affectus (nisi ratio et vigilantia adsit), et consequenter cupiditates, etiam quae ex iisdem generantur, excessum.", "en": "Since, therefore, those things are good which help the parts of the body to perform their function, and joy consists in this, that man’s power, insofar as he consists of mind and body, is aided or increased, all those things which bring joy are good. Yet since, on the other hand, things do not act with the aim of affecting us with joy, nor is their power of acting tempered to our advantage, and finally since joy is generally related chiefly to one part of the body, the affects of joy (unless reason and watchfulness are present), and consequently also the desires which are generated from them, are generally excessive."}, {"id": "E4App30", "p": "part4", "l": "4App30", "la": "Ad quod accedit, quod ex affectu id primum habeamus, quod in praesentia suave est, nec futura aequali animi affectu aestimare possumus. Vide schol. prop. 44. et schol. prop. 60. P. 4.", "en": "Add to this that from affect we hold first what is pleasant in the present, and cannot value future things with an equal affect of mind. See Schol. of Prop. 44 and Schol. of Prop. 60 of Part IV."}, {"id": "E4App31", "p": "part4", "l": "4App31", "la": "At superstitio id contra videtur statuere bonum esse, quod tristitiam, et id contra malum, quod laetitiam affert. Sed, ut iam diximus (vide schol. 2. prop. 45. P. 4.), nemo, nisi invidus, mea impotentia et incommodo delectatur. Nam quo maiore laetitia afficimur, eo ad maiorem perfectionem transimus, et consequenter eo magis de natura divina participamus; nec laetitia unquam mala esse potest, quam nostrae utilitatis vera ratio moderatur. At qui contra metu ducitur et bonum, ut malum vitet, agit, is ratione non ducitur. Vide prop. 63. P. 4.", "en": "But superstition, on the contrary, seems to maintain that what brings sadness is good, and what brings joy evil. But, as we have already said (see Schol. 2 of Prop. 45 of Part IV), no one but an envious man takes pleasure in my lack of power and misfortune. For the greater the joy with which we are affected, the greater the perfection to which we pass, and consequently the more we participate in the divine nature; nor can joy ever be evil which the true principle of our advantage moderates. But he who, on the contrary, is led by fear, and does good in order to avoid evil, is not led by reason. See Prop. 63 of Part IV."}, {"id": "E4App32", "p": "part4", "l": "4App32", "la": "Sed humana potentia admodum limitata est et a potentia causarum externarum infinite superatur; atque adeo potestatem absolutam non habemus, res, quae extra nos sunt, ad nostrum usum aptandi. Attamen ea quae nobis eveniunt contra id, quod nostrae utilitatis ratio postulat, aequo animo feremus, si conscii simus nos functos nostro officio fuisse, et potentiam, quam habemus, non potuisse se eo usque extendere, ut eadem vitare possemus, nosque partem totius naturae esse, cuius ordinem sequimur.", "en": "But human power is very limited and is infinitely surpassed by the power of external causes; and so we do not have an absolute power of adapting things outside us to our use. Nevertheless, we shall bear with equanimity whatever happens to us contrary to what the principle of our advantage requires, if we are conscious that we have done our duty, that the power we have could not have extended so far as to avoid those things, and that we are a part of the whole of nature, whose order we follow."}, {"id": "E4App32", "p": "part4", "l": "4App32", "la": "Quod si clare et distincte intelligamus, pars illa nostri, quae *intelligentia* definitur, hoc est, pars melior nostri, in eo plane acquiescet et in ea acquiescentia perseverare conabitur. Nam quatenus intelligimus, nihil appetere nisi id quod necessarium est, nec absolute, nisi in veris acquiescere possumus; adeoque quatenus haec recte intelligimus, eatenus conatus melioris partis nostri cum ordine totius naturae convenit.", "en": "If we understand this clearly and distinctly, that part of us which is defined by understanding, that is, the better part of us, will be entirely content with it, and will strive to persevere in that contentment. For insofar as we understand, we can want nothing but what is necessary, nor be content absolutely with anything but what is true; and so, insofar as we understand these things rightly, to that extent the striving of the better part of us agrees with the order of the whole of nature."}, {"id": "E5Pref", "p": "part5", "l": "5Pref", "la": "Transeo tandem ad *alteram ethices partem, quae est de modo sive via, quae ad libertatem ducit*. In hac ergo de *potentia rationis* agam ostendens, quid ipsa ratio in affectus possit, et deinde, quid *mentis libertas* seu *beatitudo* sit; ex quibus videbimus, quantum sapiens potior sit ignaro.", "en": "I pass, finally, to the other part of ethics, which concerns the manner, or way, which leads to freedom. In this Part, therefore, I shall treat of the power of reason, showing what reason itself can do against the affects, and then what freedom of mind, or blessedness, is; from which we shall see how much more powerful the wise man is than the ignorant."}, {"id": "E5Pref", "p": "part5", "l": "5Pref", "la": "Quomodo autem et qua via debeat intellectus perfici, et qua deinde arte corpus sit curandum, ut possit suo officio recte fungi, huc non pertinet; hoc enim ad *medicinam*, illud autem ad *logicam* spectat. Hic igitur, ut dixi, de sola mentis seu rationis potentia agam, et ante omnia, quantum et quale imperium in affectus habeat ad eosdem coercendum et moderandum, ostendam. Nam nos in ipsos imperium absolutum non habere, iam supra demonstravimus.", "en": "But how and by what way the intellect must be perfected, and then by what art the body must be cared for so that it can perform its function properly, does not belong here; for the latter pertains to medicine, the former to logic. Here, therefore, as I have said, I shall treat only of the power of the mind, or of reason, and above all I shall show how great and of what kind its dominion over the affects is, for restraining and moderating them. For we have already demonstrated above that we do not have absolute dominion over them."}, {"id": "E5Pref", "p": "part5", "l": "5Pref", "la": "*Stoici* tamen putarunt, eosdem a nostra voluntate absolute pendere, nosque iis absolute imperare posse. Attamen ab experientia reclamante, non vero ex suis principiis coacti sunt fateri, usum et studium non parvum requiri ad eosdem coercendum et moderandum. Quod quidam exemplo duorum canum (si recte memini), unius scilicet domestici, alterius venatici, conatus est ostendere; nempe quia usu efficere tandem potuit ut domesticus venari, venaticus contra a leporibus sectandis abstinere assuesceret.", "en": "The Stoics, however, thought that they depend absolutely on our will, and that we can command them absolutely. Yet they were compelled, by the protests of experience and not by their own principles, to admit that no little practice and effort are required to restrain and moderate them. Someone tried to show this by the example of two dogs (if I remember rightly), one a house dog, the other a hunting dog; namely, because by practice he was finally able to bring it about that the house dog grew accustomed to hunting, and the hunting dog, on the contrary, to refraining from chasing hares."}, {"id": "E5Pref", "p": "part5", "l": "5Pref", "la": "Huic opinioni non parum favet *Cartesius*. Nam statuit animam seu mentem unitam praecipue esse cuidam parti cerebri, glandulae scilicet pineali dictae, cuius ope mens motus omnes, qui in corpore excitantur, et obiecta externa sentit, quamque mens eo solo, quod vult, varie movere potest. Hanc glandulam in medio cerebri ita suspensam esse statuit, ut minimo spirituum animalium motu possit moveri.", "en": "Descartes inclines not a little to this opinion. For he maintained that the soul, or mind, is united chiefly to a certain part of the brain, namely the so-called pineal gland, by means of which the mind senses all the motions excited in the body, and external objects, and which the mind can move in various ways merely by willing. He maintained that this gland is so suspended in the middle of the brain that it can be moved by the least motion of the animal spirits."}, {"id": "E5Pref", "p": "part5", "l": "5Pref", "la": "Deinde statuit, quod haec glans tot variis modis in medio cerebro suspendatur, quot variis modis spiritus animales in eandem impingunt, et quod praeterea tot varia vestigia in eadem imprimantur, quot varia obiecta externa ipsos spiritus animales versus eandem propellunt; unde fit, ut si glans postea ab animae voluntate illam diversimode movente hoc aut illo modo suspendatur, quo semel fuit suspensa a spiritibus hoc aut illo modo agitatis, tum ipsa glans ipsos spiritus animales eodem modo propellet et determinabit, ac antea a simili glandulae suspensione repulsi fuerant.", "en": "Next, he maintained that this gland is suspended in the middle of the brain in as many different ways as there are different ways in which the animal spirits strike against it, and that, besides, as many different traces are impressed on it as there are different external objects which drive the animal spirits towards it; from which it comes about that if the gland is afterwards suspended in this or that way by the will of the soul, which moves it in different ways, in the way in which it was once suspended by the spirits agitated in this or that way, then the gland itself will drive and determine the animal spirits in the same way as they had been repelled before by a similar suspension of the gland."}, {"id": "E5Pref", "p": "part5", "l": "5Pref", "la": "Praeterea statuit, unamquamque mentis voluntatem natura esse unitam certo cuidam glandis motui. Ex. gr. si quis voluntatem habet obiectum remotum intuendi, haec voluntas efficiet, ut pupilla dilatetur; sed si de sola dilatanda pupilla cogitet, nihil proderit eius rei habere voluntatem, quia natura non iunxit motum glandis, qui inservit impellendis spiritibus versus nervum opticum modo conveniente dilatandae vel contrahendae pupillae, cum voluntate eandem dilatandi vel contrahendi; sed demum cum voluntate intuendi obiecta remota vel proxima.", "en": "Besides, he maintained that each will of the mind is united by nature to a certain motion of the gland. For example, if someone has the will to look at a distant object, this will brings it about that the pupil is dilated; but if he thinks only of dilating the pupil, it will be of no use to have the will to do so, because nature has not joined the motion of the gland which serves to drive the spirits towards the optic nerve in the way suitable for dilating or contracting the pupil with the will to dilate or contract it, but only with the will to look at distant or near objects."}, {"id": "E5Pref", "p": "part5", "l": "5Pref", "la": "Denique statuit, quod, etsi unusquisque motus huius glandulae videatur connexus esse per naturam singulis ex nostris cogitationibus ab initio nostrae vitae, aliis tamen per habitum possunt iungi, quod probare conatur artic. 50. P. 1. de passionibus animae. Ex his concludit, nullam esse tam imbecillem animam quae non possit, cum bene dirigitur, acquirere potestatem absolutam in suas passiones.", "en": "Finally, he maintained that although each motion of this gland seems to have been connected by nature from the beginning of our life with each of our thoughts, they can nevertheless be joined to others by habit, which he tries to prove in art. 50 of Part I of The Passions of the Soul. From this he concludes that there is no soul so weak that it cannot, when it is well directed, acquire an absolute power over its passions."}, {"id": "E5Pref", "p": "part5", "l": "5Pref", "la": "Nam hae, ut ab eo definiuntur, sunt perceptiones aut sensus aut commotiones animae, quae ad eam speciatim referuntur, quaeque NB. producuntur, conservantur et corroborantur per aliquem motum spirituum. Vide Cartes. artic. 27. P. 1. passionum animae.", "en": "For these, as he defines them, are perceptions, or feelings, or commotions of the soul, which are related to it in particular, and which (note well!) are produced, preserved and strengthened by some motion of the spirits. See Descartes, art. 27 of Part I of The Passions of the Soul."}, {"id": "E5Pref", "p": "part5", "l": "5Pref", "la": "At quandoquidem cuilibet voluntati possumus iungere motum quemcumque glandis, et consequenter spirituum, et determinatio voluntatis a sola nostra potestate pendet, si igitur nostram voluntatem certis et firmis iudiciis, secundum quae nostrae vitae actiones dirigere volumus, determinemus et motus passionum, quas habere volumus, hisce iudiciis iungamus, imperium acquiremus absolutum in nostras passiones.", "en": "But since we can join any motion of the gland, and consequently of the spirits, to any will, and the determination of the will depends on our power alone, if, therefore, we determine our will by sure and firm judgments according to which we want to direct the actions of our life, and join to these judgments the motions of the passions which we want to have, we shall acquire an absolute dominion over our passions."}, {"id": "E5Pref", "p": "part5", "l": "5Pref", "la": "Haec est clarissimi huius viri sententia (quantum ex ipsius verbis coniicio), quam ego vix credidissem a tanto viro prolatam esse, si minus acuta fuisset. Profecto mirari satis non possum, quod vir philosophus, qui firmiter statuerat, nihil deducere nisi ex principiis per se notis, et nihil affirmare nisi quod clare et distincte perciperet, et qui toties scholasticos reprehenderat, quod per occultas qualitates res obscuras voluerint explicare, hypothesin sumat omni occulta qualitate occultiorem.", "en": "This is the opinion of this most distinguished man (so far as I can conjecture from his words); and I would hardly have believed it to have been put forward by so great a man, if it had been less acute. Indeed, I cannot wonder enough that a philosopher who had firmly resolved to deduce nothing except from self-evident principles, and to affirm nothing except what he perceived clearly and distinctly, and who had so often reproached the scholastics for wanting to explain obscure things by occult qualities, should assume a hypothesis more occult than any occult quality."}, {"id": "E5Pref", "p": "part5", "l": "5Pref", "la": "Quid, quaeso, per mentis et corporis unionem intelligit? Quem, inquam, clarum et distinctum conceptum habet cogitationis arctissime unitae cuidam quantitatis portiunculae? Vellem sane, ut hanc unionem per proximam suam causam explicuisset. Sed ille mentem a corpore adeo distinctam conceperat, ut nec huius unionis, nec ipsius mentis ullam singularem causam assignare potuerit, sed necesse ipsi fuerit, ad causam totius universi, hoc est, ad Deum recurrere.", "en": "What, I ask, does he understand by the union of mind and body? What clear and distinct concept, I say, has he of a thought most closely united to a certain small portion of quantity? I should indeed have liked him to explain this union through its proximate cause. But he had conceived the mind to be so distinct from the body that he could assign no singular cause either of this union or of the mind itself, but had to have recourse to the cause of the whole universe, that is, to God."}, {"id": "E5Pref", "p": "part5", "l": "5Pref", "la": "Deinde pervelim scire: quot motus gradus potest glandulae isti pineali mens tribuere, et quanta cum vi eandem suspensam tenere potest? Nam nescio an haec glans tardius vel celerius a mente circumagatur, quam a spiritibus animalibus, et an motus passionum, quos firmis iudiciis arcte iunximus, non possint ab iisdem iterum a causis corporeis disiungi; ex quo sequeretur, ut, quamvis mens firmiter proposuerit contra pericula ire, atque huic decreto motus audaciae iunxerit, viso tamen periculo glans ita suspendatur, ut mens non nisi de fuga possit cogitare.", "en": "Next, I should very much like to know how many degrees of motion the mind can give to this pineal gland, and with how much force it can hold it suspended. For I do not know whether this gland is driven about more slowly or more quickly by the mind than by the animal spirits, and whether the motions of the passions which we have closely joined to firm judgments cannot be disjoined from them again by corporeal causes; from which it would follow that, although the mind had firmly resolved to go against dangers, and had joined the motions of daring to this decree, nevertheless, at the sight of the danger, the gland would be so suspended that the mind could think only of flight."}, {"id": "E5Pref", "p": "part5", "l": "5Pref", "la": "Et sane cum nulla detur ratio voluntatis ad motum, nulla etiam datur comparatio inter mentis et corporis potentiam seu vires; et consequenter huius vires nequaquam viribus illius determinari possunt. His adde, quod nec haec glans ita in medio cerebro sita reperiatur, ut tam facile totque modis circumagi possit, et quod non omnes nervi ad cavitates usque cerebri protendantur. Denique omnia, quae de voluntate eiusque libertate asserit, omitto, quandoquidem haec falsa esse satis superque ostenderim. Igitur quia mentis potentia, ut supra ostendi, sola intelligentia definitur, affectuum remedia, quae omnes experiri quidem, sed non accurate observare, nec distincte videre credo, sola mentis cognitione determinabimus, et ex eadem illa omnia, quae ad ipsius beatitudinem spectant, deducemus.", "en": "And indeed, since there is no ratio of will to motion, there is also no comparison between the power, or forces, of the mind and those of the body; and consequently the forces of the latter can by no means be determined by the forces of the former. Add to this that this gland is not found to be so situated in the middle of the brain that it can be driven about so easily and in so many ways, and that not all the nerves extend as far as the cavities of the brain. Finally, I pass over everything he asserts about the will and its freedom, since I have shown more than enough that it is false. Therefore, since the power of the mind, as I have shown above, is defined by understanding alone, we shall determine the remedies for the affects, which I believe all experience but do not observe accurately or see distinctly, by the knowledge of the mind alone, and from that same knowledge we shall deduce all that concerns its blessedness."}, {"id": "E5A1", "p": "part5", "l": "5A1", "la": "Si in eodem subiecto duae contrariae actiones excitentur, debebit necessario vel in utraque, vel in una sola mutatio fieri, donec desinant contrariae esse.", "en": "If two contrary actions are aroused in the same subject, a change will necessarily have to take place either in both or in one only, until they cease to be contrary."}, {"id": "E5A2", "p": "part5", "l": "5A2", "la": "Effectus potentia definitur potentia ipsius causae, quatenus eius essentia per ipsius causae essentiam explicatur vel definitur. Patet hoc axioma ex prop. 7. P. 3.", "en": "The power of an effect is defined by the power of its cause, insofar as its essence is explained or defined through the essence of its cause. This axiom is evident from Prop. 7 of Part III."}, {"id": "E5P1", "p": "part5", "l": "5P1", "la": "Prout cogitationes rerumque ideae ordinantur et concatenantur in mente, ita corporis affectiones seu rerum imagines ad amussim ordinantur et concatenantur in corpore.", "en": "In just the way that thoughts and the ideas of things are ordered and connected in the mind, so the affections of the body, or the images of things, are ordered and connected exactly in the body."}, {"id": "E5P1D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P1D", "la": "Ordo et connexio idearum idem est (per prop. 7. P. 2.) ac ordo et connexio rerum, et vice versa ordo et connexio rerum idem est (per coroll. prop. 6. et 7. P. 2.) ac ordo et connexio idearum. Quare sicuti ordo et connexio idearum in mente fit secundum ordinem et concatenationem affectionum corporis (per prop. 18. P. 2.), sic vice versa (per prop. 2. P. 3.) ordo et connexio affectionum corporis fit, prout cogitationes rerumque ideae ordinantur et concatenantur in mente. Q. E. D.", "en": "The order and connection of ideas is the same (by Prop. 7 of Part II) as the order and connection of things, and conversely the order and connection of things is the same (by Cors. of Props. 6 and 7 of Part II) as the order and connection of ideas. Hence, just as the order and connection of ideas in the mind happens according to the order and concatenation of the affections of the body (by Prop. 18 of Part II), so conversely (by Prop. 2 of Part III) the order and connection of the affections of the body happens as thoughts and the ideas of things are ordered and connected in the mind. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P2", "p": "part5", "l": "5P2", "la": "Si animi commotionem seu affectum a causae externae cogitatione amoveamus et aliis iungamus cogitationibus, tum amor seu odium erga causam externam, ut et animi fluctuationes, quae ex his affectibus oriuntur, destruentur.", "en": "If we separate a commotion of the mind, or affect, from the thought of an external cause, and join it to other thoughts, then the love or hatred towards the external cause, as well as the fluctuations of mind which arise from these affects, will be destroyed."}, {"id": "E5P2D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P2D", "la": "Id enim, quod formam amoris vel odii constituit, est laetitia vel tristitia concomitante idea causae externae (per aff. defin. 6. et 7.). Hac igitur sublata amoris vel odii forma simul tollitur; adeoque hi affectus, et qui ex his oriuntur, destruuntur. Q. E. D.", "en": "For what constitutes the form of love or hatred is joy or sadness accompanied by the idea of an external cause (by Defs. Aff. 6 and 7). When this is taken away, therefore, the form of love or hatred is taken away at the same time; and so these affects, and those which arise from them, are destroyed. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P3", "p": "part5", "l": "5P3", "la": "Affectus, qui passio est, desinit esse passio, simulatque eius claram et distinctam formamus ideam.", "en": "An affect which is a passion ceases to be a passion as soon as we form a clear and distinct idea of it."}, {"id": "E5P3D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P3D", "la": "Affectus, qui passio est, idea est confusa (per gener. aff. defin.). Si itaque ipsius affectus claram et distinctam formemus ideam, haec idea ab ipso affectu, quatenus ad solam mentem refertur, non nisi ratione distinguetur (per prop. 21. P. 2. cum eiusdem schol.); adeoque (per prop. 3. P. 3.) affectus desinet esse passio. Q. E. D.", "en": "An affect which is a passion is a confused idea (by the General Definition of the Affects). If, therefore, we form a clear and distinct idea of the affect itself, this idea will be distinguished from the affect itself, insofar as it is related to the mind alone, only by reason (by Prop. 21 of Part II with its Schol.); and so (by Prop. 3 of Part III) the affect will cease to be a passion. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P3C", "p": "part5", "l": "5P3C", "la": "Affectus igitur eo magis in nostra potestate est et mens ab eo minus patitur, quo nobis est notior.", "en": "An affect, therefore, is the more in our power, and the mind is acted on by it the less, the better it is known to us."}, {"id": "E5P4", "p": "part5", "l": "5P4", "la": "Nulla est corporis affectio, cuius aliquem clarum et distinctum non possumus formare conceptum.", "en": "There is no affection of the body of which we cannot form some clear and distinct concept."}, {"id": "E5P4D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P4D", "la": "Quae omnibus communia sunt, non possunt concipi nisi adaequate (per prop. 38. P. 2.). Adeoque (per prop. 12. et lemm. 2. quod habetur post schol. prop. 13. P. 2.) nulla est corporis affectio, cuius aliquem clarum et distinctum non possumus formare conceptum. Q. E. D.", "en": "Those things which are common to all can only be conceived adequately (by Prop. 38 of Part II). And so (by Prop. 12 and Lemma 2, which is found after the Schol. of Prop. 13 of Part II) there is no affection of the body of which we cannot form some clear and distinct concept. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P4C", "p": "part5", "l": "5P4C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, nullum esse affectum, cuius non possumus aliquem clarum et distinctum formare conceptum. Est namque affectus corporis affectionis idea (per gener. aff. defin.), quae propterea (per prop. praeced.) aliquem clarum et distinctum involvere debet conceptum.", "en": "Hence it follows that there is no affect of which we cannot form some clear and distinct concept. For an affect is the idea of an affection of the body (by the General Definition of the Affects), which therefore (by the preceding Prop.) must involve some clear and distinct concept."}, {"id": "E5P4S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P4S", "la": "Quandoquidem nihil datur, ex quo aliquis effectus non sequatur (per prop. 36. P. 1.), et quicquid ex idea, quae in nobis est adaequata, sequitur, id omne clare et distincte intelligimus (per prop. 40. P. 2.); hinc sequitur, unumquemque potestatem habere, se suosque affectus, si non absolute, ex parte saltem clare et distincte intelligendi, et consequenter efficiendi, ut ab iisdem minus patiatur. Huic igitur rei praecipue danda est opera, ut unumquemque affectum, quantum fieri potest, clare et distincte cognoscamus, ut sic mens ex affectu ad illa cogitandum determinetur, quae clare et distincte percipit, et in quibus plane acquiescit; atque adeo ut ipse affectus a cogitatione causae externae separetur et veris iungatur cogitationibus. Ex quo fiet, ut non tantum amor, odium etc. destruantur (per prop. 2. huius), sed ut etiam appetitus seu cupiditates, quae ex tali affectu oriri solent, excessum habere nequeant (per prop. 61. P. 4.).", "en": "Since there is nothing from which some effect does not follow (by Prop. 36 of Part I), and whatever follows from an idea which is adequate in us we understand clearly and distinctly (by Prop. 40 of Part II), it follows that each one has the power of understanding himself and his affects clearly and distinctly, if not absolutely, at least in part, and consequently of bringing it about that he is acted on less by them. To this, therefore, we must above all devote our effort, that we know each affect, as far as possible, clearly and distinctly, so that the mind may be determined by the affect to think those things which it perceives clearly and distinctly, and in which it is fully content; and so that the affect itself may be separated from the thought of an external cause and joined to true thoughts. From this it will come about not only that love, hatred, etc. are destroyed (by Prop. 2 of this Part), but also that the appetites, or desires, which usually arise from such an affect cannot be excessive (by Prop. 61 of Part IV)."}, {"id": "E5P4S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P4S", "la": "Nam apprime notandum est, unum eundemque esse appetitum, per quem homo tam agere quam pati dicitur. Ex. gr. cum natura humana ita comparatum esse ostendimus, ut unusquisque appetat, ut reliqui ex ipsius ingenio vivant (vide schol. prop. 31. P. 3.); qui quidem appetitus in homine, qui ratione non ducitur, passio est, quae ambitio vocatur, nec multum a superbia discrepat; et contra in homine, qui ex rationis dictamine vivit, actio seu virtus est, quae pietas appellatur (vide schol. 1. prop. 37. P. 4. et 2. demonstr. eiusdem prop.).", "en": "For it should be noted above all that it is one and the same appetite through which a man is said both to act and to be acted on. For example, since we have shown that human nature is so constituted that each one wants the others to live according to his temperament (see Schol. of Prop. 31 of Part III), this appetite, in a man who is not led by reason, is a passion, which is called ambition and does not differ much from pride; and on the other hand in a man who lives from the dictate of reason it is an action, or virtue, which is called piety (see Schol. 1 of Prop. 37 of Part IV and the second demonstration of the same Prop.)."}, {"id": "E5P4S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P4S", "la": "Et hoc modo omnes appetitus seu cupiditates eatenus tantum passiones sunt, quatenus ex ideis inadaequatis oriuntur; atque eaedem virtuti accensentur, quando ab ideis adaequatis excitantur vel generantur. Nam omnes cupiditates, quibus ad aliquid agendum determinamur, tam oriri possunt ab adaequatis, quam ab inadaequatis ideis (vide prop. 59. P. 4.). Atque hoc (ut eo, unde digressus sum, revertar) affectuum remedio, quod scilicet in eorum vera cognitione consistit, nullum praestantius aliud, quod a nostra potestate pendeat, excogitari potest, quandoquidem nulla alia mentis potentia datur, quam cogitandi et adaequatas ideas formandi, ut supra (per prop. 3. P. 3.) ostendimus.", "en": "And in this way all appetites, or desires, are passions only insofar as they arise from inadequate ideas, and the same are counted as virtue when they are aroused or generated by adequate ideas. For all the desires by which we are determined to do something can arise as much from adequate as from inadequate ideas (see Prop. 59 of Part IV). And (to return to the point from which I digressed) no remedy for the affects which depends on our power can be thought of that is more excellent than this one, which consists in a true knowledge of them, since there is no other power of the mind than that of thinking and forming adequate ideas, as we have shown above (by Prop. 3 of Part III)."}, {"id": "E5P5", "p": "part5", "l": "5P5", "la": "Affectus erga rem, quam simpliciter et non ut necessariam neque ut possibilem, neque ut contingentem imaginamur, ceteris paribus omnium est maximus.", "en": "An affect towards a thing which we imagine simply, and not as necessary, or as possible, or as contingent, is, other things being equal, the greatest of all."}, {"id": "E5P5D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P5D", "la": "Affectus erga rem, quam liberam esse imaginamur, maior est, quam erga necessariam (per prop. 49. P. 3.), et consequenter adhuc maior, quam erga illam, quam ut possibilem vel contingentem imaginamur (per prop. 11. P. 4.). At rem aliquam ut liberam imaginari nihil aliud esse potest, quam quod rem simpliciter imaginamur, dum causas, a quibus ipsa ad agendum determinata fuit, ignoramus (per illa quae in schol. prop. 35. P. 2. ostendimus). Ergo affectus erga rem, quam simpliciter imaginamur, ceteris paribus maior est, quam erga necessariam, possibilem vel contingentem, et consequenter maximus. Q. E. D.", "en": "An affect towards a thing which we imagine to be free is greater than towards a necessary thing (by Prop. 49 of Part III), and consequently still greater than towards one which we imagine as possible or contingent (by Prop. 11 of Part IV). But to imagine a thing as free can be nothing other than to imagine the thing simply, while we are ignorant of the causes by which it was determined to act (by what we showed in Schol. of Prop. 35 of Part II). Therefore an affect towards a thing which we imagine simply is, other things being equal, greater than towards a necessary, possible or contingent thing, and consequently the greatest. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P6", "p": "part5", "l": "5P6", "la": "Quatenus mens res omnes ut necessarias intelligit, eatenus maiorem in affectus potentiam habet, seu minus ab iisdem patitur.", "en": "Insofar as the mind understands all things as necessary, to that extent it has a greater power over the affects, or is acted on less by them."}, {"id": "E5P6D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P6D", "la": "Mens res omnes necessarias esse intelligit (per prop. 29. P. 1.), et infinito causarum nexu determinari ad existendum et operandum (per prop. 28. P. 1.). Adeoque (per prop. praeced.) eatenus efficit, ut ab affectibus, qui ex iis oriuntur, minus patiatur et (per prop. 48. P. 3.) minus erga ipsas afficiatur. Q. E. D.", "en": "The mind understands all things to be necessary (by Prop. 29 of Part I) and to be determined to exist and to operate by an infinite connection of causes (by Prop. 28 of Part I). And so (by the preceding Prop.) to that extent it brings it about that it is acted on less by the affects which arise from them, and (by Prop. 48 of Part III) that it is less affected towards them. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P6S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P6S", "la": "Quo haec cognitio, quod scilicet res necessariae sint, magis circa res singulares, quas distinctius et magis vivide imaginamur, versatur, eo haec mentis in affectus potentia maior est, quod ipsa etiam experientia testatur. Videmus enim tristitiam boni alicuius, quod periit, mitigari simulac homo, qui id perdidit, considerat bonum illud servari nulla ratione potuisse. Sic etiam videmus, quod nemo miseretur infantis, propterea quod nescit loqui, ambulare, ratiocinari, et quod denique tot annos quasi sui inscius vivat. At si plerique adulti, et unus aut alter infans nascerentur, tum unumquemque misereret infantum, quia tum ipsam infantiam non ut rem naturalem et necessariam, sed ut naturae vitium seu peccatum consideraret. Et ad hunc modum plura alia notare possemus.", "en": "The more this knowledge, that things are necessary, concerns singular things which we imagine more distinctly and vividly, the greater is this power of the mind over the affects, as experience itself also testifies. For we see that the sadness over a good which has perished is eased as soon as the man who has lost it considers that the good could not have been kept in any way. So also we see that no one pities an infant because it does not know how to speak, walk or reason, and because, finally, it lives so many years as if unconscious of itself. But if most men were born adults, and only one or another an infant, then everyone would pity infants, because he would then consider infancy itself not as a natural and necessary thing, but as a vice or fault of nature. And we could note many other things of this kind."}, {"id": "E5P7", "p": "part5", "l": "5P7", "la": "Affectus, qui ex ratione oriuntur vel excitantur, si ratio temporis habeatur, potentiores sunt iis, qui ad res singulares referuntur, quas ut absentes contemplamur.", "en": "Affects which arise from or are aroused by reason are, if account is taken of time, more powerful than those which are related to singular things which we contemplate as absent."}, {"id": "E5P7D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P7D", "la": "Rem aliquam ut absentem non contemplamur ex affectu, quo eandem imaginamur, sed ex eo, quod corpus alio afficitur affectu, qui eiusdem rei existentiam secludit (per prop. 17. P. 2.). Quare affectus, qui ad rem, quam ut absentem contemplamur, refertur, eius naturae non est, ut reliquas hominis actiones et potentiam superet (de quibus vide prop. 6. P. 4.), sed contra eius naturae est, ut ab iis affectionibus, quae existentiam externae eius causae secludunt, coerceri aliquo modo possit (per prop. 9. P. 4.).", "en": "We contemplate a thing as absent not from the affect by which we imagine it, but from the fact that the body is affected with another affect which excludes the existence of the thing (by Prop. 17 of Part II). Hence the affect which is related to a thing which we contemplate as absent is not of such a nature that it surpasses the other actions and power of a man (on which see Prop. 6 of Part IV), but on the contrary is of such a nature that it can be restrained in some way by those affections which exclude the existence of its external cause (by Prop. 9 of Part IV)."}, {"id": "E5P7D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P7D", "la": "At affectus, qui ex ratione oritur, refertur necessario ad communes rerum proprietates (vide rationis definitionem in schol. 2. prop. 40. P. 2.), quas semper ut praesentes contemplamur (nam nihil dari potest, quod earum praesentem existentiam secludat), et quas semper eodem modo imaginamur (per prop. 38. P. 2.). Quare talis affectus idem semper manet, et consequenter (per axiom. 1. huius) affectus, qui eidem sunt contrarii, quique a suis causis externis non foventur, eidem magis magisque sese accommodare debebunt, donec non amplius sint contrarii, et eatenus affectus, qui ex ratione oritur, est potentior. Q. E. D.", "en": "But an affect which arises from reason is necessarily related to the common properties of things (see the definition of reason in Schol. 2 of Prop. 40 of Part II), which we always contemplate as present (for there can be nothing which excludes their present existence), and which we always imagine in the same way (by Prop. 38 of Part II). Hence such an affect always remains the same, and consequently (by Axiom 1 of this Part) the affects which are contrary to it, and which are not encouraged by their external causes, will have to accommodate themselves to it more and more, until they are no longer contrary; and to that extent an affect which arises from reason is more powerful. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P8", "p": "part5", "l": "5P8", "la": "Quo affectus aliquis a pluribus causis simul concurrentibus excitatur, eo maior est.", "en": "The more causes concurring at the same time by which some affect is aroused, the greater it is."}, {"id": "E5P8D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P8D", "la": "Plures causae simul plus possunt, quam si pauciores essent (per prop. 7. P. 3.). Adeoque (per prop. 5. P. 4.) quo affectus aliquis a pluribus causis simul excitatur, eo fortior est. Q. E. D.", "en": "Many causes together can do more than if they were fewer (by Prop. 7 of Part III). And so (by Prop. 5 of Part IV) the more causes at the same time by which some affect is aroused, the stronger it is. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P8S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P8S", "la": "Haec propositio patet etiam ex axiom. 2. huius partis.", "en": "This Proposition is also evident from Axiom 2 of this Part."}, {"id": "E5P9", "p": "part5", "l": "5P9", "la": "Affectus, qui ad plures et diversas causas refertur, quas mens cum ipso affectu simul contemplatur, minus noxius est, et minus per ipsum patimur, et erga unamquamque causam minus afficimur, quam alius aeque magnus affectus, qui ad unam solam vel pauciores causas refertur.", "en": "An affect which is related to many and different causes, which the mind contemplates together with the affect itself, is less harmful, and we are acted on less by it and are affected less towards each cause, than by another equally great affect which is related to one only or to fewer causes."}, {"id": "E5P9D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P9D", "la": "Affectus eatenus tantum malus seu noxius est, quatenus mens ab eo impeditur, quominus possit cogitare (per propositiones 26. et 27. P. 4.). Adeoque ille affectus, a quo mens ad plura simul obiecta contemplandum determinatur, minus noxius est, quam alius aeque magnus affectus, qui mentem in sola unius aut pauciorum obiectorum contemplatione ita detinet, ut de aliis cogitare nequeat; quod erat primum.", "en": "An affect is evil, or harmful, only insofar as the mind is prevented by it from being able to think (by Props. 26 and 27 of Part IV). And so that affect by which the mind is determined to contemplate many objects at once is less harmful than another equally great affect which so holds the mind in the contemplation of one or a few objects alone that it cannot think of others; which was the first point."}, {"id": "E5P9D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P9D", "la": "Deinde quia mentis essentia, hoc est (per prop. 7. P. 3.) potentia, in sola cogitatione consistit (per prop. 11. P. 2.); ergo mens per affectum, a quo ad plura simul contemplandum determinatur, minus patitur, quam per aeque magnum affectum, qui mentem in sola unius aut pauciorum obiectorum contemplatione occupatam tenet; quod erat secundum. Denique hic affectus (per prop. 48. P. 3.) quatenus ad plures causas externas refertur, est etiam erga unamquamque minor. Q. E. D.", "en": "Next, because the essence of the mind, that is (by Prop. 7 of Part III), its power, consists in thought alone (by Prop. 11 of Part II), the mind is acted on less by an affect by which it is determined to contemplate many things at once than by an equally great affect which keeps the mind occupied in the contemplation of one or a few objects alone; which was the second point. Finally, this affect (by Prop. 48 of Part III), insofar as it is related to many external causes, is also less towards each. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P10", "p": "part5", "l": "5P10", "la": "Quamdiu affectibus, qui nostrae naturae sunt contrarii, non conflictamur, tamdiu potestatem habemus ordinandi et concatenandi corporis affectiones secundum ordinem ad intellectum.", "en": "So long as we are not troubled by affects which are contrary to our nature, we have the power of ordering and connecting the affections of the body according to the order of the intellect."}, {"id": "E5P10D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P10D", "la": "Affectus, qui nostrae naturae sunt contrarii, hoc est (per prop. 30. P. 4.) qui mali sunt, eatenus mali sunt, quatenus impediunt, quominus mens intelligat (per prop. 27. P. 4.). Quamdiu igitur affectibus, qui nostrae naturae contrarii sunt, non conflictamur, tamdiu mentis potentia, qua res intelligere conatur (per prop. 26. P. 4.) non impeditur. Atque adeo tamdiu potestatem habet claras et distinctas ideas formandi et alias ex aliis deducendi (vide schol. 2. prop. 40. et schol. prop. 47. P. 2.); et consequenter (per prop. 1. huius) tamdiu potestatem habemus ordinandi et concatenandi affectiones corporis secundum ordinem ad intellectum. Q. E. D.", "en": "Affects which are contrary to our nature, that is (by Prop. 30 of Part IV), which are evil, are evil insofar as they prevent the mind from understanding (by Prop. 27 of Part IV). So long, therefore, as we are not troubled by affects which are contrary to our nature, the power of the mind by which it strives to understand things (by Prop. 26 of Part IV) is not hindered. And so for that long it has the power of forming clear and distinct ideas and of deducing some from others (see Schol. 2 of Prop. 40 and Schol. of Prop. 47 of Part II); and consequently (by Prop. 1 of this Part) for that long we have the power of ordering and connecting the affections of the body according to the order of the intellect. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P10S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P10S", "la": "Hac potestate recte ordinandi et concatenandi corporis affectiones efficere possumus, ut non facile malis affectibus afficiamur. Nam (per prop. 7. huius) maior vis requiritur ad affectus secundum ordinem ad intellectum ordinatos et concatenatos coercendum, quam incertos et vagos. Optimum igitur, quod efficere possumus, quamdiu nostrorum affectuum perfectam cognitionem non habemus, est rectam vivendi rationem seu certa vitae dogmata concipere, eaque memoriae mandare, et rebus particularibus in vita frequenter obviis continuo applicare, ut sic nostra imaginatio late iisdem afficiatur, et nobis in promptu sint semper.", "en": "By this power of rightly ordering and connecting the affections of the body we can bring it about that we are not easily affected by evil affects. For (by Prop. 7 of this Part) a greater force is required to restrain affects ordered and connected according to the order of the intellect than to restrain uncertain and random ones. The best thing, therefore, that we can do, so long as we do not have perfect knowledge of our affects, is to conceive a right way of living, or sure principles of life, to commit them to memory, and to apply them continually to the particular things frequently met with in life, so that in this way our imagination may be widely affected by them, and they may always be ready to hand for us."}, {"id": "E5P10S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P10S", "la": "Ex. gr. inter vitae dogmata posuimus (vide prop. 46. P. 4. cum eiusdem schol.), odium amore seu generositate vincendum, non autem reciproco odio compensandum. Ut autem hoc rationis praescriptum semper in promptu habeamus, ubi usus erit, cogitandae et saepe meditandae sunt communes hominum iniuriae, et quomodo et qua via generositate optime propulsentur. Sic enim imaginem iniuriae imaginationi huius dogmatis iungemus, et nobis (per prop. 18. P. 2.) in promptu semper erit, ubi nobis iniuria afferetur.", "en": "For example, we have laid down among the principles of life (see Prop. 46 of Part IV with its Schol.) that hatred is to be conquered by love, or nobility, and not repaid with reciprocal hatred. But in order that we may always have this prescription of reason ready to hand when it is needed, we must think about and often meditate on the common injuries of men, and on how and by what way they are best warded off by nobility. For in this way we shall join the image of an injury to the imagination of this principle, and it will always be ready to hand for us (by Prop. 18 of Part II) when an injury is done to us."}, {"id": "E5P10S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P10S", "la": "Quod si etiam in promptu habuerimus rationem nostri veri utilis ac etiam boni, quod ex mutua amicitia et communi societate sequitur, et praeterea quod ex recta vivendi ratione summa animi acquiescentia oriatur (per prop. 52. P. 4.), et quod homines, ut reliqua, ex naturae necessitate agant; tum iniuria sive odium, quod ex eadem oriri solet, minimam imaginationis partem occupabit et facile superabitur; vel si ira, quae ex maximis iniuriis oriri solet, non adeo facile superetur, superabitur tamen, quamvis non sine animi fluctuatione longe minore temporis spatio, quam si haec non ita praemeditata habuissemus, ut patet ex prop. 6., 7. et 8. huius partis.", "en": "If we also have ready to hand the principle of our true advantage, and also of the good which follows from mutual friendship and common society, and besides that the highest contentment of mind arises from the right way of living (by Prop. 52 of Part IV), and that men, like other things, act from the necessity of nature, then the injury, or the hatred which usually arises from it, will occupy the least part of the imagination and will easily be overcome; or if the anger which usually arises from the greatest injuries is not so easily overcome, it will still be overcome, though not without fluctuation of mind, in a far shorter space of time than if we had not thus meditated on these things beforehand, as is evident from Props. 6, 7 and 8 of this Part."}, {"id": "E5P10S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P10S", "la": "De animositate ad metum deponendum eodem modo cogitandum est; enumeranda scilicet sunt et saepe imaginanda communia vitae pericula, et quomodo animi praesentia et fortitudine optime vitari et superari possunt. Sed notandum, quod nobis in ordinandis nostris cogitationibus et imaginibus semper attendendum est (per coroll. prop. 63. P. 4. et prop. 59. P. 3.) ad illa, quae in unaquaque re bona sunt, ut sic semper ex laetitiae affectu ad agendum determinemur.", "en": "Of tenacity, for laying aside fear, we must think in the same way: the common dangers of life must be enumerated and often imagined, and how they can best be avoided and overcome by presence of mind and strength of character. But it should be noted that in ordering our thoughts and images we must always attend (by Cor. of Prop. 63 of Part IV and Prop. 59 of Part III) to those things which are good in each thing, so that in this way we are always determined to act from an affect of joy."}, {"id": "E5P10S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P10S", "la": "Ex. gr. si quis videt, se nimis gloriam sectari, de eius recto usu cogitet, et in quem finem sectanda sit, et quibus mediis acquiri possit; sed non de ipsius abusu et vanitate et hominum inconstantia vel aliis huiusmodi, de quibus nemo nisi ex animi aegritudine cogitat. Talibus enim cogitationibus maxime ambitiosi se maxime afflictant, quando de assequendo honore, quem ambiunt, desperant; et dum iram evomunt, sapientes videri volunt. Quare certum est, eos gloriae maxime esse cupidos, qui de ipsius abusu et mundi vanitate maxime clamant. Nec hoc ambitiosis proprium, sed omnibus commune est, quibus fortuna est adversa, et qui animo impotentes sunt.", "en": "For example, if someone sees that he pursues glory too much, let him think of its right use, and for what end it is to be pursued, and by what means it can be acquired; but not of its abuse and emptiness, and of the inconstancy of men, or other things of this kind, about which no one thinks except from sickness of mind. For with such thoughts the most ambitious torment themselves most, when they despair of attaining the honour which they are seeking; and while they vomit up their anger, they want to seem wise. So it is certain that those are most desirous of glory who cry out most about its abuse and the emptiness of the world. Nor is this peculiar to the ambitious, but it is common to all to whom fortune is adverse and who are lacking in power of mind."}, {"id": "E5P10S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P10S", "la": "Nam pauper etiam avarus de abusu pecuniae et divitum vitiis non cessat loqui; quo nihil aliud efficit, quam se afflictare et aliis ostendere, se non tantum paupertatem suam, sed etiam aliorum divitias iniquo animo ferre. Sic etiam qui male ab amasia excepti sunt, nihil cogitant, quam de mulierum inconstantia et fallaci animo et reliquis earundem decantatis vitiis, quae omnia statim oblivioni tradunt, simulac ab amasia iterum recipiuntur.", "en": "For the poor man too, who is greedy, does not cease to speak of the abuse of money and the vices of the rich; by which he achieves nothing but to torment himself and to show others that he bears resentfully not only his own poverty but also the riches of others. So also those who have been badly received by a mistress think of nothing but the inconstancy of women, their deceitful hearts and their other much-sung vices, all of which they at once consign to oblivion as soon as they are received again by the mistress."}, {"id": "E5P10S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P10S", "la": "Qui itaque suos affectus et appetitus ex solo libertatis amore moderari studet, is, quantum potest, nitetur, virtutes earumque causas noscere, et animum gaudio, quod ex earum vera cognitione oritur, implere; at minime hominum vitia contemplari, hominesque obtrectare et falsa libertatis specie gaudere. Atque haec qui diligenter observabit (neque enim difficilia sunt) et exercebit, nae ille brevi temporis spatio actiones suas ex rationis imperio plerumque dirigere poterit.", "en": "He, therefore, who strives to moderate his affects and appetites from the love of freedom alone will strive as far as he can to know the virtues and their causes, and to fill his mind with the gladness which arises from the true knowledge of them, but by no means to contemplate the vices of men, to disparage men, and to rejoice in a false appearance of freedom. And he who observes these things diligently (for they are not difficult) and practises them will certainly in a short space of time be able to direct his actions for the most part by the rule of reason."}, {"id": "E5P11", "p": "part5", "l": "5P11", "la": "Quo imago aliqua ad plures res refertur, eo frequentior est seu saepius viget, et mentem magis occupat.", "en": "The more things an image is related to, the more frequent it is, or the more often it is vivid, and the more it occupies the mind."}, {"id": "E5P11D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P11D", "la": "Quo enim imago seu affectus ad plures res refertur, eo plures dantur causae, a quibus excitari et foveri potest, quas omnes mens (per hypothesin) ex ipso affectu simul contemplatur. Atque adeo affectus eo frequentior est seu saepius viget, et (per prop. 8. huius) mentem magis occupat. Q. E. D.", "en": "For the more things an image, or affect, is related to, the more causes there are by which it can be aroused and encouraged, all of which the mind (by hypothesis) contemplates at once from the affect itself. And so the affect is the more frequent, or the more often vivid, and (by Prop. 8 of this Part) occupies the mind more. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P12", "p": "part5", "l": "5P12", "la": "Rerum imagines facilius imaginibus, quae ad res referuntur, quas clare et distincte intelligimus, iunguntur, quam aliis.", "en": "The images of things are more easily joined to images related to things which we understand clearly and distinctly than to others."}, {"id": "E5P12D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P12D", "la": "Res, quas clare et distincte intelligimus, vel rerum communes proprietates sunt, vel quae ex iis deducuntur (vide rationis definitionem in schol. 2. prop. 40. P. 2.), et consequenter saepius (per prop. praeced.) in nobis excitantur. Adeoque facilius fieri potest, ut res alias simul cum his, quam cum aliis contemplemur, et consequenter (per prop. 18. P. 2.) ut facilius cum his, quam cum aliis iungantur. Q. E. D.", "en": "Things which we understand clearly and distinctly are either the common properties of things or what is deduced from them (see the definition of reason in Schol. 2 of Prop. 40 of Part II), and consequently (by the preceding Prop.) they are aroused in us more often. And so it can more easily happen that we contemplate other things together with these than with others, and consequently (by Prop. 18 of Part II) that they are more easily joined with these than with others. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P13", "p": "part5", "l": "5P13", "la": "Quo imago aliqua pluribus aliis iuncta est, eo saepius viget.", "en": "The more other images an image is joined to, the more often it is vivid."}, {"id": "E5P13D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P13D", "la": "Nam quo imago aliqua pluribus aliis iuncta est, eo (per prop. 18. P. 2.) plures causae dantur, a quibus excitari potest. Q. E. D.", "en": "For the more other images an image is joined to, the more causes there are (by Prop. 18 of Part II) by which it can be aroused. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P14", "p": "part5", "l": "5P14", "la": "Mens efficere potest, ut omnes corporis affectiones seu rerum imagines ad Dei ideam referantur.", "en": "The mind can bring it about that all the affections of the body, or images of things, are related to the idea of God."}, {"id": "E5P14D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P14D", "la": "Nulla est corporis affectio, cuius aliquem clarum et distinctum non possit mens formare conceptum (per prop. 4. huius). Adeoque efficere potest (per prop. 15. P. 1.), ut omnes ad Dei ideam referuntur. Q. E. D.", "en": "There is no affection of the body of which the mind cannot form some clear and distinct concept (by Prop. 4 of this Part). And so it can bring it about (by Prop. 15 of Part I) that they are all related to the idea of God. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P15", "p": "part5", "l": "5P15", "la": "Qui se suosque affectus clare et distincte intelligit, Deum amat, et eo magis, quo se suosque affectus magis intelligit.", "en": "He who understands himself and his affects clearly and distinctly loves God, and the more so the more he understands himself and his affects."}, {"id": "E5P15D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P15D", "la": "Qui se suosque affectus clare et distincte intelligit, laetatur (per prop. 53. P. 3.), idque concomitante idea Dei (per prop. praeced.). Atque adeo (per aff. defin. 6.) Deum amat, et (per eandem rationem) eo magis, quo se suosque affectus magis intelligit. Q. E. D.", "en": "He who understands himself and his affects clearly and distinctly rejoices (by Prop. 53 of Part III), and that accompanied by the idea of God (by the preceding Prop.). And so (by Def. Aff. 6) he loves God, and (by the same reasoning) the more so the more he understands himself and his affects. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P16", "p": "part5", "l": "5P16", "la": "Hic erga Deum amor mentem maxime occupare debet.", "en": "This love towards God must occupy the mind most."}, {"id": "E5P16D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P16D", "la": "Est enim hic amor iunctus omnibus corporis affectionibus (per prop. 14. huius), quibus omnibus fovetur (per prop. 15. huius). Atque adeo (per prop. 11. huius) mentem maxime occupare debet. Q. E. D.", "en": "For this love is joined to all the affections of the body (by Prop. 14 of this Part), by all of which it is encouraged (by Prop. 15 of this Part). And so (by Prop. 11 of this Part) it must occupy the mind most. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P17", "p": "part5", "l": "5P17", "la": "Deus expers est passionum, nec ullo laetitiae aut tristitiae affectu afficitur.", "en": "God is without passions, and is not affected with any affect of joy or sadness."}, {"id": "E5P17D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P17D", "la": "Ideae omnes, quatenus ad Deum referuntur, verae sunt (per prop. 32. P. 2.), hoc est (per defin. 4. P. 2.) adaequatae; atque adeo (per gener. aff. defin.) Deus expers est passionum. Deinde Deus neque ad maiorem neque ad minorem perfectionem transire potest (per coroll. 2. prop. 20. P. 1.); adeoque (per aff. defin. 2. et 3.) nullo laetitiae neque tristitiae affectu afficitur. Q. E. D.", "en": "All ideas, insofar as they are related to God, are true (by Prop. 32 of Part II), that is (by Def. 4 of Part II), adequate; and so (by the General Definition of the Affects) God is without passions. Next, God can pass neither to a greater nor to a lesser perfection (by Cor. 2 of Prop. 20 of Part I); and so (by Defs. Aff. 2 and 3) he is not affected with any affect of joy or sadness. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P17C", "p": "part5", "l": "5P17C", "la": "Deus proprie loquendo neminem amat, neque odio habet. Nam Deus (per prop. praeced.) nullo laetitiae neque tristitiae affectu afficitur, et consequenter (per aff. defin. 6. et 7.) neminem etiam amat, neque odio habet.", "en": "Strictly speaking, God loves no one and hates no one. For God (by the preceding Prop.) is not affected with any affect of joy or sadness, and consequently (by Defs. Aff. 6 and 7) he also loves no one and hates no one."}, {"id": "E5P18", "p": "part5", "l": "5P18", "la": "Nemo potest Deum odio habere.", "en": "No one can hate God."}, {"id": "E5P18D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P18D", "la": "Idea Dei, quae in nobis est, est adaequata et perfecta (per prop. 46. et 47. P. 2.). Adeoque quatenus Deum contemplamur, eatenus agimus (per prop. 3. P. 3.), et consequenter (per prop. 59. P. 3.) nulla potest dari tristitia concomitante idea Dei, hoc est (per aff. defin. 7.) nemo Deum odio habere potest. Q. E. D.", "en": "The idea of God which is in us is adequate and perfect (by Props. 46 and 47 of Part II). And so insofar as we contemplate God, to that extent we act (by Prop. 3 of Part III), and consequently (by Prop. 59 of Part III) there can be no sadness accompanied by the idea of God, that is (by Def. Aff. 7), no one can hate God. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P18C", "p": "part5", "l": "5P18C", "la": "Amor erga Deum in odium verti nequit.", "en": "Love towards God cannot be turned into hatred."}, {"id": "E5P18S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P18S", "la": "At obiici potest, quod dum Deum omnium rerum causam intelligimus, eo ipso Deum tristitiae causam consideramus. Sed ad hoc respondeo, quod quatenus tristitiae causas intelligimus, eatenus (per prop. 3. huius) ipsa desinit esse passio, hoc est (per prop. 59. P. 3.) eatenus desinit esse tristitia; atque adeo quatenus Deum tristitiae causam esse intelligimus, eatenus laetamur.", "en": "But it can be objected that while we understand God as the cause of all things, by that very fact we consider God to be the cause of sadness. But to this I reply that insofar as we understand the causes of sadness, to that extent (by Prop. 3 of this Part) it ceases to be a passion, that is (by Prop. 59 of Part III), to that extent it ceases to be sadness; and so insofar as we understand God to be the cause of sadness, to that extent we rejoice."}, {"id": "E5P19", "p": "part5", "l": "5P19", "la": "Qui Deum amat, conari non potest, ut Deus ipsum contra amet.", "en": "He who loves God cannot strive that God should love him in return."}, {"id": "E5P19D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P19D", "la": "Si homo id conaretur, cuperet ergo (per coroll. prop. 17. huius) ut Deus, quem amat, non esset Deus, et consequenter (per prop. 19. P. 3.) contristari cuperet; quod (per prop. 28. P. 3.) est absurdum. Ergo qui Deum amat etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "If a man strove for this, he would therefore desire (by Cor. of Prop. 17 of this Part) that God, whom he loves, should not be God, and consequently (by Prop. 19 of Part III) he would desire to be saddened, which (by Prop. 28 of Part III) is absurd. Therefore he who loves God, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P20", "p": "part5", "l": "5P20", "la": "Hic erga Deum amor neque invidiae neque zelotypiae affectu inquinari potest; sed eo magis fovetur, quo plures homines eodem amoris vinculo cum Deo iunctos imaginamur.", "en": "This love towards God cannot be tainted by the affect of envy or of jealousy; but it is the more encouraged, the more men we imagine to be joined to God by the same bond of love."}, {"id": "E5P20D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P20D", "la": "Hic erga Deum amor summum bonum est, quod ex dictamine rationis appetere possumus (per prop. 28. P. 4.), et omnibus hominibus commune est (per prop. 36. P. 4.), et omnes ut eodem gaudeant, cupimus (per prop. 37. P. 4.). Atque adeo (per aff. defin. 23.) invidiae affectu maculari nequit, neque etiam (per prop. 18. huius et definitionem zelotypiae, quam vide in schol. prop. 35. P. 3.) zelotypiae affectu; sed contra (per prop. 31. P. 3.) eo magis foveri debet, quo plures homines eodem gaudere imaginamur. Q. E. D.", "en": "This love towards God is the highest good which we can want from the dictate of reason (by Prop. 28 of Part IV), and is common to all men (by Prop. 36 of Part IV), and we desire that all should enjoy it (by Prop. 37 of Part IV). And so (by Def. Aff. 23) it cannot be stained by the affect of envy, nor (by Prop. 18 of this Part and the definition of jealousy, which see in Schol. of Prop. 35 of Part III) by the affect of jealousy; but on the contrary (by Prop. 31 of Part III) it must be the more encouraged, the more men we imagine to enjoy it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P20S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P20S", "la": "Possumus hoc eodem modo ostendere, nullum dari affectum, qui huic amori directe sit contrarius, a quo hic ipse amor possit destrui: atque adeo concludere possumus, hunc erga Deum amorem omnium affectuum esse constantissimum, nec quatenus ad corpus refertur, posse destrui, nisi cum ipso corpore. Cuius autem naturae sit, quatenus ad solam mentem refertur, postea videbimus. Atque his omnia affectuum remedia, sive id, omne quod mens in se sola considerata adversus affectus potest, comprehendi.", "en": "We can show in the same way that there is no affect which is directly contrary to this love and by which this love itself could be destroyed; and so we can conclude that this love towards God is the most constant of all the affects, and that, insofar as it is related to the body, it cannot be destroyed except with the body itself. What its nature is insofar as it is related to the mind alone, we shall see afterwards. And with these I have covered all the remedies for the affects, or all that the mind, considered in itself alone, can do against the affects."}, {"id": "E5P20S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P20S", "la": "Ex quibus apparet, mentis in affectus potentiam consistere 1. in ipsa affectuum cognitione. Vide schol. prop. 4. huius. 2. In eo, quod affectus a cogitatione causae externae, quam confuse imaginamur, separat. Vide prop. 2. cum eodem schol. prop. 4. huius. 3. In tempore, quo affectiones, quae ad res quas intelligimus referuntur, illas superant, quae ad res referuntur, quas confuse seu mutilate concipimus. Vide prop. 7. huius. 4. In multitudine causarum, a quibus affectiones, quae ad rerum communes proprietates vel ad Deum referuntur, foventur. Vide prop. 9. et 11. huius. 5. Denique in ordine, quo mens suos affectus ordinare et invicem concatenare potest. Vide schol. prop. 10. et insuper prop. 12., 13. et 14. huius.", "en": "From which it is evident that the power of the mind over the affects consists: 1. In the knowledge itself of the affects (see Schol. of Prop. 4 of this Part). 2. In its separating the affects from the thought of an external cause, which we imagine confusedly (see Prop. 2 with the same Schol. of Prop. 4 of this Part). 3. In time, by which the affections related to things which we understand surpass those related to things which we conceive confusedly, or in a mutilated way (see Prop. 7 of this Part). 4. In the multitude of causes by which affections related to the common properties of things, or to God, are encouraged (see Props. 9 and 11 of this Part). 5. Finally, in the order in which the mind can order its affects and connect them with one another (see Schol. of Prop. 10, and besides Props. 12, 13 and 14 of this Part)."}, {"id": "E5P20S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P20S", "la": "Sed ut haec mentis in affectus potentia melius intelligatur, venit apprime notandum, quod affectus a nobis magni appellantur, quando unius hominis affectum cum affectu alterius comparamus, et unum magis, quam alium eodem affectu conflictari videmus; vel quando unius eiusdemque hominis affectus ad invicem comparamus, eundemque uno affectu magis, quam alio affici sive moveri comperimus. Nam (per prop. 5. P. 4.) vis cuiuscumque affectus definitur potentia causae externae cum nostra comparata. At mentis potentia sola cognitione definitur; impotentia autem seu passio a sola cognitionis privatione, hoc est, ab eo, per quod ideae dicuntur inadaequatae, aestimatur.", "en": "But in order that this power of the mind over the affects may be better understood, it should above all be noted that we call affects great when we compare the affect of one man with that of another, and see that one is troubled more than another by the same affect; or when we compare the affects of one and the same man with one another, and find that he is affected, or moved, more by one affect than by another. For (by Prop. 5 of Part IV) the force of any affect is defined by the power of the external cause compared with our own. But the power of the mind is defined by knowledge alone, and its lack of power, or passion, is judged by the privation of knowledge alone, that is, by that through which ideas are called inadequate."}, {"id": "E5P20S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P20S", "la": "Ex quo sequitur, mentem illam maxime pati, cuius maximam partem ideae inadaequatae constituunt, ita ut magis per id, quod patitur, quam per id quod agit, dignoscatur; et illam contra maxime agere, cuius maximam partem ideae adaequatae constituunt, ita ut, quamvis huic tot inadaequatae ideae, quam illi insint, magis tamen per illas, quae humanae virtuti tribuuntur, quam per has, quae humanam impotentiam arguunt, dignoscatur.", "en": "From this it follows that that mind is acted on most whose greatest part is constituted by inadequate ideas, so that it is known more by what it undergoes than by what it does; and that that mind, on the contrary, acts most whose greatest part is constituted by adequate ideas, so that, although it may have as many inadequate ideas in it as the other, it is still known more by those which are attributed to human virtue than by those which show human lack of power."}, {"id": "E5P20S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P20S", "la": "Deinde notandum, animi aegritudines et infortunia potissimum originem trahere ex nimio amore erga rem, quae multis variationibus est obnoxia, et cuius nunquam compotes esse possumus. Nam nemo de re ulla, nisi quam amat, sollicitus anxiusve est, neque iniuriae, suspiciones, inimicitiae etc. oriuntur, nisi ex amore erga res, quarum nemo potest revera esse compos.", "en": "Next, it should be noted that sicknesses of the mind and misfortunes take their origin chiefly from excessive love towards a thing which is subject to many variations, and which we can never possess. For no one is anxious or troubled about anything unless he loves it, nor do injuries, suspicions, enmities, etc. arise except from love towards things which no one can really possess."}, {"id": "E5P20S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P20S", "la": "Ex his itaque facile concipimus, quid clara et distincta cognitio, et praecipue tertium illud cognitionis genus (de quo vide schol. prop. 47. P. 2.), cuius fundamentum est ipsa Dei cognitio, in affectus potest, quos nempe, quatenus passiones sunt, si non absolute tollit (vide prop. 3. cum schol. prop. 4. huius), saltem efficit, ut minimam mentis partem constituant. Vide prop. 14. huius. Deinde amorem gignit erga rem immutabilem et aeternam (vide prop. 15. huius), et cuius revera sumus compotes (vide prop. 45. P. 2.), et qui propterea nullis vitiis, quae in communi amore insunt, inquinari, sed semper maior ac maior esse potest (per prop. 15. huius), et mentis maximam partem occupare (per prop. 16. huius) lateque afficere.", "en": "From these things, therefore, we easily conceive what clear and distinct knowledge, and especially that third kind of knowledge (on which see Schol. of Prop. 47 of Part II), whose foundation is the knowledge of God itself, can do against the affects: namely, that if it does not take them away absolutely insofar as they are passions (see Prop. 3 with Schol. of Prop. 4 of this Part), at least it brings it about that they constitute the smallest part of the mind (see Prop. 14 of this Part). Next, it begets love towards an immutable and eternal thing (see Prop. 15 of this Part), which we really possess (see Prop. 45 of Part II); a love which therefore cannot be tainted by any of the vices which are in common love, but can always become greater and greater (by Prop. 15 of this Part), and occupy the greatest part of the mind (by Prop. 16 of this Part) and affect it widely."}, {"id": "E5P20S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P20S", "la": "Atque his omnia, quae praesentem hanc vitam spectant, absolvi. Nam quod in huius scholii principio dixi, me his paucis omnia affectuum remedia amplexum esse, facile poterit unusquisque videre qui ad haec quae in hoc scholio diximus, et simul ad mentis eiusque affectuum definitiones, et denique ad prop. 1. et 3. P. 3. attenderit. Tempus igitur iam est, ut ad illa transeam quae ad mentis durationem sine relatione ad corpus pertinent.", "en": "And with these I have completed everything which concerns this present life. For anyone will easily be able to see what I said at the beginning of this Scholium, that I have covered all the remedies for the affects in these few words, if he attends to what we have said in this Scholium, and at the same time to the definitions of the mind and its affects, and finally to Props. 1 and 3 of Part III. It is time, therefore, to pass to those things which pertain to the duration of the mind without relation to the body."}, {"id": "E5P21", "p": "part5", "l": "5P21", "la": "Mens nihil imaginari potest, neque rerum praeteritarum recordari, nisi durante corpore.", "en": "The mind can imagine nothing, nor recollect past things, except while the body endures."}, {"id": "E5P21D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P21D", "la": "Mens actualem sui corporis existentiam non exprimit, neque etiam corporis affectiones ut actuales concipit, nisi durante corpore (per coroll. prop. 8. P. 2.), et consequenter (per prop. 26. P. 2.) nullum corpus ut actu existens concipit, nisi durante suo corpore. Ac proinde nihil imaginari (vide imaginationis definitionem in schol. prop. 17. P. 2.), neque rerum praeteritarum recordari potest, nisi durante corpore (vide definitionem memoriae in schol. prop. 18. P. 2.). Q. E. D.", "en": "The mind does not express the actual existence of its body, nor does it conceive the affections of the body as actual, except while the body endures (by Cor. of Prop. 8 of Part II), and consequently (by Prop. 26 of Part II) it conceives no body as actually existing except while its body endures. And so it can imagine nothing (see the definition of imagination in Schol. of Prop. 17 of Part II), nor recollect past things, except while the body endures (see the definition of memory in Schol. of Prop. 18 of Part II). Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P22", "p": "part5", "l": "5P22", "la": "In Deo tamen datur necessario idea, quae huius et illius corporis humani essentiam sub aeternitatis specie exprimit.", "en": "Nevertheless, there is necessarily in God an idea which expresses the essence of this or that human body under a species of eternity."}, {"id": "E5P22D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P22D", "la": "Deus non tantum est causa huius et illius corporis humani existentiae, sed etiam essentiae (per prop. 25. P. 1.), quae propterea per ipsam Dei essentiam necessario debet concipi (per axiom. 4. P. 1.), idque aeterna quadam necessitate (per prop. 16. P. 1.), qui quidem conceptus necessario in Deo dari debet (per prop. 3. P. 2.). Q. E. D.", "en": "God is the cause not only of the existence of this or that human body, but also of its essence (by Prop. 25 of Part I), which therefore must necessarily be conceived through the very essence of God (by Axiom 4 of Part I), and that by a certain eternal necessity (by Prop. 16 of Part I); and this concept must necessarily be in God (by Prop. 3 of Part II). Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P23", "p": "part5", "l": "5P23", "la": "Mens humana non potest cum corpore absolute destrui, sed eius aliquid remanet quod aeternum est.", "en": "The human mind cannot be absolutely destroyed with the body, but something of it remains which is eternal."}, {"id": "E5P23D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P23D", "la": "In Deo datur necessario conceptus seu idea, quae corporis humani essentiam exprimit (per prop. praeced.), quae propterea aliquid necessario est, quod ad essentiam mentis humanae pertinet (per prop. 13. P. 2.). Sed menti humanae nullam durationem, quae tempore definiri potest, tribuimus, nisi quatenus corporis actualem existentiam, quae per durationem explicatur et tempore definiri potest, exprimit, hoc est (per coroll. prop. 8. P. 2.) ipsi durationem non tribuimus nisi durante corpore. Cum tamen aliquid nihilominus sit id, quod aeterna quadam necessitate per ipsam Dei essentiam concipitur (per prop. praeced.), erit necessario hoc aliquid, quod ad mentis essentiam pertinet, aeternum. Q. E. D.", "en": "In God there is necessarily a concept, or idea, which expresses the essence of the human body (by the preceding Prop.), and which therefore is necessarily something that pertains to the essence of the human mind (by Prop. 13 of Part II). But we attribute to the human mind no duration which can be defined by time, except insofar as it expresses the actual existence of the body, which is explained by duration and can be defined by time; that is (by Cor. of Prop. 8 of Part II), we do not attribute duration to it except while the body endures. Since, however, there is nevertheless something which is conceived by a certain eternal necessity through the very essence of God (by the preceding Prop.), this something, which pertains to the essence of the mind, will necessarily be eternal. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P23S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P23S", "la": "Est, uti diximus, haec idea, quae corporis essentiam sub specie aeternitatis exprimit, certus cogitandi modus, qui ad mentis essentiam pertinet, quique necessario aeternus est. Nec tamen fieri potest, ut recordemur nos ante corpus exstitisse, quandoquidem nec in corpore ulla eius vestigia dari, nec aeternitas tempore definiri, nec ullam ad tempus relationem habere potest.", "en": "This idea, which expresses the essence of the body under a species of eternity, is, as we have said, a certain mode of thinking which pertains to the essence of the mind and which is necessarily eternal. And yet it cannot happen that we should remember that we existed before the body, since there can be no traces of this in the body, nor can eternity be defined by time or have any relation to time."}, {"id": "E5P23S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P23S", "la": "At nihilominus sentimus experimurque, nos aeternos esse. Nam mens non minus res illas sentit, quas intelligendo concipit, quam quas in memoria habet. Mentis enim oculi, quibus res videt observatque, sunt ipsae demonstrationes.", "en": "But nevertheless we feel and experience that we are eternal. For the mind feels those things which it conceives in understanding no less than those which it has in memory. For the eyes of the mind, by which it sees and observes things, are the demonstrations themselves."}, {"id": "E5P23S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P23S", "la": "Quamvis itaque non recordemur nos ante corpus exstitisse, sentimus tamen mentem nostram, quatenus corporis essentiam sub aeternitatis specie involvit, aeternam esse, et hanc eius existentiam tempore definiri sive per durationem explicari non posse. Mens igitur nostra eatenus tantum potest dici durare, eiusque existentia certo tempore definiri potest, quatenus actualem corporis existentiam involvit, et eatenus tantum potentiam habet rerum existentiam tempore determinandi, easque sub duratione concipiendi.", "en": "Although, therefore, we do not remember that we existed before the body, we nevertheless feel that our mind, insofar as it involves the essence of the body under a species of eternity, is eternal, and that this existence of it cannot be defined by time or explained through duration. Our mind, therefore, can be said to endure, and its existence can be defined by a certain time, only insofar as it involves the actual existence of the body, and only to that extent does it have the power of determining the existence of things by time and of conceiving them under duration."}, {"id": "E5P24", "p": "part5", "l": "5P24", "la": "Quo magis res singulares intelligimus, eo magis Deum intelligimus.", "en": "The more we understand singular things, the more we understand God."}, {"id": "E5P24D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P24D", "la": "Patet ex coroll. prop. 25. P. 1.", "en": "This is evident from Cor. of Prop. 25 of Part I."}, {"id": "E5P25", "p": "part5", "l": "5P25", "la": "Summus mentis conatus summaque virtus est res intelligere tertio cognitionis genere.", "en": "The highest striving of the mind, and its highest virtue, is to understand things by the third kind of knowledge."}, {"id": "E5P25D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P25D", "la": "Tertium cognitionis genus procedit ab adaequata idea quorundam Dei attributorum ad adaequatam cognitionem essentiae rerum (vide huius definitionem in schol. 2. prop. 40. P. 2.), et quo magis hoc modo res intelligimus, eo magis (per prop. praeced.) Deum intelligimus. Ac proinde (per prop. 28. P. 4.) summa mentis virtus, hoc est (per defin. 8. P. 4.) mentis potentia seu natura, sive (per prop. 7. P. 3.) summus conatus est res intelligere tertio cognitionis genere. Q. E. D.", "en": "The third kind of knowledge proceeds from an adequate idea of certain attributes of God to the adequate knowledge of the essence of things (see its definition in Schol. 2 of Prop. 40 of Part II); and the more we understand things in this way, the more (by the preceding Prop.) we understand God. And so (by Prop. 28 of Part IV) the highest virtue of the mind, that is (by Def. 8 of Part IV), the power, or nature, of the mind, or (by Prop. 7 of Part III) its highest striving, is to understand things by the third kind of knowledge. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P26", "p": "part5", "l": "5P26", "la": "Quo mens aptior est ad res tertio cognitionis genere intelligendum, eo magis cupit res eodem hoc cognitionis genere intelligere.", "en": "The more capable the mind is of understanding things by the third kind of knowledge, the more it desires to understand things by this same kind of knowledge."}, {"id": "E5P26D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P26D", "la": "Patet. Nam quatenus concipimus mentem aptam esse ad res hoc cognitionis genere intelligendum, eatenus eandem determinatam concipimus ad res eodem cognitionis genere intelligendum, et consequenter (per aff. defin. 1.) quo mens ad hoc aptior est, eo magis hoc cupit. Q. E. D.", "en": "This is evident. For insofar as we conceive the mind to be capable of understanding things by this kind of knowledge, to that extent we conceive it to be determined to understand things by the same kind of knowledge; and consequently (by Def. Aff. 1), the more capable the mind is of this, the more it desires it. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P27", "p": "part5", "l": "5P27", "la": "Ex hoc tertio cognitionis genere summa, quae dari potest, mentis acquiescentia oritur.", "en": "From this third kind of knowledge arises the greatest contentment of mind there can be."}, {"id": "E5P27D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P27D", "la": "Summa mentis virtus est Deum cognoscere (per prop. 28. P. 4.), sive res tertio cognitionis genere intelligere (per prop. 25. huius); quae quidem virtus eo maior est, quo mens hoc cognitionis genere magis res cognoscit (per prop. 24. huius). Adeoque qui res hoc cognitionis genere cognoscit, is ad summam humanam perfectionem transit, et consequenter (per aff. defin. 2.) summa laetitia afficitur, idque (per prop. 43. P. 2.) concomitante idea sui suaeque virtutis; ac proinde (per aff. defin. 25.) ex hoc cognitionis genere summa, quae dari potest, oritur acquiescentia. Q. E. D.", "en": "The highest virtue of the mind is to know God (by Prop. 28 of Part IV), or to understand things by the third kind of knowledge (by Prop. 25 of this Part); and this virtue is the greater, the more the mind knows things by this kind of knowledge (by Prop. 24 of this Part). And so he who knows things by this kind of knowledge passes to the highest human perfection, and consequently (by Def. Aff. 2) is affected with the highest joy, and that (by Prop. 43 of Part II) accompanied by the idea of himself and of his virtue; and accordingly (by Def. Aff. 25) from this kind of knowledge arises the greatest contentment there can be. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P28", "p": "part5", "l": "5P28", "la": "Conatus seu cupiditas cognoscendi res tertio cognitionis genere oriri non potest ex primo, at quidem ex secundo cognitionis genere.", "en": "The striving, or desire, to know things by the third kind of knowledge cannot arise from the first kind of knowledge, but it can from the second."}, {"id": "E5P28D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P28D", "la": "Haec propositio per se patet. Nam quicquid clare et distincte intelligimus, id vel per se vel per aliud, quod per se concipitur, intelligimus; hoc est, ideae, quae in nobis clarae et distinctae sunt, sive quae ad tertium cognitionis genus referuntur (vide schol. 2. prop. 40. P. 2.), non possunt sequi ex ideis mutilatis et confusis, quae (per idem schol.) ad primum cognitionis genus referuntur, sed ex ideis adaequatis, sive (per idem schol.) ex secundo et tertio cognitionis genere. Ac proinde (per aff. defin. 1.) cupiditas cognoscendi res tertio cognitionis genere non potest oriri ex primo, at quidem ex secundo. Q. E. D.", "en": "This proposition is evident through itself. For whatever we understand clearly and distinctly, we understand either through itself or through something else which is conceived through itself; that is, ideas which are clear and distinct in us, or which are related to the third kind of knowledge (see Schol. 2 of Prop. 40 of Part II), cannot follow from mutilated and confused ideas, which (by the same Schol.) are related to the first kind of knowledge, but from adequate ideas, or (by the same Schol.) from the second and third kind of knowledge. And accordingly (by Def. Aff. 1) the desire to know things by the third kind of knowledge cannot arise from the first, but it can from the second. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P29", "p": "part5", "l": "5P29", "la": "Quicquid mens sub specie aeternitatis intelligit, id ex eo non intelligit, quod corporis praesentem actualem existentiam concipit; sed ex eo, quod corporis essentiam concipit sub specie aeternitatis.", "en": "Whatever the mind understands under a species of eternity, it understands not from the fact that it conceives the present actual existence of the body, but from the fact that it conceives the essence of the body under a species of eternity."}, {"id": "E5P29D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P29D", "la": "Quatenus mens praesentem sui corporis existentiam concipit, eatenus durationem concipit, quae tempore determinari potest, et eatenus tantum potentiam habet concipiendi res cum relatione ad tempus (per prop. 21. huius et prop. 26. P. 2.). At aeternitas per durationem explicari nequit (per defin. 8. P. 1. et ipsius explic.).", "en": "Insofar as the mind conceives the present existence of its body, to that extent it conceives duration, which can be determined by time, and to that extent only does it have the power of conceiving things in relation to time (by Prop. 21 of this Part and Prop. 26 of Part II). But eternity cannot be explained by duration (by Def. 8 of Part I and its Explanation)."}, {"id": "E5P29D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P29D", "la": "Ergo mens eatenus potestatem non habet concipiendi res sub specie aeternitatis; sed quia de natura rationis est, res sub specie aeternitatis concipere (per coroll. 2. prop. 44. P. 2.), et ad mentis naturam etiam pertinet, corporis essentiam sub specie aeternitatis concipere (per prop. 23. huius), et praeter haec duo nihil aliud ad mentis essentiam pertinet (per prop. 13. P. 2.), ergo haec potentia concipiendi res sub specie aeternitatis ad mentem non pertinet, nisi quatenus corporis essentiam sub specie aeternitatis concipit. Q. E. D.", "en": "Therefore to that extent the mind does not have the power of conceiving things under a species of eternity; but because it is of the nature of reason to conceive things under a species of eternity (by Cor. 2 of Prop. 44 of Part II), and it also pertains to the nature of the mind to conceive the essence of the body under a species of eternity (by Prop. 23 of this Part), and besides these two nothing else pertains to the essence of the mind (by Prop. 13 of Part II), therefore this power of conceiving things under a species of eternity does not pertain to the mind except insofar as it conceives the essence of the body under a species of eternity. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P29S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P29S", "la": "Res duobus modis a nobis ut actuales concipiuntur, vel quatenus easdem cum relatione ad certum tempus et locum existere, vel quatenus ipsas in Deo contineri et ex naturae divinae necessitate consequi concipimus. Quae autem hoc secundo modo ut verae seu reales concipiuntur, eas sub aeternitatis specie concipimus, et earum ideae aeternam et infinitam Dei essentiam involvunt, ut prop. 45. P. 2. ostendimus, cuius etiam schol. vide.", "en": "Things are conceived by us as actual in two ways: either insofar as we conceive them to exist in relation to a certain time and place, or insofar as we conceive them to be contained in God and to follow from the necessity of the divine nature. But those which are conceived as true, or real, in this second way, we conceive under a species of eternity, and their ideas involve the eternal and infinite essence of God, as we showed in Prop. 45 of Part II; see also its Schol.."}, {"id": "E5P30", "p": "part5", "l": "5P30", "la": "Mens nostra quatenus se et corpus sub aeternitatis specie cognoscit, eatenus Dei cognitionem necessario habet, scitque se in Deo esse et per Deum concipi.", "en": "Our mind, insofar as it knows itself and the body under a species of eternity, to that extent necessarily has knowledge of God, and knows that it is in God and is conceived through God."}, {"id": "E5P30D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P30D", "la": "Aeternitas est ipsa Dei essentia, quatenus haec necessariam involvit existentiam (per defin. 8. P. 1.). Res igitur sub specie aeternitatis concipere est res concipere, quatenus per Dei essentiam ut entia realia concipiuntur, sive quatenus per Dei essentiam involvunt existentiam. Adeoque mens nostra quatenus se et corpus sub specie aeternitatis concipit, eatenus Dei cognitionem necessario habet, scitque etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "Eternity is the very essence of God insofar as this involves necessary existence (by Def. 8 of Part I). To conceive things under a species of eternity, therefore, is to conceive things insofar as they are conceived as real beings through the essence of God, or insofar as through the essence of God they involve existence. And so our mind, insofar as it conceives itself and the body under a species of eternity, to that extent necessarily has knowledge of God, and knows, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P31", "p": "part5", "l": "5P31", "la": "Tertium cognitionis genus pendet a mente, tamquam a formali causa, quatenus mens ipsa aeterna est.", "en": "The third kind of knowledge depends on the mind as on its formal cause, insofar as the mind itself is eternal."}, {"id": "E5P31D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P31D", "la": "Mens nihil sub aeternitatis specie concipit, nisi quatenus sui corporis essentiam sub aeternitatis specie concipit (per prop. 29. huius), hoc est (per prop. 21. et 23. huius) nisi quatenus aeterna est. Adeoque (per prop. praeced.) quatenus aeterna est, Dei habet cognitionem, quae quidem cognitio est necessario adaequata (per prop. 46. P. 2.); ac proinde mens quatenus aeterna est, ad illa omnia cognoscendum est apta, quae ex data hac Dei cognitione consequi possunt (per prop. 40. P. 2.), hoc est, ad res tertio cognitionis genere cognoscendum (vide huius definitionem in schol. 2. prop. 40. P. 2.), cuius propterea mens (per defin. 1. P. 3.) quatenus aeterna est, causa est adaequata seu formalis. Q. E. D.", "en": "The mind conceives nothing under a species of eternity except insofar as it conceives the essence of its body under a species of eternity (by Prop. 29 of this Part), that is (by Props. 21 and 23 of this Part), except insofar as it is eternal. And so (by the preceding Prop.) insofar as it is eternal it has knowledge of God, which is necessarily adequate (by Prop. 46 of Part II); and accordingly the mind, insofar as it is eternal, is capable of knowing all those things which can follow from this given knowledge of God (by Prop. 40 of Part II), that is, of knowing things by the third kind of knowledge (see its definition in Schol. 2 of Prop. 40 of Part II), of which the mind, insofar as it is eternal, is therefore (by Def. 1 of Part III) the adequate, or formal, cause. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P31S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P31S", "la": "Quo igitur unusquisque hoc cognitionis genere plus pollet, eo melius sui et Dei conscius est, hoc est, eo est perfectior et beatior, quod adhuc clarius ex seqq. patebit. Sed hic notandum, quod, tametsi iam certi sumus, mentem aeternam esse, quatenus res sub aeternitatis specie concipit, nos tamen, ut ea, quae ostendere volumus, facilius explicentur et melius intelligantur, ipsam, tamquam iam inciperet esse, et res sub aeternitatis specie intelligere iam inciperet, considerabimus, ut hucusque fecimus; quod nobis absque ullo erroris periculo facere licet, modo nobis cautio sit nihil concludere, nisi ex perspicuis praemissis.", "en": "The more, therefore, each one is capable in this kind of knowledge, the better he is conscious of himself and of God, that is, the more perfect and blessed he is, as will be evident still more clearly from what follows. But it should be noted here that, although we are already certain that the mind is eternal insofar as it conceives things under a species of eternity, nevertheless, so that what we wish to show may be explained more easily and better understood, we shall consider it, as we have done so far, as if it were now beginning to be, and were now beginning to understand things under a species of eternity; which we may do without any danger of error, provided we take care to conclude nothing except from clear premises."}, {"id": "E5P32", "p": "part5", "l": "5P32", "la": "Quicquid intelligimus tertio cognitionis genere, eo delectamur, et quidem concomitante idea Dei tamquam causa.", "en": "Whatever we understand by the third kind of knowledge we take delight in, and our delight is accompanied by the idea of God as its cause."}, {"id": "E5P32D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P32D", "la": "Ex hoc cognitionis genere summa, quae dari potest, mentis acquiescentia (per prop. 27. huius), hoc est (per aff. defin. 25.) laetitia oritur, eaque concomitante idea sui, et consequenter (per prop. 30. huius) concomitante etiam idea Dei tamquam causa. Q. E. D.", "en": "From this kind of knowledge arises the greatest contentment of mind there can be (by Prop. 27 of this Part), that is (by Def. Aff. 25), joy, and this accompanied by the idea of oneself, and consequently (by Prop. 30 of this Part) accompanied also by the idea of God as its cause. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P32C", "p": "part5", "l": "5P32C", "la": "Ex tertio cognitionis genere oritur necessario *amor Dei intellectualis*. Nam ex hoc cognitionis genere oritur (per prop. praeced.) laetitia concomitante idea Dei tamquam causa, hoc est (per aff. defin. 6.) amor Dei, non quatenus ipsum ut praesentem imaginamur (per prop. 29. huius), sed quatenus Deum aeternum esse intelligimus, et hoc est, quod amorem Dei intellectualem voco.", "en": "From the third kind of knowledge there necessarily arises the intellectual love of God. For from this kind of knowledge there arises (by the preceding Prop.) joy accompanied by the idea of God as its cause, that is (by Def. Aff. 6), the love of God, not insofar as we imagine him as present (by Prop. 29 of this Part), but insofar as we understand God to be eternal; and this is what I call the intellectual love of God."}, {"id": "E5P33", "p": "part5", "l": "5P33", "la": "Amor Dei intellectualis, qui ex tertio cognitionis genere oritur, est aeternus.", "en": "The intellectual love of God, which arises from the third kind of knowledge, is eternal."}, {"id": "E5P33D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P33D", "la": "Tertium enim cognitionis genus (per prop. 31. huius et axiom. 3. P. 1.) est aeternum; adeoque (per idem axiom. P. 1.) amor, qui ex eodem oritur, est etiam necessario aeternus. Q. E. D.", "en": "For the third kind of knowledge (by Prop. 31 of this Part and Axiom 3 of Part I) is eternal; and so (by the same Axiom of Part I) the love which arises from it is also necessarily eternal. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P33S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P33S", "la": "Quamvis hic erga Deum amor principium non habuerit (per prop. praeced.), habet tamen omnes amoris perfectiones, perinde ac si ortus fuisset, sicut in coroll. prop. praeced. finximus. Nec ulla hic est differentia, nisi quod mens easdem has perfectiones, quas eidem iam accedere finximus, aeternas habuerit, idque concomitante idea Dei tamquam causa aeterna. Quod si laetitia in transitione ad maiorem perfectionem consistit, beatitudo sane in eo consistere debet, quod mens ipsa perfectione sit praedita.", "en": "Although this love towards God has had no beginning (by the preceding Prop.), it nevertheless has all the perfections of love, just as if it had come into being, as we feigned in the Cor. of the preceding Prop.. Nor is there any difference here, except that the mind has had eternally these same perfections which we feigned to be now accruing to it, and that accompanied by the idea of God as eternal cause. But if joy consists in the passage to a greater perfection, blessedness must surely consist in this, that the mind is endowed with perfection itself."}, {"id": "E5P34", "p": "part5", "l": "5P34", "la": "Mens non nisi durante corpore obnoxia est affectibus, qui ad passiones referuntur.", "en": "Only while the body endures is the mind subject to affects which are related to passions."}, {"id": "E5P34D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P34D", "la": "Imaginatio est idea, qua mens rem aliquam ut praesentem contemplatur (vide eius definitionem in schol. prop. 17. P. 2.), quae tamen magis corporis humani praesentem constitutionem, quam rei externae naturam indicat (per coroll. 2. prop. 16. P. 2.). Est igitur affectus (per gener. aff. defin.) imaginatio, quatenus corporis praesentem constitutionem indicat; atque adeo (per prop. 21. huius) mens non nisi durante corpore obnoxia est affectibus, qui ad passiones referuntur. Q. E. D.", "en": "An imagination is an idea by which the mind contemplates some thing as present (see its definition in Schol. of Prop. 17 of Part II), but which indicates the present constitution of the human body more than the nature of the external thing (by Cor. 2 of Prop. 16 of Part II). An affect, therefore (by the General Definition of the Affects), is an imagination, insofar as it indicates the present constitution of the body; and so (by Prop. 21 of this Part) only while the body endures is the mind subject to affects which are related to passions. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P34C", "p": "part5", "l": "5P34C", "la": "Hinc sequitur nullum amorem praeter amorem intellectualem esse aeternum.", "en": "Hence it follows that no love except intellectual love is eternal."}, {"id": "E5P34S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P34S", "la": "Si ad hominum communem opinionem attendamus, videbimus, eos suae mentis aeternitatis esse quidem conscios, sed ipsos eandem cum duratione confundere, eamque imaginationi seu memoriae tribuere, quam post mortem remanere credunt.", "en": "If we attend to the common opinion of men, we shall see that they are indeed conscious of the eternity of their mind, but that they confuse it with duration and attribute it to the imagination, or memory, which they believe remains after death."}, {"id": "E5P35", "p": "part5", "l": "5P35", "la": "Deus se ipsum amore intellectuali infinito amat.", "en": "God loves himself with an infinite intellectual love."}, {"id": "E5P35D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P35D", "la": "Deus est absolute infinitus (per defin. 6. P. 1.), hoc est (per defin. 6. P. 2.) Dei natura gaudet infinita perfectione, idque (per prop. 3. P. 2.) concomitante idea sui, hoc est (per prop. 11. et defin. 1. P. 1.) idea suae causae, et hoc est, quod in coroll. prop. 32. huius amorem intellectualem esse diximus.", "en": "God is absolutely infinite (by Def. 6 of Part I), that is (by Def. 6 of Part II), the nature of God enjoys infinite perfection, and that (by Prop. 3 of Part II) accompanied by the idea of himself, that is (by Prop. 11 and Def. 1 of Part I), by the idea of his own cause; and this is what we said in the Cor. of Prop. 32 of this Part is intellectual love."}, {"id": "E5P36", "p": "part5", "l": "5P36", "la": "Mentis amor intellectualis erga Deum est ipse Dei amor, quo Deus se ipsum amat, non quatenus infinitus est, sed quatenus per essentiam humanae mentis sub specie aeternitatis consideratam explicari potest, hoc est, mentis erga Deum amor intellectualis pars est infiniti amoris, quo Deus se ipsum amat.", "en": "The intellectual love of the mind towards God is the very love of God by which God loves himself, not insofar as he is infinite, but insofar as he can be explained through the essence of the human mind considered under a species of eternity; that is, the intellectual love of the mind towards God is part of the infinite love by which God loves himself."}, {"id": "E5P36D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P36D", "la": "Hic mentis amor ad mentis actiones referri debet (per coroll. prop. 32. huius et per prop. 3. P. 3.), qui proinde actio est, qua mens se ipsam contemplatur concomitante idea Dei tamquam causa (per prop. 32. huius et eius coroll.), hoc est (per coroll. prop. 25. P. 1. et coroll. prop. 11. P. 2.) actio, qua Deus, quatenus per mentem humanam explicari potest, seipsum contemplatur concomitante idea sui. Atque adeo (per prop. praeced.) hic mentis amor pars est infiniti amoris, quo Deus seipsum amat. Q. E. D.", "en": "This love of the mind must be related to the actions of the mind (by Cor. of Prop. 32 of this Part and by Prop. 3 of Part III); it is therefore an action by which the mind contemplates itself, accompanied by the idea of God as its cause (by Prop. 32 of this Part and its Cor.), that is (by Cor. of Prop. 25 of Part I and Cor. of Prop. 11 of Part II), an action by which God, insofar as he can be explained through the human mind, contemplates himself, accompanied by the idea of himself. And so (by the preceding Prop.) this love of the mind is part of the infinite love by which God loves himself. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P36C", "p": "part5", "l": "5P36C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, quod Deus, quatenus seipsum amat, homines amat, et consequenter quod amor Dei erga homines et mentis erga Deum amor intellectualis unum et idem sit.", "en": "Hence it follows that God, insofar as he loves himself, loves men, and consequently that the love of God towards men and the intellectual love of the mind towards God are one and the same."}, {"id": "E5P36S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P36S", "la": "Ex his clare intelligimus, qua in re nostra salus seu beatitudo seu libertas consistit, nempe in constanti et aeterno erga Deum amore, sive in amore Dei erga homines. Atque hic amor, seu beatitudo in sacris codicibus gloria appellatur; nec immerito.", "en": "From these things we understand clearly in what our salvation, or blessedness, or freedom consists: namely, in a constant and eternal love towards God, or in the love of God towards men. And this love, or blessedness, is called glory in the sacred books, and not without reason."}, {"id": "E5P36S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P36S", "la": "Nam sive hic amor ad Deum referatur sive ad mentem, recte animi acquiescentia, quae revera a gloria (per aff. defin. 25. et 30.) non distinguitur, appellari potest. Nam quatenus ad Deum refertur, est (per prop. 35. huius) laetitia (liceat hoc adhuc vocabulo uti) concomitante idea sui, ut et quatenus ad mentem refertur (per prop. 27. huius).", "en": "For whether this love is related to God or to the mind, it can rightly be called contentment of mind, which in reality is not distinguished from glory (by Defs. Aff. 25 and 30). For insofar as it is related to God, it is (by Prop. 35 of this Part) joy (if we may still use this word), accompanied by the idea of himself, as it is also insofar as it is related to the mind (by Prop. 27 of this Part)."}, {"id": "E5P36S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P36S", "la": "Deinde quia nostrae mentis essentia in sola cognitione consistit, cuius principium et fundamentum Deus est (per prop. 15. P. 1. et schol. prop. 47. P. 2.), hinc perspicuum nobis fit, quomodo et qua ratione mens nostra secundum essentiam et existentiam ex natura divina sequatur et continuo a Deo pendeat; quod hic notare operae pretium duxi, ut hoc exemplo ostenderem, quantum rerum singularium cognitio, quam intuitivam sive tertii generis appellavi (vide schol. 2. prop. 40. P. 2.), polleat, potiorque sit cognitione universali, quam secundi generis esse dixi.", "en": "Next, because the essence of our mind consists in knowledge alone, of which God is the principle and foundation (by Prop. 15 of Part I and Schol. of Prop. 47 of Part II), it becomes clear to us how and in what way our mind follows from the divine nature according to essence and existence, and continually depends on God, which I thought it worthwhile to note here, in order to show by this example how much the knowledge of singular things which I have called intuitive, or of the third kind (see Schol. 2 of Prop. 40 of Part II), can do, and how much more powerful it is than the universal knowledge which I have said is of the second kind."}, {"id": "E5P36S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P36S", "la": "Nam quamvis in prima parte generaliter ostenderim, omnia (et consequenter mentem etiam humanam) a Deo secundum essentiam et existentiam pendere, illa tamen demonstratio tametsi legitima sit et extra dubitationis aleam posita, non ita tamen mentem nostram afficit, quam quando id ipsum ex ipsa essentia rei cuiuscumque singularis, quam a Deo pendere dicimus, concluditur.", "en": "For although I have shown generally in Part I that all things (and consequently the human mind also) depend on God according to essence and existence, that demonstration, although it is legitimate and placed beyond the hazard of doubt, still does not affect our mind as much as when this same thing is concluded from the very essence of any singular thing which we say depends on God."}, {"id": "E5P37", "p": "part5", "l": "5P37", "la": "Nihil in natura datur, quod huic amori intellectuali sit contrarium, sive quod ipsum possit tollere.", "en": "There is nothing in nature which is contrary to this intellectual love, or which can take it away."}, {"id": "E5P37D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P37D", "la": "Hic intellectualis amor ex mentis natura necessario sequitur, quatenus ipsa ut aeterna veritas per Dei naturam consideratur (per prop. 33. et 29. huius). Si quid ergo daretur, quod huic amori esset contrarium, id contrarium esset vero, et consequenter id, quod hunc amorem posset tollere, efficeret, ut id, quod verum est, falsum esset; quod (ut per se notum) est absurdum. Ergo nihil in natura datur etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "This intellectual love follows necessarily from the nature of the mind, insofar as the mind is considered as an eternal truth through the nature of God (by Props. 33 and 29 of this Part). If, therefore, there were anything which was contrary to this love, it would be contrary to the true; and consequently that which could take this love away would bring it about that what is true would be false, which (as is self-evident) is absurd. Therefore there is nothing in nature, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P37S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P37S", "la": "Partis quartae axioma res singulares respicit, quatenus cum relatione ad certum tempus et locum considerantur, de quo neminem dubitare credo.", "en": "The axiom of Part IV concerns singular things insofar as they are considered in relation to a certain time and place, of which I believe no one has any doubt."}, {"id": "E5P38", "p": "part5", "l": "5P38", "la": "Quo plures res secundo et tertio cognitionis genere mens intelligit, eo minus ipsa ab affectibus, qui mali sunt, patitur et mortem minus timet.", "en": "The more things the mind understands by the second and third kinds of knowledge, the less it is acted on by affects which are evil, and the less it fears death."}, {"id": "E5P38D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P38D", "la": "Mentis essentia in cognitione consistit (per prop. 11. P. 2.); quo igitur mens plures res cognoscit secundo et tertio cognitionis genere, eo maior eius pars remanet (per prop. 23. et 29. huius), et consequenter (per prop. praeced.) eo maior eius pars non tangitur ab affectibus, qui nostrae naturae sunt contrarii, hoc est (per prop. 30. P. 4.) qui mali sunt. Quo itaque mens plures res secundo et tertio cognitionis genere intelligit, eo maior eius pars illaesa manet, et consequenter minus ab affectibus patitur etc. Q. E. D.", "en": "The essence of the mind consists in knowledge (by Prop. 11 of Part II); the more things, therefore, the mind knows by the second and third kinds of knowledge, the greater the part of it that remains (by Props. 23 and 29 of this Part), and consequently (by the preceding Prop.) the greater the part of it that is not touched by affects which are contrary to our nature, that is (by Prop. 30 of Part IV), which are evil. The more things, therefore, the mind understands by the second and third kinds of knowledge, the greater the part of it that remains unharmed, and consequently the less it is acted on by affects, etc. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P38S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P38S", "la": "Hinc intelligimus id quod in schol. prop. 39. P. 4. attigi et quod in hac parte explicare promisi; nempe quod mors eo minus est noxia, quo mentis clara et distincta cognitio maior est, et consequenter quo mens magis Deum amat. Deinde quia (per prop. 27. huius) ex tertio cognitionis genere summa, quae dari potest, oritur acquiescentia, hinc sequitur mentem humanam posse eius naturae esse, ut id, quod eius cum corpore perire ostendimus (vide prop. 21. huius), in respectu ad id, quod ipsius remanet, nullius sit momenti. Sed de his mox prolixius.", "en": "From this we understand what I touched on in Schol. of Prop. 39 of Part IV, and what I promised to explain in this Part: namely, that death is the less harmful, the greater the mind’s clear and distinct knowledge, and consequently the more the mind loves God. Next, because (by Prop. 27 of this Part) the greatest contentment there can be arises from the third kind of knowledge, it follows from this that the human mind can be of such a nature that what of it we have shown perishes with the body (see Prop. 21 of this Part) is of no importance in relation to what of it remains. But of these things more fully presently."}, {"id": "E5P39", "p": "part5", "l": "5P39", "la": "Qui corpus ad plurima aptum habet, is mentem habet, cuius maxima pars est aeterna.", "en": "He who has a body capable of a great many things has a mind whose greatest part is eternal."}, {"id": "E5P39D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P39D", "la": "Qui corpus ad plurima agendum aptum habet, is minime affectibus, qui mali sunt, conflictatur (per prop. 38. P. 4.), hoc est (per prop. 30. P. 4.) affectibus qui naturae nostrae sunt contrarii. Atque adeo (per prop. 10. huius) potestatem habet ordinandi et concatenandi corporis affectiones secundum ordinem ad intellectum, et consequenter efficiendi (per prop. 14. huius) ut omnes corporis affectiones ad Dei ideam referantur, ex quo fiet (per prop. 15. huius) ut erga Deum afficiatur amore, qui (per prop. 16. huius) mentis maximam partem occupare sive constituere debet; ac proinde (per prop. 33. huius) mentem habet, cuius maxima pars est aeterna. Q. E. D.", "en": "He who has a body capable of doing a great many things is least troubled by affects which are evil (by Prop. 38 of Part IV), that is (by Prop. 30 of Part IV), by affects which are contrary to our nature. And so (by Prop. 10 of this Part) he has the power of ordering and connecting the affections of the body according to the order of the intellect, and consequently of bringing it about (by Prop. 14 of this Part) that all the affections of the body are related to the idea of God; from which it will come about (by Prop. 15 of this Part) that he is affected with a love towards God which (by Prop. 16 of this Part) must occupy, or constitute, the greatest part of the mind; and accordingly (by Prop. 33 of this Part) he has a mind whose greatest part is eternal. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P39S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P39S", "la": "Quia corpora humana ad plurima apta sunt, non dubium est, quin eius naturae possint esse, ut ad mentes referantur, quae magnam sui et Dei habeant cognitionem, et quarum maxima seu praecipua pars est aeterna, atque adeo ut mortem vix timeant. Sed ut haec clarius intelligantur, animadvertendum hic est, quod nos in continua vivimus variatione, et prout in melius sive in peius mutamur, eo *felices* aut *infelices* dicimur.", "en": "Because human bodies are capable of a great many things, there is no doubt that they can be of such a nature that they are related to minds which have a great knowledge of themselves and of God, and whose greatest, or principal, part is eternal, so that they hardly fear death. But in order that these things may be understood more clearly, it should be noted here that we live in continual change, and are called happy or unhappy according as we change for the better or for the worse."}, {"id": "E5P39S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P39S", "la": "Qui enim ex infante vel puero in cadaver transiit, infelix dicitur, et contra id felicitati tribuitur, quod totum vitae spatium mente sana in corpore sano percurrere potuerimus. Et revera qui corpus habet, ut infans vel puer, ad paucissima aptum et maxime pendens a causis externis, mentem habet, quae in se sola considerata nihil fere sui, nec Dei, nec rerum sit conscia; et contra, qui corpus habet ad plurima aptum, mentem habet, quae in se sola considerata multum sui et Dei et rerum sit conscia.", "en": "For he who has passed from being an infant or a child into a corpse is called unhappy, and on the other hand it is counted as happiness if we have been able to pass through the whole span of life with a sound mind in a sound body. And in truth, he who, like an infant or a child, has a body capable of very few things and very greatly dependent on external causes, has a mind which, considered in itself alone, is conscious of almost nothing of itself, or of God, or of things; and on the other hand, he who has a body capable of a great many things has a mind which, considered in itself alone, is very much conscious of itself and of God and of things."}, {"id": "E5P39S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P39S", "la": "In hac vita igitur apprime conamur, ut corpus infantiae in aliud, quantum eius natura patitur eique conducit, mutetur, quod ad plurima aptum sit, quodque ad mentem referatur, quae sui et Dei et rerum plurimum sit conscia; atque ita ut id omne, quod ad ipsius memoriam vel imaginationem refertur, in respectu ad intellectum vix alicuius sit momenti, ut in schol. prop. praeced. iam dixi.", "en": "In this life, therefore, we strive above all that the body of infancy may change, as far as its nature allows and is conducive to it, into another which is capable of a great many things, and which is related to a mind very much conscious of itself and of God and of things; and so that everything which is related to its memory or imagination may be of hardly any importance in relation to the intellect, as I have already said in the Schol. of the preceding Prop.."}, {"id": "E5P40", "p": "part5", "l": "5P40", "la": "Quo unaquaeque res plus perfectionis habet, eo magis agit et minus patitur, et contra quo magis agit, eo perfectior est.", "en": "The more perfection each thing has, the more it acts and the less it is acted on; and conversely, the more it acts, the more perfect it is."}, {"id": "E5P40D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P40D", "la": "Quo unaquaeque res perfectior est, eo plus habet realitatis (per defin. 6. P. 2.), et consequenter (per prop. 3. P. 3. cum eius schol.) eo magis agit et minus patitur; quae quidem demonstratio inverso ordine eodem modo procedit; ex quo sequitur, ut res contra eo sit perfectior, quo magis agit. Q. E. D.", "en": "The more perfect each thing is, the more reality it has (by Def. 6 of Part II), and consequently (by Prop. 3 of Part III with its Schol.) the more it acts and the less it is acted on; and this demonstration proceeds in the same way in reverse order, from which it follows that, conversely, a thing is the more perfect the more it acts. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P40C", "p": "part5", "l": "5P40C", "la": "Hinc sequitur, partem mentis, quae remanet, quantacumque ea sit, perfectiorem esse reliqua. Nam pars mentis aeterna (per prop. 23. et 29. huius) est intellectus, per quem solum nos agere dicimur (per prop. 3. P. 3.); illa autem, quam perire ostendimus, est ipsa imaginatio (per prop. 21. huius), per quam solam dicimur pati (per prop. 3. P. 3. et gener. aff. defin.). Atque adeo (per prop. praeced.) illa, quantacumque ea sit, hac est perfectior. Q. E. D.", "en": "Hence it follows that the part of the mind which remains, however great it may be, is more perfect than the rest. For the eternal part of the mind (by Props. 23 and 29 of this Part) is the intellect, through which alone we are said to act (by Prop. 3 of Part III); but the part which we have shown perishes is the imagination itself (by Prop. 21 of this Part), through which alone we are said to be acted on (by Prop. 3 of Part III and the General Definition of the Affects). And so (by the preceding Prop.) the former, however great it may be, is more perfect than the latter. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P40S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P40S", "la": "Haec sunt, quae de mente, quatenus sine relatione ad corporis existentiam consideratur, ostendere constitueram. Ex quibus et simul ex prop. 21. P. 1. et aliis apparet, quod mens nostra, quatenus intelligit, aeternus cogitandi modus sit, qui alio aeterno cogitandi modo determinatur, et hic iterum ab alio, et sic in infinitum; ita ut omnes simul Dei aeternum et infinitum intellectum constituant.", "en": "These are the things I had decided to show concerning the mind insofar as it is considered without relation to the existence of the body. From these, and at the same time from Prop. 21 of Part I and other places, it is evident that our mind, insofar as it understands, is an eternal mode of thinking, which is determined by another eternal mode of thinking, and this again by another, and so to infinity; so that all together constitute the eternal and infinite intellect of God."}, {"id": "E5P41", "p": "part5", "l": "5P41", "la": "Quamvis nesciremus, mentem nostram aeternam esse, pietatem tamen et religionem et absolute omnia, quae ad animositatem et generositatem referri ostendimus in quarta parte, prima haberemus.", "en": "Even if we did not know that our mind is eternal, we would still regard as of first importance piety and religion, and absolutely all the things which we showed in Part IV to be related to tenacity and nobility."}, {"id": "E5P41D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P41D", "la": "Primum et unicum virtutis seu recte vivendi rationis fundamentum (per coroll. prop. 22. et per prop. 24. P. 4.) est suum utile quaerere. Ad illa autem determinandum, quae ratio utilia esse dictat, nullam rationem habuimus mentis aeternitatis, quam demum in hac quinta parte novimus. Quamvis igitur tum temporis ignoraverimus, mentem esse aeternam, illa tamen, quae ad animositatem et generositatem referri ostendimus, prima habuimus. Atque adeo quamvis etiam nunc hoc ipsum ignoraremus, eadem tamen rationis praescripta prima haberemus. Q. E. D.", "en": "The first and only foundation of virtue, or of the principle of living rightly (by Cor. of Prop. 22 and by Prop. 24 of Part IV), is to seek one’s own advantage. But in determining those things which reason dictates to be useful, we took no account of the eternity of the mind, which we have come to know only in this fifth Part. Although, therefore, we were at that time ignorant that the mind is eternal, we nevertheless regarded as of first importance those things which we showed to be related to tenacity and nobility. And so, even if we were still now ignorant of this, we would still regard the same prescriptions of reason as of first importance. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P41S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P41S", "la": "Communis vulgi persuasio alia videtur esse. Nam plerique videntur credere, se eatenus liberos esse, quatenus libidini parere licet, et eatenus de suo iure cedere, quatenus ex legis divinae praescripto vivere tenentur. Pietatem igitur et religionem et absolute omnia, quae ad animi fortitudinem referuntur, onera esse credunt, quae post mortem deponere, et pretium servitutis, nempe pietatis et religionis, accipere sperant.", "en": "The common persuasion of the multitude seems to be otherwise. For most seem to believe that they are free insofar as they are permitted to obey their lust, and that they give up their right insofar as they are bound to live according to the prescription of the divine law. Piety, therefore, and religion, and absolutely all the things which are related to strength of character, they believe to be burdens, which they hope to lay down after death, and to receive the reward of their servitude, namely of their piety and religion."}, {"id": "E5P41S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P41S", "la": "Nec hac spe sola, sed etiam et praecipue metu, ne diris scilicet suppliciis post mortem puniantur, inducuntur, ut ex legis divinae praescripto, quantum eorum fert tenuitas et impotens animus, vivant; et nisi haec spes et metus hominibus inessent, at contra si crederent, mentes cum corpore interire, nec restare miseris pietatis onere confectis, vivere longius, ad ingenium redirent, et ex libidine omnia moderari et fortunae potius, quam sibi parere vellent.", "en": "And they are induced, not by this hope alone but also and chiefly by fear, namely the fear of being punished with dreadful punishments after death, to live according to the prescription of the divine law as far as their feebleness and lack of power of mind allow; and if this hope and fear were not in men, but if, on the contrary, they believed that minds perish with the body, and that no longer life remains for the wretched who are worn out by the burden of piety, they would return to their own temperament, and would want to govern everything according to their lust and to obey fortune rather than themselves."}, {"id": "E5P41S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P41S", "la": "Quae mihi non minus absurda videntur, quam si quis propterea, quod non credit se posse bonis alimentis corpus in aeternum nutrire, venenis potius et letiferis se exsaturare vellet; vel quia videt mentem non esse aeternam seu immortalem, ideo amens mavult esse et sine ratione vivere; quae adeo absurda sunt, ut vix recenseri mereantur.", "en": "These things seem to me no less absurd than if someone, because he does not believe that he can nourish his body with good food for eternity, should prefer to fill himself with poisons and deadly things; or because he sees that the mind is not eternal, or immortal, should prefer to be out of his mind and to live without reason; which are so absurd that they hardly deserve to be mentioned."}, {"id": "E5P42", "p": "part5", "l": "5P42", "la": "Beatitudo non est virtutis praemium, sed ipsa virtus; nec eadem gaudemus, quia libidines coercemus; sed contra quia eadem gaudemus, ideo libidines coercere possumus.", "en": "Blessedness is not the reward of virtue, but virtue itself; nor do we enjoy it because we restrain our lusts, but on the contrary, because we enjoy it, we can restrain our lusts."}, {"id": "E5P42D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P42D", "la": "Beatitudo in amore erga Deum consistit (per prop. 36. huius et eius schol.), qui quidem amor ex tertio cognitionis genere oritur (per coroll. prop. 32. huius). Atque adeo hic amor (per prop. 59. et 3. P. 3.) ad mentem, quatenus agit, referri debet, ac proinde (per defin. 8. P. 4.) ipsa virtus est; quod erat primum.", "en": "Blessedness consists in love towards God (by Prop. 36 of this Part and its Schol.), which love arises from the third kind of knowledge (by Cor. of Prop. 32 of this Part). And so this love (by Props. 59 and 3 of Part III) must be related to the mind insofar as it acts; and accordingly (by Def. 8 of Part IV) it is virtue itself; which was the first point."}, {"id": "E5P42D", "p": "part5", "l": "5P42D", "la": "Deinde quo mens hoc amore divino seu beatitudine magis gaudet, eo plus intelligit (per prop. 32. huius), hoc est (per coroll. prop. 3. huius) eo maiorem in affectus habet potentiam, et (per prop. 38. huius) eo minus ab affectibus, qui mali sunt, patitur. Atque adeo ex eo, quod mens hoc amore divino seu beatitudine gaudet, potestatem habet libidines coercendi; et quia humana potentia ad coercendos affectus in solo intellectu consistit, ergo nemo beatitudine gaudet, quia affectus coercuit, sed contra potestas libidines coercendi ex ipsa beatitudine oritur. Q. E. D.", "en": "Next, the more the mind enjoys this divine love, or blessedness, the more it understands (by Prop. 32 of this Part), that is (by Cor. of Prop. 3 of this Part), the greater the power it has over the affects, and (by Prop. 38 of this Part) the less it is acted on by affects which are evil. And so, from the fact that the mind enjoys this divine love, or blessedness, it has the power of restraining lusts; and because human power to restrain the affects consists in the intellect alone, no one enjoys blessedness because he has restrained the affects, but on the contrary the power of restraining lusts arises from blessedness itself. Q.E.D."}, {"id": "E5P42S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P42S", "la": "His omnia, quae de mentis in affectus potentia, quaeque de mentis libertate ostendere volueram, absolvi. Ex quibus apparet, quantum sapiens polleat, potiorque sit ignaro, qui sola libidine agitur. Ignarus enim, praeterquam quod a causis externis multis modis agitatur, nec unquam vera animi acquiescentia potitur, vivit praeterea sui et Dei et rerum quasi inscius, et simulac pati desinit, simul etiam esse desinit. Cum contra sapiens, quatenus ut talis consideratur, vix animo movetur, sed sui et Dei et rerum aeterna quadam necessitate conscius, nunquam esse desinit, sed semper vera animi acquiescentia potitur.", "en": "With these I have completed all that I wished to show concerning the power of the mind over the affects and concerning the freedom of the mind. From which it is evident how much the wise man is capable of, and how much more powerful he is than the ignorant man, who is driven by lust alone. For the ignorant man, besides being driven about in many ways by external causes and never possessing true contentment of mind, lives as if unconscious of himself, and of God, and of things, and as soon as he ceases to be acted on, he also ceases to be. The wise man, on the other hand, insofar as he is considered as such, is hardly moved in mind, but, being conscious of himself, and of God, and of things by a certain eternal necessity, never ceases to be, but always possesses true contentment of mind."}, {"id": "E5P42S", "p": "part5", "l": "5P42S", "la": "Si iam via, quam ad haec ducere ostendi, perardua videatur, inveniri tamen potest. Et sane arduum debet esse, quod adeo raro reperitur. Qui enim posset fieri, si salus in promptu esset et sine magno labore reperiri posset, ut ab omnibus fere negligeretur? Sed omnia praeclara tam difficilia, quam rara sunt.", "en": "If the way which I have shown to lead to these things now seems very hard, it can nevertheless be found. And indeed what is so rarely found must be hard. For how could it happen, if salvation were ready to hand and could be found without great labour, that it should be neglected by almost everyone? But all things excellent are as difficult as they are rare."}, {"id": "Ep8", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Iamdudum exoptavi tibi semel adesse, sed tempus, magnaque hyems non satis mihi propitia fuerunt. Aliquando de sorte mea queror, quod nobis intercedit spatium, quod nos ab invicem tam longe dividit.", "en": "For a long time I have wished to be with you once, but the season and the hard winter have not been kind enough to me. Sometimes I complain of my lot, that there lies between us a distance which separates us so far from one another."}, {"id": "Ep8", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Felix, imo Felicissimus tuus socius Casuarius sub eodem tecto remorans, qui inter prandendum, coenandum, ambulandumque tecum optimis de rebus sermones habere potest.", "en": "Happy, indeed most happy, is your companion Casearius, who lives under the same roof and can talk with you of the best things at dinner, at supper and on your walks."}, {"id": "Ep8", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Quamvis autem corpora ab invicem tam longe divisa sint, animo tamen saepissime praesens adfuisti meo, praesertim tuis in scriptis cum versor, manibusque tracto. Sed cum nobis collegis non omnia satis clara appareant, (ideoque iterum colegii initium fecimus) neque me tui immemorem esse putes, animum ad hasce litteras scribendas appuli.", "en": "But although our bodies are separated so far from one another, you have very often been present to my mind, especially when I am occupied with your writings and have them in my hands. But since not everything appears clear enough to us members of the group (and for this reason we have begun our meetings again), and so that you should not think me forgetful of you, I have set myself to write this letter."}, {"id": "Ep8.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Collegium quod attinet, eo instituitur modo: Unus (sed suae cuique vices) perlegit, pro suo conceptu explicat, porroque omnia demonstrat, secundum tuarum propositionum seriem, ac ordinem; tum si accidat, ut alter alteri satisfacere non possit, operae pretium esse duximus, illud annotare, atque ad te scribere, ut, si possibile, nobis clarius reddatur, et duce te contra superstitiose religiosos, Christianosque veritatem defendere, tum totius impetum mundi stare possimus. Quare, cum prius perlegentibus, explicantibusque, definitiones, non omnes nobis clarae videbantur, non idem de natura definitionis iudicium tulimus.", "en": "As for the group, it is arranged in this way. One of us (each in his turn) reads the text through, explains it as he understands it, and then demonstrates everything, following the sequence and order of your propositions. Then, if it happens that one cannot satisfy another, we have thought it worth while to note the point and write to you, so that, if possible, it may be made clearer to us, and so that under your guidance we may defend the truth against those who are superstitiously religious and Christian, and withstand the onslaught of the whole world. So, since not all the definitions seemed clear to us when we first read them through and explained them, we did not all form the same judgment about the nature of definition."}, {"id": "Ep8.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Interim te absente authorem quendam, mathematicum scil., qui Borellus vocatur, consuluimus; ille dum definitionis naturae, axiomatis, et postulati mentionem facit, atque aliorum de ea re sententias adfert.", "en": "Meanwhile, in your absence, we consulted an author, namely a mathematician called Borelli; he, while he mentions the nature of definition, of axiom and of postulate, and brings forward the opinions of others on the subject."}, {"id": "Ep8.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Ipsius vero sententia sic sonat: Definitiones adhibentur in demonstratione, ut praemissae. Quare necesse est, ut sint evidenter cognitae, alias cognitio scientifica, seu evidentissima ex eis acquiri non potest. alio loco: Non temere, sed maxima cautione eligi debet ratio structurae, aut essentialis passio prima, et notissima alicuius subiecti. Nam si constructio, et passio nominata sit impossibilis, tunc non efficietur definitio scientifica, ut si quis diceret: Duae rectae lineae spatium comprehendentes, vocentur figurales, essent definitiones non entium, et impossibiles; et propterea potius ignorantia, quam scientia ex iis deduceretur. Deinde si constructio, aut passio nominata sit quidem possibilis et vera, sed nobis ignota, aut dubia; tunc bona definitio non erit: Nam conclusiones ab ignoto, et dubio ortae, incertae quoque, et dubiae erunt; et ideo suspisionem, aut opinionem; non autem scientiam certam afferent.", "en": "His own opinion runs thus: ‘Definitions are used in demonstration as premises. So it is necessary that they should be evidently known; otherwise scientific, or most evident, knowledge cannot be acquired from them.’ And in another place: ‘The principle of the construction, or the first and best-known essential property of a subject, must be chosen not rashly but with the greatest caution. For if the construction and the property named are impossible, then no scientific definition will be produced; as if someone were to say, Let two straight lines enclosing a space be called figurals: these would be definitions of non-beings, and impossible, and so ignorance rather than knowledge would be deduced from them. Next, if the construction or the property named is indeed possible and true, but unknown or doubtful to us, then the definition will not be a good one; for conclusions which arise from what is unknown and doubtful will also be uncertain and doubtful, and so will produce suspicion or opinion, but not certain knowledge.’"}, {"id": "Ep8.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "A cuius opinione dissentire videtur Tacquet, qui autumat ex falso pronuntiato directe ad veram conclusionem procedi posse, uti tibi notum.", "en": "Tacquet seems to dissent from this opinion, who maintains, as you know, that one can proceed directly from a false statement to a true conclusion."}, {"id": "Ep8.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Clavius vero, cuius etiam sententiam introducit, sic sentit: Definitiones sunt artis vocabula, neque opus est, ut ratio afferatur, cur res aliqua hoc aut illo definiatur modo; sed satis esse, ut nunquam res definita asseratur alicui convenire, nisi prius definitionem traditam eidem convenire demonstretur. Ita ut Borellus velit, quod definitio alicuius subiecti debeat constare ex passione sive structura prima, essentiali, nobis notissima, et vera; Clavius non ita, sive prima, sive notissima, sive vera nec ne, nihil refert, modo ne definitionem, quam tradidimus, alicui convenire asseratur, nisi prius definitionem traditam eidem convenire demonstretur.", "en": "Clavius, however, whose opinion he also introduces, thinks thus: ‘Definitions are terms of art, and there is no need to give a reason why a thing is defined in this way or in that; it is enough that the thing defined should never be asserted to agree with anything unless it is first demonstrated that the definition given agrees with it.’ So Borelli holds that the definition of a subject must consist of a property, or construction, that is first, essential, best known to us, and true; Clavius not so: whether it is first, or best known, or true or not, makes no difference, provided that the definition we have given is not asserted to agree with anything unless it is first demonstrated that the definition given agrees with it."}, {"id": "Ep8.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Nos potius pedibus in sententiam Borelli iremus; utri dominus, an neutri horum concinnat, non bene scimus: Igitur, cum tales de definitionis natura, quae inter principia demonstrationis numeratur, disceptationes variae moventur, et, si animus non vindicatus est ab eius difficultatibus, etiam non de iis, quae de ea deducuntur; magnum nobis esset desiderium, ut dominatio vestra ad nos, multum nimis negotium tibi si non facessamus, perque otium tuum liceat, scriberet, quid ipsa de ea re sentiat, etiam quae est distinctio inter axiomata et definitiones; Borellus vero nullam veram admittit, nisi quoad nomen, sed te aliam ponere credo.", "en": "We would rather side with Borelli’s opinion; but which of the two you, Sir, agree with, or whether with neither, we do not well know. Since, therefore, such various disputes are raised about the nature of definition, which is counted among the principles of demonstration, and since, if the mind is not freed from its difficulties, it is not freed from those either which concern what is deduced from it, we should very much like you, Sir, to write to us, if we are not giving you too much trouble and your leisure allows, what you think of the matter, and also what the distinction is between axioms and definitions. Borelli admits no true distinction except as to the name; but I believe that you posit another."}, {"id": "Ep8.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Porro definitio 3tia nobis non satis constat, in exemplum attuli, quod dominus mihi dixit Hagaecomitis, scil.: quod res duobus modis potest considerari, vel prout in se est, vel prout respectum habet ad aliud, uti intellectus, vel enim potest considerari sub cogitatione, vel ut constans ideis. Sed, quaenam hic esset distinctio, non bene videmus; nam existimamus, quod nos, si cogitationem recte concipiamus, oporteat comprehendere sub ideis, quia remotis ab ea omnibus ideis cogitationem destrueremus. Qua de re, cum exemplum nobis satis clarum non est, manet adhuc res ipsa aliquo modo obscura, et ulteriore explicatione indigemus.", "en": "Further, the third definition is not clear enough to us. As an example I brought forward what you, Sir, told me at The Hague, namely that a thing can be considered in two ways, either as it is in itself, or as it has a relation to something else; as the intellect, for it can be considered either under thought or as consisting of ideas. But we do not well see what the distinction would be here; for we think that if we conceive thought rightly, we must comprehend it under ideas, since if all ideas were removed from it we would destroy thought. On this account, since the example is not clear enough to us, the matter itself still remains somewhat obscure, and we need a further explanation."}, {"id": "Ep8.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Novissime, in scholio 3tio prop. 8. habetur ab initio, sic scl: Ex his apparet, quod quamvis duo attributa, realiter distincta concipiantur (hoc est unum sine alterius ope) neque ideo ipsa duo entia sive duas diversas substantias constituunt; ratio est, quia de natura substantiae est, ut omnia eius attributa, unumquodque inquam, per se concipiatur, quandoquidem simul in ipsa fuerunt.", "en": "Lastly, at the beginning of the third scholium to Prop. 8 there is this: ‘From these things it is apparent that although two attributes are conceived as really distinct (that is, one without the aid of the other), they do not on that account constitute two beings, or two different substances; the reason is that it is of the nature of substance that all its attributes, each one, I say, should be conceived through itself, since they have been in it together.’"}, {"id": "Ep8.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Sic dominus videtur supponere, substantiae naturam ita esse constitutam, ut plura possit habere attributa, quod nondum demonstravit, nisi quintam definitionem substantiae absolute infinitae, sive Dei respiceret; alias, si dicam, unamquamque substantiam tantum habere unum attributum, et mihi esset idea duorum attributorum, recte concludere possem, quod ubi duo diversa attributa, ibi duae diversae sint substantiae: in quod etiam clariorem explicationem te rogemus.", "en": "So you seem to suppose that the nature of substance is so constituted that it can have several attributes, which you have not yet demonstrated, unless you are referring to the fifth definition, of absolutely infinite substance, or God. Otherwise, if I were to say that each substance has only one attribute, and I had the idea of two attributes, I could rightly conclude that where there are two different attributes, there are two different substances. On this too we ask you for a clearer explanation."}, {"id": "Ep8.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Porro gratias ago maximas pro tuis scriptis, mihi a P. Balling communicatis; quae magno me gaudio affecerunt. Sed potissimum scholium prop. 19.; si hic et tibi, in aliquo, quod in mea potestate sit, servire possim, ibi tibi sum, tantum opus, ut me scientem facias. Ego collegium annotomiae inivi, medium fere absolvi, absoluto, incipiam chimicum, sicque suasore te percurram totam medicinam.", "en": "Further, I thank you very much for your writings, which P. Balling passed on to me, and which delighted me greatly. But above all the scholium to Prop. 19; if here too I can serve you in anything that is in my power, I am at your service; you have only to let me know. I have begun a course in anatomy and am nearly halfway through it; when it is finished I shall begin chemistry, and so, on your advice, I shall go through the whole of medicine."}, {"id": "Ep8.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Desino, et exspecto responsionem, et a me sis salutatus, qui sum", "en": "I stop, and await your reply; and receive my greetings, as from one who is"}, {"id": "Ep8.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Tibi addictissimus S. I. de Vries.", "en": "Yours most devotedly, S. J. de Vries."}, {"id": "Ep8.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 8", "la": "Datum Amstelodami die 24 februarii 1663.", "en": "Given at Amsterdam, 24 February 1663."}, {"id": "Ep9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 9", "la": "Quod ad quaestiones a te propositas attinet. Video te iis haerere, propterea quod non distinguis inter genera definitionum. Nempe inter definitionem, quae inservit ad rem, cuius essentia tantum quaeritur, et de qua sola dubitatur, explicandam, et inter definitionem, quae ut solum examinetur, proponitur. Illa enim, quia determinatum habet obiectum, vera debet esse; haec vero id non requirit.", "en": "As for the questions you have put to me. I see that you are held up by them because you do not distinguish between the kinds of definition: namely between a definition which serves to explain a thing whose essence alone is sought, and about which alone there is doubt, and a definition which is proposed only in order to be examined. For the former, because it has a determinate object, must be true; the latter does not require this."}, {"id": "Ep9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 9", "la": "Ex. gr. si quis templi Solomonis descriptionem me roget, ipsi veram templi descriptionem tradere debeo, nisi cum ipso cupiam garrire. Sed si ego templum aliquod in mente concinnavi, quod aedificare cupio: ex cuius descriptione concludo me talem fundum, totque millia lapidum, aliorumque materialium emere debere, aliquisne sanae mentis mihi dicet, me male conclusisse, ex eo quod forte falsam adhibui definitionem? vel an aliquis a me exiget, ut meam definitionem probem? Is sane nihil aliud mihi dicit, quam quod id, quod conceperam, non conceperim, vel a me exiget, ut id, quod conceperam, probem, me concepisse, quod sane est nugari.", "en": "For example, if someone asks me for a description of Solomon’s temple, I must give him a true description of the temple, unless I want to talk nonsense with him. But if I have devised in my mind some temple which I want to build, and conclude from its description that I must buy such a piece of ground and so many thousand stones and other materials, will anyone of sound mind tell me that I have concluded badly because I have perhaps used a false definition? Or will anyone require me to prove my definition? He would certainly be telling me nothing other than that I have not conceived what I had conceived, or requiring me to prove that I have conceived what I had conceived; which is certainly to trifle."}, {"id": "Ep9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 9", "la": "Quare definitio vel explicat rem, prout est extra intellectum, et tum vera debet esse, et a propositione, vel axiomate non differre, nisi quod illa tantum circa rerum, rerumve affectionum essentias versatur; hoc vero latius, nempe ad aeternas veritates etiam se extendit: Vel explicat rem, prout a nobis concipitur, vel concipi potest, tumque in eo etiam differt ab axiomate et propositione, quod non exigit, nisi ut concipiatur absolute, non ut axioma sub ratione veri. Quare mala definitio illa est, quae non concipitur.", "en": "So a definition either explains a thing as it is outside the intellect, and then it must be true, and must not differ from a proposition, or axiom, except in that the former is concerned only with the essences of things, or of the affections of things, whereas the latter extends more widely, namely to eternal truths as well; or it explains a thing as it is conceived by us, or can be conceived, and then it differs from an axiom and a proposition in this too, that it requires only that it be conceived absolutely, not, like an axiom, under the aspect of the true. So a bad definition is one which is not conceived."}, {"id": "Ep9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 9", "la": "Quod ut intelligatur, Borelli exemplum capiam. Nempe si quis diceret, duae rectae lineae, spatium comprehendentes, vocentur figurales. Si is per lineam rectam, intelligit id, quod omnes per lineam curvam, tum bona est definitio, (per illam enim definitionem intelligeretur figura, ut [figurae], aut similes) modo quadrata, et alias postea non intelligat figuras. Verum si per lineam rectam intelligat id, quod communiter intelligimus, res est plane inconceptibilis, ideoque nulla est definitio. Quae omnia a Borello, in cuius sententia amplectenda pronius es, plane confunduntur.", "en": "To make this understood, I shall take Borelli’s example: namely, if someone were to say, Let two straight lines enclosing a space be called figurals. If by a straight line he understands what everyone understands by a curved line, then the definition is good (for by that definition would be understood figures such as [figures], or similar ones), provided that he does not afterwards understand by it squares and other figures. But if by a straight line he understands what we commonly understand, the thing is entirely inconceivable, and so it is no definition. All these things are completely confused by Borelli, to whose opinion you are more inclined."}, {"id": "Ep9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 9", "la": "Aliud exemplum addo, id nempe, quod in fine adfers. Si dicam unamquamque substantiam unum tantum habere attributum, mera est propositio, et eget demonstratione. Si vero dicam per substantiam intelligo id, quod uno tantum attributo constat, bona erit definitio, modo postea entia pluribus attributis constantia alio nomine a substantia diverso insigniantur.", "en": "I add another example, namely the one you bring forward at the end. If I say that each substance has only one attribute, it is a mere proposition, and needs demonstration. But if I say, By substance I understand that which consists of only one attribute, it will be a good definition, provided that afterwards beings consisting of several attributes are designated by another name, different from substance."}, {"id": "Ep9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 9", "la": "Quod autem dicis, me non demonstrare substantiam (sive ens) plura habere posse attributa, forte ad demonstrationes noluisti attendere. Duas enim adhibui, prima quod nihil nobis evidentius, quam quod unumquodque ens sub aliquo attributo a nobis concipiatur, et quo plus realitatis, aut esse aliquod ens habet, eo plura attributa ei sunt tribuenda. Unde ens absolute infinitum definiendum, etc. Secunda, et quam ego palmariam iudico, est, quod quo plura attributa alicui enti tribuo, eo magis cogor, ipsi existentiam tribuere, hoc est, eo magis sub ratione veri ipsum concipio, quod plane contrarium esset, si ego chymaeram, aut quid simile finxissem.", "en": "As for your saying that I do not demonstrate that substance (or being) can have several attributes, perhaps you did not want to attend to the demonstrations. For I used two. The first: that nothing is more evident to us than that each being is conceived by us under some attribute, and that the more reality, or being, a being has, the more attributes must be attributed to it. Hence a being absolutely infinite must be defined, etc. The second, which I judge the chief one, is that the more attributes I attribute to a being, the more I am compelled to attribute existence to it, that is, the more I conceive it under the aspect of the true; which would be quite the contrary if I had feigned a chimera or something similar."}, {"id": "Ep9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 9", "la": "Quod autem dicis, te non concipere cogitationem, nisi sub ideis, quia remotis ideis cogitationem destruis, credo id tibi contingere propterea, quod dum tu, res scilicet cogitans, id facis, omnes tuas cogitationes et conceptus seponis. Quare non mirum est, quod ubi omnes tuas cogitationes seposuisti, nihil postea tibi cogitandum supersit.", "en": "As for your saying that you do not conceive thought except under ideas, because when the ideas are removed you destroy thought, I believe this happens to you because, while you, a thinking thing, do this, you set aside all your thoughts and concepts. So it is no wonder that when you have set aside all your thoughts, nothing afterwards remains for you to think."}, {"id": "Ep9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 9", "la": "Quod autem ad rem attinet, puto me satis clare, et evidenter demonstrasse, intellectum, quamvis infinitum, ad naturam naturatam, non vero ad naturantem pertinere. Porro quid hoc ad tertiam definitionem intelligendum faciat, nondum video, nec etiam cur ea moram iniiciat.", "en": "As for the matter itself, I think I have demonstrated clearly and evidently enough that the intellect, even though infinite, pertains to Natura naturata, not to Natura naturans. Further, I do not yet see what this has to do with understanding the third definition, nor why it should hold you up."}, {"id": "Ep9.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 9", "la": "Ipsa enim definitio, ut ipsam, ni fallor, tibi tradidi, sic sonat: Per substantiam intelligo id, quod in se est, et per se concipitur, hoc est, cuius conceptus non involvit conceptum alterius rei. Idem per attributum intelligo, nisi quod attributum dicatur respectu intellectus, substantiae certam talem naturam tribuentis. Haec, inquam, definitio satis clare, quid per substantiam, sive attributum intelligere volo, explicat.", "en": "For the definition itself, as I gave it to you, if I am not mistaken, runs thus: ‘By substance I understand that which is in itself and is conceived through itself, that is, whose concept does not involve the concept of another thing. By attribute I understand the same, except that it is called attribute in relation to the intellect, which attributes such a definite nature to substance.’ This definition, I say, explains clearly enough what I want to understand by substance, or attribute."}, {"id": "Ep9.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 9", "la": "Vis tamen, quod minime opus est, ut exemplo explicem, quomodo una, eademque res duobus nominibus insigniri possit. Sed ne parcus videar duo adhibebo.", "en": "You want me nevertheless, though there is no need, to explain by an example how one and the same thing can be designated by two names. But so as not to seem stingy, I shall give two."}, {"id": "Ep9.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 9", "la": "Primo dico per Israelem intelligi tertium patriarcham, idem per Iacobum intelligo, quod nomen Iacobi ipsi imponebatur propterea, quod calcem fratris apprehenderat. Secundo per planum intelligo id, quod omnes radios lucis sine ulla mutatione reflectit, idem per album intelligo, nisi quod album dicatur respectu hominis planum intuentis. etc.", "en": "First, I say that by Israel is understood the third patriarch; I understand the same by Jacob, the name Jacob having been given to him because he had seized his brother’s heel. Second, by plane I understand that which reflects all rays of light without any change; I understand the same by white, except that it is called white in relation to a man looking at the plane, etc."}, {"id": "Ep10", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 10", "la": "Petis a me, an egeamus experientia ad sciendum, utrum definitio alicuius attributi sit vera? Ad hoc respondeo, nos nunquam egere experientia, nisi ad illa, quae ex rei definitione non possunt concludi, ut, ex. gr. existentia modorum: haec enim a rei definitione non potest concludi. Non vero ad illa, quorum existentia ab eorundem essentia non distinguitur, ac proinde ab eorum definitione concluditur.", "en": "You ask me whether we need experience to know whether the definition of some attribute is true. To this I answer that we never need experience except for those things which cannot be inferred from the definition of the thing, as, for example, the existence of modes; for this cannot be inferred from the definition of the thing. But not for those things whose existence is not distinguished from their essence, and is therefore inferred from their definition."}, {"id": "Ep10", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 10", "la": "Imo nulla experientia id unquam nos edocere poterit: nam experientia nullas rerum essentias docet; sed summum, quod efficere potest, est mentem nostram determinare, ut circa certas tantum rerum essentias cogitet. Quare, cum existentia attributorum ab eorum essentia non differat, eam nulla experientia poterimus assequi.", "en": "Indeed no experience will ever be able to teach us this; for experience teaches no essences of things, but the most it can do is to determine our mind to think only about certain essences of things. So, since the existence of the attributes does not differ from their essence, we shall not be able to attain it by any experience."}, {"id": "Ep10.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 10", "la": "Quod porro petis, anne res etiam, rerumve affectiones sint aeternae veritates? Dico omnino.", "en": "As for your further question, whether things too, or the affections of things, are eternal truths: I say, certainly."}, {"id": "Ep10.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 10", "la": "Si regeris, cur eas aeternas veritates non voco? respondeo, ut eas distinguam, uti omnes solent, ab iis, quae nullam rem, reive affectionem explicant, ut, ex. gr. a nihilo nihil fit; haec, inquam, similesque propositiones vocantur absolute aeternae veritates, sub quo nihil aliud significare volunt, quam quod talia nullam sedem habent extra mentem etc.", "en": "If you reply, why then do I not call them eternal truths? I answer: to distinguish them, as everyone usually does, from those which explain no thing, or affection of a thing, as, for example, nothing comes from nothing; these and similar propositions, I say, are called eternal truths absolutely, by which they mean nothing else than that such things have no seat outside the mind, etc."}, {"id": "Ep12", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Duas abs te accepi epistolas, unam die 11 Ianuarii datam, et ab amico N.N. mihi traditam; alteram vero 26 die Martii, et ab amico, nescio quo, Leida missam. Pergratae mihi ambae fuerunt; praecipue, ubi ex iis, omnia tua optime se habere, teque mei saepe memorem intellexi. Porro pro tua erga me humanitate, et honore, quo semper me dignatus es afficere, maximas, quas debeo, ago gratias; simulque precor, ut me tibi non minus addictum credas, quod semper data occasione, quantum mea ferre poterit tenuitas, ostendere conabor. Atque hoc ut incipiam, ad id, quod in tuis epistolis ex me quaeris, respondere curabo.", "en": "I have received two letters from you, one dated 11 January and handed to me by our friend N.N., the other dated 26 March and sent from Leiden by some friend, I do not know whom. Both were very welcome to me, especially since I learned from them that all is well with you and that you often think of me. Further, I give you the greatest thanks I owe for the kindness and honour which you have always deigned to show me; and at the same time I beg you to believe that I am no less devoted to you, which, whenever the opportunity is given, I shall try to show, as far as my slender abilities allow. And to make a beginning, I shall see to answering what you ask of me in your letters."}, {"id": "Ep12", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Petis autem, ut, quae de infinito excogitata habeam, tibi communicem, quod libentissime faciam. Quaestio de infinito omnibus semper difficillima, imo inextricabilis visa fuit, propterea quod non distinxerunt inter id, quod sua natura, sive vi suae definitionis sequitur esse infinitum; et id, quod nullos fines habet, non quidem vi suae essentiae; sed vi suae causae. Ac etiam, quia non distinxerunt inter id, quod infinitum dicitur, quia nullos habet fines; et id, cuius partes, quamvis eius maximum et minimum habeamus; nullo tamen numero adaequare, et explicare possumus. Denique quia non distinxerunt inter id, quod solummodo intelligere, non vero imaginari; et inter id, quod etiam imaginari possumus.", "en": "You ask, then, that I communicate to you what I have thought out concerning the infinite, which I shall most gladly do. The question of the infinite has always seemed to everyone most difficult, indeed inextricable, because they have not distinguished between that which follows to be infinite by its own nature, or by the force of its definition, and that which has no limits, not indeed by the force of its essence, but by the force of its cause. And also because they have not distinguished between that which is called infinite because it has no limits, and that whose parts, though we have its maximum and minimum, we cannot equal or explain by any number. And finally because they have not distinguished between that which we can only understand, but not imagine, and that which we can also imagine."}, {"id": "Ep12", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Ad haec, inquam, si attendissent, nunquam tam ingenti difficultatum turba obruti fuissent. Clare enim tum intellexissent, quale infinitum in nullas partes dividi, seu nullas partes habere potest; quale vero contra, idque sine contradictione. Porro etiam intellexissent, quale infinitum maius alio infinito sine ulla implicantia; quale vero non item concipi potest; quod ex mox dicendis clare apparebit.", "en": "If they had attended to these things, I say, they would never have been overwhelmed by so great a crowd of difficulties. For they would then have understood clearly what kind of infinite cannot be divided into any parts, or can have no parts, and what kind, on the contrary, can, and that without contradiction. Further, they would also have understood what kind of infinite can be conceived greater than another infinite without any contradiction, and what kind cannot; as will appear clearly from what I am about to say."}, {"id": "Ep12", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Verum prius haec quatuor paucis exponam, videlicet substantiam, modum, aeternitatem, et durationem. Quae circa substantiam considerari velim, sunt.", "en": "But first I shall briefly explain these four: namely substance, mode, eternity and duration. What I should like to be considered concerning substance is this."}, {"id": "Ep12", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Primo, quod ad eius essentiam pertinet existentia, hoc est, quod ex sola eius essentia, et definitione sequatur eam existere; quod, nisi me mea fallit memoria, antehac tibi viva voce absque ope aliarum propositionum demonstravi. Secundum, et quod ex hoc primo sequitur, est, quod substantia non multiplex; sed unica duntaxat eiusdem naturae existat. Tertium denique, quod omnis substantia non nisi infinita possit intelligi.", "en": "First, that existence pertains to its essence, that is, that it follows from its essence and definition alone that it exists; which, if my memory does not deceive me, I demonstrated to you earlier by word of mouth, without the help of other propositions. Second, and following from the first, that substance does not exist as many, but only as one of the same nature. Third, finally, that every substance can only be understood as infinite."}, {"id": "Ep12", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Substantiae vero affectiones modos voco, quorum definitio, quatenus non est ipsa substantiae definitio, nullam existentiam involvere potest. Quapropter, quamvis existant, eos ut non existentes concipere possumus: ex quo porro sequitur, nos, ubi ad solam modorum essentiam; non vero ad ordinem totius naturae attendimus, non posse concludere ex eo, quod iam existant, ipsos postea exstituros, aut non exstituros, vel antea exstitisse, aut non exstitisse. Unde clare apparet, nos existentiam substantiae toto genere a modorum existentia diversam concipere.", "en": "But the affections of substance I call modes, whose definition, insofar as it is not the very definition of substance, can involve no existence. So, even though they exist, we can conceive them as not existing; from which it further follows that, when we attend only to the essence of modes and not to the order of the whole of nature, we cannot conclude from their existing now that they will exist afterwards or will not exist, or that they existed before or did not exist. From this it is clearly apparent that we conceive the existence of substance as entirely different in kind from the existence of modes."}, {"id": "Ep12", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Ex quo oritur differentia inter aeternitatem, et durationem; per durationem enim modorum tantum existentiam explicare possumus; substantiae vero per aeternitatem, hoc est, infinitam existendi, sive, invita latinitate, essendi fruitionem.", "en": "From this arises the difference between eternity and duration; for by duration we can explain only the existence of modes, but that of substance by eternity, that is, the infinite enjoyment of existing, or, in defiance of Latinity, of being."}, {"id": "Ep12.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Ex quibus omnibus clare constat, nos modorum existentiam et durationem, ubi, ut saepissime fit, ad solam eorum essentiam; non vero ad ordinem naturae attendimus, ad libitum, et quidem propterea nullatenus, quem eorum habemus conceptum, destruendo, determinare, maiorem minoremque concipere, atque in partes dividere posse: Aeternitatem vero, et substantiam, quandoquidem non nisi infinitae concipi possunt, nihil eorum pati posse; nisi simul eorum conceptum destruamus.", "en": "From all this it is clearly established that when, as very often happens, we attend only to the essence of modes and not to the order of nature, we can determine at will the existence and duration of modes, conceive it as greater or less, and divide it into parts, without thereby in any way destroying the concept we have of them; but that eternity and substance, since they can only be conceived as infinite, can undergo none of these things unless we destroy their concept at the same time."}, {"id": "Ep12.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Quare ii prorsus garriunt, ne dicam insaniunt, qui substantiam extensam ex partibus, sive corporibus ab invicem realiter distinctis conflatam esse putant. Perinde enim est, ac si quis ex sola additione, et coacervatione multorum circulorum quadratum, aut triangulum, aut quid aliud, tota essentia diversum, conflare studeat.", "en": "So those who think that extended substance is put together from parts, or bodies really distinct from one another, are talking utter nonsense, not to say raving. For it is just as if someone were to try, by the mere addition and heaping up of many circles, to put together a square, or a triangle, or something else entirely different in its whole essence."}, {"id": "Ep12.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Quare omnis illa farrago argumentorum, quibus substantiam extensam finitam esse ostendere philosophi vulgo moliuntur, sua sponte ruit: Omnia enim illa substantiam corpoream ex partibus conflatam supponunt. Ad eundem etiam modum alii, qui postquam sibi persuaserunt, lineam ex punctis componi, multa invenire potuerunt argumenta, quibus ostenderent lineam non esse in infinitum divisibilem.", "en": "So that whole jumble of arguments by which philosophers commonly strive to show that extended substance is finite collapses of its own accord; for all of them suppose that corporeal substance is put together from parts. In the same way others too, having persuaded themselves that a line is composed of points, have been able to find many arguments to show that a line is not divisible to infinity."}, {"id": "Ep12.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Si tamen quaeras, cur naturae impulsu adeo propensi simus ad dividendam substantiam extensam: ad id respondeo, quod quantitas duobus modis a nobis concipiatur; abstracte scilicet, sive superficialiter, prout ope sensuum eam in imaginatione habemus; vel ut substantia, quod non nisi a solo intellectu fit. Itaque si ad quantitatem, prout est in imaginatione, attendimus, quod saepissime, et facilius fit, ea divisibilis, finita, ex partibus composita, et multiplex reperietur. Sin ad eandem, prout est in intellectu, attendamus, et res, ut in se est, percipiatur, quod difficillime fit, tum, ut satis antehac tibi demonstravi, infinita, indivisibilis, et unica reperietur.", "en": "If, however, you ask why we are so inclined by the impulse of nature to divide extended substance, I reply that quantity is conceived by us in two ways: namely abstractly, or superficially, as we have it in the imagination with the help of the senses; or as substance, which is done only by the intellect. So if we attend to quantity as it is in the imagination, which is done very often and more easily, it will be found divisible, finite, composed of parts, and many. But if we attend to it as it is in the intellect, and the thing is perceived as it is in itself, which is done with great difficulty, then, as I have sufficiently demonstrated to you before, it will be found infinite, indivisible, and unique."}, {"id": "Ep12.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Porro ex eo, quod durationem, et quantitatem pro libitu determinare possumus, ubi scilicet hanc a substantia abstractam concipimus, et illam a modo, quo a rebus aeternis fluit, separamus, oritur tempus, et mensura; tempus nempe ad durationem; mensura ad quantitatem tali modo determinandam, ut, quoad fieri potest, eas facile imaginemur.", "en": "Further, from the fact that we can determine duration and quantity at our pleasure, namely when we conceive the latter abstracted from substance and separate the former from the way in which it flows from eternal things, there arise time and measure: time, namely, for determining duration, and measure for determining quantity, in such a way that we may imagine them easily, as far as can be done."}, {"id": "Ep12.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Deinde ex eo, quod affectiones substantiae ab ipsa substantia separamus, et ad classes, ut eas quoad fieri potest, facile imaginemur, redigimus, oritur numerus, quo ipsas determinamus. Ex quibus clare videre est, mensuram, tempus, et numerum nihil esse praeter cogitandi, seu potius imaginandi modos.", "en": "Next, from the fact that we separate the affections of substance from substance itself and reduce them to classes, so that we may imagine them easily, as far as can be done, there arises number, by which we determine them. From these things it can be clearly seen that measure, time and number are nothing but modes of thinking, or rather of imagining."}, {"id": "Ep12.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Quare non mirum est, quod omnes, qui similibus notionibus, et quidem praeterea male intellectis, progressum naturae intelligere conati sunt, adeo mirifice se intricarint, ut tandem se extricare nequiverint, nisi omnia perrumpendo, et absurda etiam absurdissima admittendo.", "en": "So it is no wonder that all who have tried to understand the progress of nature by such notions, and moreover badly understood ones, have entangled themselves so marvellously that in the end they could not extricate themselves except by breaking through everything and admitting even the most absurd absurdities."}, {"id": "Ep12.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Nam cum multa sint, quae nequaquam imaginatione; sed solo intellectu assequi possumus, qualia sunt substantia, aeternitas, et alia. Si quis talia eiusmodi notionibus, quae duntaxat auxilia imaginationis sunt, explicare conatur: nihilo plus agit, quam si det operam, ut sua imaginatione insaniat.", "en": "For since there are many things which we can attain not by the imagination at all, but only by the intellect, such as substance, eternity and others. If anyone tries to explain such things by notions of this kind, which are merely aids of the imagination, he does nothing more than if he were to take pains to go mad with his imagination."}, {"id": "Ep12.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Neque etiam ipsi substantiae modi, si cum eiusmodi entibus rationis, seu imaginationis auxiliis confundantur, unquam recte intelligi poterunt. Nam cum id facimus, eos a substantia, et modo, quo ab aeternitate fluunt, separamus; sine quibus tamen recte intelligi nequeunt.", "en": "Nor will the modes of substance themselves ever be rightly understood if they are confused with beings of reason of this kind, or aids of the imagination. For when we do this, we separate them from substance and from the way in which they flow from eternity, without which, however, they cannot be rightly understood."}, {"id": "Ep12.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Quod ut adhuc clarius videas, cape hoc exemplum: nempe, ubi quis durationem abstracte conceperit, eamque cum tempore confundendo in partes dividere inceperit, nunquam poterit intelligere, qua ratione hora ex. grat. transire possit. Nam ut hora transeat, necesse erit, eius dimidium prius transire, et postea dimidium reliqui, et deinde dimidium, quod huius reliqui superest; et si sic porro infinite dimidium a reliquo subtrahas, nunquam ad finem horae pervenire poteris.", "en": "To see this still more clearly, take this example: when someone has conceived duration abstractly and, confusing it with time, has begun to divide it into parts, he will never be able to understand how an hour, for example, can pass. For for an hour to pass, it will be necessary that half of it pass first, and then half of the remainder, and then half of what is left of this remainder; and if you go on in this way, subtracting half from the remainder to infinity, you will never be able to reach the end of the hour."}, {"id": "Ep12.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Quare multi, qui entia rationis a realibus distinguere assueti non sunt, durationem ex momentis componi, ausi sunt asseverare, et sic in Scyllam inciderunt cupientes vitare Charybdim. Idem enim est durationem ex momentis componere, quam numerum ex sola nullitatum additione.", "en": "So many who are not accustomed to distinguish beings of reason from real ones have dared to assert that duration is composed of moments, and so have fallen into Scylla in their desire to avoid Charybdis. For it is the same to compose duration of moments as to compose number by the mere addition of nothings."}, {"id": "Ep12.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Porro cum ex modo dictis satis pateat, nec numerum, nec mensuram, nec tempus, quandoquidem non nisi auxilia imaginationis sunt, posse esse infinitos: Nam alias numerus non esset numerus, nec mensura mensura, nec tempus tempus. Hinc clare videre est, cur multi, qui haec tria cum rebus ipsis confundebant, propterea quod veram rerum naturam ignorabant, infinitum actu negarunt.", "en": "Further, since it is sufficiently evident from what has just been said that neither number nor measure nor time can be infinite, since they are only aids of the imagination: for otherwise number would not be number, nor measure measure, nor time time. Hence it can be clearly seen why many who confused these three with the things themselves, because they were ignorant of the true nature of things, denied the actual infinite."}, {"id": "Ep12.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Sed quam misere ratiocinati sint, iudicent mathematici, quibus huius farinae argumenta nullam moram iniicere potuerunt in rebus, ab ipsis clare, distincteque perceptis. Nam praeterquam quod multa invenerunt, quae nullo numero explicari possunt; quod satis numerorum defectum ad omnia determinandum patefacit: multa etiam habent, quae nullo numero adaequari possunt; sed omnem, qui dari potest, numerum superant.", "en": "But how wretchedly they have reasoned, let the mathematicians judge, whom arguments of this sort have been unable to hold up at all in matters clearly and distinctly perceived by them. For besides having found many things which cannot be explained by any number, which sufficiently reveals the inadequacy of numbers for determining everything, they also have many things which cannot be equalled by any number, but surpass every number that can be given."}, {"id": "Ep12.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Nec tamen concludunt, talia omnem numerum superare ex partium multitudine: sed ex eo, quod rei natura non sine manifesta contradictione numerum pati potest, ut ex. grat. omnes inaequalitates spatii duobus circulis AB et CD interpositi, omnesque variationes, quas materia, in eo mota, pati debeat, omnem numerum superant.", "en": "Yet they do not conclude that such things surpass every number from the multitude of their parts, but from the fact that the nature of the thing cannot admit number without manifest contradiction; as, for example, all the inequalities of the space interposed between the two circles AB and CD, and all the variations which matter moved in it must undergo, surpass every number."}, {"id": "Ep12.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Idque non concluditur, ex nimia spatii interpositi magnitudine: Nam quantumvis parvam eius portionem capiamus, huius tamen parvae portionis inaequalitates omnem numerum superabunt.", "en": "And this is not concluded from the excessive size of the interposed space. For however small a portion of it we take, the inequalities of this small portion will still surpass every number."}, {"id": "Ep12.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Neque etiam idcirco concluditur, ut in aliis contingit, quod eius maximum, et minimum non habeamus: utrumque enim in hoc nostro exemplo habemus, maximum nempe AB, minimum vero CD: Sed ex eo tantum concluditur, quod natura spatii inter duos circulos, diversa centra habentes, interpositi nihil tale pati possit. Ideoque siquis omnes illas inaequalitates certo aliquo numero determinare velit, simul efficere debebit, ut circulus non sit circulus.", "en": "Nor, again, is it concluded, as happens in other cases, from our not having its maximum and minimum; for we have both in this example of ours, the maximum, namely AB, and the minimum, CD. It is concluded only from the fact that the nature of the space interposed between two circles having different centres can admit nothing of the kind. And so, if anyone wanted to determine all those inequalities by some definite number, he would at the same time have to bring it about that a circle is not a circle."}, {"id": "Ep12.7", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Sic etiam, ut ad nostrum propositum revertar, si quis omnes materiae motus, qui hucusque fuerunt, determinare volet, eos scilicet, eorumque durationem ad certum numerum, et tempus redigendo; is certe nihil aliud conabitur, quam substantiam corpoream, quam non nisi existentem concipere possumus, suis affectionibus privare, et, ut naturam, quam habet, non habeat, efficere.", "en": "So too, to return to our purpose, if anyone wants to determine all the motions of matter that there have been up to now, namely by reducing them and their duration to a definite number and time, he will certainly be attempting nothing else than to deprive corporeal substance, which we can conceive only as existing, of its affections, and to bring it about that it does not have the nature it has."}, {"id": "Ep12.7", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Quae clare demonstrare hic possem, ut et alia multa, quae in hac epistola attigi, nisi id superfluum iudicarem. Ex omnibus iam dictis clare videre est, quaedam sua natura esse infinita, nec ullo modo finita concipi posse; quaedam vero vi causae, cui inhaerent, quae tamen, ubi abstracte concipiuntur, in partes possunt dividi, et ut finita spectari; quaedam denique infinita, vel, si mavis, indefinita dici, propterea quod nullo numero adaequari queant, quae tamen maiora, et minora possunt concipi; quia non sequitur, illa necessario debere esse aequalia, quae numero adaequari nequeunt, ut ex allato exemplo, et aliis multis satis est manifestum.", "en": "I could demonstrate these things clearly here, as well as many others which I have touched on in this letter, if I did not judge it superfluous. From all that has now been said it can be clearly seen that some things are infinite by their nature and cannot in any way be conceived as finite; some by the force of the cause in which they inhere, which however, when they are conceived abstractly, can be divided into parts and regarded as finite; and finally some which are called infinite, or, if you prefer, indefinite, because they cannot be equalled by any number, which can nevertheless be conceived as greater and less; since it does not follow that things which cannot be equalled by number must necessarily be equal, as is sufficiently manifest from the example given and from many others."}, {"id": "Ep12.8", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Denique causas errorum, et confusionum, quae circa quaestionem de infinito ortae sunt, breviter ob oculos posui, easque, ni fallor, ita explicui omnes, ut non putem ullam superesse circa infinitum quaestionem, quam hic non attigi, aut quae ex dictis facillime solvi non queat. Quare in his te detinere diutius, operae pretium esse non iudico.", "en": "I have now briefly set before your eyes the causes of the errors and confusions that have arisen concerning the question of the infinite, and, unless I am mistaken, I have explained them all in such a way that I do not think any question concerning the infinite remains which I have not touched on here, or which cannot very easily be solved from what has been said. So I do not judge it worth while to detain you longer on these matters."}, {"id": "Ep12.9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Verum hic obiter adhuc notari velim, quod Peripatetici recentiores, ut quidem puto, male intellexerunt demonstrationem veterum, qua ostendere nitebantur Dei existentiam. Nam, ut ipsam apud Iudaeum quendam Rab Ghasdai vocatum, reperio, sic sonat.", "en": "But I should like it to be noted here in passing that the more recent Peripatetics, as I at least think, have misunderstood the demonstration of the ancients by which they strove to show God’s existence. For as I find it in a certain Jew called Rab Ghasdai, it runs thus."}, {"id": "Ep12.9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Si datur progressus causarum in infinitum, erunt omnia, quae sunt, etiam causata: Atqui nulli, quod causatum est, competit, vi suae naturae necessario existere: Ergo nihil est in natura ad cuius essentiam pertinet necessario existere. Sed hoc est absurdum: ergo et illud.", "en": "If there is a progression of causes to infinity, all things that are will also be caused; but it belongs to nothing that is caused to exist necessarily by the force of its own nature; therefore there is nothing in nature to whose essence it pertains to exist necessarily. But this is absurd; therefore that is too."}, {"id": "Ep12.9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Quare vis argumenti non in eo sita est, quod impossibile sit, dari actu infinitum, aut progressus causarum in infinitum: Sed tantum in eo, quod supponatur, res, quae sua natura non necessario existunt, non determinari ad existendum a re sua natura necessario existenti.", "en": "So the force of the argument does not lie in the impossibility of there being an actual infinite, or a progression of causes to infinity, but only in the supposition that things which do not exist necessarily by their own nature are not determined to exist by a thing which exists necessarily by its own nature."}, {"id": "Ep12.10", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 12", "la": "Transirem iam, quia tempus me festinare cogit, ad secundam tuam epistolam: sed ad ea, quae isthac continentur, commodius, cum dignatus fueris me invisere, respondere potero. Quaeso itaque, si fieri potest, ut quamprimum venias; nam tempus migrandi festinanter accedit. Tantum est. Vale, meique memor vive, qui sum, etc.", "en": "I would now pass on, since time compels me to hurry, to your second letter; but I shall be able to reply more conveniently to what it contains when you deign to visit me. So I beg you to come as soon as possible, if it can be done; for the time of my moving is fast approaching. That is all. Farewell, and remember me, who am, etc."}, {"id": "Ep18", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Iam saepiuscule tuum tractatum, una cum eius appendice, nuper in lucem editum, attente evolvi. Magis me decebit, ut aliis, quam tibi narrem summam, quam in eo reperi, soliditatem, et quam inde percepi voluptatem: id tamen reticere nequeo, quod, quo frequentius cum attentione eum percurro, eo magis mihi placeat, et continuo aliquid observem, quod antea non animadverteram.", "en": "I have now quite often read attentively your treatise, together with its appendix, recently published. It would become me better to tell others than you of the very great solidity I found in it and the pleasure I took from it; yet I cannot keep silent about this, that the more often I go through it attentively, the more it pleases me, and I continually notice something I had not observed before."}, {"id": "Ep18", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Verumenimvero (ne hac in epistola adulator videar) nolo eius auctorem nimium admirari: Novi, deos omnia laboribus vendere.", "en": "But truly (lest I seem a flatterer in this letter), I do not wish to admire its author too much: I know that the gods sell everything for labour."}, {"id": "Ep18", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Ne autem te in admiratione nimis diu detineam, quis sit, et qui fiat, ut tibi ignotus tanta ad te scribendi libertate utatur, dicam tibi, eum esse, qui desiderio purae, et sincerae veritatis impulsus, in brevi hac et fragili vita pedes suos in scientia, quantum humanum nostrum patitur ingenium, figere studet: quique nullum alium, ad indagandum veritatem, scopum sibi praefixit, quam ipsam veritatem: quique scientia nec honores, nec divitias; sed meram veritatem, tranquillitatemque, tanquam veritatis effectum, sibi acquirere conatur: et qui ex omnibus veritatibus, ac scientiis, nullis magis, quam metaphysicis, si non omnibus, saltem aliqua earum parte se oblectat: et qui omnem suam vitae delectationem in eo collocat, ut horas otiosas, et subcisivas in iis transigat.", "en": "But not to keep you too long in wonder as to who he is, and how it comes about that someone unknown to you takes such liberty in writing to you, I shall tell you that he is one who, driven by the desire for pure and sincere truth, strives in this short and fragile life to set his feet firmly in knowledge, as far as our human understanding allows; who has set himself no other goal in seeking out truth than truth itself; who tries to gain by knowledge neither honours nor riches, but truth alone, and tranquillity as the effect of truth; who, of all truths and sciences, delights in none more than in metaphysics, if not in all of it, at least in some part of it; and who places all the delight of his life in spending his idle and spare hours on these things."}, {"id": "Ep18", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Verum nec quivis adeo beatus est, nec tantum adhibet studium, quantum mihi persuadeo, te adhibuisse, et propterea quivis eo perfectionis non pervenit, quo te iam pervenisse, ex opere tuo perspicio. Verbo ut absolvam, is ipse est, quem familiarius cognoscere tibi licebit, si eum tibi tantopere devincire placeat, ut aperias, et quasi perfores haerentes eius cogitationes.", "en": "But not everyone is so blessed, nor does everyone apply as much study as I am persuaded you have applied; and therefore not everyone arrives at that degree of perfection which I perceive from your work that you have already reached. To finish in a word: he is the man whom you will be able to know more closely, if it please you to oblige him so far as to open up, and as it were bore through, his halting thoughts."}, {"id": "Ep18.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Sed ad tractatum tuum redeo. Quemadmodum inibi multa inveni, quae summopere palato arridebant, ita etiam quaedam obtulerunt sese, concoctu difficilia, quaeque mihi tibi ignoto ita obiicere haud aequum foret, eoque magis, quod grata, nec ne haec sint futura, me latet: et haec est causa, cur haec praemittam, et rogem, an liceat, si his vesperis Hiemalibus tempus superfuerit, tibique placuerit, ad meas difficultates, quae mihi in libro tuo adhuc restant, respondere, nonnullas earum transmittere; hac tamen lege, et obtestatione, ne ego rei magis necessariae, tibique magis acceptae impedimento sim: quoniam nihil intensius, iuxta promissa in libro tuo facta, quam latiorem opinionum tuarum explicationem, ac editionem desidero.", "en": "But I return to your treatise. Just as I found many things in it which were very much to my taste, so also some offered themselves that were hard to digest, which it would hardly be fair for me, as one unknown to you, to put to you thus, the more so since I do not know whether they will be welcome or not; and this is the reason why I send this ahead, and ask whether, if time remains on these winter evenings and it pleases you to answer the difficulties which still remain for me in your book, I may send you some of them; on this condition, however, and with this entreaty, that I should not keep you from anything more necessary or more agreeable to you; since there is nothing I desire more intensely than the fuller explanation and publication of your opinions, in accordance with the promises made in your book."}, {"id": "Ep18.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Ipse id, quod tandem calamo, et chartae credo, coram salute impertita, praestitissem; quia vero primo tuum hospitium mihi ignotum erat, dein morbus contagiosus, et denique munus meum impediebant, hoc ipsum in aliud, ac aliud tempus dilatum est.", "en": "What I now at last entrust to pen and paper I should myself have done in person, with a greeting; but because, first, your lodging was unknown to me, then the contagious disease, and finally my office prevented me, it has been put off from one time to another."}, {"id": "Ep18.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Ne vero haec epistola plane sit vacua, et quia simul spe ducor, non tibi fore ingratum, unicum tantum tibi proponam: nempe, te passim tam in principiis, quam in cogitatis metaphysicis statuere, sive ut propriam sententiam, sive ut Cartesium, cuius philosophiam docebas, explicares, creare, et conservare unum et idem esse, (quod per se adeo clarum est iis, qui eo suas cogitationes direxerunt, ut etiam prima sit notio) et Deum non modo substantias; sed etiam motum in substantiis creasse, hoc est, Deum non solum substantias in statu suo continua creatione; verum etiam earundem motum, ac conatum conservare.", "en": "But so that this letter may not be altogether empty, and since I am led at the same time by the hope that it will not be unwelcome to you, I shall put forward just one thing: namely, that in various places, both in the Principles and in the Metaphysical Thoughts, whether as your own opinion or in explaining Descartes, whose philosophy you were teaching, you maintain that to create and to preserve are one and the same (which is so clear in itself to those who have turned their thoughts to it that it is even a first notion), and that God has created not only substances but also motion in substances; that is, that God preserves by continual creation not only substances in their state, but also their motion and striving."}, {"id": "Ep18.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Deus, verbi causa, non tantum efficit, ut anima, immediata Dei voluntate, atque operatione (perinde est, quomodo id nomines) diutius existat, inque suo perseveret statu; verum etiam causa est, ut tali modo sese ad animae motum habeat: hoc est, quemadmodum continua Dei creatio efficit, ut res diutius existant, ita quoque conatus, vel rerum motus per eandem causam fit in rebus; quandoquidem extra Deum nulla motus datur causa.", "en": "God, for example, not only brings it about, by his immediate will and operation (it makes no difference what you call it), that the soul continues to exist and perseveres in its state, but is also the cause that he stands in such a relation to the motion of the soul; that is, just as God’s continual creation brings it about that things continue to exist, so also the striving or motion of things comes about in things through the same cause; since outside God there is no cause of motion."}, {"id": "Ep18.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Sequitur igitur, Deum non tantum causam esse substantiae mentis, sed etiam cuiuscunque conatus, vel motus mentis, quem voluntatem nuncupamus, veluti passim statuis: ex qua assertione etiam necessario sequi videtur, vel nil mali in motu, sive mentis voluntate esse, vel Deum ipsum illud malum immediate operari. Nam ea quoque, quae mala appellamus, per animam, et consequenter per eiusmodi immediatum influxum, et Dei concursum fiunt.", "en": "It follows, therefore, that God is the cause not only of the substance of the mind, but also of every striving or motion of the mind, which we call will, as you maintain throughout; and from this assertion it also seems necessarily to follow either that there is no evil in the motion or will of the mind, or that God himself works that evil immediately. For the things which we call evil also come about through the soul, and consequently through such an immediate influence and concurrence of God."}, {"id": "Ep18.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Exempli gratia, anima Adami vult edere de fructu prohibito, efficitur ergo, iuxta superius tradita; non tantum ut illa Adami voluntas per Dei influxum velit; sed etiam, quemadmodum statim ostendetur, ut tali modo velit; adeo ut ille Adami prohibitus actus, quatenus Deus non modo voluntatem eius movebat, sed et quatenus eam tali modo movebat, vel in se non sit malus, vel ut Deus ipse illud operari videatur, quod nos malum vocamus.", "en": "For example, Adam’s soul wills to eat of the forbidden fruit; it is brought about, therefore, according to what was said above, not only that this will of Adam wills through God’s influence, but also, as will be shown presently, that it wills in such a way; so that this forbidden act of Adam, insofar as God not only moved his will but also moved it in such a way, is either not evil in itself, or God himself seems to work what we call evil."}, {"id": "Ep18.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Nec aut tu aut D. Cartesius hunc nodum solvere videris, dicendo malum esse non ens, cum quo Deus non concurrit: unde enim voluntas edendi, aut: voluntas Diabolorum ad superbiam procedebat?", "en": "Nor do you or Mr Descartes seem to untie this knot by saying that evil is a non-being, with which God does not concur: for where, then, did the will to eat, or the will of the devils to pride, come from?"}, {"id": "Ep18.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Nam quoniam voluntas (ut recte animadvertis) non sit diversum quid ab ipsa mente; sed sit hic, aut ille motus, vel mentis conatus, tam ad hanc, quam ad illam motionem concursu Dei ei opus erit; iam vero Dei concursus, ut ex scriptis tuis intelligo, nihil aliud est, quam rem per suam voluntatem hoc, vel illo pacto determinare: sequitur, ergo Deum aeque cum mala voluntate, quatenus mala est, ac cum bona, quatenus bona est, concurrere, hoc est, eam determinare. Nam voluntas Dei, quae causa absoluta est omnium, quae existunt tam in substantia, quam in conatu, videtur etiam prima causa esse malae voluntatis, quatenus mala est.", "en": "For since the will (as you rightly observe) is not something different from the mind itself, but is this or that motion or striving of the mind, it will need God’s concurrence as much for the one motion as for the other; and God’s concurrence, as I understand from your writings, is nothing other than to determine a thing by his will in this or that way: it follows, therefore, that God concurs with the evil will, insofar as it is evil, just as much as with the good will, insofar as it is good, that is, determines it. For the will of God, which is the absolute cause of everything that exists, both in substance and in striving, seems also to be the first cause of the evil will, insofar as it is evil."}, {"id": "Ep18.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Deinde nulla in nobis fit voluntatis determinatio, quin Deus eam ab aeterno sciverit; alioquin, si nesciverit, in Deo imperfectionem statuimus; sed qui Deus illam aliter sciverit, quam ex suis decretis? Sunt igitur eius decreta nostrarum determinationum causa; et ita rursum sequi videtur, malam voluntatem vel non esse malum quid, vel Deum illius mali causam esse immediatam, et id operari.", "en": "Next, no determination of the will occurs in us without God’s having known it from eternity; otherwise, if he did not know it, we ascribe an imperfection to God; but how could God have known it otherwise than from his decrees? His decrees, then, are the cause of our determinations; and so again it seems to follow either that the evil will is not something evil, or that God is the immediate cause of that evil and works it."}, {"id": "Ep18.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Neque hic distinctio Theologorum de differentia inter actum, et malum actui adhaerens locum habere potest: nam Deus cum actum, tum modum actus decrevit, hoc est, Deus non solummodo decrevit, ut Adamus ederet, verum etiam ut necessario contra mandatum ederet. Adeo ut iterum sequi videatur, vel τὸ edere Adami adversus praeceptum non esse malum, vel Deum ipsum illud operari.", "en": "Nor can the theologians’ distinction between the act and the evil adhering to the act have any place here: for God decreed both the act and the manner of the act; that is, God decreed not only that Adam should eat, but also that he should necessarily eat against the command. So that again it seems to follow either that Adam’s eating against the command is not evil, or that God himself works it."}, {"id": "Ep18.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Haec sunt, vir clarissime, quae in praesentia in tractatu tuo percipere nequeo: extrema namque utrinque statuere durum est. Tale autem responsum a perspicaci tuo iudicio et industria, quod mihi faciat satis, exspecto, et spero me sequentibus meis ostensurum, quantum tibi propterea debeam.", "en": "These, most distinguished Sir, are the things which at present I cannot understand in your treatise: for it is hard to maintain either extreme. But I expect from your perspicacious judgment and diligence such an answer as will satisfy me, and I hope to show in my following letters how much I shall owe you for it."}, {"id": "Ep18.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Certo tibi, vir Clar. persuadeas, me haec nulla alia de causa, quam studio veritatis quaerere: liber sum, nulli adstrictus professioni, honestis mercaturis me alo, et tempus, quod mihi reliquum est, hisce rebus impendo. Submisse quoque rogo, ut meae difficultates tibi non sint ingratae; si ad hasce respondere animus est, id quod ardentissime cupio, scribe ad, etc.", "en": "Be assured, most distinguished Sir, that I ask these things for no other reason than the pursuit of truth: I am free, bound to no profession; I support myself by honest trade, and spend the time that remains to me on these matters. I humbly ask, too, that my difficulties may not be unwelcome to you; if you have a mind to answer them, which I most ardently desire, write to, etc."}, {"id": "Ep18.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Guil. de Blyenbergh.", "en": "Willem van Blyenbergh."}, {"id": "Ep18.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 18", "la": "Dordraci 12. Decemb. 1664.", "en": "Dordrecht, 12 December 1664."}, {"id": "Ep19", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Epistolam tuam 12. Decembris datam, et in alia inclusam, scripta 21. eiusdem mensis, 26. Schiedami demum accepi; unde intensum tuum veritatis amorem, eamque solummodo omnium studiorum tuorum scopum esse, intelligebam: quod me, qui etiam in nihil aliud animum intendo, ut concluderem coegit, non tantum tuam petitionem, nempe ut ad tuas, quas iam mittis, et imposterum mittes quaestiones, pro viribus mei intellectus responderem, plene concedere; verum omnia quoque pro mea parte conferre, quae ulteriori notitiae, sinceraeque amicitiae inservire queunt.", "en": "Your letter dated 12 December, enclosed in another written on the 21st of the same month, I received at last at Schiedam on the 26th; and from it I understood your intense love of truth, and that truth alone is the aim of all your studies: which compelled me, who also set my mind on nothing else, to resolve not only to grant your request fully, namely to answer, as far as my intellect allows, the questions which you send now and will send in future, but also to contribute everything on my part that can serve further acquaintance and sincere friendship."}, {"id": "Ep19", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Quantum enim ad me attinet: Nullas ex omnibus rebus, quae in potestate mea non sunt, pluris facio, quam cum Viris veritatem sincere amantibus foedus inire amicitiae; quia credo, nos nihil omnino in mundo, quod nostrae potestatis non est, tranquillius, quam istiusmodi homines, posse amare; quoniam tam impossibile est amorem dissolvere, quem ii erga invicem habent, eo quod amore, quem unusquisque erga veritatis cognitionem habet, fundatus est, quam ipsam veritatem semel perceptam non amplecti. Est insuper summus, ac gratissimus, quae in rebus, quae nostri arbitrii non sunt, dari potest; quandoquidem nulla res, praeter veritatem, diversos sensus, et animos penitus unire valet.", "en": "For as far as I am concerned, of all the things which are not in my power I value none more highly than to enter into a bond of friendship with men who sincerely love truth; because I believe that of the things in the world which are not in our power we can love nothing at all with more tranquillity than such men; since it is as impossible to dissolve the love which they have for one another, founded as it is on the love which each has for the knowledge of truth, as not to embrace truth itself once it has been perceived. This love is, moreover, the highest and most pleasing that can be had among things not within our choice; since nothing but truth can unite different opinions and minds completely."}, {"id": "Ep19", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Taceo maximas utilitates, quae inde fluunt, ut rebus, quas procul dubio ipse nosti, diutius te non detineam, quod tamen huc usque feci, quo eo melius ostenderem, quam gratum mihi, et imposterum futurum sit, occasionem nancisci ad meum tibi officium praestandum.", "en": "I pass over the very great advantages which flow from it, so as not to keep you longer with things which you doubtless know yourself; which I have nevertheless done so far, the better to show how welcome it is to me, and will be in future, to have the opportunity of doing you a service."}, {"id": "Ep19", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Ut autem praesentem captem, propius accedam, et ad quaestionem tuam respondebo, quae in eo vertitur cardine, nimirum; quod clare videatur sequi, tam ex Dei providentia, quae ab eius voluntate non differt, quam ex Dei concursu, rerumque creatione continua, vel nulla dari peccata, nullumve malum, vel Deum ea peccata, atque illud malum efficere. Verum non explicas, quid per malum intelligas, et quantum ex exemplo de determinata voluntate Adami perspicere licet, videris per malum intelligere ipsam voluntatem, quatenus tali modo determinata conciperetur, vel quatenus Dei mandato repugnaret, et propterea ais, magnam esse absurditatem (ut et ego, si se ita res haberet) alterutrum horum statuere, nempe, Deum ipsum res, quae contra suam voluntatem sunt, operari, vel eas bonas fore, licet voluntati Dei repugnarent.", "en": "But to seize the present one, I shall come closer and answer your question, which turns on this hinge: that it seems clearly to follow, both from God’s providence, which does not differ from his will, and from God’s concurrence and continual creation of things, either that there are no sins and no evil, or that God brings about those sins and that evil. But you do not explain what you understand by evil, and as far as one can see from the example of Adam’s determinate will, you seem to understand by evil the will itself, insofar as it is conceived as determined in such a way, or insofar as it conflicted with God’s command; and therefore you say that it is a great absurdity (as I too would, if the matter stood so) to maintain either of these: namely, that God himself works things which are against his will, or that they would be good although they conflicted with God’s will."}, {"id": "Ep19", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Quoad me, non possum concedere peccata, et malum quid positivum esse, multo minus, aliquid esse, aut fieri contra Dei voluntatem. Econtra dico, non solum peccata non esse quid positivum; verum etiam affirmo, nos non nisi improprie, vel humano more loquendo dicere posse, nos erga Deum peccare, ut cum dicimus, homines Deum offendere.", "en": "For my part, I cannot grant that sins and evil are something positive, much less that anything is or happens against God’s will. On the contrary, I say not only that sins are not something positive; I also affirm that we can say only improperly, or speaking in human fashion, that we sin against God, as when we say that men offend God."}, {"id": "Ep19", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Nam, primum quod spectat, novimus quicquid est, in se consideratum sine respectu ad aliud quid, perfectionem includere, quae sese eo usque in quacunque re extendit, quo se extendit ipsa rei essentia: nam essentia etiam nihil aliud est.", "en": "For as regards the first point, we know that whatever is, considered in itself without relation to anything else, includes perfection, which in each thing extends as far as the very essence of the thing extends: for essence too is nothing else."}, {"id": "Ep19", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Sumo, exempli gratia, consilium, sive determinatam Adami voluntatem ad edendum de fructu prohibito: hoc consilium, sive illa determinata voluntas in se sola considerata tantum perfectionis includit, quantum realitatis exprimit; et hoc inde intelligi potest, nempe, quod in rebus nullam imperfectionem possimus concipere, nisi ad alias res attendamus, quae plus habent realitatis; et propterea in Adami decreto, quando illud in se consideramus, nec cum aliis perfectioribus, perfectioremve statum ostendentibus, comparamus, nullam poterimus invenire imperfectionem; imo cum infinitis aliis comparare respectu illius longe imperfectioribus, uti lapidibus, truncis, etc. licet.", "en": "Take, for example, the resolve, or determinate will, of Adam to eat of the forbidden fruit: this resolve, or that determinate will, considered in itself alone, includes as much perfection as it expresses reality; and this can be understood from the fact that we can conceive no imperfection in things unless we attend to other things which have more reality; and therefore in Adam’s decree, when we consider it in itself and do not compare it with other things more perfect or showing a more perfect state, we shall be able to find no imperfection; indeed, we may compare it with countless other things, far less perfect in relation to it, such as stones, tree-trunks, etc."}, {"id": "Ep19", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Et hoc quivis revera quoque concedit: nam quilibet res, quas in hominibus detestatur, cumque aversione contemplatur, in animalibus cum admiratione intuetur, uti apum bella, atque columbarum zelotypiam, etc. quae in hominibus spernuntur, et nihilominus ob ea animalia perfectiora iudicamus. Haec cum ita sint, clare sequitur, peccata, quandoquidem ea nil nisi imperfectionem indicant, non posse in aliquo consistere, quod realitatem exprimit, sicuti Adami decreto, eiusque executione.", "en": "And this, in truth, everyone also concedes: for everyone looks with wonder, in animals, on things which he detests in men and contemplates with aversion, such as the wars of bees and the jealousy of doves, etc., which are despised in men, and yet on account of them we judge the animals more perfect. Since this is so, it clearly follows that sins, since they indicate nothing but imperfection, cannot consist in something which expresses reality, such as Adam’s decree and its execution."}, {"id": "Ep19.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Praeterea, neque dicere valemus, Adami voluntatem cum lege Dei pugnare, ac ideo illam esse malam, quia Deo displiceret: nam, praeterquam quod magnam in Deo poneret imperfectionem, si quicquam contra eius voluntatem fieret, sique quid desideraret, cuius compos non fieret, eiusque natura tali modo determinata esset, ut, quemadmodum creaturae, cum his sympathiam, cum illis antipathiam haberet; etiam omnino cum voluntatis divinae natura pugnaret, quia enim illa ab eius intellectu non discrepat, impossibile aeque est, aliquid fieri contra eius voluntatem, ac contra eius intellectum, hoc est, id, quod contra eius voluntatem fieret, talis deberet esse naturae, ut eius etiam intellectui repugnaret, ut quadratum Rotundum.", "en": "Further, neither can we say that Adam’s will conflicts with God’s law, and that it is evil because it displeases God: for besides the fact that it would posit a great imperfection in God if anything happened against his will, and if he desired something which he did not obtain, and if his nature were so determined that, like creatures, he had sympathy with some things and antipathy towards others; it would also conflict altogether with the nature of the divine will; for since that does not differ from his intellect, it is as impossible for anything to happen against his will as against his intellect; that is, what happened against his will would have to be of such a nature as to conflict with his intellect too, like a round square."}, {"id": "Ep19.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Quandoquidem ergo voluntas, decretumve Adami, in se spectatum, nec malum, nec quoque proprie loquendo contra Dei voluntatem erat; sequitur Deum eius causam posse, immo, iuxta eam rationem, quam animadvertis, debere esse; non vero quatenus malum erat: nam malum, quod in eo erat, non erat aliud, quam privationis perfectioris status, quem propter illud opus Adamus amittere debebat, et certum est, privationem non esse quid positivum, eamque respectu nostri, non vero Dei intellectus ita nominari.", "en": "Since, then, Adam’s will or decree, regarded in itself, was neither evil nor, properly speaking, against God’s will, it follows that God can be its cause, indeed, by the reasoning which you point out, must be; but not insofar as it was evil: for the evil that was in it was nothing other than the privation of a more perfect state which Adam had to lose because of that deed; and it is certain that a privation is not something positive, and that it is so called in relation to our intellect, not God’s."}, {"id": "Ep19.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Hoc autem hinc oritur, quia cuncta eiusdem generis singularia, ea omnia, verbi gratia, quae externam hominum figuram habent, una eademque definitione exprimimus, et idcirco iudicamus, ea omnia aeque apta esse ad summam perfectionem, quam ex eiusmodi definitione deducere possumus; quando autem unum invenimus, cuius opera cum ista pugnant perfectione, tunc id ea privatum esse iudicamus, et a sua natura aberrare, quod haud faceremus, si id ad eiusmodi definitionem non retulissemus, talemque ei naturam affinxissemus. Quoniam vero Deus res nec abstracte novit, nec id genus generales format definitiones, nec plus realitatis rebus competit, quam iis divinus intellectus, et potentia immisit, et revera tribuit, manifesto sequitur, privationem istam solummodo respectu nostri intellectus, non vero respectu Dei dici posse.", "en": "This arises from the fact that we express all singulars of the same kind, for example all those which have the external figure of men, by one and the same definition, and therefore judge that they are all equally fit for the highest perfection which we can deduce from a definition of that kind; but when we find one whose works conflict with that perfection, we judge that it is deprived of it and departs from its nature; which we would not do if we had not referred it to such a definition and ascribed such a nature to it. But since God neither knows things abstractly nor forms general definitions of that kind, and no more reality belongs to things than the divine intellect and power has put into them and really given them, it plainly follows that this privation can be spoken of only in relation to our intellect, not in relation to God."}, {"id": "Ep19.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Hisce, mihi ut videtur, quaestio penitus soluta est.", "en": "With this, it seems to me, the question is entirely solved."}, {"id": "Ep19.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Ut vero viam magis planam reddam, omnemque scrupulum evellam, necesse habeo, ad has duas sequentes quaestiones respondere, nempe primo, quare S. Scriptura dicat Deum flagitare, ut se improbi convertant, et etiam quare Adamo prohibuerit ex arbore non edere, cum tamen contrarium conclusisset. Secundo, quod ex dictis meis sequi videatur, improbos superbia, avaritia, desperatione. etc. aeque Deum colere, ac probos generositate, patientia, amore, etc. propterea quod Dei voluntatem exsequuntur.", "en": "But to make the way smoother and remove every scruple, I must answer the two following questions: first, why Holy Scripture says that God demands that the wicked be converted, and also why he forbade Adam to eat from the tree when he had nevertheless decreed the contrary. Second, that it seems to follow from what I have said that the wicked worship God by their pride, greed, despair, etc., just as much as the good by their nobility, patience, love, etc., because they carry out God’s will."}, {"id": "Ep19.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Ad primum ut respondeam, dico Scripturam, quia plebi praecipue convenit, et inservit, continuo humano more loqui: plebs etenim rebus sublimibus percipiendis inepta est; et haec est ratio, cur mihi persuadeam ea omnia, quae Deus prophetis revelavit ad salutem esse necessaria, legum modo scribi; et hoc pacto integras Parabolas prophetae finxerunt, nempe primo Deum, quia salutis, et perditionis media revelarat, quorumque erat causa, instar Regis, ac Legislatoris adumbrarunt; media, quae nil nisi causae sunt, leges appellarunt, et ad modum Legum conscripserunt; salutem, perditionemque, quae nil nisi effectus sunt, qui necessario ex illis mediis fluunt, tanquam praemium, et poenam proposuerunt, atque iuxta hanc Parabolam magis, quam iuxta veritatem omnia sua verba ordinarunt, Deumque ad instar hominis passim expresserunt, modo iratum, modo misericordem, iam futura desiderantem, iam zelo, et suspicione captum, quin ab ipso Diabolo deceptum; adeo ut philosophi, et simul omnes, qui sunt supra legem, hoc est, qui virtutem, non ut legem; sed ex amore, quia praestantissima est, sequuntur, eiusmodi verbis non debeant offendi.", "en": "To answer the first, I say that Scripture, because it is suited chiefly to the common people and serves them, continually speaks in human fashion: for the common people are incapable of perceiving sublime things; and this is the reason why I am persuaded that everything which God revealed to the prophets as necessary for salvation is written in the manner of laws; and in this way the prophets devised whole parables: namely, first, because God had revealed the means of salvation and perdition, and was their cause, they represented him as a king and lawgiver; the means, which are nothing but causes, they called laws and wrote down in the manner of laws; salvation and perdition, which are nothing but effects that follow necessarily from those means, they set forth as reward and punishment; and they ordered all their words more according to this parable than according to the truth, and everywhere depicted God after the likeness of a man, now angry, now merciful, now desiring things to come, now seized by jealousy and suspicion, indeed deceived by the Devil himself; so that philosophers, and with them all who are above the law, that is, who follow virtue not as a law but from love, because it is the most excellent thing, ought not to be offended by words of this kind."}, {"id": "Ep19.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Edictum ergo Adamo factum in hoc solummodo consistebat, nempe quod Deus Adamo revelavit, τὸ edere ex illa arbore mortem operari, quemadmodum et nobis per naturalem intellectum revelat, venenum esse mortiferum. Si vero roges, in quem finem hoc ei revelaret? responsum do, ut eum tanto scientia perfectiorem redderet.", "en": "The command given to Adam, then, consisted solely in this: that God revealed to Adam that eating of that tree brought about death, just as he also reveals to us through the natural intellect that poison is deadly. If you ask to what end he revealed this to him, I reply: to make him so much the more perfect in knowledge."}, {"id": "Ep19.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Deum ergo interrogare, quamobrem ipsi perfectiorem non dederit voluntatem, est aeque absurdum, ac interrogare, cur circulo omnes globi proprietates non fuerit largitus, uti ex supra dictis perspicue sequitur, et ego scholio prop. 15. Partis primae Princip. Cartes. more geometrico demonstrat. demonstravi.", "en": "To ask God, therefore, why he did not give him a more perfect will is as absurd as to ask why he did not bestow on the circle all the properties of the sphere, as follows clearly from what has been said above, and as I have demonstrated in the Scholium to Prop. 15 of Part I of Descartes’s Principles Demonstrated in the Geometrical Manner."}, {"id": "Ep19.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Secundam difficultatem quod attinet, verum quidem est, impios Dei voluntatem suo modo exprimere; ideo tamen cum probis nullatenus sunt comparandi: quo enim res aliqua plus perfectionis habet, eo etiam magis de Deitate participat, Deique perfectionem exprimit magis.", "en": "As for the second difficulty, it is true indeed that the impious express God’s will in their own way; but they are not on that account in any way to be compared with the good: for the more perfection a thing has, the more it also participates in the Deity, and the more it expresses God’s perfection."}, {"id": "Ep19.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 19", "la": "Quum ergo probi inaestimabiliter plus perfectionis, quam improbi habeant, virtus eorum cum improborum virtute comparari nequit, eo quod improbi amore divino, qui ex Dei profluit cognitione, et quo solo nos pro humano nostro intellectu Dei servi dicimur, carent. Imo, quia Deum non cognoscunt, non sunt nisi instrumentum in manu artificis, quod inscium servit, et serviendo consumitur; probi contra conscii serviunt, et serviendo perfectiores evadunt.", "en": "Since, then, the good have incomparably more perfection than the wicked, their virtue cannot be compared with the virtue of the wicked, because the wicked lack the divine love which flows from the knowledge of God, and by which alone, according to our human intellect, we are said to be servants of God. Indeed, because they do not know God, they are nothing but a tool in the hand of the craftsman, which serves without knowing it and is used up in serving; the good, on the contrary, serve knowingly, and become more perfect by serving."}, {"id": "Ep20", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Ubi tua mihi primum tradebatur epistola, eaque a me cursim evolvebatur, non tantum statim respondere, sed et multa refutare animus erat. Verum quo eam magis volvebam, eo minorem obiectionum materiam inveniebam, et quantum desiderium legendi me ceperat, tantam legendo percepi voluptatem.", "en": "When your letter was first handed to me and I ran through it hastily, I had a mind not only to answer at once but also to refute many things. But the more I turned it over, the less matter for objections I found, and as great as the desire to read it that had seized me, so great was the pleasure I took in reading it."}, {"id": "Ep20", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Antequam vero ad postulatum accedam, ut nimirum quasdam difficultates solveres, imprimis sciendum est; me duas generales habere regulas, iuxta quas semper studeo philosophari, prior regula est clarus, et distinctus mei intellectus conceptus, posterior est verbum Dei revelatum, vel Dei voluntas. Iuxta priorem amator veritatis, iuxta utramque vero Christianus ut sim philosophus conor; et siquando post longum accideret examen, ut naturalis mea cognitio vel videretur cum hoc verbo pugnare, vel minus bene cum eo convenire, tantum hoc verbum apud me habet auctoritatis, ut conceptus, quos claros esse mihi imaginor, mihi sint potius suspecti, quam ut eos supra et contra illam veritatem, quam in illo libro mihi praescriptam puto, collocarem.", "en": "But before I come to my request, namely that you should resolve certain difficulties, you must know first of all that I have two general rules according to which I always try to philosophize: the first rule is the clear and distinct conception of my intellect, the second is the revealed word of God, or the will of God. According to the first I try to be a lover of truth, but according to both a Christian philosopher; and if it should ever happen, after long examination, that my natural knowledge seemed either to conflict with this word or to agree with it less well, this word has so much authority with me that the concepts which I imagine to be clear are rather suspect to me than that I should place them above and against the truth which I think is prescribed to me in that book."}, {"id": "Ep20", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Et quid mirum? nam constanter credere volo, illud verbum esse Dei verbum, hoc est, id provenisse a summo, et perfectissimo Deo, qui plures perfectiones includit, quam ego capere possum; et forte de se ipso, suisque operibus plures voluit perfectiones praedicare, quam ego finito meo intellectu hodie, hodie inquam, percipere queo: fieri enim potest, ut memet operibus meis maioribus perfectionibus privarim; et ideo, si forte ea praeditus essem perfectione, qua actionibus propriis privatus sum, percipere possem omne id, quod in eo verbo nobis proponitur, et docetur, cum sanissimis meae mentis conceptibus congruere; verum quia mihi ipsi suspectus sum, annon continuo errore memet meliore privaverim conditione, et, ut statuis, Princ. Part. 1. Prop. 15. nostra cognitio, licet clarissima, adhuc imperfectionem includat; potius, etiam sine ratione, ad illud verbum inclino, hoc nisus fundamento, quod a perfectissimo prodiit, (hoc quippe iam praesuppono, quia eius probatio hic non haberet locum, vel nimis longa foret) et propterea a me credi debet.", "en": "And what wonder? For I wish to believe constantly that this word is the word of God, that is, that it has come from the highest and most perfect God, who includes more perfections than I can grasp; and perhaps he willed to declare more perfections of himself and his works than I can perceive today, today, I say, with my finite intellect: for it may be that I have deprived myself by my works of greater perfections; and therefore, if I were perhaps endowed with the perfection of which I have been deprived by my own actions, I could perceive that everything which is set before us and taught in that word agrees with the soundest concepts of my mind; but because I am suspicious of myself, whether I have not deprived myself of a better condition by continual error, and since, as you maintain in the Principles, Part I, Prop. 15, our knowledge, however clear, still includes imperfection, I incline rather to that word, even without reason, relying on this foundation: that it has come from the most perfect being (this indeed I now presuppose, because its proof would have no place here, or would be too long), and therefore must be believed by me."}, {"id": "Ep20", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Si iam solo ductu regulae meae primae, exclusa secunda, quasi eam non haberem, aut ea non extaret, de tua epistola iudicarem, multa mihi essent concedenda, ut et concedo, subtilesque conceptus tui essent suspiciendi; secunda vero regula longius a te me dissentire cogit. Verum pro epistolae modo ductu et huius et illius regulae aliquanto latius eos examinabo.", "en": "If I were now to judge your letter by the guidance of my first rule alone, the second excluded, as if I did not have it or it did not exist, I should have to grant many things, as I do grant them, and your subtle concepts would have to be admired; but the second rule compels me to disagree with you more widely. But as far as a letter allows I shall examine them somewhat more fully under the guidance of both the one rule and the other."}, {"id": "Ep20.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Primo, iuxta primo positam regulam, rogaveram, quia, secundum tuam positionem, creare, et conservare unum, idemque erant, et quia Deus non tantum res; sed motus quoque, et rerum modos in statu suo perseverare faciebat, hoc est, cum iis concurrebat; annon videretur sequi, nullum esse malum, aut, ipsum Deum malum operari; hac nixus regula, quod nihil contra Dei voluntatem potest fieri, alioquin involveret imperfectionem, aut res, quas Deus operatur, (quibus etiam res, quas malas dicimus, comprehensae videntur) etiam deberent malae esse. Verum quia et hoc contradictionem includit, et, quocunque eam verterem modo, me a contradictione statuenda liberare non possem, propterea me ad te recipiebam, utpote optimum tuorum conceptuum interpretem.", "en": "First, according to the first rule laid down, I had asked whether, since according to your position to create and to preserve were one and the same, and since God made not only things but also the motions and modes of things persevere in their state, that is, concurred with them, it did not seem to follow that there is no evil, or that God himself works evil; relying on this rule, that nothing can happen against God’s will, since otherwise it would involve imperfection, or the things which God works (among which the things we call evil also seem to be included) would also have to be evil. But because this too includes a contradiction, and in whatever way I turned it I could not free myself from asserting a contradiction, I had recourse to you, as the best interpreter of your own concepts."}, {"id": "Ep20.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "In responsione dicis, te persistere in prima sententia, nempe nihil contra Dei voluntatem vel fieri, vel posse fieri: Verum quum huic difficultati respondendum esset, annon igitur Deus nullum faciat malum, negas peccatum quid positivum esse, addisque, non nisi valde improprie dici posse nos in Deum, peccare; et appendicis parte I. Cap. 6. dicis, malum absolutum nullum dari, ut per se est manifestum: Nam quicquid existit, in se consideratum, sine respectu ad aliud quid, perfectionem includit, quae sese semper eo usque in quacunque re extendit, quo se extendit ipsa rei essentia, et propterea evidenter sequitur, peccata, quia ea nihil nisi imperfectionem denotant, non posse in aliquo, quod essentiam exprimit, consistere.", "en": "In your reply you say that you persist in your first opinion, namely that nothing either happens or can happen against God’s will. But when the difficulty had to be answered whether God then does no evil, you deny that sin is something positive, and add that only very improperly can we be said to sin against God; and in the Appendix, Part I, ch. 6, you say that ‘there is no absolute evil, as is self-evident’: ‘For whatever exists, considered in itself without relation to anything else, includes perfection, which always extends in each thing as far as the very essence of the thing extends; and therefore it evidently follows that sins, because they denote nothing but imperfection, cannot consist in something which expresses essence.’"}, {"id": "Ep20.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Si peccatum, malum, error, vel quocunque id appelles nomine, nihil aliud est, quam perfectiorem statum amittere, aut eo privari, utique sequi videtur, τὸ existere non quidem malum, aut imperfectionem esse; sed aliquod malum in re existente posse oriri. Nam perfectum per aeque perfectam actionem perfectiore statu non privabitur; sed quidem per id, quod ad aliquod imperfectum, quia concessis nobis viribus abutimur, inclinamus.", "en": "If sin, evil, error, or whatever name you call it by, is nothing other than to lose a more perfect state, or to be deprived of it, it certainly seems to follow that to exist is not indeed evil or imperfection, but that some evil can arise in a thing that exists. For what is perfect will not be deprived of a more perfect state by an equally perfect action, but by our inclining to something imperfect, because we misuse the powers granted to us."}, {"id": "Ep20.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Hoc videris non malum; sed bonum minus appellare, quia res in se consideratae perfectionem includunt: deinde, quia rebus, ut ais, non plus essentiae competit, quam divinus intellectus, et potentia eis tribuit, et revera confert; et propterea non plus etiam existentiae in actionibus suis ostendere, quam essentiae accepere, possunt.", "en": "This you seem to call not evil but a lesser good, because things considered in themselves include perfection; and next because, as you say, no more essence belongs to things than the divine intellect and power attributes to them and really confers; and therefore they can show no more existence in their actions than the essence they have received."}, {"id": "Ep20.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Nam si nec plures, nec pauciores possum edere operationes, quam quantum essentiae accepi, nulla perfectioris status privatio fingi potest: si enim nihil contra Dei voluntatem fit, et si solummodo tantum fit, quantum essentiae collatum est, qua via malum, quod melioris conditionis privationem nuncupas, excogitari potest? Quomodo quis per statutum adeo, et dependens opus perfectiorem statum amittere potis est?", "en": "For if I can bring forth neither more nor fewer operations than the essence I have received, no privation of a more perfect state can be feigned: for if nothing happens against God’s will, and if only as much happens as there is essence conferred, by what way can evil, which you call the privation of a better condition, be thought up? How can anyone lose a more perfect state through a work so fixed and dependent?"}, {"id": "Ep20.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Adeo ut mihi persuadeam, Virum clarissimum alterutrum debere statuere, vel aliquod esse malum, vel si minus, nullam esse posse melioris status privationem: Nam nullum esse malum et meliori conditione orbari contradictorium mihi videtur. At dices, per perfectioris status privationem ad minus quidem bonum; sed non ad absolutum malum recidimus; sed (Append. Part. 1. Cap. 3.) me docuisti, non esse de verbis litigandum.", "en": "So that I am persuaded that you, most distinguished Sir, must maintain one of two things: either that there is some evil, or, if not, that there can be no privation of a better state: for that there is no evil and that one is bereft of a better condition seem to me contradictory. But you will say that through the privation of a more perfect state we fall back into a lesser good, but not into absolute evil; but you have taught me (Appendix, Part I, ch. 3) that one should not quarrel about words."}, {"id": "Ep20.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Quare, an id absolutum, nec ne dicendum sit, iam non disputo; sed solum, annon a meliori in peiorem statum delabi apud nos iure peior status, aut status malus dicatur, et dici debeat. At regeres, malus ille status multum adhuc boni continet; ego vero interrogo, annon ille homo, qui per imprudentiam suam in causa fuit, quod perfectiore statu orbatus est, et per consequens nunc minor est, quam antea fuit, malus vocari queat.", "en": "Therefore I do not now dispute whether it should be called absolute or not; but only whether falling from a better into a worse state is not rightly called among us, and ought to be called, a worse state, or an evil state. But you will reply that this evil state still contains much good; I, however, ask whether the man who through his imprudence was the cause of his being bereft of a more perfect state, and is consequently now less than he was before, cannot be called evil."}, {"id": "Ep20.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Ut autem antecedens ratiocinium, quia nonnullae circa id supersunt tibi difficultates, declines, affirmas malum quidem esse, et in Adamo fuisse, verum id non esse positivum quid, et respectu nostri, non autem Dei intellectus tale dici, nostrique respectu privationem, (sed tantum quatenus eo optima libertate, quae ad nostram naturam spectat, et in nostra potestate est, nosmet ipsos privamus) Dei vero respectu negationem esse. Hic autem examinemus, an illud, quod malum vocas, siquidem duntaxat nostri respectu malum foret, malum non esset; deinde an malum, ut tu vis, acceptum, Dei respectu tantum negatio dici debeat.", "en": "But to evade the preceding reasoning, since some difficulties about it remain for you, you affirm that there is indeed evil, and that it was in Adam, but that it is not something positive, and is so called in relation to our intellect, not God’s; and that in relation to us it is privation (‘but only insofar as we thereby deprive ourselves of the best freedom, which belongs to our nature and is in our power’), but in relation to God negation. Here, however, let us examine whether what you call evil, if it were evil only in relation to us, would not be evil; and next whether evil, taken as you wish, should be called only negation in relation to God."}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Ad primum supra aliquo modo videor respondisse: et quamvis concederem, minus alio ente perfectum esse in me nullum ponere posse malum, eo quod meliorem statum a creatore flagitare nequeo, et tantum efficere, ut meus status gradibus differat; non tamen ideo potero concedere, et fateri, si iam imperfectior sum, quam ante fui, eamque imperfectionem mea culpa mihi creavi, me eatenus non tanto peiorem esse; si, inquam, meipsum, antequam in imperfectionem unquam incideram, considero, et me cum aliis tunc maiore, quam ego praeditis perfectione, comparo, minor illa perfectio non malum; sed secundum gradus minus bonum erit: Si vero me ipsum, postquam perfectiore statu excidi, eoque propria orbatus imprudentia, cum prima mea forma, qua ex manu mei Creatoris prodii, et perfectior eram, comparo, me esse deteriorem, quam antea iudicare debeo: nam non creator; sed ego me ipsum eo redegi; vires enim mihi me ab errore praeservandi, uti et tu fateris, suppetebant.", "en": "To the first I seem to have answered in some way above; and although I would grant that to be less perfect than another being can posit no evil in me, because I cannot demand a better state from the creator, and it only makes my state differ by degrees; yet I shall not on that account be able to grant and confess, if I am now more imperfect than I was before and have created that imperfection for myself by my own fault, that I am not to that extent so much the worse; if, I say, I consider myself before I ever fell into imperfection, and compare myself with others then endowed with greater perfection than I, that lesser perfection will not be an evil but a lesser good according to degree; but if I compare myself, after I have fallen from a more perfect state and been bereft of it by my own imprudence, with my first form, in which I came forth from the hand of my Creator and was more perfect, I must judge that I am worse than before: for not the creator but I myself brought myself to this; for I had the powers to preserve myself from error, as you too confess."}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Quod secundum spectat, nempe an malum, quod statuis consistere in melioris status privatione, quem non tantum Adamus; sed et nos omnes subita, et inordinata nimis actione amisimus, an illud, inquam, respectu Dei mera sit negatio. Ut hoc vero sana mente examinemus, videndum nobis est, quomodo hominem constituas et a Deo ante omnem errorem dependentem facias, et quomodo post errorem eundem hominem constituas.", "en": "As regards the second: namely whether the evil which you hold to consist in the privation of a better state, which not only Adam but all of us lost by a too sudden and disordered action, whether that, I say, is a mere negation in relation to God. To examine this with a sound mind, we must see how you constitute man and make him dependent on God before all error, and how you constitute the same man after error."}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Ante errorem eum describis, quod non plus ipsi essentiae competit, quam quantum ei divinus intellectus, et potentia tribuit, et revera confert, hoc est, (nisi a mente tua aberrem) quod homo nec plus, nec minus perfectionis potest habere, quam quantum essentiae Deus in ipso posuit: hoc vero est hominem tali modo a Deo dependentem facere, quemadmodum elementa, lapides, herbas, etc. Si autem haec tua sit sententia, quid sibi velint verba Princip. P. 1. Prop. 15. non percipio.", "en": "Before error you describe him as having no more essence than the divine intellect and power attributes to him and really confers; that is (unless I mistake your mind), that man can have neither more nor less perfection than the essence God has put in him: but this is to make man dependent on God in the same way as the elements, stones, plants, etc. But if this is your opinion, I do not understand what the words of the Principles, Part I, Prop. 15, mean."}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Cum autem voluntas, inquis, libera sit ad se determinandum: sequitur nos potestatem habere facultatem assentiendi intra limites intellectus continendi, ac proinde efficiendi, ne in errorem incidamus. Annon videtur contradictio, voluntatem tam liberam, ut se ab errore possit praeservare, et una a Deo dependentem facere, ut nec plus, nec minus perfectionis queat proferre, quam quantum essentiae Deus ipsi dedit?", "en": "‘But since the will’, you say, ‘is free to determine itself, it follows that we have the power of containing the faculty of assenting within the limits of the intellect, and so of bringing it about that we do not fall into error.’ Does it not seem a contradiction to make the will so free that it can preserve itself from error, and at the same time so dependent on God that it can bring forth neither more nor less perfection than the essence God has given it?"}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Ad secundum, nempe quomodo hominem constituis post errorem, dicis, hominem subita nimis actione, nimirum voluntatem non continendo intra intellectus limites, seipsum perfectiore conditione privare: verum mihi videtur, tibi et hic, et in principiis utraque huius privationis extrema propius fuisse explicanda, quid ante privationem possideret quidque post amissionem perfecti istius status (ut eum appellas) sibi servaret. Dicitur quidem quid perdiderimus; sed non quid retinuerimus Princ. Part. 1. Prop. 15. Tota igitur imperfectio erroris in sola optimae libertatis privatione consistet, quae error vocatur.", "en": "As to the second, namely how you constitute man after error, you say that man by too sudden an action, namely by not containing the will within the limits of the intellect, deprives himself of a more perfect condition; but it seems to me that both here and in the Principles you should have explained more closely both extremes of this privation: what he possessed before the privation, and what he kept for himself after the loss of that perfect state (as you call it). We are told indeed what we have lost, but not what we have retained (Principles, Part I, Prop. 15): ‘The whole imperfection of error, therefore, will consist solely in the privation of the best freedom, which is called error.’"}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Examinemus utcumque hoc ea ratione, qua a te statuitur. Statuis non solum tam diversos cogitandi modos in nobis dari, quorum alios volendi, aliosque intelligendi nuncupamus; sed etiam inter eos talem esse ordinem, ut res non debeamus velle, antequam eas clare intelligamus; affirmas etiam, quod, si voluntatem nostram intra limites intellectus continemus, nunquam errabimus, et quod denique in nostra potestate est voluntatem intra limites intellectus continere.", "en": "Let us examine this, at any rate, in the way you lay it down. You maintain not only that there are in us such different modes of thinking, of which we call some willing and others understanding, but also that there is such an order between them that we ought not to will things before we understand them clearly; you affirm too that if we contain our will within the limits of the intellect we shall never err, and finally that it is in our power to contain the will within the limits of the intellect."}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Cum haec serio mente agito, alterutrum ut verum sit, necessum est; vel omne id, quod ponitur, est fictum, vel Deus eundem ordinem nobis impressit. Si impressit, annon absurdum esset affirmare id sine fine factum esse Deumque, ut ullum ordinem observemus et sequamur, non exigere: nam illud in Deo contradictionem poneret.", "en": "When I turn this over seriously in my mind, one of two things must be true: either everything that is laid down is feigned, or God has impressed this same order upon us. If he has impressed it, would it not be absurd to affirm that this was done to no purpose, and that God does not require us to observe and follow any order? For that would posit a contradiction in God."}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Et si ordinem in nobis positum debemus observare, quomodo adeo a Deo dependentes possumus et esse, et manere: si enim nemo nec plus, nec minus perfectionis habet, quam quantum essentiae accepit, et si haec vis ex effectibus debeat cognosci, ille, qui voluntatem suam ultra limites intellectus extendit, non tantum virium a Deo accepit, alioquin eas in effectum deduceret; et per consequens is, qui errat, perfectionem non errandi a Deo non accepit, alias nunquam erraret. Nam secundum te tantum essentiae datum est, quantum perfectionis efficitur.", "en": "And if we must observe the order placed in us, how can we be and remain so dependent on God? For if no one has more or less perfection than the essence he has received, and if this power must be known from its effects, then he who extends his will beyond the limits of the intellect has not received so much power from God, otherwise he would bring it into effect; and consequently he who errs has not received from God the perfection of not erring, otherwise he would never err. For according to you as much essence has been given as perfection is brought about."}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Deinde si Deus nobis tantum essentiae tribuit, ut eum observare queamus ordinem, ut tu nos eum posse servare affirmas, et si tantum perfectionis producimus, quantum essentiae nacti sumus, qui fit, ut eum transgrediamur, qui, ut eum possimus transgredi ordinem, utque voluntatem non semper intra limites intellectus contineamus.", "en": "Next, if God has given us so much essence that we can observe that order, as you affirm that we can keep it, and if we produce as much perfection as the essence we have obtained, how does it come about that we transgress it, that we can transgress that order, and that we do not always contain the will within the limits of the intellect."}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Tertio, si a Deo tantopere dependeo, ut supra te statuere ostendi, ut nec intra, nec extra limites intellectus voluntatem continere possim, nisi mihi Deus in antecessum tantum dederit essentiae, et sua voluntate prius alterutrum determinaverit: quomodo ergo mihi, si hoc penitius consideremus, voluntatis libertas usu venire potest.", "en": "Third, if I depend on God so much, as I have shown above that you hold, that I can contain the will neither within nor beyond the limits of the intellect unless God has given me so much essence beforehand and has first determined the one or the other by his will: how then, if we consider this more deeply, can freedom of the will fall to me."}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Nonne in Deo contradictionem ponere videtur, nobis praescribere ordinem nostram voluntatem intra limites nostri intellectus continendi, et non praebere tantum essentiae, vel perfectionum, ut eum observemus; et si, iuxta tuam sententiam, tantum perfectionis nobis concessit, nunquam profecto errare possemus: quantum enim essentiae possidemus, tantum perfectionis producere oportet, semperque concessas vires in nostris operationibus ostendere: nostri autem errores sunt argumentum, nos eiusmodi potentiam a Deo ita dependentem (ut statuis) non possidere; adeo ut alterutrum verum esse debeat, vel nos non adeo Deo dependere, vel nos in nobis potentiam non errandi non habere; at potentiam non errandi habemus, prout statuis. Ergo a Deo non adeo dependemus.", "en": "Does it not seem to posit a contradiction in God, to prescribe to us the order of containing our will within the limits of our intellect, and not to provide essence or perfection enough for us to observe it; and if, according to your opinion, he has granted us so much perfection, we could certainly never err: for as much essence as we possess, so much perfection we must produce, and always show in our operations the powers granted; but our errors are proof that we do not possess such a power so dependent on God (as you hold); so that one of two things must be true: either we do not depend on God so much, or we do not have in us the power of not erring; but we do have the power of not erring, as you hold. Therefore we do not depend on God so much."}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Ex dictis iam clare patere videtur, impossibile esse, ut malum aut meliori statu privari respectu Dei esset negatio. Quid enim significat privari, aut perfectiorem statum amittere? nonne est a maiori ad minorem perfectionem, et per consequens a maiori ad minorem essentiam transire; et a Deo in certa mensura perfectionis, et essentiae collocari? nonne est velle, nos alium, praeter sui perfectam notitiam, non posse acquirere statum, nisi aliter decrevisset, ac voluisset?", "en": "From what has been said it now seems clearly evident that it is impossible that evil, or being deprived of a better state, should be a negation in relation to God. For what does it mean to be deprived, or to lose a more perfect state? Is it not to pass from greater to lesser perfection, and consequently from greater to lesser essence, and to be placed by God in a certain measure of perfection and essence? Is it not his willing that we can acquire no other state outside his perfect knowledge, unless he had decreed and willed otherwise?"}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Fierine potest, ut illa creatura ab omniscio, et summe perfecto ente producta, quod voluit, ut talem essentiae statum retineret, imo, cum qua Deus, ut eam in eo statu retineret, continuo concurrit; ut, inquam, in essentia declinaret, hoc est, in perfectione praeter Dei notitiam minor fieret? Haec res absurditatem involvere videtur.", "en": "Can it be that this creature, produced by an omniscient and supremely perfect being which willed that it should retain such a state of essence, indeed, with which God continually concurs in order to keep it in that state, can it be, I say, that it should decline in essence, that is, become less in perfection, without God’s knowledge? This seems to involve absurdity."}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Annon absurdum est dicere, Adamum perfectiorem amisisse conditionem, et consequenter ineptum fuisse ad eum ordinem, quem Deus in anima eius posuerat, Deumque nullam habere notitiam, qualis, quantaeque perfectionis Adamus iacturam fecerat? Potestne comprehendi, Deum ens aliquod constituere ita dependens, ut non nisi eiusmodi opus produceret, et tum propter illud opus perfectiorem perderet statum: (praeterquam quod eius absoluta esset causa) et tamen nullam eius haberet notitiam?", "en": "Is it not absurd to say that Adam lost a more perfect condition, and was consequently unfit for the order which God had placed in his soul, and that God has no knowledge of what kind and how great a perfection Adam had lost? Can it be understood that God should constitute some being so dependent that it could produce no work but such a one, and that it should then lose a more perfect state because of that work (besides the fact that he would be its absolute cause), and yet that he should have no knowledge of it?"}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Concedo inter actum, et malum actui adhaerens dari discrimen; sed vero malum respectu Dei esse negationem superat meum captum. Deum actum scire, eundem determinare, et cum eo concurrere, et tamen malum, quod in illo actu est, eiusque exitum non cognoscere, mihi in Deo impossibile videtur.", "en": "I grant that there is a distinction between the act and the evil adhering to the act; but that evil should be a negation in relation to God surpasses my understanding. That God should know the act, determine it and concur with it, and yet not know the evil which is in that act, and its outcome, seems to me impossible in God."}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Adverte mecum, Deum in actu meo progignendi cum uxore mea concurrere: est enim positivum quid, et per consequens claram eius habet Deus scientiam; verum quatenus eo actu abutor, et cum alterius uxore contra datam fidem, et iusiurandum meum rem habeo, illum actum malum comitatur. Quid iam hic respectu Dei esset negativum? non quod actum progignendi faciam: quatenus etenim is positivus est, cum eodem Deus concurrit. Debet ergo istud malum, quod illum actum comitatur, tantummodo esse, quod ego contra proprium foedus, vel Dei mandatum cum aliena congredior, cum qua non licet.", "en": "Consider with me: God concurs in my act of begetting with my wife; for it is something positive, and consequently God has clear knowledge of it; but insofar as I misuse this act, and have to do with another man’s wife against my given faith and oath, evil accompanies that act. What here would now be negative in relation to God? Not that I perform the act of begetting: for insofar as it is positive, God concurs with it. This evil, then, which accompanies that act, must be only this: that against my own covenant, or God’s command, I go with another’s wife, with whom it is not lawful."}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Iam vero, potestne capi, Deum nostras scire actiones, cum iis concurrere, et tamen ignorare, quacum illam actionem producimus, eo magis quod Deus etiam cum illius foeminae actione, cum qua rem habebam, concurrit? Id de Deo sentire durum videtur. Attende occidendi actum; quatenus est actus positivus, cum eo Deus concurrit; sed vero istius actionis effectum, nempe alicuius entis destructionem, et Dei creaturae dissolutionem anne ignoraret? quasi proprium opus Deo foret incognitum.", "en": "Now can it be grasped that God knows our actions and concurs with them, and yet does not know with whom we produce that action, the more so since God also concurs with the action of the woman with whom I had to do? It seems hard to think this of God. Consider the act of killing: insofar as it is a positive act, God concurs with it; but would he be ignorant of the effect of this action, namely the destruction of some being and the dissolution of God’s creature? As if his own work were unknown to God."}, {"id": "Ep20.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "(Metuo ne mentem tuam bene percipiam: Conceptus quippe tui, quam ut tam foedum committas errorem, perspicaciores sunt) fortasse instabis, eos actus, prout eos pono, mere esse bonos, et nullum eos comitari malum; sed tum, quid malum nuncupes, capere nequeo, quod perfectioris conditionis privationem sequitur; et tum mundus in aeterna, et perpetua poneretur confusione, nosque bestiis similes redderemur. Vide quaeso, quam utilitatem ea sententia mundo conferret.", "en": "(I fear that I do not perceive your mind well: for your concepts are too perspicacious for you to commit so foul an error.) Perhaps you will insist that these acts, as I put them, are purely good, and that no evil accompanies them; but then I cannot grasp what you call the evil which follows the privation of a more perfect condition; and then the world would be placed in eternal and perpetual confusion, and we should be made like the beasts. See, I pray, what benefit this opinion would bring the world."}, {"id": "Ep20.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Vulgarem hominis descriptionem reiicis, et unicuique homini tantum tribuis perfectionis, quantum Deus ipsi, ut operetur, largitus est. Sed ea ratione mihi videris statuere impios Deum aeque, ac pios, suis operibus colere: Quare? quia utrique perfectiora nequeunt producere opera, quam quantum utrisque essentiae datum est, et suis demonstrant effectibus.", "en": "You reject the common description of man, and attribute to each man as much perfection as God has bestowed on him for acting. But in that way you seem to me to maintain that the impious worship God by their works as much as the pious: Why? Because neither can produce more perfect works than the essence given to each, as they show by their effects."}, {"id": "Ep20.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Nec mihi quaestioni satisfacere in secunda responsione videris, dicendo, quo res aliqua plus perfectionis habet, eo etiam magis de Deitate participat, Deique perfectionem exprimit magis. Quum ergo probi inaestimabiliter plus perfectionis, quam improbi habeant, virtus eorum cum improborum virtute comparari nequit. Improbi, quia Deum non cognoscunt, non sunt nisi instrumentum in manu artificis, quod inscium servit et serviendo consumitur: Probi contra conscii serviunt, et serviendo perfectiores evadunt.", "en": "Nor do you seem to me to satisfy the question in your second answer, by saying: ‘the more perfection a thing has, the more it also participates in the Deity, and the more it expresses God’s perfection. Since, then, the good have incomparably more perfection than the wicked, their virtue cannot be compared with the virtue of the wicked. The wicked, because they do not know God, are nothing but a tool in the hand of the craftsman, which serves without knowing it and is used up in serving: the good, on the contrary, serve knowingly, and become more perfect by serving.’"}, {"id": "Ep20.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "In utrisque tamen hoc verum est, eos amplius non posse operari; quanto etenim plus perfectionis hic prae illo efficit, tanto plus essentiae unus prae alio accepit. Annon igitur exigua sua perfectione impii aeque, ac pii, Deum colunt? Nam, secundum tuam sententiam, nihil amplius ab impiis Deus flagitat, alioquin plus essentiae in eos contulisset; sed vero plus essentiae non dedit, uti ex effectibus liquet. Ergo amplius ab eis non petit.", "en": "Yet in both this is true, that they cannot do more; for the more perfection the one brings about compared with the other, the more essence the one has received compared with the other. Do not the impious, then, with their slight perfection, worship God as much as the pious? For according to your opinion God demands nothing more from the impious, otherwise he would have conferred more essence on them; but he has not given more essence, as is clear from the effects. Therefore he does not ask more of them."}, {"id": "Ep20.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Et siquando unusquisque in specie, nec plus, nec minus, quam Deus vult, facit, cur is, qui parum operatur, sed tamen tantum, quantum Deus ab ipso exigit, Deo aeque, ac probus, non esset acceptus?", "en": "And if each one in particular does neither more nor less than God wills, why should he who does little, but yet as much as God requires of him, not be as acceptable to God as the good man?"}, {"id": "Ep20.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Insuper, quemadmodum per malum, quod actum comitatur, nostra imprudentia, ex tua sententia, perfectiorem perdimus statum, ita et hic videris statuere, quod, continendo voluntatem intra limites intellectus, non tantum adeo perfecti manemus, ac sumus; sed quod praeterea serviendo perfectiores evadimus; quod contradictionem involvere mihi persuadeo, si modo tantopere a Deo dependemus, ut nec plus, nec minus perfectionis queamus efficere, quam quantum essentiae accepimus, hoc est, quam Deus voluit, ut tunc per imprudentiam peiores, aut per prudentiam meliores fiamus. Adeo ut nihil aliud statuere videris, quam, si homo talis sit, ac eum describis, impios suis operibus, aeque, ac pios suis, Deum colere, et hac ratione ita, ut elementa, herbae, lapides etc. a Deo dependentes reddimur.", "en": "Moreover, just as through the evil which accompanies the act we lose a more perfect state by our imprudence, according to your opinion, so here too you seem to maintain that by containing the will within the limits of the intellect we not only remain as perfect as we are, but besides become more perfect by serving; which I am persuaded involves a contradiction, if only we depend on God so much that we can bring about neither more nor less perfection than the essence we have received, that is, than God has willed, so that we should then become worse through imprudence or better through prudence. So that you seem to maintain nothing other than that, if man is such as you describe him, the impious worship God by their works as much as the pious by theirs, and in this way we are made dependent on God as the elements, plants, stones, etc. are."}, {"id": "Ep20.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Cui rei igitur inserviet noster intellectus? cui voluntatem intra limites intellectus continendi potestas? qua de causa ordo ille nobis infixus est? Et vide, quaeso ab alia parte, qua re nos privemus, nempe anxia, et seria meditatione ad nos ipsos secundum regulam perfectionis Dei, et ordinem nobis impressum perfectos reddendum: nosmet precatione, et ad Deum suspiriis, quibus tam saepe extraordinariam confortationem accepisse percepimus, orbamus: nosmet tota religione, ac omni illa spe, et acquiescentia, quam ex precibus, et religione speramus, orbamus.", "en": "To what purpose, then, will our intellect serve? To what purpose the power of containing the will within the limits of the intellect? For what reason has that order been fixed in us? And see, I pray, on the other side, of what we deprive ourselves: namely, of anxious and serious meditation on making ourselves perfect according to the rule of God’s perfection and the order impressed on us; we bereave ourselves of prayer and of sighs to God, by which we have so often perceived that we received extraordinary strengthening; we bereave ourselves of all religion, and of all the hope and contentment which we hope for from prayers and religion."}, {"id": "Ep20.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Enimvero, si nullam mali habet Deus cognitionem, multo minus credibile est, eum malum puniturum. Quaenam ergo supersunt rationes, quo minus facinora quaevis (si modo iudicem effugio) avide perpetrem? cur non detestandis mediis divitias mihi acquiro? cur absque discrimine, et, quo caro nos trahit, id, quod lubet, non perpetro?", "en": "Indeed, if God has no knowledge of evil, it is much less credible that he will punish evil. What reasons, then, remain why I should not eagerly commit any crimes (if only I escape the judge)? Why should I not acquire riches by detestable means? Why should I not do whatever I please, without distinction, wherever the flesh draws us?"}, {"id": "Ep20.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Dices, quia virtus propter se est amanda. Qui vero virtutem amare possum? mihi tantum essentiae, ac perfectionis non est tributum; et si tantum acquiescentiae, et ex hoc et ex illo habere licet, cur mihi vim, ut voluntatem intra limites intellectus contineam, infero? cur id non facio, quo me affectus trahunt? cur non hominem, qui mihi alicubi obest, clam occido? etc.", "en": "You will say: because virtue is to be loved for its own sake. But how can I love virtue? So much essence and perfection has not been given me; and if I may have as much contentment from the one as from the other, why should I do violence to myself to contain the will within the limits of the intellect? Why should I not do what my affects draw me to? Why should I not secretly kill a man who stands in my way somewhere? etc."}, {"id": "Ep20.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Ecce quam cunctis impiis, et impietati demus occasionem? nos truncis, omnesque nostras actiones motibus horologiorum similes reddimus.", "en": "See what occasion we give to all the impious and to impiety? We make ourselves like tree-trunks, and all our actions like the motions of clocks."}, {"id": "Ep20.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Ex dictis valde durum mihi videtur, quod improprie tantum in Deum peccare possimus dici: cui enim rei data nobis voluntatem intra limites intellectus continendi potentia inservit, si, ubi eam transgredimur, contra eum peccamus ordinem? Regeres forte, id non esse peccatum in Deum; sed in nos ipsos: nam si proprie erga Deum peccare diceremur, etiam dicendum esset, aliquid contra Dei voluntatem fieri; quod iuxta te est impossibile: Ergo etiam peccatum.", "en": "From what has been said it seems to me very hard that we can be said to sin against God only improperly: for to what purpose does the power given us of containing the will within the limits of the intellect serve, if, when we transgress it, we sin against that order? You will perhaps reply that it is not a sin against God but against ourselves: for if we were said to sin properly against God, it would also have to be said that something happens against God’s will; which according to you is impossible: therefore so is sin."}, {"id": "Ep20.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Interim alterutrum verum sit necesse est, Deum vel velle, vel nolle. Si vult, quomodo nostri respectu malum esse potest? Si non vult, id, ex tua opinione, non fieret. Quamvis autem hoc aliquam, ut tua fert sententia, involveret absurditatem, praedictas tamen absurditates admittere periculosissimum videtur. Quis novit, si anxie inquirerem, annon remedium posset inveniri his aliquo modo conciliandis.", "en": "Meanwhile, one of two things must be true: God either wills it or does not will it. If he wills it, how can it be evil in relation to us? If he does not will it, on your opinion it would not happen. But although this, as your opinion has it, would involve some absurdity, it still seems most dangerous to admit the absurdities stated above. Who knows, if I were to inquire anxiously, whether a remedy could not be found for reconciling these in some way."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Atque sic tuae epistolae examen iuxta regulam meam primam generalem finiam: antequam vero ad examen iuxta secundam regulam accedam, duo adhuc proponam, quae epistolam tuam spectant, quaeque scripsisti Princ. Part. 1. Prop. 15 prius est, quod affirmas, nos potestatem volendi, et iudicandi intra limites intellectus retinere posse: quod absolute concedere nequeo.", "en": "And so I shall finish the examination of your letter according to my first general rule; but before I come to the examination according to the second rule, I shall put forward two more points which concern your letter and what you wrote in the Principles, Part I, Prop. 15. The first is that you affirm that we can keep the power of willing and judging within the limits of the intellect: which I cannot grant absolutely."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Nam hoc si foret verum, certe ex innumerabilibus vel unus reperiretur homo, qui se ea praeditum potestate ostenderet: etiam quivis apud se experiri potest, se quantas etiam impendat vires, eum scopum attingere non posse. Et si quis de hoc negotio dubitat, seipsum examinet, quoties invito intellectu passiones rationem suam vincant, etiam tum quum summis obnititur viribus.", "en": "For if this were true, surely among countless men at least one would be found who showed himself endowed with that power; and anyone can find by experience in himself that, however much force he applies, he cannot reach that goal. And if anyone doubts this matter, let him examine himself, how often, against the will of his intellect, the passions conquer his reason, even when he resists with all his might."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "At dices, quod hoc minus praestamus, non est, quia nobis impossibile est; sed quia sufficientem non adhibemus diligentiam; regero, quod, si hoc possibile esset, saltem ex tot millibus vel unus inveniretur; sed ne unus omnium hominum exstitit, vel existit, qui auderet gloriari, se in errores haud incidisse. Quaenam vero huius rei certiora, quam ipsa exempla, possunt adduci argumenta? Si pauci essent, unus esset; iam vero quum nullus sit, nulla eius quoque datur probatio.", "en": "But you will say that our failing to do this is not because it is impossible for us, but because we do not apply sufficient diligence; I reply that if this were possible, at least one out of so many thousands would be found; but not one of all men has existed or exists who would dare to boast that he has not fallen into errors. And what more certain arguments for this can be brought than the examples themselves? If there were a few, there would be one; but now, since there is none, there is no proof of it either."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Instabis et dices; si fieri potest, ut iudicium suspendendo, et voluntatem intra terminos intellectus continendo, semel, ne errem, efficere queo, quare, ubi eandem adhibeo diligentiam, semper id efficere non possem? Respondeo, me videre non posse, nos hodie tantum virium habere, ut in eo semper perseveremus; semel una hora, quando omnes intendo nervos, iter duorum milliarium conficere potis sum, verum semper id praestare non possum; ita me summo studio ab errore semel praeservare possum, sed vires ad id semper praestandum deficiunt.", "en": "You will insist and say: if it can happen that by suspending judgment and containing the will within the bounds of the intellect I can once bring it about that I do not err, why could I not always bring it about when I apply the same diligence? I answer that I cannot see that we today have so much strength that we can always persevere in it; once, in one hour, when I strain every nerve, I am able to cover a journey of two miles, but I cannot always do it; so I can once preserve myself from error with the greatest effort, but the strength to do so always is lacking."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Clarum mihi videtur, primum hominem, e manu perfecti illius artificis prodeuntem, iis praeditum fuisse viribus; sed (ut in hoc tecum sentio) eum, illis viribus non satis utendo, vel abutendo, statum suum amisisse perfectum ad praestandum, quod antea sui erat arbitrii. Haecque multis rationibus, ni nimius essem, firmarem.", "en": "It seems clear to me that the first man, coming forth from the hand of that perfect craftsman, was endowed with those powers; but (as I agree with you in this) that by not using those powers enough, or by misusing them, he lost his perfect state for doing what had before been within his choice. And this I would confirm by many reasons, if I were not being too long."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Et hoc in negotio omnem Sacrae Scripturae essentiam, quae propterea apud nos in honore esse debet, sitam esse opinor, quoniam nos id, quod tam clare intellectus noster naturalis confirmat, docet: nempe, lapsum ex nostra prima perfectione nostra imprudentia factum esse. Quid igitur istius lapsus emendatione magis necessarium? Atque hominem lapsum ad Deum reducere Sacrae Scripturae unicus quoque est scopus.", "en": "And in this matter I think the whole essence of Holy Scripture lies, which is why it ought to be held in honour among us, since it teaches us what our natural intellect so clearly confirms: namely, that the fall from our first perfection came about through our imprudence. What, then, is more necessary than the amendment of this fall? And to bring fallen man back to God is also the sole aim of Holy Scripture."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Posterius est, Princip. Part. 1. Prop. 15. quod affirmas, res clare et distincte intelligere repugnare naturae hominis, unde tandem concludis, longe melius esse rebus, quamvis confusis assentiri, et libertatem exercere, quam semper indifferentem, hoc est, in infimo gradu libertatis manere.", "en": "The second is what you affirm in the Principles, Part I, Prop. 15: that to understand things clearly and distinctly is contrary to the nature of man; from which you finally conclude that it is far better to assent to things, even if confused, and to exercise freedom, than always to remain indifferent, that is, in the lowest degree of freedom."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Quam ut concedam conclusionem, impedit in me obscuritas: nam suspensum iudicium nos in eo statu, in quo a creatore sumus creati, conservat; verum rebus confuse est rebus non intellectis assentiri, et ita aeque facile vero ac falso assentiri.", "en": "An obscurity in me prevents me from granting this conclusion: for suspended judgment keeps us in the state in which we were created by the creator; but to assent to things confusedly is to assent to things not understood, and so to assent as easily to the false as to the true."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Et si (ut D. Des Cartes alicubi docet) eum ordinem in assentiendo non usurpamus, quem Deus inter nostrum intellectum, nostramque voluntatem constituit, nempe ut non nisi clare perceptis assentiamur: quamvis tunc casu in veritatem incidamus, tamen quia verum non eo, quo Deus voluit ordine, amplectimur, peccamus; et per consequens, ut assensus cohibitio nos in statu, in quo a Deo constituti sumus, conservat, ita confusus assensus nostrum statum peiorem reddit; erroris quippe ponit fundamentum, quo perfectum deinceps amittimus statum.", "en": "And if (as Mr Descartes teaches somewhere) we do not use in assenting the order which God has established between our intellect and our will, namely that we assent only to things clearly perceived, then, although we may by chance hit upon the truth, yet because we do not embrace the truth in the order God willed, we sin; and consequently, just as the withholding of assent keeps us in the state in which we were placed by God, so confused assent makes our state worse; for it lays the foundation of error, through which we then lose our perfect state."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Sed te dicentem audio: nonne praestat, ut nos perfectiores reddamus, rebus licet confusis assentiendo, quam ut non assentiendo semper in infimo perfectionis, et libertatis gradu maneamus? At praeterquam quod hoc negaverimus, et aliquo modo ostenderimus, nos nosmet non meliores; sed deteriores reddidisse; nobis etiam impossibile, et quasi contradictio videtur, Deum cognitionem rerum, ab ipso determinatarum, longius extendere, ac eam, quam nobis dedit; imo Deum tunc absolutam nostrorum errorum causam involvere.", "en": "But I hear you saying: is it not better to make ourselves more perfect by assenting to things, though confused, than by not assenting always to remain in the lowest degree of perfection and freedom? But besides the fact that we have denied this, and have shown in some way that we have made ourselves not better but worse, it also seems to us impossible, and as it were a contradiction, that God should extend the knowledge of things determined by him further than the knowledge which he has given us; indeed, that God would then be involved as the absolute cause of our errors."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Nec huic contrarium est, quod nos Deum, ut plura in nos conferret, quam contulit, non possumus accusare; quia ad id non tenebatur. Verum quidem est, Deum non teneri, ut plura, quam dedit, daret; sed summa Dei etiam infert perfectio, ut creatura ab ipso procedens nullam contradictionem involveret, prout tum videretur sequi.", "en": "Nor is it contrary to this that we cannot accuse God of not conferring more on us than he has conferred, because he was not bound to do so. It is true indeed that God is not bound to give more than he has given; but God’s supreme perfection also implies that a creature proceeding from him should involve no contradiction, as would then seem to follow."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Nullibi namque in creata natura, praeterquam in nostro intellectu, deprehendimus scientiam. In quem alium finem ille nobis concessus esset, nisi ut Dei opera contemplaremur et cognosceremus? Et quidnam evidentius sequi videtur, quam quod harmonia inter res cognoscendas, nostrumque intellectum dari debet?", "en": "For nowhere in created nature, except in our intellect, do we find knowledge. To what other end would it have been granted us, unless that we should contemplate and know God’s works? And what seems to follow more evidently than that there must be harmony between the things to be known and our intellect?"}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Si tuam epistolam circa ea, quae modo diximus, ad meam secundam regulam generalem examinarem, magis, quam in prima, discreparemus. Mihi namque videtur, (aberranti commonstra viam) te Sacrae Scripturae eam infallibilem veritatem et divinitatem non adscribere, quam ego in ea esse mihi persuadeo. Verum quidem est, quod te credere ais, Deum res Sacrae Scripturae prophetis revelasse; sed modo adeo imperfecto, ut, si factum est, prout tu statuis, in Deo contradictionem involveret.", "en": "If I were to examine your letter, as regards what we have just said, by my second general rule, we should differ more than under the first. For it seems to me (show the way to one who strays) that you do not ascribe to Holy Scripture the infallible truth and divinity which I am persuaded is in it. It is true indeed that you say you believe that God revealed the things of Holy Scripture to the prophets; but in so imperfect a manner that, if it happened as you maintain, it would involve a contradiction in God."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Si enim Deus suum verbum, ac voluntatem hominibus manifestavit, certum in finem, idque clare iis manifestavit. Si iam prophetae ex illo verbo, quod acceperant, Parabolam finxissent, id Deus vel voluisset, vel noluisset. Si voluisset, ut exin Parabolam fingerent, hoc est, ut ab eius mente aberrarent; Deus istius erroris esset causa, aliquidque quod contrariaretur sibi, vellet. Si noluisset, impossibile erat, ut prophetae inde Parabolam effingerent.", "en": "For if God has manifested his word and will to men, he has manifested it to a certain end, and clearly. If, then, the prophets had devised a parable from the word they had received, God either willed it or did not will it. If he had willed that they should devise a parable from it, that is, that they should stray from his mind, God would be the cause of that error, and would will something contrary to himself. If he had not willed it, it was impossible for the prophets to devise a parable from it."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Praeterea credibile est, si supponitur, Deum verbum suum prophetis dedisse, quod ita eis dedit, ut illud accipiendo non erraverint: nam Deus in verbo suo revelando certum finem habere debebat; at sibi finem, ut homines in errorem duceret, proponere nequit: Id enim in Deo esset contradictio. Etiam homo contra Dei voluntatem errare non poterat; nam id, secundum te, fieri nequit.", "en": "Moreover, it is credible, if it is supposed that God gave his word to the prophets, that he gave it to them in such a way that they did not err in receiving it: for God must have had a certain end in revealing his word; but he cannot set himself the end of leading men into error: for that would be a contradiction in God. Nor could man err against God’s will; for that, according to you, cannot happen."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Praeter haec omnia, de illo summe perfecto Deo credi non potest, eum permittere, ut suo verbo, prophetis dato, ad illud populo explicandum, alius a prophetis tribueretur sensus, quam Deus voluit: Si enim statuimus, Deum prophetis suum concessisse verbum, simul asserimus, Deum modo extraordinario prophetis apparuisse, vel cum iis loquutum fuisse.", "en": "Besides all this, it cannot be believed of that supremely perfect God that he would allow a sense other than he willed to be given by the prophets to his word, given to the prophets for explaining it to the people: for if we maintain that God granted his word to the prophets, we assert at the same time that God appeared to the prophets in an extraordinary way, or spoke with them."}, {"id": "Ep20.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Si iam prophetae de hoc tradito verbo Parabolam fingunt, hoc est, alium ei sensum tribuunt, quam, ut tribuerent, voluit, Deus hanc rem eos edocuisset. Est etiam tam respectu prophetarum impossibile, quam respectu Dei contradictio, prophetas alium sensum potuisse habere, quam eos habere voluit Deus.", "en": "If, then, the prophets devise a parable from this word handed down to them, that is, give it a sense other than he willed them to give, God would have taught them this. It is also as impossible with respect to the prophets as it is a contradiction with respect to God that the prophets could have had a sense other than God willed them to have."}, {"id": "Ep20.7", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Deum suum ita revelasse verbum, uti tu vis, parum probas, nempe eum salutem, et perditionem tantum revelasse, certa eum in finem decrevisse media, atque salutem, ac perditionem non nisi decretorum mediorum effectus esse.", "en": "That God revealed his word in the way you would have it, namely that he revealed only salvation and perdition, that he decreed certain means to that end, and that salvation and perdition are nothing but the effects of the decreed means, you prove poorly."}, {"id": "Ep20.7", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Nam profecto, si prophetae Dei verbum isthoc sensu acceperant, quas rationes, ad alium illi adscribendum sensum, habuissent? Sed et nullam adducis probationem, qua nos, ut hanc tuam supra prophetarum sententiam poneremus, convinceres.", "en": "For surely, if the prophets had received God’s word in this sense, what reasons would they have had for ascribing another sense to it? But you also bring no proof by which to convince us to set this opinion of yours above that of the prophets."}, {"id": "Ep20.7", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Si vero existimas, hanc esse probationem, quod alioquin illud verbum multas imperfectiones, et contrarietates includeret, dico, hoc duntaxat dici, non vero probari. Et quis novit, si uterque sensus produceretur, uter minores includeret imperfectiones? Denique Enti summe perfecto bene perspectum erat, quid populus caperet, quaeque optima esset methodus, iuxta quam populus institui deberet.", "en": "And if you think the proof is that otherwise that word would include many imperfections and contrarieties, I say that this is merely said, not proved. And who knows, if both senses were brought forward, which would include fewer imperfections? Finally, the supremely perfect Being well understood what the people could grasp, and what was the best method by which the people ought to be instructed."}, {"id": "Ep20.8", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Quantum ad secundum primae tuae quaestionis membrum attinet, quaeris ex te ipso, quare Deus Adamo, ne ex arbore ederet, cum tamen contrarium decrevisset, edixit; respondes vero, edictum Adamo factum in hoc solo consistere, quod nimirum Deus Adamo revelavit τὸ edere ex arbore mortis esse causam, quemadmodum nobis per naturalem rationem venenum mortiferum esse revelat.", "en": "As regards the second part of your first question, you ask yourself why God commanded Adam not to eat from the tree when he had nevertheless decreed the contrary; and you answer that ‘the command given to Adam consisted only in this, that God revealed to Adam that eating from the tree was the cause of death, just as he reveals to us by natural reason that poison is deadly’."}, {"id": "Ep20.8", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Si statuitur, Deum aliquid Adamo interdixisse, quaenam rationes sunt, quo magis crederem modum prohibitionis, quem tu adfers, quam quem adducunt prophetae, quibus Deus ipse modum prohibitionis revelavit? Dices, mea prohibendi ratio magis naturalis est, et ideo magis veritati, Deoque conveniens. Sed hoc omne nego.", "en": "If it is maintained that God forbade Adam something, what reasons are there why I should believe the manner of prohibition which you bring forward rather than the one which the prophets adduce, to whom God himself revealed the manner of prohibition? You will say: my account of the prohibition is more natural, and therefore more in keeping with truth and with God. But all this I deny."}, {"id": "Ep20.8", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Nec capio, quod Deus per naturalem intellectum nobis, venenum lethale esse, revelavit; nec rationes video, quibus unquam scirem, aliquid esse venenatum, nisi malos veneni effectus in aliis vidissem, et audivissem. Quod homines, quia venenum non noscunt, inscii edant et moriantur, quotidiana nos docet experientia.", "en": "Nor do I grasp that God has revealed to us through the natural intellect that poison is deadly; nor do I see reasons by which I would ever know that something is poisonous, unless I had seen and heard of the bad effects of poison in others. That men, because they do not know poison, eat it unknowingly and die, daily experience teaches us."}, {"id": "Ep20.8", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Dices, si homines scirent, id venenum esse, etiam malum id esse, eos non lateret; sed respondeo neminem veneni habere notitiam, aut posse habere; nisi qui vidit, aut audivit, aliquem eius usu damnum sibi dedisse; et si ponimus, nos ad hunc usque diem nunquam audivisse, vel vidisse, aliquem eius usu sibi nocuisse, non tantum nunc quoque ignoraremus; sed et sine metu in proprium damnum id usurparemus, quemadmodum tales veritates indies docemur.", "en": "You will say that if men knew that it is poison, it would not be hidden from them that it is also evil; but I answer that no one has, or can have, knowledge of poison except one who has seen or heard that someone has done himself harm by using it; and if we suppose that up to this day we had never heard or seen that anyone had harmed himself by using it, not only would we still be ignorant of it now, but we would also use it to our own harm without fear, as we are taught such truths daily."}, {"id": "Ep20.9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Quid magis in hac vita candidum atque erectum animum, quam illius perfectae Deitatis contemplatio, delectat? Nam quemadmodum circa perfectissimum versatur, ita etiam perfectissimum, quod in finitum nostrum intellectum cadere potest, involvere debet. Nec mihi in vita quicquam est, quod cum ea mutarem delectatione.", "en": "What delights a candid and upright mind more in this life than the contemplation of that perfect Deity? For as it is concerned with what is most perfect, so it must also involve the most perfect that can fall within our finite intellect. Nor is there anything in my life for which I would exchange that delight."}, {"id": "Ep20.9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "In hac, caelesti instigatus appetitu, multum temporis insumere possum; sed et simul tristitia affici, quando intueor tam multa finito meo intellectui deesse: verum tristitiam sedo spe ea, quam possideo, quae mihi vita charior est, quod posthac existam, et permanebo et eam Deitatem maiori perfectione, quam nunc, intuebor.", "en": "In it, moved by a heavenly appetite, I can spend much time; but I can also at the same time be affected with sadness when I see how much is lacking to my finite intellect: but I calm the sadness with the hope I possess, which is dearer to me than life, that I shall exist hereafter and remain, and shall behold that Deity with greater perfection than now."}, {"id": "Ep20.9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Ubi brevem, praetervolantemque considero vitam, in qua singulis momentis mortem exspecto; si deberem credere me finem habiturum, sanctaque illa, et praestantissima cariturum contemplatione, certe omnium creaturarum, quibus cognitio finis sui deest, miserrimus essem. Quippe metus mortis ante obitum miserum me redderet, et post cum plane nihil, et per consequens miser essem, quia divina illa contemplatione privatus essem. Huc vero me tuae videntur ducere opiniones, quod ubi hic esse desino, etiam in aeternum desinam, cum econtra illud verbum, et Dei voluntas consolentur interno suo in anima mea testimonio, quod post hanc vitam in statu perfectiore me aliquando, contemplando summe perfectam Deitatem, oblectabo.", "en": "When I consider this short and fleeting life, in which I expect death at every moment, if I had to believe that I would have an end and be deprived of that holy and most excellent contemplation, I would certainly be the most miserable of all creatures, which lack knowledge of their end. For fear of death before my death would make me miserable, and after it I would be altogether nothing, and consequently miserable, because I would be deprived of that divine contemplation. And this is where your opinions seem to lead me: that when I cease to be here, I shall also cease for eternity; whereas, on the contrary, that word and the will of God console me with their inner testimony in my soul that after this life I shall one day delight, in a more perfect state, in contemplating the supremely perfect Deity."}, {"id": "Ep20.9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Profecto licet spes ista aliquando falsa esse deprehenderetur, me tamen, dum spero, beatum reddit.", "en": "Certainly, though this hope should one day be found false, yet while I hope it makes me blessed."}, {"id": "Ep20.9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Unicum hoc est, quod precibus, suspiriis, et seriis votis a Deo peto, et petam, (utinam ad id plus conferre liceret!) quamdiu spiritus hos reget artus, ut ipsi placeat, me sua bonitate tam beatum reddere, ut, quando hoc corpus dissolvitur, ens intellectuale ad perfectissimam illam Deitatem contemplandum maneam, et si hoc tantum nanciscor, mihi, quomodo hic credatur, quid invicem persuadeatur, an aliquid naturali intellectu fundetur, et percipi ab eo possit, nec ne, perinde est.", "en": "This is the one thing that I ask and shall ask of God with prayers, sighs and earnest vows (would that I could contribute more to it!), as long as breath shall rule these limbs: that it may please him, in his goodness, to make me so blessed that when this body is dissolved I may remain an intellectual being to contemplate that most perfect Deity; and if I obtain only this, it is all the same to me how it is believed here, what people persuade one another of, whether something is founded on the natural intellect and can be perceived by it or not."}, {"id": "Ep20.9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Hoc, hoc solummodo est votum meum, meum desiderium, continuaeque preces, ut Deus hanc certitudinem in anima mea confirmet, et, si eam possideo, (ah me miserrimum, si eo destituar!) prae desiderio anima mea exclamet, quemadmodum cervus anhelat aquarum rivos, ita te, o Deus vivens, anima mea desiderat: ah! quando veniet dies, quod tecum ero, et te adspiciam. Si hoc tantum obtineo, omne animae meae studium, et desiderium possideo.", "en": "This, this alone, is my vow, my desire and my continual prayer: that God may confirm this certainty in my soul, and, if I possess it (ah, most wretched me, if I should lack it!), that my soul may cry out in longing: As the hart pants after the brooks of water, so my soul longs for thee, O living God: ah! when will the day come that I shall be with thee and behold thee? If I obtain only this, I possess all that my soul strives for and desires."}, {"id": "Ep20.9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Quia vero nostrum opus Deo displicet, spem istam, ex tua sententia, non video: Nec intelligo, Deum, (siquidem de eo humano more loqui liceat) si nullam ex opere nostro, et laudatione capit voluptatem, nos produxisse, et conservasse. Si vero a tua sententia aberro, tuam explicationem expeto.", "en": "But since our work displeases God, I do not see this hope on your opinion: nor do I understand that God (if we may speak of him in human fashion) produced and preserved us if he takes no pleasure in our work and praise. But if I stray from your opinion, I ask for your explanation."}, {"id": "Ep20.9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Verum me, et forte te solito diutius moratus sum; quia vero mihi chartam deesse video, finiam. Horum solutionem videre aveo. Forte hic illic aliquam ex epistola tua eduxi conclusionem, quae fortassis tua non erit opinio; sed super ea re explicationem tuam audire cupio.", "en": "But I have detained myself, and perhaps you, longer than usual; and since I see that I am running out of paper, I shall end. I am eager to see the solution of these things. Perhaps here and there I have drawn from your letter some conclusion which will perhaps not be your opinion; but I wish to hear your explanation of it."}, {"id": "Ep20.10", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Nuper occupatus fui in quibusdam Dei attributis perpendendis, in quo me tua non parum iuvit appendix. Mentem tuam solummodo fusius explicui, quae mihi non nisi demonstrationes exhibere videtur, et ideo demiror in praefatione affirmari, te illa non sentire; sed obligatum fuisse, ut ita tuum, uti promiseras, Cartesii philosophiam discipulum doceres; et te longe aliam, tam de Deo, quam de anima, speciatim de animae voluntate fovere opinionem: video quoque in illa praefatione dici, te haec cogitata metaphysica brevi prolixiora editurum, quae duo avide desidero: nam de iis aliquid singulare spero.", "en": "I have lately been occupied in weighing certain attributes of God, in which your appendix has helped me not a little. I have merely set out your view more fully, which seems to me to present nothing but demonstrations; and so I am surprised that it is affirmed in the preface that you do not hold these views, but were obliged to teach your pupil Descartes’s philosophy as you had promised, and that you hold a very different opinion both of God and of the soul, especially of the soul’s will: I see also that it is said in that preface that you will shortly publish these Metaphysical Thoughts at greater length, both of which I eagerly desire: for I hope for something singular from them."}, {"id": "Ep20.10", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Mea autem non fert consuetudo aliquem laudibus extollere. Scripsi haec sincero animo, et amicitia non fucata, prout in epistola tua petiisti, utque veritas detegeretur. Excusa nimiam, praeter intentionem, prolixitatem. Si ad has responderis, me tibi summopere devincies.", "en": "But it is not my custom to extol anyone with praises. I have written this with a sincere mind and unfeigned friendship, as you asked in your letter, and so that the truth may be uncovered. Excuse the excessive length, beyond my intention. If you answer this, you will oblige me greatly."}, {"id": "Ep20.10", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Non recuso, si ea lingua, in qua educatus sum, scribere animus est, vel alia, modo Latina, aut Gallica sit; hoc vero responsum, ut eadem exares lingua, peto, quia mentem tuam in ea bene, quod forte in lingua Latina non fieret, percipiebam.", "en": "I do not object if you have a mind to write in the language in which I was brought up, or in another, provided it is Latin or French; but this reply I ask you to compose in the same language, because I understood your mind well in it, which perhaps would not happen in Latin."}, {"id": "Ep20.10", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Ita me tibi devincies, egoque ero, ac permanebo, Mi Domine, tibi addictissimus atque officiosissimus,", "en": "So you will oblige me, and I shall be and remain, Sir, your most devoted and most dutiful"}, {"id": "Ep20.10", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "G. de Blyenbergh.", "en": "W. van Blyenbergh."}, {"id": "Ep20.11", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "Dordraci 16. Ianuarii 1665.", "en": "Dordrecht, 16 January 1665."}, {"id": "Ep20.12", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 20", "la": "In responsione tua prolixius, quid per negationem in Deo intelligas, erudiri exopto.", "en": "In your reply I should like to be taught more fully what you understand by negation in God."}, {"id": "Ep21", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Quum primam tuam epistolam legebam, existimabam nostras opiniones fere concordare; sed vero ex secunda, quae 21 huius mensis mihi tradita est, longe aliter se rem habere intelligo, videoque nos non tantum dissentire de iis, quae longe ex primis principiis sunt petenda; sed etiam de eisdem ipsis principiis: adeo ut vix credam fore, ut nos epistolis invicem erudire possimus.", "en": "When I read your first letter, I thought our opinions almost agreed; but from the second, which was delivered to me on the 21st of this month, I understand that the matter stands far otherwise, and I see that we disagree not only about the things that must be derived at long remove from first principles, but also about the principles themselves: so that I hardly believe that we can instruct each other by letters."}, {"id": "Ep21", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Video namque nullam demonstrationem, licet pro demonstrationis legibus solidissimam, apud te valere, nisi conveniat cum ea explicatione, quam vel ipse, vel alii theologi, tibi haud ignoti, sacrae Scripturae tribuunt. Verum si deprehendas, Deum per sacram Scripturam clarius, et efficacius loqui, quam per lumen naturalis intellectus, quod nobis etiam concessit, ac assiduo sapientia sua divina firmiter, et incorrupte conservat, validas habes rationes, ut intellectum flectas ad opiniones, quas sacrae Scripturae tribuis, quin ipse haud aliter facere possem.", "en": "For I see that no demonstration, however solid according to the laws of demonstration, has weight with you unless it agrees with the explanation which either you yourself or other theologians known to you attribute to Holy Scripture. But if you find that God speaks more clearly and effectively through Holy Scripture than through the light of the natural intellect, which he has also granted us and constantly preserves, firm and uncorrupted, by his divine wisdom, you have strong reasons for bending your intellect to the opinions you attribute to Holy Scripture; indeed, I myself could not do otherwise."}, {"id": "Ep21", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Quod vero me spectat, quia plane, et sine ambagibus profiteor, me S. Scripturam non intelligere, licet aliquot annos in ea insumpserim, et quia me haud fugit, quando solidam nactus sum demonstrationem, me non posse in tales cogitationes incidere, ut unquam de ea dubitare queam, omnino in eo, quod mihi intellectus monstrat, acquiesco sine ulla suspicione, me ea in re deceptum esse, nec Sacram Scripturam, quamvis eam non investigem, ei contradicere posse; quia veritas veritati non repugnat, ut iam ante in appendice mea (caput indicare nequeo, nec enim liber hic ruri penes me est) clare indicavi; et si fructum, quem iam ex intellectu naturali cepi, vel semel falsum esse deprehenderem, me fortunatum redderet, quoniam fruor, et vitam non maerore et gemitu, sed tranquillitate, laetitia, et hilaritate transigere studeo et subinde gradum unum adscendo.", "en": "But as for me, since I profess plainly and without circumlocution that I do not understand Holy Scripture, though I have spent some years on it, and since it does not escape me that when I have obtained a solid demonstration I cannot fall into such thoughts that I could ever doubt it, I acquiesce entirely in what the intellect shows me, without any suspicion that I have been deceived in it, or that Holy Scripture, even though I do not search it, can contradict it; because truth does not conflict with truth, as I have already clearly indicated in my appendix (I cannot give the chapter, for the book is not with me here in the country); and if I were even once to find that the fruit which I have already gathered from the natural intellect was false, it would still make me fortunate, since I enjoy it, and try to pass my life not in sorrow and sighing but in tranquillity, joy and cheerfulness, and from time to time I climb a step higher."}, {"id": "Ep21", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Agnosco interim (id, quod summam mihi praebet satisfactionem, et mentis tranquillitatem) cuncta potentia entis summe perfecti, ac eius immutabili ita fieri decreto.", "en": "Meanwhile I recognize (which gives me the greatest satisfaction and tranquillity of mind) that all things happen thus by the power of a supremely perfect being and by his immutable decree."}, {"id": "Ep21.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Ad tuam vero ut redeam epistolam, dico me tibi summas ex animo agere gratias, quod mihi tuam in tempore philosophandi rationem aperuisti; sed quod tu talia, qualia vis ex mea epistola educere, mihi affingas, pro eo nullas tibi gratis ago. Quam, quaeso, materiam mea praebuit epistola has mihi affingendi opiniones, homines nempe bestiis esse similes, homines bestiarum more mori, et interire, nostra Deo displicere opera, etc. (licet in postremo hoc summopere dissentiamus, quia aliter te non percipio, quam quod intelligis, Deum nostris operibus delectari, tanquam qui finem suum assecutus est, eo quod res ex voto successit).", "en": "But to return to your letter, I thank you most heartily for opening to me in time your manner of philosophizing; but for imputing to me such things as you wish to draw out of my letter, I give you no thanks. What material, I ask, did my letter offer for imputing to me these opinions: namely, that men are like beasts, that men die and perish in the manner of beasts, that our works displease God, etc. (though on this last we disagree greatly, since I do not understand you otherwise than as meaning that God delights in our works, as one who has attained his end because the thing has turned out as he wished)."}, {"id": "Ep21.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Quantum ad me, ego profecto clare, dixi, probos Deum colere, et assidue colendo perfectiores evadere, Deum amare; hoccine est eos bestiis similes reddere; aut eos instar bestiarum perire, vel denique eorum opera Deo non placere?", "en": "As for me, I certainly said clearly that the good worship God, and by constantly worshipping become more perfect, and love God; is this to make them like beasts, or to have them perish like beasts, or finally to say that their works do not please God?"}, {"id": "Ep21.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Si meas literas maiore cum attentione legisses, clare perspexisses, nostrum dissensum in hoc solo positum esse; scilicet an Deus, ut Deus, hoc est, absolute, nulla ei humana attributa adscribendo, perfectiones, quas probi accipiunt, eis communicet; (ut ego intelligo) anve ut iudex, quod ultimum tu statuis; et ea de causa defendis impios, quia iuxta Dei decretum, quicquid possunt, faciunt, Deo aeque, ac pios servire. Sed vero secundum mea dicta id nullatenus sequitur: quia Deum tanquam iudicem non introduco, et ideo ego opera ex operis qualitate, non vero ex potentia operatoris aestimo, et merces, quae opus consequitur, tam necessario id consequitur, quam ex natura trianguli sequitur, tres eius angulos duobus rectis aequales esse debere.", "en": "If you had read my letter with greater attention, you would have seen clearly that our disagreement lies in this alone: namely, whether God, as God, that is, absolutely, without ascribing any human attributes to him, communicates to the good the perfections which they receive (as I understand it), or as a judge, which is what you maintain; and for that reason you maintain that the impious, because according to God’s decree they do whatever they can, serve God as much as the pious. But according to what I said this by no means follows: because I do not introduce God as a judge, and therefore I value works by the quality of the work, not by the power of the worker; and the reward which follows the work follows it as necessarily as it follows from the nature of a triangle that its three angles must be equal to two right angles."}, {"id": "Ep21.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Et hoc unusquisque intelliget, qui tantum ad id, quod summa nostra beatitudo in amore erga Deum consistit, attendit, quodque ille amor necessario ex Dei cognitione, quae nobis tantopere commendatur, fluit. Hoc autem generaliter facillime demonstrari potest, si modo ad naturam Decreti Dei attendatur, quemadmodum in mea appendice explicui: Verum fateor, eos omnes, qui divinam naturam cum humana confundunt, valde ad id intelligendum ineptos esse.", "en": "And everyone will understand this who attends only to this, that our highest blessedness consists in love towards God, and that this love flows necessarily from the knowledge of God which is so much commended to us. This can be demonstrated very easily in general, if only one attends to the nature of God’s decree, as I have explained in my appendix: but I confess that all who confuse the divine nature with the human are very unfit to understand it."}, {"id": "Ep21.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Hisce literis finem hic imponere animus erat, ne tibi amplius essem molestus iis rebus, quae tantummodo (uti clarum est ex valde devoto additamento fini tuae epistolae affixo) pro ioco, et risu inserviunt, nulli autem usui sunt. Ne vero petitionem tuam omni modo reiiciam, ulterius ad explicationem vocum negationis, et privationis, et breviter ad id, quod necessarium est ad sensum praecedentis meae epistolae dilucidius enucleandum, progrediar.", "en": "I had intended to put an end to this letter here, so as not to trouble you further with things which serve only (as is clear from the very devout addition appended to the end of your letter) for jest and laughter, and are of no use. But so as not to reject your request altogether, I shall go on further to the explanation of the words negation and privation, and briefly to what is necessary to unfold more clearly the sense of my preceding letter."}, {"id": "Ep21.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Dico igitur primo privationem non esse privandi actum; sed tantum simplicem, et meram carentiam, quae in se nihil est: est quippe ens rationis tantum, vel modus cogitandi, quem formamus, quum res invicem comparamus. Dicimus, exempli gratia, caecum visu privatum esse, quia eum facile, ut videntem imaginamur, sive imaginatio haec hinc oriatur, quod cum aliis, qui vident, sive quod praesentem suum statum cum praeterito, quum videbat, comparamus; et cum hunc virum ea ratione consideramus, nempe comparando suam naturam cum natura aliorum, vel cum praeterita sua, tunc affirmamus, visum ad suam naturam pertinere, et propterea dicimus, eum eo privatum esse.", "en": "I say, then, first, that privation is not the act of depriving, but only a simple and mere lack, which in itself is nothing: it is indeed only a being of reason, or mode of thinking, which we form when we compare things with one another. We say, for example, that a blind man is deprived of sight, because we easily imagine him as seeing, whether this imagination arises from our comparing him with others who see, or his present state with his past one, when he saw; and when we consider this man in that way, namely by comparing his nature with the nature of others or with his own past nature, then we affirm that sight belongs to his nature, and therefore say that he is deprived of it."}, {"id": "Ep21.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Verum quum Dei decretum, eiusque natura consideratur, non magis de illo homine, quam de Lapide possumus affirmare, eum visu orbatum esse, quia illo tempore non magis sine contradictione illi homini visus, quam lapidi competit; quoniam ad hominem illum nil amplius pertinet, et suum est, quam id, quod divinus intellectus et voluntas ei tribuit. Et propterea Deus non magis est causa τοῦ illius non videre, quam τοῦ non videre lapidis: id quod mera est negatio.", "en": "But when God’s decree and its nature are considered, we can no more affirm of that man than of a stone that he is bereft of sight, because at that time sight belongs to that man no more without contradiction than to the stone; since nothing more belongs to that man, and is his, than what the divine intellect and will attribute to him. And therefore God is no more the cause of that man’s not seeing than of the stone’s not seeing: which is mere negation."}, {"id": "Ep21.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Ita etiam, cum ad naturam hominis, qui appetitu libidinis ducitur, attendimus, praesentemque appetitum cum eo, qui in probis est, vel cum eo, quem ipse alias habuit, comparamus; affirmamus, eum hominem meliore appetitu privatum esse, quia tunc isti virtutis appetitum competere iudicamus; quod facere non possumus, si ad naturam Divini decreti, et intellectus attendimus: nam eo respectu melior ille appetitus non magis ad naturam istius hominis eo tempore, quam ad naturam Diaboli, vel lapidis pertinet, et idcirco eo respectu melior appetitus non est privatio, sed negatio. Adeo ut privatio nihil aliud sit, quam aliquid de re negare, quod iudicamus ad suam naturam pertinere, et negatio nil aliud, quam aliquid de re negare, quia ad suam naturam non pertinet.", "en": "So also, when we attend to the nature of a man who is led by the appetite of lust, and compare his present appetite with that which is in the good, or with the one he himself had at another time, we affirm that this man is deprived of a better appetite, because we then judge that the appetite of virtue belongs to him; which we cannot do if we attend to the nature of the divine decree and intellect: for in that respect that better appetite no more belongs to the nature of this man at that time than to the nature of the Devil or of a stone; and therefore in that respect the better appetite is not a privation but a negation. So that privation is nothing other than to deny of a thing something which we judge to belong to its nature, and negation nothing other than to deny of a thing something because it does not belong to its nature."}, {"id": "Ep21.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Et hinc liquet, quare Adami appetitus rerum terrenarum nostri, non vero intellectus Dei respectu tantum malus esset. Licet enim Deus praeteritum et praesentem Adami statum sciret, non idcirco Adamum praeterito statu privatum esse intelligebat, hoc est, praeteritum ad suam naturam pertinere: Nam tunc Deus aliquid contra suam voluntatem, hoc est, contra proprium suum intellectum intelligeret.", "en": "And from this it is clear why Adam’s appetite for earthly things was evil only in relation to our intellect, and not to God’s. For although God knew Adam’s past and present state, he did not on that account understand Adam to be deprived of his past state, that is, the past state to belong to his nature: for then God would understand something against his will, that is, against his own intellect."}, {"id": "Ep21.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Hoc si bene percepisses, simulque me illam libertatem, quam Cartesius menti adscribit, non concedere, quemadmodum L. M. in praefatione meo nomine testatus est, vel minimam in meis verbis non invenires contradictionem.", "en": "If you had perceived this well, and at the same time that I do not grant the freedom which Descartes ascribes to the mind, as L. M. testified in my name in the preface, you would not find the least contradiction in my words."}, {"id": "Ep21.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Sed video, me melius multo facturum fuisse, si in prima mea epistola Cartesii verbis respondissem, dicendo, nos non posse scire, quomodo nostra libertas, et quicquid ab ea dependet cum Dei providentia et libertate congruat, (sicuti in appendice diversis locis feci) adeo ut ex Dei creatione nullam in libertate nostra queamus invenire contradictionem, quia capere haud possumus, quomodo Deus res creavit, et (quod idem est) quomodo eas conservat. Verum putabam, te praefationem legisse, meque, si ex animi sententia non responderem, in amicitiae officium, quam animitus offerebam, peccaturum fuisse. Sed haec susque deque fero.", "en": "But I see that I should have done much better if in my first letter I had answered in Descartes’s words, saying that we cannot know how our freedom, and whatever depends on it, agrees with God’s providence and freedom (as I did in several places in the appendix), so that we can find no contradiction to our freedom in God’s creation, because we cannot grasp how God created things and (what is the same) how he preserves them. But I thought that you had read the preface, and that if I did not answer according to my real view I should sin against the duty of the friendship which I was heartily offering. But I care little for all this."}, {"id": "Ep21.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Quia tamen video, te Cartesii mentem hactenus non bene percepisse, peto, ut haec duo attendas: Primo neque me, neque Cartesium unquam dixisse, quod ad nostram naturam pertinet, ut nostram voluntatem intra limites intellectus contineamus; sed tantum, quod Deus nobis intellectum determinatum, et voluntatem indeterminatam dedit, ita tamen, ut ignoremus, in quem finem nos creavit; porro, quod istiusmodi indeterminata, vel perfecta voluntas nos non solum perfectiores reddit; sed quoque, ut tibi in sequentibus dicam, quod ea nobis sit valde necessaria.", "en": "Since, however, I see that you have not so far perceived Descartes’s mind well, I ask you to attend to these two points. First, that neither I nor Descartes ever said that it belongs to our nature to contain our will within the limits of the intellect; but only that God has given us a determinate intellect and an indeterminate will, in such a way, however, that we do not know to what end he created us; further, that such an indeterminate or perfect will not only makes us more perfect, but also, as I shall tell you below, is very necessary to us."}, {"id": "Ep21.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Secundo quod nostra libertas nec in quadam contingentia, nec in quadam indifferentia sita est; sed in modo affirmandi, et negandi; adeo ut, quo rem aliquam minus indifferenter affirmamus, aut negamus, eo liberiores simus: Verbi causa, si Dei natura nobis est cognita, tam necessario ex natura nostra τὸ affirmare sequitur, Deum existere, quam ex natura trianguli eius tres angulos duobus rectis aequari fluit; et tamen nunquam magis liberi sumus, quam quum rem tali modo affirmamus.", "en": "Second, that our freedom lies neither in a certain contingency nor in a certain indifference, but in the manner of affirming and denying; so that the less indifferently we affirm or deny something, the freer we are: for example, if the nature of God is known to us, the affirming that God exists follows from our nature as necessarily as it flows from the nature of a triangle that its three angles are equal to two right angles; and yet we are never more free than when we affirm a thing in this way."}, {"id": "Ep21.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Quia vero haec necessitas nihil aliud est, quam Dei decretum, veluti in mea appendice clare ostendi; hinc quodammodo intelligere licet, quo pacto rem libere agamus, eiusque causa simus, non obstante, quod eam necessario, et ex Dei decreto agamus.", "en": "But because this necessity is nothing other than God’s decree, as I have shown clearly in my appendix, it can in some way be understood from this how we act freely and are the cause of the action, notwithstanding that we do it necessarily and from God’s decree."}, {"id": "Ep21.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Hoc, inquam, aliquo modo possumus intelligere, cum quid affirmamus, quod clare, et distincte percipimus, ubi vero quicquam, quod clare ac distincte non capimus, asserimus, hoc est, cum patimur, ut voluntas extra nostri intellectus limites exspatietur, tum illam necessitatem, Deique decreta non ita percipere possumus; sed nostram quidem libertatem, quam nostra voluntas semper includit, (quo respectu opera nostra solummodo bona, vel mala appellantur) et si tunc conamur libertatem nostram cum Dei decreto, et continua creatione conciliare, confundimus id, quod clare, et distincte intelligimus, cum eo, quod non percipimus, et propterea frustra id conamur.", "en": "This, I say, we can understand in some way when we affirm something which we perceive clearly and distinctly; but when we assert anything which we do not grasp clearly and distinctly, that is, when we allow the will to stray beyond the limits of our intellect, then we cannot perceive that necessity and God’s decrees in the same way, but we do indeed perceive our freedom, which our will always includes (in which respect alone our works are called good or evil); and if we then try to reconcile our freedom with God’s decree and continual creation, we confuse what we understand clearly and distinctly with what we do not perceive, and therefore we try in vain."}, {"id": "Ep21.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Sufficit ergo nobis, quod scimus nos liberos esse, et nos tales esse posse non obstante Dei decreto, nosque mali causam esse, eo quod nullus actus, nisi respectu nostrae libertatis, tantum malus nominari potest. Haec sunt, quae Cartesium spectant, ut demonstrarem eius dicta ab ea parte nullam pati contradictionem.", "en": "It is enough for us, then, that we know that we are free, and that we can be so notwithstanding God’s decree, and that we are the cause of evil, because no act can be called evil except in relation to our freedom alone. These are the things that concern Descartes, to show that his words suffer no contradiction on that side."}, {"id": "Ep21.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Iam ad ea, quae me attinent, me convertam, ac primo breviter referam utilitatem, quae ex mea opinione nascitur, quae praecipue in eo est sita, quod scilicet noster intellectus Deo mentem, et corpus extra omnem superstitionem offert, nec preces nobis valde utiles esse nego: nam meus intellectus est nimis parvus ad omnia media determinandum, quae Deus habet, quibus homines ad sui amorem, hoc est, ad salutem perducat; adeo ut tantum absit hanc opinionem noxiam futuram esse, ut econtra iis, qui nullis praeiudiciis nec puerili superstitione praeoccupati sunt, unicum sit medium ad summum beatitudinis gradum perveniendi.", "en": "Now I shall turn to what concerns me, and first I shall briefly report the usefulness which arises from my opinion, which lies chiefly in this, that our intellect offers mind and body to God free of all superstition; nor do I deny that prayers are very useful to us: for my intellect is too small to determine all the means God has by which to bring men to love of him, that is, to salvation; so that this opinion is so far from being harmful that, on the contrary, for those who are not taken up with prejudices and childish superstition, it is the only means of reaching the highest degree of blessedness."}, {"id": "Ep21.7", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Quod vero ais, me homines, eos a Deo tam dependentes faciendo, ideo elementis, herbis, et lapidibus similes reddere, id sufficienter ostendit te meam opinionem perversissime intelligere, et res, quae intellectum spectant, cum imaginatione confundere.", "en": "But when you say that by making men so dependent on God I make them like the elements, plants and stones, this shows sufficiently that you understand my opinion most perversely, and confuse the things that concern the intellect with the imagination."}, {"id": "Ep21.7", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Si enim puro intellectu percepisses, quid sit a Deo dependere, certe non cogitares, res, quatenus a Deo dependent, mortuas, corporeas, et imperfectas esse, (quis unquam de ente summe perfecto tam viliter ausus fuit loqui) econtra caperes, ea de causa, et quatenus a Deo dependent, perfectas esse. Adeo ut hanc dependentiam, atque necessariam operationem quam optime per Dei decretum intelligamus, quando non ad truncos, et herbas; sed ad maxime intelligibiles, et res creatas perfectissimas attendimus, ut ex illo, quod secundo de Cartesii mente iam ante memoravimus, clare apparet, quod attendere debuisses.", "en": "For if you had perceived with pure intellect what it is to depend on God, you would certainly not think that things, insofar as they depend on God, are dead, corporeal and imperfect (who ever dared to speak so meanly of the supremely perfect being?); on the contrary, you would grasp that they are perfect for that reason, and insofar as they depend on God. So that we understand this dependence and necessary operation best through God’s decree when we attend not to tree-trunks and plants but to the most intelligible and most perfect created things, as is clear from what we mentioned second above concerning Descartes’s mind, to which you should have attended."}, {"id": "Ep21.8", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Nec reticere possum, me id summopere mirari, quod dicis: si Deus delictum non puniret, (hoc est, tanquam iudex tali poena, quam ipsum delictum non inferret: Id enim solum nostra est quaestio.) quaenam ratio impedit, quo minus quaevis scelera avide perpetrem?", "en": "Nor can I keep silent that I am greatly surprised at what you say: if God did not punish wrongdoing (that is, as a judge, with such a punishment as the wrongdoing itself does not bring with it: for that alone is our question), what reason prevents me from eagerly committing any crimes?"}, {"id": "Ep21.8", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Certe qui illud tantum (quod de te non spero) formidine poenae omittit, is nulla ratione ex amore operatur, et quam minime virtutem amplectitur. Quantum ad me, ea omitto, vel omittere studeo, quia expresse cum mea singulari natura pugnant, meque a Dei amore, et cognitione aberrare facerent.", "en": "Certainly he who refrains from them only for fear of punishment (which I do not hope of you) in no way acts from love, and embraces virtue as little as possible. As for me, I refrain from them, or try to refrain, because they are expressly contrary to my singular nature, and would make me stray from the love and knowledge of God."}, {"id": "Ep21.9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Porro si ad naturam humanam paulum attendisses, et naturam Dei decreti, prout in appendice explicui, percepisses, tandemque scivisses, quomodo res esset deducenda, antequam ad conclusionem perveniatur, non adeo temere dixisses, hanc opinionem nos truncis, etc. similes reddere; nec tam multas absurditates, quam imaginaris, mihi affinxisses.", "en": "Further, if you had attended a little to human nature, and had perceived the nature of God’s decree as I explained it in the appendix, and had known, finally, how a thing must be deduced before one comes to the conclusion, you would not have said so rashly that this opinion makes us like tree-trunks, etc.; nor would you have imputed to me so many absurdities as you imagine."}, {"id": "Ep21.10", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "De duabus illis rebus, quas ais, priusquam ad secundam tuam regulam pergis, te percipere haud valere, respondeo primo Cartesium sufficere ad conclusionem tuam faciendam, quod nempe, si modo attendis ad naturam tuam, experiris, te posse iudicium tuum suspendere; si vero dicas, te in te ipso non experiri, nos tantum virium hodie in rationem obtinere, ut hoc semper continuare possemus, id idem Cartesio esset, ac nos hodie non posse videre, nos, quamdiu existimus, futuros semper res cogitantes, vel naturam rei cogitantis retenturos, quod profecto contradictionem involvit.", "en": "Of those two things which you say you cannot perceive before you go on to your second rule, I answer, first, that Descartes suffices for drawing your conclusion: namely, that if only you attend to your nature, you find by experience that you can suspend your judgment; but if you say that you do not find in yourself that we today have so much power over reason that we could always continue this, that would be the same for Descartes as that we cannot see today that we shall always, as long as we exist, be thinking things, or retain the nature of a thinking thing; which certainly involves a contradiction."}, {"id": "Ep21.10", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Ad secundum cum Cartesio dico, si voluntatem nostram extra limites intellectus nostri valde limitati extendere non possemus, nos miserrimos futuros, neque in nostra potestate fore vel frustum panis comedere, vel passum progredi, vel subsistere: omnia enim incerta, atque periculis sunt plena.", "en": "To the second I say with Descartes that if we could not extend our will beyond the limits of our very limited intellect, we should be most miserable, and it would not be in our power either to eat a piece of bread, or to take a step forward, or to stand still: for all things are uncertain and full of dangers."}, {"id": "Ep21.11", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Transeo nunc ad secundam tuam regulam, asseroque, me quidem credere, quod ego eam veritatem, quam tu in scriptura esse credis, illi non tribuam, et tamen credo, quod ego illi tantum, si non plus adscribam authoritatis; quodque longe cautius, quam alii, caveam, ne illi pueriles quasdam, et absurdas sententias affingam, quod nemo praestare potest, nisi is, qui philosophiam bene intelligit, vel Divinas habet revelationes; adeo ut me valde parum moveant explicationes, quas vulgares theologi de scriptura adferunt, praesertim si istius sint farinae, ut Scripturam semper iuxta litteram, sensumque externum sumant; et nunquam, praeter Socinianos, crassum adeo vidi theologum, qui non percipit Sacram Scripturam creberrime more humano de Deo loqui, ac suum sensum Parabolis exprimere; et quod ad contradictionem, quam frustra (mea quidem opinione) conaris ostendere, credo te per Parabolam omnino aliud quid, quam vulgo fit, intelligere: nam quis unquam audivit, eum, qui suos conceptus parabolis exprimit, a suo sensu aberrare.", "en": "I pass now to your second rule, and assert that I do indeed believe that I do not attribute to Scripture the truth which you believe is in it, and yet I believe that I ascribe to it as much authority, if not more; and that I take far more care than others not to impute to it certain childish and absurd opinions, which no one can achieve unless he understands philosophy well or has divine revelations; so that I am very little moved by the explanations which the common theologians bring to Scripture, especially if they are of the sort that always take Scripture according to the letter and the outward sense; and, apart from the Socinians, I have never seen a theologian so dull as not to perceive that Holy Scripture very often speaks of God in human fashion and expresses its sense in parables; and as for the contradiction which you try in vain (in my opinion at least) to show, I believe that by a parable you understand something altogether different from what is commonly understood: for who ever heard that he who expresses his concepts in parables strays from his own sense."}, {"id": "Ep21.11", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Quum Micha Regi Aghabo diceret, se Deum in solio suo sedentem vidisse, ac exercitus caelestes a dextra, et sinistra stantes, Deumque ex illis quaerere, quisnam Aghabum deciperet, illud ipsum certe erat Parabola, qua propheta praecipuum, quod ea occasione (quae non erat, ut sublimia theologiae dogmata doceret) Dei nomine debebat manifestare, sufficienter exprimebat; adeo ut nullo modo a sensu suo aberraret.", "en": "When Micaiah told King Ahab that he had seen God sitting on his throne, and the heavenly hosts standing on the right and on the left, and that God asked them which of them would deceive Ahab, that was certainly a parable, by which the prophet sufficiently expressed the chief thing which he had to make known in God’s name on that occasion (which was not one for teaching the sublime dogmas of theology); so that he in no way strayed from his own sense."}, {"id": "Ep21.11", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Sic etiam caeteri prophetae Dei verbum iussu Dei ea ratione populo manifestarunt, tanquam optimo medio, non vero tanquam illo, quod Deus petebat, populum ad scopum Scripturae primarium ducendi, qui iuxta ipsius Christi dictum in hoc consistit, nimirum in amore Dei supra omnia, et proximi tanquam sui ipsius. Sublimes speculationes, credo, Scripturam minime tangunt. Me quod spectat, nulla Dei aeterna attributa ex sacra scriptura didici, nec discere potui.", "en": "So too the other prophets made God’s word known to the people at God’s command in that way, as the best means, but not as the means which God required, of leading the people to the primary aim of Scripture, which according to the saying of Christ himself consists in this: namely, in the love of God above all things, and of one’s neighbour as oneself. Sublime speculations, I believe, do not touch Scripture at all. As for me, I have not learned, nor could I learn, any eternal attributes of God from Holy Scripture."}, {"id": "Ep21.12", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Quantum vero quintum attinet argumentum (scilicet prophetas Dei verbum tali ratione manifestum fecisse, quoniam veritas veritati non est contraria) nihil reliqui est, quam ut (quemadmodum quivis, qui demonstrandi methodum percipit, iudicabit) demonstrem, Scripturam, prout est, esse Dei verum verbum revelatum. Cuius mathematicam demonstrationem, nisi divina Revelatione, habere nequeo.", "en": "As for the fifth argument (namely, that the prophets made God’s word known in such a way, since truth is not contrary to truth), nothing remains for me (as anyone who perceives the method of demonstrating will judge) but to demonstrate that Scripture, as it is, is the true revealed word of God. Of this I can have no mathematical demonstration except by divine revelation."}, {"id": "Ep21.12", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Et ideo dixi, credo, sed non more mathematico scio, omnia, quae Deus prophetis etc. quia firmiter credo, non vero mathematice scio, prophetas Dei intimos Consiliarios, et Legatos fuisse fidos; adeo ut in iis, quae ego affirmavi, nulla omnino sit contradictio; cum e contrario ab altera parte haud paucae inveniantur.", "en": "And therefore I said, ‘I believe’, but not ‘I know in the mathematical manner’, ‘that all the things which God [revealed] to the prophets’, etc.; because I firmly believe, but do not know mathematically, that the prophets were God’s intimate counsellors and faithful ambassadors; so that in what I have affirmed there is no contradiction at all, whereas on the other side not a few are found."}, {"id": "Ep21.13", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Reliquum tuae epistolae, nempe ubi dicis: Denique ens summe perfectum noverat, etc. et deinde quod contra exemplum de veneno adfers, ac ultimo, quod appendicem, et id, quod sequitur, spectat, dico hanc praesentem quaestionem non respicere.", "en": "The rest of your letter, namely where you say, ‘Finally, the supremely perfect Being knew’, etc., and then what you bring against the example of poison, and lastly what concerns the appendix and what follows, does not, I say, bear on the present question."}, {"id": "Ep21.13", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "Praefationem L. M. quod attinet, in ipsa certe simul ostenditur, quid Cartesio adhuc probandum esset, ut solidam de libero arbitrio demonstrationem formaret, additurque, me contrariam fovere sententiam, et quomodo eam foveam: quam forte suo tempore indicabo, nunc vero animus non est.", "en": "As for L. M.’s preface, it certainly shows at the same time what Descartes still had to prove in order to form a solid demonstration of free will, and adds that I hold the contrary opinion, and how I hold it: which I shall perhaps make known in due time, but now I have no mind to."}, {"id": "Ep21.14", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 21", "la": "De opere vero super Cartesium nec cogitavi, nec ulteriorem eius gessi curam, postquam sermone Belgico prodiit: et quidem non sine ratione, quam hic recensere longum foret. Adeo ut nihil dicendum restet, quam me etc.", "en": "As for the work on Descartes, I have neither given it a thought nor taken any further care of it since it came out in Dutch: and indeed not without reason, which it would take long to recount here. So that nothing remains to be said but that I am, etc."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Epistolam tuam, 28 Ianuarii scriptam, suo tempore accepi; aliae occupationes praeter studia impediverunt, quo minus ad tuam citius responderim: et quoniam hic illic acribus tua referta est reprehensionibus, quid de ea iudicarem, vix sciebam: nam in prima, 5 Ianuarii exarata, tam liberaliter amicitiam tuam ex animo mihi obtuleras, hac addita obtestatione, meam non solum illo tempore scriptam; sed et subsequentes valde gratas fore; quin amice flagitabas, ut libere, si quas insuper movere possem difficultates, obiicerem, uti in mea, 16 Ianuarii data, prolixius paulo praestiti.", "en": "Your letter written on 28 January I received in due time; occupations other than my studies prevented me from answering it sooner; and since it is filled here and there with sharp reproofs, I hardly knew what to think of it: for in the first, written on 5 January, you had so generously offered me your friendship from the heart, adding the assurance that not only my letter written at that time but also later ones would be very welcome; indeed you asked me in friendly fashion to put forward freely any further difficulties I could raise, as I did at somewhat greater length in mine of 16 January."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Ad eam amicam, et erudientem, iuxta postulatum, et promissum tuum, exspectabam responsionem; sed econtra talem, quae nimiam non redolet amicitiam, accepi; nempe quod nullae demonstrationes, licet validissimae apud me valent, quod mentem Cartesii non percipio, quod res spirituales nimium cum terrenis confundo, etc. ita ut invicem epistolis diutius erudire non possemus.", "en": "To that I expected a friendly and instructive reply, in accordance with your request and promise; but instead I received one which does not smell of much friendship: namely, that no demonstrations, however strong, have weight with me, that I do not perceive Descartes’s mind, that I confuse spiritual things too much with earthly ones, etc., so that we could no longer instruct each other by letters."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Ad quae amice respondeo, me firmiter credere, te supra dicta melius me intelligere, et assuetum magis esse res corporeas a spiritualibus discernere: in metaphysica enim, quam ego inchoo, summum adscendisti gradum, et ideo, ut erudirer, favorem tuum captabam; nunquam vero existimabam, me obiectionibus, libere factis, offensionis causam praebiturum.", "en": "To which I reply in friendly fashion that I firmly believe that you understand the things mentioned above better than I, and are more accustomed to distinguish corporeal things from spiritual: for in metaphysics, which I am only beginning, you have climbed to the highest degree, and that is why I sought your favour, to be instructed; but I never thought that by objections freely made I would give cause for offence."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Summas tibi ex animo ago gratias, quod tantum laborem in duabus epistolis exarandis, praecipue in secunda, suscepisti; ex posteriore, quam ex priore, tuam mentem clarius percepi, et nihilominus ei assensum praebere nequeo, nisi difficultates, quas adhuc in ea invenio, tollantur: nec hoc offensionis causam dare potest. Nam illud vitium in intellectu nostro arguit, si veritati sine fundamento necessario assentimur: Licet tui veri essent conceptus, iis assensum praebere non licet, quamdiu in me quaedam obscuritatis, vel dubitationis rationes supersunt, quamvis dubitationes non ex re proposita, sed ex mei intellectus imperfectione orirentur.", "en": "I thank you most heartily for undertaking so much labour in writing two letters, especially the second; from the later I perceived your mind more clearly than from the earlier, and nevertheless I cannot give it my assent unless the difficulties which I still find in it are removed: nor can this give cause for offence. For it argues a fault in our intellect if we assent to the truth without a necessary foundation: even if your concepts were true, I may not give them assent so long as some reasons for obscurity or doubt remain in me, even if the doubts arose not from the matter proposed but from the imperfection of my intellect."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Et quia hoc abunde tibi notum est, aegre ferre non debes, si rursus quasdam formem obiectiones: cogor ita facere, quamdiu rem clare percipere nequeo; nam hoc in alium non fit finem, quam ad veritatem inveniendam; non vero ad tuam mentem praeter intentionem tuam detorquendam; et propterea ad haec pauca amicam peto responsionem.", "en": "And since this is abundantly known to you, you ought not to take it ill if I again form some objections: I am compelled to do so as long as I cannot perceive the matter clearly; for this is done to no other end than to find the truth, and not to twist your mind against your intention; and therefore I ask a friendly reply to these few points."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Dicis, nihil ad essentiam rei amplius pertinere, quam id, quod divina voluntas, potentiaque ei concedit, et revera tribuit; et cum ad naturam hominis, qui appetitu libidinis ducitur, attendimus, praesentemque appetitum cum eo, qui in probis est, vel cum eo, quem ipse alias habuit, comparamus, affirmamus eum hominem meliore appetitu privatum esse, quia tunc isti virtutis appetitum competere iudicamus; quod facere non possumus, si ad naturam divini decreti et intellectus attendimus: nam eo respectu melior ille appetitus non magis ad naturam istius hominis eo tempore, quam ad naturam Diaboli, aut lapidis pertinet, etc. Licet enim Deus praeteritum, et praesentem Adami statum sciret, non idcirco Adamum praeterito statu privatum esse intelligebat, hoc est, praeteritum ad suam praesentem naturam pertinere, etc.", "en": "You say that nothing more belongs to the essence of a thing than what the divine will and power grants and really attributes to it; and ‘when we attend to the nature of a man who is led by the appetite of lust, and compare his present appetite with that which is in the good, or with the one he himself had at another time, we affirm that this man is deprived of a better appetite, because we then judge that the appetite of virtue belongs to him; which we cannot do if we attend to the nature of the divine decree and intellect: for in that respect that better appetite no more belongs to the nature of this man at that time than to the nature of the Devil or of a stone’, etc. ‘For although God knew Adam’s past and present state, he did not on that account understand Adam to be deprived of his past state, that is, the past state to belong to his present nature’, etc."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Ex quibus verbis clare sequi videtur, iuxta tuam sententiam, nihil aliud ad essentiam pertinere, quam quod eo momento, quo percipitur, res habet, hoc est, si me voluptatis desiderium tenet, illud desiderium ad meam illo tempore pertinet essentiam, et si me non tenet, illud non-desiderium ad meam eo, quo non desidero, tempore essentiam pertinet, unde infallibiliter consequitur, me tum respectu Dei aeque perfectionem in meis operibus includere, (solummodo gradu differentibus) quando voluptatum cupiditate, quam quando ea non teneor; quando omnis generis scelera perpetro, quam quando virtutem, et iustitiam exerceo.", "en": "From these words it seems clearly to follow, according to your opinion, that nothing else belongs to the essence than what the thing has at the moment at which it is perceived; that is, if the longing for pleasure holds me, that longing belongs to my essence at that time, and if it does not hold me, that non-longing belongs to my essence at the time when I do not long for it; from which it follows infallibly that, in relation to God, I include perfection in my works equally (differing only in degree) when I am held by the desire for pleasures as when I am not; when I commit crimes of every kind as when I practise virtue and justice."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Ad meam enim essentiam eo tempore tantum duntaxat, quantum operor, pertinet: nec enim plus, nec minus, secundum tuam positionem, operari possum, quam quantum revera accepi perfectionis, quia voluptatum, scelerumque cupiditas ad meam eo tempore, quo facio, pertinet essentiam, et eo tempore illam, non vero maiorem, a divina potentia accipio: Exigit ergo divina potentia eiusmodi duntaxat opera. Et ita ex tua positione clare videtur sequi, Deum scelera una, eademque ratione velle, qua ea vult, quae tu nomine virtutis insignis.", "en": "For only so much belongs to my essence at that time as I do: for according to your position I can do neither more nor less than the perfection I have really received, since the desire for pleasures and crimes belongs to my essence at the time when I act, and at that time I receive that essence, and not a greater, from the divine power: the divine power, then, requires only works of that kind. And so from your position it seems clearly to follow that God wills crimes in one and the same way as he wills the things which you mark with the name of virtue."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Ponamus iam, quod Deus, ut Deus, non vero ut iudex, piis, et impiis talem, et tantum essentiae largitur, quantum vult, ut illi efficiant; quaenam rationes dantur, ut unius opus non eodem modo, quam alterius velit? Nam quoniam unicuique ad opus suum qualitatem concedit, sequitur utique, quod, quibus minus largitus est, eadem ratione tantum ab ipsis petit, quantum ab illis, quibus plus dedit; et per consequens Deus sui ipsius respectu maiorem, minoremve nostrorum operum perfectionem, voluptatum et virtutum cupiditatem eodem modo exigit.", "en": "Let us now suppose that God, as God and not as judge, bestows on the pious and the impious such and so much essence as he wills them to bring about; what reasons are there why he should not will the work of the one in the same way as that of the other? For since he grants each one the quality for his work, it surely follows that from those to whom he has given less he asks, in the same proportion, as much as from those to whom he has given more; and consequently God, in relation to himself, requires the greater or lesser perfection of our works, the desire for pleasures and for virtues, in the same way."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Adeo ut, qui scelera patrat, necessario eadem patrare debeat, quia nihil aliud ad suam eo tempore essentiam spectat; uti is, qui virtutem exercet, ideo virtutem exercet, quia Dei potentia voluit, ut ad suam eo tempore essentiam id pertineret: Rursum mihi videtur Deum aeque, et eodem modo scelera, quam virtutem velle, quatenus autem utraque vult, tam huius, quam illius est causa, ipsique eatenus grata esse debent. Quod de Deo concipere durum est.", "en": "So that he who commits crimes must necessarily commit them, because nothing else belongs to his essence at that time; just as he who practises virtue practises virtue because God’s power willed that it should belong to his essence at that time: again it seems to me that God wills crimes equally and in the same way as virtue, and insofar as he wills both, he is the cause of the one as much as of the other, and to that extent they must both be pleasing to him. Which is hard to conceive of God."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Dicis, ut video, probos Deum colere; verum ex tuis scriptis nihil aliud percipio, quam quod Deo servire tantum sit talia efficere opera, quae Deus, ut operaremur, voluit; idem adscribis impiis, et libidinosis; quaenam ergo est differentia, respectu Dei, inter proborum, et improborum cultum?", "en": "You say, as I see, that the good worship God; but from your writings I perceive nothing else than that to serve God is only to bring about such works as God willed us to bring about; you ascribe the same to the impious and lustful; what difference is there, then, in relation to God, between the worship of the good and of the wicked?"}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Ais etiam, probos Deo servire, et serviendo continuo perfectiores evadere; sed non percipio, quid per τὸ perfectiores evadere intelligas, nec quid significet τὸ continuo perfectiores evadere. Nam et impii, et pii suam essentiam, et conservationem, aut continuam creationem a Deo, ut Deo, non vero, ut iudice, nanciscuntur, voluntatemque eius iuxta Dei decretum utrique eodem modo exequuntur. Quaenam ergo differentia, respectu Dei, inter utrosque potest esse?", "en": "You also say that the good serve God and by serving become continually more perfect; but I do not perceive what you understand by ‘becoming more perfect’, nor what ‘becoming continually more perfect’ means. For both the impious and the pious obtain their essence, and their preservation or continual creation, from God as God, not as judge, and both carry out his will in the same way according to God’s decree. What difference, then, can there be between the two in relation to God?"}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Nam τὸ perfectiores continuo evadere non ex opere; sed ex Dei voluntate fluit; adeo ut, si impii per opera sua imperfectiores fiunt, illud non fluat ex operibus; sed ex sola Dei voluntate; et utrique voluntatem Dei tantum exequuntur: nequit ergo in his duobus, ratione Dei, dari discrimen. Quaenam sunt ergo rationes, ut hi per opus suum continuo perfectiores, illi vero deteriores evadant?", "en": "For becoming continually more perfect flows not from the work but from God’s will; so that if the impious become more imperfect through their works, this flows not from the works but from God’s will alone; and both only carry out God’s will: there can be no distinction between these two, then, in respect of God. What, then, are the reasons why these become continually more perfect through their work, and those worse?"}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Sed vero discrimen operis horum supra alterorum opus in hoc ponere videris, quod hoc opus plus perfectionis, quam illud includit. In his tuum, vel meum certo confido latere errorem: nam nullam in tuis scriptis reperire licet regulam, secundum quam res magis, minusve perfecta dicitur, quam quando plus, minusve habet essentiae.", "en": "But you seem to place the distinction between the work of the one and the work of the others in this, that this work includes more perfection than that. In this, I am confident, your error or mine certainly lies: for in your writings no rule can be found according to which a thing is called more or less perfect, except when it has more or less essence."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Si iam haec sit perfectionis regula, sunt igitur scelera, Dei respectu, ei aeque, ac opera proborum grata: nam Deus ea, ut Deus, hoc est, sui respectu, eodem modo vult, quia utraque ex Dei fluunt decreto. Si haec sit sola perfectionis regula, errores non nisi improprie tales dici possunt; sed revera nulli errores, nulla dantur scelera: et quicquid est, illam tantummodo, et talem, quam Deus dedit, essentiam complectitur, quae semper, utcunque ea se habeat, perfectionem involvit.", "en": "If this, then, is the rule of perfection, crimes are as pleasing to God, in relation to him, as the works of the good: for God, as God, that is, in relation to himself, wills them in the same way, because both flow from God’s decree. If this is the only rule of perfection, errors can be called errors only improperly; but in truth there are no errors, there are no crimes: and whatever is includes only that essence, and such as God gave it, which always, however it stands, involves perfection."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Me hoc clare non posse percipere, fateor, et tu quaerenti, an occidere Deo aeque placeat, ac eleemosynas largiri? an furtum committere, Dei respectu, aeque bonum sit, ac iustum esse? condona. Si neges, quaenam sunt rationes? si affirmes, quae apud me existunt rationes, quibus moverer, ut hoc opus, quod tu virtutem appellas, magis, quam aliud operarer? quaenam lex magis hoc, quam illud, prohibet?", "en": "I confess that I cannot perceive this clearly; and you must pardon me for asking whether killing is as pleasing to God as giving alms? Whether to commit theft is, in relation to God, as good as to be just? If you deny it, what are the reasons? If you affirm it, what reasons do I have by which I should be moved to do this work which you call virtue rather than another? What law forbids this rather than that?"}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Si ipsam nomines virtutis legem, fatendum mihi certe est, me nullam apud te reperire, iuxta quam virtus moderanda, et ex qua cognoscenda sit. Quicquid enim est, a Dei voluntate inseparabiliter dependet, et per consequens et hoc, et illud ex aequo virtuosum est. Et, quid tibi virtus, vel lex virtutis sit, non capio; ideo etiam, quod asseris, nos ex amore virtutis debere operari, non intelligo.", "en": "If you name the law of virtue itself, I must certainly confess that I find none in you according to which virtue is to be regulated and from which it is to be known. For whatever is depends inseparably on God’s will, and consequently this and that are equally virtuous. And I do not grasp what virtue, or the law of virtue, is for you; and so I also do not understand what you assert, that we ought to act from love of virtue."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Dicis quidem, te scelera, et vitia omittere, quia ea cum singulari tua natura pugnant, et te ab divina cognitione, et amore seducunt; verum nullam horum in omnibus tuis scriptis invenio vel regulam, vel probationem. Quin excusa me, si contrarium ex illis sequi dicam.", "en": "You do say that you refrain from crimes and vices because they conflict with your singular nature and lead you away from the divine knowledge and love; but in all your writings I find neither rule nor proof of these things. Indeed, forgive me if I say that the contrary follows from them."}, {"id": "Ep22", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Omittis ea, quae vitia appello, quia cum singulari tua natura pugnant, non autem quia vitia complectuntur; omittis ea, quemadmodum cibus, a quo natura nostra abhorret, relinquitur. Certe qui mala omittet, eo quod sua natura ab eis abhorret, parum de virtute gloriari poterit.", "en": "You refrain from what I call vices because they conflict with your singular nature, not because they contain vices; you refrain from them as one leaves alone food which our nature abhors. Certainly he who refrains from evils because his nature abhors them can boast little of virtue."}, {"id": "Ep22.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Hic iam rursum quaestio moveri potest, an, si daretur animus, cum cuius singulari natura non pugnaret; sed conveniret voluptates, sceleraque patrare, an, inquam, ratio virtutis sit, quae ipsum ad virtutem praestandam, malumque omittendum moveret? Sed qui fieri potest, ut quis cupiditatem voluptatis amitteret, quum eius cupiditas eo tempore de sua sit essentia, ipsamque iam modo acceperit, neque dimittere possit?", "en": "Here again the question can be raised whether, if there were a mind with whose singular nature it did not conflict, but agreed, to pursue pleasures and commit crimes, whether, I say, there is a reason of virtue which would move it to practise virtue and refrain from evil? But how can it happen that someone should lose the desire for pleasure, when that desire belongs at that time to his essence, and he has only just received it, and cannot let it go?"}, {"id": "Ep22.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Hanc etiam consequentiam in tuis scriptis non video, quod nimirum eae actiones, quas scelerum nomine insignio, te a Dei cognitione, et eius amore seducerent. Nam voluntatem Dei tantum exequutus es, neque amplius praestare poteras, quia ad tuam isto tempore essentiam constituendam a divina voluntate, ac potentia nihil amplius datum erat.", "en": "Nor do I see in your writings this consequence, namely that those actions which I mark with the name of crimes would lead you away from the knowledge of God and from his love. For you have only carried out God’s will, and could do no more, because nothing more had been given by the divine will and power to constitute your essence at that time."}, {"id": "Ep22.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Quomodo sic determinatum, ac dependens opus te a divino amore aberrare facit? aberrare est confusum, et independens esse, et hoc secundum te est impossibile. Nam sive hoc, sive illud, sive plus, sive minus perfectionis edimus, illud ad esse nostrum pro illo tempore immediate a Deo accipimus: quomodo ergo possumus aberrare? vel ego, per errorem quid intelligatur, non capio. Attamen in hoc, in hoc, inquam, solo causa vel mei, vel tui erroris latere debet.", "en": "How does a work so determined and dependent make you stray from the divine love? To stray is to be confused and independent, and this according to you is impossible. For whether we bring forth this or that, more or less perfection, we receive it for our being at that time immediately from God: how, then, can we stray? Or else I do not grasp what is understood by error. And yet in this, in this alone, I say, the cause of either my error or yours must lie."}, {"id": "Ep22.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Hic multa alia dicerem, et quaererem, primo, an substantiae intellectuales alio modo, quam vitae expertes a Deo dependeant? licet enim entia intellectualia plus essentiae, quam vita carentia involvant, annon utraque Deo, Deique decreto ad suum motum in genere, et ad talem motum in specie conservandum opus habent, et per consequens, quatenus dependent, annon una, eademque ratione dependeant?", "en": "Here I would say and ask many other things: first, whether intellectual substances depend on God in another way than lifeless ones? For although intellectual beings involve more essence than lifeless ones, do not both need God, and God’s decree, to preserve their motion in general and such a motion in particular, and consequently, insofar as they depend, do they not depend in one and the same way?"}, {"id": "Ep22.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Secundo, quia, quam ei D. des Cartes adsignavit, libertatem animae non concedis, quaenam sit differentia inter dependentiam intellectualium, et anima carentium substantiarum? et si nullam volendi habent libertatem, quomodo tu eas a Deo dependere concipias? et quo pacto anima a Deo dependeat? Tertio, siquidem nostra anima ea non sit praedita libertate, anne nostra actio proprie Dei actio, et nostra voluntas proprie Dei sit voluntas?", "en": "Second, since you do not grant the soul the freedom which Mr Descartes assigned to it, what difference is there between the dependence of intellectual substances and of soulless ones? And if they have no freedom of willing, how do you conceive that they depend on God? And in what way does the soul depend on God? Third, if our soul is not endowed with that freedom, is not our action properly God’s action, and our will properly God’s will?"}, {"id": "Ep22.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Plura alia quaerere possem, sed tam multa a te petere non ausim: solummodo ad praecedentia responsum tuum brevi exspecto, an forte hoc medio adhibito sententiam tuam melius intelligam, ut postea de hac re coram tecum pluribus agerem. Ubi enim tuum nactus fuero responsum, Leydam profectus, si gratum sit, in transitu te salutabo.", "en": "I could ask many other things, but I would not dare to ask so much of you: I only await your reply to the preceding points soon, to see whether by this means I may perhaps understand your opinion better, so that I may afterwards treat this matter at more length with you in person. For when I have obtained your reply, I shall go to Leiden, and, if it is agreeable, I shall greet you on the way."}, {"id": "Ep22.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "His nixus, dico ex animo, tibi salute dicta, me manere tibi addictissimum, atque officiosissimum", "en": "Relying on this, I say from my heart, with greetings to you, that I remain your most devoted and most dutiful"}, {"id": "Ep22.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "G. de Blyenbergh.", "en": "W. van Blyenbergh."}, {"id": "Ep22.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "Dordraci 19. Febr. 1665.", "en": "Dordrecht, 19 February 1665."}, {"id": "Ep22.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 22", "la": "P.S. Nimia festinatione hanc inserere quaestionem oblitus sum: an quod alias nobis eveniret, prudentia nostra non impedire queamus.", "en": "P.S. In too great haste I forgot to insert this question: whether we cannot by our prudence prevent what would otherwise happen to us."}, {"id": "Ep23", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Duas abs te hac septimana accepi epistolas; posterior, 9 Martii exarata, tantum ei inserviebat, ut me de priore, 19 Februarii scripta, et mihi Schiedamo missa, certiorem redderet.", "en": "I received two letters from you this week; the later, written on 9 March, served only to inform me of the earlier, written on 19 February and sent on to me from Schiedam."}, {"id": "Ep23", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "In hac priore te de eo queri video, quod dixerim nullam apud te demonstrationem locum habere posse etc. quasi illud mearum rationum respectu, quia statim tibi non fecerunt satis, locutus fuerim, quod a mente mea longe abest: respiciebam propria tua verba, quae sic habent. Et siquando post diutinam investigationem accideret, ut naturalis mea scientia videretur cum hoc verbo vel pugnare, vel non satis bene etc. illud verbum tantae apud me est authoritatis, ut conceptus, quos existimo clare percipere, mihi potius suspecti sint, etc.", "en": "In the earlier I see that you complain of my having said that no demonstration could have a place with you, etc., as if I had said it with regard to my reasons, because they did not satisfy you at once; which is far from my mind: I had in view your own words, which run thus: ‘And if it should ever happen after long investigation that my natural knowledge seemed either to conflict with this word, or not well enough, etc., that word has such authority with me that the concepts which I think I perceive clearly are rather suspect to me’, etc."}, {"id": "Ep23", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Adeo ut tua tantum verba breviter repetierim, nec propterea credo, me ulla in re irae causam praebuisse, eo magis, quod ea, tanquam rationem, adducebam, quibus magnum nostrum dissensum ostenderem.", "en": "So that I only repeated your words briefly, and I do not therefore believe that I gave you cause for anger in anything, the more so since I adduced them as a reason, to show our great disagreement."}, {"id": "Ep23.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Porro, quia in calce secundae epistolae scripseras, te tantummodo sperare, et optare, ut in fide, ac spe perseveres, atque reliqua, quae nobis invicem de intellectu naturali persuademus, tibi esse indifferentia; animo volvebam, uti et iam revolvo, meas nulli usui futuras, mihique ea de causa consultum magis esse, ut studia (quae alioqui tamdiu intermittere cogor) non negligerem propter res, quae nullum fructum possunt largiri.", "en": "Further, because at the end of your second letter you had written that you only hoped and wished to persevere in faith and hope, and that the rest, which we persuade each other of concerning the natural intellect, was indifferent to you, I turned it over in my mind, as I still do, that my letters would be of no use, and that for that reason it would be wiser for me not to neglect my studies (which I am otherwise forced to interrupt for so long) for the sake of things which can yield no fruit."}, {"id": "Ep23.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Nec hoc primae epistolae meae contrariatur: quia ibi te, ut merum philosophum, qui (sicuti haud pauci, qui se Christianos autumant, concedunt) nullum alium veritatis Lydium habet lapidem, praeter naturalem intellectum, non vero theologiam, considerabam. Sed de hac re me aliter edocuisti, simulque ostendisti, quod fundamentum, cui nostram amicitiam superaedificare animus erat, non, ut arbitrabar, esset iactum.", "en": "Nor does this contradict my first letter: for there I considered you as a pure philosopher, who (as not a few who call themselves Christians grant) has no other touchstone of truth than the natural intellect, and not theology. But on this matter you have taught me otherwise, and at the same time shown that the foundation on which I intended to build our friendship had not, as I thought, been laid."}, {"id": "Ep23.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Denique reliqua quod attinet, ea plerumque tali modo inter disputandum contingunt, ut propterea humanitatis limites non transgrediamur, et hac de causa in secunda tua epistola, nec non in hac his similia, tanquam non animadversa, praetermittam. Haec de tua offensione, ut, me ei nullam dedisse causam, ostenderem, multo minus me ferre haud posse, ut mihi quisquam obloquatur. Nunc, quo ad tuas obiectiones rursus respondeam, me converto.", "en": "Finally, as for the rest, such things mostly happen in disputing in such a way that we do not on that account transgress the limits of courtesy; and for this reason I shall pass over things of this kind in your second letter, and also in this one, as if I had not noticed them. So much for your offence, to show that I gave no cause for it, much less that I cannot bear anyone to contradict me. Now I turn to answering your objections again."}, {"id": "Ep23.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Statuo ergo primo, Deum absolute, et revera causam esse omnium, quae essentiam habent, quaecunque etiam illa sint. Si iam poteris demonstrare, malum, Errorem, Scelera, etc. quicquam esse, quod essentiam exprimit, tibi penitus concedam, Deum scelerum, mali, erroris, etc. causam esse. Videor mihi sufficienter ostendisse id, quod formam mali, erroris, sceleris ponit, non in aliquo, quod essentiam exprimit, consistere, ideoque dici non posse, Deum eius esse causam.", "en": "I maintain, then, first, that God is absolutely and really the cause of all things that have essence, whatever they may be. If you can now demonstrate that evil, error, crimes, etc. are anything that expresses essence, I shall grant you entirely that God is the cause of crimes, of evil, of error, etc. I seem to myself to have shown sufficiently that what posits the form of evil, error or crime does not consist in anything that expresses essence, and that therefore it cannot be said that God is its cause."}, {"id": "Ep23.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Neronis, verbi gratia, matricidium, quatenus aliquid positivum comprehendebat, scelus non erat: nam facinus externum fecit, simulque intentionem ad trucidandam Matrem Orestes habuit, et tamen, saltem uti Nero, ita non accusatur. Quodnam ergo Neronis scelus? Non aliud, quam quod hoc facinore ostenderet se ingratum, immisericordem, ac inobedientem esse. Certum autem est, nihil horum aliquid essentiae exprimere, et idcirco Deum eorum etiam non fuisse causam, licet causa actus, et intentionis Neronis fuerit.", "en": "Nero’s matricide, for example, insofar as it comprehended something positive, was not a crime: for Orestes did the same outward deed, and at the same time had the intention of killing his mother, and yet he is not accused, at least not as Nero is. What, then, was Nero’s crime? Nothing other than that by this deed he showed himself ungrateful, unmerciful and disobedient. But it is certain that none of these expresses any essence, and therefore God was not their cause either, though he was the cause of Nero’s act and intention."}, {"id": "Ep23.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Porro velim hic notari, nos, dum philosophice loquimur, non debere Phrasibus theologiae uti: Nam, quia theologia Deum passim, nec temere, ut hominem perfectum repraesentat, propterea oportunum est, ut in theologia dicatur Deum quicquam cupere, Deum taedio operibus improborum affici, et proborum delectari; in philosophia vero, ubi clare percipimus, quod Deo illa attributa, quae hominem perfectum reddunt, tam aegre possunt tribui, et adsignari, quam si ea, quae elephantum, asinumve perficiunt, homini tribueremus: Ibi haec, et his similia verba nullum obtinent locum, nec ibi sine nostrorum conceptuum summa confusione ea usurpare licet.", "en": "Further, I should like it noted here that when we speak philosophically we ought not to use the phrases of theology: for because theology often, and not without reason, represents God as a perfect man, it is fitting that in theology it should be said that God desires something, that God is affected with distaste by the works of the wicked and takes delight in those of the good; but in philosophy, where we perceive clearly that the attributes which make a man perfect can be ascribed and assigned to God as little as if we were to ascribe to a man those which make an elephant or an ass perfect, there these and similar words have no place, nor may we use them there without the greatest confusion of our concepts."}, {"id": "Ep23.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Quare philosophice loquendo, dici nequit, Deum a quoquam quicquam petere, neque ei taediosum, aut gratum quid esse; haec quippe omnia humana sunt attributa, quae in Deo locum non habent.", "en": "Therefore, speaking philosophically, it cannot be said that God asks anything of anyone, nor that anything is distasteful or pleasing to him; for all these are human attributes, which have no place in God."}, {"id": "Ep23.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Notari tandem voluissem, quod, quamvis opera proborum, (hoc est, eorum, qui claram Dei habent ideam, ad quam cuncta eorum opera, ut et cogitationes determinantur) et improborum, (hoc est, eorum, qui Dei ideam haud possident, sed tantum rerum terrenarum ideas, ad quas eorum opera, cogitationesque determinantur) et denique omnium eorum, quae sunt, ex Dei aeternis legibus, et decretis necessario profluant, continuoque a Deo dependeant, attamen non solum gradibus; sed et essentia ab invicem differunt: licet etenim mus aeque, ac angelus, et aeque tristitia, ac laetitia, a Deo dependeant, nequit tamen mus species angeli, et tristitia species laetitiae esse.", "en": "Finally, I should have wished it noted that although the works of the good (that is, of those who have a clear idea of God, to which all their works and thoughts are determined), and of the wicked (that is, of those who do not possess the idea of God, but only ideas of earthly things, to which their works and thoughts are determined), and finally of all things that are, flow necessarily from God’s eternal laws and decrees and depend continually on God, yet they differ from one another not only in degree but also in essence: for although a mouse depends on God as much as an angel, and sadness as much as joy, yet a mouse cannot be a species of angel, nor sadness a species of joy."}, {"id": "Ep23.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Hisce opinor me tuis Obiectionibus (si eas probe intellexi; quippe interdum dubius haereo, num conclusiones, quas inde deducis, non differant ab ipsa propositione, quam demonstrare suscipis) respondisse.", "en": "With this I think I have answered your objections (if I have understood them properly; for at times I am in doubt whether the conclusions you draw from them do not differ from the very proposition you undertake to demonstrate)."}, {"id": "Ep23.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Verum illud clarius patebit, si ad quaestiones propositas ex his fundamentis respondeam. Prima est, an occidere Deo adeo acceptum sit, ac eleemosynas elargiri? altera est, an furari Dei respectu aeque bonum sit, ac iustum esse? tertia denique est, an, si daretur animus, cum cuius singulari natura non pugnaret; sed conveniret, libidinibus obtemperare, et scelera perpetrare, an in eo, inquam, ratio virtutis daretur, quae ipsi, ut bonum faceret, et malum omitteret, persuaderet?", "en": "But this will be clearer if I answer the questions proposed on these foundations. The first is whether killing is as acceptable to God as giving alms? The second is whether stealing is, in relation to God, as good as being just? The third, finally, is whether, if there were a mind with whose singular nature it did not conflict, but agreed, to obey its lusts and commit crimes, whether, I say, there would be in it a reason of virtue which would persuade it to do good and refrain from evil?"}, {"id": "Ep23.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Ad primam hoc do responsum, me (Philosophice loquendo) quid his verbis, Deo acceptum esse, velis, nescire. Si roges, an Deus hunc non odio habeat, illum vero diligat? an Deum alius contumelia affecerit, alius favore prosecutus fuerit? respondeo, quod non.", "en": "To the first I give this answer: that (speaking philosophically) I do not know what you mean by these words, ‘to be acceptable to God’. If you ask whether God does not hate the one and love the other? Whether one has affronted God and the other shown him favour? I answer no."}, {"id": "Ep23.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Si vero quaestio est, numne homines, qui occidunt, et eleemosynas distribuunt, aeque probi, et perfecti sint? Rursus negando respondeo.", "en": "But if the question is whether men who kill and men who give alms are equally good and perfect? Again I answer in the negative."}, {"id": "Ep23.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Ad secundam regero: si bonum respectu Dei inferat, iustum aliquid boni Deo praestare, et furem aliquid mali, respondeo neque iustum, neque furem in Deo delectationem, aut taedium posse causari; si vero quaeratur, an utrumque illud opus, quatenus aliquid reale, et a Deo causatum est, aeque sit perfectum? dico, si ad opera tantum animum advertamus, et ad talem modum, posse fieri, ut utrumque aeque fit perfectum.", "en": "To the second I reply: if ‘good in relation to God’ implies that the just man does God some good and the thief some evil, I answer that neither the just man nor the thief can cause God delight or distaste; but if it is asked whether both those works, insofar as they are something real and caused by God, are equally perfect, I say that if we attend only to the works, and in such a way, it may be that both are equally perfect."}, {"id": "Ep23.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Si igitur interroges, numne fur, et iustus aeque sint perfecti, beatique? respondeo, non. Per iustum namque eum intelligo, qui constanter cupit, ut unusquisque, quod suum est, possideat; quam cupiditatem ego in mea Ethica (necdum edita) in piis ex clara, quam de seipsis, et Deo habent, cognitione necessario originem ducere, demonstro. Et quoniam eius generis cupiditatem fur non habet, necessario Dei, et sui ipsius cognitione, hoc est, primario, quod nos homines reddit, destituitur.", "en": "If, then, you ask whether the thief and the just man are equally perfect and blessed? I answer no. For by a just man I understand one who constantly desires that each should possess what is his; and I demonstrate in my Ethics (not yet published) that this desire necessarily arises in the pious from the clear knowledge which they have of themselves and of God. And since the thief does not have a desire of that kind, he necessarily lacks the knowledge of God and of himself, that is, the chief thing that makes us men."}, {"id": "Ep23.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Si tamen ulterius quaeras, quid te queat movere, ut magis hoc opus, quod virtutem nuncupo, quam aliud facias? dico, me non posse scire, qua via, ex infinitis, Deus utatur, ut te ad hoc opus determinet. Posset esse, ut Deus tibi clare sui ideam impresserit, ut mundum oblivioni propter sui amorem traderes, reliquosque homines, sicut te ipsum, amares; et perspicuum est huiusmodi animi constitutionem cum caeteris omnibus, quae malae vocantur, pugnare, et ea de causa in uno subiecto non posse esse.", "en": "If, however, you ask further what could move you to do this work which I call virtue rather than another, I say that I cannot know which way, out of infinitely many, God uses to determine you to this work. It could be that God has impressed on you so clear an idea of himself that for love of him you would consign the world to oblivion and love other men as yourself; and it is plain that such a constitution of mind conflicts with all the others that are called evil, and for that reason they cannot be in one subject."}, {"id": "Ep23.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Porro fundamenta ethices explicandi, et etiam cuncta mea dicta demonstrandi non est hic locus, quia tantum in eo sum, ut ad quaestiones tuas responsum dem, et eas a me avertam, arceamque.", "en": "Further, this is not the place to explain the foundations of ethics and also to demonstrate all that I have said, since I am only concerned to answer your questions, and to turn them aside and ward them off from me."}, {"id": "Ep23.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Quantum denique tertiam quaestionem attinet, ea contradictionem supponit, mihique aeque videbatur, ac si quis rogaret: Si melius cum alicuius natura conveniret, ut seipsum suspenderet, an rationes darentur, ut se non suspenderet? Verum, eiusmodi dari naturam, sit possibile, tunc affirmo, (etiamsi liberum arbitrium concederem, sive non concederem) si quis videt, se commodius in cruce posse vivere, quam mensae suae accumbentem, eum stultissime agere, si se ipsum non suspenderet; et is, qui clare videret, se scelera patrando revera perfectiore et meliore vita, vel essentia, quam virtutem sectando, posse frui, is etiam stultus foret, si illa non faceret. Nam scelera respectu istiusmodi naturae humanae perversae virtus essent.", "en": "As for the third question, finally, it supposes a contradiction, and seemed to me just as if someone were to ask: If it agreed better with someone’s nature to hang himself, would there be reasons why he should not hang himself? But suppose it possible that there is such a nature; then I affirm (whether I granted free will or not) that if someone sees that he can live more comfortably on the gallows than sitting at his table, he would act most foolishly if he did not hang himself; and he who saw clearly that by committing crimes he could really enjoy a more perfect and better life or essence than by pursuing virtue would also be a fool if he did not commit them. For crimes would be virtue in relation to such a perverted human nature."}, {"id": "Ep23.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 23", "la": "Ad aliam quaestionem, quam calci epistolae addidisti, quia una hora vel centum eius generis quaerere possemus, nec tamen unquam ad conclusionem unius perveniremus, et ipse responsionem non adeo urges, non respondebo: hoc tempore tantum dicam, etc.", "en": "To the other question which you added at the foot of your letter, since we could ask a hundred of that kind in an hour without ever coming to a conclusion about one, and you yourself do not press much for an answer, I shall not reply: at this time I shall only say, etc."}, {"id": "Ep24", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 24", "la": "Ubi tua honorabar praesentia, ut in ea perseverarem non tempus, multo minus memoria permittebat, ut quae colloquentes tractavimus, ei mandarem; licet ego quam primum a te discesseram, omnes memoriae vires, quo audita retinerem, colligerem.", "en": "When I was honoured with your presence, time did not allow me to remain in it, much less did memory allow me to commit to it what we discussed together; although as soon as I had left you I gathered all the powers of my memory to retain what I had heard."}, {"id": "Ep24", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 24", "la": "In proximum ergo profectus locum tuas opiniones chartae mandare conabar; sed experiebar tunc, me revera ne quartam quidem tractatorum partem retinuisse; adeo ut tibi sim excusandus, si adhuc semel, tantum quaerendo de iis, in quibus mentem tuam, vel non bene perceperim, vel non bene retinuerim, tibi molestus sim. Optem, ut hunc laborem aliquo beneficio tibi compensare facultas daretur.", "en": "Having gone on to the next place, then, I tried to commit your opinions to paper; but I then found that I had in fact retained not even a quarter of what was discussed; so that you must excuse me if I trouble you once more, asking only about those things in which I either did not perceive your mind well or did not retain it well. I wish that I had the means of repaying this labour to you with some favour."}, {"id": "Ep24", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 24", "la": "Primum erat, quomodo, ubi principia, et cogitata tua metaphysica lego, quae tua, quaeque Cartesii sit sententia, internoscere possim?", "en": "The first was: how, when I read your Principles and Metaphysical Thoughts, can I distinguish which is your opinion and which Descartes’s?"}, {"id": "Ep24.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 24", "la": "Secundum, an proprie detur error, et in quo consistat?", "en": "The second: whether there is properly error, and in what it consists?"}, {"id": "Ep24.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 24", "la": "Tertium, qua ratione statuas, voluntatem non esse liberam.", "en": "The third: on what ground you maintain that the will is not free."}, {"id": "Ep24.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 24", "la": "Quartum, quid his verbis, quae L. M. in praefatione tuo nomine scripsit, intelligas, nempe. Cum contra author noster admittat quidem in rerum natura esse substantiam cogitantem: attamen neget illam constituere essentiam mentis humanae; sed statuat, eodem modo, quo extensio nullis limitibus determinata est, cogitationem etiam nullis limitibus determinari; adeoque quemadmodum corpus humanum non est absolute, sed tantum certo modo secundum leges naturae extensae per motum et quietem determinata extensio; sic etiam mentem, sive animam humanam non esse absolute, sed tantum secundum leges naturae cogitantis per ideas certo modo determinatam cogitationem; quae necessario dari concluditur, ubi corpus humanum existere incipit.", "en": "The fourth: what you understand by these words which L. M. wrote in your name in the preface, namely: ‘Whereas our author admits indeed that there is in nature a thinking substance, yet denies that it constitutes the essence of the human mind; but maintains that, in the same way in which extension is determined by no limits, thought too is determined by no limits; and so, just as the human body is not extension absolutely, but only extension determined in a certain way according to the laws of extended nature by motion and rest, so also the human mind or soul is not thought absolutely, but only thought determined in a certain way according to the laws of thinking nature by ideas; which is concluded to exist necessarily when the human body begins to exist.’"}, {"id": "Ep24.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 24", "la": "Unde sequi videtur, sicut corpus humanum ex millenis compositum est corporibus, ita etiam humanam mentem ex millenis constare cogitationibus;. et quemadmodum humanum corpus in millena resolvitur, unde componebatur, corpora, sic etiam mentem nostram, ubi corpus deserit, in tam multiplices, ex quibus constabat, cogitationes resolvi: et sicuti resolutae humani nostri corporis partes non amplius unitae manent; sed alia corpora inter ea se insinuant; ita sequi quoque videtur, mente nostra dissoluta, illas innumeras cogitationes, quibus constabat, non amplius combinatas, sed divisas esse: et quemadmodum corpora soluta corpora quidem manent; sed non humana, ita a morte quoque nostram substantiam cogitantem quidem resolvi, ut cogitationes, vel substantiae cogitantes maneant, non vero ita, ut essentia erat earum, quum mens humana dicebantur.", "en": "From which it seems to follow that, just as the human body is composed of thousands of bodies, so also the human mind consists of thousands of thoughts; and as the human body is resolved into the thousands of bodies of which it was composed, so also our mind, when it leaves the body, is resolved into the many thoughts of which it consisted; and as the resolved parts of our human body no longer remain united, but other bodies insinuate themselves between them, so it seems to follow too that when our mind is dissolved, those countless thoughts of which it consisted are no longer combined but divided; and as dissolved bodies remain bodies but not human ones, so too after death our thinking substance is resolved in such a way that thoughts or thinking substances remain, but not such as their essence was when they were called a human mind."}, {"id": "Ep24.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 24", "la": "Unde mihi videtur, ac si statueres, substantiam hominis cogitantem mutari, et instar corporum resolvi, quin imo quasdam ita, ut de impiis (ni me fallat memoria) affirmabas, omnino perire, nullamque sibi retinere cogitationem reliquam. Et sicut D. des Cartes, ut L. M. habet, mentem esse substantiam absolute cogitantem tantum praesupponit, ita et Tu, et L. M. praesupponitis maximam partem uti mihi videmini: quare tuam hoc in negotio mentem clare non percipio.", "en": "From which it seems to me as if you maintained that the thinking substance of man is changed and resolved like bodies, indeed some of them in such a way that, as you affirmed of the impious (if my memory does not deceive me), they perish altogether and retain no thought left to them. And just as Mr Descartes, as L. M. has it, only presupposes that the mind is an absolutely thinking substance, so you too and L. M. presuppose for the most part, as it seems to me: therefore I do not perceive your mind clearly in this matter."}, {"id": "Ep24.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 24", "la": "Quintum est, quod tam in colloquio, quam in postrema tua epistola, 13. Martii scripta, statuis, ex clara Dei, et nostri cognitione oriri constantiam, qua, ut quilibet suum sibi habeat, cupimus: Verum hic explicandum restat, qua ratione Dei, et nostri cognitio in nobis constantem voluntatem producat, ut unusquisque, quod suum est, possideat, hoc est, qua via id ex Dei cognitione fluat, vel nos obstringat, ut virtutem amemus, et ea negligamus opera, quae vitia dicimus; et unde procedat (quandoquidem occidere, et furari, iuxta te, aeque quid positivum, ac eleemosynas dare, comprehendant) cur caedem committere non tantum perfectionis, beatitudinis, et acquiescentiae, quantum eleemosynas distribuere, involvat.", "en": "The fifth is that, both in conversation and in your last letter, written on 13 March, you maintain that from the clear knowledge of God and of ourselves arises the constancy by which we desire that each should have his own: but here it remains to be explained in what way the knowledge of God and of ourselves produces in us the constant will that each should possess what is his; that is, by what way this flows from the knowledge of God, or binds us to love virtue and to neglect the works which we call vices; and whence it comes (since killing and stealing, according to you, comprehend something positive as much as giving alms) that committing murder does not involve as much perfection, blessedness and contentment as distributing alms."}, {"id": "Ep24.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 24", "la": "Dices forte, ut in postrema, quae 13. Martii consignata est, hanc quaestionem ad Ethicam spectare, eamque inibi a te moveri; verum quum sine huius quaestionis, ut et praecedentium elucidatione, ego tuam nequeam percipere mentem, quin semper absurditates supersint, quas conciliare non possum, amice rogo ut latius mihi ad eas respondeas; praesertim ut praecipuas quasdam definitiones, postulata, et axiomata, quibus tua Ethica, et haec imprimis quaestio innititur, proponas, et explanes. Forte, quia labor deterret, te excusabis, verum ut meae satisfacias petitioni saltem hac vice flagito, eo quod absque ultimae quaestionis solutione mentem tuam nunquam recte sim percepturus.", "en": "You will perhaps say, as in your last letter, dated 13 March, that this question belongs to the Ethics and is treated there by you; but since without the elucidation of this question, as of the preceding ones, I cannot perceive your mind without absurdities always remaining which I cannot reconcile, I ask you in friendly fashion to answer me more fully on them; especially to set out and explain certain chief definitions, postulates and axioms on which your Ethics, and this question above all, rests. Perhaps you will excuse yourself because the labour deters you; but I beg you to satisfy my request at least this once, because without the solution of the last question I shall never perceive your mind rightly."}, {"id": "Ep24.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 24", "la": "Optem, ut laborem tuum aliquo beneficio compensare liceret. Tempus duarum, triumve septimanarum praescribere non audeo; tantum ut ante iter tuum Amstelaedamense ad has responsum des, requiro.", "en": "I wish that I could repay your labour with some favour. I do not dare prescribe a time of two or three weeks; I only ask that you answer this before your journey to Amsterdam."}, {"id": "Ep24.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 24", "la": "Hoc ubi praestiteris, me tibi summopere devincies, egoque ostendam me esse et permanere, Mi Domine, tuum ad quaevis officia paratissimum,", "en": "When you have done this, you will oblige me greatly, and I shall show that I am and remain, Sir, most ready for any service to you,"}, {"id": "Ep24.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 24", "la": "G. de Blyenberg.", "en": "W. van Blyenbergh."}, {"id": "Ep24.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 24", "la": "Dordraci 27. Martii 1665.", "en": "Dordrecht, 27 March 1665."}, {"id": "Ep27", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 27", "la": "Ubi tua, 27. Martii ad me data, mihi tradebatur, ut Amstelaedamum proficiscerer, in procinctu stabam. Quare, ubi dimidium eius perlegeram, domi relinquebam, donec rediissem, ut tum responderem; quoniam putabam, eam nihil, nisi quaestiones, ad primam spectantes controversiam, comprehendere.", "en": "When your letter of 27 March was delivered to me, I was on the point of setting out for Amsterdam. So when I had read half of it, I left it at home until I returned, so as to answer it then; because I thought that it contained nothing but questions concerning our first controversy."}, {"id": "Ep27", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 27", "la": "Ego vero postea eam perlegens longe aliud eius argumentum esse deprehendebam, in eaque non modo probationem eorum, quae in praefatione mearum demonstrationum Geometricarum in Cartesii principia curavi scribi, eo tantum fine, ut meam sententiam cuilibet indicarem, non vero probarem, et hominibus persuaderem; sed etiam magnam ethices partem, quae, ut cuivis notum, metaphysica, et Physica fundari debet, desiderari.", "en": "But reading it through afterwards, I found that its argument was quite different, and that in it you ask not only for the proof of what I had had written in the preface to my Geometrical Demonstrations of Descartes’s Principles, only in order to indicate my opinion to anyone, not to prove it and persuade men of it, but also for a great part of ethics, which, as everyone knows, must be founded on metaphysics and physics."}, {"id": "Ep27", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 27", "la": "Qua de causa, ut tuis facerem satis quaestionibus, a me impetrare non potui; sed occasionem exspectare volui, quo amicissime coram, ut postulato desistas, a te peterem, meae negationis rationem darem, et denique ostenderem eas ad primae nostrae controversiae solutionem non facere; sed econtra maximam partem a solutione istius litis pendere. Tantum ergo abest, ut mea opinio, rerum necessitatem spectans, sine iis non possit percipi; quia hae revera percipi nequeunt, antequam ea in antecessum intelligatur.", "en": "For this reason I could not bring myself to satisfy your questions; but I wished to wait for an opportunity to ask you in person, in the friendliest way, to desist from your request, to give the reason for my refusal, and finally to show that they do not contribute to the solution of our first controversy, but on the contrary depend for the most part on the solution of that dispute. So far is it, then, from being the case that my opinion concerning the necessity of things cannot be perceived without them, that they in fact cannot be perceived before it is understood."}, {"id": "Ep27", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 27", "la": "Antequam vero offerebatur occasio, hac hebdomada alterum adhuc mihi tradebatur epistolium, quod aliquam displicentiam, ex nimia mora, prae se ferre videtur. Et ideo haec pauca ad te scribere necessitas me coegit, ut de proposito, et decreto meo certiorem, ut iam reddidi, te redderem.", "en": "But before the opportunity offered, another short letter was delivered to me this week, which seems to show some displeasure at the long delay. And so necessity compelled me to write these few lines to you, to inform you of my intention and decision, as I have now done."}, {"id": "Ep27", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 27", "la": "Spero te, negotio perpenso, ultro a tua petitione destiturum, et tamen animum in me propensum servaturum. Ego a mea parte pro viribus meis in omnibus demonstrabo, me esse, etc.", "en": "I hope that, having weighed the matter, you will of your own accord desist from your request, and yet keep your kindly disposition towards me. For my part I shall show in all things, as far as I can, that I am, etc."}, {"id": "Ep32", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Quod me ad philosophandum tu, et Nobilissimus D. Boylius benigne hortamini, maximas habeo gratias; ego quidem pro tenuitate mei ingenii, quantum queo, pergo, non dubitans interim de vestro auxilio, et benevolentia.", "en": "I give you the greatest thanks that you and the most noble Mr Boyle kindly urge me to philosophize; for my part, I go on as far as I can, according to the slenderness of my talents, not doubting meanwhile your help and goodwill."}, {"id": "Ep32", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Ubi quaeris, quid sentiam circa quaestionem, quae in eo versatur, ut cognoscamus, quomodo unaquaeque pars naturae cum suo toto conveniat, et qua ratione cum reliquis cohaereat, puto te rogare rationes, quibus persuademur unamquamque naturae partem cum suo toto convenire, et cum reliquis cohaerere. Nam cognoscere, quomodo revera cohaereant, et unaquaeque pars cum suo toto conveniat, id me ignorare dixi in antecedenti mea epistola; quia ad hoc cognoscendum requireretur totam naturam, omnesque eius partes cognoscere.", "en": "When you ask what I think about the question which turns on our knowing how each part of nature agrees with its whole and in what way it coheres with the rest, I think you are asking for the reasons by which we are persuaded that each part of nature agrees with its whole and coheres with the rest. For to know how they really cohere, and how each part agrees with its whole, I said in my preceding letter that I do not know, because to know this it would be required to know the whole of nature and all its parts."}, {"id": "Ep32", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Conabor igitur rationem ostendere, quae me id affirmare cogit; attamen prius monere velim, me naturae non tribuere pulchritudinem, deformitatem, ordinem, neque confusionem. Nam res non, nisi respective ad nostram imaginationem, possunt dici pulchrae, aut deformes, ordinatae, aut confusae.", "en": "I shall try, therefore, to show the reason which compels me to affirm it; but first I should like to point out that I do not attribute to nature beauty, ugliness, order or confusion. For things cannot be called beautiful or ugly, ordered or confused, except in relation to our imagination."}, {"id": "Ep32.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Per partium igitur cohaerentiam nihil aliud intelligo, quam quod leges, sive natura unius partis ita sese accommodat legibus, sive naturae alterius, ut quam minime sibi contrarientur. Circa totum, et partes considero res eatenus, ut partes alicuius totius, quatenus earum natura invicem se accommodat, ut, quoad fieri potest, inter se consentiant, quatenus vero inter se discrepant, eatenus unaquaeque ideam ab aliis distinctam in nostra mente format, ac proinde, ut totum, non ut pars, consideratur.", "en": "By the coherence of parts, then, I understand nothing else than that the laws, or nature, of one part so adapts itself to the laws, or nature, of another that they are as little contrary to one another as possible. Concerning whole and parts, I consider things as parts of some whole insofar as their natures so adapt themselves to one another that they agree among themselves as far as possible; but insofar as they differ among themselves, to that extent each forms in our mind an idea distinct from the others, and so is considered as a whole, not as a part."}, {"id": "Ep32.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Ex. gr. cum motus particularum lymphae, chyli, etc. invicem pro ratione magnitudinis, et figurae ita se accommodant, ut plane inter se consentiant, unumque fluidum simul omnes constituant, eatenus tantum chylus, lympha, etc. ut partes sanguinis considerantur: quatenus vero concipimus particulas lymphaticas ratione figurae, et motus, a particulis chyli discrepare, eatenus eas, ut totum, non ut partem, consideramus.", "en": "For example, when the motions of the particles of lymph, chyle, etc. so adapt themselves to one another, according to their size and shape, that they agree entirely among themselves and all together constitute one fluid, to that extent only are chyle, lymph, etc. considered as parts of the blood; but insofar as we conceive the lymphatic particles as differing from the particles of chyle by reason of their shape and motion, to that extent we consider them as a whole, not as a part."}, {"id": "Ep32.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Fingamus iam, si placet vermiculum in sanguine vivere, qui visu ad discernendas particulas sanguinis, lymphae, etc. valeret, et ratione ad observandum, quomodo unaquaeque particula ex alterius occursu, vel resilit, vel partem sui motus communicat, etc. Ille quidem in hoc sanguine, ut nos in hac parte universi, viveret, et unamquamque sanguinis particulam, ut totum, non vero ut partem, consideraret, nec scire posset, quomodo partes omnes ab universali natura sanguinis moderantur, et invicem, prout universalis natura sanguinis exigit, se accommodare coguntur, ut certa ratione inter se consentiant.", "en": "Let us now feign, if you please, that a little worm lives in the blood, which had sight sharp enough to distinguish the particles of blood, lymph, etc., and reason to observe how each particle, on meeting another, either rebounds or communicates part of its motion, etc. It would live in this blood as we live in this part of the universe, and would consider each particle of blood as a whole, not as a part, and could not know how all the parts are regulated by the universal nature of the blood and compelled to adapt themselves to one another, as the universal nature of the blood requires, so as to agree among themselves in a certain ratio."}, {"id": "Ep32.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Nam si fingamus, nullas dari causas extra sanguinem, quae novos motus sanguini communicarent, nec ullum dari spatium extra sanguinem, nec alia corpora, in quae particulae sanguinis suum motum transferre possent, certum est, sanguinem in suo statu semper mansurum, et eius particulas nullas alias variationes passuras, quam eas, quae possunt concipi ex data ratione motus sanguinis ad lympham, chylum, etc. et sic sanguis semper, ut totum, non vero ut pars, considerari deberet.", "en": "For if we feign that there are no causes outside the blood which would communicate new motions to the blood, nor any space outside the blood, nor other bodies to which the particles of blood could transfer their motion, it is certain that the blood would always remain in its state and its particles would undergo no variations other than those which can be conceived from the given ratio of the motion of the blood to the lymph, chyle, etc.; and so the blood would always have to be considered as a whole, not as a part."}, {"id": "Ep32.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Verum quia plurimae aliae causae dantur, quae leges naturae sanguinis certo modo moderantur, et vicissim illae a sanguine, hinc fit, ut alii motus, aliaeque variationes in sanguine oriantur, quae consequuntur non a sola ratione motus eius partium ad invicem, sed a ratione motus, sanguinis, et causarum externarum simul ad invicem: hoc modo sanguis rationem partis, non vero totius habet. De toto, et parte modo dixi.", "en": "But since there are very many other causes which regulate the laws of the nature of the blood in a certain way, and are in turn regulated by the blood, it comes about that other motions and other variations arise in the blood, which follow not only from the ratio of the motion of its parts to one another, but from the ratio of the motion of the blood and of the external causes together to one another; in this way the blood has the character of a part, not of a whole. I have just spoken of whole and part."}, {"id": "Ep32.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Iam cum omnia naturae corpora eodem modo possint, et debeant concipi, ac nos hic sanguinem concepimus: omnia enim corpora ab aliis circumcinguntur, et ab invicem determinantur ad existendum, et operandum certa, ac determinata ratione, servata semper in omnibus simul, hoc est, in toto universo eadem ratione motus ad quietem; hinc sequitur omne corpus, quatenus certo modo modificatum existit, ut partem totius universi, considerari debere, cum suo toto convenire, et cum reliquis cohaerere; et quoniam natura universi non est, ut natura sanguinis, limitata; sed absolute infinita, ideo ab hac infinitae potentiae natura eius partes infinitis modis moderantur, et infinitas variationes pati coguntur.", "en": "Now since all the bodies of nature can and must be conceived in the same way as we have here conceived the blood (for all bodies are surrounded by others, and are determined by one another to exist and to operate in a certain and determinate manner, the same ratio of motion to rest being always preserved in all of them together, that is, in the whole universe), it follows that every body, insofar as it exists modified in a certain way, must be considered as a part of the whole universe, and that it agrees with its whole and coheres with the rest. And since the nature of the universe is not limited, as the nature of the blood is, but absolutely infinite, its parts are regulated in infinite ways by this nature of infinite power, and are compelled to undergo infinite variations."}, {"id": "Ep32.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Verum ratione substantiae unamquamque partem arctiorem unionem cum suo toto habere concipio. Nam ut antehac in prima mea epistola, quam Rhenoburgi adhuc habitans tibi scripsi, conatus sum demonstrare, cum de natura substantiae sit esse infinitam, sequi ad naturam substantiae corporeae unamquamque partem pertinere, nec sine ea esse, aut concipi posse.", "en": "But with respect to substance I conceive that each part has a closer union with its whole. For, as I tried to demonstrate earlier in my first letter, which I wrote to you while I was still living at Rijnsburg, since it is of the nature of substance to be infinite, it follows that each part pertains to the nature of corporeal substance and can neither be nor be conceived without it."}, {"id": "Ep32.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Vides igitur, qua ratione, et rationem, cur sentiam corpus humanum partem esse naturae: quod autem ad mentem humanam attinet, eam etiam partem naturae esse censeo; nempe quia statuo, dari etiam in natura potentiam infinitam cogitandi, quae, quatenus infinita, in se continet totam naturam obiective, et cuius cogitationes procedunt eodem modo, ac natura, eius nimirum ideatum.", "en": "You see, therefore, in what way, and for what reason, I think that the human body is a part of nature. As for the human mind, I think that it too is a part of nature; namely, because I maintain that there is also in nature an infinite power of thinking which, insofar as it is infinite, contains in itself the whole of nature objectively, and whose thoughts proceed in the same way as nature, namely its object."}, {"id": "Ep32.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Deinde mentem humanam hanc eandem potentiam statuo, non quatenus infinitam, et totam naturam percipientem; sed finitam, nempe quatenus tantum humanum corpus percipit, et hac ratione mentem humanam partem cuiusdam infiniti intellectus statuo.", "en": "Next, I maintain that the human mind is this same power, not insofar as it is infinite and perceives the whole of nature, but insofar as it is finite, namely insofar as it perceives only the human body; and in this way I maintain that the human mind is a part of a certain infinite intellect."}, {"id": "Ep32.7", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Verum haec omnia, et quae huic rei annexa sunt, hic accurate explicare, et demonstrare, res esset nimis prolixa, nec puto te id impraesentiarum a me exspectare. Imo dubito, an mentem tuam satis perceperim, atque aliud responderim, ac rogaveris, quod ex te scire desidero.", "en": "But to explain and demonstrate all these things, and what is connected with them, accurately here would be too long a business, and I do not think you expect it of me at present. Indeed I doubt whether I have grasped your meaning well enough, and whether I have not answered something other than what you asked; which I should like to know from you."}, {"id": "Ep32.8", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Quod deinde scribis, me innuisse Cartesii regulas motus falsas fere omnes esse, si recte memini, D. Hugenium id sentire dixi, nec ullam aliam falsam esse affirmavi, quam regulam sextam Cartesii, circa quam D. Hugenium etiam errare me putare dixi; qua occasione petii, ut mihi communicares experimentum, quod secundum eam hypothesin experti estis in vestra Regia societate; sed tibi id non licere iudico, quia de hoc nihil respondes.", "en": "As for your writing next that I hinted that almost all of Descartes’s rules of motion are false: if I remember rightly, I said that Mr Huygens thinks so, and I affirmed no other to be false than Descartes’s sixth rule, about which I said that I thought Mr Huygens too was in error; on which occasion I asked you to communicate to me the experiment which you made in your Royal Society according to that hypothesis; but I judge that you are not permitted to do so, since you reply nothing about it."}, {"id": "Ep32.9", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 32", "la": "Dictus Hugenius totus occupatus fuit, et adhuc est in expoliendis vitris dioptricis; in quem finem fabricam adornavit, in qua et patinas tornare potest, satis quidem nitidam: quid autem ea promoverit, adhuc nescio, nec, ut verum fateor, valde scire desidero. Nam me experientia satis docuit in patinis sphaericis libera manu tutius, et melius expoliri, quam quavis machina. De pendulorum successu, et tempore transmigrationis in Galliam nondum aliquid certi possum scribere. etc.", "en": "The said Huygens has been, and still is, wholly occupied in polishing dioptrical glasses; for this purpose he has fitted up a workshop, quite a neat one indeed, in which he can also turn dishes; but what he has achieved with it I do not yet know, nor, to tell the truth, do I much wish to know. For experience has taught me well enough that with spherical dishes it is safer and better to polish by free hand than by any machine. Of the success of the pendulums and the time of his moving to France I can write nothing certain yet, etc."}, {"id": "Ep50", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 50", "la": "Quantum ad Politicam spectat, discrimen inter me, et Hobbesium, de quo interrogas, in hoc consistit, quod ego naturale ius semper sartum tectum conservo, quodque Supremo Magistratui in qualibet Urbe non plus in subditos iuris, quam iuxta mensuram potestatis, qua subditum superat, competere statuo, quod in statu naturali semper locum habet.", "en": "With regard to politics, the difference between me and Hobbes, which you ask about, consists in this: that I always keep natural right intact, and that I hold that the supreme magistrate in any city has no more right over its subjects than in proportion to the power by which it exceeds the subject; which is always the case in the natural state."}, {"id": "Ep50.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 50", "la": "Porro, quod demonstrationem attinet, quam ego in appendice Geometricarum in Cartesii principia demonstrationum stabilio, nempe Deum non, nisi valde improprie, unum, vel unicum dici posse; respondeo, rem solummodo existentiae, non vero essentiae respectu unam, vel unicam dici: res enim sub numeris, nisi postquam ad commune genus redactae fuerunt, non concipimus.", "en": "Further, as regards the demonstration which I establish in the appendix to the Geometrical Demonstrations of Descartes’s Principles, namely that God cannot be called one or unique except very improperly: I reply that a thing is called one or unique only in respect of its existence, not of its essence; for we do not conceive things under numbers until they have been brought under a common genus."}, {"id": "Ep50.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 50", "la": "Qui, verbi gratia, sestertium, et imperialem manu tenet, de numero binario non cogitabit, nisi hunc sestertium, et imperialem uno, eodemque, nempe nummorum, vel monetarum, nomine vocare queat: nam tunc, se duos nummos, vel monetas habere, potest affirmare; quoniam non modo sestertium; sed etiam imperialem nummi, vel monetae nomine insignit.", "en": "Whoever, for example, holds a sesterce and an imperial in his hand will not think of the number two unless he can call this sesterce and this imperial by one and the same name, namely that of coins or pieces of money: for then he can affirm that he has two coins or two pieces of money, since he designates not only the sesterce but also the imperial by the name of coin or piece of money."}, {"id": "Ep50.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 50", "la": "Hinc ergo clare patet, nullam rem unam, aut unicam nominari, nisi postquam alia res concepta fuit, quae (ut dictum est) cum ea convenit.", "en": "From this, then, it is clear that no thing is called one or unique until another thing has been conceived which (as has been said) agrees with it."}, {"id": "Ep50.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 50", "la": "Quoniam vero Dei existentia ipsius sit essentia, deque eius essentia universalem non possimus formare ideam, certum est, eum, qui Deum unum, vel unicum nuncupat, nullam de Deo veram habere ideam, vel improprie de eo loqui.", "en": "But since God’s existence is his essence itself, and we cannot form a universal idea of his essence, it is certain that whoever calls God one or unique has no true idea of God, or speaks improperly of him."}, {"id": "Ep50.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 50", "la": "Quantum ad hoc, quod figura negatio, non vero aliquid positivum est; manifestum est, integram materiam, indefinite consideratam, nullam posse habere figuram; figuramque in finitis, ac determinatis corporibus locum tantum obtinere. Qui enim se figuram percipere ait, nil aliud eo indicat, quam se rem determinatam, et quo pacto ea sit determinata, concipere.", "en": "As to this, that figure is a negation and not something positive: it is evident that matter as a whole, considered indefinitely, can have no figure, and that figure has a place only in finite and determinate bodies. For whoever says that he perceives a figure indicates by this nothing other than that he conceives a determinate thing, and in what way it is determined."}, {"id": "Ep50.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 50", "la": "Haec ergo determinatio ad rem iuxta suum esse non pertinet: sed econtra est eius non esse. Quia ergo figura non aliud, quam determinatio, et determinatio negatio est; non poterit, ut dictum, aliud quid, quam negatio, esse.", "en": "This determination, therefore, does not pertain to the thing according to its being, but on the contrary is its non-being. Since, then, figure is nothing other than determination, and determination is negation, it cannot, as has been said, be anything other than negation."}, {"id": "Ep50.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 50", "la": "Librum, quem Ultraiectinus professor in meum scripsit, quique post obitum eius luci expositus est, e fenestra Bibliopolae pendentem vidi: et ex paucis, quae tum temporis in eo legeram, eum lectu, multo minus responsione indignum iudicabam. Relinquebam ergo librum, eiusque authorem.", "en": "The book which the Utrecht professor wrote against mine, and which was published after his death, I saw hanging in a bookseller’s window; and from the little I read in it at that time I judged it unworthy of being read, much less of being answered. So I left the book, and its author."}, {"id": "Ep50.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 50", "la": "Mente, subridens volvebam ignarissimos quosque passim audacissimos, et ad scribendum paratissimos esse. Mihi **** merces suas eodem modo, ac propolae, venum exponere videntur, qui semper, quod vilius est, primo loco ostendunt: Ajunt, Diabolum esse vaferrimum; mihi vero, eorum genius longe hunc vafritie superare videtur. Vale.", "en": "Smiling, I turned it over in my mind that the most ignorant are everywhere the boldest and the readiest to write. The  seem to me to set out their wares for sale in the same way as hucksters, who always show what is cheapest first: they say the Devil is most cunning; but to me their genius seems far to surpass him in cunning. Farewell."}, {"id": "Ep50.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 50", "la": "Hagae Comitis 2. Iunii 1674.", "en": "The Hague, 2 June 1674."}, {"id": "Ep57", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 57", "la": "Miror saltem, quod eo ipso, quo demonstrent philosophi, aliquid falsum esse, eadem ratione ostendant eius veritatem: certitudinem enim intellectus in suae methodi initio Cartesius omnibus aequalem esse putat; in meditationibus autem demonstrat. Idem illi probant, qui putant se aliquid certi demonstrare posse hac ratione, ut pro indubitato a singulis hominibus accipiatur.", "en": "I am surprised, at least, that philosophers show the truth of something by the very same reasoning by which they demonstrate that it is false. For Descartes, at the beginning of his Method, holds that the certainty of the intellect is equal in all; in the Meditations, however, he demonstrates it. Those prove the same who think they can demonstrate something certain in such a way that it is accepted by every single man as beyond doubt."}, {"id": "Ep57.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 57", "la": "Sed his missis, experientiam provoco, et, ut ad haec accurate attendas, submisse rogo, sic enim deprehendetur, si ex duobus unus aliquid affirmet, alter vero neget, et ita, ut sibi eius sint conscii, loquantur, quod licet verbis contrarii videantur; attamen perpensis eorum conceptibus, ambo (unusquisque pro suo conceptu) vera dicant.", "en": "But leaving these things aside, I appeal to experience, and humbly ask you to attend to this carefully. For so it will be found that if, of two people, one affirms something and the other denies it, and they speak in such a way that they are conscious of what they say, then although they seem to contradict each other in words, yet when their concepts are weighed, both speak the truth (each according to his own concept)."}, {"id": "Ep57.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 57", "la": "Quod quidem refero, cum immensae sit in vita communi utilitatis, et possent hoc unico observato innumerae controversiae, et inde sequentes contentiones praecaveri; quamvis veritas haec in conceptu non semper absolute vera sit, sed positis tantum iis, quae in intellectu supponuntur, ut vera. Quae regula etiam adeo est universalis, ut apud omnes homines, ne dementibus quidem, ac dormientibus exceptis, reperiatur: quicquid enim hi dicunt, se videre (licet nobis non ita appareat) aut vidisse, certissimum est, haec revera ita se habere.", "en": "I mention this because it is of immense use in common life, and by observing this one thing countless controversies, and the quarrels that follow from them, could be prevented; although this truth in the concept is not always absolutely true, but true only given what is supposed in the intellect as true. This rule is also so universal that it is found among all men, not even the mad and the sleeping excepted; for whatever they say they see (though it does not appear so to us), or have seen, it is most certain that these things really are so."}, {"id": "Ep57.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 57", "la": "Quod etiam in casu proposito, de libero nempe arbitrio, clarissime conspicitur. Uterque enim tam qui pro, quam qui contra disputat, verum mihi dicere videtur, prout nimirum quilibet libertatem concipit; liberum enim dicit Cartesius, quod a nulla causa cogitur. Et tu econtra, quod a nulla causa determinatur ad aliquid.", "en": "This is also seen most clearly in the case proposed, namely that of free will. For both he who argues for it and he who argues against it seem to me to speak the truth, according, namely, to how each conceives freedom. For Descartes calls free that which is compelled by no cause; and you, on the contrary, that which is determined to something by no cause."}, {"id": "Ep57.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 57", "la": "Fateor itaque tecum, nos in omnibus rebus a certa causa ad aliquid determinari, et sic nullum nos habere liberum arbitrium: sed contra quoque puto cum Cartesio in certis rebus (quod statim aperiam) nos nullatenus cogi, atque ita habere liberum arbitrium. Exemplum formabo a praesenti.", "en": "I agree with you, therefore, that in all things we are determined to something by a certain cause, and so have no free will; but on the other hand I also think, with Descartes, that in certain things (as I shall show at once) we are in no way compelled, and so have free will. I shall take an example from the present case."}, {"id": "Ep57.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 57", "la": "Triplex autem est status quaestionis: primo, an in res, quae extra nos sunt, absolute aliquam habeamus potestatem? Quod negatur. Exempli gratia, quod hanc nunc exaro epistolam non est absolute in mea potestate, quandoquidem certe prius scripsissem, ni vel absentia, vel amicorum praesentia impeditus fuissem.", "en": "The question, then, has three parts. First, whether we have any absolute power over the things which are outside us? This is denied. For example, that I am now writing this letter is not absolutely in my power, since I would certainly have written earlier if I had not been prevented either by absence or by the presence of friends."}, {"id": "Ep57.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 57", "la": "Secundo, an nos in motus corporis nostri, qui sequuntur, voluntate eos ad id determinante, absolute habeamus potestatem? Respondeo limitando, si nimirum sano corpore vivamus. Si enim valeo, semper me ad scribendum applicare, vel non applicare possum.", "en": "Second, whether we have absolute power over the motions of our body which follow when the will determines them to it? I answer with a limitation: namely, if we live with a healthy body. For if I am well, I can always apply myself to writing or not apply myself."}, {"id": "Ep57.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 57", "la": "Tertio, an, quando mihi meum rationis exercitium usurpare licet, eo liberrime, hoc est, absolute uti possim? Ad quod respondeo affirmative. Quis enim mihi negaret, nisi contradicendo propriae conscientiae, quod non possum in meis cogitationibus cogitare, me velle scribere, aut non scribere.", "en": "Third, whether, when I am permitted the use of my reason, I can use it most freely, that is, absolutely? To this I answer affirmatively. For who would deny me, unless by contradicting his own consciousness, that I can think in my thoughts that I want to write, or not to write?"}, {"id": "Ep57.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 57", "la": "Et quoad operationem quoque, quia hoc externae causae permittunt, (quod secundum concernit casum) quod equidem tam scribendi, quam non scribendi facultatem habeam: fateor quidem tecum, dari causas, quae me ad id determinant, quod iam scribo, quia scilicet primo mihi scripsisti, eademque opera postulavisti, ut prima occasione rescriberem, et, quia impraesentiarum datur occasio, eam non libenter amitterem. Pro certo etiam affirmo, teste conscientia, cum Cartesio, istiusmodi res me propterea non cogere, meque revera (quod negatu videtur impossibile) id nihilominus omittere posse, non obstantibus his rationibus.", "en": "And as regards the act too, since external causes permit this (which concerns the second case), namely that I do indeed have the faculty both of writing and of not writing: I admit indeed with you that there are causes which determine me to this, that I am now writing, namely that you wrote to me first, and at the same time asked me to reply at the first opportunity, and since the opportunity now offers, I would not willingly lose it. I also affirm for certain, with Descartes, on the testimony of consciousness, that things of this kind do not on that account compel me, and that I really could (which seems impossible to deny) nevertheless refrain from writing, notwithstanding these reasons."}, {"id": "Ep57.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 57", "la": "Si quoque cogeremur a rebus externis, cui possibile est habitum virtutis acquirere? imo hoc posito omnis malitia excusabilis esset. Sed quot modis non fit, ut, si a rebus externis ad aliquid determinemur, obfirmato tamen, ac constanti ei resistamus animo?", "en": "Again, if we were compelled by external things, who could acquire the habit of virtue? Indeed, on this supposition all wickedness would be excusable. But in how many ways does it not happen that, when we are determined to something by external things, we nevertheless resist it with a firm and constant mind?"}, {"id": "Ep57.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 57", "la": "Ut itaque superioris regulae clariorem dem explicationem. Ambo equidem verum dicitis proprium iuxta conceptum: si autem absolutam veritatem spectemus, haec tantum competit sententiae Cartesii.", "en": "So, to give a clearer explanation of the rule stated above. You both speak the truth according to your own concepts; but if we look to absolute truth, it belongs only to Descartes’s opinion."}, {"id": "Ep57.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 57", "la": "Supponis enim in conceptu, ut certum, libertatis essentiam in eo consistere, quod a nulla re determinamur. Hoc sic posito, utrumque verum erit; veruntamen cum essentia cuiuslibet rei in eo consistat, sine quo ne quidem concipi possit, et clare sane concipiatur libertas, licet ab externis causis in nostris actionibus ad aliquid determinemur; sive licet semper causae sint, quae incitamento nobis sunt, ut actiones nostras tali modo dirigamus, cum tamen id plane non efficiant; verum nullatenus, posito quod cogamur.", "en": "For you suppose in your concept, as certain, that the essence of freedom consists in our being determined by nothing. On this supposition both will be true; but since the essence of any thing consists in that without which it cannot even be conceived, and freedom is certainly conceived clearly even though in our actions we are determined to something by external causes, or even though there are always causes which serve as incitements to us to direct our actions in such a way, without, however, bringing this about at all; but by no means, on the supposition that we are compelled."}, {"id": "Ep57.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 57", "la": "Vide praeterea Cartesii I. Tom. Epist. 8. et 9. item II. Tom. pag. 4. Sed haec sufficiant. Rogo, ut ad has difficultates respondeas,", "en": "See also Descartes, vol. I, Letters 8 and 9, and vol. II, p. 4. But let this suffice. I ask you to reply to these difficulties,"}, {"id": "Ep57.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 57", "la": "8. Octob. 1674.", "en": "8 October 1674."}, {"id": "Ep58", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Misit mihi amicus noster I. R. literas, quas ad me dignatus es scribere, una cum iudicio tui amici de mea, et Cartesii de libero arbitrio sententia, quae mihi gratissima fuerunt. Et, quamvis in praesentia, praeterquam quod valetudinem non satis firmam habeam, aliis rebus admodum distrahar, tamen vel singularis tua humanitas, vel quod ego praecipuum puto, veritatis studium, quo teneris, me cogit, ut tuo desiderio pro mei ingenii tenuitate, satisfaciam.", "en": "Our friend J. R. has sent me the letter which you were good enough to write to me, together with your friend’s judgment of my opinion and of Descartes’s on free will, which were most welcome to me. And although at present, besides not having very firm health, I am much distracted by other matters, yet either your singular kindness or, what I consider the chief thing, the zeal for truth by which you are possessed, compels me to satisfy your wish, as far as my slender talents allow."}, {"id": "Ep58", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Etenim quid tuus amicus velit, antequam experientiam provocat, et accuratam attentionem petit, nescio. Quod deinde addit, si quando inter duos alter de re quapiam quid affirmat, alter autem neget etc. Verum est, si intelligit illos duos, quamvis iisdem utantur vocabulis, de rebus tamen diversis cogitare, cuius rei exempla aliquot amico I. R. olim misi, cui iam scribo, ut tibi eadem communicet.", "en": "For what your friend means before he appeals to experience and asks for careful attention, I do not know. As for what he adds next, if ever, between two people, one affirms something about some matter and the other denies it, etc.: it is true if he means that the two, although they use the same words, are nevertheless thinking of different things; of which I once sent some examples to our friend J. R., to whom I am now writing that he should communicate them to you."}, {"id": "Ep58", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Transeo igitur ad illam libertatis definitionem, quam meam esse ait; sed nescio, unde illam sumpserit. Ego eam rem liberam esse dico, quae ex sola suae naturae necessitate existit, et agit; coactam autem, quae ab alio determinatur, ad existendum, et operandum certa, ac determinata ratione.", "en": "I pass, therefore, to that definition of freedom which he says is mine; but I do not know where he took it from. I say that that thing is free which exists and acts from the necessity of its nature alone; and compelled, that which is determined by another to exist and to operate in a certain and determinate manner."}, {"id": "Ep58", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Ex. gr. Deus, tametsi necessario, libere tamen existit, quia ex sola suae naturae necessitate existit. Sic etiam Deus se, et absolute omnia libere intelligit, quia ex sola ipsius naturae necessitate sequitur, ut omnia intelligat. Vides igitur me libertatem non in libero decreto; sed in libera necessitate ponere.", "en": "For example, God, though necessarily, nevertheless exists freely, because he exists from the necessity of his nature alone. So too God understands himself and absolutely all things freely, because it follows from the necessity of his nature alone that he understands all things. You see, therefore, that I place freedom not in a free decree, but in a free necessity."}, {"id": "Ep58", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Sed ad res creatas descendamus, quae omnes a causis externis determinantur ad existendum, et operandum certa, ac determinata ratione. Quod ut clare intelligatur, rem simplicissimam concipiamus. Ex. gr. Lapis a causa externa, ipsum impellente, certam motus quantitatem accipit, qua postea, cessante causae externae impulsu, moveri necessario perget. Haec igitur lapidis in motu permanentia coacta est, non quia necessaria; sed quia impulsu causae externae definiri debet; et quod hic de lapide, id de quacunque re singulari, quantumvis illa composita, et ad plurima apta esse concipiatur, intelligendum est, quod scilicet unaquaeque res necessario a causa externa aliqua determinatur ad existendum, et operandum certa, ac determinata ratione.", "en": "But let us descend to created things, which are all determined by external causes to exist and to operate in a certain and determinate manner. To understand this clearly, let us conceive a very simple thing. For example, a stone receives from an external cause which impels it a certain quantity of motion, by which it will afterwards necessarily continue to move when the impulse of the external cause has ceased. This continuance of the stone in motion is therefore compelled, not because it is necessary, but because it must be defined by the impulse of the external cause; and what is to be understood here of the stone must be understood of any singular thing, however composite and capable of many things it is conceived to be: namely, that each thing is necessarily determined by some external cause to exist and to operate in a certain and determinate manner."}, {"id": "Ep58.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Porro, concipe iam, si placet, lapidem, dum moveri pergit, cogitare, et scire, se, quantum potest, conari, ut moveri pergat. Hic sane lapis, quandoquidem sui tantummodo conatus est conscius, et minime indifferens, se liberrimum esse, et nulla alia de causa in motu perseverare credet, quam quia vult. Atque haec humana illa libertas est, quam omnes habere iactant, et quae in hoc solo consistit, quod homines sui appetitus sint conscii, et causarum, a quibus determinantur, ignari.", "en": "Now conceive, if you please, that the stone, while it continues to move, thinks, and knows that it is striving, as far as it can, to continue to move. This stone, surely, since it is conscious only of its striving, and is by no means indifferent, will believe that it is most free and that it perseveres in motion for no other cause than because it wills to. And this is that human freedom which all boast of having, and which consists only in this, that men are conscious of their appetite and ignorant of the causes by which they are determined."}, {"id": "Ep58.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Sic infans se lac libere appetere credit; puer autem iratus vindictam velle, et timidus fugam. Ebrius deinde credit, se ex libero mentis decreto ea loqui, quae postea sobrius tacuisse vellet. Sic delirans, garrulus, et huius farinae plurimi se ex libero mentis decreto agere, non autem impetu ferri credunt.", "en": "So the infant believes that it freely wants milk; the angry boy that he wants vengeance, and the timid one flight. The drunk, again, believes that he says from a free decree of the mind what afterwards, when sober, he would wish he had kept silent about. So the delirious man, the chatterer, and very many of this stamp believe that they act from a free decree of the mind, and are not carried away by impulse."}, {"id": "Ep58.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Et quia hoc praeiudicium omnibus hominibus innatum est, non ita facile eodem liberantur. Nam quamvis experientia satis superque doceat, homines nihil minus posse, quam appetitus moderari suos, et quod saepe, dum contrariis affectibus conflictantur, meliora videant, et deteriora sequantur, se tamen liberos esse credunt, idque propterea, quod res quasdam leviter appetant, et quarum appetitus facile potest contrahi memoria alterius rei, cuius frequenter recordamur.", "en": "And since this prejudice is innate in all men, they are not so easily freed from it. For although experience teaches more than enough that men can do nothing less than moderate their appetites, and that often, when they are torn by contrary affects, they see the better and follow the worse, they nevertheless believe themselves free; and that because they want some things lightly, and their appetite for these can easily be reduced by the memory of another thing which we frequently recollect."}, {"id": "Ep58.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "His, quaenam mea de libera, et coacta necessitate, deque ficta humana libertate sit sententia, satis, ni fallor, explicui; ex quibus facile ad tui amici obiectiones respondetur. Nam, quod cum Cartesio ait, illum liberum esse, qui a nulla causa externa cogitur, si per hominem coactum intelligit eum, qui invitus agit, concedo nos quibusdam in rebus nullatenus cogi, hocque respectu habere liberum arbitrium; sed si per coactum intelligit, qui quamvis non invitus, necessario tamen agit, (ut supra explicui) nego nos aliqua in re liberos esse.", "en": "With these things I have, unless I am mistaken, explained sufficiently what my opinion is of free and compelled necessity, and of feigned human freedom; from which your friend’s objections are easily answered. For when he says, with Descartes, that he is free who is compelled by no external cause: if by a man compelled he understands one who acts against his will, I grant that in certain things we are in no way compelled, and in this respect have free will; but if by compelled he understands one who, though not against his will, nevertheless acts necessarily (as I explained above), I deny that we are free in anything."}, {"id": "Ep58.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "At amicus tuus contra affirmat; nos rationis exercitio liberrime, hoc est, absolute uti posse, qua in re satis, ne dicam nimis confidenter perstat. Quis enim, ait, nisi propriae contradicendo conscientiae, negaret, me cogitationibus meis cogitare posse, quod vellem, et quod non vellem scribere.", "en": "But your friend, on the contrary, affirms that we can use the exercise of reason most freely, that is, absolutely; in which he persists confidently enough, not to say too confidently. For who, he says, would deny, unless by contradicting his own consciousness, that I can think in my thoughts that I want to write, and that I do not want to write?"}, {"id": "Ep58.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Pervelim scire, quam ille conscientiam, praeter illam, quam supra exemplo lapidis explicui, narrat: Ego sane, ne meae conscientiae, hoc est, ne rationi, et experientiae contradicam, et ne praeiudicia, et ignorantiam foveam, nego, me ulla absoluta cogitandi potentia cogitare posse, quod vellem, et quod non vellem scribere.", "en": "I should very much like to know what consciousness he speaks of, other than the one I explained above with the example of the stone. For my part, so as not to contradict my consciousness, that is, reason and experience, and so as not to foster prejudices and ignorance, I deny that I can, by any absolute power of thinking, think that I want to write and that I do not want to write."}, {"id": "Ep58.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Sed ipsius conscientiam appello, qui sine dubio expertus est, se in somnis non habere potestatem cogitandi, quod vellet et quod non vellet scribere; nec cum somniat se velle scribere, potestatem habet, non somniandi se velle scribere; nec minus expertum illum esse credo, quod mens non semper aeque apta sit ad cogitandum de eodem obiecto; sed prout corpus aptius est, ut in eo huius, vel illius obiecti imago excitetur, ita mens aptior est ad hoc, vel illud obiectum contemplandum.", "en": "But I appeal to his own consciousness. He has no doubt experienced that in sleep he does not have the power of thinking that he wants to write and that he does not want to; nor, when he dreams that he wants to write, does he have the power not to dream that he wants to write. Nor do I believe that he has any less experienced that the mind is not always equally capable of thinking of the same object, but that, as the body is more capable of having the image of this or that object aroused in it, so the mind is more capable of contemplating this or that object."}, {"id": "Ep58.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Cum praeterea addit, quod causae, cur animum ad scribendum applicuerit, ipsum quidem ad scribendum impulerint; sed non coegerint, nihil aliud significat, (si rem aequo pondere examinare velis) quam quod ipsius animus ita tum erat constitutus, ut causae, quae ipsum alias, cum scilicet magno aliquo affectu conflictatur, non potuissent, nunc facile potuerunt flectere, hoc est, causae, quae ipsum alias non potuissent cogere, coegerunt iam, non ut invitus scriberet; sed ut necessario scribendi esset cupidus.", "en": "When he adds, further, that the causes for which he applied his mind to writing impelled him to write but did not compel him, he means nothing else (if you are willing to weigh the matter fairly) than that his mind was then so constituted that causes which at other times, namely when he was torn by some great affect, could not have bent him, now could easily do so; that is, causes which at other times could not have compelled him have now compelled him, not to write against his will, but to be necessarily desirous of writing."}, {"id": "Ep58.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Quod porro statuit: quod si a causis externis cogeremur, virtutis habitum acquirere possit nemo; nescio, quis ipsi dixerit, non posse ex fatali necessitate; sed tantummodo ex libero mentis decreto, fieri, ut firmato, et constanti simus animo.", "en": "As for his further statement that if we were compelled by external causes, no one could acquire the habit of virtue: I do not know who told him that it cannot happen from fatal necessity, but only from a free decree of the mind, that we are of a firm and constant mind."}, {"id": "Ep58.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Et quod denique addit: quod hoc posito omnis malitia excusabilis esset. Quid inde? Nam homines mali non minus timendi sunt, nec minus perniciosi, quando necessario mali sunt.", "en": "And what he finally adds: that on this supposition all wickedness would be excusable. What then? For wicked men are no less to be feared, and no less pernicious, when they are necessarily wicked."}, {"id": "Ep58.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Sed de his si placet, vide meae appendicis ad Cartesii principiorum lib. I. et II. ordine geometrico demonstrator. Partis II. Caput VIII.", "en": "But on these matters see, if you please, Part II, Chapter 8, of my Appendix to the first and second books of Descartes’s Principles demonstrated in geometrical order."}, {"id": "Ep58.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 58", "la": "Denique tuus amicus, qui haec mihi obiicit, vellem, ut mihi responderet, qua ratione ille humanam virtutem, quae ex libero mentis decreto oritur, simul concipiat cum Dei praeordinatione. Quod si cum Cartesio fatetur, se haec nescire conciliare, ergo telum, quo ipse transfixus iam est, in me vibrare conatur. Sed frustra. Nam si meam sententiam attento animo examinare velis, omnia congruere videbis, etc.", "en": "Finally, I should like your friend, who makes these objections to me, to answer me how he conceives human virtue, which arises from a free decree of the mind, together with God’s preordination. If he admits, with Descartes, that he does not know how to reconcile these, then he is trying to hurl at me the weapon by which he himself has already been pierced. But in vain. For if you are willing to examine my opinion attentively, you will see that everything agrees, etc."}, {"id": "Ep63", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 63", "la": "Serio abs te peto, ut dubitationes hic motas solvere, tuamque ad eas responsionem mittere placeat. Sit, rogo, demonstratio ea ostensiva; non vero ad impossibile deducens, num scilicet primo nos plura de Deo attributa, quam cogitationem, extensionemque possimus cognoscere? Porro num inde sequatur creaturas, aliis constantes attributis, nullam posse concipere extensionem? Unde sequeretur tot constituendos esse mundos, quot dantur Dei attributa.", "en": "I earnestly ask you to be good enough to resolve the doubts raised here, and to send me your reply to them. Let the demonstration, I ask, be ostensive, not one leading to the impossible: namely, first, whether we can know more attributes of God than thought and extension? Further, whether it follows from this that creatures consisting of other attributes can conceive no extension? From which it would follow that as many worlds must be constituted as there are attributes of God."}, {"id": "Ep63", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 63", "la": "Quantae ex. gr. extensionis noster existeret mundus, tantae quoque extensionis mundi, aliis affecti attributis, essent: quemadmodum vero, praeter cogitationem, non nisi extensionem percipimus, ita etiam istius mundi creaturae, non nisi sui mundi attributa, atque cogitationem perciperent.", "en": "For example, of however great an extension our world exists, of so great an extension also would the worlds be that are affected with other attributes; and just as we perceive, besides thought, nothing but extension, so too the creatures of such a world would perceive nothing but the attributes of their world, and thought."}, {"id": "Ep63.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 63", "la": "Secundo, quoniam Dei intellectus tam essentia, quam existentia a nostro differt intellectu; nihil ergo cum nostro commune habebit; ac proinde (per 3 Ethic. Prop. part. 1.) Dei intellectus nostri causa esse nequit.", "en": "Second, since God’s intellect differs from our intellect both in essence and in existence, it will therefore have nothing in common with ours; and so (by Prop. 3 of Part I of the Ethics) God’s intellect cannot be the cause of ours."}, {"id": "Ep63.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 63", "la": "Tertio, in schol. 10. Prop. part. 1. Ethic. ais nihil in natura clarius esse, quam quod unumquodque ens sub aliquo attributo debeat concipi; (quod optime capio) et quo plus realitatis, aut esse habeat, eo plura ei competant attributa. Hinc sequi videtur, dari entia, quae tria, quatuor, aliaque plura habent attributa, licet ex demonstratis colligere licuerit, unumquodque ens duobus tantum constare attributis, nimirum certo quodam Dei attributo, eiusdemque attributi idea.", "en": "Third, in the scholium to Prop. 10 of Part I of the Ethics you say that nothing in nature is clearer than that each being must be conceived under some attribute (which I understand very well), and that the more reality, or being, it has, the more attributes belong to it. From this it seems to follow that there are beings which have three, four, or still more attributes, although from what has been demonstrated it could be gathered that each being consists of only two attributes, namely a certain attribute of God and the idea of that attribute."}, {"id": "Ep63.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 63", "la": "Quarto, eorum, quae a Deo immediate producta sunt, quaeque mediante infinita quadam modificatione producuntur, exempla desiderarem. Prioris generis cogitatio, ac extensio; posterioris vero intellectus in cogitatione, motus in extensione esse videntur.", "en": "Fourth, I should like examples of those things which are produced immediately by God, and of those which are produced by the mediation of some infinite modification. Of the first kind seem to be thought and extension; of the second, intellect in thought, motion in extension."}, {"id": "Ep63.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 63", "la": "Haec sunt, quae abs te, siquidem tempus aliquod tibi sit reliquum, peto. Vale etc.", "en": "These are the things I ask of you, if you have any time left over. Farewell, etc."}, {"id": "Ep63.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 63", "la": "25 Iulii 1675.", "en": "25 July 1675."}, {"id": "Ep64", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 64", "la": "Gaudeo, quod tandem occasio tibi oblata fuerit, ut me tuis literis, mihi semper gratissimis, recreares, quod ut frequenter facias, enixe rogo, etc.", "en": "I am glad that an opportunity has at last been offered you to refresh me with a letter of yours, always most welcome to me; and I earnestly ask you to do so often, etc."}, {"id": "Ep64.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 64", "la": "Ad dubia pergo, et quidem ad primum dico, mentem humanam illa tantummodo posse cognitione assequi, quae idea corporis actu existentis involvit, vel quod ex hac ipsa idea potest concludi. Nam cuiuscunque rei potentia sola eius essentia definitur, (per prop. 7. p. 3. Ethices) mentis autem essentia (per prop. 13. p. 2.) in hoc solo consistit, quod sit idea corporis actu existentis, ac proinde mentis intelligendi potentia ad ea tantum se extendit, quae haec idea corporis in se continet, vel quae ex eadem sequuntur.", "en": "I proceed to the doubts, and to the first I say that the human mind can attain to knowledge only of those things which the idea of a body actually existing involves, or which can be inferred from this idea itself. For the power of any thing is defined by its essence alone (by Prop. 7, Part III of the Ethics), and the essence of the mind (by Prop. 13, Part II) consists only in this, that it is the idea of a body actually existing; and so the mind’s power of understanding extends only to those things which this idea of the body contains in itself, or which follow from it."}, {"id": "Ep64.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 64", "la": "At haec corporis idea nulla alia Dei attributa involvit, neque exprimit, quam extensionem, et cogitationem. Nam eius ideatum, nempe corpus (per prop. 6. p. 2.) Deum pro causa habet, quatenus sub attributo extensionis, et non quatenus sub ullo alio consideratur, atque adeo (per axiom. 6. p. 1.) haec corporis idea Dei cognitionem involvit, quatenus tantummodo sub extensionis attributo consideratur. Deinde haec idea, quatenus cogitandi modus est, Deum etiam (per eandem prop.) pro causa habet, quatenus res est cogitans, et non quatenus sub alio attributo consideratur; adeoque (per idem axiom.) huius ideae idea Dei cognitionem involvit, quatenus sub cogitationis, et non quatenus sub alio attributo consideratur.", "en": "But this idea of the body involves and expresses no attributes of God other than extension and thought. For its object, namely the body (by Prop. 6, Part II), has God for its cause insofar as he is considered under the attribute of extension, and not insofar as he is considered under any other; and so (by Ax. 6, Part I) this idea of the body involves the knowledge of God only insofar as he is considered under the attribute of extension. Next, this idea, insofar as it is a mode of thinking, also has God for its cause (by the same Prop.) insofar as he is a thinking thing, and not insofar as he is considered under another attribute; and so (by the same axiom) the idea of this idea involves the knowledge of God insofar as he is considered under the attribute of thought, and not insofar as he is considered under another attribute."}, {"id": "Ep64.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 64", "la": "Apparet itaque mentem humanam, sive corporis humani ideam praeter haec duo nulla alia Dei attributa involvere, neque exprimere. Caeterum ex his duobus attributis, vel eorundem affectionibus nullum aliud Dei attributum (per prop. 10. p. 1.) concludi, neque concipi potest. Atque adeo concludo mentem humanam nullum Dei attributum praeter haec posse cognitione assequi, ut fuit propositum.", "en": "It is apparent, therefore, that the human mind, or the idea of the human body, involves and expresses no attributes of God besides these two. Moreover, from these two attributes, or from their affections, no other attribute of God can be inferred or conceived (by Prop. 10, Part I). And so I conclude that the human mind can attain to knowledge of no attribute of God besides these, as was proposed."}, {"id": "Ep64.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 64", "la": "Quod autem addis, an ergo tot mundi constituendi sunt, quot dantur attributa? vide schol. Prop. 7. p. 2. Ethices. Posset praeterea haec propositio facilius demonstrari, deducendo rem ad absurdum; quod quidem demonstrandi genus, quando propositio negativa est, prae altero eligere soleo, quia cum natura similium magis convenit.", "en": "As for your further question, whether therefore as many worlds must be constituted as there are attributes, see the scholium to Prop. 7, Part II of the Ethics. This proposition could, moreover, be demonstrated more easily by reducing the matter to absurdity; which kind of demonstration, when the proposition is negative, I usually choose in preference to the other, because it agrees more with the nature of such things."}, {"id": "Ep64.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 64", "la": "Sed quia positivum tantummodo petis, ad alterum transeo, quod est, an id possit ab alio produci, in quo tam essentia, quam existentia discrepat: nam quae ab invicem ita differunt nihil commune habere videntur. Sed cum omnia singularia, praeter illa, quae a suis similibus producuntur, differant a suis causis, tam essentia, quam existentia, nullam hic dubitandi rationem video.", "en": "But since you ask only for a positive one, I pass to the other question, which is whether a thing can be produced by another from which it differs both in essence and in existence: for things which differ from one another in this way seem to have nothing in common. But since all singular things, except those produced by their like, differ from their causes both in essence and in existence, I see no reason for doubt here."}, {"id": "Ep64.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 64", "la": "Quo autem ego sensu intelligam, quod Deus sit causa efficiens rerum, tam essentiae, quam existentiae, credo me satis explicuisse in schol. et coroll. Prop. 25. p. 1. Ethic.", "en": "In what sense I understand that God is the efficient cause of things, both of their essence and of their existence, I think I have explained sufficiently in the scholium and corollary of Prop. 25, Part I of the Ethics."}, {"id": "Ep64.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 64", "la": "Axioma scholii prop. 10. p. 1. ut in fine eiusd. Scholii innui, formamus ex idea, quam habemus entis absolute infiniti, et non ex eo, quod dentur, aut possint dari entia, quae tria, quatuor, etc. attributa habeant.", "en": "The axiom of the scholium to Prop. 10, Part I we form, as I indicated at the end of that scholium, from the idea which we have of a being absolutely infinite, and not from there being, or being able to be, beings which have three, four, etc. attributes."}, {"id": "Ep64.5", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 64", "la": "Denique exempla, quae petis, primi generis sunt in cogitatione, intellectus absolute infinitus; in extensione autem motus et quies; secundi autem, facies totius universi, quae quamvis infinitis modis variet, manet tamen semper eadem, de quo vide schol. 7. Lemmatis ante prop. 14. p. 2.", "en": "Finally, the examples you ask for: of the first kind are, in thought, absolutely infinite intellect; in extension, motion and rest; of the second, the face of the whole universe, which, although it varies in infinite ways, nevertheless remains always the same; on which see the scholium to Lemma 7, before Prop. 14, Part II."}, {"id": "Ep64.6", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 64", "la": "His, vir praestantissime, ad tuas, nostrique amici obiectiones respondisse me credo, si tamen scrupulum adhuc remanere existimas, rogo, ut mihi significare non graveris, ut eum etiam, si possim, evellam. Vale, etc.", "en": "With these I think, most excellent Sir, I have answered the objections of yourself and our friend; if, however, you think some difficulty still remains, I beg you not to hesitate to tell me of it, so that I may remove it too, if I can. Farewell, etc."}, {"id": "Ep64.7", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 64", "la": "Hagae Com. 29 Iulii, 1675.", "en": "The Hague, 29 July 1675."}, {"id": "Ep65", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 65", "la": "Abs te peto demonstrationem eius, quod dicis; nempe quod anima non possit plura attributa Dei, quam extensionem, et cogitationem percipere. Quod quidem licet evidenter videam, contrarium tamen ex schol prop. 7. part. 2. Ethices posse deduci, mihi videtur, forte non aliam ob causam, quam quia sensum huius scholii non satis recte percipio. Constitui ergo, haec qua ratione deducam, exponere, te, vir Cl. obnixe rogans, ut mihi velis, ubicunque sensum tuum non recte assequor, solita tua humanitate sucurrere. Ea autem sic sese habent.", "en": "I ask you for a demonstration of what you say, namely that the soul cannot perceive more attributes of God than extension and thought. Although I see this evidently, it nevertheless seems to me that the contrary can be deduced from the scholium to Prop. 7, Part II of the Ethics, perhaps for no other reason than that I do not grasp the sense of this scholium rightly enough. I have decided, therefore, to set out how I deduce it, earnestly asking you, distinguished Sir, to be good enough to help me with your usual kindness wherever I do not follow your meaning rightly. The matter stands as follows."}, {"id": "Ep65", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 65", "la": "Quod, licet inde colligam, mundum utique unicum esse, id tamen exinde non minus quoque clarum est, eum ipsum infinitis modis expressum; ac proinde unamquamque rem singularem infinitis modis expressam esse. Unde videtur sequi, quod modificatio illa, quae mentem meam constituit; ac modificatio illa, quae corpus meum exprimit, licet una, et eadem sit modificatio, ea tamen infinitis modis sit expressa, uno modo per cogitationem, altero per extensionem, tertio per attributum Dei mihi incognitum, atque sic porro in infinitum, quia infinita dantur attributa Dei, et ordo, et connexio modificationum videtur esse eadem in omnibus.", "en": "Although I gather from it that the world is certainly one, it is nevertheless no less clear from it that it is expressed in infinite ways, and so that each singular thing is expressed in infinite ways. From this it seems to follow that the modification which constitutes my mind and the modification which expresses my body, although it is one and the same modification, is nevertheless expressed in infinite ways: in one way through thought, in another through extension, in a third through an attribute of God unknown to me, and so on to infinity, since there are infinite attributes of God, and the order and connection of the modifications seems to be the same in all."}, {"id": "Ep65", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 65", "la": "Hinc iam quaestio oritur, quare mens, quae certam modificationem repraesentat, et quae eadem modificatio non solum extensione, sed infinitis aliis modis est expressa; quare, inquam, tantum modificationem illam per extensionem expressam, hoc est, corpus humanum, et nullam aliam expressionem per alia attributa percipiat. Sed tempus mihi non permittit, ut ea prolixius prosequar, forte haec dubia omnia crebrioribus meditationibus eximentur.", "en": "Hence the question now arises why the mind, which represents a certain modification, and which same modification is expressed not only in extension but in infinite other ways, why, I say, it perceives only that modification expressed through extension, that is, the human body, and no other expression through the other attributes. But time does not allow me to pursue these things at greater length; perhaps all these doubts will be removed by more frequent meditation."}, {"id": "Ep65.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 65", "la": "Londini, 12. August. 1675.", "en": "London, 12 August 1675."}, {"id": "Ep66", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 66", "la": "Caeterum, ad tuam obiectionem ut respondeam, dico, quod quamvis unaquaeque res infinitis modis expressa sit in infinito Dei intellectu, illae tamen infinitae ideae, quibus exprimitur, unam eandemque rei singularis mentem constituere nequeunt; sed infinitas: quandoquidem unaquaeque harum infinitarum idearum nullam connexionem cum invicem habent, ut in eodem scholio propositionis 7. Part. 2. Ethic. explicui, et ex prop. 10. Part. 1. patet. Ad haec si aliquantulum attendas, nihil difficultatis superesse videbis, etc.", "en": "But, to reply to your objection, I say that although each thing is expressed in infinite ways in the infinite intellect of God, those infinite ideas by which it is expressed cannot constitute one and the same mind of a singular thing, but infinite minds; since each of these infinite ideas has no connection with the others, as I explained in the same scholium to Prop. 7, Part II of the Ethics, and as is evident from Prop. 10, Part I. If you attend to these things a little, you will see that no difficulty remains, etc."}, {"id": "Ep66.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 66", "la": "Hagae 18 August. 1675.", "en": "The Hague, 18 August 1675."}, {"id": "Ep73", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 73", "la": "Perbreves tuas literas, 15. Nov. ad me datas, die Saturni elapsa accepi: in iis ea tantummodo indicas, quae in tractatu theologico-Politico crucem lectoribus fixere. Cum tamen ex iis etiam cognoscere speraverim, quaenam eae opiniones essent, quae religiosae virtutis praxin labefactare viderentur, de quibus antea monueras.", "en": "Your very short letter, sent to me on 15 November, I received last Saturday: in it you indicate only those things in the Theological-Political Treatise which have been a torment to its readers. Though I had hoped also to learn from it what those opinions were which seemed to undermine the practice of religious virtue, of which you had warned me before."}, {"id": "Ep73", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 73", "la": "Sed, ut de tribus illis capitibus, quae notas, mentem meam tibi aperiam, dico, et quidem ad primum, me de Deo, et natura sententiam fovere longe diversam ab ea, quam Neoterici Christiani defendere solent. Deum enim rerum omnium causam immanentem, ut aiunt, non vero transeuntem statuo.", "en": "But to open my mind to you on those three heads which you note, I say, and indeed as to the first, that I hold an opinion of God and nature far different from the one which the modern Christians usually defend. For I hold that God is the immanent cause, as they say, of all things, and not the transitive cause."}, {"id": "Ep73", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 73", "la": "Omnia, inquam, in Deo esse, et in Deo moveri cum Paulo affirmo, et forte etiam cum omnibus antiquis philosophis, licet alio modo; et auderem etiam dicere, cum antiquis omnibus Hebraeis, quantum ex quibusdam traditionibus, tametsi multis modis adulteratis, coniicere licet. Attamen quod quidam putant, tractatum theologico-Politicum eo niti, quod Deus, et natura (per quam massam quandam, sive materiam corpoream intelligunt) unum, et idem sint, tota errant via.", "en": "All things, I say, are in God and move in God: this I affirm with Paul, and perhaps also with all the ancient philosophers, though in another way; and I would even dare to say, with all the ancient Hebrews, so far as may be conjectured from certain traditions, even though these have been corrupted in many ways. Yet those who think that the Theological-Political Treatise rests on this, that God and nature (by which they understand a certain mass, or corporeal matter) are one and the same, are entirely mistaken."}, {"id": "Ep73", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 73", "la": "Ad miracula deinde quod attinet, mihi contra persuasum est, divinae revelationis certitudinem sola doctrinae sapientia, non autem miraculis, hoc est, ignorantia adstrui posse, quod satis prolixe cap. VI. de miraculis ostendi.", "en": "Next, as for miracles, I am on the contrary persuaded that the certainty of divine revelation can be built up only on the wisdom of its teaching, and not on miracles, that is, on ignorance; which I have shown at some length in chapter VI, on miracles."}, {"id": "Ep73", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 73", "la": "Hoc tantum hic addo, me inter religionem, et superstitionem hanc praecipuam agnoscere differentiam, quod haec ignorantiam, illa autem sapientiam pro fundamento habeat, et hanc causam esse credo, cur Christiani non fide, neque charitate, neque reliquis spiritus Sancti fructibus; sed sola opinione inter reliquos dignoscuntur; nempe, quia, ut omnes, solis miraculis, hoc est ignorantia, quae omnis malitiae fons est, se defendunt; atque adeo fidem, licet veram, in superstitionem vertunt. Verum an huic malo remedium adhibere reges unquam concedent, valde dubito.", "en": "I add only this here, that I recognize this chief difference between religion and superstition: that the latter has ignorance for its foundation, the former wisdom; and I believe that this is the reason why Christians are distinguished from others not by faith, nor by charity, nor by the other fruits of the Holy Spirit, but by opinion alone; namely, because, like everyone, they defend themselves by miracles alone, that is, by ignorance, which is the source of all wickedness; and so they turn faith, though true, into superstition. But whether kings will ever allow a remedy to be applied to this evil, I very much doubt."}, {"id": "Ep73", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 73", "la": "Denique, ut de tertio etiam capite mentem meam clarius aperiam, dico ad salutem non esse omnino necesse, Christum secundum carnem noscere; sed de aeterno illo filio Dei, hoc est, Dei aeterna sapientia, quae sese in omnibus rebus, et maxime in mente humana, et omnium maxime in Christo Iesu manifestavit, longe aliter sentiendum.", "en": "Finally, to open my mind more clearly on the third head too, I say that for salvation it is not altogether necessary to know Christ according to the flesh; but of that eternal son of God, that is, the eternal wisdom of God, which has manifested itself in all things, and chiefly in the human mind, and most of all in Christ Jesus, one must think quite otherwise."}, {"id": "Ep73", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 73", "la": "Nam nemo absque hac ad statum beatitudinis potest pervenire, utpote quae sola docet, quid verum et falsum, bonum et malum sit. Et quia, uti dixi, haec sapientia per Iesum Christum maxime manifestata fuit, ideo ipsius discipuli eandem, quatenus ab ipso ipsis fuit revelata, praedicaverunt, seseque spiritu illo Christi supra reliquos gloriari posse ostenderunt.", "en": "For no one can attain to the state of blessedness without this, since it alone teaches what is true and false, good and evil. And because, as I said, this wisdom was manifested most of all through Jesus Christ, his disciples preached it, insofar as it was revealed to them by him, and showed that they could glory in that spirit of Christ above others."}, {"id": "Ep73", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 73", "la": "Caeterum quod quaedam Ecclesiae his addunt, quod Deus naturam humanam assumpserit, monui expresse, me, quid dicant, nescire; imo, ut verum fatear, non minus absurde mihi loqui videntur, quam si quis mihi diceret, quod circulus naturam quadrati induerit.", "en": "As for what certain Churches add to these things, that God took on human nature, I have expressly pointed out that I do not know what they say; indeed, to confess the truth, they seem to me to speak no less absurdly than if someone were to tell me that a circle had put on the nature of a square."}, {"id": "Ep73", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 73", "la": "Atque haec sufficere arbitror ad explicandum, quid de tribus illis capitibus sentiam. An eadem Christianis, quos nosti, placitura sint, id tu melius scire poteris. Vale.", "en": "And this, I think, is enough to explain what I think about those three heads. Whether the same will please the Christians you know, you will be able to know better. Farewell."}, {"id": "Ep74", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 74", "la": "Quandoquidem accusare me videris nimiae brevitatis, culpam illam hac vice nimia prolixitate eluam. Exspectaveras, ut video, earum in scriptis tuis opinionum enarrationem, quae religiosae virtutis praxin convellere Lectoribus tuis videantur. Dicam quid sit rei, quod potissimum eos excruciet.", "en": "Since you seem to accuse me of excessive brevity, I shall wash that fault away this time with excessive prolixity. You had expected, as I see, an account of those opinions in your writings which seem to your readers to overturn the practice of religious virtue. I shall tell you what the matter is that chiefly torments them."}, {"id": "Ep74", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 74", "la": "Fatalem videris rerum, et actionum omnium necessitatem adstruere: atqui illa concessa, assertaque, legum omnium, omnis virtutis, et religionis incidi nervos, omnesque remunerationes, et poenas inanes esse, autumant. Quicquid cogit, vel necessitatem infert, excusare iidem arbitrantur; proindeque neminem inexcusabilem in Dei conspectu fore censent. Si fatis agamur, duraque revoluta manu omnia certo, et inevitabili tramite vadunt, quis culpae, poenarumque sit locus, illi equidem non assequuntur.", "en": "You seem to establish a fatal necessity of all things and actions; but if this is granted and asserted, they declare, the sinews of all laws, of all virtue and religion are cut, and all rewards and punishments are empty. Whatever compels, or brings necessity with it, they think excuses; and so they consider that no one will be inexcusable in the sight of God. If we are driven by the fates, and all things, unwound by a hard hand, go along a certain and inevitable path, they for their part cannot see what place there is for blame and punishment."}, {"id": "Ep74", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 74", "la": "Quis huic nodo adhiberi possit cuneus, perquam ardua res dictu est. Tu quid opis hanc in rem suppeditare posses, scire et discere pervelim.", "en": "What wedge can be applied to this knot is a very hard thing to say. What help you could supply in this matter I should very much like to know and to learn."}, {"id": "Ep74.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 74", "la": "Ad sententiam illam tuam, quam de tribus capitibus, a me notatis, aperire mihi dignaris, haec inquirenda subeunt. Primo, quonam sensu miracula, et ignorantiam pro Synonymis, et aequipollentibus habeas, ut in Novissimis tuis sentire videris; cum Lazari a mortuis resuscitatio, et Iesu Christi a morte resurrectio omnem naturae creatae vim superare, et soli potentiae divinae competere videatur; neque id ignorantiam culpabilem arguat, quod intelligentiae finitae, certisque repagulis constrictae limites excedat necessum est.", "en": "On that opinion of yours which you deign to open to me on the three heads I noted, these questions occur to me. First, in what sense you hold miracles and ignorance to be synonyms and equivalents, as you seem to think in your last letter; since the raising of Lazarus from the dead and the resurrection of Jesus Christ from death seem to surpass all the power of created nature and to belong to the divine power alone; nor does that which must exceed the limits of a finite intelligence, confined within fixed bounds, argue a culpable ignorance."}, {"id": "Ep74.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 74", "la": "An non convenire censes creatae menti et scientiae, increatae mentis, ac supremi Numinis talem scientiam, potentiamque agnoscere, quae penetrare, ac praestare ea possit, quorum ratio, ac modus a nobis homuncionibus reddi, et explicari nequeat? Homines sumus, humani nihil a nobis alienum ducendum videtur.", "en": "Do you not think it fitting for a created mind and knowledge to acknowledge in the uncreated mind and supreme Deity such knowledge and power as can penetrate and accomplish things whose reason and manner cannot be rendered and explained by us little men? We are men; nothing human, it seems, should be thought foreign to us."}, {"id": "Ep74.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 74", "la": "Deinde, cum capere te nequire fatearis, Deum revera naturam humanam assumpsisse, quaerere ex te fas sit, quomodo illa Euangelii nostri, et epistolae ad Hebraeos scriptae locos intelligas, quorum prior affirmat, verbum carnem factum esse; posterior, Filium Dei non Angelos, sed semen Abrahae assumpsisse.", "en": "Next, since you confess that you cannot grasp that God really took on human nature, may I ask you how you understand those passages of our Gospel and of the Epistle written to the Hebrews, of which the former affirms that the Word was made flesh, the latter that the Son of God took on not angels but the seed of Abraham?"}, {"id": "Ep74.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 74", "la": "Et totius Euangelii tenorem id inferre putem Filium Dei unigenitum λόγον, (qui et Deus, et apud Deum erat) in natura humana se ostendisse, et pro nobis peccatoribus ἀντίλυτρον, redemptionis precium, passione, et morte sua exsolvisse. Quid de his, et similibus dicendum, ut sua constet Euangelio, et Christianae religioni, cui te favere opinor, veritas, lubens edoceri vellem.", "en": "And I should think that the tenor of the whole Gospel implies that the only-begotten Son of God, the λόγον (‘Word’), who was both God and with God, showed himself in human nature, and by his passion and death paid for us sinners the ἀντίλυτρον (‘ransom’), the price of redemption. What should be said about these and similar things, so that the Gospel and the Christian religion, which I believe you favour, may keep their truth, I should gladly be taught."}, {"id": "Ep74.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 74", "la": "Plura scribere statueram, sed interpellant amici invisentes, quibus negare humanitatis officia nefas duco. Sed et haec, quae congessi hac epistola, suffecerint, et fortasse taedium tibi philosophanti creaverint. Vale igitur, et me iugem Eruditionis et scientiae tuae cultorem crede.", "en": "I had decided to write more, but friends calling on me interrupt, to whom I hold it wrong to refuse the duties of courtesy. But these things too which I have gathered in this letter will suffice, and will perhaps have wearied you as you philosophize. Farewell, then, and believe me a constant admirer of your learning and knowledge."}, {"id": "Ep74.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 74", "la": "Dab. Londini die 16. Decemb. 1675.", "en": "Given at London, 16 December 1675."}, {"id": "Ep75", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 75", "la": "Video tandem, quid id fuerit, quod a me postulabas ne evulgarem; sed quia id ipsum praecipuum est fundamentum eorum omnium, quae in tractatu, quem edere destinaveram, habentur, volo hic paucis explicare, qua ratione ego fatalem omnium rerum, et actionum necessitatem statuam.", "en": "I see at last what it was that you asked me not to make public; but since this very thing is the chief foundation of everything in the treatise which I had intended to publish, I wish to explain here briefly in what way I hold the fatal necessity of all things and actions."}, {"id": "Ep75", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 75", "la": "Nam Deum nullo modo fato subiicio, sed omnia inevitabili necessitate ex Dei natura sequi concipio eodem modo, ac omnes concipiunt, ex ipsius Dei natura sequi, ut Deus se ipsum intelligat; quod sane nemo negat ex divina natura necessario sequi, et tamen nemo concipit, Deum fato aliquo coactum, sed omnino libere, tametsi necessario se ipsum intelligere.", "en": "For I in no way subject God to fate, but I conceive that all things follow with inevitable necessity from the nature of God in the same way that everyone conceives that it follows from the very nature of God that God understands himself; which surely no one denies follows necessarily from the divine nature, and yet no one conceives that God understands himself compelled by some fate, but that he understands himself altogether freely, although necessarily."}, {"id": "Ep75.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 75", "la": "Deinde haec inevitabilis rerum necessitas nec iura divina, nec humana tollit. Nam ipsa moralia documenta, sive formam legis, seu iuris ab ipso Deo accipiant, sive non, divina tamen sunt, et salutaria, et si bonum, quod ex virtute, et amore divino sequitur, a Deo tanquam Iudice accipiamus, vel ex necessitate divinae naturae emanet, non erit propterea magis, aut minus optabile, ut nec contra mala, quae ex pravis actionibus, et affectibus sequuntur, ideo, quia necessario ex iisdem sequuntur, minus timenda sunt, et denique sive ea, quae agimus, necessario, vel contingenter agamus, spe tamen, et metu ducimur.", "en": "Next, this inevitable necessity of things takes away neither divine nor human laws. For the moral teachings themselves, whether or not they receive the form of law or right from God himself, are nevertheless divine and salutary; and whether we receive the good which follows from virtue and the divine love from God as a judge, or it emanates from the necessity of the divine nature, it will not on that account be more or less desirable; just as, on the other hand, the evils which follow from wicked actions and affects are not less to be feared because they follow from them necessarily; and finally, whether we do what we do necessarily or contingently, we are still led by hope and fear."}, {"id": "Ep75.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 75", "la": "Porro homines coram Deo nulla alia de causa sunt inexcusabiles, quam quia in ipsius Dei potestate sunt, ut lutum in potestate figuli, qui ex eadem massa vasa facit, alia ad decus, alia ad dedecus.", "en": "Further, men are inexcusable before God for no other reason than that they are in the power of God himself, as clay is in the power of the potter, who from the same lump makes vessels, some for honour, others for dishonour."}, {"id": "Ep75.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 75", "la": "Ad haec pauca si attendere velis aliquantulum, non dubito, quin facili negotio ad omnia argumenta, quae in hanc sententiam obiici solent, respondere possis, ut multi iam mecum experti sunt.", "en": "If you will attend a little to these few points, I do not doubt that you will easily be able to answer all the arguments that are usually raised against this opinion, as many have already found with me."}, {"id": "Ep75.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 75", "la": "Miracula, et ignorantiam pro aequipollentibus sumpsi, quia ii, qui Dei existentiam, et religionem miraculis adstruere conantur, rem obscuram per aliam magis obscuram, et quam maxime ignorant, ostendere volunt, atque ita novum argumentandi genus adferunt, redigendo scilicet non ad impossibile, ut aiunt, sed ignorantiam. Caeterum meam de miraculis sententiam satis, ni fallor, explicui in tractatu theologico-Politico.", "en": "I took miracles and ignorance as equivalents because those who try to establish God’s existence and religion by miracles want to show something obscure through something else more obscure, of which they are most ignorant, and so bring in a new kind of argument, reducing, namely, not to the impossible, as they say, but to ignorance. For the rest, I have explained my view of miracles sufficiently, if I am not mistaken, in the Theological-Political Treatise."}, {"id": "Ep75.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 75", "la": "Hoc tantum hic addo, quod si ad haec attendas, quod scilicet Christus non Senatui, nec Pilato, nec cuiquam infidelium; sed sanctis tantummodo apparuerit, et quod Deus neque dextram, neque sinistram habeat, nec in loco; sed ubique secundum essentiam sit, et quod materia ubique sit eadem, et quod Deus extra Mundum in spatio, quod fingunt, imaginario, sese non manifestet, et quod denique corporis humani compages intra debitos limites solo aeris pondere coerceatur, facile videbis, hanc Christi apparitionem non absimilem esse illi, qua Deus Abrahamo apparuit, quando tres vidit homines, quos ad secum prandendum invitavit.", "en": "I add only this here: that if you attend to these things, namely that Christ appeared not to the Senate, nor to Pilate, nor to any of the unbelievers, but only to the saints; and that God has neither right hand nor left, and is not in a place, but is everywhere according to his essence; and that matter is everywhere the same; and that God does not manifest himself outside the world, in the imaginary space which they feign; and finally that the frame of the human body is held within its due limits by the weight of the air alone: you will easily see that this appearance of Christ is not unlike the one in which God appeared to Abraham, when he saw three men whom he invited to dine with him."}, {"id": "Ep75.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 75", "la": "At dices, Apostolos omnes omnino credidisse, quod Christus a morte resurrexerit, et ad coelum revera ascenderit: quod ego non nego. Nam ipse etiam Abrahamus credidit, quod Deus apud ipsum pransus fuerit, et omnes Israelitae, quod Deus e coelo igne circumdatus ad montem Sinai descenderit, et cum iis immediate locutus fuerit, cum tamen haec, et plura alia huiusmodi apparitiones, seu revelationes fuerint, captui, et opinionibus eorum hominum accommodatae, quibus Deus mentem suam iisdem revelare voluit.", "en": "But you will say that the Apostles all believed absolutely that Christ rose from death and really ascended to heaven: which I do not deny. For Abraham himself also believed that God had dined with him, and all the Israelites that God had descended from heaven surrounded by fire onto Mount Sinai and had spoken with them directly; whereas these, and many other things of this kind, were appearances or revelations accommodated to the understanding and opinions of those men to whom God willed to reveal his mind through them."}, {"id": "Ep75.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 75", "la": "Concludo itaque Christi a mortuis resurrectionem revera spiritualem, et solis fidelibus ad eorum captum revelatam fuisse, nempe quod Christus aeternitate donatus fuit, et a mortuis, (mortuos hic intelligo eo sensu, quo Christus dixit: sinite mortuos mortuos suos sepelire) surrexit, simulatque vita et morte singularis sanctitatis exemplum dedit, et eatenus discipulos suos a mortuis suscitat, quatenus ipsi hoc vitae eius, et mortis exemplum sequuntur.", "en": "I conclude, therefore, that Christ’s resurrection from the dead was in truth spiritual, and revealed only to the faithful according to their understanding; namely, that Christ was endowed with eternity and rose from the dead (I understand ‘the dead’ here in the sense in which Christ said: let the dead bury their dead) as soon as he gave, by his life and death, an example of singular holiness; and that he raises his disciples from the dead insofar as they themselves follow this example of his life and death."}, {"id": "Ep75.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 75", "la": "Nec difficile esset totam Euangelii doctrinam secundum hanc hypothesin explicare. Imo caput 15. Ep. 1. ad Corinthios ex sola hac hypothesi explicari potest, et Pauli argumenta intelligi, cum alias communem hypothesin sequendo infirma appareant, et facili negotio refelli possint, ut iam taceam, quod Christiani omnia, quae Iudaei carnaliter, Spiritualiter interpretati sunt.", "en": "Nor would it be difficult to explain the whole teaching of the Gospel according to this hypothesis. Indeed, chapter 15 of the First Epistle to the Corinthians can be explained by this hypothesis alone, and Paul’s arguments understood, whereas if the common hypothesis is followed they appear weak and can easily be refuted; to say nothing of the fact that Christians have interpreted spiritually everything that the Jews interpreted carnally."}, {"id": "Ep75.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 75", "la": "Humanam imbecillitatem tecum agnosco. Sed te contra rogare mihi liceat, an nos homunciones tantam naturae cognitionem habeamus, ut determinare possimus, quousque eius vis, et potentia se extendit, et quid eius vim superat? quod quia nemo sine arrogantia praesumere potest, licet ergo absque iactantia miracula per causas naturales, quantum fieri potest, explicare, et quae explicare non possumus, nec etiam demonstrare, quod absurda sint, satius erit iudicium de iis suspendere, et religionem, uti dixi, sola doctrinae sapientia adstruere.", "en": "I acknowledge human weakness with you. But may I in turn ask you whether we little men have such knowledge of nature that we can determine how far its force and power extend, and what surpasses its force? Since no one can presume this without arrogance, it is permissible, then, without boasting, to explain miracles by natural causes as far as possible; and as for those which we cannot explain, nor yet demonstrate to be absurd, it will be better to suspend judgment about them, and to establish religion, as I said, on the wisdom of its teaching alone."}, {"id": "Ep75.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 75", "la": "Loca denique Euangelii Iohannis et epistolae ad Hebraeos iis, quae dixi, repugnare credis, quia linguarum orientalium phrases Europaeis loquendi modis metiris, et quamvis Iohannes suum Euangelium Graece scripserit, hebraizat tamen.", "en": "Finally, you believe that the passages of the Gospel of John and of the Epistle to the Hebrews conflict with what I have said, because you measure the phrases of Oriental languages by European ways of speaking; and although John wrote his Gospel in Greek, he nevertheless Hebraizes."}, {"id": "Ep75.4", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 75", "la": "Quicquid sit, an credis, quando scriptura ait, quod Deus in Nube sese manifestaverit, aut quod in Tabernaculo, et in Templo habitaverit, quod ipse Deus naturam Nubis, Tabernaculi, et templi assumpserit? atqui hoc summum est, quod Christus de se ipso dixit, se scilicet templum Dei esse, nimirum quia, ut in meis praecedentibus dixi, Deus sese maxime in Christo manifestavit, quod Iohannes ut efficacius exprimeret, dixit verbum factum esse carnem. Sed de his satis.", "en": "However that may be, do you believe, when Scripture says that God manifested himself in a cloud, or that he dwelt in the Tabernacle and in the Temple, that God himself took on the nature of the cloud, the Tabernacle and the Temple? Yet this is the most that Christ said of himself, namely that he was the temple of God; namely because, as I said in my previous letter, God manifested himself most of all in Christ; which John, to express it more forcefully, put by saying that the Word was made flesh. But enough of these things."}, {"id": "Ep80", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 80", "la": "Primo difficulter admodum concipere queo, qui a priori corporum existentia demonstretur, quae motus, et figuras habent; cum in extensione, absolute rem considerando, nil tale occurrat.", "en": "First, I can only with great difficulty conceive how the existence of bodies which have motion and figure is demonstrated a priori, since in extension, considering the thing absolutely, nothing of the kind is found."}, {"id": "Ep80", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 80", "la": "Secundo erudiri abs te vellem, quomodo intelligendum sit, cuius meministi epistola de infinito his verbis. Nec tamen concludunt talia omnem numerum superare ex partium multitudine.", "en": "Second, I should like to be taught by you how what you mention in the letter on the infinite is to be understood, in these words: ‘Yet they do not conclude that such things surpass every number from the multitude of their parts.’"}, {"id": "Ep80", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 80", "la": "Nam revera omnes mathematici mihi videntur circa talia infinita semper demonstrare, quod partium numerus sit tam magnus, ut omnem assignabilem numerum superent, et in exemplo, de duobus circulis ibidem allato, non hoc ipsum declarare videris, quod tamen susceperas. Ibi enim tantum ostendis, quod non hoc ipsum concludunt ex nimia spatii interpositi magnitudine, et quod eius maximum et minimum non habeamus; sed non demonstras prout volebas, quod id non concludant ex partium multitudine.", "en": "For in truth all mathematicians seem to me always to demonstrate, concerning such infinites, that the number of parts is so great that it surpasses every assignable number; and in the example of the two circles given there you do not seem to show this very thing, which you had undertaken to do. For there you show only that they do not conclude this from the excessive size of the interposed space, and from our not having its maximum and minimum; but you do not demonstrate, as you wished, that they do not conclude it from the multitude of the parts."}, {"id": "Ep80.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 80", "la": "2. Maii, 1676.", "en": "2 May 1676."}, {"id": "Ep81", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 81", "la": "Quod in epistola de infinito dixi, quod partium infinitatem ex earum multitudine non concludant, hinc patet, quod, si ea ex earum multitudine concluderetur, non possemus maiorem partium multitudinem concipere; sed earum multitudo, quavis data, deberet esse maior, quod falsum est: nam in toto spatio, inter duos circulos, diversa centra habentes, duplo maiorem partium multitudinem concipimus, quam in eiusdem dimidio, et tamen partium numerus, tam dimidii, quam totius spatii omni assignabili numero maior est.", "en": "What I said in the letter on the infinite, that they do not conclude the infinity of the parts from their multitude, is evident from this: that if it were concluded from their multitude, we could not conceive a greater multitude of parts, but their multitude would have to be greater than any given one; which is false. For in the whole space between two circles having different centres we conceive a multitude of parts twice as great as in half of it, and yet the number of the parts, both of the half and of the whole space, is greater than any assignable number."}, {"id": "Ep81", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 81", "la": "Porro ex extensione, ut eam Cartesius concipit, molem scilicet quiescentem, corporum existentiam demonstrare non tantum difficile; ut ais; sed omnino impossibile est. Materia enim quiescens, quantum in se est, in sua quiete perseverabit, nec ad motum concitabitur, nisi a causa potentiori externa; et hac de causa non dubitavi olim affirmare; rerum naturalium principia Cartesiana inutilia esse, ne dicam absurda.", "en": "Further, from extension as Descartes conceives it, namely as a mass at rest, it is not only difficult, as you say, but altogether impossible to demonstrate the existence of bodies. For matter at rest, as far as it is in itself, will persevere in its rest, and will not be set in motion except by a more powerful external cause; and for this reason I did not hesitate to affirm once that Descartes’s principles of natural things are useless, not to say absurd."}, {"id": "Ep81.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 81", "la": "Hagae 5 Maii, 1676", "en": "The Hague, 5 May 1676."}, {"id": "Ep82", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 82", "la": "Velim, ut hac in re mihi gratificeris, indicando, qui ex conceptu extensionis secundum tuas meditationes varietas rerum a priori possit ostendi, quandoquidem meministi opinionis Cartesii, in qua Cartesius statuit, se eam ex extensione nullo alio modo deducere posse, quam supponendo motu a Deo excitato hoc effectum fuisse in extensione: deducit ergo iuxta meam opinionem corporum existentiam non ex quiescente materia, nisi forte suppositionem motoris Dei pro nihilo haberes; quandoquidem, qui illud ex essentia Dei a priori necessario sequi debeat, abs te non sit ostensum; id quod Cartesius ostensurus captum humanum superare credebat.", "en": "I should like you to do me the favour, in this matter, of indicating how, according to your meditations, the variety of things can be shown a priori from the concept of extension; since you mentioned the opinion of Descartes, in which Descartes maintains that he can deduce it from extension in no other way than by supposing that this was brought about in extension by motion excited by God. So in my opinion he deduces the existence of bodies not from matter at rest, unless perhaps you count the supposition of God as mover for nothing; since you have not shown how it must follow necessarily, a priori, from the essence of God, something which Descartes believed it beyond human grasp to show."}, {"id": "Ep82", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 82", "la": "Quare a te hanc rem requiro, sciens bene, te alias cogitationes habere, nisi alia sontica subsit forte causa, quare illud hactenus manifestum facere nolueris; et si hoc, de quo non dubito, non opus fuisset, non tale quid obscure indicares. Sed certo tibi persuasum habeas, quod sive candide mihi aliquid indices, sive celes, meus tamen erga te affectus immutatus maneat.", "en": "So I ask this of you, knowing well that you have other thoughts, unless perhaps there is some other serious cause why you have not wished to make it known until now; and if this, which I do not doubt, had not been necessary, you would not hint at any such thing obscurely. But be assured that whether you tell me something candidly or conceal it, my affection towards you will remain unchanged."}, {"id": "Ep82.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 82", "la": "Rationes tamen, cur illud specialiter desiderem, hae sunt, quod in mathematicis semper observarim, quod nos ex quavis re in se considerata, hoc est, ex definitione cuiusque rei, unicam saltem proprietatem deducere valeamus; quod si autem plures proprietates desideremus, necesse esse, ut rem definitam ad alia referamus: tunc siquidem ex coniunctione definitionum harum rerum novae proprietates resultant.", "en": "The reasons, however, why I particularly desire this are these: that in mathematics I have always observed that from any thing considered in itself, that is, from the definition of any thing, we are able to deduce only a single property; but if we desire more properties, it is necessary to relate the thing defined to other things; for then new properties result from the conjunction of the definitions of these things."}, {"id": "Ep82.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 82", "la": "Ex. gr. Si circuli peripheriam considerem solam, nihil aliud concludere potero, quam quod ubique sibi similis, sive uniformis existat, qua quidem proprietate ab omnibus aliis curvis essentialiter differt, nec ullas alias unquam potero deducere.", "en": "For example, if I consider the circumference of a circle alone, I shall be able to conclude nothing else than that it is everywhere like itself, or uniform, by which property it differs essentially from all other curves; and I shall never be able to deduce any others."}, {"id": "Ep82.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 82", "la": "Verum si ad alia referam, nimirum ad radios ex centro deductos, ad duas lineas sese intersecantes, aut plures quoque, plures utique hinc proprietates deducere valebo; quae quidem aliquo modo videntur adversari prop. 16. Ethices, quae praecipua fere est 1 lib. tui tractatus: In qua tanquam notum assumitur, posse ex data cuiuscunque rei definitione plures proprietates deduci, quod mihi videtur impossibile, si non ad alia referamus rem definitam; et quod porro effecit, ut non possim videre, qua ratione ex attributo aliquo solo considerato, ex. gr. extensione infinita corporum varietas exsurgere possit; vel si existimas hoc quoque non posse concludi ex unico solo considerato; sed omnibus simul sumptis, vellem hoc edoceri abs te, et qua ratione hoc concipiendum foret. Vale etc.", "en": "But if I relate it to other things, namely to the radii drawn from the centre, to two lines intersecting each other, or to more, I shall certainly be able to deduce more properties from this; which indeed seems in some way to conflict with Prop. 16 of the Ethics, which is almost the chief proposition of the first book of your treatise. In it it is taken as known that a number of properties can be deduced from the given definition of any thing, which seems to me impossible if we do not relate the thing defined to others; and this has further brought it about that I cannot see in what way, from some attribute considered alone, for example infinite extension, the variety of bodies can arise; or, if you think that this too cannot be inferred from one attribute considered alone, but from all taken together, I should like to be taught this by you, and in what way it is to be conceived. Farewell, etc."}, {"id": "Ep82.3", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 82", "la": "Parisiis, 23 Iun. 1676.", "en": "Paris, 23 June 1676."}, {"id": "Ep83", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 83", "la": "Quod petis, an ex solo extensionis conceptu rerum varietas a priori possit demonstrari, credo me iam satis clare ostendisse, id impossibile esse; ideoque materiam a Cartesio male definiri per extensionem; sed eam necessario debere explicari per attributum, quod aeternam, et infinitam essentiam exprimat.", "en": "As for your question whether the variety of things can be demonstrated a priori from the concept of extension alone, I believe I have already shown clearly enough that it is impossible; and that therefore matter is badly defined by Descartes through extension, but must necessarily be explained through an attribute which expresses eternal and infinite essence."}, {"id": "Ep83", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 83", "la": "Sed de his forsan aliquando, si vita suppetit, clarius tecum agam. Nam huc usque nihil de his ordine disponere mihi licuit.", "en": "But perhaps some day, if life lasts, I shall discuss these things with you more clearly. For up to now it has not been possible for me to set anything out in order on these matters."}, {"id": "Ep83", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 83", "la": "Quod autem addis, nos ex definitione cuiusque rei, in se consideratae, unicam tantum proprietatem deducere valere, locum forsan habet in rebus simplicissimis, vel entibus rationis, (ad quae figuras etiam refero) at non in realibus. Nam ex hoc solo, quod Deum definio esse ens ad cuius essentiam pertinet existentia, plures eius proprietates concludo: nempe quod necessario existit, quod sit unicus, immutabilis, infinitus, etc. et ad hunc modum plura alia exempla adferre possem, quae impraesentiarum omitto.", "en": "As for your addition that from the definition of each thing, considered in itself, we can deduce only one property: this perhaps holds in the simplest things, or beings of reason (to which I also refer figures), but not in real things. For from this alone, that I define God to be a being to whose essence existence pertains, I infer a number of his properties: namely that he exists necessarily, that he is unique, immutable, infinite, etc.; and in this way I could bring many other examples, which I omit for the present."}, {"id": "Ep83", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 83", "la": "Denique rogo, ut inquiras, an tractatus D. Huet, (nempe contra tractatum theologico-Politicum) de quo antea scripsisti, lucem iam viderit, et an mihi exemplar transmittere poteris, deinde an iam noveris, quaenam ea sint, quae de refractione noviter inventa sint.", "en": "Finally, I ask you to find out whether the treatise of Mr Huet (namely, against the Theological-Political Treatise), of which you wrote earlier, has yet seen the light, and whether you could send me a copy; and next, whether you already know what the things are that have recently been discovered about refraction."}, {"id": "Ep83.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 83", "la": "Hisce vale, Nobilissime vir, et amare perge, etc.", "en": "Farewell with these, most noble Sir, and continue to love me, etc."}, {"id": "Ep83.2", "p": "letters", "l": "Letter 83", "la": "Hagae Com. 15 Iulii. 1676.", "en": "The Hague, 15 July 1676."}];
